","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,500]],"l"],"C0662":["Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit High","Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit High: the signal voltage is above the expected range. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to battery"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"],["l","Supply wire pinched against B+ source"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,500]],"l"],"C0663":["Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic","Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic: the signal is intermittent or erratic. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Connector loose or oxidized"],["l","Connector with intermittent contact under vibration"],["l","Harness routed against vibrating component"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,400]],"l"],"C0664":["Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Correlation","Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Correlation: the value does not correlate with the related sensor or expected pattern. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Sensor or tone ring fault"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[100,800]],"l"],"C0665":["Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Incorrect Component Installed","Left Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Incorrect Component Installed: the installed component does not match the type the vehicle expects. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Wrong replacement part"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[60,400]],"l"],"C0666":["Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit/Open","Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit/Open: the circuit is open. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Sensor wiring broken"],["l","Pin pushed back out of connector body"],["l","Cracked solder joint inside the module or sensor"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,500]],"l"],"C0667":["Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Range/Performance","Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Range/Performance: the signal is implausible or outside the valid range. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Tone ring damaged or contaminated"],["l","Sensor element aged beyond calibration window"],["l","Sensor mounting loose - signal damping"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,600]],"l"],"C0668":["Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit Low","Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit Low: the signal voltage is below the expected range. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"],["l","Harness chafing through insulation"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,500]],"l"],"C0669":["Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit High","Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Circuit High: the signal voltage is above the expected range. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to battery"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"],["l","Supply wire pinched against B+ source"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,500]],"l"],"C066A":["Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic","Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic: the signal is intermittent or erratic. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Connector loose or oxidized"],["l","Connector with intermittent contact under vibration"],["l","Harness routed against vibrating component"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,400]],"l"],"C066B":["Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Correlation","Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Correlation: the value does not correlate with the related sensor or expected pattern. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Sensor or tone ring fault"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[100,800]],"l"],"C066C":["Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Incorrect Component Installed","Right Rear Wheel Speed Sensor B Incorrect Component Installed: the installed component does not match the type the vehicle expects. Wheel-speed signals feed ABS, ESC, traction control and the speedometer.",[["h","Wrong replacement part"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[60,400]],"l"],"C066D":["Service/Park Brake Chamber Pressure Sensor","Generic fault on service/park brake chamber pressure sensor — used on air-brake systems with combined service and spring-applied parking brake chamber.",[["h","Pressure sensor failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[200,1500]],"l"],"C066E":["Service/Park Brake Chamber Pressure Too High","Pressure in the service/park brake chamber exceeded safe limit.",[["h","Pressure regulator stuck"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,3000]],"l"],"C066F":["Service/Park Brake Chamber Pressure Too Low","Pressure in the service/park brake chamber dropped below required minimum — risk of insufficient braking force.",[["h","Air leak in brake system"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[200,2500]],"l"],"C0670":["Electric Park Brake Actuator Left Circuit","The left electric park brake caliper actuator does not respond as commanded.",[["h","Actuator seized due to corrosion"],["m","Motor winding open"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[200,800]],"l"],"C0671":["Electric Park Brake Actuator Right Circuit","The right electric park brake caliper actuator does not respond as commanded.",[["h","Actuator seized due to corrosion"],["m","Motor winding open"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[200,800]],"l"],"C0680":["Electric Park Brake Module Internal Fault","The EPB control module fails its internal self-test and the park brake function may be limited.",[["h","Module internal failure"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1400]],"l"],"C0700":["Active Steering Module Communication","A communication fault between the chassis control module and the active steering module has been detected. Active steering varies the steering ratio with speed, and a fault disables this assistance and may store secondary codes in other modules.",[["h","Communication bus fault"],["m","Active steering module failed"],["l","Power supply or ground fault to module"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[200,2000]],"l"],"C0710":["Steering Position Signal Malfunction","The chassis or stability control module has received an invalid or missing steering position signal — often via the communication bus from the steering control module. ESP, lane assist and electric power steering may be limited.",[["h","Steering angle sensor or steering control module failed"],["m","Communication bus fault between modules"],["l","Steering angle not calibrated"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[150,1000]],"l"],"C0712":["Power Steering Position Sensor Range/Performance","The power steering control module has detected that the rack or column position signal is out of range or implausible. Steering assist may be reduced.",[["h","Steering position sensor failed"],["m","Sensor calibration required after service"],["l","Sensor element aged beyond calibration window"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[150,900]],"l"],"C0715":["Power Steering Pressure Sensor Range/Performance","The power steering module has detected that the hydraulic pressure sensor signal is out of range — relevant for hydraulic and electro-hydraulic systems. Assist regulation may run in fallback mode.",[["h","Pressure sensor failed"],["m","Low power steering fluid level"],["l","Sensor element aged beyond calibration window"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[120,800]],"l"],"C0720":["Electric Power Steering Torque Sensor Circuit","The torque sensor in the EPS column or rack reports an electrical fault. Power assist is reduced or disabled.",[["h","Torque sensor failed"],["m","Wiring shorted"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1800]],"l"],"C0725":["Electric Power Steering Motor Circuit","The EPS assist motor circuit reports an electrical fault.",[["h","Motor winding open"],["m","Short to ground in motor wiring"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1800]],"l"],"C0727":["Trailer Connector Circuit Malfunction","The chassis or trailer brake module has detected a fault in the trailer wiring connector — for example open lighting circuit or short to ground. Trailer brake and lighting functions may be unavailable.",[["h","Connector pins corroded or bent"],["m","Short or open in trailer wiring"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[40,300]],"l"],"C0730":["Electric Power Steering Motor Position Sensor Circuit","The EPS motor position sensor (resolver/Hall) reports an implausible or missing signal.",[["h","Position sensor failed"],["m","Connector corroded"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[300,1400]],"l"],"C0735":["Electric Power Steering Module Internal Fault","The EPS control module fails its internal self-test. Power assist is disabled.",[["h","Module internal failure"],["m","Supply voltage unstable"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1800]],"l"],"C0740":["Electric Power Steering Voltage Supply","The supply voltage to the EPS module is out of range, reducing or disabling power assist.",[["h","Battery undervoltage during high steering load"],["m","Ground point corroded"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,400]],"l"],"C0745":["Electric Power Steering Overtemperature Protection","The EPS module has reduced or disabled assistance to protect the motor from overheating, typically after extended low-speed manoeuvring.",[["h","Prolonged low-speed operation (e.g. parking)"],["l","Motor thermal sensor drift"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[0,200]],"l"],"C0750":["Cruise Control Brake Switch","The cruise control or chassis module has detected an implausible signal from the dedicated cruise control brake switch. This switch is independent from the stop lamp switch and disengages cruise control when the driver brakes.",[["h","Cruise control brake switch failed or misadjusted"],["m","Wiring or connector fault"],["l","Internal cruise control module fault"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[30,300]],"l"],"C0784":["Trailer Brake Force Sensor Circuit Malfunction","The integrated trailer brake controller has detected an electrical fault in the brake force sensor circuit. Proportional trailer brake control is disabled — the controller defaults to a manual or fixed-gain mode.",[["h","Trailer brake force sensor failed"],["m","Wiring or connector fault"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[150,700]],"l"],"C0800":["Drive System Voltage Low","The chassis control module has detected that the supply voltage is below the operating threshold required for ABS, ESP or steering systems. Many chassis functions are limited or disabled until the voltage returns to a normal range.",[["h","Battery weak or discharged"],["m","Alternator output fault"],["l","High-resistance ground or supply connection"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[50,800]],"l"],"C0805":["Steering Angle Sensor Calibration","The chassis or stability control module reports that the steering angle sensor has not been calibrated or that the calibration data has been lost. ESP requires a calibrated zero position and is disabled until calibration is performed.",[["h","Calibration required after battery disconnect or repair"],["m","Sensor lost calibration data"],["l","Wheel alignment incorrect, blocking calibration"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[40,200]],"l"],"C0810":["Air Suspension Compressor Circuit","The air suspension compressor motor circuit reports an electrical fault. Levelling is disabled.",[["h","Compressor motor failed"],["m","Compressor relay failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[300,1500]],"l"],"C0815":["Air Suspension Compressor Overtemperature","The air suspension compressor has been disabled to protect against overheating, typically after a slow leak.",[["h","Slow leak in air spring or line"],["m","Compressor worn"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[200,2000]],"l"],"C0820":["Air Suspension Height Sensor Front Left","The front left ride height sensor reports an implausible or missing signal.",[["h","Sensor linkage broken"],["m","Sensor failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[120,500]],"l"],"C0821":["Air Suspension Height Sensor Front Right","The front right ride height sensor reports an implausible or missing signal.",[["h","Sensor linkage broken"],["m","Sensor failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[120,500]],"l"],"C0822":["Air Suspension Height Sensor Rear Left","The rear left ride height sensor reports an implausible or missing signal.",[["h","Sensor linkage broken"],["m","Sensor failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[120,500]],"l"],"C0823":["Air Suspension Height Sensor Rear Right","The rear right ride height sensor reports an implausible or missing signal.",[["h","Sensor linkage broken"],["m","Sensor failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[120,500]],"l"],"C0825":["Steering Effort Sensor Range/Performance","The electric power steering module has detected that the torque (effort) sensor signal is out of the expected range. Steering assist is reduced or disabled.",[["h","Steering torque sensor failed"],["m","Calibration required after service"],["l","Sensor element aged beyond calibration window"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[300,1500]],"l"],"C0827":["Steering Effort Sensor Stuck","The power steering module has detected that the torque sensor signal does not change despite steering input — the sensor appears stuck. Assist is disabled to avoid uncontrolled steering torque.",[["h","Sensor mechanically stuck"],["m","Sensor electronics failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[300,1500]],"l"],"C0830":["Steering Wheel Angle Sensor Center Position Not Learned","The chassis control module has not stored a valid center (zero) position for the steering wheel angle sensor. ESP, lane assist and electric power steering may be limited until calibration is performed.",[["h","Sensor calibration not performed after replacement"],["m","Wheel alignment changed without calibration"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[50,300]],"l"],"C0840":["Air Suspension Solenoid Valve Circuit","One of the level control solenoid valves reports an electrical fault. The corresponding axle cannot be levelled.",[["h","Solenoid coil open"],["m","Connector water damage"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[200,900]],"l"],"C0850":["Active Damping Control Front Left","The front left adjustable damper reports an electrical fault.",[["h","Damper solenoid coil open"],["m","Wiring chafe at strut"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1800]],"l"],"C0851":["Active Damping Control Front Right","The front right adjustable damper reports an electrical fault.",[["h","Damper solenoid coil open"],["m","Wiring chafe at strut"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1800]],"l"],"C0852":["Active Damping Control Rear Left","The rear left adjustable damper reports an electrical fault.",[["h","Damper solenoid coil open"],["m","Wiring chafe"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1800]],"l"],"C0853":["Active Damping Control Rear Right","The rear right adjustable damper reports an electrical fault.",[["h","Damper solenoid coil open"],["m","Wiring chafe"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1800]],"l"],"C0860":["Trailer Stability Assist Performance","The trailer stability assist function detects implausible trailer sway response or sensor input.",[["m","Trailer module communication fault"],["m","Yaw sensor drift"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,700]],"l"],"C0870":["Adaptive Cruise Control Brake Request Performance","A brake request from adaptive cruise control or AEB cannot be executed within the expected response time.",[["h","Bus communication latency"],["m","Hydraulic block slow to build pressure"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[200,1600]],"l"],"C0900":["TPMS Sensor 1 Battery","TPMS Sensor 1 Battery: the control module has detected this fault condition.",[["h","TPMS sensor battery depleted (end of life)"],["l","Sensor electronics failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0901":["TPMS Sensor 1 Position Performance","The tire pressure monitoring receiver cannot reliably localize sensor 1 to a wheel position. Pressure values may be assigned to the wrong wheel until relearn is completed.",[["h","Position relearn required after rotation"],["m","Weak sensor signal at receiver"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[0,80]],"l"],"C0902":["TPMS Sensor 2 Battery","TPMS Sensor 2 Battery: the control module has detected this fault condition.",[["h","TPMS sensor battery depleted (end of life)"],["l","Sensor electronics failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0903":["TPMS Sensor 2 Position Performance","The tire pressure monitoring receiver cannot reliably localize sensor 2 to a wheel position. Pressure values may be assigned to the wrong wheel until relearn is completed.",[["h","Position relearn required"],["m","Weak sensor signal at receiver"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["easy",[0,80]],"l"],"C0905":["TPMS Sensor 3 Battery Low","The TPMS receiver reports that the internal battery of sensor 3 is at end of life. The sensor must be replaced as the battery is sealed inside the housing.",[["h","TPMS sensor battery depleted"],["l","Sensor electronics failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0907":["TPMS Sensor 4 Battery Low","The TPMS receiver reports that the internal battery of sensor 4 is at end of life. The sensor must be replaced as the battery is sealed inside the housing.",[["h","TPMS sensor battery depleted"],["l","Sensor electronics failed"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0910":["TPMS Receiver Circuit Malfunction","The body or chassis module has detected a fault in the TPMS RF receiver — for example open antenna or internal failure. No tire pressure data can be received.",[["h","TPMS receiver failed"],["m","Antenna lead open or shorted"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[120,600]],"l"],"C0912":["TPMS Initiator Front Circuit Malfunction","The TPMS module has detected an electrical fault in the front low-frequency initiator (used to wake and locate sensors). Position learning of the front sensors fails.",[["h","LF initiator coil failed"],["m","Wiring or connector fault"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[120,500]],"l"],"C0915":["Active Roll Stabilisation Front Actuator Circuit","The front active anti-roll bar actuator reports an electrical fault.",[["h","Actuator motor winding open"],["m","Hydraulic supply pressure low"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[600,2500]],"l"],"C0916":["Active Roll Stabilisation Rear Actuator Circuit","The rear active anti-roll bar actuator reports an electrical fault.",[["h","Actuator motor winding open"],["m","Hydraulic supply pressure low"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[600,2500]],"l"],"C0920":["Suspension Control Module Internal Fault","The suspension control module fails its internal self-test. Adaptive damping or air suspension is disabled.",[["h","Module internal failure"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[400,1600]],"l"],"C0950":["Tire Pressure Monitoring Sensor Front Left","The TPMS sensor at the front left wheel does not transmit a valid signal. Indirect TPMS variants raise this when the wheel-speed-based estimation fails for that wheel.",[["h","Sensor battery depleted"],["m","Sensor damaged during tire change"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0951":["Tire Pressure Monitoring Sensor Front Right","The TPMS sensor at the front right wheel does not transmit a valid signal.",[["h","Sensor battery depleted"],["m","Sensor damaged during tire change"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0952":["Tire Pressure Monitoring Sensor Rear Left","The TPMS sensor at the rear left wheel does not transmit a valid signal.",[["h","Sensor battery depleted"],["m","Sensor damaged during tire change"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0953":["Tire Pressure Monitoring Sensor Rear Right","The TPMS sensor at the rear right wheel does not transmit a valid signal.",[["h","Sensor battery depleted"],["m","Sensor damaged during tire change"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["medium",[60,250]],"l"],"C0960":["Tire Pressure Monitoring Module Internal Fault","The tire pressure monitoring module fails its internal self-test.",[["h","Module internal failure"]],["ABS warning light on","ESC/stability control deactivated","Possible reduced brake assist"],["hard",[200,800]],"l"],"P0001":["Fuel Volume Regulator A Control Circuit/Open","The fuel volume regulator (also called metering unit or suction control valve) is a solenoid valve on the inlet side of the high-pressure pump, used mainly on common-rail diesel engines. The engine control module pulses it to set how much fuel the pump draws in and delivers to the rail, and in this way controls rail pressure.",[["h","Connector at the regulator loose, corroded or unplugged"],["m","Solenoid coil of the regulator open"],["m","Broken wire in the harness, e.g. chafing or rodent damage"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Engine cranks but does not start, or stalls (normally closed valve)","Loss of power or limp mode (normally open valve)"],["medium",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0002":["Fuel Volume Regulator A Control Circuit Range/Performance","The fuel volume regulator (also called metering unit or suction control valve) is a solenoid valve on the inlet side of the high-pressure pump, used mainly on common-rail diesel engines. The engine control module pulses it to set how much fuel the pump draws in and delivers to the rail, and in this way controls rail pressure.",[["h","Regulator sticking or worn due to dirt, debris or water in the fuel"],["m","High resistance at connector or wiring causing wrong current"],["m","Low-pressure supply problem: clogged filter or air in the fuel"],["l","Rail pressure sensor reading wrong values"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode","Jerking or unstable idle"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0003":["Fuel Volume Regulator A Control Circuit Low","The fuel volume regulator (also called metering unit or suction control valve) is a solenoid valve on the inlet side of the high-pressure pump, used mainly on common-rail diesel engines. The engine control module pulses it to set how much fuel the pump draws in and delivers to the rail, and in this way controls rail pressure.",[["h","Harness chafed through, short to ground"],["m","Shorted turns in the regulator coil (resistance too low)"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling","Loss of power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0004":["Fuel Volume Regulator A Control Circuit High","The fuel volume regulator (also called metering unit or suction control valve) is a solenoid valve on the inlet side of the high-pressure pump, used mainly on common-rail diesel engines. The engine control module pulses it to set how much fuel the pump draws in and delivers to the rail, and in this way controls rail pressure.",[["h","Short to battery voltage in the harness"],["m","Open coil or wire, reported as high with ground-side switching"],["m","Connector fault at the regulator"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode","Rough running or diesel knock possible"],["medium",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0005":["Fuel Shutoff Valve A Control Circuit/Open","The fuel shutoff valve is a solenoid valve that opens or blocks the fuel supply. On diesel engines it is usually closed without current, so that the engine stops reliably when switched off.",[["h","Wire or connector interrupted or corroded"],["m","Valve coil open"],["m","Fuse or relay in the valve supply faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Engine cranks but does not start","Engine cuts out suddenly","Check engine light on"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0006":["Fuel Shutoff Valve A Control Circuit Low","The fuel shutoff valve is a solenoid valve that opens or blocks the fuel supply. On diesel engines it is usually closed without current, so that the engine stops reliably when switched off.",[["h","Short to ground in the harness"],["m","Shorted turns in the valve coil"],["m","Corroded connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or no start","Engine stalls"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0007":["Fuel Shutoff Valve A Control Circuit High","The fuel shutoff valve is a solenoid valve that opens or blocks the fuel supply. On diesel engines it is usually closed without current, so that the engine stops reliably when switched off.",[["h","Control wire open or shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Valve coil open"],["m","Connector fault at the valve"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or no start","Engine stalls"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0008":["Engine Position System Performance Bank 1","The engine control module learns how far each camshaft of bank 1 is offset from the crankshaft and compares this with a reference. P0008 is set when both camshafts of this bank are shifted in the same direction beyond a threshold.",[["h","Primary or timing chain stretched"],["m","Chain tensioner worn or loose"],["m","Timing drive fitted incorrectly after an engine repair"],["l","Crankshaft reluctor wheel slipped"]],["Check engine light on","Timing chain rattle, especially at cold start","Loss of power or rough running"],["shop_only",[1500,4000]],"c"],"P0009":["Engine Position System Performance Bank 2","The engine control module learns how far each camshaft of bank 2 is offset from the crankshaft and compares this with a reference. P0009 is set when both camshafts of this bank are shifted in the same direction beyond a threshold.",[["h","Primary or timing chain stretched"],["m","Chain tensioner worn or loose"],["m","Timing drive fitted incorrectly after an engine repair"],["l","Crankshaft reluctor wheel slipped"]],["Check engine light on","Timing chain rattle, especially at cold start","Loss of power or rough running"],["shop_only",[1500,4000]],"c"],"P000A":["A Camshaft Position Slow Response Bank 1","This monitor does not judge the end position of the intake camshaft on bank 1 but how fast it follows a change of the commanded angle. The code is set when the actual angle lags behind the target for too long during several adjustments.",[["h","Oil too thick or too thin, sludged, or oil level low"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve partly clogged"],["m","Oil control valve sluggish"],["m","Cam phaser leaking internally or worn"]],["Check engine light on","Slight hesitation when accelerating","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[60,1500]],"c"],"P000B":["B Camshaft Position Slow Response Bank 1","This monitor does not judge the end position of the exhaust camshaft on bank 1 but how fast it follows a change of the commanded angle. The code is set when the actual angle lags behind the target for too long during several adjustments.",[["h","Oil too thick or too thin, sludged, or oil level low"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve partly clogged"],["m","Oil control valve sluggish"],["m","Cam phaser leaking internally or worn"]],["Check engine light on","Slight hesitation when accelerating","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[60,1500]],"c"],"P000C":["A Camshaft Position Slow Response Bank 2","This monitor does not judge the end position of the intake camshaft on bank 2 but how fast it follows a change of the commanded angle. The code is set when the actual angle lags behind the target for too long during several adjustments.",[["h","Oil too thick or too thin, sludged, or oil level low"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve partly clogged"],["m","Oil control valve sluggish"],["m","Cam phaser leaking internally or worn"]],["Check engine light on","Slight hesitation when accelerating","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[60,1500]],"c"],"P000D":["B Camshaft Position Slow Response Bank 2","This monitor does not judge the end position of the exhaust camshaft on bank 2 but how fast it follows a change of the commanded angle. The code is set when the actual angle lags behind the target for too long during several adjustments.",[["h","Oil too thick or too thin, sludged, or oil level low"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve partly clogged"],["m","Oil control valve sluggish"],["m","Cam phaser leaking internally or worn"]],["Check engine light on","Slight hesitation when accelerating","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[60,1500]],"c"],"P000E":["Fuel Volume Regulator Control Exceeded Learning Limit","The fuel volume regulator (also called metering unit or suction control valve) is a solenoid valve on the inlet side of the high-pressure pump, used mainly on common-rail diesel engines. The engine control module pulses it to set how much fuel the pump draws in and delivers to the rail, and in this way controls rail pressure.",[["h","Fuel volume regulator worn or contaminated"],["m","High-pressure pump worn, delivering less"],["m","Low-pressure side: clogged filter, weak lift pump or air in the fuel"],["m","Not calibrated after pump or regulator replacement"]],["Check engine light on","Often hardly noticeable at first, later loss of power","Hard starting if rail pressure is wrong"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P000F":["Fuel System Over Pressure Relief Valve Activated","On common-rail diesel and some gasoline direct-injection systems a spring-loaded pressure relief valve protects the rail and injectors: if pressure rises too far, it opens and returns fuel to the tank, often holding a reduced pressure for limp-home operation. The control module recognises the opening from the rail pressure signal and stores P000F.",[["h","Fuel volume regulator stuck open or without current, pump over-delivers"],["m","Rail pressure sensor reading wrong, control runs into overpressure"],["m","Pressure control valve faulty, where fitted"],["l","Fuel return line blocked or kinked"]],["Check engine light on","Sudden loss of power or limp mode","Engine may stall"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0010":["\"A\" Camshaft Position Actuator Circuit (Bank 1)","The engine control module drives the oil control valve (a pulse-width modulated solenoid) of the variable valve timing on the intake camshaft on bank 1. It checks the electrical state of this output and sets the code when the circuit is open, or when the measured state does not match the command, for example because of a short.",[["h","Solenoid coil of the oil control valve open or shorted"],["h","Connector corroded, loose or soaked with engine oil wicking through the valve"],["m","Wiring harness chafed through or broken"],["m","Supply voltage missing, e.g. blown fuse"]],["Check engine light on","Less pulling power at low and medium engine speed","Rough idle in some engines"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0011":["\"A\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Advanced or System Performance (Bank 1)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the intake camshaft on bank 1 with the actual position it calculates from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft sits further advanced than commanded for longer than a calibrated time, or, depending on the manufacturer, when the target is missed in either direction (\"system performance\").",[["h","Oil level too low, oil sludged or wrong viscosity"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve clogged with debris"],["h","Oil control valve mechanically sticking"],["m","Cam phaser worn or its locking pin sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Rough or unstable idle, as valve overlap is too large","Engine may stall at idle"],["medium",[60,1800]],"c"],"P0012":["\"A\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Retarded (Bank 1)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the intake camshaft on bank 1 with the actual position from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft stays further retarded than commanded for longer than a calibrated time.",[["h","Oil pressure or oil level too low, oil worn out"],["h","Oil control valve sticking closed or its screen blocked"],["m","Cam phaser defective or seized in its rest position"],["m","Timing chain stretched or jumped a tooth"]],["Check engine light on","Noticeably less torque and pulling power","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0013":["\"B\" Camshaft Position Actuator Circuit (Bank 1)","The engine control module drives the oil control valve (a pulse-width modulated solenoid) of the variable valve timing on the exhaust camshaft on bank 1. It checks the electrical state of this output and sets the code when the circuit is open, or when the measured state does not match the command, for example because of a short.",[["h","Solenoid coil of the oil control valve open or shorted"],["h","Connector corroded, loose or soaked with engine oil wicking through the valve"],["m","Wiring harness chafed through or broken"],["m","Supply voltage missing, e.g. blown fuse"]],["Check engine light on","Less pulling power at low and medium engine speed","Rough idle in some engines"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0014":["\"B\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Advanced or System Performance (Bank 1)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the exhaust camshaft on bank 1 with the actual position it calculates from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft sits further advanced than commanded for longer than a calibrated time, or, depending on the manufacturer, when the target is missed in either direction (\"system performance\").",[["h","Oil level too low, oil sludged or wrong viscosity"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve clogged with debris"],["h","Oil control valve mechanically sticking"],["m","Cam phaser worn or its locking pin sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle and hesitation","Engine may stall at idle"],["medium",[60,1800]],"c"],"P0015":["\"B\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Retarded (Bank 1)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the exhaust camshaft on bank 1 with the actual position from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft stays further retarded than commanded for longer than a calibrated time.",[["h","Oil pressure or oil level too low, oil worn out"],["h","Oil control valve sticking closed or its screen blocked"],["m","Cam phaser defective or seized in its rest position"],["m","Timing chain stretched or jumped a tooth"]],["Check engine light on","Noticeably less torque and pulling power","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0016":["Crankshaft Position - Camshaft Position Correlation (Bank 1 Sensor A)","The engine control module checks whether the signal of the intake camshaft on bank 1 (sensor A) arrives at the expected crankshaft position, usually while the camshaft phaser is in its rest position. P0016 is set when the deviation stays outside a calibrated window.",[["h","Timing chain stretched, or belt or chain jumped a tooth"],["m","Camshaft phaser stuck at an end stop, oil control valve or oil pressure faulty"],["m","Incorrect assembly after a belt or chain replacement"],["l","Camshaft or crankshaft sensor signal disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Long cranking or hard starting","Rough idle and loss of power"],["hard",[60,4000]],"c"],"P0017":["Crankshaft Position - Camshaft Position Correlation (Bank 1 Sensor B)","The engine control module checks whether the signal of the exhaust camshaft on bank 1 (sensor B) arrives at the expected crankshaft position, usually while the camshaft phaser is in its rest position. P0017 is set when the deviation stays outside a calibrated window.",[["h","Timing chain stretched, or belt or chain jumped a tooth"],["m","Camshaft phaser stuck at an end stop, oil control valve or oil pressure faulty"],["m","Incorrect assembly after a belt or chain replacement"],["l","Camshaft or crankshaft sensor signal disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Long cranking or hard starting","Rough idle and loss of power"],["hard",[60,4000]],"c"],"P0018":["Crankshaft Position - Camshaft Position Correlation (Bank 2 Sensor A)","The engine control module checks whether the signal of the intake camshaft on bank 2 (sensor A) arrives at the expected crankshaft position, usually while the camshaft phaser is in its rest position. P0018 is set when the deviation stays outside a calibrated window.",[["h","Timing chain stretched, or belt or chain jumped a tooth"],["m","Camshaft phaser stuck at an end stop, oil control valve or oil pressure faulty"],["m","Incorrect assembly after a belt or chain replacement"],["l","Camshaft or crankshaft sensor signal disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Long cranking or hard starting","Rough idle and loss of power"],["hard",[60,4000]],"c"],"P0019":["Crankshaft Position - Camshaft Position Correlation (Bank 2 Sensor B)","The engine control module checks whether the signal of the exhaust camshaft on bank 2 (sensor B) arrives at the expected crankshaft position, usually while the camshaft phaser is in its rest position. P0019 is set when the deviation stays outside a calibrated window.",[["h","Timing chain stretched, or belt or chain jumped a tooth"],["m","Camshaft phaser stuck at an end stop, oil control valve or oil pressure faulty"],["m","Incorrect assembly after a belt or chain replacement"],["l","Camshaft or crankshaft sensor signal disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Long cranking or hard starting","Rough idle and loss of power"],["hard",[60,4000]],"c"],"P001A":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Circuit/Open Bank 1","A camshaft profile actuator switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 1 between two or more fixed cam profiles with different valve lift and duration, for example by shifting a cam piece axially or by locking rocker arms or tappets with oil pressure. Unlike a camshaft phaser it does not shift the timing continuously.",[["h","Connector at the actuator corroded, oil-soaked or loose"],["m","Wire broken, e.g. at the valve cover"],["m","Actuator coil open"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power at high engine speed because the profile does not switch","Higher consumption or uneven part-load running, depending on the profile"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P001B":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Circuit Low Bank 1","A camshaft profile actuator switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 1 between two or more fixed cam profiles with different valve lift and duration, for example by shifting a cam piece axially or by locking rocker arms or tappets with oil pressure. Unlike a camshaft phaser it does not shift the timing continuously.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Connector corroded or contaminated with oil"],["m","Shorted turns in the actuator coil"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power at high engine speed because the profile does not switch","Higher consumption or uneven part-load running, depending on the profile"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P001C":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Circuit High Bank 1","A camshaft profile actuator switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 1 between two or more fixed cam profiles with different valve lift and duration, for example by shifting a cam piece axially or by locking rocker arms or tappets with oil pressure. Unlike a camshaft phaser it does not shift the timing continuously.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Actuator coil shorted, causing overcurrent"],["m","Connector corroded or contaminated with oil"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power at high engine speed because the profile does not switch","Higher consumption or uneven part-load running, depending on the profile"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P001D":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Circuit/Open Bank 2","A camshaft profile actuator switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 2 between two or more fixed cam profiles with different valve lift and duration, for example by shifting a cam piece axially or by locking rocker arms or tappets with oil pressure. Unlike a camshaft phaser it does not shift the timing continuously.",[["h","Connector at the actuator corroded, oil-soaked or loose"],["m","Wire broken, e.g. at the valve cover"],["m","Actuator coil open"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power at high engine speed because the profile does not switch","Higher consumption or uneven part-load running, depending on the profile"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P001E":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Circuit Low Bank 2","A camshaft profile actuator switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 2 between two or more fixed cam profiles with different valve lift and duration, for example by shifting a cam piece axially or by locking rocker arms or tappets with oil pressure. Unlike a camshaft phaser it does not shift the timing continuously.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Connector corroded or contaminated with oil"],["m","Shorted turns in the actuator coil"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power at high engine speed because the profile does not switch","Higher consumption or uneven part-load running, depending on the profile"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P001F":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Circuit High Bank 2","A camshaft profile actuator switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 2 between two or more fixed cam profiles with different valve lift and duration, for example by shifting a cam piece axially or by locking rocker arms or tappets with oil pressure. Unlike a camshaft phaser it does not shift the timing continuously.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Actuator coil shorted, causing overcurrent"],["m","Connector corroded or contaminated with oil"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power at high engine speed because the profile does not switch","Higher consumption or uneven part-load running, depending on the profile"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0020":["\"A\" Camshaft Position Actuator Circuit (Bank 2)","The engine control module drives the oil control valve (a pulse-width modulated solenoid) of the variable valve timing on the intake camshaft on bank 2. It checks the electrical state of this output and sets the code when the circuit is open, or when the measured state does not match the command, for example because of a short.",[["h","Solenoid coil of the oil control valve open or shorted"],["h","Connector corroded, loose or soaked with engine oil wicking through the valve"],["m","Wiring harness chafed through or broken"],["m","Supply voltage missing, e.g. blown fuse"]],["Check engine light on","Less pulling power at low and medium engine speed","Rough idle in some engines"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0021":["\"A\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Advanced or System Performance (Bank 2)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the intake camshaft on bank 2 with the actual position it calculates from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft sits further advanced than commanded for longer than a calibrated time, or, depending on the manufacturer, when the target is missed in either direction (\"system performance\").",[["h","Oil level too low, oil sludged or wrong viscosity"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve clogged with debris"],["h","Oil control valve mechanically sticking"],["m","Cam phaser worn or its locking pin sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Rough or unstable idle, as valve overlap is too large","Engine may stall at idle"],["medium",[60,1800]],"c"],"P0022":["\"A\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Retarded (Bank 2)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the intake camshaft on bank 2 with the actual position from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft stays further retarded than commanded for longer than a calibrated time.",[["h","Oil pressure or oil level too low, oil worn out"],["h","Oil control valve sticking closed or its screen blocked"],["m","Cam phaser defective or seized in its rest position"],["m","Timing chain stretched or jumped a tooth"]],["Check engine light on","Noticeably less torque and pulling power","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0023":["\"B\" Camshaft Position Actuator Circuit (Bank 2)","The engine control module drives the oil control valve (a pulse-width modulated solenoid) of the variable valve timing on the exhaust camshaft on bank 2. It checks the electrical state of this output and sets the code when the circuit is open, or when the measured state does not match the command, for example because of a short.",[["h","Solenoid coil of the oil control valve open or shorted"],["h","Connector corroded, loose or soaked with engine oil wicking through the valve"],["m","Wiring harness chafed through or broken"],["m","Supply voltage missing, e.g. blown fuse"]],["Check engine light on","Less pulling power at low and medium engine speed","Rough idle in some engines"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0024":["\"B\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Advanced or System Performance (Bank 2)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the exhaust camshaft on bank 2 with the actual position it calculates from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft sits further advanced than commanded for longer than a calibrated time, or, depending on the manufacturer, when the target is missed in either direction (\"system performance\").",[["h","Oil level too low, oil sludged or wrong viscosity"],["h","Screen at the oil control valve clogged with debris"],["h","Oil control valve mechanically sticking"],["m","Cam phaser worn or its locking pin sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle and hesitation","Engine may stall at idle"],["medium",[60,1800]],"c"],"P0025":["\"B\" Camshaft Position - Timing Over-Retarded (Bank 2)","The engine control module compares the commanded position of the exhaust camshaft on bank 2 with the actual position from the camshaft and crankshaft sensors. This code is set when the camshaft stays further retarded than commanded for longer than a calibrated time.",[["h","Oil pressure or oil level too low, oil worn out"],["h","Oil control valve sticking closed or its screen blocked"],["m","Cam phaser defective or seized in its rest position"],["m","Timing chain stretched or jumped a tooth"]],["Check engine light on","Noticeably less torque and pulling power","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0026":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Range/Performance (Bank 1)","A solenoid valve routes engine oil to a valve control element on the intake side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer this is a valve lift changeover or a camshaft phaser.",[["h","Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["h","Feedback oil pressure switch faulty or leaking (systems with switch)"],["m","Screen in front of the solenoid clogged, or solenoid sticking"],["m","Mechanical fault in the valve train, e.g. locking pin or phaser sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, no extra pull at higher engine speed","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0027":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Range/Performance (Bank 1)","A solenoid valve routes engine oil to a valve control element on the exhaust side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer this is a valve lift changeover or a camshaft phaser.",[["h","Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["h","Feedback oil pressure switch faulty or leaking (systems with switch)"],["m","Screen in front of the solenoid clogged, or solenoid sticking"],["m","Mechanical fault in the valve train, e.g. locking pin or phaser sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, no extra pull at higher engine speed","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0028":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Range/Performance (Bank 2)","A solenoid valve routes engine oil to a valve control element on the intake side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer this is a valve lift changeover or a camshaft phaser.",[["h","Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["h","Feedback oil pressure switch faulty or leaking (systems with switch)"],["m","Screen in front of the solenoid clogged, or solenoid sticking"],["m","Mechanical fault in the valve train, e.g. locking pin or phaser sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, no extra pull at higher engine speed","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0029":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Range/Performance (Bank 2)","A solenoid valve routes engine oil to a valve control element on the exhaust side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer this is a valve lift changeover or a camshaft phaser.",[["h","Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["h","Feedback oil pressure switch faulty or leaking (systems with switch)"],["m","Screen in front of the solenoid clogged, or solenoid sticking"],["m","Mechanical fault in the valve train, e.g. locking pin or phaser sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, no extra pull at higher engine speed","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0030":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 1 Sensor 1)","The upstream oxygen sensor on bank 1 (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) only delivers a usable signal once its ceramic element is hot. A heating element inside the sensor gets it there quickly after a cold start.",[["h","Heater circuit interrupted, e.g. broken or chafed wire near the exhaust"],["h","Heating element inside the sensor burnt out"],["m","Connector corroded or terminal pushed back"],["m","Heater supply fuse or relay faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0031":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 1 Sensor 1)","The upstream oxygen sensor on bank 1 (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) only delivers a usable signal once its ceramic element is hot. A heating element inside the sensor gets it there quickly after a cold start.",[["h","Heating element burnt out or high resistance (too little current)"],["h","Supply wire interrupted or fuse blown"],["m","Control wire to the engine control module interrupted"],["m","Corroded connector with high contact resistance"]],["Check engine light on","Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start","Further heater codes if a shared fuse has blown"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0032":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 1 Sensor 1)","The upstream oxygen sensor on bank 1 (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) only delivers a usable signal once its ceramic element is hot. A heating element inside the sensor gets it there quickly after a cold start.",[["h","Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness"],["m","Heating element shorted internally (resistance too low)"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector causing leakage between pins"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start","Fuse may blow repeatedly"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P0033":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit","The turbocharger bypass valve A is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve: it connects the pressure side of the compressor with the intake side upstream of the turbocharger. It opens when the throttle closes quickly at high boost, so the compressor does not surge and the compressor wheel keeps its speed for the next acceleration.",[["h","Wire or connector interrupted, corroded or chafed"],["h","Solenoid coil or electric bypass valve faulty"],["m","Supply missing, e.g. fuse or main relay"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or hissing when lifting off the throttle if the valve stays closed","Weak response after gear changes"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0034":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit Low","The turbocharger bypass valve A is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve: it connects the pressure side of the compressor with the intake side upstream of the turbocharger. It opens when the throttle closes quickly at high boost, so the compressor does not surge and the compressor wheel keeps its speed for the next acceleration.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Valve shorted internally"],["m","Supply missing, e.g. fuse or main relay"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or hissing when lifting off the throttle if the valve stays closed","Weak response after gear changes"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0035":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit High","The turbocharger bypass valve A is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve: it connects the pressure side of the compressor with the intake side upstream of the turbocharger. It opens when the throttle closes quickly at high boost, so the compressor does not surge and the compressor wheel keeps its speed for the next acceleration.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Loose contact or broken wire, e.g. harness vibrating from heat"],["m","Solenoid coil faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or hissing when lifting off the throttle if the valve stays closed","Weak response after gear changes"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0036":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 1 Sensor 2)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 1 is the downstream sensor 2 behind the (first) catalytic converter, used mainly to monitor the converter. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heating element inside the sensor burnt out"],["h","Wire or connector damaged by heat or road spray"],["m","Heater fuse blown; a shared fuse can set several heater codes"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0037":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 1 Sensor 2)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 1 is the downstream sensor 2 behind the (first) catalytic converter, used mainly to monitor the converter. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or heating element burnt out (system-dependent)"],["m","Supply interrupted or fuse blown"],["m","Corroded connector with high contact resistance"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0038":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 1 Sensor 2)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 1 is the downstream sensor 2 behind the (first) catalytic converter, used mainly to monitor the converter. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness"],["m","Heating element shorted internally"],["m","Water in the connector causing leakage between pins"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0039":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit Range/Performance","The turbocharger bypass valve A is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve: it connects the pressure side of the compressor with the intake side upstream of the turbocharger. It opens when the throttle closes quickly at high boost, so the compressor does not surge and the compressor wheel keeps its speed for the next acceleration.",[["h","Valve sticking from carbon or oil mist, or diaphragm torn"],["m","Vacuum line or switching valve leaking (pneumatic design)"],["m","Position sensor in the valve faulty"],["l","Increased resistance in the wiring"]],["Check engine light on","Fluctuating or too little boost","Loss of power"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P003A":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Position Exceeded Learning Limit","The electric boost actuator A positions the variable turbine vanes or the wastegate and reports its position through a sensor. In a learning run, for example with the engine warm at idle or after switching off, the control module drives the actuator to its mechanical end stops and stores the sensor values as reference.",[["h","Electronic actuator or its control module faulty"],["m","Vane mechanism, sector gear or linkage worn or sticking"],["m","Replacement not learned in, or learning run aborted (e.g. low voltage)"],["l","Actuator lever broken"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power mode","Underboost or overboost codes possible"],["shop_only",[400,3500]],"c"],"P003B":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Position Exceeded Learning Limit","The electric boost actuator B positions the variable turbine vanes or the wastegate and reports its position through a sensor. In a learning run, for example with the engine warm at idle or after switching off, the control module drives the actuator to its mechanical end stops and stores the sensor values as reference.",[["h","Electronic actuator or its control module faulty"],["m","Vane mechanism, sector gear or linkage worn or sticking"],["m","Replacement not learned in, or learning run aborted (e.g. low voltage)"],["l","Actuator lever broken"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power mode","Underboost or overboost codes possible"],["shop_only",[400,3500]],"c"],"P003C":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Performance/Stuck Off Bank 1","The camshaft profile actuator A switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 1 between fixed cam profiles, usually a low-lift base profile and a high-lift profile. In most designs the unactuated state (\"off\") is the base profile; which profile counts as \"on\" is defined by the manufacturer.",[["h","Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludged oil, so switching pressure is missing"],["h","Profile switching solenoid sticking or its screen clogged"],["m","Oil supply to the switching elements leaking or restricted, e.g. worn seals"],["m","Locking pin in tappet or rocker arm, or sliding cam piece, sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power at higher engine speed","Reduced power mode on some vehicles"],["medium",[80,3500]],"c"],"P003D":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Stuck On Bank 1","The camshaft profile actuator A switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 1 between fixed cam profiles, usually a low-lift base profile and a high-lift profile. In most designs the unactuated state (\"off\") is the base profile; which profile counts as \"on\" is defined by the manufacturer.",[["h","Profile switching solenoid stuck open, oil pressure is not released"],["m","Locking pin does not return (weak spring, burr or dirt)"],["m","Oil pressure too high or drain from the switching gallery blocked"],["l","Sliding cam piece stuck in the high-lift position"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle and jerking at low engine speed","Poor running when cold"],["medium",[80,3500]],"c"],"P003E":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Performance/Stuck Off Bank 2","The camshaft profile actuator A switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 2 between fixed cam profiles, usually a low-lift base profile and a high-lift profile. In most designs the unactuated state (\"off\") is the base profile; which profile counts as \"on\" is defined by the manufacturer.",[["h","Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludged oil, so switching pressure is missing"],["h","Profile switching solenoid sticking or its screen clogged"],["m","Oil supply to the switching elements leaking or restricted, e.g. worn seals"],["m","Locking pin in tappet or rocker arm, or sliding cam piece, sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power at higher engine speed","Reduced power mode on some vehicles"],["medium",[80,3500]],"c"],"P003F":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Stuck On Bank 2","The camshaft profile actuator A switches the intake camshaft (camshaft A) on bank 2 between fixed cam profiles, usually a low-lift base profile and a high-lift profile. In most designs the unactuated state (\"off\") is the base profile; which profile counts as \"on\" is defined by the manufacturer.",[["h","Profile switching solenoid stuck open, oil pressure is not released"],["m","Locking pin does not return (weak spring, burr or dirt)"],["m","Oil pressure too high or drain from the switching gallery blocked"],["l","Sliding cam piece stuck in the high-lift position"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle and jerking at low engine speed","Poor running when cold"],["medium",[80,3500]],"c"],"P0040":["O2 Sensor Signals Swapped Bank 1 Sensor 1/Bank 2 Sensor 1","On engines with two exhaust banks, the control module checks whether the upstream oxygen sensors are assigned to the right bank. It changes the mixture on one bank only, actively or as part of normal bank-wise control, and watches which sensor responds.",[["h","Sensor connectors plugged in crosswise after a repair"],["m","Harness routed wrongly or pins swapped in a connector"],["l","Wrong harness, control module or calibration after a replacement"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel trim and mixture codes possible","Unstable idle or higher consumption possible"],["easy",[0,150]],"c"],"P0041":["O2 Sensor Signals Swapped Bank 1 Sensor 2/Bank 2 Sensor 2","On engines with two exhaust banks, the control module checks whether the downstream oxygen sensors are assigned to the right bank. It changes the mixture on one bank only, actively or as part of normal bank-wise control, and watches which sensor responds.",[["h","Sensor connectors plugged in crosswise after a repair"],["m","Harness routed wrongly or pins swapped in a connector"],["l","Wrong harness, control module or calibration after a replacement"]],["Check engine light on","Catalyst efficiency code possible"],["easy",[0,150]],"c"],"P0042":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 1 Sensor 3)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 1 is the third sensor further downstream, which exists only on systems with two catalyst stages. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heating element inside the sensor burnt out"],["h","Wire or connector damaged by heat or road spray"],["m","Heater fuse blown; a shared fuse can set several heater codes"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0043":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 1 Sensor 3)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 1 is the third sensor further downstream, which exists only on systems with two catalyst stages. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or heating element burnt out (system-dependent)"],["m","Supply interrupted or fuse blown"],["m","Corroded connector with high contact resistance"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0044":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 1 Sensor 3)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 1 is the third sensor further downstream, which exists only on systems with two catalyst stages. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness"],["m","Heating element shorted internally"],["m","Water in the connector causing leakage between pins"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0045":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Circuit/Open","\"Boost control A\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. The letters A and B denote two separate actuators, for example on the low- and high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system, not the cylinder banks.",[["h","Connector at the actuator corroded or loose"],["h","Wire broken or chafed through"],["m","Solenoid coil in the actuator burnt out"],["m","Fuse or ignition supply faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0046":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Circuit Range/Performance","\"Boost control A\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. The letters A and B denote two separate actuators, for example on the low- and high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system, not the cylinder banks.",[["h","Turbine vanes or wastegate linkage sooted or sticking"],["h","Electric actuator faulty"],["m","Actuator learn value invalid, e.g. after low battery voltage at start"],["m","Vacuum leak at a pneumatic actuator"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["hard",[0,3000]],"c"],"P0047":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Circuit Low","\"Boost control A\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. The letters A and B denote two separate actuators, for example on the low- and high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system, not the cylinder banks.",[["h","Harness chafed, short to ground"],["m","Actuator coil shorted"],["m","Water in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0048":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Circuit High","\"Boost control A\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. The letters A and B denote two separate actuators, for example on the low- and high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system, not the cylinder banks.",[["h","Harness chafed, short to battery voltage"],["m","Actuator coil shorted"],["m","Water in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0049":["Turbocharger/Supercharger A Turbine Overspeed","The control module has detected that turbocharger A runs above its permitted maximum speed. Engines equipped for it measure turbine speed with a dedicated sensor on the shaft; while the fault is active some systems continue with an estimated speed and reduce power.",[["m","Wastegate or turbine vanes stuck closed, boost control fails"],["m","Turbocharger speed sensor or its wiring faulty"],["l","Charge-air leak forcing the turbocharger to higher speed"],["l","Engine control module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power or limp mode","Whistling from the turbocharger"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P004A":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Circuit/Open","\"Boost control B\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. B is the second actuator, for example on the high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system or on the second turbocharger; it does not denote cylinder bank 2.",[["h","Connector at the actuator corroded or loose"],["h","Wire broken or chafed through"],["m","Solenoid coil in the actuator burnt out"],["m","Fuse or ignition supply faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P004B":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Circuit Range/Performance","\"Boost control B\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. B is the second actuator, for example on the high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system or on the second turbocharger; it does not denote cylinder bank 2.",[["h","Turbine vanes or wastegate linkage sooted or sticking"],["h","Electric actuator faulty"],["m","Actuator learn value invalid, e.g. after low battery voltage at start"],["m","Vacuum leak at a pneumatic actuator"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["hard",[0,3000]],"c"],"P004C":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Circuit Low","\"Boost control B\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. B is the second actuator, for example on the high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system or on the second turbocharger; it does not denote cylinder bank 2.",[["h","Harness chafed, short to ground"],["m","Actuator coil shorted"],["m","Water in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P004D":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Circuit High","\"Boost control B\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. B is the second actuator, for example on the high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system or on the second turbocharger; it does not denote cylinder bank 2.",[["h","Harness chafed, short to battery voltage"],["m","Actuator coil shorted"],["m","Water in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P004E":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","\"Boost control A\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. The letters A and B denote two separate actuators, for example on the low- and high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system, not the cylinder banks.",[["h","Loose contact at the actuator connector"],["h","Wire broken internally by engine movement"],["m","Corroded terminals"],["l","Actuator itself faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only intermittently","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Sudden, jerky loss of power"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P004F":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","\"Boost control B\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. B is the second actuator, for example on the high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system or on the second turbocharger; it does not denote cylinder bank 2.",[["h","Loose contact at the actuator connector"],["h","Wire broken internally by engine movement"],["m","Corroded terminals"],["l","Actuator itself faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only intermittently","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Sudden, jerky loss of power"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0050":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 2 Sensor 1)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the upstream sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter), used for mixture control. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heating element inside the sensor burnt out"],["h","Wire or connector damaged by heat or road spray"],["m","Heater fuse blown; a shared fuse can set several heater codes"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0051":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 2 Sensor 1)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the upstream sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter), used for mixture control. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or heating element burnt out (system-dependent)"],["m","Supply interrupted or fuse blown"],["m","Corroded connector with high contact resistance"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0052":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 2 Sensor 1)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the upstream sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter), used for mixture control. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness"],["m","Heating element shorted internally"],["m","Water in the connector causing leakage between pins"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0053":["HO2S Heater Resistance Bank 1 Sensor 1","On bank 1, this monitor checks the heating element of the upstream sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) not for an open or short circuit, but for its resistance. Many systems measure it once after a genuine cold start, when coolant and intake air temperatures show that the sensor is at ambient temperature, and correct the value for temperature; some manufacturers evaluate the heater current instead.",[["h","Heating element aged or partly damaged"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at connector or pins"],["m","Replacement sensor with a different heater type fitted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only after a cold start","Readiness for the sensor heater stays incomplete","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0054":["HO2S Heater Resistance Bank 1 Sensor 2","On bank 1, this monitor checks the heating element of sensor 2 behind the catalytic converter not for an open or short circuit, but for its resistance. Many systems measure it once after a genuine cold start, when coolant and intake air temperatures show that the sensor is at ambient temperature, and correct the value for temperature; some manufacturers evaluate the heater current instead.",[["h","Heating element aged or partly damaged"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at connector or pins"],["m","Replacement sensor with a different heater type fitted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only after a cold start","Readiness for the sensor heater stays incomplete","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0055":["HO2S Heater Resistance Bank 1 Sensor 3","On bank 1, this monitor checks the heating element of sensor 3 further downstream not for an open or short circuit, but for its resistance. Many systems measure it once after a genuine cold start, when coolant and intake air temperatures show that the sensor is at ambient temperature, and correct the value for temperature; some manufacturers evaluate the heater current instead.",[["h","Heating element aged or partly damaged"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at connector or pins"],["m","Replacement sensor with a different heater type fitted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only after a cold start","Readiness for the sensor heater stays incomplete","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0056":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 2 Sensor 2)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the downstream sensor 2 behind the (first) catalytic converter, used mainly to monitor the converter. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heating element inside the sensor burnt out"],["h","Wire or connector damaged by heat or road spray"],["m","Heater fuse blown; a shared fuse can set several heater codes"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0057":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 2 Sensor 2)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the downstream sensor 2 behind the (first) catalytic converter, used mainly to monitor the converter. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or heating element burnt out (system-dependent)"],["m","Supply interrupted or fuse blown"],["m","Corroded connector with high contact resistance"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0058":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 2 Sensor 2)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the downstream sensor 2 behind the (first) catalytic converter, used mainly to monitor the converter. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness"],["m","Heating element shorted internally"],["m","Water in the connector causing leakage between pins"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0059":["HO2S Heater Resistance Bank 2 Sensor 1","On bank 2, this monitor checks the heating element of the upstream sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) not for an open or short circuit, but for its resistance. Many systems measure it once after a genuine cold start, when coolant and intake air temperatures show that the sensor is at ambient temperature, and correct the value for temperature; some manufacturers evaluate the heater current instead.",[["h","Heating element aged or partly damaged"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at connector or pins"],["m","Replacement sensor with a different heater type fitted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only after a cold start","Readiness for the sensor heater stays incomplete","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P005E":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Supply Voltage Circuit Low","\"Boost control B\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. B is the second actuator, for example on the high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system or on the second turbocharger; it does not denote cylinder bank 2.",[["h","Supply wire shorted to ground or interrupted"],["m","Fuse or relay of the supply faulty"],["m","Internal short in the actuator"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P005F":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Supply Voltage Circuit High","\"Boost control B\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure: depending on the engine a pulse-width modulated solenoid or vacuum converter at the wastegate, the control valve of variable turbine vanes, or an electric actuator with position feedback. B is the second actuator, for example on the high-pressure turbocharger of a two-stage system or on the second turbocharger; it does not denote cylinder bank 2.",[["h","Supply wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Internal fault in the actuator"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0060":["HO2S Heater Resistance Bank 2 Sensor 2","On bank 2, this monitor checks the heating element of sensor 2 behind the catalytic converter not for an open or short circuit, but for its resistance. Many systems measure it once after a genuine cold start, when coolant and intake air temperatures show that the sensor is at ambient temperature, and correct the value for temperature; some manufacturers evaluate the heater current instead.",[["h","Heating element aged or partly damaged"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at connector or pins"],["m","Replacement sensor with a different heater type fitted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only after a cold start","Readiness for the sensor heater stays incomplete","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0061":["HO2S Heater Resistance Bank 2 Sensor 3","On bank 2, this monitor checks the heating element of sensor 3 further downstream not for an open or short circuit, but for its resistance. Many systems measure it once after a genuine cold start, when coolant and intake air temperatures show that the sensor is at ambient temperature, and correct the value for temperature; some manufacturers evaluate the heater current instead.",[["h","Heating element aged or partly damaged"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at connector or pins"],["m","Replacement sensor with a different heater type fitted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, sometimes only after a cold start","Readiness for the sensor heater stays incomplete","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0062":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 2 Sensor 3)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the third sensor further downstream, which exists only on systems with two catalyst stages. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heating element inside the sensor burnt out"],["h","Wire or connector damaged by heat or road spray"],["m","Heater fuse blown; a shared fuse can set several heater codes"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0063":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 2 Sensor 3)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the third sensor further downstream, which exists only on systems with two catalyst stages. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or heating element burnt out (system-dependent)"],["m","Supply interrupted or fuse blown"],["m","Corroded connector with high contact resistance"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0064":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 2 Sensor 3)","The heated oxygen sensor on bank 2 is the third sensor further downstream, which exists only on systems with two catalyst stages. Its heating element brings it quickly to operating temperature.",[["h","Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness"],["m","Heating element shorted internally"],["m","Water in the connector causing leakage between pins"],["l","Output driver or software of the engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst monitor does not complete"],["medium",[80,350]],"c"],"P0065":["Air Assisted Injector Control Range/Performance","Air-assisted injectors have an air sleeve around the nozzle tip: air flowing past at high speed breaks the fuel spray into finer droplets, which improves combustion and cold starts and lowers hydrocarbon emissions. On port-injected engines this air is taken upstream of the throttle and dosed by a pulse-width modulated bypass solenoid; because it bypasses the throttle, it is part of the idle air.",[["h","Air-assist solenoid sticking or dirty"],["h","Air hoses to the injectors leaking, disconnected or blocked"],["m","Air passages at the injectors coked"],["m","Air leak distorting idle control"]],["Check engine light on","Rough or unstable idle","Poorer cold starting"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0066":["Air Assisted Injector Control Circuit Low","Air-assisted injectors have an air sleeve around the nozzle tip: air flowing past at high speed breaks the fuel spray into finer droplets, which improves combustion and cold starts and lowers hydrocarbon emissions. On port-injected engines this air is taken upstream of the throttle and dosed by a pulse-width modulated bypass solenoid; because it bypasses the throttle, it is part of the idle air.",[["h","Short to ground or open circuit in the control or supply wire"],["h","Solenoid coil open or high resistance"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Unstable idle","Poorer cold starting"],["medium",[40,150]],"c"],"P0067":["Air Assisted Injector Control Circuit High","Air-assisted injectors have an air sleeve around the nozzle tip: air flowing past at high speed breaks the fuel spray into finer droplets, which improves combustion and cold starts and lowers hydrocarbon emissions. On port-injected engines this air is taken upstream of the throttle and dosed by a pulse-width modulated bypass solenoid; because it bypasses the throttle, it is part of the idle air.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Solenoid coil shorted"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Unstable idle","Poorer cold starting"],["medium",[40,150]],"c"],"P0068":["MAP/MAF - Throttle Position Correlation","The engine control module compares the air quantity measured by the manifold pressure and/or mass air flow sensor with the quantity the throttle position leads it to expect. P0068 is set when they do not match, typically because more air arrives than the nearly closed throttle allows, i.e.",[["h","Air leak behind the throttle: intake manifold, vacuum or MAP hoses"],["h","Throttle body dirty"],["m","Mass air flow or manifold pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Engine control software (manufacturer updates exist)"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power mode","Rough or unstable idle"],["medium",[0,300]],"c"],"P0069":["Manifold Absolute Pressure - Barometric Pressure Correlation","With the ignition on and the engine stopped, the intake manifold is at ambient pressure, so the manifold pressure sensor and the barometric pressure sensor (often inside the control module) must read almost the same. P0069 is set when they differ by more than a threshold.",[["h","Manifold pressure sensor drifting"],["h","MAP sensor hose blocked, kinked or leaking"],["m","Barometric pressure sensor (often inside the control module) faulty"],["m","5 V reference or connector faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Often no further symptoms","Mixture or power deviations possible"],["medium",[30,900]],"c"],"P006A":["MAP - Mass or Volume Air Flow Correlation Bank 1","The engine control module compares the air quantity measured by the mass air flow sensor with the quantity it calculates from manifold pressure, engine speed and temperature. P006A is set when the two deviate too far.",[["h","EGR valve dirty or sticking open"],["m","Air leak or crankcase ventilation hose leaking"],["m","Mass air flow sensor dirty or manifold pressure sensor faulty"],["l","Air filter or intake restricted"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running","Slight hesitation when accelerating"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P006B":["MAP - Exhaust Pressure Correlation","On engines with an exhaust pressure sensor, mostly turbocharged diesels, the control module checks whether manifold pressure and exhaust back pressure fit each other, for example with the engine stopped, when both should be at ambient pressure. P006B is set when they do not.",[["m","Exhaust pressure sensor faulty or its hose sooted or blocked"],["m","Manifold pressure sensor faulty"],["l","EGR or turbocharger fault distorting pressures"]],["Check engine light on","Often no further symptoms"],["medium",[50,250]],"c"],"P006C":["MAP - Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Correlation","On turbocharged or supercharged engines the control module compares manifold pressure with the pressure measured upstream of the throttle, for example at idle or with the throttle wide open, where the two must lie close together. P006C is set when the difference exceeds a threshold.",[["h","Leak or disconnected hose in the charge-air path"],["m","Boost or inlet pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Manifold pressure sensor faulty"],["l","Throttle body fault"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Hissing from a charge-air leak"],["medium",[40,250]],"c"],"P006D":["Barometric Pressure - Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Correlation Bank 1","Without boost, for example with the engine stopped or at low load, the pressure sensor ahead of the turbocharger or supercharger must read about the same as the barometric pressure sensor. P006D is set when the difference is too large; some manufacturers compare against an estimated barometric value instead and run the check once per ignition cycle.",[["h","Inlet pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Barometric pressure sensor or control module faulty"],["m","Wiring, connector or 5 V reference faulty"],["l","Clogged air filter lowering pressure ahead of the compressor"]],["Check engine light on","Often no further symptoms","Reduced boost possible"],["medium",[40,250]],"c"],"P006E":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Supply Voltage Circuit Low","\"Boost control A\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure, for example a wastegate solenoid, the control valve of variable turbine vanes or an electric actuator. Some engines supply it through two driver stages in the control module: a high side that switches the supply voltage and a low side that switches ground.",[["h","Supply wire shorted to ground or interrupted"],["m","Connector at the actuator corroded or loose"],["m","Internal short in the actuator"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P006F":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Supply Voltage Circuit High","\"Boost control A\" is the electrically driven actuator the engine control module uses to set boost pressure, for example a wastegate solenoid, the control valve of variable turbine vanes or an electric actuator. Some engines supply it through two driver stages in the control module: a high side that switches the supply voltage and a low side that switches ground.",[["h","Supply wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Internal fault in the actuator"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Weak acceleration, noticeable turbo lag"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0070":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit A","The ambient air temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as temperature rises. Its value is used by the climate control, the outside temperature display and the engine control module, which needs it to enable other diagnostics and to correct its maps.",[["h","Sensor faulty (exposed to spray and stone chips at the front)"],["h","Connector corroded or wire damaged, e.g. after front-end damage"],["m","Instrument cluster or data transmission faulty (vehicles with bus-supplied value)"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Wrong or missing outside temperature display","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P0071":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit A Range/Performance","The ambient air temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as temperature rises. Its value is used by the climate control, the outside temperature display and the engine control module, which needs it to enable other diagnostics and to correct its maps.",[["h","Sensor drifting"],["m","Sensor mounted in a heat-affected spot, e.g. after a repair"],["m","Actually the comparison sensor (intake air or coolant) is faulty"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"]],["Wrong or missing outside temperature display","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P0072":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit A Low","The ambient air temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as temperature rises. Its value is used by the climate control, the outside temperature display and the engine control module, which needs it to enable other diagnostics and to correct its maps.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["h","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Instrument cluster or data transmission faulty (vehicles with bus-supplied value)"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Outside temperature display shows implausibly high values","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P0073":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit A High","The ambient air temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as temperature rises. Its value is used by the climate control, the outside temperature display and the engine control module, which needs it to enable other diagnostics and to correct its maps.",[["h","Wire broken or connector unplugged"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Instrument cluster or data transmission faulty (vehicles with bus-supplied value)"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Outside temperature display shows implausibly low values","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P0074":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit A Intermittent/Erratic","The ambient air temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as temperature rises. Its value is used by the climate control, the outside temperature display and the engine control module, which needs it to enable other diagnostics and to correct its maps.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Wire broken inside the harness"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Outside temperature display jumps","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P0075":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Bank 1","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the intake side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["h","Wiring or connector interrupted, chafed or corroded"],["m","Oil at the connector, e.g. from a leaking valve O-ring"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0076":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Low Bank 1","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the intake side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or solenoid winding open (driver-dependent)"],["m","Shorted turns in the solenoid coil"],["m","Corroded or oil-soaked connector"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0077":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit High Bank 1","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the intake side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Solenoid coil faulty"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0078":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Bank 1","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the exhaust side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["h","Wiring or connector interrupted, chafed or corroded"],["m","Oil at the connector, e.g. from a leaking valve O-ring"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0079":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Low Bank 1","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the exhaust side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or solenoid winding open (driver-dependent)"],["m","Shorted turns in the solenoid coil"],["m","Corroded or oil-soaked connector"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P007A":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 1","The charge air cooler temperature sensor (an NTC thermistor) measures the temperature of the compressed charge air at the charge air cooler, depending on the manufacturer upstream or downstream of it, and is often combined with the boost pressure sensor. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control, component protection and to monitor cooler efficiency.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty, oil in the connector from crankcase vapours"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Ground or 5 V supply disturbed (often shared with the boost pressure sensor)"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible, especially on diesels","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[80,250]],"c"],"P007B":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1","The charge air cooler temperature sensor (an NTC thermistor) measures the temperature of the compressed charge air at the charge air cooler, depending on the manufacturer upstream or downstream of it, and is often combined with the boost pressure sensor. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control, component protection and to monitor cooler efficiency.",[["h","Sensor drifting or oil-coated and slow to respond"],["m","Wrong sensor fitted"],["m","Comparison sensor faulty"],["l","Charge air cooler efficiency or airflow impaired"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible, especially on diesels","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[80,250]],"c"],"P007C":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 1","The charge air cooler temperature sensor (an NTC thermistor) measures the temperature of the compressed charge air at the charge air cooler, depending on the manufacturer upstream or downstream of it, and is often combined with the boost pressure sensor. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control, component protection and to monitor cooler efficiency.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["h","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible, especially on diesels","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[80,250]],"c"],"P007D":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 1","The charge air cooler temperature sensor (an NTC thermistor) measures the temperature of the compressed charge air at the charge air cooler, depending on the manufacturer upstream or downstream of it, and is often combined with the boost pressure sensor. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control, component protection and to monitor cooler efficiency.",[["h","Wire broken or connector unplugged"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Signal wire shorted to the reference or battery voltage"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible, especially on diesels","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[80,250]],"c"],"P007E":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 1","The charge air cooler temperature sensor (an NTC thermistor) measures the temperature of the compressed charge air at the charge air cooler, depending on the manufacturer upstream or downstream of it, and is often combined with the boost pressure sensor. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control, component protection and to monitor cooler efficiency.",[["h","Loose contact or corroded connector"],["m","Wire chafed at the engine"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible, especially on diesels","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[80,250]],"c"],"P007F":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Bank1/Bank2 Correlation","The charge air cooler temperature sensor (an NTC thermistor) measures the temperature of the compressed charge air at the charge air cooler, depending on the manufacturer upstream or downstream of it, and is often combined with the boost pressure sensor. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control, component protection and to monitor cooler efficiency.",[["h","Sensor on one bank faulty or oil-coated"],["m","Leak or contamination in only one charge-air path"],["m","Connector fault"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible, especially on diesels","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[80,250]],"c"],"P0080":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit High Bank 1","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the exhaust side of bank 1. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Solenoid coil faulty"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0081":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Bank 2","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the intake side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["h","Wiring or connector interrupted, chafed or corroded"],["m","Oil at the connector, e.g. from a leaking valve O-ring"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0082":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Low Bank 2","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the intake side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or solenoid winding open (driver-dependent)"],["m","Shorted turns in the solenoid coil"],["m","Corroded or oil-soaked connector"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0083":["Intake Valve Control Solenoid Circuit High Bank 2","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the intake side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Solenoid coil faulty"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0084":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Bank 2","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the exhaust side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["h","Wiring or connector interrupted, chafed or corroded"],["m","Oil at the connector, e.g. from a leaking valve O-ring"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0085":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit Low Bank 2","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the exhaust side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or solenoid winding open (driver-dependent)"],["m","Shorted turns in the solenoid coil"],["m","Corroded or oil-soaked connector"],["l","Output driver or supply in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0086":["Exhaust Valve Control Solenoid Circuit High Bank 2","A solenoid valve on the cylinder head controls oil flow for the variable valve control on the exhaust side of bank 2. Depending on the manufacturer it drives a camshaft phaser (for example on Nissan engines) or a valve lift changeover (for example on Subaru engines).",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Solenoid coil faulty"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Less power, especially at high engine speed with lift changeover","Rough idle possible"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0087":["Fuel Rail/System Pressure - Too Low","On common-rail diesel and gasoline direct-injection engines, a high-pressure pump feeds the fuel rail, a sensor measures rail pressure, and the engine control module regulates it towards a target from its maps. P0087 is set when the actual pressure stays below the target by more than a threshold for some time.",[["h","Low-pressure supply too weak: lift pump, clogged filter, low tank level or waxing"],["h","High-pressure pump worn"],["m","Pressure control or metering valve sticking"],["m","High-pressure leak or excessive injector return flow"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Hard starting or stalling"],["hard",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0088":["Fuel Rail/System Pressure - Too High","On common-rail diesel and gasoline direct-injection engines, a high-pressure pump feeds the fuel rail, a sensor measures rail pressure, and the engine control module regulates it towards a target from its maps. P0088 is set when the actual pressure stays above the target by more than a threshold for some time.",[["h","Pressure control or metering valve sticking"],["m","Rail pressure sensor drifting"],["m","Fuel return blocked (diesel with pressure control valve)"],["l","Engine control software"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Harsh combustion noise"],["hard",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0089":["Fuel Pressure Regulator A Performance","On common-rail diesel and gasoline direct-injection engines, a high-pressure pump feeds the fuel rail, a sensor measures rail pressure, and the engine control module regulates it towards a target from its maps. The fuel pressure regulator A is the actuator the manufacturer designates for this: depending on the system a pressure control valve that releases fuel from the rail, or the control valve on the high-pressure pump.",[["h","Pressure regulator or pump control valve sticking or worn"],["m","High-pressure pump weak"],["m","Rail pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Low-pressure supply too weak"]],["Check engine light on","Jerking or hesitation","Loss of power, reduced power mode possible"],["hard",[150,1200]],"c"],"P008A":["Low Pressure Fuel System Pressure - Too Low","Some engines, mainly gasoline direct injection with a demand-controlled in-tank pump, have their own pressure sensor in the low-pressure line between the tank pump and the high-pressure pump. The engine control module compares this supply pressure with its target.",[["h","Tank run empty or very low fuel level"],["h","In-tank pump or its control unit faulty"],["m","Filter or line restricted"],["m","Low-pressure sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting","Jerking under load"],["medium",[60,700]],"c"],"P008B":["Low Pressure Fuel System Pressure - Too High","Some engines, mainly gasoline direct injection with a demand-controlled in-tank pump, have their own pressure sensor in the low-pressure line between the tank pump and the high-pressure pump. The engine control module compares this supply pressure with its target.",[["h","Low-pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Pump control unit or software"],["l","Return line or regulator blocked"]],["Check engine light on","Often no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[60,700]],"c"],"P008C":["Fuel Cooler Pump Control Circuit/Open","On some common-rail diesels, fuel returning from the injection system is cooled in a heat exchanger connected to a low-temperature coolant circuit. An electric coolant pump, switched by the engine control module depending on fuel temperature, drives this circuit.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Fuel cooler pump faulty"],["m","Relay or fuse faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Often no further symptoms","Reduced power when fuel gets hot possible"],["medium",[100,400]],"c"],"P008D":["Fuel Cooler Pump Control Circuit Low","On some common-rail diesels, fuel returning from the injection system is cooled in a heat exchanger connected to a low-temperature coolant circuit. An electric coolant pump, switched by the engine control module depending on fuel temperature, drives this circuit.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Fuel cooler pump faulty"],["m","Relay or fuse faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Often no further symptoms","Reduced power when fuel gets hot possible"],["medium",[100,400]],"c"],"P008E":["Fuel Cooler Pump Control Circuit High","On some common-rail diesels, fuel returning from the injection system is cooled in a heat exchanger connected to a low-temperature coolant circuit. An electric coolant pump, switched by the engine control module depending on fuel temperature, drives this circuit.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Fuel cooler pump faulty"],["m","Relay or fuse faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Often no further symptoms","Reduced power when fuel gets hot possible"],["medium",[100,400]],"c"],"P008F":["Engine Coolant Temperature/Fuel Temperature Correlation","After the engine has been switched off long enough, coolant and fuel should have approached the same temperature. The engine control module compares the coolant temperature sensor with the fuel temperature sensor at the next cold start and sets P008F if they differ too much.",[["h","Coolant temperature sensor drifting"],["h","Fuel temperature sensor drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at a connector"],["l","Values distorted, e.g. by an auxiliary heater"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no drivability symptoms","Cold-start behaviour may be affected"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P0090":["Fuel Pressure Regulator A Control Circuit/Open","On common-rail diesel and gasoline direct-injection engines, a high-pressure pump feeds the fuel rail, a sensor measures rail pressure, and the engine control module regulates it towards a target from its maps. The fuel pressure regulator A is the actuator the manufacturer designates for this: depending on the system a pressure control valve that releases fuel from the rail, or the control valve on the high-pressure pump.",[["h","Wire broken or connector corroded or loose"],["h","Regulator coil faulty"],["m","Fuse or supply voltage faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power mode","Hard starting or stalling"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0091":["Fuel Pressure Regulator A Control Circuit Low","On common-rail diesel and gasoline direct-injection engines, a high-pressure pump feeds the fuel rail, a sensor measures rail pressure, and the engine control module regulates it towards a target from its maps. The fuel pressure regulator A is the actuator the manufacturer designates for this: depending on the system a pressure control valve that releases fuel from the rail, or the control valve on the high-pressure pump.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, or connector corroded"],["h","Regulator coil faulty"],["m","Fuse or supply voltage faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power mode","Hard starting or stalling"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0092":["Fuel Pressure Regulator A Control Circuit High","On common-rail diesel and gasoline direct-injection engines, a high-pressure pump feeds the fuel rail, a sensor measures rail pressure, and the engine control module regulates it towards a target from its maps. The fuel pressure regulator A is the actuator the manufacturer designates for this: depending on the system a pressure control valve that releases fuel from the rail, or the control valve on the high-pressure pump.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage, or connector corroded"],["h","Regulator coil faulty"],["m","Fuse or supply voltage faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power mode","Hard starting or stalling"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0093":["Fuel System Leak Detected - Large Leak","A leak in the high-pressure fuel system is not measured directly but inferred from a pressure and volume balance. P0093 means the pump is already delivering at its limit and rail pressure still stays well below target.",[["h","High-pressure line or rail fitting leaking"],["h","Injector with excessive leak-off"],["m","Pressure relief or control valve leaking"],["m","Weak pump or low-pressure supply mistaken for a leak"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or reduced power mode","Long cranking"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0094":["Fuel System Leak Detected - Small Leak","A leak in the high-pressure fuel system is not measured directly but inferred from a pressure and volume balance. P0094 is typically detected when rail pressure drops too fast during overrun with injection cut off, or when more pump delivery than expected is needed at idle.",[["h","Injector with excessive leak-off"],["h","High-pressure fitting slightly leaking"],["m","Pressure relief valve leaking"],["l","Engine control software (manufacturer updates exist)"]],["Check engine light on","Long cranking","Rising engine oil level from fuel dilution"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0095":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path. On many turbocharged engines it sits in the intake manifold behind the compressor and charge air cooler, often combined with the manifold pressure sensor, and is used to calculate cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Sensor or combined sensor faulty"],["m","Supply or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or retarded ignition possible"],["easy",[70,220]],"c"],"P0096":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path. On many turbocharged engines it sits in the intake manifold behind the compressor and charge air cooler, often combined with the manifold pressure sensor, and is used to calculate cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Sensor drifting or coated with oil or soot from crankcase and exhaust gas recirculation"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","Comparison sensor faulty"],["l","Heat soak or leak in the intake manifold"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or retarded ignition possible"],["easy",[70,220]],"c"],"P0097":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Low Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path; on many turbocharged engines it sits in the intake manifold, often combined with the manifold pressure sensor, and is used to calculate cylinder filling and ignition timing. For P0097, the sensor signal is below its valid range.",[["h","Sensor shorted internally or signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Connector damp or corroded"],["m","On frequency-output sensors: open circuit"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0098":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit High Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path; on many turbocharged engines it sits in the intake manifold, often combined with the manifold pressure sensor, and is used to calculate cylinder filling and ignition timing. For P0098, the sensor signal is above its valid range.",[["h","Connector loose or corroded, or wire interrupted"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["m","Sensor ground interrupted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0099":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path; on many turbocharged engines it sits in the intake manifold, often combined with the manifold pressure sensor, and is used to calculate cylinder filling and ignition timing. For P0099, the signal jumps faster than a real temperature can change, which typically points to a loose contact.",[["h","Loose contact or wire broken by movement"],["m","Sensor failing intermittently"],["l","Interference on the signal wire"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P009A":["Intake Air Temperature/Ambient Air Temperature Correlation","After a long standstill with the engine cold, intake air temperature and ambient air temperature should be about the same. P009A is set when the two sensors disagree by more than a threshold at the next start.",[["h","Intake air temperature sensor drifting or contaminated"],["m","Ambient temperature sensor faulty or in a heat-affected position"],["m","Contact resistance at a connector"],["l","Engine block heater or auxiliary heater distorting the comparison"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[40,200]],"c"],"P009B":["Fuel Pressure Relief Control Circuit/Open","The fuel pressure relief control is an electrically controlled valve on the fuel rail that the engine control module opens to reduce rail pressure deliberately, for example on overrun, on load drops or when the engine is switched off. It differs from the purely mechanical pressure relief valve, which opens passively at overpressure, and from the pressure regulator, which controls pressure continuously.",[["h","Wire broken or connector corroded"],["m","Valve coil faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power (derate) possible"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P009C":["Fuel Pressure Relief Control Circuit Low","The fuel pressure relief control is an electrically controlled valve on the fuel rail that the engine control module opens to reduce rail pressure deliberately, for example on overrun, on load drops or when the engine is switched off. It differs from the purely mechanical pressure relief valve, which opens passively at overpressure, and from the pressure regulator, which controls pressure continuously.",[["h","Wiring chafed with short to ground, or connector corroded"],["m","Valve coil faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power (derate) possible"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P009D":["Fuel Pressure Relief Control Circuit High","The fuel pressure relief control is an electrically controlled valve on the fuel rail that the engine control module opens to reduce rail pressure deliberately, for example on overrun, on load drops or when the engine is switched off. It differs from the purely mechanical pressure relief valve, which opens passively at overpressure, and from the pressure regulator, which controls pressure continuously.",[["h","Wiring chafed with short to battery voltage, or connector corroded"],["m","Valve coil faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power (derate) possible"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P009E":["Fuel Pressure Relief Control Performance/Stuck Off","The fuel pressure relief control is an electrically controlled valve on the fuel rail that the engine control module opens to reduce rail pressure deliberately, for example on overrun, on load drops or when the engine is switched off. It differs from the purely mechanical pressure relief valve, which opens passively at overpressure, and from the pressure regulator, which controls pressure continuously.",[["m","False detection by engine control software (updates exist for some trucks)"],["m","Valve sticking closed, e.g. from contamination"],["l","Rail pressure sensor inaccurate"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Sometimes no further symptoms"],["hard",[80,900]],"c"],"P009F":["Fuel Pressure Relief Control Stuck On","The fuel pressure relief control is an electrically controlled valve on the fuel rail that the engine control module opens to reduce rail pressure deliberately, for example on overrun, on load drops or when the engine is switched off. It differs from the purely mechanical pressure relief valve, which opens passively at overpressure, and from the pressure regulator, which controls pressure continuously.",[["h","Valve leaking internally or stuck open"],["m","Fuel contamination"],["l","Short circuit keeping the valve energised"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting","Reduced power"],["hard",[300,900]],"c"],"P00A0":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 2","The charge air cooler temperature sensor for bank 2 (an NTC thermistor) measures the charge air temperature in the second charge-air path, for example on V engines with two charge air coolers. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control and component protection.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Supply or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A1":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2","The charge air cooler temperature sensor for bank 2 (an NTC thermistor) measures the charge air temperature in the second charge-air path, for example on V engines with two charge air coolers. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control and component protection.",[["h","Sensor drifting or coated with oil or soot"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","Comparison sensor faulty"],["l","Engine block heater or auxiliary heater distorting the start comparison"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A2":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 2","The charge air cooler temperature sensor for bank 2 (an NTC thermistor) measures the charge air temperature in the second charge-air path, for example on V engines with two charge air coolers. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control and component protection.",[["h","Sensor shorted internally or signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Connector damp or corroded"],["m","On frequency-output sensors: open circuit"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A3":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 2","The charge air cooler temperature sensor for bank 2 (an NTC thermistor) measures the charge air temperature in the second charge-air path, for example on V engines with two charge air coolers. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control and component protection.",[["h","Connector loose or corroded, or wire interrupted"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["m","Sensor ground interrupted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A4":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 2","The charge air cooler temperature sensor for bank 2 (an NTC thermistor) measures the charge air temperature in the second charge-air path, for example on V engines with two charge air coolers. The engine control module uses it for the air mass calculation, boost control and component protection.",[["h","Loose contact or wire broken by movement"],["m","Sensor failing intermittently"],["l","Interference on the signal wire"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A5":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 2 for bank 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path of the second cylinder bank, for example in the intake manifold behind the charge air cooler on engines with two intake paths. For P00A5, this is the general circuit fault; low and high signals have their own codes.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Supply or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A6":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 2 for bank 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path of the second cylinder bank, for example in the intake manifold behind the charge air cooler on engines with two intake paths. For P00A6, this is a plausibility check: at a start after a long standstill the sensor is compared with other temperature sensors (for example coolant temperature or the matching sensor of the other bank), which should then agree.",[["h","Sensor drifting or coated with oil or soot"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","Comparison sensor faulty"],["l","Engine block heater or auxiliary heater distorting the start comparison"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A7":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Low Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 2 for bank 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path of the second cylinder bank, for example in the intake manifold behind the charge air cooler on engines with two intake paths. For P00A7, the sensor signal is below its valid range.",[["h","Sensor shorted internally or signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Connector damp or corroded"],["m","On frequency-output sensors: open circuit"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A8":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit High Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 2 for bank 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path of the second cylinder bank, for example in the intake manifold behind the charge air cooler on engines with two intake paths. For P00A8, the sensor signal is above its valid range.",[["h","Connector loose or corroded, or wire interrupted"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["m","Sensor ground interrupted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00A9":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 2 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 2 for bank 2 is the second air temperature sensor in the intake path of the second cylinder bank, for example in the intake manifold behind the charge air cooler on engines with two intake paths. For P00A9, the signal jumps faster than a real temperature can change, which typically points to a loose contact.",[["h","Loose contact or wire broken by movement"],["m","Sensor failing intermittently"],["l","Interference on the signal wire"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00AA":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 1 for bank 2 measures the intake air temperature in the second intake path, typically near the air filter or in the air flow meter of that bank, on engines with two separate intake paths. For P00AA, this is the general circuit fault; low and high signals have their own codes.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Supply or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00AB":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 1 for bank 2 measures the intake air temperature in the second intake path, typically near the air filter or in the air flow meter of that bank, on engines with two separate intake paths. For P00AB, this is a plausibility check: at a start after a long standstill the sensor is compared with other temperature sensors (for example coolant temperature or the matching sensor of the other bank), which should then agree.",[["h","Sensor drifting or coated with oil or soot"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","Comparison sensor faulty"],["l","Engine block heater or auxiliary heater distorting the start comparison"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00AC":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Low Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 1 for bank 2 measures the intake air temperature in the second intake path, typically near the air filter or in the air flow meter of that bank, on engines with two separate intake paths. For P00AC, the sensor signal is below its valid range.",[["h","Sensor shorted internally or signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Connector damp or corroded"],["m","On frequency-output sensors: open circuit"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00AD":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit High Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 1 for bank 2 measures the intake air temperature in the second intake path, typically near the air filter or in the air flow meter of that bank, on engines with two separate intake paths. For P00AD, the sensor signal is above its valid range.",[["h","Connector loose or corroded, or wire interrupted"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["m","Sensor ground interrupted"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed power or knock control with an implausible reading"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00AE":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Intermittent Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 1 for bank 2 measures the intake air temperature in the second intake path, typically near the air filter or in the air flow meter of that bank, on engines with two separate intake paths. For P00AE, the signal jumps faster than a real temperature can change, which typically points to a loose contact.",[["h","Loose contact or wire broken by movement"],["m","Sensor failing intermittently"],["l","Interference on the signal wire"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Emissions test or readiness check may fail"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P00AF":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Module Performance","Boost control A here is an electronic actuator with its own electronics (a \"smart\" actuator), usually on the variable turbine geometry, with its own position sensor and controlled over a data bus or a pulsed signal. It checks itself and reports internal faults to the engine control module; documented examples are pattern errors, overcurrent and checksum errors.",[["h","Electronics or motor inside the actuator faulty"],["m","Sector gear or vane mechanism sticking (soot, coking), causing overcurrent"],["m","Weak supply or ground, corroded connector"],["l","Software of actuator or control module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode with reduced boost","Often stored with actuator position or boost codes"],["hard",[400,3500]],"c"],"P00B0":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Module Performance","Boost control B here is an electronic actuator with its own electronics (a \"smart\" actuator), usually on the variable turbine geometry, with its own position sensor and controlled over a data bus or a pulsed signal. It checks itself and reports internal faults to the engine control module; documented examples are pattern errors, overcurrent and checksum errors.",[["h","Electronics or motor inside the actuator faulty"],["m","Sector gear or vane mechanism sticking (soot, coking), causing overcurrent"],["m","Weak supply or ground, corroded connector"],["l","Software of actuator or control module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode with reduced boost"],["hard",[400,3500]],"c"],"P00B1":["Radiator Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit","The radiator coolant temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor that measures coolant temperature on the radiator side, typically at the radiator outlet; the exact position depends on the manufacturer. Together with the engine coolant temperature sensor it gives a second measuring point, which the control module uses to diagnose the thermostat and coolant flow, for example on engines with an electrically heated map-controlled thermostat.",[["h","Connector or wiring at the radiator corroded or damaged (exposed front position)"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Loose contact in the circuit"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Thermostat and coolant flow diagnostics are suspended","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[60,150]],"c"],"P00B2":["Radiator Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The radiator coolant temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor that measures coolant temperature on the radiator side, typically at the radiator outlet; the exact position depends on the manufacturer. Together with the engine coolant temperature sensor it gives a second measuring point, which the control module uses to diagnose the thermostat and coolant flow, for example on engines with an electrically heated map-controlled thermostat.",[["h","Sensor drifting"],["m","Air at the sensor or low coolant level"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Thermostat and coolant flow diagnostics are suspended","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[60,150]],"c"],"P00B3":["Radiator Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","The radiator coolant temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor that measures coolant temperature on the radiator side, typically at the radiator outlet; the exact position depends on the manufacturer. Together with the engine coolant temperature sensor it gives a second measuring point, which the control module uses to diagnose the thermostat and coolant flow, for example on engines with an electrically heated map-controlled thermostat.",[["h","Connector or wiring at the radiator corroded or damaged (exposed front position)"],["h","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Thermostat and coolant flow diagnostics are suspended","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[60,150]],"c"],"P00B4":["Radiator Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit High","The radiator coolant temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor that measures coolant temperature on the radiator side, typically at the radiator outlet; the exact position depends on the manufacturer. Together with the engine coolant temperature sensor it gives a second measuring point, which the control module uses to diagnose the thermostat and coolant flow, for example on engines with an electrically heated map-controlled thermostat.",[["h","Connector or wiring at the radiator corroded or damaged (exposed front position)"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Signal wire broken or shorted to reference or battery voltage"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Thermostat and coolant flow diagnostics are suspended","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[60,150]],"c"],"P00B5":["Radiator Coolant Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The radiator coolant temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor that measures coolant temperature on the radiator side, typically at the radiator outlet; the exact position depends on the manufacturer. Together with the engine coolant temperature sensor it gives a second measuring point, which the control module uses to diagnose the thermostat and coolant flow, for example on engines with an electrically heated map-controlled thermostat.",[["h","Connector or wiring at the radiator corroded or damaged (exposed front position)"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Loose contact in the circuit"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Thermostat and coolant flow diagnostics are suspended","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[60,150]],"c"],"P00B6":["Radiator Coolant Temperature/Engine Coolant Temperature Correlation","The radiator coolant temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor that measures coolant temperature on the radiator side, typically at the radiator outlet; the exact position depends on the manufacturer. Together with the engine coolant temperature sensor it gives a second measuring point, which the control module uses to diagnose the thermostat and coolant flow, for example on engines with an electrically heated map-controlled thermostat.",[["h","Radiator coolant temperature sensor drifting"],["h","Engine coolant temperature sensor drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at a connector"],["m","Retrofitted engine preheater or auxiliary heater not detected by the system"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[50,200]],"c"],"P00B7":["Engine Coolant Flow Low/Performance","When the engine is hot, the thermostat should be open and coolant should flow through the radiator. P00B7 is set when the engine coolant temperature is very high (above about 117 to 119 °C in documented cases) while the radiator side stays much colder, so too little coolant flows through the radiator.",[["h","Thermostat stuck closed"],["h","Low coolant level or air in the cooling system"],["m","Coolant pump delivering too little (impeller, drive; known cases on some engines)"],["m","Radiator or hoses blocked"]],["Check engine light on","Coolant warning light","High engine temperature up to overheating"],["medium",[100,700]],"c"],"P00B8":["MAP - Mass or Volume Air Flow Correlation Bank 2","On engines with two intake paths, the control module compares the manifold pressure of bank 2 with the measured or calculated air quantity of that bank. P00B8 is set when the two do not fit together; the code does not say which value is wrong.",[["h","Air leak in the intake of bank 2"],["m","EGR valve dirty or sticking open"],["m","Manifold pressure sensor or air flow meter dirty or drifting"],["l","Wiring or connector faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running","Mixture codes possible"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P00B9":["Low Pressure Fuel System Pressure - Too Low. Low Ambient Temperature","This code reports too low a pressure in the low-pressure fuel supply between the lift pump and the high-pressure pump under the additional condition of low ambient temperature. It separates cold-related supply problems from the general case.",[["h","Wax separation from summer or insufficiently winterised diesel in frost"],["m","Fuel filter clogged or water in the filter frozen"],["m","Fuel filter heater faulty"],["l","Lift pump weak"]],["Check engine light on","Hard or no cold start","Jerking or loss of power after starting"],["easy",[30,600]],"c"],"P00BA":["Low Fuel Pressure - Forced Limited Power","\"Forced limited power\" is a term for a protective strategy in which the engine control module deliberately limits engine power. Regulations require the check engine light to come on within seconds when such an emissions-relevant limp-home mode is active.",[["h","Fuel filter clogged, suction-side leak or air in the system"],["m","Lift pump or high-pressure pump weak"],["m","Pressure regulator or relief valve leaking"],["m","Tank run empty"]],["Check engine light on","Marked loss of power, limp mode","Hard starting or jerking"],["hard",[50,1500]],"c"],"P00BB":["Fuel Injector Insufficient Flow - Forced Limited Power","\"Forced limited power\" is a term for a protective strategy in which the engine control module deliberately limits engine power. Regulations require the check engine light to come on within seconds when such an emissions-relevant limp-home mode is active.",[["h","Injectors coked or worn (flow drift)"],["m","Fuel contamination"],["m","Rail pressure too low"],["l","Injector coding missing after replacement"]],["Check engine light on","Marked loss of power, limp mode","Rough running"],["shop_only",[250,2500]],"c"],"P00BC":["Mass or Volume Air Flow A Circuit Range/Performance - Air Flow Too Low","The control module compares the air mass measured by the air flow meter with the air mass it expects from a model, for example from manifold pressure, boost pressure and throttle position. These codes report the deviation with its direction: \"too low\" means the meter measures less air than expected, \"too high\" more.",[["h","Air leak behind the meter or charge-air leak"],["m","Air flow meter dirty"],["m","Air filter or intake restricted"],["l","Wiring or connector faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power","Lean mixture codes possible"],["easy",[0,350]],"c"],"P00BD":["Mass or Volume Air Flow A Circuit Range/Performance - Air Flow Too High","The control module compares the air mass measured by the air flow meter with the air mass it expects from a model, for example from manifold pressure, boost pressure and throttle position. These codes report the deviation with its direction: \"too low\" means the meter measures less air than expected, \"too high\" more.",[["h","Air flow meter drifting or oil-coated"],["m","Model value wrong, e.g. faulty manifold pressure sensor or EGR valve stuck open"],["l","Leak upstream of the meter causing pulsations"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power","Rich mixture codes possible"],["easy",[0,350]],"c"],"P00BE":["Mass or Volume Air Flow B Circuit Range/Performance - Air Flow Too Low","The control module compares the air mass measured by the air flow meter with the air mass it expects from a model, for example from manifold pressure, boost pressure and throttle position. These codes report the deviation with its direction: \"too low\" means the meter measures less air than expected, \"too high\" more.",[["h","Air leak behind the meter or charge-air leak"],["m","Air flow meter dirty"],["m","Air filter or intake restricted"],["l","Wiring or connector faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power","Lean mixture codes possible"],["easy",[0,350]],"c"],"P00BF":["Mass or Volume Air Flow B Circuit Range/Performance - Air Flow Too High","The control module compares the air mass measured by the air flow meter with the air mass it expects from a model, for example from manifold pressure, boost pressure and throttle position. These codes report the deviation with its direction: \"too low\" means the meter measures less air than expected, \"too high\" more.",[["h","Air flow meter drifting or oil-coated"],["m","Model value wrong, e.g. faulty manifold pressure sensor or EGR valve stuck open"],["l","Leak upstream of the meter causing pulsations"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power","Rich mixture codes possible"],["easy",[0,350]],"c"],"P00C0":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve B Control Circuit","Bypass valve B is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve of turbocharger B, not the wastegate: it lets charge air flow back to the compressor inlet when the throttle closes quickly, so the compressor does not surge. Turbocharger B is the second turbocharger in exhaust flow on series systems and the turbocharger of bank 2 on parallel twin-turbo engines.",[["h","Wiring or connector damaged by turbocharger heat"],["m","Valve coil open or shorted"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or surging noise when lifting off the throttle","Boost fluctuations, loss of power"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P00C1":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve B Control Circuit Low","Bypass valve B is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve of turbocharger B, not the wastegate: it lets charge air flow back to the compressor inlet when the throttle closes quickly, so the compressor does not surge. Turbocharger B is the second turbocharger in exhaust flow on series systems and the turbocharger of bank 2 on parallel twin-turbo engines.",[["h","Wiring or connector damaged by turbocharger heat"],["m","Valve coil open or shorted"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or surging noise when lifting off the throttle","Boost fluctuations, loss of power"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P00C2":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve B Control Circuit High","Bypass valve B is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve of turbocharger B, not the wastegate: it lets charge air flow back to the compressor inlet when the throttle closes quickly, so the compressor does not surge. Turbocharger B is the second turbocharger in exhaust flow on series systems and the turbocharger of bank 2 on parallel twin-turbo engines.",[["h","Wiring or connector damaged by turbocharger heat"],["m","Valve coil open or shorted"],["l","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or surging noise when lifting off the throttle","Boost fluctuations, loss of power"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P00C3":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve B Control Circuit Range/Performance","Bypass valve B is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve of turbocharger B, not the wastegate: it lets charge air flow back to the compressor inlet when the throttle closes quickly, so the compressor does not surge. Turbocharger B is the second turbocharger in exhaust flow on series systems and the turbocharger of bank 2 on parallel twin-turbo engines.",[["h","Valve sticking or coked"],["m","Diaphragm or piston worn"],["m","Position feedback implausible"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or surging noise when lifting off the throttle","Boost fluctuations, loss of power"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P00C4":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve B - Mechanical","Bypass valve B is the compressor recirculation (diverter) valve of turbocharger B, not the wastegate: it lets charge air flow back to the compressor inlet when the throttle closes quickly, so the compressor does not surge. Turbocharger B is the second turbocharger in exhaust flow on series systems and the turbocharger of bank 2 on parallel twin-turbo engines.",[["h","Valve stuck closed or coked"],["m","Diaphragm or piston worn"],["m","Valve not actuated correctly"],["l","Consequence of a boost or air flow fault"]],["Check engine light on","Fluttering or surging noise when lifting off the throttle","Boost fluctuations, loss of power"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P00C5":["Turbocharger/Supercharger B Turbine Overspeed","The control module has detected that turbocharger B runs above its permitted maximum speed. Speed is measured by a dedicated sensor per turbocharger where fitted, or estimated from a model of pressure ratio and air mass.",[["h","Boost control of turbocharger B sticking (wastegate or vanes)"],["m","Leak on the pressure side making the turbocharger work harder"],["m","Speed sensor or its wiring delivering wrong values"],["l","Operation at high altitude or software"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode with reduced power","Whistling from the turbocharger"],["shop_only",[150,3500]],"u"],"P00C6":["Fuel Rail Pressure Too Low - Engine Cranking","Direct-injection engines release injection only once the rail has reached a minimum pressure. P00C6 is set when this pressure is not reached quickly enough while cranking, or, in another documented variant, when it drops below the minimum again right after the high-pressure start.",[["h","High-pressure pump weak, or its drive cam or follower worn"],["m","Lift pump or low-pressure supply too weak"],["m","Control valve on the pump faulty"],["m","Leak or check valve fault letting pressure bleed off after shutdown"]],["Check engine light on","Long cranking","No start"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P00C7":["Intake Air Pressure Measurement System - Multiple Sensor Correlation Bank 1","With the engine stopped, all pressure sensors in the intake system (manifold pressure, boost pressure, barometric pressure) should read ambient pressure; some diesels also compare them at idle. The control module compares them: if one sensor stands out, it sets that sensor's own code.",[["h","Shared 5 V reference or ground of the pressure sensors disturbed"],["m","Two sensors drifting at the same time (contamination, oil mist, ageing)"],["m","Pressure hose to a sensor leaking or blocked"],["l","Engine control module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Often no further symptoms","Substitute values with loss of power possible"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P00C8":["Fuel Pressure Regulator A Solenoid Supply Voltage Control Circuit/Open","Some engine control modules switch the solenoid of fuel pressure regulator A on both sides: the ground side is monitored by P0090 to P0092, the supply side (high-side driver) by P00C8 to P00CA. On gasoline direct injection the regulator is typically the control valve on the high-pressure pump.",[["h","Supply wire or connector to the pump solenoid damaged by heat or chafing"],["m","Solenoid coil open"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling","Loss of power or limp mode"],["medium",[50,2000]],"u"],"P00C9":["Fuel Pressure Regulator A Solenoid Supply Voltage Control Circuit Low","Some engine control modules switch the solenoid of fuel pressure regulator A on both sides: the ground side is monitored by P0090 to P0092, the supply side (high-side driver) by P00C8 to P00CA. On gasoline direct injection the regulator is typically the control valve on the high-pressure pump; on some diesels it is the regulator designated as regulator 1.",[["h","Supply wire to the solenoid chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Solenoid coil shorted"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling","Loss of power or limp mode"],["medium",[50,2000]],"u"],"P00CA":["Fuel Pressure Regulator A Solenoid Supply Voltage Control Circuit High","Some engine control modules switch the solenoid of fuel pressure regulator A on both sides: the ground side is monitored by P0090 to P0092, the supply side (high-side driver) by P00C8 to P00CA. On gasoline direct injection the regulator is typically the control valve on the high-pressure pump; on some diesels it is the regulator designated as regulator 1.",[["h","Supply wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Internal fault in the solenoid"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling","Loss of power or limp mode"],["medium",[50,2000]],"u"],"P00CB":["Fuel Volume Regulator Solenoid Supply Voltage Control Circuit/Open","The fuel volume regulator is the solenoid valve that meters how much fuel the high-pressure pump delivers (metering unit on diesels, pump control valve on some gasoline direct-injection engines). Many control modules switch such solenoids on both sides; P0001 to P0004 cover the control side, while these codes cover the supply side (high-side driver).",[["h","Supply wire broken or connector corroded"],["m","Regulator coil open or shorted (often built into the pump)"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode","Hard starting or jerking"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P00CC":["Fuel Volume Regulator Solenoid Supply Voltage Control Circuit Low","The fuel volume regulator is the solenoid valve that meters how much fuel the high-pressure pump delivers (metering unit on diesels, pump control valve on some gasoline direct-injection engines). Many control modules switch such solenoids on both sides; P0001 to P0004 cover the control side, while these codes cover the supply side (high-side driver).",[["h","Supply wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Regulator coil open or shorted (often built into the pump)"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode","Hard starting or jerking"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P00CD":["Fuel Volume Regulator Solenoid Supply Voltage Control Circuit High","The fuel volume regulator is the solenoid valve that meters how much fuel the high-pressure pump delivers (metering unit on diesels, pump control valve on some gasoline direct-injection engines). Many control modules switch such solenoids on both sides; P0001 to P0004 cover the control side, while these codes cover the supply side (high-side driver).",[["h","Supply wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Regulator coil open or shorted (often built into the pump)"],["l","High-side driver in the engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode","Hard starting or jerking"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P00CE":["Intake Air Temperature Measurement System - Multiple Sensor Correlation Bank 1","After a long standstill (six hours in a documented case) all intake air temperature sensors, for example intake air, charge air cooler and manifold temperature, should read about the same. The control module compares them in pairs: if one sensor stands out, it sets that sensor's own plausibility code.",[["m","One temperature sensor drifting without setting its own code"],["m","Uneven cooling, undetected preheater or strong sunshine"],["m","Shared ground or reference line disturbed, falsifying several sensors"],["l","Engine control module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Start-stop disabled on some vehicles","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P00CF":["Barometric Pressure - Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor A Correlation","With the engine stopped or without boost, the boost pressure sensor A (between compressor and throttle) must read practically ambient pressure. This code is set when it deviates from the barometric pressure sensor by more than a threshold; the code does not say which of the two is wrong.",[["h","Boost pressure sensor dirty, oil-coated or drifting"],["m","Barometric sensor drifting, also when inside the control module"],["m","Contact resistance at connector, ground or 5 V reference"],["m","Sensor port or hose leaking or blocked"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced boost or limp mode possible","Often no further symptoms"],["easy",[40,300]],"u"],"P00D0":["Barometric Pressure - Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor B Correlation","With the engine stopped or without boost, the boost pressure sensor B (between compressor and throttle) must read practically ambient pressure. This code is set when it deviates from the barometric pressure sensor by more than a threshold; the code does not say which of the two is wrong.",[["h","Boost pressure sensor dirty, oil-coated or drifting"],["m","Barometric sensor drifting, also when inside the control module"],["m","Contact resistance at connector, ground or 5 V reference"],["m","Sensor port or hose leaking or blocked"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced boost or limp mode possible","Often no further symptoms"],["easy",[40,300]],"u"],"P00D1":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1 Sensor 1","This code concerns the heated oxygen sensor upstream of the catalytic converter (sensor 1) on bank 1. Unlike the heater circuit codes, which report an open or short circuit, this code means the heater is driven but does not bring the oxygen sensor to the expected temperature.",[["h","Heating element aged, resistance increased"],["h","Corrosion or contact resistance at connector or harness of the heater circuit"],["m","Too low supply voltage at the sensor connector, e.g. poor ground"],["m","Diagnostic software (manufacturer updates exist for some vehicles)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Oxygen sensor heater readiness stays incomplete"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P00D2":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1 Sensor 2","This code concerns the heated oxygen sensor behind the catalytic converter (sensor 2) on bank 1. Unlike the heater circuit codes, which report an open or short circuit, this code means the heater is driven but does not bring the oxygen sensor to the expected temperature.",[["h","Heating element aged, resistance increased"],["h","Corrosion or contact resistance at connector or harness of the heater circuit"],["m","Too low supply voltage at the sensor connector, e.g. poor ground"],["m","Diagnostic software (manufacturer updates exist for some vehicles)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Oxygen sensor heater readiness stays incomplete"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P00D3":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2 Sensor 1","This code concerns the heated oxygen sensor upstream of the catalytic converter (sensor 1) on bank 2. Unlike the heater circuit codes, which report an open or short circuit, this code means the heater is driven but does not bring the oxygen sensor to the expected temperature.",[["h","Heating element aged, resistance increased"],["h","Corrosion or contact resistance at connector or harness of the heater circuit"],["m","Too low supply voltage at the sensor connector, e.g. poor ground"],["m","Diagnostic software (manufacturer updates exist for some vehicles)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Oxygen sensor heater readiness stays incomplete"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P00D4":["HO2S Heater Control Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2 Sensor 2","This code concerns the heated oxygen sensor behind the catalytic converter (sensor 2) on bank 2. Unlike the heater circuit codes, which report an open or short circuit, this code means the heater is driven but does not bring the oxygen sensor to the expected temperature.",[["h","Heating element aged, resistance increased"],["h","Corrosion or contact resistance at connector or harness of the heater circuit"],["m","Too low supply voltage at the sensor connector, e.g. poor ground"],["m","Diagnostic software (manufacturer updates exist for some vehicles)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Oxygen sensor heater readiness stays incomplete"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P00D5":["HO2S Sensor Correlation Bank 1 Sensor 1/Bank 1 Sensor 2","During fuel cut-off on overrun the exhaust gas is pure air, so the oxygen sensors before and behind the catalytic converter must both report the same lean value. A documented method averages and normalises the signals of both sensors during fuel cut-off and sets the code when they differ by more than a limit; it runs once per drive cycle and needs several overrun phases.",[["h","Upstream sensor aged or shifted"],["h","Downstream sensor aged or shifted"],["m","Air or exhaust leak between the sensors falsifying the lean signal"],["l","Retrofitted sensor with wrong characteristic, or software"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst readiness may stay incomplete"],["medium",[200,700]],"u"],"P00D6":["HO2S Sensor Correlation Bank 2 Sensor 1/Bank 2 Sensor 2","During fuel cut-off on overrun the exhaust gas is pure air, so the oxygen sensors before and behind the catalytic converter must both report the same lean value. A documented method averages and normalises the signals of both sensors during fuel cut-off and sets the code when they differ by more than a limit; it runs once per drive cycle and needs several overrun phases.",[["h","Upstream sensor aged or shifted"],["h","Downstream sensor aged or shifted"],["m","Air or exhaust leak between the sensors falsifying the lean signal"],["l","Retrofitted sensor with wrong characteristic, or software"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable drivability problem","Catalyst readiness may stay incomplete"],["medium",[200,700]],"u"],"P00D7":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Temperature Too High","Bypass valve A is the electric compressor recirculation valve, mounted close to the hot compressor and turbine housing. This code reports that its temperature is too high.",[["m","Valve energised for unusually long periods, e.g. due to boost control problems"],["m","Coil with shorted turns or aged"],["m","Heat shield missing or exhaust leak near the turbocharger"],["l","Contact resistance or supply fault distorting the estimate"]],["Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)","Whistling or fluttering when lifting off the throttle","Slight loss of power"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P00D8":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve B Temperature Too High","Bypass valve B is the electric compressor recirculation valve, mounted close to the hot compressor and turbine housing. This code reports that its temperature is too high.",[["m","Valve energised for unusually long periods, e.g. due to boost control problems"],["m","Coil with shorted turns or aged"],["m","Heat shield missing or exhaust leak near the turbocharger"],["l","Contact resistance or supply fault distorting the estimate"]],["Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)","Whistling or fluttering when lifting off the throttle","Slight loss of power"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P00D9":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit B","Ambient air temperature sensor B is a second, separate ambient temperature circuit in addition to sensor A. What the second measuring point is used for in a given vehicle could not be documented; the value may be wired directly to the control module or come over the data bus.",[["h","Sensor faulty (exposed to spray and stone chips)"],["h","Connector corroded or wiring damaged, often after front-end work"],["m","Module supplying the value over the data bus faulty"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Wrong or missing outside temperature display","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[50,150]],"u"],"P00DA":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit B Range/Performance","Ambient air temperature sensor B is a second, separate ambient temperature circuit in addition to sensor A. What the second measuring point is used for in a given vehicle could not be documented; the value may be wired directly to the control module or come over the data bus.",[["m","Sensor in a heat-affected position (engine, sun)"],["m","Comparison sensor faulty"],["m","Sensor drifting"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Wrong or missing outside temperature display","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[50,150]],"u"],"P00DB":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit B Low","Ambient air temperature sensor B is a second, separate ambient temperature circuit in addition to sensor A. What the second measuring point is used for in a given vehicle could not be documented; the value may be wired directly to the control module or come over the data bus.",[["h","Sensor faulty (exposed to spray and stone chips)"],["h","Connector corroded or wiring damaged, often after front-end work"],["m","Module supplying the value over the data bus faulty"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Wrong or missing outside temperature display","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[50,150]],"u"],"P00DC":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit B High","Ambient air temperature sensor B is a second, separate ambient temperature circuit in addition to sensor A. What the second measuring point is used for in a given vehicle could not be documented; the value may be wired directly to the control module or come over the data bus.",[["h","Sensor faulty (exposed to spray and stone chips)"],["h","Connector corroded or wiring damaged, often after front-end work"],["m","Module supplying the value over the data bus faulty"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Wrong or missing outside temperature display","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[50,150]],"u"],"P00DD":["Ambient Air Temperature Sensor Circuit B Intermittent/Erratic","Ambient air temperature sensor B is a second, separate ambient temperature circuit in addition to sensor A. What the second measuring point is used for in a given vehicle could not be documented; the value may be wired directly to the control module or come over the data bus.",[["h","Sensor faulty (exposed to spray and stone chips)"],["h","Connector corroded or wiring damaged, often after front-end work"],["m","Module supplying the value over the data bus faulty"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Wrong or missing outside temperature display","Climate control regulates incorrectly","Check engine light on (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[50,150]],"u"],"P00DE":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit","With indirect charge air cooling, a water-to-air charge air cooler cools the charge air; it sits in a separate low-temperature coolant circuit with an electric pump and its own radiator. The charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor A measures the coolant temperature of this circuit.",[["h","Wiring harness chafed or fuse blown (documented by manufacturer bulletins)"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or water ingress"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[100,300]],"u"],"P00DF":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","With indirect charge air cooling, a water-to-air charge air cooler cools the charge air; it sits in a separate low-temperature coolant circuit with an electric pump and its own radiator. The charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor A measures the coolant temperature of this circuit.",[["h","Wiring harness chafed or fuse blown (documented by manufacturer bulletins)"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or water ingress"],["m","Low coolant, air in the low-temperature circuit or pump faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[100,300]],"u"],"P00E0":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","With indirect charge air cooling, a water-to-air charge air cooler cools the charge air; it sits in a separate low-temperature coolant circuit with an electric pump and its own radiator. The charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor A measures the coolant temperature of this circuit.",[["h","Wiring harness chafed or fuse blown (documented by manufacturer bulletins)"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or water ingress"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[100,300]],"u"],"P00E1":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","With indirect charge air cooling, a water-to-air charge air cooler cools the charge air; it sits in a separate low-temperature coolant circuit with an electric pump and its own radiator. The charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor A measures the coolant temperature of this circuit.",[["h","Wiring harness chafed or fuse blown (documented by manufacturer bulletins)"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or water ingress"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[100,300]],"u"],"P00E2":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor A Intermittent/Erratic","With indirect charge air cooling, a water-to-air charge air cooler cools the charge air; it sits in a separate low-temperature coolant circuit with an electric pump and its own radiator. The charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor A measures the coolant temperature of this circuit.",[["h","Wiring harness chafed or fuse blown (documented by manufacturer bulletins)"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or water ingress"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[100,300]],"u"],"P00E3":["Exhaust Heat Exchanger Exhaust Bypass Valve A Stuck Open","Some engines, mainly hybrids and fuel-saving designs, have an exhaust heat exchanger behind the catalytic converter that transfers exhaust heat into the coolant for faster warm-up of engine and cabin. A bypass valve sends the exhaust either through the heat exchanger or around it.",[["h","Valve flap coked or corroded and sticking"],["m","Actuator faulty (electric or vacuum)"],["m","Linkage or return spring broken"],["l","Heat exchanger temperature sensor faulty, simulating a stuck valve"]],["Check engine light on","Slow warm-up and weak heating in winter","Or high coolant temperature and long fan run-on"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P00E4":["Exhaust Heat Exchanger Exhaust Bypass Valve A Stuck Closed","Some engines, mainly hybrids and fuel-saving designs, have an exhaust heat exchanger behind the catalytic converter that transfers exhaust heat into the coolant for faster warm-up of engine and cabin. A bypass valve sends the exhaust either through the heat exchanger or around it.",[["h","Valve flap coked or corroded and sticking"],["m","Actuator faulty (electric or vacuum)"],["m","Linkage or return spring broken"],["l","Heat exchanger temperature sensor faulty, simulating a stuck valve"]],["Check engine light on","Slow warm-up and weak heating in winter","Or high coolant temperature and long fan run-on"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P00E5":["Intake Air Temperature/Fuel Temperature Correlation","After a long standstill, intake air and fuel should be at about ambient temperature. This code is set when the intake air and fuel temperature sensors differ too much at the next start; it does not say which sensor is wrong.",[["h","Fuel or intake air temperature sensor drifting"],["m","Block heater, sunshine, very warm or cold fuel from refuelling, or a short standstill"],["m","Contact resistance at a connector"],["l","Engine control module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Cold-start behaviour slightly affected possible"],["medium",[60,250]],"u"],"P00E6":["Intake Air Pressure Measurement System - Multiple Sensor Correlation Bank 2","This is the bank 2 counterpart of the multiple pressure sensor check for engines with separate pressure measurement per bank, such as twin-turbo V engines. With the engine stopped, all intake pressure sensors should read ambient pressure.",[["h","A pressure sensor on bank 2 drifting"],["m","Shared 5 V reference or ground faulty"],["m","Sensor port blocked or hose leaking"],["l","Barometric sensor drifting"]],["Check engine light on","Boost limitation or uneven power possible"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P00E7":["Intake Air Temperature Measurement System - Multiple Sensor Correlation Bank 2","After a long standstill (six hours in a documented case) all intake air temperature sensors, for example intake air, charge air cooler and manifold temperature, should read about the same. The control module compares them in pairs: if one sensor stands out, it sets that sensor's own plausibility code.",[["m","One temperature sensor drifting without setting its own code"],["m","Uneven cooling, undetected preheater or strong sunshine"],["m","Shared ground or reference line disturbed, falsifying several sensors"],["l","Engine control module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no drivability symptoms"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P00E8":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00E9":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","A reference sensor reading wrong"],["l","Sensor mounted wrongly or exposed to external heat"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00EA":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Low Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00EB":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit High Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Open circuit, connector off or corroded"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Ground wire interrupted or control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00EC":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Loose contact or wire broken by engine movement"],["m","Moisture in the connector"],["l","Interference or control module fault"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00ED":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Connector or wiring faulty"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00EE":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","A reference sensor reading wrong"],["l","Sensor mounted wrongly or exposed to external heat"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00EF":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Low Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00F0":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit High Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Open circuit, connector off or corroded"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Ground wire interrupted or control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00F1":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 2","Intake air temperature sensor 3 is a third air temperature sensor in the intake path. On documented turbocharged engines with a humidity sensor it is the manifold temperature sensor behind the charge air cooler, an NTC thermistor read directly by the engine control module and used for cylinder filling and ignition timing.",[["h","Loose contact or wire broken by engine movement"],["m","Moisture in the connector"],["l","Interference or control module fault"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms; substitute value used","Slightly reduced power through knock protection possible"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P00F2":["Humidity Sensor Circuit/Open","The humidity sensor measures the relative humidity of the intake air; on documented vehicles it is built into the mass air flow sensor or a combined sensor. The engine control module uses the value, for example, to correct torque and in emission models.",[["h","Connector or wire interrupted"],["m","Sensor or combined air flow sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly uneven torque or higher consumption possible"],["easy",[80,450]],"u"],"P00F3":["Humidity Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The humidity sensor measures the relative humidity of the intake air; on documented vehicles it is built into the mass air flow sensor or a combined sensor. The engine control module uses the value, for example, to correct torque and in emission models.",[["h","Humidity sensor or combined air flow sensor faulty"],["m","Extremely dry and hot air making the test borderline"],["l","Contamination or oil mist on the sensing element"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly uneven torque or higher consumption possible"],["easy",[80,450]],"u"],"P00F4":["Humidity Sensor Circuit Low","The humidity sensor measures the relative humidity of the intake air; on documented vehicles it is built into the mass air flow sensor or a combined sensor. The engine control module uses the value, for example, to correct torque and in emission models.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor or combined air flow sensor faulty"],["m","Sensor supply missing"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly uneven torque or higher consumption possible"],["easy",[80,450]],"u"],"P00F5":["Humidity Sensor Circuit High","The humidity sensor measures the relative humidity of the intake air; on documented vehicles it is built into the mass air flow sensor or a combined sensor. The engine control module uses the value, for example, to correct torque and in emission models.",[["h","Sensor or combined air flow sensor faulty"],["m","Signal wire shorted to battery voltage"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly uneven torque or higher consumption possible"],["easy",[80,450]],"u"],"P00F6":["Humidity Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The humidity sensor measures the relative humidity of the intake air; on documented vehicles it is built into the mass air flow sensor or a combined sensor. The engine control module uses the value, for example, to correct torque and in emission models.",[["h","Loose contact"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Interference on the signal wire"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly uneven torque or higher consumption possible"],["easy",[80,450]],"u"],"P00F7":["Barometric Pressure - Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Correlation Bank 2","This is the bank 2 counterpart of the comparison between barometric pressure and the pressure ahead of the turbocharger or supercharger. At low air flow the inlet pressure sensor reads close to ambient pressure, minus the small loss across the air filter, so it must match the barometric reading.",[["h","Inlet pressure sensor of bank 2 drifting or dirty"],["m","Air filter or intake of bank 2 clogged"],["m","Intake hose between air flow meter and turbocharger leaking or slipped off"],["l","Barometric sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power or boost limitation possible","Whistling from an intake leak"],["easy",[20,200]],"u"],"P00F8":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit","The turbocharger outlet pressure sensor A measures the pressure directly at the compressor outlet. It is a separate code family from the boost pressure sensor, which usually measures the pressure just ahead of the throttle; engines use it where the pressure behind the compressor is needed in addition, for example to judge the pressure loss across the charge air cooler or to protect against compressor surge.",[["h","Connector loose or not fully engaged, or sensor not seated properly"],["h","Wire interrupted, short to ground or 5 V reference missing"],["m","Leak at the sensor flange or seal"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode with boost limitation","Jerking under load possible"],["easy",[0,250]],"u"],"P00F9":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor A Range/Performance","The turbocharger outlet pressure sensor A measures the pressure directly at the compressor outlet. It is a separate code family from the boost pressure sensor, which usually measures the pressure just ahead of the throttle; engines use it where the pressure behind the compressor is needed in addition, for example to judge the pressure loss across the charge air cooler or to protect against compressor surge.",[["h","Connector loose or not fully engaged, or sensor not seated properly"],["m","Sensor oil-coated from turbocharger oil mist or characteristic drifted"],["m","Leak at the sensor flange or seal"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode with boost limitation","Jerking under load possible"],["easy",[0,250]],"u"],"P00FA":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit Low","The turbocharger outlet pressure sensor A measures the pressure directly at the compressor outlet. It is a separate code family from the boost pressure sensor, which usually measures the pressure just ahead of the throttle; engines use it where the pressure behind the compressor is needed in addition, for example to judge the pressure loss across the charge air cooler or to protect against compressor surge.",[["h","Connector loose or not fully engaged, or sensor not seated properly"],["h","Wire interrupted, short to ground or 5 V reference missing"],["m","Leak at the sensor flange or seal"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode with boost limitation","Jerking under load possible"],["easy",[0,250]],"u"],"P00FB":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit High","The turbocharger outlet pressure sensor A measures the pressure directly after the compressor, usually ahead of the charge air cooler; FCA fits it as a combined temperature and pressure sensor. Boost control and compressor protection use the value.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to 5 V reference or battery voltage"],["m","Ground (low reference) wire open or connector corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (oil mist, heat at compressor outlet)"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode with boost limit","Further pressure plausibility codes possible"],["easy",[20,250]],"u"],"P00FC":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor A Intermittent/Erratic","The turbocharger outlet pressure sensor A measures the pressure directly after the compressor, usually ahead of the charge air cooler; FCA fits it as a combined temperature and pressure sensor. Boost control and compressor protection use the value.",[["h","Connector loose, not latched, or terminals corroded or spread"],["m","Harness chafed or broken near the turbocharger (heat, vibration)"],["m","Sensor with internal dropouts"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, possibly only after several drives","Occasional hesitation or brief loss of power under load","Fault comes and goes"],["easy",[20,250]],"u"],"P00FD":["Battery B State of Charge Performance","'Battery B' here is not a hybrid high-voltage battery (those have codes from P0B27) but, in the only documented use by FCA/Stellantis, the 12 V auxiliary battery of the start-stop system. When the engine restarts automatically, a relay separates main and auxiliary battery so that the auxiliary one keeps the control units stable while the main battery cranks.",[["h","Auxiliary battery discharged or aged"],["h","Loose or corroded positive or ground connections at either battery"],["m","150 A fuse or fuse array in the power distribution centre open or poorly contacting"],["l","Battery separation relay stuck"]],["Start-stop out of service, 'Service Start/Stop' message","Engine does not crank once","'Service Shifter' message with electronic gear selector"],["easy",[0,250]],"u"],"P00FE":["EVAP System Tank Vapor Line Restricted/Blocked","The evaporative emission system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister and later purges it into the engine. P00FE means the vapour path between tank and canister is restricted or blocked.",[["h","Kinked, pinched or misrouted vapour line between tank and canister"],["m","Charcoal canister broken down, grains blocking lines or valves"],["m","Rollover or fill-limit valve at the tank stuck closed (e.g. after overfilling)"],["l","Purge valve or leak detection module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel nozzle clicks off early when refuelling","Hiss or vacuum when opening the fuel cap"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P00FF":["Body Control Module Requested MIL Illumination","P00FF is an information code in the engine control module: the body control module has detected an emissions-relevant fault of its own and asks over the data bus for the check engine light. The engine module stores P00FF so that a generic OBD scanner can see why the MIL is on; the actual fault is stored in the body control module as B or U codes and must be read there.",[["h","Emissions-relevant fault in the body control module or its sensors/actuators (own B/U codes)"],["m","Software or calibration fault in the body control module"],["l","Communication fault on the data bus"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on without an engine fault","Other messages depending on the body module fault","Driving behaviour unchanged"],["medium",[0,300]],"u"],"P0100":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor A Circuit","The mass air flow sensor (MAF) sits between the air filter and the throttle or compressor and measures how much air the engine draws in; fuel quantity, load and ignition are calculated from it. Most are hot-film or hot-wire sensors that output either a voltage or, on GM, Saab and newer Ford systems, a frequency.",[["h","Connector unplugged or corroded, signal or supply wire open"],["h","Sensor faulty internally (broken element, failed electronics)"],["m","Supply fuse or relay failed"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, hesitation, lack of power (substitute value)","Hard starting or stalling"],["easy",[20,300]],"c"],"P0101":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The mass air flow sensor (MAF) sits between the air filter and the throttle or compressor and measures how much air the engine draws in; fuel quantity, load and ignition are calculated from it. Most are hot-film or hot-wire sensors that output either a voltage or, on GM, Saab and newer Ford systems, a frequency.",[["h","Air leak between sensor and engine (cracked or loose intake duct, boost leak)"],["h","Contaminated sensing element (oil from oiled sport filter, dust)"],["m","Leaking or disconnected crankcase ventilation"],["m","Clogged or wrongly fitted air filter, modified intake"]],["Check engine light on, often without driveability problems","Hesitation, flat spot, poor throttle response","Rough idle or stalling"],["easy",[10,350]],"c"],"P0102":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor A Circuit Low","The mass air flow sensor (MAF) sits between the air filter and the throttle or compressor and measures how much air the engine draws in; fuel quantity, load and ignition are calculated from it. Most are hot-film or hot-wire sensors that output either a voltage or, on GM, Saab and newer Ford systems, a frequency.",[["h","Signal wire open or shorted to ground, connector corroded or loose"],["h","Sensor electronics failed"],["m","Missing supply voltage (fuse, relay) or poor ground"],["l","Large leak or disconnected duct between sensor and throttle"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, hesitation, substitute value mode","Rough idle or stalling"],["easy",[20,300]],"c"],"P0103":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor A Circuit High","The mass air flow sensor (MAF) sits between the air filter and the throttle or compressor and measures how much air the engine draws in; fuel quantity, load and ignition are calculated from it. Most are hot-film or hot-wire sensors that output either a voltage or, on GM, Saab and newer Ford systems, a frequency.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to voltage or to the supply wire"],["h","Sensor electronics failed"],["m","Interference from harness routed near ignition coils, relays or motors"],["l","Water in the connector or on the element"]],["Check engine light on","Substitute value mode, lack of power","Rich mixture possible (smoke, consumption)"],["easy",[20,300]],"c"],"P0104":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The mass air flow sensor (MAF) sits between the air filter and the throttle or compressor and measures how much air the engine draws in; fuel quantity, load and ignition are calculated from it. Most are hot-film or hot-wire sensors that output either a voltage or, on GM, Saab and newer Ford systems, a frequency.",[["h","Loose contact at the sensor connector (corrosion, spread terminals, broken latch)"],["m","Chafed or partly broken wire moved by the engine"],["m","Sensor electronics dropping out (temperature-dependent)"],["l","Poor ground or electrical interference"]],["Check engine light on, may go out again","Occasional hesitation, misfire-like stumble, flat spot","Intermittent rough idle or stalling"],["medium",[20,300]],"c"],"P0105":["Manifold Absolute Pressure/Barometric Pressure Sensor Circuit","The manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor measures the pressure behind the throttle; on vehicles without a separate barometric sensor it also supplies the ambient pressure, read at key-on or wide-open throttle – hence 'MAP/BARO'. It is a three-wire sensor with 5 V reference, ground and signal.",[["h","Connector or wiring to the sensor open, shorted or loose"],["m","MAP sensor faulty (oiled, failed internally, SENT status fault)"],["m","Sensor hose disconnected, vacuum or boost leak"],["l","5 V reference pulled down by another sensor on the same circuit"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode with torque limit (diesel)","Rough idle, hard starting, higher consumption (petrol)"],["easy",[20,200]],"c"],"P0106":["Manifold Absolute Pressure/Barometric Pressure Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor measures the pressure behind the throttle; on vehicles without a separate barometric sensor it also supplies the ambient pressure, read at key-on or wide-open throttle – hence 'MAP/BARO'. It is a three-wire sensor with 5 V reference, ground and signal.",[["h","Vacuum leak at the intake manifold or boost leak (hose, clamp, charge air cooler)"],["h","Sensor oiled or sooted, port blocked (EGR soot), characteristic drifted"],["m","High resistance in signal, reference or ground wire"],["m","Comparison sensor wrong, or mechanical cause (dirty throttle, EGR stuck open)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, hesitation, poor response","Higher consumption, black smoke possible on diesels"],["medium",[20,300]],"c"],"P0107":["Manifold Absolute Pressure/Barometric Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","The manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor measures the pressure behind the throttle; on vehicles without a separate barometric sensor it also supplies the ambient pressure, read at key-on or wide-open throttle – hence 'MAP/BARO'. It is a three-wire sensor with 5 V reference, ground and signal.",[["h","Connector unplugged or corroded, signal or reference wire open"],["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","5 V reference shorted by another faulty sensor on the same circuit"],["m","MAP sensor failed internally"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting, rough idle, stalling possible","Wrong mixture from substitute value, higher consumption"],["easy",[20,200]],"c"],"P0108":["Manifold Absolute Pressure/Barometric Pressure Sensor Circuit High","The manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor measures the pressure behind the throttle; on vehicles without a separate barometric sensor it also supplies the ambient pressure, read at key-on or wide-open throttle – hence 'MAP/BARO'. It is a three-wire sensor with 5 V reference, ground and signal.",[["h","Ground (low reference) wire open or connector corroded"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","MAP sensor failed internally"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Rich mixture, sooting, higher consumption","Rough idle, loss of power or limp mode on turbo engines"],["easy",[20,200]],"c"],"P0109":["Manifold Absolute Pressure/Barometric Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor measures the pressure behind the throttle; on vehicles without a separate barometric sensor it also supplies the ambient pressure, read at key-on or wide-open throttle – hence 'MAP/BARO'. It is a three-wire sensor with 5 V reference, ground and signal.",[["h","Loose contact at the MAP connector, terminals spread or corroded"],["m","Chafed or broken wire that makes contact with engine movement"],["m","Sensor with internal dropouts"],["l","Ground problem or control module"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Occasional hesitation or stumble","Fault comes and goes"],["easy",[20,200]],"c"],"P010A":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor B Circuit","Sensor B is a second mass air flow sensor in a second intake path, found on engines with two air filter boxes – for example the Nissan VR30DDTT twin-turbo V6 (where sensor B belongs to bank 2) or GM's 3.0 litre twin-turbo V6. P010A is the general circuit fault of sensor B: no usable signal at all, just like P0100 for sensor A.",[["h","Connector or wiring of sensor B open"],["h","Sensor B faulty internally (broken element)"],["m","Supply of sensor B missing (fuse, relay)"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, hesitation (substitute value)","Rough idle"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P010B":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Sensor B is a second mass air flow sensor in a second intake path, found on engines with two air filter boxes – for example the Nissan VR30DDTT twin-turbo V6 (where sensor B belongs to bank 2) or GM's 3.0 litre twin-turbo V6. P010B is the plausibility fault of sensor B: the signal is electrically valid but does not fit.",[["h","Intake pipe on the sensor B side leaking or not seated"],["h","Sensor B element contaminated (oil, dust)"],["m","Air filter on that side clogged or wrongly fitted"],["m","Boost leak on that bank, or sensor/connector problem"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, hesitation","Rough idle"],["easy",[10,400]],"c"],"P010C":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor B Circuit Low","Sensor B is a second mass air flow sensor in a second intake path, found on engines with two air filter boxes – for example the Nissan VR30DDTT twin-turbo V6 (where sensor B belongs to bank 2) or GM's 3.0 litre twin-turbo V6. P010C means the signal of sensor B is below its electrical lower limit – the counterpart of P0102.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor B open or grounded, connector corroded"],["h","Sensor B electronics failed"],["m","Supply or ground of sensor B faulty"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, substitute value mode","Rough idle, hesitation"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P010D":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor B Circuit High","Sensor B is a second mass air flow sensor in a second intake path, found on engines with two air filter boxes – for example the Nissan VR30DDTT twin-turbo V6 (where sensor B belongs to bank 2) or GM's 3.0 litre twin-turbo V6. P010D means the signal of sensor B is above its electrical upper limit – the counterpart of P0103.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor B shorted to voltage"],["h","Sensor B electronics failed"],["m","Interference from harness routing"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Substitute value mode, lack of power","Rich mixture and higher consumption possible"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P010E":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Sensor B is a second mass air flow sensor in a second intake path, found on engines with two air filter boxes – for example the Nissan VR30DDTT twin-turbo V6 (where sensor B belongs to bank 2) or GM's 3.0 litre twin-turbo V6. P010E covers brief dropouts or jumps in the signal of sensor B without a lasting low or high fault, the counterpart of P0104.",[["h","Loose contact at the sensor B connector"],["m","Chafed or partly broken wire"],["m","Sensor B electronics dropping out"],["l","Poor ground or interference"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","Occasional hesitation or stumble","Intermittent loss of power"],["medium",[20,350]],"c"],"P010F":["Mass or Volume Air Flow Sensor A/B Correlation","Engines with two intake paths have two mass air flow sensors, A and B. On a symmetrical V engine both sides should draw about the same amount of air, so the readings can be compared with each other.",[["h","Air leak or loose intake pipe on one side"],["h","One of the two sensors contaminated or aged"],["m","Air filter on one side clogged or wrongly fitted"],["l","Turbocharger or boost problem on one bank"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, uneven running","Mixture codes for one bank (P0171/P0174) possible"],["medium",[10,700]],"c"],"P0110":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 1 is the first air temperature sensor in the intake path; depending on the vehicle it is built into the mass air flow sensor, mounted near the air filter or, on some naturally aspirated GM engines, fitted in the intake manifold. Most versions are NTC thermistors in a 5 V divider: the hotter the air, the lower the signal voltage.",[["h","Connector or wiring to the sensor open or corroded"],["h","Sensor or mass air flow sensor with built-in temperature sensor faulty"],["m","Sensor supply or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power or torque limitation on some systems","Otherwise usually inconspicuous; cold start may be slightly worse"],["easy",[40,300]],"c"],"P0111":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 1 is the first air temperature sensor in the intake path; depending on the vehicle it is built into the mass air flow sensor, mounted near the air filter or, on some naturally aspirated GM engines, fitted in the intake manifold. Most versions are NTC thermistors in a 5 V divider: the hotter the air, the lower the signal voltage.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck at one value (ageing, oil from crankcase ventilation)"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or wiring distorts the reading"],["m","Comparison sensor (coolant, IAT2, charge air cooler) wrong"],["l","Undetected preheater, heat soak or sensor mounted near a heat source"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Slightly changed idle, power or cold start possible"],["easy",[40,300]],"c"],"P0112":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Low Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 1 is the first air temperature sensor in the intake path; depending on the vehicle it is built into the mass air flow sensor, mounted near the air filter or, on some naturally aspirated GM engines, fitted in the intake manifold. Most versions are NTC thermistors in a 5 V divider: the hotter the air, the lower the signal voltage.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed insulation)"],["h","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually hardly noticeable","Retarded ignition, reduced power or torque limit on some systems"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0113":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit High Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 1 is the first air temperature sensor in the intake path; depending on the vehicle it is built into the mass air flow sensor, mounted near the air filter or, on some naturally aspirated GM engines, fitted in the intake manifold. Most versions are NTC thermistors in a 5 V divider: the hotter the air, the lower the signal voltage.",[["h","Connector unplugged or loose, or wire broken"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Sensor return (ground) wire open"],["l","Signal wire shorted to a voltage source"]],["Check engine light on","Usually hardly noticeable","Slightly richer mixture and higher consumption possible"],["easy",[20,250]],"c"],"P0114":["Intake Air Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 1","Intake air temperature sensor 1 is the first air temperature sensor in the intake path; depending on the vehicle it is built into the mass air flow sensor, mounted near the air filter or, on some naturally aspirated GM engines, fitted in the intake manifold. Most versions are NTC thermistors in a 5 V divider: the hotter the air, the lower the signal voltage.",[["h","Loose contact at the connector (corrosion, weak terminal tension)"],["h","Partly broken wire that opens with engine movement"],["m","Sensor with internal contact problem"],["l","Electrical interference or ground fault"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Occasional brief stumble or rough idle possible"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0115":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit","The engine coolant temperature sensor 1 is an NTC thermistor: the control module feeds 5 V through a resistor, and as the coolant warms the resistance and signal voltage fall. Its value drives cold-start enrichment, idle speed, ignition timing, the radiator fan and the release of closed-loop mixture control.",[["h","Connector corroded or loose, terminal spread"],["m","Broken or chafed signal or ground wire"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (short, open, coolant inside)"],["l","Control module input or 5 V supply faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Poor cold or hot starting","Radiator fan runs constantly"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0116":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Range/Performance","The engine coolant temperature sensor 1 is an NTC thermistor: the control module feeds 5 V through a resistor, and as the coolant warms the resistance and signal voltage fall. Its value drives cold-start enrichment, idle speed, ignition timing, the radiator fan and the release of closed-loop mixture control.",[["h","Sensor drifting and reading too warm (ageing, coolant ingress)"],["m","Corrosion or contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["m","Block heater or preheater not detected"],["l","Comparison sensor (intake air, ambient) wrong"]],["Check engine light on","Poor cold start (too little enrichment)","Higher consumption or idle problems"],["easy",[30,180]],"c"],"P0117":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Low","The engine coolant temperature sensor 1 is an NTC thermistor: the control module feeds 5 V through a resistor, and as the coolant warms the resistance and signal voltage fall. Its value drives cold-start enrichment, idle speed, ignition timing, the radiator fan and the release of closed-loop mixture control.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafing, pinched harness)"],["h","Sensor shorted internally or coolant inside the sensor"],["m","Moisture or coolant in the connector"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Radiator fan runs constantly","Hard cold start (no enrichment, engine reported hot)"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0118":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit High","The engine coolant temperature sensor 1 is an NTC thermistor: the control module feeds 5 V through a resistor, and as the coolant warms the resistance and signal voltage fall. Its value drives cold-start enrichment, idle speed, ignition timing, the radiator fan and the release of closed-loop mixture control.",[["h","Connector unplugged or corroded, contact open"],["m","Broken signal or sensor ground wire"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"]],["Check engine light on","Radiator fan runs constantly","Temperature gauge stays cold"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0119":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 1 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The engine coolant temperature sensor 1 is an NTC thermistor: the control module feeds 5 V through a resistor, and as the coolant warms the resistance and signal voltage fall. Its value drives cold-start enrichment, idle speed, ignition timing, the radiator fan and the release of closed-loop mixture control.",[["h","Loose contact or corroded connector pins"],["m","Harness chafing that briefly grounds or opens with engine movement"],["m","Sensor with internal crack or contact fault"],["l","Air pockets at the sensor (poorly bled cooling system)"]],["Check engine light on","Temperature gauge jumps","Radiator fan switches on for no reason"],["medium",[20,200]],"c"],"P011A":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 1/2 Correlation","Some engines have two coolant temperature sensors that check each other. P011A is set when both are electrically fine but their readings differ more than a calibrated amount under conditions where they should agree.",[["h","Clogged heater core or restricted coolant flow at sensor 2"],["m","One of the two sensors drifting"],["m","Air in the cooling system or low coolant level"],["l","Block heater use in frost"]],["Check engine light on","Weak cabin heating (typical on Mazda)","Otherwise often no driving symptoms"],["medium",[50,1200]],"c"],"P011B":["Engine Coolant Temperature/Intake Air Temperature Correlation","After a long standstill the coolant and the intake air should both have settled close to ambient temperature. P011B compares the two sensors at start-up and is set when they disagree without either sensor showing a circuit fault.",[["h","Coolant temperature sensor stuck or drifting"],["m","Intake air temperature sensor drifting or heat-soaked"],["m","Undetected preheater or uneven cooling"],["m","Contact resistance at a sensor connector or ground"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","With a wrong coolant reading: poor cold start, higher consumption or odd fan behaviour"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P011C":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature/Intake Air Temperature Correlation Bank 1","On turbocharged engines a charge air cooler temperature sensor sits downstream of the compressor, usually after the charge air cooler, and intake air temperature sensor 1 sits upstream near the air filter. While driving they differ by design, because compression heats the air.",[["h","Charge air cooler temperature sensor drifting, oily or stuck"],["m","Intake air temperature sensor 1 drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or shared ground"],["l","Undetected preheating or uneven cooling (sun, residual heat)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Code often returns only after an overnight soak"],["easy",[50,300]],"c"],"P011D":["Charge Air Cooler Temperature/Intake Air Temperature Correlation Bank 2","This is the bank 2 counterpart of P011C and only exists on engines with two separate intake paths, each with its own charge air cooler temperature sensor and intake air temperature sensor – for example V engines with twin turbochargers and separate charge air coolers. After a long standstill both bank 2 sensors should read nearly the same; if they disagree at start-up, without either showing a circuit fault, P011D is set.",[["h","Bank 2 charge air cooler temperature sensor drifting, oily or stuck"],["m","Bank 2 intake air temperature sensor drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or ground"],["l","Undetected preheating or uneven cooling"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Code often returns only after an overnight soak"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P011E":["Engine Coolant Temperature 1 / Ambient Air Temperature Correlation","After a long standstill, typically six to eight hours, the coolant should have cooled to roughly the outside air temperature measured by the ambient sensor in the bumper or mirror. P011E compares coolant temperature sensor 1 with the ambient sensor at start-up and is set when they differ beyond a calibrated limit and no preheater was detected.",[["m","Coolant temperature sensor 1 drifting"],["m","Ambient temperature sensor drifting or affected by engine heat or sun"],["m","Preheater not detected"],["l","Contact resistance in one sensor's connector or wiring"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driving symptoms","Outside temperature display possibly wrong"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P011F":["Engine Coolant Temperature 2 / Ambient Air Temperature Correlation","This is the counterpart of P011E for the second coolant temperature sensor and only exists on engines that have one – for example at the engine outlet, the radiator or in the heater circuit. After a long standstill sensor 2 should also read roughly the outside temperature; if it disagrees with the ambient sensor beyond a calibrated limit at start-up, P011F is set without saying which sensor is wrong.",[["m","Coolant temperature sensor 2 drifting"],["m","Ambient temperature sensor faulty or influenced"],["m","Preheater or block heater"],["l","Contact resistance at sensor 2"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driving symptoms"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P0120":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit","Sensor A is, in practice, throttle position sensor 1 at the throttle body; the accelerator pedal has its own codes (P2120 onwards). With a cable-operated throttle there is a single potentiometer (about 0.5 V closed to 4.5 V wide open).",[["h","Connector at the throttle body corroded or loose, broken wire"],["m","Sensor or throttle body with integrated sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference disturbed (short on another sensor of the same circuit)"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light, on electronic throttles also wrench lamp","Limp mode with reduced power ('Reduced Engine Power') on electronic throttles","Raised or unsteady idle (cable systems)"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P0121":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit Range/Performance","Sensor A is, in practice, throttle position sensor 1 at the throttle body; the accelerator pedal has its own codes (P2120 onwards). With a cable-operated throttle there is a single potentiometer (about 0.5 V closed to 4.5 V wide open).",[["h","Carboned or mechanically sticking throttle plate"],["m","Worn potentiometer track (signal sticks or jumps)"],["m","Air leak or faulty MAF/MAP sensor (model comparison)"],["m","Contact resistance in signal or ground wire"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode with reduced power ('Reduced Engine Power') on electronic throttles","Rough or too high idle"],["medium",[20,500]],"c"],"P0122":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit Low","Sensor A is, in practice, throttle position sensor 1 at the throttle body; the accelerator pedal has its own codes (P2120 onwards). With a cable-operated throttle there is a single potentiometer (about 0.5 V closed to 4.5 V wide open).",[["h","Signal wire open or shorted to ground"],["h","Connector at the throttle body loose or corroded"],["m","5 V reference missing (open, or shorted by another sensor)"],["m","Sensor or throttle body faulty"]],["Check engine light, on electronic throttles also wrench lamp","Limp mode with reduced power ('Reduced Engine Power') on electronic throttles","Engine barely responds to the pedal or stalls at idle"],["medium",[20,450]],"c"],"P0123":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit High","Sensor A is, in practice, throttle position sensor 1 at the throttle body; the accelerator pedal has its own codes (P2120 onwards). With a cable-operated throttle there is a single potentiometer (about 0.5 V closed to 4.5 V wide open).",[["h","Sensor ground (low reference) open or high-resistance"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or 12 V"],["m","Sensor or throttle body faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Check engine light, on electronic throttles also wrench lamp","Limp mode with reduced power ('Reduced Engine Power') on electronic throttles","Raised idle or delayed throttle response"],["medium",[20,450]],"c"],"P0124":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit Intermittent","Sensor A is, in practice, throttle position sensor 1 at the throttle body; the accelerator pedal has its own codes (P2120 onwards). With a cable-operated throttle there is a single potentiometer (about 0.5 V closed to 4.5 V wide open).",[["h","Loose contact in the connector (spread terminals, corrosion)"],["m","Chafed or partly broken wire in the harness"],["m","Worn potentiometer track (dropout at a certain position)"],["l","Sensor electronics faulty (SENT)"]],["Check engine light, possibly only intermittently","Brief hesitation or power drop","Limp mode that disappears after restart"],["medium",[20,450]],"c"],"P0125":["Insufficient Coolant Temperature for Closed Loop Fuel Control","Closed-loop mixture control using the oxygen sensors is only released once the coolant has reached a minimum temperature. CARB requires that this happens within set times – from 2006 two minutes if the engine starts up to 8 °C below the release temperature, five minutes if 8–19 °C below.",[["h","Thermostat stuck open (especially in cold weather)"],["h","Coolant sensor reading too cold or slow (drift, contact resistance)"],["m","Low coolant level or air at the sensor"],["l","Much idling or coasting in frost"]],["Check engine light on","Engine warms up slowly, heater stays cold for a long time","Higher consumption during warm-up"],["medium",[40,350]],"c"],"P0126":["Insufficient Coolant Temperature for Stable Operation","The generic title refers to a coolant temperature that is not reached for stable operation, i.e. for releasing further functions and diagnostics; how manufacturers implement it differs.",[["h","Coolant sensor frozen or faulty (resistance does not change)"],["m","Thermostat stuck open or low coolant"],["l","Contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Idle speed stays raised","Engine and heater warm up slowly"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0127":["Intake Air Temperature Too High","P0127 is not a circuit fault but a functional check of the air path: the measured intake air temperature is too high for the operating state. The logic is documented for a Bosch EDC16 diesel (Saab 9-3): if the intake air exceeds 130 °C and does not fall below it within 45 s after the exhaust gas recirculation valve is closed, hot exhaust gas is evidently still entering or the sensor is wrong.",[["h","EGR valve stuck open or not sealing (diesel)"],["m","EGR cooler clogged or bypass flap stuck in bypass"],["m","Charge air cooler dirty or airflow through it blocked"],["m","Sensor reading wrong, or hot engine-bay air drawn in (loose intake duct)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode with torque limit","EGR switched off"],["medium",[50,1200]],"c"],"P0128":["Coolant Thermostat (Coolant Temperature Below Thermostat Regulating Temperature)","After a cold start the thermostat stays closed so the engine warms up quickly. CARB requires the coolant to reach a temperature at most 11 °C below the thermostat's rated value within a set time.",[["h","Thermostat stuck open or opening too early"],["m","Coolant sensor reading too low, or corrosion and resistance in its circuit"],["m","Low coolant level or air in the system"],["l","Radiator fan running constantly (relay stuck, wrong control)"]],["Check engine light on","Temperature gauge stays low or drops while driving","Heater does not get properly warm"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0129":["Barometric Pressure Too Low","The engine control module needs the ambient air pressure for mixture, altitude correction and boost limits. It comes either from a dedicated barometric sensor or from the MAP sensor, read at key-on with the engine stopped and updated at wide-open throttle.",[["h","MAP or baro sensor reading too low (dirty, oiled, drifted)"],["m","High resistance or corrosion in signal wire or connector"],["m","Sensor port or hose blocked"],["l","Control module (baro sensor often built in) or software"]],["Check engine light on","Rich mixture, higher consumption, rough idle","Loss of power (altitude-corrected boost limits)"],["easy",[20,250]],"c"],"P012A":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit","The turbocharger/supercharger inlet pressure sensor A sits between the air filter and the compressor. The engine uses it for the compressor pressure ratio, to judge air filter restriction (ambient minus inlet pressure) and to cross-check the other pressure sensors.",[["h","Connector or wiring open, corroded or shorted"],["m","Sensor faulty (SENT sensor reports internal error)"],["m","Control module software (false setting)"],["l","Engine control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable symptoms","Power limitation possible"],["easy",[20,250]],"c"],"P012B":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The turbocharger/supercharger inlet pressure sensor A sits between the air filter and the compressor. The engine uses it for the compressor pressure ratio, to judge air filter restriction (ambient minus inlet pressure) and to cross-check the other pressure sensors.",[["h","Sensor oiled (crankcase vent often joins before the compressor) or drifted"],["m","Air filter heavily clogged or intake blocked or collapsed"],["m","Leak in the intake pipe between filter and compressor or at the sensor flange"],["m","High resistance in the wiring"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or boost limitation","Possibly no noticeable symptoms"],["easy",[20,250]],"c"],"P012C":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit Low","The turbocharger/supercharger inlet pressure sensor A sits between the air filter and the compressor. The engine uses it for the compressor pressure ratio, to judge air filter restriction (ambient minus inlet pressure) and to cross-check the other pressure sensors.",[["h","Connector unplugged or corroded, signal or reference wire open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor failed internally"],["l","Control module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable symptoms","Power limitation possible"],["easy",[20,250]],"c"],"P012D":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit High","The turbocharger/supercharger inlet pressure sensor A sits ahead of the compressor – between air filter and turbocharger, or on GM's supercharged V8 between throttle and supercharger. It is an analogue 5 V pressure sensor used for the compressor pressure ratio, filter restriction and cross-checks with the other pressure sensors.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to 5 V reference or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor ground open or connector corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Control module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power or limp mode possible","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P012E":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The turbocharger/supercharger inlet pressure sensor A sits ahead of the compressor – between air filter and turbocharger, or on GM's supercharged V8 between throttle and supercharger. It is an analogue 5 V pressure sensor used for the compressor pressure ratio, filter restriction and cross-checks with the other pressure sensors.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor connector"],["m","Broken or chafed wire making intermittent contact"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (erratic output)"],["l","Control module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Occasional power reduction or boost fluctuation","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P012F":["Engine Coolant Temperature / Engine Oil Temperature Correlation","P012F compares the coolant temperature sensor with the engine oil temperature sensor. The documented user is Ford's Power Stroke diesel.",[["h","Oil cooler (oil-to-coolant heat exchanger) clogged – oil much hotter than coolant"],["m","Engine module software (false setting, especially in cold weather)"],["m","Coolant or oil temperature sensor inaccurate, or its wiring faulty"],["l","Thermostat stuck open or low coolant"]],["Check engine light and/or wrench lamp (Ford)","Raised oil temperature with a clogged oil cooler","Often no driving symptoms when software is the cause"],["hard",[80,2500]],"u"],"P0130":["O2 Sensor Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Wiring or connector of the bank 1 upstream sensor damaged, corroded or wet"],["h","Oxygen sensor faulty internally"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor or mixture fault (plausibility versions)"],["l","Wrong or aftermarket sensor type"]],["Check engine light on","Higher consumption, possibly rough running (no mixture control)","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0131":["O2 Sensor Circuit Low Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Signal wire (or a wide-band sensor wire) shorted to ground, e.g. chafed on exhaust or gearbox"],["h","Sensor faulty or poisoned (permanently lean signal)"],["m","Large exhaust leak ahead of the sensor or misfires"],["m","Connector wet or corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Higher consumption, possibly rough running (no mixture control)","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0132":["O2 Sensor Circuit High Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to the heater supply or battery voltage (melted or chafed harness)"],["h","Sensor faulty internally (heater-to-signal short)"],["m","Open or high-resistance signal or ground wire (GM reads its bias voltage)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Higher consumption, possibly soot or fuel smell","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0133":["O2 Sensor Circuit Slow Response Bank 1 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Aged, sooted or poisoned upstream sensor (oil, silicone, coolant, additives)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold gasket, crack)"],["m","Sensor heater weak or intermittent (sensor too cold)"],["m","Mixture fault: air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Slightly higher consumption","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0134":["O2 Sensor Circuit No Activity Detected Bank 1 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Open or high-resistance signal or ground wire, connector loose or corroded"],["h","Sensor faulty (cell high-resistance, worn out)"],["m","Sensor heater faulty – sensor does not get hot enough"],["l","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor or cracked sensor"]],["Check engine light on","No mixture control (open loop), higher consumption","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0135":["O2 Sensor Heater Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor on bank 1 (sensor 1) has an electric heater so that it reaches its working temperature of about 300 °C soon after a cold start. The control module switches the heater on the ground side and checks driver voltage and heater current: GM fails it outside 0.3–2.5 A (or 3.1 A) and calls it 'heater performance', Ford outside roughly 0.22–0.465 A minimum to 3 A, Saab Trionic outside 0.5–2.3 A for 5 s.",[["h","Heater element of the sensor burnt out (sensor must be replaced)"],["m","Fuse or relay of the heater supply faulty"],["m","Heater wiring or connector open, corroded or shorted"],["l","Unsuitable replacement sensor (different heater resistance)"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cold-start phase without mixture control, more fuel and emissions","Follow-up codes P0133/P0134 possible (cold sensor)"],["easy",[5,300]],"c"],"P0136":["O2 Sensor Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. P0136 is the general circuit code, and its exact meaning varies by manufacturer.",[["h","Downstream sensor aged or poisoned (stays in the middle band)"],["m","Signal or ground wire open or high-resistance, connector corroded"],["m","Sensor heater failed (sensor stays cold)"],["l","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Slightly higher consumption possible without downstream trim"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0137":["O2 Sensor Circuit Low Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. P0137 means the signal stays below the lower limit.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or melted against the exhaust"],["h","Sensor faulty (cracked element, thermal shock from water)"],["m","Signal circuit open (counts as low on Ford)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (lean reading)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0138":["O2 Sensor Circuit High Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. P0138 means the signal stays above the upper limit.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to heater supply or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (heater-to-signal short)"],["m","Signal circuit open or high-resistance (GM bias logic)"],["l","Genuinely rich exhaust or oil/coolant contamination"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0139":["O2 Sensor Circuit Slow Response Bank 1 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. P0139 means the sensor reacts too slowly to mixture changes.",[["h","Sensor aged or contaminated (oil ash, silicone, additives)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"],["m","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"],["l","High-resistance ground or connector"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","P0420 may follow"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P013A":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Rich to Lean Bank 1 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["l","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P013B":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Lean to Rich Bank 1 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["l","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P013C":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Rich to Lean Bank 2 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["l","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P013D":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Lean to Rich Bank 2 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["l","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P013E":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Rich to Lean Bank 1 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["m","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P013F":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Lean to Rich Bank 1 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["m","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0140":["O2 Sensor Circuit No Activity Detected Bank 1 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 1 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. P0140 means the sensor produces no voltage of its own and the control module only sees its own reference (bias) voltage.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector off or corroded"],["m","Sensor heater failed, sensor never becomes active"],["m","Sensor worn out (no longer produces voltage)"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Live data: sensor 2 stuck at about 450 mV (or 1.9 V)"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0141":["O2 Sensor Heater Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor on bank 1 (sensor 2) has an electric heater so it reaches its working temperature quickly. The control module switches the heater on the ground side and checks both the driver voltage against the commanded state and the heater current.",[["h","Heater element in the sensor burnt out (replace sensor)"],["m","Heater supply (fuse, relay) or control wire open"],["m","Short in the heater (current too high)"],["l","Connector corroded, contact resistance"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Follow-up codes P0140/P0136 possible"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0142":["O2 Sensor Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 is a third oxygen sensor on bank 1, behind a second converter or converter brick. It only exists on some vehicles – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's 3.0 V6 supercharged (2013–2016, where sensor 2 is 'mid-cat' and sensor 3 'post-cat') and Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Sensor 3 faulty (thermal shock, ageing)"],["m","Wiring or connector under the car damaged or corroded"],["m","Engine module software (Jaguar Land Rover)"],["l","Exhaust leak ahead of sensor 3"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0143":["O2 Sensor Circuit Low Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 is a third oxygen sensor on bank 1, behind a second converter or converter brick. It only exists on some vehicles – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's 3.0 V6 supercharged (2013–2016, where sensor 2 is 'mid-cat' and sensor 3 'post-cat') and Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or melted against the exhaust"],["h","Sensor faulty (cracked element, thermal shock from water)"],["m","Signal circuit open (counts as low on Ford)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (lean reading)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0144":["O2 Sensor Circuit High Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 is a third oxygen sensor on bank 1, behind a second converter or converter brick. It only exists on some vehicles – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's 3.0 V6 supercharged (2013–2016, where sensor 2 is 'mid-cat' and sensor 3 'post-cat') and Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to heater supply or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (heater-to-signal short)"],["m","Signal circuit open or high-resistance (GM bias logic)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0145":["O2 Sensor Circuit Slow Response Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 is a third oxygen sensor on bank 1, behind a second converter or converter brick. It only exists on some vehicles – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's 3.0 V6 supercharged (2013–2016, where sensor 2 is 'mid-cat' and sensor 3 'post-cat') and Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Sensor aged or contaminated (oil ash, silicone, additives)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"],["m","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0146":["O2 Sensor Circuit No Activity Detected Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 is a third oxygen sensor on bank 1, behind a second converter or converter brick. It only exists on some vehicles – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's 3.0 V6 supercharged (2013–2016, where sensor 2 is 'mid-cat' and sensor 3 'post-cat') and Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector off or corroded"],["m","Sensor heater failed, sensor never becomes active"],["m","Sensor worn out (no longer produces voltage)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0147":["O2 Sensor Heater Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 is a third oxygen sensor on bank 1, behind a second converter or converter brick. It only exists on some vehicles – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's 3.0 V6 supercharged (2013–2016, where sensor 2 is 'mid-cat' and sensor 3 'post-cat') and Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Heater element in the sensor burnt out (replace sensor)"],["m","Heater supply (fuse, relay) or control wire open"],["l","Connector corroded, contact resistance"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Follow-up codes for sensor 3 possible","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0148":["Fuel Delivery Error","P0148 is an early, rather unspecific OBD II code: the control module finds that the fuel delivered does not match what was requested. Saab's 3.0 V6 diesel (Isuzu) uses it for leak detection: from the balance of quantity and rail pressure it recognises a leak of 90 mm³ per crankshaft revolution, monitored continuously up to 6,400 rpm, and then shuts the engine off – a restart is only possible in the next drive cycle.",[["h","Fuel leak in the high-pressure or return section (line, rail, injector leakage)"],["m","Fuel filter clogged or lift pump weak"],["m","High-pressure or injection pump worn"],["l","Pressure or volume control valve faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Engine stalls or will not restart (Saab: shut-off until next drive cycle)","Loss of power, stumbling"],["hard",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0149":["Fuel Timing Error","P0149 is a diesel code for a deviation of the actual from the target start of injection. On distributor pumps such as the Bosch VP44, a timing device sets the delivery start, and the pump control unit compares the actual position with the target.",[["h","Injection pump wrongly set or drive (belt, key) shifted"],["m","Timing device or timing solenoid of the pump faulty"],["m","Air in the fuel or low supply pressure"],["l","Pump control unit faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode with power limitation","Starting problems up to no start"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P014A":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Rich to Lean Bank 2 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["m","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P014B":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Lean to Rich Bank 2 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor (sensor 2, after the catalytic converter) is used for fine mixture trim and to monitor the converter. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Downstream oxygen sensor aged or contaminated (soot, oil, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Exhaust leak between manifold/turbo and the sensor"],["m","Converter faulty or non-original with different oxygen storage"],["l","Control module software too sensitive (update available)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change in driving","Catalyst monitor may stay incomplete (inspection readiness missing)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P014C":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Rich to Lean Bank 1 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P014D":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Lean to Rich Bank 1 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P014E":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Rich to Lean Bank 2 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P014F":["O2 Sensor Slow Response - Lean to Rich Bank 2 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Slow response' means the signal transition itself is too sluggish – it takes too long to pass between two voltage levels.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P0150":["O2 Sensor Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Wiring or connector of the bank 2 upstream sensor damaged, corroded or wet"],["h","Oxygen sensor faulty internally"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor or mixture fault (plausibility versions)"],["l","Control module or sensor interface chip faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Higher consumption, possibly rough running (no mixture control)","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0151":["O2 Sensor Circuit Low Voltage Bank 2 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Signal wire (or a wide-band sensor wire) shorted to ground, e.g. chafed on exhaust or gearbox"],["h","Sensor faulty or poisoned (permanently lean signal)"],["m","Large exhaust leak ahead of the sensor or misfires"],["m","Connector wet or corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Higher consumption, possibly rough running (no mixture control)","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0152":["O2 Sensor Circuit High Voltage Bank 2 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to the heater supply or battery voltage (melted or chafed harness)"],["h","Sensor faulty internally (heater-to-signal short)"],["m","Open or high-resistance signal or ground wire (GM reads its bias voltage)"],["l","Sensor contaminated by silicone, coolant or oil"]],["Check engine light on","Higher consumption, possibly rough running (no mixture control)","Higher CO/HC values"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0153":["O2 Sensor Circuit Slow Response Bank 2 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Aged, sooted or poisoned upstream sensor (oil, silicone, coolant, additives)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold gasket, crack)"],["m","Sensor heater weak or intermittent (sensor too cold)"],["m","Mixture fault: air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Slightly higher consumption","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0154":["O2 Sensor Circuit No Activity Detected Bank 2 Sensor 1","The oxygen sensor before the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 1) is the main control sensor for the mixture. It is either a switching sensor (about 0.1 V lean to 0.9 V rich) or a wide-band sensor that works with a pump current evaluated by an interface chip.",[["h","Open or high-resistance signal or ground wire, connector loose or corroded"],["h","Sensor faulty (cell high-resistance, worn out)"],["m","Sensor heater faulty – sensor does not get hot enough"],["l","Control module bias or measuring circuit faulty"]],["Check engine light on","No mixture control (open loop), higher consumption","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0155":["O2 Sensor Heater Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor on bank 2 (sensor 1) has its own electric heater. P0155 is the bank 2 counterpart of P0135: the heater current is outside its window or the driver voltage does not follow the command.",[["h","Heater element of the sensor burnt out (sensor must be replaced)"],["m","Fuse or relay of the heater supply faulty"],["m","Heater wiring or connector open, corroded or shorted"],["l","Heater driver in the control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Higher emissions and consumption after cold start","Follow-up codes P0153/P0154 possible"],["medium",[5,300]],"c"],"P0156":["O2 Sensor Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. Bank 2 exists only on V and boxer engines with a separate downstream sensor per bank.",[["h","Downstream sensor aged or poisoned (stays in the middle band)"],["m","Signal or ground wire open or high-resistance, connector corroded"],["m","Sensor heater failed (sensor stays cold)"],["l","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0157":["O2 Sensor Circuit Low Voltage Bank 2 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. Bank 2 exists only on V and boxer engines with a separate downstream sensor per bank.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or melted against the exhaust"],["h","Sensor faulty (cracked element, thermal shock from water)"],["m","Signal circuit open (counts as low on Ford)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (lean reading)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0158":["O2 Sensor Circuit High Voltage Bank 2 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. Bank 2 exists only on V and boxer engines with a separate downstream sensor per bank.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to heater supply or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (heater-to-signal short)"],["m","Signal circuit open or high-resistance (GM bias logic)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0159":["O2 Sensor Circuit Slow Response Bank 2 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor (about 0.1–0.9 V) used for converter monitoring and fine mixture trim. Bank 2 exists only on V and boxer engines with a separate downstream sensor per bank.",[["h","Sensor aged or contaminated (oil ash, silicone, additives)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"],["m","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"],["l","Thermal shock damage from water"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness not set","P0430 may follow"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P015A":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Rich to Lean Bank 1 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P015B":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Lean to Rich Bank 1 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P015C":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Rich to Lean Bank 2 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P015D":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Lean to Rich Bank 2 Sensor 1","The upstream oxygen sensor (sensor 1, before the catalytic converter) is the main control sensor for the air-fuel mixture. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late, or does not reach the target level at all, after the mixture has changed.",[["h","Upstream oxygen sensor aged or poisoned (silicone, oil, coolant, soot)"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (manifold, flange)"],["l","Mixture fault: intake air leak, injectors, fuel pressure, air flow sensor"],["l","Sensor heater weak, sensor too cold"]],["Check engine light on","Slightly higher consumption or rough idle possible","Higher emissions, possible emissions test failure"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P015E":["Excessive Time to Enter Closed Loop Fuel Timing Control","Diesel fuel systems correct injection timing and quantity in closed loop, often with learning routines that run during suitable overrun phases. CARB requires a fault when such feedback control does not start within a manufacturer-defined time.",[["h","Learning conditions (overrun phases, temperatures) met too rarely"],["m","Control module software"],["m","Injectors outside tolerance or unstable fuel pressure"],["l","Injector wiring or sensors faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driving symptoms","Possibly louder combustion noise or uneven running with unlearned injectors"],["medium",[0,1500]],"u"],"P015F":["Excessive Time to Enter Closed Loop Cylinder Balance Control","Cylinder balance control evens out the contribution of individual cylinders – on diesels by adjusting each injector's quantity based on crankshaft speed fluctuations. P015F, by its title, is set when this closed-loop control does not become active within the expected time after starting, for example because the crankshaft wheel has not been learned or the conditions are missing.",[["m","Crankshaft wheel adaptation not learned or speed signal disturbed"],["m","Injector deviating strongly or injector circuit fault"],["l","Control module software"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly rough idle","Often no symptoms"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P0160":["O2 Sensor Circuit No Activity Detected Bank 2 Sensor 2","The oxygen sensor after the catalytic converter on bank 2 (sensor 2) is a switching sensor used for converter monitoring and downstream mixture trim; it exists only on V and boxer engines with a separate downstream sensor per bank. P0160 means the sensor shows no change in voltage although it is heated and the engine is in closed loop; the control module only sees its own bias voltage, so the circuit is open or high-resistance or the sensor is dead.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open or high-resistance, connector corroded"],["h","Sensor dead, poisoned or damaged by thermal shock"],["m","Heater failed or fuse blown by a heater short"],["l","Signal wire shorted to voltage"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness for bank 2 not set","Often with P0161 or P0157/P0158"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0161":["O2 Sensor Heater Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 2","The downstream oxygen sensor on bank 2 (sensor 2) has an electric heater so that it reaches working temperature quickly. P0161 concerns this heater circuit and its performance.",[["h","Heater element of the sensor burnt out"],["m","Heater wiring, connector or fuse open or corroded"],["l","Short in the heater circuit (current too high)"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Follow-up codes P0159/P0160 possible","Catalyst monitor readiness for bank 2 not set"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0162":["O2 Sensor Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick. It is rare – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (2013–2016), where it is the 'post-cat' sensor of the second bank, and for Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Sensor faulty or damaged by thermal shock"],["m","Wiring or connector under the car damaged"],["m","Engine module software (Jaguar Land Rover)"],["l","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst readiness incomplete","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0163":["O2 Sensor Circuit Low Voltage Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick. It is rare – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (2013–2016), where it is the 'post-cat' sensor of the second bank, and for Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed or melted)"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor (apparently lean)"],["l","Engine module software (Jaguar Land Rover)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst readiness incomplete","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0164":["O2 Sensor Circuit High Voltage Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick. It is rare – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (2013–2016), where it is the 'post-cat' sensor of the second bank, and for Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to voltage or to the heater wire"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (heater-to-signal short)"],["l","Sensor ground open (GM logic)"],["l","Engine module software (Jaguar Land Rover)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst readiness incomplete","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0165":["O2 Sensor Circuit Slow Response Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick. It is rare – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (2013–2016), where it is the 'post-cat' sensor of the second bank, and for Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Sensor aged or contaminated"],["m","Sensor heater weak"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"],["l","Wiring or connector high-resistance"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst readiness incomplete","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0166":["O2 Sensor Circuit No Activity Detected Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick. It is rare – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (2013–2016), where it is the 'post-cat' sensor of the second bank, and for Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector corroded"],["h","Sensor dead"],["m","Heater failed (sensor stays cold)"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Often together with P0167","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0167":["O2 Sensor Heater Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick. It is rare – documented for Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (2013–2016), where it is the 'post-cat' sensor of the second bank, and for Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume.",[["h","Heater element burnt out"],["m","Heater wiring, connector or fuse"],["l","Short circuit (current above 3 A)"],["l","Engine module driver or software (Jaguar Land Rover)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","P0165/P0166 may follow","Usually no noticeable change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0168":["Fuel Temperature Too High","P0168 is not a sensor fault but an operating condition, almost only on diesels: the fuel temperature sensor reports fuel hotter than a protective limit. The fuel heats up through the return flow from the high-pressure pump and injectors, especially with little fuel in the tank, at idle after full load, in hot weather or with a failed fuel cooler; one manufacturer patent names about 80 °C as the upper limit to protect seals, against a normal range of roughly 5–60 °C.",[["h","Fuel cooler (air or water cooler in the return) dirty or faulty, coolant circuit disturbed"],["m","Very little fuel in the tank at high load or heat"],["m","Excessive return flow from worn high-pressure pump or injectors, or faulty overflow valve"],["m","Fuel temperature sensor drifting and reading too high"]],["Loss of power, limp mode (reduced injection quantity)","Warning light depending on manufacturer","Occurs in heat, at full load, with little fuel in the tank"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0169":["Incorrect Fuel Composition","According to its title, P0169 means the control module has detected a fuel composition outside what is expected – wrong fuel or an impermissible ethanol or water content – while the composition sensor itself works. None of the manufacturer OBD documents checked (GM, Ford) uses the code: GM reports water or conductive fuel via the ethanol sensor frequency as P2269, and Ford has had no composition sensor since 2000.",[["h","Wrong fuel (E85 in a non-flex-fuel vehicle, ethanol content outside calibration)"],["m","Water or contamination in the fuel"],["m","Fuel composition sensor drifting (value in range but wrong)"],["l","Control module software"]],["Check engine light on","Poor cold start, stumbling, loss of power","Mixture adaptation codes (P0171/P0172)"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P016A":["Excessive Time To Enter Closed Loop Air/Fuel Ratio Control","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Oxygen sensor heater not working (fuse, fuse holder, wiring, heater element)"],["m","Oxygen sensor sluggish or aged"],["m","Control module software (false setting)"],["l","Engine not warming up (thermostat open, coolant sensor)"]],["Check engine light on","Higher consumption after cold start","Often with oxygen sensor or heater codes"],["medium",[10,400]],"u"],"P016B":["Closed Loop Air/Fuel Ratio Control At Limit - System Too Rich","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["m","Fuel pressure too high / high-pressure pump or regulator fault (direct injection)"],["m","Leaking injectors"],["m","Purge valve open"],["l","Oxygen sensor or air flow/pressure sensor offset, control module software"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Poor cold start, rough idle","Higher consumption, fuel smell"],["medium",[50,900]],"u"],"P016C":["Closed Loop Air/Fuel Ratio Control At Limit - System Too Lean","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Air leak (intake, crankcase ventilation)"],["m","Fuel pressure or delivery too low"],["m","Wrongly learned adaptation / control module software"],["l","Air flow or pressure sensor reading low, injectors clogged"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Stalling when stopping or at idle","Rough idle, hesitation"],["medium",[0,600]],"u"],"P016D":["Excessive Time To Enter Closed Loop Fuel Pressure Control","Common-rail diesels regulate rail pressure in closed loop: the control module compares the rail pressure sensor with the target and adjusts a volume or pressure control valve, learning correction factors as it goes. CARB requires faults to be flagged when this feedback control does not start in time or runs out of authority.",[["h","Rail pressure sensor drifting (wrong slope)"],["m","Volume or pressure control valve sticking or worn"],["m","Unstable low-pressure supply (filter, lift pump, air in system)"],["l","Fuel leak or high injector return flow"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable symptoms","Occasional power reduction or rough idle"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P016E":["Closed Loop Fuel Pressure Control At Limit - Pressure Too High","Common-rail diesels regulate rail pressure in closed loop: the control module compares the rail pressure sensor with the target and adjusts a volume or pressure control valve, learning correction factors as it goes. CARB requires faults to be flagged when this feedback control does not start in time or runs out of authority.",[["h","Rail pressure sensor or its contacts/wiring (corrosion) giving a falsely high signal"],["m","Pressure or volume control valve sticking (lets too much through)"],["m","Return line from control valve or rail blocked or kinked"],["l","Control module software or calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Hard combustion noise, knocking","Power reduction (limp mode)"],["medium",[150,1500]],"u"],"P016F":["Closed Loop Fuel Pressure Control At Limit - Pressure Too Low","Common-rail diesels regulate rail pressure in closed loop: the control module compares the rail pressure sensor with the target and adjusts a volume or pressure control valve, learning correction factors as it goes. CARB requires faults to be flagged when this feedback control does not start in time or runs out of authority.",[["h","Weak low-pressure supply: lift/tank pump, pump control module, clogged filter"],["h","Connector or ground problems (corrosion, water) at pump module, low-pressure sensor or ground point"],["m","Pressure relief valve open or its control circuit interrupted"],["m","Worn high-pressure pump or internal leak"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish acceleration","Hard starting, long cranking, stalling after start"],["hard",[100,2000]],"u"],"P0170":["Fuel Trim Bank 1","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Air flow sensor dirty or drifting"],["m","Air leak or crankcase ventilation leaking"],["m","Injection quantity error (diesel: injector drift, rail pressure sensor; petrol: injectors, fuel pressure)"],["l","Oxygen or NOx sensor offset"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Rough idle or hesitation","Higher consumption"],["medium",[20,600]],"u"],"P0171":["System Too Lean Bank 1","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Air leak after the air flow sensor (intake manifold gasket, vacuum hoses, throttle body gasket, brake booster line)"],["h","Crankcase ventilation leaking or faulty (PCV hose, diaphragm in valve cover, oil cap, dipstick)"],["m","Air flow sensor dirty or drifting (measures too little air)"],["m","Fuel pressure or delivery too low (weak pump, clogged filter, regulator, tank nearly empty)"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Rough or unsteady idle, possibly stalling","Hesitation or lack of power when accelerating"],["medium",[20,600]],"c"],"P0172":["System Too Rich Bank 1","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Purge valve stuck open or charcoal canister saturated"],["m","Leaking or dripping injectors (also direct injection)"],["m","Fuel pressure too high (faulty regulator, blocked return, regulator vacuum hose)"],["m","Fuel in the engine oil (short trips, leaking injectors), vapour via crankcase ventilation"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Higher consumption, fuel smell","Black smoke, sooted spark plugs"],["medium",[40,900]],"c"],"P0173":["Fuel Trim Bank 2","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Air flow sensor dirty or faulty"],["m","Air leak on bank 2 or at the crankcase ventilation"],["m","Fuel pressure or bank 2 injectors"],["l","Exhaust leak ahead of the bank 2 oxygen sensor"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Rough idle","Higher consumption"],["medium",[20,600]],"u"],"P0174":["System Too Lean Bank 2","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Air leak after the air flow sensor on bank 2 (intake manifold gasket, vacuum hoses, throttle body gasket, brake booster line)"],["h","Crankcase ventilation leaking or faulty (PCV hose, diaphragm in valve cover, oil cap, dipstick)"],["m","Air flow sensor dirty or drifting (measures too little air)"],["m","Fuel pressure or delivery too low (weak pump, clogged filter, regulator, tank nearly empty)"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Rough or unsteady idle, possibly stalling","Hesitation or lack of power when accelerating"],["medium",[20,700]],"c"],"P0175":["System Too Rich Bank 2","With closed-loop control the engine module corrects the injected fuel using the oxygen sensor: short-term fuel trim reacts within fractions of a second, long-term trim stores a learned base correction per load and speed range. Positive values mean fuel is being added because the mixture was lean, negative values mean fuel is being removed; around ±10 % is normal.",[["h","Purge valve stuck open or charcoal canister saturated"],["m","Leaking or dripping injectors on bank 2 (also direct injection)"],["m","Fuel pressure too high (faulty regulator, blocked return, regulator vacuum hose)"],["m","Fuel in the engine oil (short trips, leaking injectors), vapour via crankcase ventilation"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Higher consumption, fuel smell","Black smoke, sooted spark plugs"],["medium",[40,900]],"c"],"P0176":["Fuel Composition Sensor Circuit","The fuel composition sensor is fitted only to flex-fuel vehicles that can run on petrol or ethanol up to E85. GM uses a capacitive sensor in the fuel line whose square-wave output frequency gives the ethanol content – 50 Hz for E0 up to 150 Hz for E100 – while the pulse width carries the fuel temperature.",[["h","Flex-fuel sensor electrically failed (corrosion from road salt)"],["h","Connector or wire corroded, open or shorted"],["m","Supply (relay, fuse) missing"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Ethanol content in scan data implausible or frozen","Starting problems on E85"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P0177":["Fuel Composition Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The fuel composition sensor is fitted only to flex-fuel vehicles that can run on petrol or ethanol up to E85. GM uses a capacitive sensor in the fuel line whose square-wave output frequency gives the ethanol content – 50 Hz for E0 up to 150 Hz for E100 – while the pulse width carries the fuel temperature.",[["h","Sensor drifting or dirty inside"],["m","Water or contamination in the fuel"],["m","Loose contact in the connector"],["l","Control module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Wrong ethanol value, stumbling or starting problems","Adaptation codes P0171/P0172"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P0178":["Fuel Composition Sensor Circuit Low","The fuel composition sensor is fitted only to flex-fuel vehicles that can run on petrol or ethanol up to E85. GM uses a capacitive sensor in the fuel line whose square-wave output frequency gives the ethanol content – 50 Hz for E0 up to 150 Hz for E100 – while the pulse width carries the fuel temperature.",[["h","Signal wire open or shorted to ground"],["h","Sensor without supply (relay, fuse, ground)"],["h","Sensor electrically failed (corrosion)"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Ethanol value 0 % or frozen","Starting problems on E85"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P0179":["Fuel Composition Sensor Circuit High","The fuel composition sensor is fitted only to flex-fuel vehicles that can run on petrol or ethanol up to E85. GM uses a capacitive sensor in the fuel line whose square-wave output frequency gives the ethanol content – 50 Hz for E0 up to 150 Hz for E100 – while the pulse width carries the fuel temperature.",[["h","Sensor electronics faulty"],["m","Interference or short on the signal wire"],["m","Water or conductive fuel near the P2269 limit"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Ethanol value implausibly high (100 %)","Rich mixture, stumbling, higher consumption"],["easy",[80,300]],"c"],"P017A":["Cylinder Head Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The cylinder head temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor that measures the temperature of the cylinder head metal or of the coolant in the head. Some Ford engines use it instead of a coolant temperature sensor and calculate a coolant value from it; VW group V8 petrol engines use it as sender G407.",[["h","Wiring or connector open or corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty (moisture in the housing)"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Coolant temperature warning or wrong gauge reading","Radiator fan runs loud or constantly"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P017B":["Cylinder Head Temperature Sensor A Range/Performance","The cylinder head temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor that measures the temperature of the cylinder head metal or of the coolant in the head. Some Ford engines use it instead of a coolant temperature sensor and calculate a coolant value from it; VW group V8 petrol engines use it as sender G407.",[["h","Sensor damaged by moisture (O-ring)"],["m","Genuine temperature problem (air in cooling system, thermostat)"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["l","Software or calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Red coolant warning or overheating warning","Radiator fan runs loud or constantly"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P017C":["Cylinder Head Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The cylinder head temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor that measures the temperature of the cylinder head metal or of the coolant in the head. Some Ford engines use it instead of a coolant temperature sensor and calculate a coolant value from it; VW group V8 petrol engines use it as sender G407.",[["h","Water or coolant inside the sensor (O-ring damaged)"],["m","Signal wire chafed against ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Overheating or coolant warning despite a cold engine","Radiator fan runs constantly"],["medium",[50,600]],"u"],"P017D":["Cylinder Head Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","The cylinder head temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor that measures the temperature of the cylinder head metal or of the coolant in the head. Some Ford engines use it instead of a coolant temperature sensor and calculate a coolant value from it; VW group V8 petrol engines use it as sender G407.",[["h","Sensor faulty (series fault on Ford 5.0 litre 2019)"],["h","Connector unplugged or corroded, wire open"],["l","Signal wire shorted to voltage"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Temperature gauge cold or missing","Radiator fan runs constantly"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P017E":["Cylinder Head Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The cylinder head temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor that measures the temperature of the cylinder head metal or of the coolant in the head. Some Ford engines use it instead of a coolant temperature sensor and calculate a coolant value from it; VW group V8 petrol engines use it as sender G407.",[["h","Moisture inside the sensor (O-ring)"],["h","Loose contact at connector or pin"],["m","Wire broken by engine movement or heat"],["l","Sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Intermittent coolant or overheating warning","Temperature gauge jumps"],["medium",[50,600]],"u"],"P017F":["EVAP System Leak Detection Pump Module Air Filter Restricted","Leak detection pump modules in the evaporative emission system (for example Bosch's DM-TL, used by VW, BMW, Porsche, Volvo, Mini, Land Rover and Kia/Hyundai) draw fresh air through their own filter, often mounted separately via a hose; at rest the charcoal canister is also vented through it. P017F means this filter is restricted – by dust, mud or water when fitted near a wheel arch.",[["h","Leak detection pump filter dirty or blocked"],["m","Moisture or ice in filter or hose"],["m","Hose between filter and pump kinked or blocked"],["l","Pump module faulty (wrong reference)"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set","Possibly refuelling difficulties (venting)"],["easy",[20,250]],"u"],"P0180":["Fuel Temperature Sensor A Circuit","Fuel temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Wire or connector open or corroded"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["l","Control module reference or ground"]],["Check engine light on","Substitute value; possibly starting problems in cold or hot conditions"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0181":["Fuel Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","Fuel temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Sensor drifting (characteristic shifted)"],["m","Comparison sensor wrong (charge air, coolant, intake air)"],["m","Block heater or unusual temperatures (cold fuel during self-test)"],["m","Wiring or connector (e.g. routing at the pump unit)"]],["Check engine light on (often without change in driving)","Possibly harder cold or hot start","Diesel: less accurate rail pressure and quantity correction"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0182":["Fuel Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","Fuel temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["h","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel temperature in scan data implausibly high (about 150 °C)","Possibly cold-start problems"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0183":["Fuel Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","Fuel temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Connector off or wire open"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Wiring routing or chafing at the pump unit"],["m","Sensor ground open or short to 5 V"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel temperature in scan data about −40 °C","Usually no change in driving; possibly hot-start problems"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0184":["Fuel Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","Fuel temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Loose contact or weak terminal tension in the connector"],["h","Wire break or chafing from vibration"],["m","Sensor with internal dropouts"],["m","Corrosion from moisture"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel temperature in scan data jumps","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[30,350]],"c"],"P0185":["Fuel Temperature Sensor B Circuit","Fuel temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Wire or connector open or corroded"],["h","Sensor faulty"],["l","Control module reference or ground"]],["Check engine light on","Substitute value, usually no change in driving"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0186":["Fuel Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Fuel temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Sensor drifting (characteristic shifted)"],["m","Comparison sensor wrong (charge air, coolant, intake air)"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0187":["Fuel Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","Fuel temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["h","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Implausibly high fuel temperature in scan data","Possibly starting problems"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0188":["Fuel Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","Fuel temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Connector off or wire open"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Sensor ground open or short to 5 V"],["l","Engine control module"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel temperature about −40/−50 °C in scan data","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0189":["Fuel Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent","Fuel temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor: the hotter the fuel, the lower its resistance and signal voltage. On diesels it sits in the injection pump, rail or return line; on Ford petrol direct-injection engines it is built into the rail pressure sensor, and GM uses digital rail temperature sensors on its direct-injection engines.",[["h","Loose contact or weak terminal tension in the connector"],["h","Wire break or chafing from vibration"],["m","Sensor with internal dropouts"]],["Check engine light on","Jumping readings"],["medium",[30,350]],"c"],"P018A":["Fuel Pressure Sensor B Circuit","Fuel pressure sensor B is a second fuel pressure sensor; which one is up to the manufacturer. On GM it is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line of direct-injection engines (0–743 kPa), read by the engine module or the fuel pump control module; on Ford engines with dual injection it is the sensor on the port-injection rail (0.5–4.5 V for about 0–70 psi).",[["h","Connector corroded or loose, broken wire"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference disturbed (shared with other sensors)"],["l","Engine or fuel pump control module, or CAN transmission"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change in driving","Starting problems or hesitation with fixed pump control"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P018B":["Fuel Pressure Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Fuel pressure sensor B is a second fuel pressure sensor; which one is up to the manufacturer. On GM it is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line of direct-injection engines (0–743 kPa), read by the engine module or the fuel pump control module; on Ford engines with dual injection it is the sensor on the port-injection rail (0.5–4.5 V for about 0–70 psi).",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck (diaphragm, electronics)"],["m","Genuine low-pressure fault: weak pump, clogged filter, faulty regulator"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or ground"],["l","Fuel pump control module or its control"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or hesitation when accelerating","Stumbling under load"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P018C":["Fuel Pressure Sensor B Circuit Low","Fuel pressure sensor B is a second fuel pressure sensor; which one is up to the manufacturer. On GM it is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line of direct-injection engines (0–743 kPa), read by the engine module or the fuel pump control module; on Ford engines with dual injection it is the sensor on the port-injection rail (0.5–4.5 V for about 0–70 psi).",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or open"],["m","5 V reference missing or shorted (also by another sensor on the circuit)"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Starting problems","Lack of power or stumbling"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P018D":["Fuel Pressure Sensor B Circuit High","Fuel pressure sensor B is a second fuel pressure sensor; which one is up to the manufacturer. On GM it is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line of direct-injection engines (0–743 kPa), read by the engine module or the fuel pump control module; on Ford engines with dual injection it is the sensor on the port-injection rail (0.5–4.5 V for about 0–70 psi).",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corrosion or water ingress"]],["Check engine light on","Starting problems","Lack of power"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P018E":["Fuel Pressure Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Fuel pressure sensor B is a second fuel pressure sensor; which one is up to the manufacturer. On GM it is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line of direct-injection engines (0–743 kPa), read by the engine module or the fuel pump control module; on Ford engines with dual injection it is the sensor on the port-injection rail (0.5–4.5 V for about 0–70 psi).",[["h","Loose contact or connector corrosion"],["m","Chafed harness"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","CAN transmission from the pump module disturbed"]],["Check engine light on (often intermittent)","Brief stumble or hesitation","Occasional starting problems"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P018F":["Fuel System Over Pressure Relief Valve Frequent Activation","Common-rail diesels have a pressure relief valve in the rail – usually a spring-loaded valve, on some truck engines electronically controlled. It opens on overpressure and then holds a low limp-home pressure; every opening wears the seat, and after many openings it may no longer close tightly.",[["h","Volume control valve sticking – repeated overpressure"],["m","Rail pressure sensor reading wrong (controller targets too high)"],["m","Relief valve fatigued, opening too early"],["l","Return blocked or pressure control valve faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Sudden loss of power (limp mode with low rail pressure)","Starting problems once the valve no longer seals"],["hard",[150,2000]],"c"],"P0190":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor A Circuit","The rail pressure sensor A measures the pressure in the high-pressure fuel rail of common-rail diesels and petrol direct-injection engines – a three-wire sensor with 5 V reference. P0190 is the general circuit code used when a manufacturer does not split low and high; GM and Ford use P0192/P0193 instead.",[["h","Connector corroded or water ingress at the rail pressure sensor"],["m","Rail pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Harness damaged (heat, chafing)"],["l","5 V reference disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode, loss of power","Stalling or no start (sometimes only after a waiting time)"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0191":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The rail pressure sensor A measures the pressure in the high-pressure fuel rail of common-rail diesels and petrol direct-injection engines; it is either a three-wire sensor (5 V reference, ground, signal rising with pressure, about 0.5 V at 0 bar) or a digital SENT sensor with two measuring channels. The engine module uses it as the feedback signal for rail pressure control.",[["h","Rail pressure sensor drifting or stuck (wrong offset at 0 bar)"],["m","Genuine pressure fault: high-pressure pump, pressure or volume control valve, tank pump"],["m","Connector corroded, contact resistance in signal or ground"],["l","Fuel shortage or clogged filter"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or 'Reduced Engine Power'","Hard starting, stalling"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0192":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor A Circuit Low","The rail pressure sensor A measures the pressure in the high-pressure fuel rail of common-rail diesels and petrol direct-injection engines; it is either a three-wire sensor (5 V reference, ground, signal rising with pressure, about 0.5 V at 0 bar) or a digital SENT sensor with two measuring channels. The engine module uses it as the feedback signal for rail pressure control.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground, harness chafed"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted"],["m","Connector corroded or loose"],["m","Rail pressure sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or 'Reduced Engine Power'","Hard starting, stalling"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0193":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor A Circuit High","The rail pressure sensor A measures the pressure in the high-pressure fuel rail of common-rail diesels and petrol direct-injection engines; it is either a three-wire sensor (5 V reference, ground, signal rising with pressure, about 0.5 V at 0 bar) or a digital SENT sensor with two measuring channels. The engine module uses it as the feedback signal for rail pressure control.",[["h","Sensor ground open or high-resistance"],["m","Signal wire open (with pull-up) or shorted to voltage"],["m","Connector corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or 'Reduced Engine Power'","Hard starting, stalling"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0194":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The rail pressure sensor A measures the pressure in the high-pressure fuel rail of common-rail diesels and petrol direct-injection engines; it is either a three-wire sensor (5 V reference, ground, signal rising with pressure, about 0.5 V at 0 bar) or a digital SENT sensor with two measuring channels. The engine module uses it as the feedback signal for rail pressure control.",[["h","Corrosion or wear at sensor and harness contacts"],["m","Harness chafed or loose contact"],["m","Rail pressure sensor faulty internally"],["l","Ground problem or control module"]],["Check engine light on (not on GM 2016 diesels)","Brief stumbling or misfire-like dropouts","Occasional limp mode, fault often not reproducible"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0195":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The engine oil temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Connector or harness damaged, corroded or oil-soaked"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (electronics fault on SENT sensors)"],["m","5 V reference or ground problem in the sensor circuit"],["l","Control module or software"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Oil temperature display missing or fixed","Restricted cam timing, slightly reduced power"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0196":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Range/Performance","The engine oil temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Oil temperature sensor drifting or stuck (offset)"],["m","Comparison sensor (coolant, intake air) or its wiring wrong"],["m","Thermal management or coolant control valve, or thermostat faulty (oil genuinely too hot or cold)"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drives)","Implausible oil temperature reading","Possibly a cam timing code alongside"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P0197":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The engine oil temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Signal wire chafed against ground or the engine block"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Oil or water in the connector (leakage current)"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Oil temperature display at maximum or oil temperature warning","Fan running constantly or power reduction possible"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0198":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","The engine oil temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Connector unplugged or pins corroded, signal or ground wire broken"],["m","Sensor element open"],["l","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["l","Control module pull-up or input faulty"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Oil temperature display stays at minimum or blank","Restricted cam timing, possibly slightly less power"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0199":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The engine oil temperature sensor A is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Loose contact or corroded pins at the sensor connector"],["m","Harness chafing intermittently (engine movement)"],["m","Sensor with internal hairline crack"],["l","Control module ground connection"]],["Check engine light or only a stored code","Oil temperature reading jumps","Occasionally restricted cam timing"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P019A":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Rich to Lean Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 1 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick; it exists on Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (on both banks) and on Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late after a mixture change – unlike 'slow response' (P0145/P0165), where the transition itself is too flat.",[["h","Sensor 3 aged, sooted or oil-poisoned"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of sensor 3"],["l","Converter with strongly differing oxygen storage (aftermarket or new)"],["l","Sensor 3 heater weak"]],["Check engine light on","No noticeable change in driving","Catalyst readiness not set (emissions test)"],["easy",[60,250]],"u"],"P019B":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Lean to Rich Bank 1 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 1 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick; it exists on Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (on both banks) and on Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late after a mixture change – unlike 'slow response' (P0145/P0165), where the transition itself is too flat.",[["h","Sensor 3 aged, sooted or oil-poisoned"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of sensor 3"],["l","Converter with strongly differing oxygen storage (aftermarket or new)"],["l","Sensor 3 heater weak"]],["Check engine light on","No noticeable change in driving","Catalyst readiness not set (emissions test)"],["easy",[60,250]],"u"],"P019C":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Rich to Lean Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick; it exists on Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (on both banks) and on Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late after a mixture change – unlike 'slow response' (P0145/P0165), where the transition itself is too flat.",[["h","Sensor 3 aged, sooted or oil-poisoned"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of sensor 3"],["l","Converter with strongly differing oxygen storage (aftermarket or new)"],["l","Sensor 3 heater weak"]],["Check engine light on","No noticeable change in driving","Catalyst readiness not set (emissions test)"],["easy",[60,250]],"u"],"P019D":["O2 Sensor Delayed Response - Lean to Rich Bank 2 Sensor 3","Sensor 3 on bank 2 is a switching oxygen sensor behind a second converter or converter brick; it exists on Jaguar Land Rover's supercharged V6 and V8 (on both banks) and on Ford systems that monitor part of the converter volume. 'Delayed response' means the sensor starts reacting too late after a mixture change – unlike 'slow response' (P0145/P0165), where the transition itself is too flat.",[["h","Sensor 3 aged, sooted or oil-poisoned"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of sensor 3"],["l","Converter with strongly differing oxygen storage (aftermarket or new)"],["l","Sensor 3 heater weak"]],["Check engine light on","No noticeable change in driving","Catalyst readiness not set (emissions test)"],["easy",[60,250]],"u"],"P019E":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Pressure Sensor Circuit","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Connector or harness at the tank pressure sensor (at the regulator) corroded, loose or chafed"],["m","Tank pressure sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine or alternative fuel control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level gauge wrong, jumping or showing empty","Gas operation blocked or automatic switch to petrol"],["hard",[120,600]],"u"],"P019F":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Pressure Sensor Performance","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Tank pressure sensor drifting or stuck (contamination, oil residue from the gas)"],["m","Tank shut-off valve not opening or manual tank valve closed"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["l","Tank temperature sensor wrong (pressure/temperature check)"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level gauge wrong, jumping or showing empty","Gas operation blocked or automatic switch to petrol"],["hard",[150,700]],"u"],"P01A0":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or open"],["m","5 V reference missing (open, or short in the shared 5 V circuit)"],["m","Tank pressure sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector at the regulator corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level gauge wrong, jumping or showing empty","Gas operation blocked or automatic switch to petrol"],["hard",[120,600]],"u"],"P01A1":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Pressure Sensor Circuit High","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Sensor ground wire open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V reference or battery voltage"],["m","Tank pressure sensor faulty internally"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level gauge wrong, jumping or showing empty","Gas operation blocked or automatic switch to petrol"],["hard",[120,600]],"u"],"P01A2":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector, wire broken by vibration"],["m","Harness chafed along the gas line or underbody"],["m","Sensor unstable internally"],["l","Poor ground point or interference"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level gauge wrong, jumping or showing empty","Gas operation blocked or automatic switch to petrol"],["hard",[120,600]],"u"],"P01A3":["Alternative Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Connector or wiring at the rail pressure sensor damaged by heat or corroded"],["m","Gas rail pressure sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Switch to petrol or gas operation not selectable","Hesitation, loss of power or mixture faults in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01A4":["Alternative Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Performance","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Rail pressure sensor drifting (oil contamination from the gas)"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["m","Regulator or shut-off valve not behaving as expected"],["l","Tank pressure sensor wrong as comparison value"]],["Check engine light on","Switch to petrol or gas operation not selectable","Hesitation, loss of power or mixture faults in gas mode"],["medium",[120,500]],"u"],"P01A5":["Alternative Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or open"],["m","5 V reference missing"],["m","Gas rail pressure sensor faulty"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Switch to petrol or gas operation not selectable","Hesitation, loss of power or mixture faults in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01A6":["Alternative Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit High","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Sensor ground wire open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Gas rail pressure sensor faulty"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Switch to petrol or gas operation not selectable","Hesitation, loss of power or mixture faults in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01A7":["Alternative Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector, wire broken by engine movement"],["m","Sensor unstable internally"],["l","Ground or reference problem"]],["Check engine light on","Switch to petrol or gas operation not selectable","Hesitation, loss of power or mixture faults in gas mode"],["medium",[80,450]],"u"],"P01A8":["Alternative Fuel Rail/System Pressure - Too Low","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Gas supply nearly empty, tank pressure close to the regulator minimum"],["m","High- or low-pressure shut-off valve not opening fully (coil, control, manual valve closed)"],["m","High-pressure filter or low-pressure filter/oil separator clogged"],["m","Pressure regulator faulty, sticking or iced (coolant heating disturbed)"]],["Check engine light on","Automatic switch to petrol or gas operation not possible","Loss of power or hesitation under load in gas mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P01A9":["Alternative Fuel Rail/System Pressure - Too High","These codes only apply to vehicles that run on a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – either as bi-fuel models alongside petrol (for example GM's 2015–2016 Impala Bi-Fuel or 2013–2016 Silverado/Sierra 2500HD) or as dedicated gas engines. CNG is stored at up to about 250 bar (3600 psi); a regulator reduces it to a few bar for the gas rail and injectors.",[["h","Regulator sticking or valve seat leaking (pressure creeps onto the low-pressure side)"],["m","Control of the electronically regulated regulator disturbed (PWM circuit, connector)"],["m","Rail pressure sensor reading plausible but too high (drift)"],["l","Contamination or oil in the regulator"]],["Check engine light on","Gas operation aborted or not possible, vehicle runs on petrol","Rough running or rich codes (P0172/P0175) in gas mode"],["hard",[300,1500]],"u"],"P01AA":["Alternative Fuel Tank Temperature Sensor Circuit","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The tank temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor: with CNG there is no float, so the gas level is calculated from tank pressure and tank temperature (GM: full at about 3600 psi at 21 °C, empty at about 400–500 psi).",[["h","Connector or wiring of the tank temperature sensor open, corroded or chafed"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Gas module, CAN link or engine module"]],["Stored fault code (warning light depending on system)","Gas level display wrong","Gas operation blocked, switch to petrol"],["hard",[100,3000]],"u"],"P01AB":["Alternative Fuel Tank Temperature Sensor Range/Performance","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The tank temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor: with CNG there is no float, so the gas level is calculated from tank pressure and tank temperature (GM: full at about 3600 psi at 21 °C, empty at about 400–500 psi).",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["l","Comparison sensor (tank pressure, ambient) wrong"]],["Stored fault code (warning light depending on system)","Gas level display wrong","Gas operation blocked, switch to petrol"],["hard",[100,3000]],"u"],"P01AC":["Alternative Fuel Tank Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The tank temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor: with CNG there is no float, so the gas level is calculated from tank pressure and tank temperature (GM: full at about 3600 psi at 21 °C, empty at about 400–500 psi).",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Gas or engine module"]],["Stored fault code (warning light depending on system)","Gas level display wrong","Gas operation blocked, switch to petrol"],["hard",[100,3000]],"u"],"P01AD":["Alternative Fuel Tank Temperature Sensor Circuit High","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The tank temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor: with CNG there is no float, so the gas level is calculated from tank pressure and tank temperature (GM: full at about 3600 psi at 21 °C, empty at about 400–500 psi).",[["h","Open circuit, connector unplugged"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Short to voltage"],["l","Gas or engine module"]],["Stored fault code (warning light depending on system)","Gas level display wrong","Gas operation blocked, switch to petrol"],["hard",[100,3000]],"u"],"P01AE":["Alternative Fuel Tank Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The tank temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor: with CNG there is no float, so the gas level is calculated from tank pressure and tank temperature (GM: full at about 3600 psi at 21 °C, empty at about 400–500 psi).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Harness chafed along the gas line or underbody"],["m","Sensor unstable internally"]],["Stored fault code (warning light depending on system)","Gas level display wrong","Gas operation blocked, switch to petrol"],["hard",[100,3000]],"u"],"P01AF":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Level Sensor Circuit","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. Tank level sensor A only exists where there is a real sender – typically LPG tanks with a float whose position is transmitted magnetically through the tank wall (Roush propane systems: 0.1–4.9 V).",[["h","Connector or wiring of the level sensor open, corroded or chafed"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Gas module, CAN link or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level display wrong, jumping or empty","Range or refuelling information unreliable"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01B0":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Level Sensor Range/Performance","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. Tank level sensor A only exists where there is a real sender – typically LPG tanks with a float whose position is transmitted magnetically through the tank wall (Roush propane systems: 0.1–4.9 V).",[["h","Float or sender mechanism sticking"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["m","Level sender faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level display wrong, jumping or empty","Range or refuelling information unreliable"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01B1":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Level Sensor Circuit Low","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. Tank level sensor A only exists where there is a real sender – typically LPG tanks with a float whose position is transmitted magnetically through the tank wall (Roush propane systems: 0.1–4.9 V).",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Gas or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level display wrong, jumping or empty","Range or refuelling information unreliable"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01B2":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Level Sensor Circuit High","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. Tank level sensor A only exists where there is a real sender – typically LPG tanks with a float whose position is transmitted magnetically through the tank wall (Roush propane systems: 0.1–4.9 V).",[["h","Open circuit, connector unplugged"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Short to voltage"],["l","Gas or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level display wrong, jumping or empty","Range or refuelling information unreliable"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01B3":["Alternative Fuel Tank A Level Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. Tank level sensor A only exists where there is a real sender – typically LPG tanks with a float whose position is transmitted magnetically through the tank wall (Roush propane systems: 0.1–4.9 V).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Harness chafed along the gas line or underbody"],["m","Sensor unstable internally"]],["Check engine light on","Gas level display wrong, jumping or empty","Range or refuelling information unreliable"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01B4":["Alternative Fuel Rail Temperature Sensor Circuit","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The gas rail temperature sensor, often combined with the rail pressure sensor, measures the gas temperature at the injectors: gas cools strongly when it expands in the regulator, and the injected mass depends on density, i.e.",[["h","Connector or wiring of the rail temperature sensor open, corroded or chafed"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Gas module, CAN link or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Gas operation blocked or switch to petrol","Mixture errors or stumbling in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01B5":["Alternative Fuel Rail Temperature Sensor Performance","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The gas rail temperature sensor, often combined with the rail pressure sensor, measures the gas temperature at the injectors: gas cools strongly when it expands in the regulator, and the injected mass depends on density, i.e.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","Regulator coolant heating faulty (gas genuinely too cold)"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Gas operation blocked or switch to petrol","Mixture errors or stumbling in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01B6":["Alternative Fuel Rail Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The gas rail temperature sensor, often combined with the rail pressure sensor, measures the gas temperature at the injectors: gas cools strongly when it expands in the regulator, and the injected mass depends on density, i.e.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Gas or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Gas operation blocked or switch to petrol","Mixture errors or stumbling in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01B7":["Alternative Fuel Rail Temperature Sensor Circuit High","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The gas rail temperature sensor, often combined with the rail pressure sensor, measures the gas temperature at the injectors: gas cools strongly when it expands in the regulator, and the injected mass depends on density, i.e.",[["h","Open circuit, connector unplugged"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Short to voltage"],["l","Gas or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Gas operation blocked or switch to petrol","Mixture errors or stumbling in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01B8":["Alternative Fuel Rail Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","These codes only apply to vehicles with a gaseous alternative fuel – compressed natural gas (CNG) or LPG – as bi-fuel models alongside petrol or as dedicated gas engines; on bi-fuel vehicles a fault usually blocks gas operation and the vehicle runs on petrol. The gas rail temperature sensor, often combined with the rail pressure sensor, measures the gas temperature at the injectors: gas cools strongly when it expands in the regulator, and the injected mass depends on density, i.e.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Harness chafed along the gas line or underbody"],["m","Sensor unstable internally"]],["Check engine light on","Gas operation blocked or switch to petrol","Mixture errors or stumbling in gas mode"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P01B9":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor B Circuit","The engine oil temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Wiring or connector of sensor B"],["m","Sensor B faulty"],["l","Reference, ground or control module"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Oil temperature reading possibly wrong","Usually no driving symptoms"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P01BA":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor B Range/Performance","The engine oil temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Sensor B drifting or stuck"],["m","Comparison sensor (oil sensor A, coolant) faulty"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["l","Thermal management or oil cooler fault (genuine deviation)"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Implausible oil temperature values in scan data","Usually no driving symptoms"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P01BB":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","The engine oil temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor B shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor B shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or oil in the connector"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Oil temperature warning or maximum reading possible","Fan possibly running constantly"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P01BC":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","The engine oil temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Connector or wire of sensor B open"],["m","Sensor B open"],["l","Short to voltage"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Oil temperature shows minimum","Usually no driving symptoms"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P01BD":["Engine Oil Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The engine oil temperature sensor B is an NTC thermistor fed with 5 V through a resistor: the hotter the oil, the lower the signal voltage. The engine module uses the oil temperature for variable cam timing (Ford disables it with cold oil or a sensor fault), engine protection and the oil life monitor; many engines have no such sensor and use a calculated oil temperature instead.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor B connector"],["m","Harness chafing intermittently"],["m","Sensor B with internal hairline crack"]],["Check engine light or only a stored code","Jumping oil temperature values","Usually no driving symptoms"],["medium",[30,250]],"u"],"P01BE":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit Bank 2","The bank 2 rail pressure sensor measures the pressure in a second, separate high-pressure rail on V engines that have one rail with its own sensor per bank. Many V engines connect both rails and use only one sensor (P0190–P0194); the only documented user of the bank 2 codes is GM's 5.5 litre LT6 (Corvette Z06 2023–2024) with a digital sensor per rail.",[["h","Connector or harness of the bank 2 sensor corroded or wet"],["m","Bank 2 rail pressure sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or 'Reduced Engine Power'","Rough running"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P01BF":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2","The bank 2 rail pressure sensor measures the pressure in a second, separate high-pressure rail on V engines that have one rail with its own sensor per bank. Many V engines connect both rails and use only one sensor (P0190–P0194); the only documented user of the bank 2 codes is GM's 5.5 litre LT6 (Corvette Z06 2023–2024) with a digital sensor per rail.",[["h","Water or corrosion at the bank 2 intermediate connector (GM LT6: wrong 12-pin connector)"],["m","Bank 2 rail pressure sensor faulty internally"],["l","Genuine pressure fault on bank 2 (pump, control valve)"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power","Starting problems (with P00C6)"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P01C0":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit Low Bank 2","The bank 2 rail pressure sensor measures the pressure in a second, separate high-pressure rail on V engines that have one rail with its own sensor per bank. Many V engines connect both rails and use only one sensor (P0190–P0194); the only documented user of the bank 2 codes is GM's 5.5 litre LT6 (Corvette Z06 2023–2024) with a digital sensor per rail.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground, harness chafed"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted"],["m","Connector corroded or loose"],["m","Rail pressure sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or 'Reduced Engine Power'","Hard starting, stalling"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P01C1":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit High Bank 2","The bank 2 rail pressure sensor measures the pressure in a second, separate high-pressure rail on V engines that have one rail with its own sensor per bank. Many V engines connect both rails and use only one sensor (P0190–P0194); the only documented user of the bank 2 codes is GM's 5.5 litre LT6 (Corvette Z06 2023–2024) with a digital sensor per rail.",[["h","Sensor ground open or high-resistance"],["m","Signal wire open (with pull-up) or shorted to voltage"],["m","Connector corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or 'Reduced Engine Power'","Hard starting, stalling"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P01C2":["Fuel Rail Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic Bank 2","The bank 2 rail pressure sensor measures the pressure in a second, separate high-pressure rail on V engines that have one rail with its own sensor per bank. Many V engines connect both rails and use only one sensor (P0190–P0194); the only documented user of the bank 2 codes is GM's 5.5 litre LT6 (Corvette Z06 2023–2024) with a digital sensor per rail.",[["h","Corrosion or wear at sensor and harness contacts"],["m","Harness chafed or loose contact"],["m","Rail pressure sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer)","Brief stumbling","Often no symptoms"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P01C3":["Fuel Pressure Sensor A Circuit","Fuel pressure sensor A is a fuel pressure sensor without 'rail' in its name and without a bank – unlike the rail pressure sensors P0190–P0194 and P01BE–P01C2; which sensor it is depends on the manufacturer. The only documented case is FCA, where sensor A is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line, read together with the fuel pump control module.",[["h","Connector corroded or wet, wire open or shorted"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Fuel pump control module or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling possible","Hesitation or lack of power under load"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P01C4":["Fuel Pressure Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","Fuel pressure sensor A is a fuel pressure sensor without 'rail' in its name and without a bank – unlike the rail pressure sensors P0190–P0194 and P01BE–P01C2; which sensor it is depends on the manufacturer. The only documented case is FCA, where sensor A is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line, read together with the fuel pump control module.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","Genuine supply fault: weak pump, clogged filter"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or ground"],["l","Fuel pump control module"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling possible","Hesitation or lack of power under load"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P01C5":["Fuel Pressure Sensor A Circuit Low","Fuel pressure sensor A is a fuel pressure sensor without 'rail' in its name and without a bank – unlike the rail pressure sensors P0190–P0194 and P01BE–P01C2; which sensor it is depends on the manufacturer. The only documented case is FCA, where sensor A is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line, read together with the fuel pump control module.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or open"],["m","5 V reference missing"],["m","Connector corroded or wet"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling possible","Hesitation or lack of power under load"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P01C6":["Fuel Pressure Sensor A Circuit High","Fuel pressure sensor A is a fuel pressure sensor without 'rail' in its name and without a bank – unlike the rail pressure sensors P0190–P0194 and P01BE–P01C2; which sensor it is depends on the manufacturer. The only documented case is FCA, where sensor A is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line, read together with the fuel pump control module.",[["h","Corrosion, water or pin damage at sensor, pump module or ground point"],["m","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stalling possible","Hesitation or lack of power under load"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P01C7":["Fuel Pressure Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Fuel pressure sensor A is a fuel pressure sensor without 'rail' in its name and without a bank – unlike the rail pressure sensors P0190–P0194 and P01BE–P01C2; which sensor it is depends on the manufacturer. The only documented case is FCA, where sensor A is the low-pressure sensor in the supply line, read together with the fuel pump control module.",[["h","Loose contact, water or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Harness chafed"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on (often intermittent)","Brief stumbling","Occasional starting problems"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P01C8":["Fuel Rail/System Pressure - Too Low Bank 2","Some V engines have a separate high-pressure fuel circuit per bank, each with its own pump or rail – for example Audi's 4.0 TFSI V8 with a second high-pressure pump for bank 2. These codes are the bank 2 counterparts of the system pressure codes for bank 1.",[["h","High-pressure pump of bank 2 weak or worn"],["m","Volume control valve faulty or its control disturbed"],["m","Supply pressure too low (lift pump, filter)"],["l","Leak in the bank 2 high-pressure circuit"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode","Hard starting, misfires on bank 2"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P01C9":["Fuel Rail/System Pressure - Too High Bank 2","Some V engines have a separate high-pressure fuel circuit per bank, each with its own pump or rail – for example Audi's 4.0 TFSI V8 with a second high-pressure pump for bank 2. These codes are the bank 2 counterparts of the system pressure codes for bank 1.",[["h","Volume control valve not controlled properly (e.g. at low supply voltage), software"],["m","Volume control valve sticking"],["m","Bank 2 rail pressure sensor reading too high"],["l","Pressure relief path blocked"]],["Check engine light on (possibly intermittent)","Noise from the bank 2 high-pressure pump","Slightly reduced power possible"],["medium",[0,1200]],"u"],"P01CA":["Fuel Rail Pressure Too Low - Engine Cranking Bank 2","Some V engines have a separate high-pressure fuel circuit per bank, each with its own pump or rail – for example Audi's 4.0 TFSI V8 with a second high-pressure pump for bank 2. These codes are the bank 2 counterparts of the system pressure codes for bank 1.",[["h","High-pressure pump of bank 2 or its drive weak"],["m","Low supply pressure at start (lift pump, check valve)"],["m","Leak or leaking injector on bank 2"],["l","Bank 2 rail pressure sensor or connector"]],["Check engine light on","Long cranking or no start","Rough running right after start"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P01CB":["Cylinder 1 Injection Timing Performance - Over Retarded","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn, coked or sticking (opens late, delivers too little)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01CC":["Cylinder 1 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01CD":["Cylinder 2 Injection Timing Performance - Over Retarded","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn, coked or sticking (opens late, delivers too little)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01CE":["Cylinder 2 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01CF":["Cylinder 3 Injection Timing Performance - Over Retarded","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn, coked or sticking (opens late, delivers too little)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01D0":["Cylinder 3 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost lea