","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01DA":["Cylinder 8 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01DB":["Cylinder 9 Injection Timing Performance - Over Retarded","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn, coked or sticking (opens late, delivers too little)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01DC":["Cylinder 9 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01DD":["Cylinder 10 Injection Timing Performance - Over Retarded","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn, coked or sticking (opens late, delivers too little)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01DE":["Cylinder 10 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01DF":["Cylinder 11 Injection Timing Performance - Over Retarded","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn, coked or sticking (opens late, delivers too little)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01E0":["Cylinder 11 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01E1":["Cylinder 12 Injection Timing Performance - Over Retarded","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn, coked or sticking (opens late, delivers too little)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01E2":["Cylinder 12 Injection Timing Performance - Over Advanced","Diesel engines must detect when fuel can no longer be injected at the correct crank angle – CARB requires this for every injector individually, hence one code per cylinder. The start of injection is not measured directly: during overrun with fuel cut-off the control module fires a tiny test injection into one cylinder (GM: about 1.5 mm³) and evaluates the resulting speed change at the crankshaft sensor.",[["h","Injector worn or leaking (delivers too much in small quantities)"],["m","False setting from software or vehicle vibration (e.g. off-road tyres)"],["m","Wrong injector correction code stored after an injector replacement"],["l","Boost leak or loose injector clamp"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Louder combustion noise or knocking","Rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"u"],"P01E3":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit","Coolant temperature sensor 3 is a third NTC coolant sensor on engines with thermal management or split cooling circuits; its position is defined by the manufacturer – on Jaguar Land Rover's 2018–2019 Ingenium petrol engines it sits in the engine block water jacket, on Audi's 3.0 TFSI V6 it is part of the thermal management. As with every NTC sensor, a low signal voltage means hot and a high one cold.",[["h","Open circuit or loose contact at connector or wiring of sensor 3"],["h","Sensor open or shorted"],["m","Short to ground or voltage in the harness"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on","Coolant temperature or cooling fan behaviour possibly affected","Often no change in driving"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P01E4":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Range/Performance","Coolant temperature sensor 3 is a third NTC coolant sensor on engines with thermal management or split cooling circuits; its position is defined by the manufacturer – on Jaguar Land Rover's 2018–2019 Ingenium petrol engines it sits in the engine block water jacket, on Audi's 3.0 TFSI V6 it is part of the thermal management. As with every NTC sensor, a low signal voltage means hot and a high one cold.",[["h","Thermostat not closing fully"],["m","Sensor 3 drifting"],["m","Low coolant or air at the sensor"],["l","Contact resistance at the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Engine warms up slowly, heater output weak","Often together with cooling system codes"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01E5":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Low","Coolant temperature sensor 3 is a third NTC coolant sensor on engines with thermal management or split cooling circuits; its position is defined by the manufacturer – on Jaguar Land Rover's 2018–2019 Ingenium petrol engines it sits in the engine block water jacket, on Audi's 3.0 TFSI V6 it is part of the thermal management. As with every NTC sensor, a low signal voltage means hot and a high one cold.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor 3 shorted to ground (chafing, water in connector)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Coolant in the connector, corrosion with leakage current"],["l","Control module input stage faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Coolant temperature or cooling fan behaviour possibly affected","Often no change in driving"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P01E6":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit High","Coolant temperature sensor 3 is a third NTC coolant sensor on engines with thermal management or split cooling circuits; its position is defined by the manufacturer – on Jaguar Land Rover's 2018–2019 Ingenium petrol engines it sits in the engine block water jacket, on Audi's 3.0 TFSI V6 it is part of the thermal management. As with every NTC sensor, a low signal voltage means hot and a high one cold.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector loose, poor terminal tension"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or 12 V"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on","Coolant temperature or cooling fan behaviour possibly affected","Often no change in driving"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P01E7":["Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor 3 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Coolant temperature sensor 3 is a third NTC coolant sensor on engines with thermal management or split cooling circuits; its position is defined by the manufacturer – on Jaguar Land Rover's 2018–2019 Ingenium petrol engines it sits in the engine block water jacket, on Audi's 3.0 TFSI V6 it is part of the thermal management. As with every NTC sensor, a low signal voltage means hot and a high one cold.",[["h","Loose contact at connector or pins"],["h","Wire break in the harness (movement, vibration)"],["m","Sensor opening internally with temperature changes"],["m","Corrosion or moisture in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Jumping coolant readings","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P01E8":["Engine Coolant Heater A Control Circuit/Open","Engine coolant heater A is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Control wire or connector to the heater or relay open"],["h","Heater element burnt out or relay coil open"],["m","Fuse in the load circuit blown"],["l","Control module output stage faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01E9":["Engine Coolant Heater A Control Circuit Low","Engine coolant heater A is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground (chafed harness)"],["m","Heater element or relay coil shorted to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Control module output stage"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01EA":["Engine Coolant Heater A Control Circuit High","Engine coolant heater A is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Relay coil or heater with too low resistance (overcurrent)"],["m","Harness damage touching a positive wire"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01EB":["Engine Coolant Heater A Control Circuit Performance","Engine coolant heater A is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Relay sticking or not switching (burnt contacts)"],["m","Heater element aged or scaled, output too low"],["m","Voltage drop in the load circuit or switched off by power management"],["l","Low coolant or air at the heater"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01EC":["Engine Coolant Heater B Control Circuit/Open","Engine coolant heater B is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Control wire or connector to the heater or relay open"],["h","Heater element burnt out or relay coil open"],["m","Fuse in the load circuit blown"],["l","Control module output stage faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01ED":["Engine Coolant Heater B Control Circuit Low","Engine coolant heater B is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground (chafed harness)"],["m","Heater element or relay coil shorted to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Control module output stage"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01EE":["Engine Coolant Heater B Control Circuit High","Engine coolant heater B is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Relay coil or heater with too low resistance (overcurrent)"],["m","Harness damage touching a positive wire"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01EF":["Engine Coolant Heater B Control Circuit Performance","Engine coolant heater B is an electric coolant heater switched by the engine module, directly or via a relay or power stage, to shorten warm-up – less cold-start emissions and faster cabin heat; A and B denote two separately switched heaters or stages. It is not the same as a mains-powered block heater (which the vehicle does not control) or the electrically heated thermostat (P0597–P0599).",[["h","Relay sticking or not switching (burnt contacts)"],["m","Heater element aged or scaled, output too low"],["m","Voltage drop in the load circuit or switched off by power management"],["l","Low coolant or air at the heater"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up, cabin heat slower in winter","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P01F0":["Coolant Temperature Relapsed Below Diagnostic Monitoring Temperature","Since model years 2019–2021 CARB requires petrol engines to detect a thermostat fault not only during warm-up but also when the coolant, after reaching operating temperature, drops back below the temperature needed for other diagnostics. P01F0 is this check.",[["h","Thermostat stuck open or not sealing"],["m","Heated map thermostat, thermal management valve or switchable water pump faulty"],["m","Radiator fan running constantly (relay stuck)"],["m","Coolant sensor drifting cold"]],["Check engine light on (after two drive cycles)","Temperature gauge drops while driving","Weak cabin heating, higher consumption"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P01F1":["O2 Sensor Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 4","Sensor 4 on bank 1 is a fourth oxygen sensor, behind a third converter or converter brick. The OBD standard provides for up to four sensors per bank, and the code standard defines sensor 4 for bank 1 only.",[["h","Sensor 4 aged or poisoned"],["m","Wiring or connector open, high-resistance or corroded"],["m","Sensor heater failed"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness possibly not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P01F2":["O2 Sensor Circuit Low Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 4","Sensor 4 on bank 1 is a fourth oxygen sensor, behind a third converter or converter brick. The OBD standard provides for up to four sensors per bank, and the code standard defines sensor 4 for bank 1 only.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness possibly not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P01F3":["O2 Sensor Circuit High Voltage Bank 1 Sensor 4","Sensor 4 on bank 1 is a fourth oxygen sensor, behind a third converter or converter brick. The OBD standard provides for up to four sensors per bank, and the code standard defines sensor 4 for bank 1 only.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to heater supply or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Sensor ground open (bias logic)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness possibly not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P01F4":["O2 Sensor Circuit Slow Response Bank 1 Sensor 4","Sensor 4 on bank 1 is a fourth oxygen sensor, behind a third converter or converter brick. The OBD standard provides for up to four sensors per bank, and the code standard defines sensor 4 for bank 1 only.",[["h","Sensor aged or contaminated"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"],["m","Sensor heater weak"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness possibly not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P01F5":["O2 Sensor Circuit No Activity Detected Bank 1 Sensor 4","Sensor 4 on bank 1 is a fourth oxygen sensor, downstream of a further converter. No manufacturer or vehicle using sensor 4 codes could be identified (GM and Ford documents stop at sensor 3); the description follows CARB's requirements for downstream sensors and GM's checks on sensor 2.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector corroded"],["m","Sensor aged or poisoned, produces no voltage"],["m","Sensor heater faulty, sensor does not reach operating temperature"],["l","Exhaust leak upstream of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness possibly not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P01F6":["O2 Sensor Signal Biased/Stuck Lean Bank 1 Sensor 4","Sensor 4 on bank 1 is a fourth oxygen sensor, downstream of a further converter. No manufacturer or vehicle using sensor 4 codes could be identified (GM and Ford documents stop at sensor 3); the description follows CARB's requirements for downstream sensors and GM's checks on sensor 2.",[["h","Sensor aged, characteristic shifted lean"],["m","Exhaust leak or false air upstream of the sensor"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground or contact resistance"],["l","Misfires or mixture fault (excess oxygen)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness possibly not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P01F7":["O2 Sensor Signal Biased/Stuck Rich Bank 1 Sensor 4","Sensor 4 on bank 1 is a fourth oxygen sensor, downstream of a further converter. No manufacturer or vehicle using sensor 4 codes could be identified (GM and Ford documents stop at sensor 3); the description follows CARB's requirements for downstream sensors and GM's checks on sensor 2.",[["h","Sensor sooted, oil-fouled or aged"],["m","Signal wire shorted to voltage (e.g. heater supply)"],["l","Mixture genuinely rich (leaking injector, coolant or oil burning)"]],["Check engine light on (usually after two drive cycles)","Catalyst monitor readiness possibly not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[60,300]],"u"],"P01F8":["Engine Coolant Heater A Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","The engine module switches an electric coolant heater A through a power stage (driver). This code means the driver itself has reported excessive current or overheating and has shut down to protect itself – it concerns the control circuit, not the coolant temperature.",[["h","Short or partial short in heater element A"],["m","Wiring or connector chafed to ground, moisture in connector"],["l","Driver stage in the module or relay faulty or overheated"],["l","Heater running without coolant (low level, air pocket)"]],["Check engine light or warning message (manufacturer-dependent)","Slow warm-up, little heat in cold weather","Further heater codes possible (P01E9)"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P01F9":["Engine Coolant Heater B Control Circuit Driver Current/Temperature Too High","The engine module switches an electric coolant heater B – a second heater or heating stage – through a power stage (driver). This code means the driver itself has reported excessive current or overheating and has shut down to protect itself – it concerns the control circuit, not the coolant temperature.",[["h","Short or partial short in heater element B"],["m","Wiring or connector chafed to ground, moisture in connector"],["l","Driver stage in the module or relay faulty or overheated"]],["Check engine light or warning message (manufacturer-dependent)","Slow warm-up, little heat in cold weather"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P01FA":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor B Circuit","Turbocharger outlet pressure sensor B is a second pressure sensor at a compressor outlet – for example one per turbo on twin-turbo engines or a second measuring point; sensor A has the codes P00F8–P00FC. It is a separate family from the boost pressure sensor: GM and Ford report the pressure ahead of the throttle under the boost sensor codes P0236–P0238.",[["h","Connector or pins at sensor B loose or corroded"],["m","Harness damaged (5 V reference, signal, ground)"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module or 5 V supply disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, boost limited","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[50,250]],"u"],"P01FB":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor B Range/Performance","Turbocharger outlet pressure sensor B is a second pressure sensor at a compressor outlet – for example one per turbo on twin-turbo engines or a second measuring point; sensor A has the codes P00F8–P00FC. It is a separate family from the boost pressure sensor: GM and Ford report the pressure ahead of the throttle under the boost sensor codes P0236–P0238.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","Leaking charge air hose or blocked pressure port"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["l","Fault in other model inputs (MAF, MAP)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, boost limited","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P01FC":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor B Circuit Low","Turbocharger outlet pressure sensor B is a second pressure sensor at a compressor outlet – for example one per turbo on twin-turbo engines or a second measuring point; sensor A has the codes P00F8–P00FC. It is a separate family from the boost pressure sensor: GM and Ford report the pressure ahead of the throttle under the boost sensor codes P0236–P0238.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, boost limited","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[50,250]],"u"],"P01FD":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor B Circuit High","Turbocharger outlet pressure sensor B is a second pressure sensor at a compressor outlet – for example one per turbo on twin-turbo engines or a second measuring point; sensor A has the codes P00F8–P00FC. It is a separate family from the boost pressure sensor: GM and Ford report the pressure ahead of the throttle under the boost sensor codes P0236–P0238.",[["h","Sensor ground or signal wire open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, boost limited","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[50,250]],"u"],"P01FE":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic","Turbocharger outlet pressure sensor B is a second pressure sensor at a compressor outlet – for example one per turbo on twin-turbo engines or a second measuring point; sensor A has the codes P00F8–P00FC. It is a separate family from the boost pressure sensor: GM and Ford report the pressure ahead of the throttle under the boost sensor codes P0236–P0238.",[["h","Loose contact or corroded connector"],["m","Wire breaking from vibration or heat"],["l","Sensor erratic internally"]],["Check engine light on (often intermittent)","Occasional power dips","Jerking under boost"],["medium",[30,250]],"u"],"P01FF":["Turbocharger Outlet Pressure Sensor A/B Correlation","Turbocharger outlet pressure sensor B is a second pressure sensor at a compressor outlet – for example one per turbo on twin-turbo engines or a second measuring point; sensor A has the codes P00F8–P00FC. It is a separate family from the boost pressure sensor: GM and Ford report the pressure ahead of the throttle under the boost sensor codes P0236–P0238.",[["h","One sensor drifting"],["m","Leak or hose damage between the measuring points or on one bank"],["m","Contact resistance at one sensor's connector or ground"],["l","Uneven turbo output (twin-turbo, wastegate or vanes on one side)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, boost limited","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P0200":["Injector Circuit/Open","P0200 is the injector circuit fault without a cylinder assignment. The large US manufacturers do not use it – GM and Ford report injector faults per cylinder (P0201 ff) or as faults of the shared injector supply (P062D/P062E, P2146 ff).",[["h","Shared injector supply open (fuse, relay, harness connector)"],["m","Separate injector power stage or its wiring faulty (diesel)"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"],["l","Ground fault of the module or power stage"]],["Check engine light on","Engine does not start or shuts down","Severe misfire on several cylinders"],["medium",[50,900]],"u"],"P0201":["Cylinder 1 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0201 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 1. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 1 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0301","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0202":["Cylinder 2 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0202 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 2. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 2 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0302","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0203":["Cylinder 3 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0203 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 3. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 3 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0303","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0204":["Cylinder 4 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0204 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 4. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 4 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0304","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0205":["Cylinder 5 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0205 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 5. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 5 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0305","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0206":["Cylinder 6 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0206 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 6. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 6 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0306","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0207":["Cylinder 7 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0207 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 7. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 7 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0307","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0208":["Cylinder 8 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0208 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 8. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 8 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0308","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0209":["Cylinder 9 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0209 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 9. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 9 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0309","Loss of power, rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P020A":["Cylinder 1 Injection Timing","P020A reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 1 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01CB/P01CC ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 1 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P020B":["Cylinder 2 Injection Timing","P020B reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 2 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01CD/P01CE ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 2 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P020C":["Cylinder 3 Injection Timing","P020C reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 3 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01CF/P01D0 ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 3 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P020D":["Cylinder 4 Injection Timing","P020D reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 4 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01D1/P01D2 ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 4 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P020E":["Cylinder 5 Injection Timing","P020E reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 5 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01D3/P01D4 ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 5 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P020F":["Cylinder 6 Injection Timing","P020F reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 6 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01D5/P01D6 ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 6 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0210":["Cylinder 10 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0210 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 10. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 10 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0310","Loss of power, rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P0211":["Cylinder 11 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0211 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 11. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 11 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0311","Loss of power, rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P0212":["Cylinder 12 Injector A Circuit/Open","P0212 reports an electrical fault, mainly an open circuit, in the injector circuit of cylinder 12. The engine module watches each injector output: GM flags an open circuit at an impedance of 200 kΩ or more – on direct injection and diesel engines in 10 of 20 or 5 of 10 samples and with the warning light in the first trip, on some port-injected engines only after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the cylinder 12 injector loose, corroded or with a broken or pushed-back pin"],["m","Injector coil open"],["m","Wire between module and injector open or chafed (heat, rodents)"],["l","Injector supply missing (fuse, relay) – then usually several cylinders at once"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0312","Loss of power, rough idle"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P0213":["Cold Start Injector 1","A cold start injector is an additional injector in the intake manifold that sprays extra fuel for a few seconds while a cold engine is cranked. In classic Bosch systems a thermo-time switch on the block powers it directly, without the engine module – such systems cannot set a code at all.",[["h","Connector or wire corroded or open"],["m","Valve coil open or valve stuck"],["m","Thermo-time switch or control relay faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Poor cold start, long cranking in cold weather","Leaking valve: hot-start problems, flooding, black smoke"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P0214":["Cold Start Injector 2","A cold start injector is an additional injector in the intake manifold that sprays extra fuel for a few seconds while a cold engine is cranked. In classic Bosch systems a thermo-time switch on the block powers it directly, without the engine module – such systems cannot set a code at all.",[["h","Connector or wire corroded or open"],["m","Valve coil open or valve stuck"],["m","Thermo-time switch or control relay faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Poor cold start, long cranking in cold weather","Leaking valve: hot-start problems, flooding, black smoke"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P0215":["Engine Shutoff Solenoid","The engine shutoff solenoid is the electric stop valve on older diesel injection pumps, mainly distributor pumps. It is energised with the ignition on and lets fuel into the pump; with no current a spring closes it, the pump runs dry and the engine stops – also as a safety backup if the governor fails.",[["h","Shutoff solenoid faulty (open coil, sticking plunger)"],["h","Relay or fuse in the supply circuit faulty"],["m","Broken wire, corrosion or ground fault at the pump connector"],["l","Pump control module faulty"]],["Engine does not start or stalls while driving","Engine keeps running after ignition off (solenoid stuck open)","Check engine light on"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0216":["Injector/Injection Timing Control Circuit","P0216 is a diesel code for the control of injection timing. On 1990s distributor pumps with an electronic timing advance it meant that actual injection timing missed its target; today manufacturers use it for the electronic injector control, with different meanings.",[["m","Injector harness or connector (broken pin) – common rail"],["m","Engine module or injector power stage faulty internally"],["m","Distributor pump electronics or timing advance faulty"],["m","Low supply pressure on distributor pumps (lift pump, filter)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode","Rough running, jerking"],["hard",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0217":["Engine Coolant Over Temperature Condition","P0217 is a condition code: the engine module reports that coolant temperature has passed a protection threshold. The limit and the reaction depend on the manufacturer – Saab's 2.2 diesel sets it at 120 °C for over 10 s, limits fuel quantity and suspends EGR and boost diagnostics until the temperature drops; heavy-duty diesels cut power sharply and may shut down; Ford petrol engines use their own code P1299 and cut fuel to individual cylinders.",[["h","Low coolant (leak, head gasket)"],["h","Thermostat stuck closed"],["m","Radiator fan, fan clutch or water pump faulty, radiator clogged"],["m","Coolant temperature sensor or harness faulty (false reading)"]],["Temperature gauge in the red, overheat warning","Loss of power, limp mode, cylinders possibly shut off","Air conditioning switches off"],["medium",[30,900]],"c"],"P0218":["Transmission Fluid Temperature Sensor A Over Temperature Condition","P0218 is a condition code: the transmission or engine module reports that transmission fluid temperature sensor A reads above an overheat limit. Saab's Aisin automatic sets it above 170 °C for more than 10 s, or above 140 °C for two minutes at high speed, shows a 'transmission overheating' warning and shifts less smoothly; afterwards a fluid change is advised because the additives are spent.",[["h","High load (towing, hills, sustained full throttle, stop-and-go in heat)"],["m","Transmission fluid low or aged"],["m","Fluid cooler, thermostat or lines clogged, weak cooling system"],["l","Torque converter clutch slipping or internal damage"]],["Transmission overheating warning","Changed or harsh shifting","Limp mode or reduced power possible"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P0219":["Engine Overspeed Condition","P0219 is a condition code: engine speed has exceeded the overspeed limit even though the rev limiter already cuts fuel – so the engine was driven from outside, usually by downshifting at too high a road speed or descending in too low a gear, or the speed signal was wrong. Limits: Ford 6.7 litre diesel above 4,200 rpm, 3.0 litre above 4,800 rpm, after 0.1 s; GM/Saab 2.8 V6 petrol with manual gearbox above 7,000 rpm for 1 s; Saab 2.2 diesel above 6,200 rpm, although fuel is cut from 5,000 rpm.",[["h","Downshift at too high a road speed (manual gearbox)"],["m","Descending in too low a gear"],["l","Fault in the speed signal (crankshaft sensor, wiring)"],["l","Diesel runaway (oil drawn into the intake) or sticking injection"]],["Usually no lasting symptoms, only a stored code","Briefly very high revs, limiter intervening","Check engine light possible (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[0,150]],"c"],"P021A":["Cylinder 7 Injection Timing","P021A reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 7 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01D7/P01D8 ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 7 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P021B":["Cylinder 8 Injection Timing","P021B reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 8 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01D9/P01DA ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 8 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P021C":["Cylinder 9 Injection Timing","P021C reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 9 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01DB/P01DC ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 9 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["l","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P021D":["Cylinder 10 Injection Timing","P021D reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 10 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01DD/P01DE ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 10 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["l","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P021E":["Cylinder 11 Injection Timing","P021E reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 11 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01DF/P01E0 ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 11 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["l","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P021F":["Cylinder 12 Injection Timing","P021F reports a fault in the injection timing of cylinder 12 on a diesel engine. Unlike P01E1/P01E2 ('over retarded/advanced', the result of the injector learning routine in overrun), this code has no direction, and what it checks is up to the manufacturer.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 12 faulty (coil, armature, wear, deposits)"],["m","Injector wiring or connector: contact resistance, corrosion, chafing"],["l","Engine software sets the code falsely (update available for some models)"],["l","Injector driver stage or boost voltage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or misfire, rough idle","Loss of power, smoke or knocking possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0220":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch B Circuit","With electronic throttle control the throttle body carries two position sensors, A and B, usually with opposite or offset signals, and the engine module compares them (correlation code P2135). GM and Ford give the pedal sensors their own codes (P2120 ff), so B is in practice the second throttle sensor.",[["h","Throttle body or sensor B faulty (worn track)"],["h","Wiring or connector at the throttle body (corrosion, break)"],["m","5 V reference disturbed (short in another sensor on the same reference)"],["l","Engine module input or monitoring processor faulty"]],["Check engine light and throttle warning light","Reduced power, limp mode","Poor throttle response, jerking"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0221":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch B Circuit Range/Performance","With electronic throttle control the throttle body carries two position sensors, A and B, usually with opposite or offset signals, and the engine module compares them (correlation code P2135). GM and Ford give the pedal sensors their own codes (P2120 ff), so B is in practice the second throttle sensor.",[["h","Worn track of sensor B (signal jumps)"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or wiring"],["m","Throttle dirty or sticking"],["l","Mass airflow sensor fault or false air"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode, reduced power","Rough idle, jerking"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0222":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch B Circuit Low","With electronic throttle control the throttle body carries two position sensors, A and B, usually with opposite or offset signals, and the engine module compares them (correlation code P2135). GM and Ford give the pedal sensors their own codes (P2120 ff), so B is in practice the second throttle sensor.",[["h","Signal or 5 V wire open or shorted to ground"],["h","Throttle body or sensor B faulty internally"],["m","5 V reference pulled down by another sensor"],["m","Connector corrosion or water ingress"]],["Check engine light and throttle warning light","Reduced power, limp mode","Poor throttle response, jerking"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0223":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch B Circuit High","With electronic throttle control the throttle body carries two position sensors, A and B, usually with opposite or offset signals, and the engine module compares them (correlation code P2135). GM and Ford give the pedal sensors their own codes (P2120 ff), so B is in practice the second throttle sensor.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["h","Sensor B or throttle body faulty"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or 12 V in the harness"],["l","Engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light and throttle warning light","Reduced power, limp mode","Poor throttle response, jerking"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0224":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch B Circuit Intermittent","With electronic throttle control the throttle body carries two position sensors, A and B, usually with opposite or offset signals, and the engine module compares them (correlation code P2135). GM and Ford give the pedal sensors their own codes (P2120 ff), so B is in practice the second throttle sensor.",[["h","Loose contact or corroded connector at the throttle body"],["h","Worn spot on the sensor track"],["m","Wire breaking from engine movement or vibration"],["l","Engine module or ground connection"]],["Occasional jerking or power dips","Brief limp mode","Check engine light (sometimes only intermittently)"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0225":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch C Circuit","Sensor C is a third throttle or pedal position sensor whose role depends on the manufacturer. Ford uses it for the sensor of the second throttle body on engines with two throttles and digital (SENT) sensors; with other makes it can be a further pedal or throttle channel.",[["h","Sensor C or second throttle body faulty"],["h","Wiring or connector (supply, signal line)"],["m","5 V reference disturbed"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light and powertrain fault lamp","Reduced power","Rough idle, jerking"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0226":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch C Circuit Range/Performance","Sensor C is a third throttle or pedal position sensor whose role depends on the manufacturer. Ford uses it for the sensor of the second throttle body on engines with two throttles and digital (SENT) sensors; with other makes it can be a further pedal or throttle channel.",[["h","Wear or dirt on sensor C or the second throttle body"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or wiring"],["m","Throttle dirty or sticking"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode, reduced power","Jerking, poor throttle response"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0227":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch C Circuit Low","Sensor C is a third throttle or pedal position signal; its role depends on the manufacturer. Ford uses it for the sensor of the second throttle body on engines with two throttles and digital (SENT) sensors, GM does not use it.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor C shorted to ground or open (chafing, connector)"],["m","5 V reference to the sensor open or loaded"],["m","Sensor C or second throttle body faulty"],["m","Corrosion or water in the throttle body connector"]],["Check engine light (Ford also the wrench light)","Limp mode with limited power","Delayed or missing throttle response"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0228":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch C Circuit High","Sensor C is a third throttle or pedal position signal; its role depends on the manufacturer. Ford uses it for the sensor of the second throttle body on engines with two throttles and digital (SENT) sensors, GM does not use it.",[["h","Sensor ground (low reference) open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or system voltage"],["m","Sensor C or throttle body faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or water ingress"]],["Check engine light (Ford also the wrench light)","Limp mode with limited power","Delayed or missing throttle response"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0229":["Throttle/Pedal Position Sensor/Switch C Circuit Intermittent","Sensor C is a third throttle or pedal position signal; its role depends on the manufacturer. Ford uses it for the sensor of the second throttle body on engines with two throttles and digital (SENT) sensors, GM does not use it.",[["h","Loose contact at the second throttle body connector (spread pins, corrosion)"],["m","Harness chafing with intermittent short or open"],["m","Sensor or second throttle body with an internal intermittent fault"],["l","Voltage dips in the supply or ground"]],["Check engine light and wrench light (Ford)","Brief jerking or loss of power","Occasional limp mode"],["medium",[30,700]],"c"],"P022A":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control A Circuit/Open","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Wire or connector at the bypass actuator open"],["m","Actuator or solenoid of the bypass flap faulty (coil, motor)"],["l","Fuse or relay of the actuator supply"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P022B":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control A Circuit Low","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Actuator winding shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P022C":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control A Circuit High","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Actuator shorted internally (overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P022D":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control B Circuit/Open","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Wire or connector at the bypass actuator open"],["m","Actuator or solenoid of the bypass flap faulty (coil, motor)"],["l","Fuse or relay of the actuator supply"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P022E":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control B Circuit Low","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Actuator winding shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P022F":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control B Circuit High","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Actuator shorted internally (overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P0230":["Fuel Pump Primary Circuit","The fuel pump circuit has two sides: the primary circuit is the engine module's output to the fuel pump relay coil (or to a pump control module), the secondary circuit is the power circuit from relay contact or module to the pump itself. P0230 concerns the primary side.",[["h","Fuel pump relay faulty (coil open or shorted)"],["m","Control wire from module to relay open or shorted to ground or voltage"],["m","Relay supply, ground or fuse missing"],["m","With a pump control module: its supply, ground or the module itself"]],["Engine cranks but does not start","Engine stalls while driving","Check engine light on"],["easy",[15,350]],"c"],"P0231":["Fuel Pump Secondary Circuit Low","The fuel pump circuit has two sides: the primary circuit is the engine module's output to the fuel pump relay coil (or to a pump control module), the secondary circuit is the power circuit from relay contact or module to the pump itself. P0231 means the secondary side reads low, and the meaning differs by system.",[["h","Power wire from relay or module to pump shorted to ground (chafing at tank or underbody)"],["h","Relay contact not closing, fuse or battery feed to the relay missing"],["m","Pump motor jammed or shorted (high current)"],["m","Connector at the tank flange corroded or burnt"]],["Engine cranks but does not start","Engine stalls while driving","Check engine light on"],["medium",[20,800]],"c"],"P0232":["Fuel Pump Secondary Circuit High","The fuel pump circuit has two sides: the primary circuit is the engine module's output to the fuel pump relay coil (or to a pump control module), the secondary circuit is the power circuit from relay contact or module to the pump itself. P0232 means there is voltage in the pump power circuit although the pump should be off – a welded relay contact or a power wire shorted to another supply.",[["h","Relay contact welded or sticking"],["m","Pump power wire shorted to another supply"],["m","Pump control module output stage shorted"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the connector causing leakage current"]],["Fuel pump keeps running after switch-off","Battery drained overnight","Check engine light on (GM after two trips)"],["easy",[15,500]],"c"],"P0233":["Fuel Pump Secondary Circuit Intermittent","The fuel pump circuit has two sides: the primary circuit is the engine module's output to the fuel pump relay coil (or to a pump control module), the secondary circuit is the power circuit from relay contact or module to the pump itself. P0233 reports brief, recurring dropouts in the pump power circuit that count neither as steadily low nor high.",[["h","Loose contact at pump connector, tank flange or relay socket"],["m","Relay contacts burnt (intermittently high resistance)"],["m","Harness chafing at underbody or tank with intermittent contact"],["l","Pump motor with commutator or brush dropouts"]],["Occasional stumbling or jerking","Engine stalls now and then","Occasional starting problems"],["medium",[20,700]],"c"],"P0234":["Turbocharger/Supercharger A Overboost Condition","P0234 is a function code, not a sensor fault: the engine module compares target and actual boost, and actual boost stays above target although the control is already lowering it (wastegate opened, variable vanes opened). GM petrol engines set it at 20–35 kPa over target with the warning light in the first trip; GM diesels with variable vanes at 7–10 kPa, after two trips; Ford EcoBoost at about 28 kPa (4 psi) over 5 s while the wastegate is already commanded fully open.",[["h","Wastegate stuck closed, actuator or linkage sticking, control hose off or blocked"],["h","Variable turbine vanes sooted or stuck closed (diesel)"],["m","Boost control valve or electric actuator faulty"],["m","Boost or manifold sensor reading too high (drift)"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode with clear loss of power","Boost overshoots on acceleration, then power drops"],["medium",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0235":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor A Circuit","Boost sensor A measures the absolute pressure between charge air cooler and throttle – GM calls it TIAP, Ford TIP – usually combined with an intake air temperature sensor; its voltage rises with pressure. With the ignition on and the engine stopped it must read the same as manifold and barometric pressure.",[["h","Connector at the boost sensor corroded or loose, broken wire"],["m","Boost sensor faulty, often oily from crankcase ventilation"],["m","Charge air leak or jammed turbo vanes causing an implausible value"],["l","5 V reference shorted by another sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Clear loss of power, only base boost","Boost reading implausible in a scan tool"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0236":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","Boost sensor A measures the absolute pressure between charge air cooler and throttle – GM calls it TIAP, Ford TIP – usually combined with an intake air temperature sensor; its voltage rises with pressure. With the ignition on and the engine stopped it must read the same as manifold and barometric pressure.",[["h","Charge air leak (hose, clamp, cooler) between turbo and throttle"],["h","Boost sensor dirty, oily or drifting"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or harness"],["m","Manifold or barometric sensor faulty (correlation partner)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, only base boost","Hissing or whistling with a leak"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0237":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor A Circuit Low","Boost sensor A measures the absolute pressure between charge air cooler and throttle – GM calls it TIAP, Ford TIP – usually combined with an intake air temperature sensor; its voltage rises with pressure. With the ignition on and the engine stopped it must read the same as manifold and barometric pressure.",[["h","Signal or 5 V reference wire open or shorted to ground"],["h","Connector unplugged or corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","5 V reference pulled down by another sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Clear loss of power, only base boost","Boost reading implausible in a scan tool"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0238":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor A Circuit High","Boost sensor A measures the absolute pressure between charge air cooler and throttle – GM calls it TIAP, Ford TIP – usually combined with an intake air temperature sensor; its voltage rises with pressure. With the ignition on and the engine stopped it must read the same as manifold and barometric pressure.",[["h","Sensor ground open, or signal wire open on a pulled-up input"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or 12 V"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector corrosion or moisture at the ground"]],["Check engine light on","Clear loss of power, only base boost","Boost reading implausible in a scan tool"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0239":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor B Circuit","Boost sensor B is a second boost pressure sensor, for a second turbo or bank on parallel twin-turbo engines or a second stage on two-stage systems. No manufacturer using sensor B codes was found – GM, Ford and Saab, even on two-stage and twin-turbo engines, use a single boost sensor; the description follows CARB and the documented sensor A codes.",[["h","Connector or wire to sensor B open or corroded"],["m","Sensor B faulty or oily"],["l","Shared 5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode","Uneven boost build-up on twin turbos"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P023A":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Pump A Control Circuit/Open","Some engines cool the charge air with a coolant-to-air cooler in its own low-temperature circuit, driven by an electric pump – GM uses this on supercharged V8s such as the LT4 (Camaro ZL1, Corvette Z06, CTS-V) and LT5 (Corvette ZR1). P023A means GM's driver diagnosis has found an open circuit (more than 200 kΩ between output and ground with the low-side driver off).",[["h","Wire or connector at the pump open (front area, corrosion)"],["m","Pump faulty (motor or electronics)"],["m","Fuse, pump relay or supply missing"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power under load or in heat (boost limited)","Follow-up code P026A possible"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P023B":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Pump A Control Circuit Low","Some engines cool the charge air with a coolant-to-air cooler in its own low-temperature circuit, driven by an electric pump – GM uses this on supercharged V8s such as the LT4 (Camaro ZL1, Corvette Z06, CTS-V) and LT5 (Corvette ZR1). P023B means GM's driver diagnosis has found a short to ground (0.5 Ω or less between output and ground with the driver off).",[["h","Control wire to the pump shorted to ground"],["m","Pump shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power under load or in heat (boost limited)","Follow-up code P026A possible"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P023C":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Pump A Control Circuit High","Some engines cool the charge air with a coolant-to-air cooler in its own low-temperature circuit, driven by an electric pump – GM uses this on supercharged V8s such as the LT4 (Camaro ZL1, Corvette Z06, CTS-V) and LT5 (Corvette ZR1). P023C means GM's driver diagnosis has found a short to voltage (0.5 Ω or less between output and battery voltage with the driver on).",[["h","Control wire to the pump shorted to voltage"],["m","Pump shorted internally (overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power under load or in heat (boost limited)","Follow-up code P026A possible"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P023D":["Manifold Absolute Pressure - Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor A Correlation","P023D compares the manifold pressure sensor (after the throttle) with boost sensor A (before the throttle): with the engine stopped both must read the same ambient pressure. Saab's 2.8 V6 checks this once per trip with the ignition on before starting and sets the code at a difference of more than 12 kPa, with the warning light and limp mode limited to 170 Nm.",[["h","Manifold or boost sensor drifting or dirty"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or ground of one sensor"],["m","Pressure port or hose of one sensor blocked (oil sludge, ice)"],["l","Engine module or reference voltage"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode, limited power","Otherwise often unremarkable"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P023E":["Manifold Absolute Pressure - Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor B Correlation","Boost sensor B is a second boost pressure sensor, for a second turbo or bank on parallel twin-turbo engines or a second stage on two-stage systems. No manufacturer using sensor B codes was found – GM, Ford and Saab, even on two-stage and twin-turbo engines, use a single boost sensor; the description follows CARB and the documented sensor A codes.",[["h","Boost sensor B or manifold sensor drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at sensor B's connector or wire"],["l","Pressure port blocked"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly limited power","Otherwise unremarkable"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P023F":["Fuel Pump Secondary Circuit/Open","The fuel pump circuit has two sides: the primary circuit is the engine module's output to the fuel pump relay coil (or to a pump control module), the secondary circuit is the power circuit from relay contact or module to the pump itself. P023F means the power circuit to the pump is open.",[["h","Pump connector at tank flange or pump module open or corroded"],["h","Fuel pump open internally (brushes, winding)"],["m","Wire from module to pump or return wire open"],["l","Pump control module faulty"]],["Engine does not start, no pump noise","Engine stalls while driving","Check engine light on"],["medium",[30,900]],"c"],"P0240":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Boost sensor B is a second boost pressure sensor, for a second turbo or bank on parallel twin-turbo engines or a second stage on two-stage systems. No manufacturer using sensor B codes was found – GM, Ford and Saab, even on two-stage and twin-turbo engines, use a single boost sensor; the description follows CARB and the documented sensor A codes.",[["h","Charge air leak on the side or stage of turbo B"],["h","Sensor B dirty or drifting"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["l","Manifold or barometric sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power","Uneven boost between the turbos"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0241":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor B Circuit Low","Boost sensor B is a second boost pressure sensor, for a second turbo or bank on parallel twin-turbo engines or a second stage on two-stage systems. No manufacturer using sensor B codes was found – GM, Ford and Saab, even on two-stage and twin-turbo engines, use a single boost sensor; the description follows CARB and the documented sensor A codes.",[["h","Signal or 5 V reference wire open or shorted to ground"],["h","Connector unplugged or corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","5 V reference pulled down by another sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode","Boost B reads very low in a scan tool"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0242":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor B Circuit High","Boost sensor B is a second boost pressure sensor, for a second turbo or bank on parallel twin-turbo engines or a second stage on two-stage systems. No manufacturer using sensor B codes was found – GM, Ford and Saab, even on two-stage and twin-turbo engines, use a single boost sensor; the description follows CARB and the documented sensor A codes.",[["h","Sensor ground open, or signal wire open on a pulled-up input"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or 12 V"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode","Boost B shows its maximum value"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0243":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator A","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator A is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. P0243 is the general circuit fault of actuator A.",[["h","Connector at the wastegate valve or actuator loose, corroded or with spread contacts"],["m","Valve coil or electric actuator winding open"],["m","Supply voltage to the valve missing (ignition, relay, fuse)"],["m","Broken control wire between module and valve"]],["Check engine light on","Clearly less power, boost only at base level","Sluggish acceleration, turbo lag"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0244":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator A Range/Performance","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator A is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. P0244 means the actuator does not respond as commanded although its circuit is electrically sound, and manufacturers implement it differently.",[["h","Wastegate rod unhooked, retaining clip missing or linkage worn"],["m","Wastegate flap coked or sticking"],["m","Control hose to the wastegate can leaking, off or swapped; diaphragm torn"],["m","Wastegate solenoid mechanically stuck"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power or surging, too high boost","Limp mode with reduced power"],["medium",[50,1500]],"c"],"P0245":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator A Low","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator A is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. P0245 means the control circuit of actuator A reads low – typically the ground-switched control wire shorted to ground, so the valve would be permanently energised.",[["h","Control wire to the wastegate valve chafed through to ground"],["m","Solenoid shorted internally"],["m","Shared supply shorted (e.g. by the bypass valve)"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Clearly less power, boost only at base level","Sluggish acceleration, turbo lag"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0246":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator A High","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator A is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. P0246 means the control circuit of actuator A reads high – the control wire is shorted to voltage, so the driver cannot switch the valve to ground.",[["h","Control wire touching voltage (damaged harness, neighbouring wire)"],["m","Solenoid shorted internally (very low resistance, driver overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Clearly less power, boost only at base level","Sluggish acceleration, turbo lag"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0247":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator B","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator B is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. B is the second turbo or actuator, so B codes only exist on engines with two.",[["h","Connector at wastegate valve or actuator B loose or corroded"],["m","Coil or motor winding of actuator B open"],["m","Broken control or supply wire"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Clearly less power, boost only at base level","Uneven boost between the turbos"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0248":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator B Range/Performance","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator B is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. B is the second turbo or actuator, so B codes only exist on engines with two.",[["h","Retaining clip of the wastegate rod missing or fitted wrongly, rod unhooked"],["m","Wastegate flap B coked or sticking"],["m","Position sensor learned value outside limits (e.g. not relearned after turbo replacement)"],["l","Pneumatic wastegate: control hose or can B leaking"]],["Check engine light on","Missing turbo boost, lack of power","Uneven boost between the turbos"],["medium",[30,1800]],"c"],"P0249":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator B Low","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator B is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. B is the second turbo or actuator, so B codes only exist on engines with two.",[["h","Control wire of wastegate B chafed through to ground"],["m","Valve or actuator winding B shorted"],["m","Connector damage or moisture"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Clearly less power, boost only at base level","Uneven boost between the turbos"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P024A":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control A Range/Performance","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Bypass flap sticking, coked or oily (oil from crankcase ventilation or turbo)"],["m","Actuator weak or vacuum line leaking"],["m","Engine software (FCA 2.0 turbo)"],["l","Position sensor inaccurate or connector corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature","Possibly less power from boost limitation"],["medium",[0,500]],"c"],"P024B":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Control A Stuck","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Flap seized or coked"],["m","Actuator faulty (gear, motor)"],["l","Vacuum supply missing (pneumatic type)"],["l","Position sensor reading constant"]],["Check engine light on","Stuck open: higher charge air temperature, loss of power","Stuck closed: longer warm-up"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P024C":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Position Sensor A Circuit","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Connector or wiring to the actuator's sensor part"],["m","Built-in position sensor faulty"],["l","5 V reference disturbed by another sensor"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P024D":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Position Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Flap or linkage misadjusted or sticking"],["m","Sensor drifting or track worn"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["l","End stops not learned after replacement"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P024E":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Position Sensor A Circuit Low","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or open"],["m","5 V reference missing"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P024F":["Charge Air Cooler Bypass Position Sensor A Circuit High","The charge air cooler bypass is a flap or valve that routes compressed air around the charge air cooler, so the engine and exhaust warm up faster after a cold start; when warm it closes or opens partly to control intake air temperature. It is driven by an electric motor with a built-in position sensor or by a solenoid and vacuum can.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or system voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Depending on the rest position: longer warm-up or higher charge air temperature with slight loss of power","Often nothing noticeable while driving"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P0250":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Wastegate Actuator B High","The wastegate lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost; actuator B is either a pulse-width-modulated solenoid on a pneumatic wastegate can or an electric actuator with a position sensor. B is the second turbo or actuator, so B codes only exist on engines with two.",[["h","Control wire of wastegate B shorted to voltage"],["m","Winding shorted internally (overcurrent)"],["m","Connector corroded or damp"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Clearly less power, boost only at base level","Uneven boost between the turbos"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0251":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control A (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump. On older distributor pumps it was a solenoid that set the injected quantity; on common rail it is usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that lets only as much fuel into the pumping elements as the rail needs, instead of spilling the excess through a pressure valve.",[["h","Metering actuator coil open"],["h","Connector at the actuator corroded or loose, broken wire"],["m","Supply voltage to the actuator missing"],["m","Distributor pump: internal electronics or quantity valve faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode","Rough idle or hunting"],["medium",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0252":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control A Range/Performance (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump. On older distributor pumps it was a solenoid that set the injected quantity; on common rail it is usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that lets only as much fuel into the pumping elements as the rail needs, instead of spilling the excess through a pressure valve.",[["h","Metering actuator with shorted turns, too low resistance (driver overheats)"],["m","Actuator mechanically sticking or dirty (fuel quality, wear debris)"],["m","Wiring with contact resistance"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode","Fluctuating rail pressure, hunting idle"],["medium",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0253":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control A Low (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump – on common rail usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that sets how much fuel is compressed. P0253 means the actuator A circuit reads low.",[["h","Control wire to the metering actuator chafed to ground"],["m","Connector or wire open (where makers include it under low)"],["m","Metering actuator faulty internally"],["l","Engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode with limited power and speed","Hard starting possible"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0254":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control A High (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump – on common rail usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that sets how much fuel is compressed. P0254 means the actuator A circuit reads high or draws too much current – typically the control wire shorted to voltage.",[["h","Control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Metering valve with shorted turns (overcurrent)"],["l","Connector corrosion with leakage current"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Engine stalls or will not start (Saab 3.0 diesel)","Limp mode, loss of power"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0255":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control A Intermittent (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump – on common rail usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that sets how much fuel is compressed. P0255 reports brief open or short circuits in the actuator A circuit that come and go – typically a loose contact or a wire breaking under vibration.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the metering actuator connector"],["m","Harness chafing intermittently (vibration)"],["l","Actuator coil with a temperature-dependent fault"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light (often goes out again)","Brief jerking or power dips","Occasional limp mode"],["medium",[50,1200]],"c"],"P0256":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control B (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump – on common rail usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that sets how much fuel is compressed. Actuator B is a second metering actuator, for example on pumps with two metering valves such as on Saab's 3.0 V6 diesel.",[["h","Metering valve B open"],["h","Connector or wire to valve B open"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode"],["medium",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0257":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control B Range/Performance (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump – on common rail usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that sets how much fuel is compressed. P0257 is the functional fault of actuator B: the metering does not achieve its target.",[["h","Rail pressure or metering target not reached (leak, relief valve open)"],["m","Poor low-pressure supply (filter, lift pump, air)"],["m","Metering actuator or pumping element worn"],["l","Software or diagnostic level"]],["Check engine light on","Power derate","Hard starting, low rail pressure when cranking"],["hard",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0258":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control B Low (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","These codes cover the electrically controlled fuel metering actuator on a diesel injection pump – on common rail usually an inlet metering valve on the high-pressure pump that sets how much fuel is compressed. P0258 means the actuator B circuit reads low.",[["h","Supply wire, fuse or relay of the metering valves open or shorted to ground"],["m","Connector at the pump corroded or loose"],["m","Metering valve shorted to ground internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on (after the second trip)","Loss of power, limp mode","Raised idle speed in limp mode"],["medium",[50,1500]],"c"],"P0259":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control B High (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","Fuel metering actuator B is a second electrically controlled metering valve on a diesel injection pump; A has the codes P0251–P0255. P0259 means its circuit reads high or draws too much current – a short to voltage or a shorted turn.",[["h","Metering valve B shorted internally"],["m","Control wire to the valve shorted to voltage, chafing"],["m","Connector at the valve corroded or wet"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode with limited power and speed","Hard starting, engine may stall"],["hard",[150,1200]],"c"],"P025A":["Fuel Pump Module A Control Circuit/Open","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. P025A means the control line from the engine module to pump module A is open or faulty.",[["h","Control line to the pump module open or shorted, often along the frame"],["h","Connector at the pump module corroded or wet (module often mounted on the frame)"],["m","Pump control module faulty (input)"],["m","Module ground or supply poor"]],["Check engine light (manufacturer-dependent)","Hard or no start","Rough idle, surging"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P025B":["Fuel Pump Module A Control Circuit Range/Performance","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. P025B means pump module A receives a signal, but an invalid command – the duty cycle is outside the valid window or implausible.",[["h","Contact resistance or corrosion in the control line or connector distorting the duty cycle"],["m","Pump control module faulty"],["m","Interference or poor ground between engine module and pump module"],["l","Engine module software or output"]],["Check engine light (manufacturer-dependent)","Hard or no start","Rough idle, surging"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P025C":["Fuel Pump Module A Control Circuit Low","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. P025C means the control circuit to pump module A reads low, typically a short to ground.",[["h","Control line chafed to ground at the frame"],["h","Water or corrosion at the pump module connector"],["m","Ground point loose or corroded"],["m","Pump control module faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Engine starts and dies again","Starting problems"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P025D":["Fuel Pump Module A Control Circuit High","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. P025D means the control circuit to pump module A reads high, typically a short to voltage.",[["h","Control line shorted to battery voltage or another wire"],["m","Moisture in the connector (leakage to voltage)"],["m","Pump control module faulty"],["l","Relay stuck or faulty (Ford 6.7)"]],["Check engine light on","Pump runs all the time or not at all","Starting problems"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P025E":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor A Intermittent/Erratic","Boost sensor A measures the pressure between charge air cooler and throttle (Ford TIP, GM TIAP). P025E counts brief excursions outside its valid voltage range that are each too short for the low or high code P0237/P0238 – Ford's limits are below 0.19 V and above 4.88 V.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor connector"],["m","Wire or 5 V reference breaking from vibration or heat"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (erratic signal)"],["l","Shared 5 V reference disturbed by another sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Occasional loss of power, boost limited","Jerking on acceleration"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P025F":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic","Boost sensor B is a second boost pressure sensor, for a second turbo or bank on twin-turbo engines or a second stage. P025F counts brief excursions of its signal outside the valid range that are too short for the low or high code – the counterpart of P025E, which Ford sets after 10 such events in one drive cycle.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor B connector"],["m","Wire breaking from vibration or heat"],["m","Sensor B faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Occasional loss of power","Boost limited"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P0260":["Injection Pump Fuel Metering Control B Intermittent (Cam/Rotor/Injector)","P0260 reports brief dropouts or jumps in the circuit of fuel metering actuator B on a diesel injection pump that do not last long enough for the low or high code (P0258/P0259) – typically a loose contact or a wire breaking under vibration. No manufacturer using P0260 was found; the description follows CARB's continuous circuit monitoring requirement and Saab's diagnosis of the same two-valve pump, which recommends a wiggle test with the valves activated.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the metering valve B connector"],["m","Broken or chafed wire in the harness to the pump (vibration, heat)"],["m","Metering valve B with internal dropouts"],["l","Unstable supply or ground"]],["Check engine light on","Occasional jerking or brief loss of power","Limp mode possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0261":["Cylinder 1 Injector A Circuit Low","P0261 means the control wire of the cylinder 1 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 1 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0301","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0262":["Cylinder 1 Injector A Circuit High","P0262 means the control wire of the cylinder 1 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 1 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0301","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0263":["Cylinder 1 Contribution/Balance","P0263 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 1 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 1 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 1 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0264":["Cylinder 2 Injector A Circuit Low","P0264 means the control wire of the cylinder 2 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 2 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0302","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0265":["Cylinder 2 Injector A Circuit High","P0265 means the control wire of the cylinder 2 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 2 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0302","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0266":["Cylinder 2 Contribution/Balance","P0266 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 2 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 2 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 2 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0267":["Cylinder 3 Injector A Circuit Low","P0267 means the control wire of the cylinder 3 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 3 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0303","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0268":["Cylinder 3 Injector A Circuit High","P0268 means the control wire of the cylinder 3 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 3 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0303","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0269":["Cylinder 3 Contribution/Balance","P0269 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 3 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 3 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 3 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P026A":["Charge Air Cooler Efficiency Below Threshold","P026A means the charge air cooler is not cooling enough. The module compares the temperature drop across the cooler with the maximum possible (inlet minus ambient) or with a model: GM's 6.6 litre LML diesel sets it below 25 % efficiency, later GM diesels below 50 %, both after two trips; Ford's 6.7 and 3.0 diesels when the measured temperature is 18–35 °C (3.0: 35–65 °C) off the model over about 10 s.",[["h","Charge air cooler clogged on the outside (insects, leaves, dirt) or fins damaged"],["h","Coolant-cooled cooler: pump faulty, air in the low-temperature circuit, low coolant"],["m","Charge air or ambient temperature sensor inaccurate"],["l","Charge air cooler bypass stuck open"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power under load or in heat","High intake air temperature in live data"],["medium",[50,900]],"u"],"P026B":["Injection Timing Performance","P026B is the engine-wide injection timing monitor: CARB requires diesels from 2010 to detect injection that is too early or too late overall, or a timing control at its limit. Unlike P020A ff (per cylinder) it concerns the engine as a whole.",[["h","Engine module software (false setting)"],["m","Injectors coked or worn (changed start of injection)"],["m","Rail pressure or fuel supply problems (pump, filter, fuel quality)"],["l","Crankshaft or camshaft signal inaccurate"]],["Check engine light on","Often nothing noticeable (false setting)","Possibly knocking, rough running or more smoke"],["hard",[100,3500]],"u"],"P026C":["Fuel Injection Quantity A Lower Than Expected","From 2010, CARB requires diesel engines to detect when the commanded injection quantity is not delivered. The module estimates the fuel actually burned from airflow and the measured lambda (from the NOx or oxygen sensor) and corrects the quantity; if that correction passes a limit, the code sets.",[["h","Injectors coked or clogged (too little flow)"],["m","Airflow or boost measurement wrong, NOx sensor lambda inaccurate"],["m","Weak low- or high-pressure supply (filter, pump, rail pressure)"],["l","Fuel quality or contamination"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power","Rough running or idle"],["hard",[100,4000]],"u"],"P026D":["Fuel Injection Quantity A Higher Than Expected","From 2010, CARB requires diesel engines to detect when the commanded injection quantity is not delivered. The module estimates the fuel actually burned from airflow and the measured lambda (from the NOx or oxygen sensor) and corrects the quantity; if that correction passes a limit, the code sets.",[["h","Injector dripping or worn, or wrong injector type fitted"],["m","EGR valve not closing or EGR differential pressure sensor wrong"],["m","Boost sensor or turbo vanes faulty, intake leak"],["m","NOx or oxygen sensor reading wrong"]],["Check engine light on","Black smoke, higher soot load, more frequent filter regeneration","Rough running or idle"],["hard",[100,4000]],"u"],"P026E":["Charge Air Cooler Coolant Pump A Performance","Some engines cool the charge air with a coolant-to-air cooler in its own low-temperature circuit with an electric pump. P026E means that pump is electrically sound but does not deliver as commanded – judged from feedback by the pump electronics or from the missing temperature drop.",[["h","Air in the low-temperature circuit or low coolant (pump runs dry)"],["h","Pump faulty internally (bearing, impeller, electronics)"],["m","Software or calibration"],["l","Weak supply or ground, corroded connector"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power in heat or under load","Follow-up code P026A possible"],["medium",[30,600]],"u"],"P026F":["Coolant Thermostat Performance","P026F is a general functional code for the coolant thermostat. CARB requires that the coolant reaches the temperature needed for other diagnostics in the approved time and comes within 11 °C of the thermostat's regulating temperature; from model years 2019–2021 petrol engines must also detect it dropping back below that temperature, and electric pumps or valves controlling coolant flow are monitored like a thermostat.",[["h","Thermostat stuck open or opening too early"],["m","Thermostat stuck closed or opening too late"],["m","Map-controlled thermostat: heating element or control faulty"],["l","Coolant temperature sensor inaccurate"]],["Check engine light on","Slow warm-up, weak heating (stuck open)","Low or fluctuating temperature gauge"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P0270":["Cylinder 4 Injector A Circuit Low","P0270 means the control wire of the cylinder 4 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 4 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0304","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0271":["Cylinder 4 Injector A Circuit High","P0271 means the control wire of the cylinder 4 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 4 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0304","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0272":["Cylinder 4 Contribution/Balance","P0272 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 4 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 4 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 4 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0273":["Cylinder 5 Injector A Circuit Low","P0273 means the control wire of the cylinder 5 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 5 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0305","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0274":["Cylinder 5 Injector A Circuit High","P0274 means the control wire of the cylinder 5 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 5 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0305","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0275":["Cylinder 5 Contribution/Balance","P0275 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 5 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 5 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 5 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0276":["Cylinder 6 Injector A Circuit Low","P0276 means the control wire of the cylinder 6 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 6 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0306","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0277":["Cylinder 6 Injector A Circuit High","P0277 means the control wire of the cylinder 6 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 6 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0306","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0278":["Cylinder 6 Contribution/Balance","P0278 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 6 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 6 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 6 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0279":["Cylinder 7 Injector A Circuit Low","P0279 means the control wire of the cylinder 7 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 7 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0307","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P027A":["Fuel Pump Module B Control Circuit/Open","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. Module B is a second pump module, for example on dual-tank commercial vehicles.",[["h","Control line to the pump module open or shorted, often along the frame"],["h","Connector at the pump module corroded or wet (module often mounted on the frame)"],["m","Pump control module faulty (input)"],["m","Module ground or supply poor"]],["Check engine light (manufacturer-dependent)","Fuel shortage when running from the second tank","Starting problems, surging"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P027B":["Fuel Pump Module B Control Circuit Range/Performance","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. Module B is a second pump module, for example on dual-tank commercial vehicles.",[["h","Contact resistance or corrosion in the control or monitor line to module B"],["m","Pump control module B faulty"],["m","Ground problems"],["l","Relay module or CAN forwarding disturbed (Ford commercial vehicles)"]],["Check engine light (vehicle-dependent)","Fuel shortage when running from the second tank","Surging, lack of power"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P027C":["Fuel Pump Module B Control Circuit Low","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. Module B is a second pump module, for example on dual-tank commercial vehicles.",[["h","Control line to module B shorted to ground"],["h","Connector at module B corroded or wet"],["m","Pump control module B faulty"],["l","Ground point"]],["Check engine light on","Starting problems or stalling","Loss of power from low supply pressure"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P027D":["Fuel Pump Module B Control Circuit High","On demand-controlled fuel systems the engine module does not drive the pump itself but sends a pump control module the desired pump output on a separate control line, usually as a duty cycle; the module reports back on a monitor line or via CAN. Module B is a second pump module, for example on dual-tank commercial vehicles.",[["h","Control line to module B shorted to voltage"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Pump control module B faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Pump B runs all the time or not at all","Starting problems"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P027E":["Cold Start Fuel Injection Quantity A Lower Than Expected","P027E is the cold-start counterpart of P026C: during cold start the engine gets less fuel than commanded, for example because a catalyst-heating injection does not arrive. The check probably works as in warm running, from airflow and lambda against the commanded quantity.",[["m","Injectors coked or clogged"],["m","Rail pressure builds too slowly at cold start (filter, wax, pump)"],["m","Airflow or lambda measurement inaccurate"],["l","Software"]],["Check engine light on","Poor cold start, rough running when cold","Longer warm-up"],["hard",[100,4000]],"u"],"P027F":["Cold Start Fuel Injection Quantity A Higher Than Expected","P027F is the cold-start counterpart of P026D: during cold start the engine gets more fuel than commanded, for example from a leaking injector or an EGR valve or throttle in the wrong position that reduces fresh air. The check probably works as in warm running, from airflow and lambda against the commanded quantity.",[["m","Injector leaking or worn"],["m","EGR valve or intake/exhaust throttle in the wrong position (less fresh air)"],["m","Lambda/NOx sensor or airflow sensor reading wrong"],["l","Software"]],["Check engine light on","White or black smoke after a cold start","Rough running when cold"],["hard",[100,4000]],"u"],"P0280":["Cylinder 7 Injector A Circuit High","P0280 means the control wire of the cylinder 7 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 7 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0307","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0281":["Cylinder 7 Contribution/Balance","P0281 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 7 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 7 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 7 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0282":["Cylinder 8 Injector A Circuit Low","P0282 means the control wire of the cylinder 8 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 8 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0308","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0283":["Cylinder 8 Injector A Circuit High","P0283 means the control wire of the cylinder 8 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 8 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0308","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0284":["Cylinder 8 Contribution/Balance","P0284 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 8 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 8 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 8 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0285":["Cylinder 9 Injector A Circuit Low","P0285 means the control wire of the cylinder 9 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 9 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0309","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0286":["Cylinder 9 Injector A Circuit High","P0286 means the control wire of the cylinder 9 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 9 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0309","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0287":["Cylinder 9 Contribution/Balance","P0287 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 9 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 9 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 9 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0288":["Cylinder 10 Injector A Circuit Low","P0288 means the control wire of the cylinder 10 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 10 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0310","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0289":["Cylinder 10 Injector A Circuit High","P0289 means the control wire of the cylinder 10 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 10 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0310","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P028A":["Charge Air Cooler Cooling Fan Control Circuit/Open","Some vehicles – mainly commercial and off-road machines, or cars with a separate cooler for a coolant-cooled charge air cooler – have their own fan for the charge air cooler; a Deere patent describes such a fan that the module switches and reverses to blow out dirt. P028A means the fan's control circuit is open or has a general fault, detected by the driver stage.",[["h","Wire or connector to the fan open or corroded"],["m","Fan relay or fan control module faulty"],["m","Fan motor open internally"],["l","Fuse blown"]],["Check engine light on","Fan does not run","Loss of power in heat or traffic"],["medium",[30,500]],"u"],"P028B":["Charge Air Cooler Cooling Fan Performance/Stuck Off","Some vehicles – mainly commercial and off-road machines, or cars with a separate cooler for a coolant-cooled charge air cooler – have their own fan for the charge air cooler, switched by the engine module; a Deere patent describes such a fan that is also reversed to blow out dirt. P028B means the module commands the fan on but sees no effect – no speed or current feedback from a fan control module, or the charge air does not cool – although the circuit is electrically sound (circuit faults are P028A, P028D and P028E).",[["h","Fan motor seized or worn"],["m","Fan blocked (debris, dirt, ice)"],["m","Fan relay or fan control module without output"],["l","Fan fuse or supply"]],["Warning light (manufacturer-dependent)","Loss of power in heat or at low speed","High charge air temperature, possibly P026A"],["medium",[80,500]],"u"],"P028C":["Charge Air Cooler Cooling Fan Stuck On","Some vehicles – mainly commercial and off-road machines, or cars with a separate cooler for a coolant-cooled charge air cooler – have their own fan for the charge air cooler, switched by the engine module; a Deere patent describes such a fan that is also reversed to blow out dirt. P028C means the fan runs although the module has switched it off – speed or current feedback, or the voltage at the output, does not match the off command.",[["h","Relay contact welded"],["m","Driver stage or fan control module shorted"],["m","Control wire shorted (to ground on low-side, to voltage on high-side control)"]],["Fan runs all the time, even after switch-off","Battery drains","Warning light (manufacturer-dependent)"],["easy",[20,300]],"u"],"P028D":["Charge Air Cooler Cooling Fan Control Circuit Low","Some vehicles – mainly commercial and off-road machines, or cars with a separate cooler for a coolant-cooled charge air cooler – have their own fan for the charge air cooler, switched by the engine module; a Deere patent describes such a fan that is also reversed to blow out dirt. P028D means the fan's control circuit reads low – typically the control wire shorted to ground, detected by the driver stage.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Relay coil or fan module input shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Warning light (manufacturer-dependent)","Fan runs permanently or not at all","Loss of power in heat possible"],["medium",[30,300]],"u"],"P028E":["Charge Air Cooler Cooling Fan Control Circuit High","Some vehicles – mainly commercial and off-road machines, or cars with a separate cooler for a coolant-cooled charge air cooler – have their own fan for the charge air cooler, switched by the engine module; a Deere patent describes such a fan that is also reversed to blow out dirt. P028E means the fan's control circuit reads high – typically the control wire shorted to voltage or, on some drivers, an open circuit, detected by the driver stage.",[["h","Control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Control wire open (on some drivers)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Warning light (manufacturer-dependent)","Fan does not switch as commanded","Loss of power in heat possible"],["medium",[30,300]],"u"],"P028F":["Charge Air Cooler Cooling Fan Control Circuit Driver Performance","Some vehicles – mainly commercial and off-road machines, or cars with a separate cooler for a coolant-cooled charge air cooler – have their own fan for the charge air cooler, switched by the engine module; a Deere patent describes such a fan that is also reversed to blow out dirt. P028F concerns the driver stage that switches the fan: it reports an internal fault such as overcurrent or overheating, or its diagnosis does not match the command.",[["h","Fan motor or wiring shorted (driver overload)"],["m","Driver in the engine or fan module faulty"],["l","Supply voltage or ground of the module poor"]],["Warning light (manufacturer-dependent)","Loss of power in heat or at low speed","High charge air temperature, possibly P026A"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P0290":["Cylinder 10 Contribution/Balance","P0290 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 10 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 10 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 10 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0291":["Cylinder 11 Injector A Circuit Low","P0291 means the control wire of the cylinder 11 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 11 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0311","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0292":["Cylinder 11 Injector A Circuit High","P0292 means the control wire of the cylinder 11 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 11 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0311","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0293":["Cylinder 11 Contribution/Balance","P0293 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 11 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 11 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 11 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0294":["Cylinder 12 Injector A Circuit Low","P0294 means the control wire of the cylinder 12 injector – the side the engine module switches to ground – is shorted to ground. GM detects this with the driver off: on direct injection at 1 V or less, on port injection and Duramax diesels at 0.5 Ω or less to ground.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 12 injector chafed to ground in the harness"],["m","Injector connector damp or corroded, leakage to ground"],["m","Injector shorted to its housing internally"],["l","Open control wire (on some GM port-injection drivers this also reads low; P020x sets too)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0312","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0295":["Cylinder 12 Injector A Circuit High","P0295 means the control wire of the cylinder 12 injector is shorted to voltage. GM detects this with the driver on: on direct injection by 25 A or more through the driver, on port injection and Duramax diesels by 0.5 Ω or less to the supply.",[["h","Control wire of the cylinder 12 injector touching battery voltage or another supply wire"],["m","Injector coil shorted internally (too low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Short between supply and control wire in harness or connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running, misfire code P0312","Loss of power, rough idle"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0296":["Cylinder 12 Contribution/Balance","P0296 comes from the cylinder balance control of common-rail diesels: the engine module measures the crankshaft acceleration of each cylinder's power stroke, compares it with the average and gives a deviating cylinder more or less fuel. The code sets when the correction for cylinder 12 reaches its limit and the cylinder still does not keep up – GM at 95 % of a limit of about ±12–17 mm³ for 30 s, Ford above 90 % of the allowed 3.5–15 mg per stroke for 7.5 s; GM needs two trips.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 12 worn, coked or leaking"],["m","Engine software outdated or diagnosis too sensitive"],["m","Injector correction codes not or wrongly programmed after replacement"],["m","Low compression on cylinder 12 (valve, rings, injector seal)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, shaking or knocking","Loss of power or smoke"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0297":["Vehicle Overspeed Condition","P0297 is a condition code: the engine module detects that road speed – from the vehicle speed sensor or wheel and output shaft speed – exceeded the calibrated maximum. Electronic speed limiters cut fuel or throttle at that limit, and the event is stored.",[["h","Limiter speed genuinely exceeded (downhill, trailer)"],["m","Wrong tyre size or axle ratio programmed, larger wheels fitted"],["m","Wheelspin on the driven axle"],["l","Speed signal disturbed (loose contact, interference, tone wheel)"]],["Usually no warning light, code only stored","Vehicle limits speed at its maximum","With a disturbed signal: jumping speedometer, odd shifting"],["easy",[0,150]],"c"],"P0298":["Engine Oil Over Temperature","P0298 is a protection code: engine oil temperature – from its own sensor, often combined with the oil level sensor, or modelled – has exceeded the protection limit. Saab's 3.0 diesel limits injection quantity when the oil stays at 135 °C or more for over 10 s, until it cools down; a heavy-duty bus engine warns in two stages and then derates.",[["h","High load in heat (trailer, mountain pass, stop-and-go)"],["m","Cooling or fan problem (clogged radiator, fan, thermostat, low coolant)"],["m","Oil cooler clogged or oil thermostat stuck"],["m","Oil temperature or level sensor reading too high"]],["Reduced power, quantity limited","Oil temperature warning","Usually no check engine light"],["medium",[0,800]],"c"],"P0299":["Turbocharger/Supercharger A Underboost Condition","P0299 means actual boost stays below target for a period although the control already demands more. Thresholds: GM petrol 30–55 kPa below target with the warning light in the first trip; GM diesels 10–50 kPa, two trips; Ford EcoBoost more than 4 psi (about 28 kPa) for 5 s with the wastegate already commanded fully closed; Ford diesels 30–40 kPa for 10–15 s, with a further underboost code P2263.",[["h","Leak in the charge air path (hose off or torn, cooler leaking, clamps)"],["h","Wastegate faulty (rod off, preload lost, vacuum or solenoid, diaphragm)"],["m","Turbo vanes sticking or turbo worn (diesel)"],["m","Bypass valve leaking (torn diaphragm) or its control"]],["Loss of power, sluggish acceleration","Limp mode, power limited","Check engine light on"],["medium",[30,2500]],"c"],"P029A":["Cylinder 1 - Fuel Trim at Max Limit","These codes belong to an individual-cylinder fuel correction: a diesel's cylinder balance control measures each cylinder's crankshaft acceleration and adjusts its injection quantity; on petrol engines a cylinder-selective mixture control does the same. CARB requires the system to report when such a correction has used up its range or one cylinder causes an imbalance.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 1 coked or partly clogged"],["m","Compression loss in this cylinder (valve, rings, leak at injector or glow plug)"],["m","Injector circuit high-resistance or connector"],["m","Petrol: false air at this cylinder's intake (manifold gasket)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, vibration","Lack of power, possibly misfire"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P029B":["Cylinder 1 - Fuel Trim at Min Limit","These codes belong to an individual-cylinder fuel correction: a diesel's cylinder balance control measures each cylinder's crankshaft acceleration and adjusts its injection quantity; on petrol engines a cylinder-selective mixture control does the same. CARB requires the system to report when such a correction has used up its range or one cylinder causes an imbalance.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 1 dripping or leaking"],["m","Injector with excessive flow (worn seat or needle)"],["l","Wrong injector correction code after replacement"],["l","Injector circuit short (extended activation)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, vibration","Higher consumption, smoke or fuel smell"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P029C":["Cylinder 1 - Injector Restricted","These codes belong to an individual-cylinder fuel correction: a diesel's cylinder balance control measures each cylinder's crankshaft acceleration and adjusts its injection quantity; on petrol engines a cylinder-selective mixture control does the same. CARB requires the system to report when such a correction has used up its range or one cylinder causes an imbalance.",[["h","Deposits or coking at the nozzle of cylinder 1"],["m","Nozzle needle sticking"],["m","Contaminated fuel or water"],["m","Injector circuit high-resistance"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, vibration","Lack of power, higher consumption or smoke"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P029D":["Cylinder 1 - Injector Leaking","These codes belong to an individual-cylinder fuel correction: a diesel's cylinder balance control measures each cylinder's crankshaft acceleration and adjusts its injection quantity; on petrol engines a cylinder-selective mixture control does the same. CARB requires the system to report when such a correction has used up its range or one cylinder causes an imbalance.",[["h","Nozzle seat or needle of the cylinder 1 injector leaking"],["m","Deposits keeping the needle from closing fully"],["l","Rail pressure too high"],["l","Wrong or wrongly coded injector"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, vibration","Smoke, fuel smell, knocking"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P029E":["Cylinder 2 - Fuel Trim at Max Limit","These codes belong to an individual-cylinder fuel correction: a diesel's cylinder balance control measures each cylinder's crankshaft acceleration and adjusts its injection quantity; on petrol engines a cylinder-selective mixture control does the same. CARB requires the system to report when such a correction has used up its range or one cylinder causes an imbalance.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 2 coked or partly clogged"],["m","Compression loss in this cylinder (valve, rings, leak at injector or glow plug)"],["m","Injector circuit high-resistance or connector"],["m","Petrol: false air at this cylinder's intake (manifold gasket)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, vibration","Lack of power, possibly misfire"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P029F":["Cylinder 2 - Fuel Trim at Min Limit","These codes belong to an individual-cylinder fuel correction: a diesel's cylinder balance control measures each cylinder's crankshaft acceleration and adjusts its injection quantity; on petrol engines a cylinder-selective mixture control does the same. CARB requires the system to report when such a correction has used up its range or one cylinder causes an imbalance.",[["h","Injector of cylinder 2 dripping or leaking"],["m","Injector with excessive flow (worn seat or needle)"],["l","Wrong injector correction code after replacement"],["l","Injector circuit short (extended activation)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, vibration","Higher consumption, smoke or fuel smell"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P02A0":["Cylinder 2 - Injector Restricted","These codes belong to an individual-cylinder fuel correction: a diesel's cylinder balance control measures each cylinder's crankshaft acceleration and adjusts its injection quantity; on petrol engines a cylinder-selective mixture control does the same. CARB requires the system to report when such a correction has used up its range or one cylin