ed after replacement"],["l","Disturbed crankshaft signal (worn drive belt, tone wheel, dual-mass flywheel)"]],["Check engine light on","Often nothing noticeable; possibly rough idle or knocking","Possibly smoke or loss of power"],["hard",[0,1200]],"c"],"P02E0":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control Circuit/Open","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Connector at the throttle corroded or loose"],["m","Broken motor wire"],["m","Motor winding of the valve open"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P02E1":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control Performance","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Coking from EGR soot and crankcase oil on flap and shaft"],["m","Valve gear or motor worn"],["l","Position sensor inaccurate"],["l","Icing in cold weather"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P02E2":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control Circuit Low","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Harness chafed to ground"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Valve motor shorted internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P02E3":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control Circuit High","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Wire shorted to a supply wire in the harness"],["m","Corroded connector bridging pins"],["m","Valve motor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P02E4":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control Stuck Open","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Coking or oil carbon preventing closing"],["m","Return spring or gear faulty, flap loose on the shaft"],["m","Motor too weak (wear)"],["l","Foreign object or ice"]],["Check engine light on","Regeneration aborts, soot load rises","Shaking at switch-off, little loss of power"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P02E5":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control Stuck Closed","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Coking or oil carbon holding the valve closed"],["m","Return spring broken"],["m","Gear blocked"],["l","Icing"]],["Check engine light on at once","Severe loss of power, limp mode","Black smoke, poor starting or stalling"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P02E6":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Position Sensor Circuit","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Sensor connector or wiring"],["m","Sensor in the valve unit faulty"],["l","5 V reference disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P02E7":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Position Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Valve stiff or coked"],["m","Position sensor drifting"],["m","Contact resistance in the sensor connector"],["l","Gear play"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P02E8":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Position Sensor Circuit Low","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or 5 V supply open"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","5 V reference loaded by another sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P02E9":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Position Sensor Circuit High","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P02EA":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Loose contact at connector or pins"],["m","Wire breaking under movement"],["m","Sensor with dropouts"],["l","Electromagnetic interference"]],["Check engine light (possibly intermittent)","Valve occasionally switched off","Irregular regeneration"],["medium",[30,500]],"c"],"P02EB":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control Motor Current Range/Performance","The diesel intake air flow valve A is the throttle flap ahead of the intake manifold of a diesel engine: it creates the vacuum needed for high EGR rates, lowers airflow to heat the particulate filter and catalyst during regeneration and closes at switch-off against run-on shaking. A spring holds it open when unpowered; an electric motor positions it, with a position sensor reporting back.",[["h","Valve motor shorted internally or worn"],["m","Valve mechanically jammed (coking)"],["m","Short between the motor wires"],["l","Engine module or current measurement"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable change","Filter regeneration does not run, soot load rises"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P02EC":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control System - High Air Flow Detected","Ford's 6.7 and 3.0 diesels control the air mass through the EGR valve and the intake throttle. P02EC means the measured air mass stays above target with the control at its limit – too little EGR, or the throttle cannot restrict enough – for 10 s (6.7) or 5 s (3.0).",[["h","EGR valve stuck closed or EGR cooler clogged"],["m","Intake throttle stuck open (P02E4)"],["m","Mass airflow sensor reading too high"],["l","Boost too high"]],["Check engine light on","Higher NOx","Often no change in driving"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P02ED":["Diesel Intake Air Flow A Control System - Low Air Flow Detected","Ford's 6.7 and 3.0 diesels control the air mass through the EGR valve and the intake throttle. P02ED means the measured air mass stays below target with the control at its limit – too much EGR, a throttle stuck closed or a clogged intake – for 10 s (6.7) or 5 s (3.0).",[["h","EGR valve stuck open"],["m","Air filter or intake clogged"],["m","Intake throttle stuck closed or coked"],["m","Mass airflow sensor reading too low (dirty)"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power","Smoke, high soot load in the filter"],["hard",[50,1500]],"c"],"P02EE":["Cylinder 1 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02EE reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 1 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0201, P0261, P0262), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of the opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 1 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 1","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02EF":["Cylinder 2 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02EF reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 2 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0202, P0264 and P0265), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 2 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 2","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F0":["Cylinder 3 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F0 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 3 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0203, P0267 and P0268), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 3 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 3","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F1":["Cylinder 4 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F1 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 4 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0204, P0270 and P0271), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 4 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 4","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F2":["Cylinder 5 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F2 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 5 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0205, P0273 and P0274), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 5 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 5","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F3":["Cylinder 6 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F3 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 6 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0206, P0276 and P0277), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 6 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 6","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F4":["Cylinder 7 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F4 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 7 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0207, P0279 and P0280), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 7 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 7","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F5":["Cylinder 8 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F5 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 8 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0208, P0282 and P0283), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. GM's 8-cylinder diesel checks the time the current takes to fall from hold to zero (outside 20–50 µs in 10 of 20 samples, two trips); GM's petrol direct injection evaluates the voltage feedback of opening and closing; Ford's diesels flag the injector's voltage or charge regulator at its limit (90 s), Ford's petrol direct injection its controlled-valve-operation adaptation at its limit.",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 8 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 8","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F6":["Cylinder 9 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F6 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 9 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0209, P0285 and P0286), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. Cylinder 9 exists only on engines with ten or more cylinders; GM and Ford documents stop at cylinder 8, so the description follows CARB and the documented checks for cylinders 1–8 (current fall time, voltage feedback, regulator at its limit).",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 9 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 9","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F7":["Cylinder 10 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F7 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 10 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0210, P0288 and P0289), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. Cylinder 10 exists only on engines with ten or more cylinders; GM and Ford documents stop at cylinder 8, so the description follows CARB and the documented checks for cylinders 1–8 (current fall time, voltage feedback, regulator at its limit).",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 10 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 10","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F8":["Cylinder 11 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F8 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 11 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0211, P0291 and P0292), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. Cylinder 11 exists only on engines with twelve or more cylinders; GM and Ford documents stop at cylinder 8, so the description follows CARB and the documented checks for cylinders 1–8 (current fall time, voltage feedback, regulator at its limit).",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 11 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 11","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02F9":["Cylinder 12 Injector Circuit Range/Performance","P02F9 reports an electrically implausible response of the cylinder 12 injector – not an open or short circuit (those are P0212, P0294 and P0295), but a current or voltage pattern outside the expected range. Cylinder 12 exists only on engines with twelve or more cylinders; GM and Ford documents stop at cylinder 8, so the description follows CARB and the documented checks for cylinders 1–8 (current fall time, voltage feedback, regulator at its limit).",[["h","Injector electrically aged (piezo capacitance, coil resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance in the injector 12 connector or wiring"],["m","Injector mechanically sticking (valve lift)"],["l","Injector driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, misfire on cylinder 12","Loss of power, smoke (diesel)"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P02FA":["Diesel Intake Air Flow Position Sensor Minimum/Maximum Stop Performance","The diesel intake throttle learns the position sensor values of its end stops, as a Toyota patent describes for the closed stop at engine stop. P02FA means a learned end-stop value is implausible.",[["h","Soot and oil deposits at flap and stop (EGR, crankcase ventilation)"],["m","Position sensor in the throttle unit worn or shifted"],["m","Flap or shaft bent, stop worn, gear worn"],["l","Wrong or not learned replacement part"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable driving problems","EGR control restricted, filter regeneration hindered"],["medium",[100,700]],"c"],"P02FB":["Cold Start Fuel Injection Control Circuit/Open","Cold start fuel injection is an extra injection system for cold starts – for example on flex-fuel engines running on high ethanol blends, where a Hyundai patent describes a small auxiliary tank, a cold-start injector and a heater. CARB lists such heated fuel preparation systems among the monitored output components.",[["h","Coil of the cold-start injector or valve open"],["m","Connector loose, corroded or pin pushed back"],["m","Broken wire in the harness"],["l","Fuse or relay of the cold-start system"]],["Check engine light on","Hard or no start in cold weather (especially with high ethanol)","Rough running right after a cold start"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P02FC":["Cold Start Fuel Injection Control Circuit Low","Cold start fuel injection is an extra injection system for cold starts – for example on flex-fuel engines running on high ethanol blends, where a Hyundai patent describes a small auxiliary tank, a cold-start injector and a heater. CARB lists such heated fuel preparation systems among the monitored output components.",[["h","Control wire chafed through, shorted to ground"],["m","Shorted turn in the cold-start injector or valve"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Hard cold start, or over-fuelling with fuel smell if the valve stays open","Rough running when cold"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P02FD":["Cold Start Fuel Injection Control Circuit High","Cold start fuel injection is an extra injection system for cold starts – for example on flex-fuel engines running on high ethanol blends, where a Hyundai patent describes a small auxiliary tank, a cold-start injector and a heater. CARB lists such heated fuel preparation systems among the monitored output components.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a supply wire (chafed or pinched)"],["m","Short in the cold-start injector or valve"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Hard or no start in cold weather (especially with high ethanol)"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P02FE":["Cold Start Fuel Injection Control Circuit Range/Performance","Cold start fuel injection is an extra injection system for cold starts – for example on flex-fuel engines running on high ethanol blends, where a Hyundai patent describes a small auxiliary tank, a cold-start injector and a heater. CARB lists such heated fuel preparation systems among the monitored output components.",[["h","Cold-start injector or valve worn, sticking or partly clogged"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or ground"],["m","Cold-start fuel supply faulty (auxiliary tank empty, pump, filter)"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Check engine light on","Long or failed cold start","Rough running when cold"],["medium",[50,450]],"u"],"P02FF":["Propulsion System Inactive Timer Performance","Many diagnostics need to know how long the engine – or, on hybrids, the whole propulsion system – has been off: thermostat and evaporative checks and the cold-start strategy, for which CARB requires at least 6 hours of standstill. A timer measures this time, and the engine module checks its plausibility, for example against coolant cooling – a GM patent describes such a check.",[["h","Time source in another module (instrument cluster, body module) gives an implausible time after a reset or voltage loss"],["h","Software level of engine, transmission or instrument cluster module"],["m","Battery discharged or disconnected, voltage dips"],["l","Instrument cluster or timer in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on, otherwise usually nothing noticeable","Clock in the instrument cluster wrong or reset","Readiness of standstill-dependent monitors incomplete"],["medium",[50,900]],"u"],"P0300":["Random/Multiple Cylinder Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","False air (vacuum leak, crankcase ventilation, intake gasket)"],["h","Worn or wrong spark plugs on several cylinders, faulty ignition coils"],["m","Fuel pressure too low or high, contaminated fuel"],["l","Valve timing (stretched chain), valves, compression on several cylinders"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["medium",[30,1500]],"c"],"P0301":["Cylinder 1 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 1 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 1 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 1 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 1 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0302":["Cylinder 2 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 2 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 2 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 2 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 2 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0303":["Cylinder 3 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 3 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 3 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 3 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 3 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0304":["Cylinder 4 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 4 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 4 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 4 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 4 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0305":["Cylinder 5 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 5 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 5 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 5 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 5 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0306":["Cylinder 6 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 6 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 6 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 6 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 6 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0307":["Cylinder 7 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 7 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 7 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 7 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 7 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0308":["Cylinder 8 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 8 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 8 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 8 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 8 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0309":["Cylinder 9 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 9 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 9 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 9 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 9 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P030A":["Ignition A Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition A is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P030B":["Ignition A Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition A is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P030C":["Ignition A Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition A is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P030D":["Ignition B Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition B is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P030E":["Ignition B Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition B is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P030F":["Ignition B Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition B is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P0310":["Cylinder 10 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 10 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 10 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 10 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 10 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0311":["Cylinder 11 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 11 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 11 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 11 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 11 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0312":["Cylinder 12 Misfire Detected","The engine module detects misfire from crankshaft speed: a cylinder that does not fire gives no acceleration impulse. At a catalyst-damaging rate (CARB: at least 5 % per 200 revolutions) the warning light flashes; at an emissions-relevant rate (at least 1 % per 1,000 revolutions – GM about 2–5 %, Ford 0.9–1.5 %) it comes on after two trips.",[["h","Ignition coil of cylinder 12 faulty (coil, connector, ground)"],["h","Spark plug of cylinder 12 worn, sooted, cracked or wrongly gapped"],["m","Injector of cylinder 12 clogged or leaking, or its control"],["l","Low compression on cylinder 12 (valve, rings, head gasket) or local false air"]],["Check engine light on, flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate","Rough idle, jerking, vibration","Loss of power, hesitation"],["easy",[30,800]],"c"],"P0313":["Misfire Detected with Low Fuel","When the tank is nearly empty the fuel pump can draw air in corners or on gradients, which causes misfires. CARB allows misfire detection to be switched off at 15 % fuel or less; some manufacturers instead report misfires detected in this state under P0313 rather than P030x.",[["h","Tank nearly empty – the fuel pump draws air (corners, gradients, braking)"],["m","Actual misfire cause (ignition, injector, false air) that happened at low fuel"],["m","Weak fuel pump, clogged strainer or filter, faulty swirl pot"],["l","Fuel level sender reading too low"]],["Check engine light on (flashing at a catalyst-damaging rate)","Jerking or power loss with an almost empty tank, especially in corners or uphill","Usually no symptoms after refuelling"],["easy",[0,600]],"c"],"P0314":["Single Cylinder Misfire (Cylinder Not Specified)","P0314 means the misfire is attributable to a single cylinder, but the module does not say which one. CARB generally requires cylinder-specific codes and allows a general code only with approval where the cylinder cannot be identified reliably; for diesels identification is required only where possible without extra hardware.",[["h","Injector of one cylinder clogged or leaking (diesel), or coil/plug (petrol)"],["m","Leaking high-pressure line, fuel bridge or injector washer"],["m","Air, water or petrol in the diesel, clogged filter, low supply pressure"],["l","Compression loss (valve, piston)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, limp mode with quantity limit (Saab diesel)","Rough running, vibration"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0315":["Crankshaft Position System Variation Not Learned","Manufacturing tolerances of the crankshaft tone wheel distort the speed fluctuations misfire detection relies on, so the engine module learns a correction for each tooth or segment. P0315 means these values have not been learned or are invalid.",[["h","Relearn not performed after replacing sensor, crankshaft, damper, engine or engine module"],["m","Learned values lost through voltage loss (battery disconnected)"],["m","Tone wheel damaged or shifted, crankshaft runout, dirt on the sensor"],["l","Interference in the crankshaft sensor circuit"]],["Check engine light on (GM; Ford depending on application)","Driving usually unaffected","Misfire readiness not set – emissions test fails"],["medium",[50,200]],"c"],"P0316":["Engine Misfire Detected on Startup (First 1000 Revolutions)","P0316 concerns misfire within the first 1,000 revolutions after start. CARB requires a pending code here after the first exceedance of the emissions-relevant rate (later in the drive only after the fourth) and the warning light if it recurs at the next start.",[["h","Moisture or water in the plug wells, coils arcing when cold or damp"],["m","Leaking injectors (fuel drips after switch-off, over-rich start)"],["m","Worn or sooted spark plugs that only fire cleanly when warm"],["l","Wrong temperature sensor value or engine software"]],["Check engine light (usually with P0300/P030x)","Rough running right after a cold start, then normal","Delayed start, fuel smell at start"],["medium",[30,1200]],"c"],"P0317":["Rough Road Hardware Not Present","Misfire detection works from crankshaft speed fluctuations, which rough roads also cause. CARB therefore allows misfire detection to pause on rough roads; many engines take a rough-road signal from the wheel speeds of the ABS module, a Ford patent describes such a detector.",[["h","Module configuration does not match the equipment (after engine or ABS module replacement)"],["m","ABS/ESP module not responding (supply, CAN line)"],["l","Separate rough-road sensor missing or unplugged, where fitted"],["l","Engine module faulty"]],["Warning light (manufacturer-dependent)","Misfire readiness not set","Possibly false misfire codes on rough roads"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0318":["Rough Road Sensor A Signal Circuit","Misfire detection works from crankshaft speed fluctuations, which rough roads also cause. CARB therefore allows misfire detection to pause on rough roads; many engines take a rough-road signal from the wheel speeds of the ABS module, a Ford patent describes such a detector.",[["h","Wheel speed sensor or wiring faulty – the brake module passes on the fault"],["m","Communication between engine and brake module lost (CAN, connector, supply)"],["l","Brake control module faulty"],["l","Separate rough-road sensor faulty, where fitted"]],["Service message (GM without warning light)","Often ABS or stability warning at the same time","Driving unaffected"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0319":["Rough Road Sensor B Signal Circuit","Misfire detection works from crankshaft speed fluctuations, which rough roads also cause. CARB therefore allows misfire detection to pause on rough roads; many engines take a rough-road signal from the wheel speeds of the ABS module, a Ford patent describes such a detector.",[["h","Signal source B or its wiring faulty"],["m","Communication fault to the supplying module (ABS, transmission)"],["l","Configuration or software"],["l","Engine module faulty"]],["Warning light (manufacturer-dependent, often none)","Possibly ABS or transmission codes","Driving unaffected"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P031A":["Ignition C Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition C is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P031B":["Ignition C Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition C is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P031C":["Ignition C Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition C is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P031D":["Ignition D Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition D is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P031E":["Ignition D Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition D is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P031F":["Ignition D Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition D is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P0320":["Ignition/Distributor Engine Speed Input Circuit","P0320 originally covered the engine speed signal from the distributor or ignition module to the engine module (early OBD II); today Ford's engine module evaluates the crankshaft sensor directly and uses this code for it. Ford checks continuously from 200–500 rpm and sets it within 5 s when the number of teeth after the gap is wrong, the tooth time is implausible (below 30 or above 9,000 rpm) or the gap appears in the wrong place; fuel and spark are then cut briefly to resynchronise, and on electronic throttle P2106 limits power.",[["h","Crankshaft or distributor speed sensor faulty or wrong air gap"],["h","Wiring, connector or shielding disturbed (loose contact, interference from ignition or radios)"],["m","Tone wheel damaged or dirty (missing tooth, metal chips)"],["m","Ignition module faulty on older vehicles"]],["Check engine light on","Engine dies or stumbles briefly","Starting problems, tachometer twitching"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0321":["Ignition/Distributor Engine Speed Input Circuit Range/Performance","P0321 belongs to the same signal as P0320 and P0322: the speed and reference input of the ignition, formerly the distributor's Hall pickup, today practically always the crankshaft position sensor. Range/Performance means a signal is present but implausible – wrong number of teeth between the reference gaps, erratic tooth times, or a speed that does not fit the camshaft signal.",[["h","Crankshaft position sensor faulty or aged (weak, noisy signal)"],["m","Sensor loose or air gap wrong"],["m","Tone wheel damaged or clogged with metal chips"],["m","Interference, poor ground or damaged wiring"]],["Check engine light on","Jerking, misfire or brief stalling","Hard starting, long cranking"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0322":["Ignition/Distributor Engine Speed Input Circuit No Signal","P0322 means the speed and reference input of the ignition – today the crankshaft position sensor – delivers no signal although the engine is turning. Ford proves the rotation with the camshaft sensor: if it shows more than one engine revolution without a crank signal (from 500 rpm, detected within 5 s), Ford sets P0322 or, depending on the engine, P0335; with electronic throttle the throttle software adds P2106 and goes into limp mode.",[["h","Crankshaft position sensor failed (often heat-related)"],["h","Open or short in signal, ground or 5 V supply, corroded connector"],["m","Sensor loose, air gap too large or tone wheel damaged"],["l","5 V reference pulled down by another sensor on the same supply"]],["Engine cranks but does not start","Engine stalls while driving","Tachometer shows zero"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0323":["Ignition/Distributor Engine Speed Input Circuit Intermittent","P0323 stands for a speed and reference signal at the ignition input – today the crankshaft position sensor – that drops out or is disturbed for moments and then recovers. None of the manufacturer documents available to us (GM 2014–2019, Ford 2012 and 2020–2023, Saab workshop information) uses P0323; the sporadic crank sensor check runs under P0339 at Ford and under P0336 at GM.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor connector"],["m","Chafed wiring, break under vibration"],["m","Sensor dropping out when hot"],["l","Interference from neighbouring wires"]],["Brief stumbling or stalling, then normal again","Sporadic misfire","Tachometer flickers"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P0324":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Control System Error","P0324 concerns the knock control as a whole rather than a single sensor circuit. GM and Saab's GM-based engines set it when excessive knock persists on one cylinder – knock despite ignition retard, or mechanical noise taken for knock – for more than 8–10 s; GM's 2018 engines in the first trip, older ones after two.",[["h","Genuine knock: low-octane fuel, carbon deposits, high intake or coolant temperature, lean mixture"],["m","Mechanical noise taken for knock (loose parts, brackets, valve train, bearings)"],["m","Knock sensor damaged, wrongly torqued or mounted with sealant"],["l","Engine software or calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power from ignition retard","Audible pinging or knocking under load"],["medium",[0,500]],"c"],"P0325":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor A Circuit","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor A is the first sensor (bank 1 or a single sensor).",[["h","Wiring or connector chafed, broken or corroded (sensor often under the intake manifold)"],["m","Knock sensor faulty (cracked piezo, moisture, heat damage)"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Engine software or diagnostic calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0326":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor A is the first sensor (bank 1 or a single sensor).",[["h","Sensor loose, wrongly torqued or mounted with sealant or on a dirty surface"],["m","Engine software or calibration (bulletins for some models)"],["m","Genuine knock on a whole bank or mechanical noise"],["l","Sensor aged or damaged (low sensitivity)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P0327":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor A Circuit Low","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor A is the first sensor (bank 1 or a single sensor).",[["h","Signal or return wire chafed to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in connector or sensor"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (series issue on some models)"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0328":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor A Circuit High","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor A is the first sensor (bank 1 or a single sensor).",[["h","Signal or return wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Corrosion or moisture in the connector"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0329":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor A is the first sensor (bank 1 or a single sensor).",[["h","Loose contact, spread pins or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Wire chafing only with engine movement or heat"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Sensor or engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P032A":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor C Circuit","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor C is a third sensor on engines with three or four knock sensors (documented on the McLaren 12C V8 and in Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Wiring or connector chafed, broken or corroded (sensor often under the intake manifold)"],["m","Knock sensor faulty (cracked piezo, moisture, heat damage)"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Engine software or diagnostic calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P032B":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor C is a third sensor on engines with three or four knock sensors (documented on the McLaren 12C V8 and in Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Sensor loose, wrongly torqued or mounted with sealant or on a dirty surface"],["m","Engine software or calibration (bulletins for some models)"],["m","Genuine knock on a whole bank or mechanical noise"],["l","Sensor aged or damaged (low sensitivity)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P032C":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor C Circuit Low","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor C is a third sensor on engines with three or four knock sensors (documented on the McLaren 12C V8 and in Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Signal or return wire chafed to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in connector or sensor"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (series issue on some models)"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P032D":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor C Circuit High","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor C is a third sensor on engines with three or four knock sensors (documented on the McLaren 12C V8 and in Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Signal or return wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Corrosion or moisture in the connector"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P032E":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor C Circuit Intermittent","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor C is a third sensor on engines with three or four knock sensors (documented on the McLaren 12C V8 and in Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Loose contact, spread pins or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Wire chafing only with engine movement or heat"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Sensor or engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P032F":["Combustion Engine Producing Insufficient Torque","P032F is a plausibility code: the engine module compares the torque the combustion engine actually delivers with the expected or requested torque. On hybrids the actual torque can be measured precisely through the generator; if it stays too low for a calibrated time, the code sets.",[["h","Internal mechanical engine damage (connecting rod bearings, crank drive)"],["m","Engine software (false setting)"],["m","Poor combustion (misfire, fuel or air shortage, low compression)"],["l","Increased internal friction (wrong oil, seizing parts)"]],["Check engine light and warning (Ford: wrench, stop safely)","Reduced power, combustion engine shut off","Knocking or metallic noise from the engine"],["hard",[100,9000]],"u"],"P0330":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor B Circuit","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor B is the second sensor – bank 2 on V engines, but on some four-cylinders also bank 1.",[["h","Wiring or connector chafed, broken or corroded (sensor often under the intake manifold)"],["m","Knock sensor faulty (cracked piezo, moisture, heat damage)"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Engine software or diagnostic calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0331":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor B is the second sensor – bank 2 on V engines, but on some four-cylinders also bank 1.",[["h","Sensor loose, wrongly torqued or mounted with sealant or on a dirty surface"],["m","Engine software or calibration (bulletins for some models)"],["m","Genuine knock on a whole bank or mechanical noise"],["l","Sensor aged or damaged (low sensitivity)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P0332":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor B Circuit Low","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor B is the second sensor – bank 2 on V engines, but on some four-cylinders also bank 1.",[["h","Signal or return wire chafed to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in connector or sensor"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (series issue on some models)"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0333":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor B Circuit High","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor B is the second sensor – bank 2 on V engines, but on some four-cylinders also bank 1.",[["h","Signal or return wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Corrosion or moisture in the connector"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0334":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor B Circuit Intermittent","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor B is the second sensor – bank 2 on V engines, but on some four-cylinders also bank 1.",[["h","Loose contact, spread pins or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Wire chafing only with engine movement or heat"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Sensor or engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P0335":["Crankshaft Position Sensor A Circuit","The crankshaft position sensor reads a tone wheel with a reference gap (GM 58X, i.e. 60-2; Ford 36-1, 40-1 or 60-2) and gives the engine module speed and crank angle for injection and ignition.",[["h","Crankshaft position sensor faulty (heat, age – typical hot-start failure)"],["h","Connector or wiring corroded, chafed, oil-soaked or open"],["m","5 V reference shorted by another sensor on the same supply"],["m","Tone wheel damaged, loose or clogged with chips; air gap wrong after engine work"]],["Engine cranks but does not start","Engine stalls while driving or at idle","Hot-start problem: starts again after cooling down"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0336":["Crankshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0336 means the crankshaft position sensor delivers pulses, but their pattern is implausible: the reference gap is missing or in the wrong place, the number of teeth between the gaps is wrong (58 on a 60-2 wheel) or the module keeps losing synchronisation. GM sets it after at least 4 resynchronisations in under 5 s, a gap not found within 0.4 s running or 1.5 s cranking, or a wrong tooth count in 8 of 10 revolutions, after two trips.",[["h","Tone wheel damaged, bent, loose or teeth clogged with chips"],["h","Sensor signal weak or noisy (air gap, ageing)"],["m","Interference, poor ground or shielding"],["m","Wrong tone wheel or flywheel fitted after a repair"]],["Misfire, jerking, rough running","Engine stalls sporadically","Hard starting"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0337":["Crankshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Low","By its J2012 name P0337 means the crankshaft position signal stays at a low level without changing state – signal wire shorted to ground, missing supply on a Hall sensor or a sensor stuck low. Few manufacturers use the code.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or open"],["h","Crankshaft position sensor faulty (output stuck low)"],["m","Sensor supply missing or shorted"],["m","Connector corroded or pin pushed back"]],["Engine cranks but does not start","Engine stalls while driving or at idle","Tachometer drops to zero or flickers"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0338":["Crankshaft Position Sensor A Circuit High","By its J2012 name P0338 means the crankshaft position signal stays at a high level – signal wire shorted to supply, or an open signal output that the module's pull-up holds high on a Hall sensor. GM 2014–2019 and Ford do not use the code.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to supply or open (pull-up holds the level high)"],["m","Interference pulses from poor ground or shielding"],["m","Crankshaft position sensor faulty"],["l","Tone wheel damaged or dirty (gap not recognised)"]],["Misfire, jerking","Engine stalls or starts poorly","Limp mode"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0339":["Crankshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","P0339 means the crankshaft position signal drops out or is disturbed for moments and then recovers. Ford's 2020–2023 petrol engines use it, depending on the engine, instead of P0320 for tooth errors: wrong tooth count after the gap, tooth time outside 30 to 9,000 rpm, gap at an unexpected position, from 500 rpm, detected within 5 s.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor connector or an in-line connector"],["m","Sensor dropping out when hot"],["m","Sensor mounting or air gap mechanically wrong (machining error, bracket)"],["l","Electrical interference"]],["Sporadic jerking or misfire","Brief stalling, hunting idle","Hard starting"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P033A":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor D Circuit","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor D is a fourth sensor on engines with four knock sensors (McLaren 12C V8, Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Wiring or connector chafed, broken or corroded (sensor often under the intake manifold)"],["m","Knock sensor faulty (cracked piezo, moisture, heat damage)"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Engine software or diagnostic calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P033B":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor D Circuit Range/Performance","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor D is a fourth sensor on engines with four knock sensors (McLaren 12C V8, Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Sensor loose, wrongly torqued or mounted with sealant or on a dirty surface"],["m","Engine software or calibration (bulletins for some models)"],["m","Genuine knock on a whole bank or mechanical noise"],["l","Sensor aged or damaged (low sensitivity)"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P033C":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor D Circuit Low","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor D is a fourth sensor on engines with four knock sensors (McLaren 12C V8, Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Signal or return wire chafed to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in connector or sensor"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (series issue on some models)"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P033D":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor D Circuit High","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor D is a fourth sensor on engines with four knock sensors (McLaren 12C V8, Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Signal or return wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Corrosion or moisture in the connector"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P033E":["Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor D Circuit Intermittent","The knock sensor is a piezoelectric structure-borne sound sensor bolted to the engine block; the module evaluates its signal per cylinder and retards ignition when it detects knock. Sensor D is a fourth sensor on engines with four knock sensors (McLaren 12C V8, Ford's 2020–23 systems).",[["h","Loose contact, spread pins or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Wire chafing only with engine movement or heat"],["m","Sensor loose or wrongly torqued"],["l","Sensor or engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power, sluggish response from ignition retard","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P033F":["Crankshaft/Camshaft Loss Of Synchronization","To know which stroke each cylinder is in, the engine module matches the crank angle with the camshaft phase. P033F means this synchronisation is lost while running or cannot be established.",[["h","Noisy or disturbed crankshaft signal (sensor, air gap, interference)"],["m","Disturbed camshaft signal"],["m","Timing chain or belt jumped or heavily stretched"],["m","Wiring or connector"]],["Jerking, brief loss of power","Engine stalls, hard starting","Misfire detection inactive"],["medium",[80,1500]],"u"],"P0340":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Bank 1 or Single Sensor","The camshaft position sensor, almost always a Hall sensor, tells the engine module which stroke cylinder 1 is in – one crank revolution alone is ambiguous – and measures the actual cam phase with variable timing. Sensor A is the intake camshaft, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Camshaft position sensor faulty (heat, moisture)"],["h","Connector or wiring corroded or open, 5 V reference disturbed"],["m","Tone wheel on the camshaft or phaser damaged, loose or at the wrong distance"],["m","Timing drive jumped or wrongly assembled"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["easy",[40,300]],"c"],"P0341":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1 or Single Sensor","P0341 means the intake camshaft sensor of bank 1 (or the only cam sensor) delivers pulses, but their number or pattern does not fit the crankshaft. GM counts the pulses while the crank is in sync: 4 to 6 in the first 12 reference events and 398 to 402 per 100 engine cycles on the 4X wheel of the cam phaser; outside that in 8 of 10 checks the code is set after two trips.",[["h","Sensor with interference pulses or weak signal, air gap"],["m","Tone wheel on the phaser damaged or wrongly fitted"],["m","Connector or wiring fault (corrosion, interference)"],["l","Crankshaft signal disturbed (wrong reference)"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P0342":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Low Bank 1 or Single Sensor","P0342 means the signal of the intake camshaft sensor of bank 1 (or the only cam sensor) stays at ground level without edges – signal wire shorted to ground, sensor supply missing or the Hall sensor faulty. Ford's 2023 diesel sets it when the signal stays at 0 V over 10 or more crank revolutions (3 s), while cranking or running.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","5 V or 12 V supply of the sensor missing"],["m","Camshaft position sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or wiring chafed"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0343":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit High Bank 1 or Single Sensor","P0343 means the signal of the intake camshaft sensor of bank 1 (or the only cam sensor) stays at supply level without edges. Causes are a signal wire shorted to positive, an open signal wire that the module's pull-up holds at 5 V, a broken sensor ground or a faulty sensor.",[["h","Signal wire open or shorted to positive"],["m","Sensor ground open"],["m","Camshaft position sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0344":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Intermittent Bank 1 or Single Sensor","P0344 means the signal of the intake camshaft sensor of bank 1 (or the only cam sensor) is disturbed for moments: edges are missing or appear at an unexpected angle. Ford checks this on the five-tooth wheel used with variable cam timing; if edges appear where none are expected, Ford sets P0344 and switches cam timing off (loss of power and torque), from 200 rpm, detected within 5 s.",[["h","Moisture or oil inside the sensor, dropping out when hot"],["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Tone wheel on the phaser damaged or loose"],["m","Interference or chafed wiring"]],["Check engine light on","Sporadic loss of power (cam timing off)","Jerking, rough idle"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0345":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Bank 2","P0345 is the bank 2 counterpart of P0340: the intake camshaft sensor on the side without cylinder 1 – only on V and boxer engines with one cam sensor per bank – delivers no or too few pulses while the crankshaft turns in sync. GM uses the same criteria as for P0340, e.g.",[["h","Camshaft position sensor bank 2 faulty"],["h","Connector or wiring of bank 2 corroded or open"],["m","Tone wheel or phaser of bank 2 damaged"],["l","Timing drive of bank 2 jumped"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0346":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2","P0346 is the bank 2 counterpart of P0341: the intake camshaft sensor on the side without cylinder 1 delivers pulses, but their number or pattern does not fit the crankshaft. GM applies the same counting criteria as for P0341, e.g.",[["h","Bank 2 sensor with disturbed signal, air gap"],["m","Tone wheel on the bank 2 phaser damaged or wrongly fitted"],["m","Connector, wiring or interference"],["l","Crankshaft reference signal disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P0347":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Low Bank 2","P0347 is the bank 2 counterpart of P0342: the signal of the intake camshaft sensor on the side without cylinder 1 stays at ground level without edges – signal wire shorted to ground, sensor supply missing or the sensor faulty. A code-specific manufacturer description exists only from GM: on the Saab 9-4X V6 (GM LAU) the signal must stay low without pulses for more than 1 s, or 10 s cumulatively, and the code follows after two trips.",[["h","Bank 2 signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply missing"],["m","Camshaft position sensor bank 2 faulty"],["m","Connector or wiring damaged"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["medium",[30,350]],"c"],"P0348":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit High Bank 2","P0348 is the bank 2 counterpart of P0343: the signal of the intake camshaft sensor on the side without cylinder 1 stays at supply level without edges. Causes are a signal wire shorted to positive, an open signal wire held high by the module's pull-up, a broken sensor ground or a faulty sensor.",[["h","Bank 2 signal wire open or shorted to positive"],["m","Sensor ground open"],["m","Camshaft position sensor bank 2 faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power (cam timing held in its base position)"],["medium",[30,350]],"c"],"P0349":["Camshaft Position Sensor A Circuit Intermittent Bank 2","P0349 is the bank 2 counterpart of P0344: the signal of the intake camshaft sensor on the side without cylinder 1 is disturbed for moments – edges are missing or appear at an unexpected angle. Ford checks this on the five-tooth wheel used with variable cam timing; if edges appear where none are expected, Ford sets P0349 and switches cam timing on bank 2 off, from 200 rpm, detected within 5 s.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the bank 2 connector"],["m","Sensor dropping out when hot or due to oil moisture"],["m","Tone wheel of bank 2 damaged"],["l","Interference or chafed wiring"]],["Check engine light on","Sporadic loss of power (cam timing off)","Rough running"],["medium",[30,350]],"c"],"P034A":["Crankshaft Position Sensor - Crankshaft Start Position Incorrect","Engines with start-stop or hybrid autostart store the crankshaft rest position when the engine stops, so that they can inject and fire correctly at once on restart. This needs a direction-sensing crank sensor, because a cylinder under compression can push the crankshaft backwards after shut-off.",[["h","Direction-sensing crankshaft position sensor faulty or aged"],["m","Interference pulses in the sensor circuit during shut-off"],["m","Air gap or tone wheel – teeth not detected at low speed"],["l","Engine module software"]],["Check engine light on","Start-stop restricted or off","Delayed or rough autostart"],["medium",[80,500]],"u"],"P034B":["Crankshaft Position Sensor - Crankshaft Direction Incorrect","Direction-sensing (bidirectional) crank sensors count the rest position for start-stop even when the crankshaft rocks back after shut-off. In normal running the sensor must never report reverse rotation – if it does, the signal is disturbed.",[["h","Interference in the sensor circuit (wiring routed next to switching lines)"],["m","Direction-sensing crankshaft position sensor faulty"],["m","Ground or connector fault"],["l","Tone wheel or air gap"]],["Check engine light on","Low idle or stalling after an autostart","Start-stop off, possibly reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P034C":["Ion Sense System Performance","With ion current sensing the spark plug serves as a sensor after the spark: a measuring voltage across the gap drives a current through the ions in the flame, from which the module detects combustion, misfire and knock – a Delphi patent describes coils with built-in ion sensing, and Saab's Trionic engines detected misfire this way. P034C means this measuring system works implausibly, so misfire and knock detection are no longer reliable.",[["h","Engine software or calibration"],["m","Spark plugs sooted, oily or wrong type or gap"],["m","Ignition coil or cassette with ion circuit faulty"],["l","Wiring or connector of the ion current return line"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or reduced power possible","Often nothing noticeable otherwise"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P0350":["Ignition Coil Primary/Secondary Circuit/Open","P0350 is the general ignition coil circuit code without assignment to a coil: the engine module detects a fault in the primary or secondary circuit of a coil but does not report which one. This typically concerns older systems with a shared output stage or ignition module, or a shared feedback line for all coils.",[["h","Ignition coil, coil pack or ignition module faulty"],["m","Shared supply (fuse, relay, ignition feed) or ground open"],["m","Harness, connector or feedback line to the coils faulty"],["l","Ignition lead or plug connector damaged (secondary side)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, rough running","Starting problems or stalling if a shared output stage fails"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0351":["Ignition Coil A Primary Control Circuit/Open","Modern engines have one coil-on-plug ignition coil per cylinder, with the output stage either in the engine module or in the coil itself. P0351 monitors the primary control circuit of coil A – the line from the engine module to the coil or its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance.",[["h","Ignition coil A faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, loose or with a spread pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Fuse or relay of the coil supply (then usually several codes)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 1, often also P0301","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0352":["Ignition Coil B Primary Control Circuit/Open","Modern engines have one coil-on-plug ignition coil per cylinder, with the output stage either in the engine module or in the coil itself. P0352 monitors the primary control circuit of coil B – the line from the engine module to the coil or its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance.",[["h","Ignition coil B faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, loose or with a spread pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Fuse or relay of the coil supply (then usually several codes)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 2, often also P0302","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0353":["Ignition Coil C Primary Control Circuit/Open","Modern engines have one coil-on-plug ignition coil per cylinder, with the output stage either in the engine module or in the coil itself. P0353 monitors the primary control circuit of coil C – the line from the engine module to the coil or its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance.",[["h","Ignition coil C faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, loose or with a spread pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Fuse or relay of the coil supply (then usually several codes)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 3, often also P0303","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0354":["Ignition Coil D Primary Control Circuit/Open","Modern engines have one coil-on-plug ignition coil per cylinder, with the output stage either in the engine module or in the coil itself. P0354 monitors the primary control circuit of coil D – the line from the engine module to the coil or its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance.",[["h","Ignition coil D faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, loose or with a spread pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Fuse or relay of the coil supply (then usually several codes)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 4, often also P0304","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0355":["Ignition Coil E Primary Control Circuit/Open","Modern engines have one coil-on-plug ignition coil per cylinder, with the output stage either in the engine module or in the coil itself. P0355 monitors the primary control circuit of coil E – the line from the engine module to the coil or its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance.",[["h","Ignition coil E faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, loose or with a spread pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Fuse or relay of the coil supply (then usually several codes)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 5, often also P0305","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0356":["Ignition Coil F Primary Control Circuit/Open","P0356 monitors the primary control circuit of ignition coil F – the line from the engine module to the coil or to its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance. Coil F usually serves cylinder 6, so the code only occurs on engines with at least 6 cylinders.",[["h","Ignition coil F faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, wet or with a loose pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty or software (permanent dwell)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 6, often also P0306","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0357":["Ignition Coil G Primary Control Circuit/Open","P0357 monitors the primary control circuit of ignition coil G – the line from the engine module to the coil or to its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance. Coil G usually serves cylinder 7, so the code only occurs on engines with at least 7 cylinders.",[["h","Ignition coil G faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, wet or with a loose pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty or software (permanent dwell)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 7, often also P0307","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0358":["Ignition Coil H Primary Control Circuit/Open","P0358 monitors the primary control circuit of ignition coil H – the line from the engine module to the coil or to its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance. Coil H usually serves cylinder 8, so the code only occurs on engines with at least 8 cylinders.",[["h","Ignition coil H faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, wet or with a loose pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty or software (permanent dwell)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 8, often also P0308","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0359":["Ignition Coil I Primary Control Circuit/Open","P0359 monitors the primary control circuit of ignition coil I – the line from the engine module to the coil or to its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance. Coil I usually serves cylinder 9, so the code only occurs on engines with at least 9 cylinders.",[["h","Ignition coil I faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, wet or with a loose pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty or software (permanent dwell)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 9, often also P0309","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P035A":["Ignition E Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition E is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P035B":["Ignition E Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition E is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P035C":["Ignition E Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition E is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P035D":["Ignition F Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition F is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P035E":["Ignition F Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition F is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P035F":["Ignition F Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition F is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P0360":["Ignition Coil J Primary Control Circuit/Open","P0360 monitors the primary control circuit of ignition coil J – the line from the engine module to the coil or to its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance. Coil J usually serves cylinder 10, so the code only occurs on engines with at least 10 cylinders.",[["h","Ignition coil J faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, wet or with a loose pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty or software (permanent dwell)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 10, often also P0310","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0361":["Ignition Coil K Primary Control Circuit/Open","P0361 monitors the primary control circuit of ignition coil K – the line from the engine module to the coil or to its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance. Coil K usually serves cylinder 11, so the code only occurs on engines with at least 11 cylinders.",[["h","Ignition coil K faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, wet or with a loose pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty or software (permanent dwell)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 11, often also P0311","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0362":["Ignition Coil L Primary Control Circuit/Open","P0362 monitors the primary control circuit of ignition coil L – the line from the engine module to the coil or to its built-in output stage – for an open circuit or high resistance. Coil L usually serves cylinder 12, so the code only occurs on engines with at least 12 cylinders.",[["h","Ignition coil L faulty (output stage in the coil or primary winding open)"],["h","Connector at the coil corroded, wet or with a loose pin"],["m","Control, supply or ground wire open or chafed"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty or software (permanent dwell)"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire and rough running on cylinder 12, often also P0312","Loss of power, jerking under load"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0363":["Misfire Detected - Fueling Disabled","P0363 is a reaction code: the engine module has detected misfire severe enough to damage the catalyst – through its crankshaft-based misfire monitor – and has therefore shut off injection to the affected cylinder or cylinders. CARB allows exactly this: a vehicle that cuts fuel immediately on catalyst-damaging misfire may turn the check engine light on steadily instead of flashing it, and may switch fuel back on to re-check at most every 30 s.",[["h","Ignition coil or spark plug of the affected cylinder faulty"],["m","Injector sticking or leaking, or fuel pressure too low"],["m","Engine module software (misfire detection, cold-start mixture)"],["l","Mechanical cause: loss of compression, valve or carbon deposits, vacuum leak"]],["Check engine light on steadily instead of flashing","Clearly rough running, one cylinder without power","Loss of power"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0364":["DC/AC Converter Control Module Control Circuit Range/Performance","By its J2012 name P0364 means the control circuit to the control module of a DC/AC converter (inverter) behaves implausibly – the command or enable line is present but its signal does not fit what the engine module expects. The code is a late assignment in the P03xx block: older code lists show the slot as reserved, and the other DC/AC converter codes sit in the P2DAx range (P2DA8–P2DAB).",[["m","Control or enable line to the converter module disturbed (connector, wiring)"],["m","Converter control module faulty internally"],["l","Software of the modules involved"]],["Warning lamp depending on the manufacturer","Converter function restricted (for example power socket or hybrid drive)"],["hard",[150,2000]],"u"],"P0365":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Bank 1","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor (bank 1 = side with cylinder 1), usually a Hall sensor on the exhaust cam phaser. P0365 means no or too few exhaust cam pulses arrive while the crankshaft turns in sync.",[["h","Exhaust camshaft position sensor faulty"],["h","Connector or wiring open or corroded, 5 V reference missing"],["m","Oil in the connector from a leaking sensor O-ring"],["l","Tone wheel on the exhaust phaser damaged or loose"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power or rough idle (exhaust cam timing in its base position)"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P0366":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor (bank 1 = side with cylinder 1), usually a Hall sensor on the exhaust cam phaser. P0366 means its pulses arrive, but their number or position does not fit the crankshaft.",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty (metal debris on the magnet)"],["m","Wiring close to ignition coils or solenoids, loose contact"],["m","Tone wheel on the phaser damaged or loose, air gap too large"],["l","Sensor or phaser wrongly fitted after timing drive work"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power or rough idle (exhaust cam timing in its base position)"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0367":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Low Bank 1","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor (bank 1 = side with cylinder 1), usually a Hall sensor on the exhaust cam phaser. The engine module holds the signal line at about 5 V and the sensor pulls it to ground with each tooth.",[["h","Signal wire chafed through to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Oil or water in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power or rough idle (exhaust cam timing in its base position)"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0368":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit High Bank 1","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor (bank 1 = side with cylinder 1), usually a Hall sensor on the exhaust cam phaser. P0368 means the signal line stays high without edges.",[["h","Connector off or corroded, signal wire open"],["m","5 V reference or ground of the sensor open"],["m","Sensor faulty (output does not switch)"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking before the engine starts","Loss of power or rough idle (exhaust cam timing in its base position)"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0369":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Intermittent Bank 1","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor (bank 1 = side with cylinder 1), usually a Hall sensor on the exhaust cam phaser. P0369 means its signal is basically present, but now and then a cam pulse arrives at a crank angle where none is expected, or pulses drop out.",[["h","Loose contact in connector or wiring"],["m","Interference from the ignition (routing, shielding)"],["m","Sensor with metal debris or borderline air gap"],["l","Tone wheel or phaser damaged"]],["Check engine light on","Sporadic jerking or stalling","Occasional starting problems"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P036A":["Ignition G Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition G is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P036B":["Ignition G Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition G is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P036C":["Ignition G Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition G is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P036D":["Ignition H Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition H is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P036E":["Ignition H Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition H is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P036F":["Ignition H Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition H is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P0370":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal A","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal, to time and meter injection precisely – the high resolution signal. GM's 6.5-litre diesel (1997) had an optical sensor in the injection pump that delivers a high resolution track and one cam reference pulse per cylinder; P0370 is set if more than 8 consecutive cam pulses pass without progress on the high resolution counter, in the first trip.",[["h","Optical sensor in the injection pump faulty (6.5-litre)"],["m","Wiring or connector between pump or sensor and module"],["m","Crankshaft sensor or tone wheel (LB7: 57X signal)"],["l","Injector driver module, engine module or CAN communication"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0371":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal A Too Many Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0371 means signal A delivers more pulses than expected within the reference window – for example more high resolution pulses per reference pulse than the nominal ratio, which on GM's 6.5-litre diesel is 64 ± 4 per cam pulse; the usual cause is interference pulses.",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["m","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0372":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal A Too Few Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0372 means signal A delivers fewer pulses than expected within the reference window – marks are missing, for example from a weak signal, a dirty sensor disc or too large an air gap; on GM's 6.5-litre diesel the nominal ratio is 64 ± 4 pulses per cam pulse.",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["m","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0373":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal A Intermittent/Erratic Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0373 means signal A delivers pulses with erratic spacing or with occasional dropouts – typical of a loose contact or electrical interference.",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["m","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0374":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal A No Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal. By its J2012 name P0374 means signal A delivers no pulses at all although the engine turns.",[["h","Sensor (pump or crankshaft) faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["m","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Injector driver module or engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0375":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal B","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0375 means a second high resolution signal, B, has a general fault or is missing; it is checked in the same way as signal A (P0370 ff).",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["l","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0376":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal B Too Many Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0376 means a second high resolution signal, B, delivers more pulses than expected within the reference window; it is checked in the same way as signal A (P0370 ff).",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["l","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0377":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal B Too Few Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0377 means a second high resolution signal, B, delivers fewer pulses than expected within the reference window; it is checked in the same way as signal A (P0370 ff).",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["l","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0378":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal B Intermittent/Erratic Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0378 means a second high resolution signal, B, delivers pulses with erratic spacing or occasional dropouts; it is checked in the same way as signal A (P0370 ff).",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["l","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0379":["Timing Reference High Resolution Signal B No Pulses","Some diesel systems give the injection control a finely divided angle signal in addition to the normal reference signal – the high resolution signal – for precise injection timing and metering. P0379 means a second high resolution signal, B, delivers no pulses at all although the engine turns; it is checked in the same way as signal A (P0370 ff).",[["h","Sensor faulty or dirty"],["m","Loose contact or interference in the signal wire"],["l","Sensor disc or tone wheel damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (limp-home fuelling)","Rough running, hard starting, smoke"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P037A":["Glow Plug/Heater Indicator Control Circuit Low","Diesel engines show the driver a wait-to-start lamp while the glow plugs preheat; the engine module switches it with a high-side driver. P037A means the lamp control circuit is too low – in practice a short to ground on the line to the instrument cluster.",[["h","Lamp control wire chafed through to ground between engine module and cluster"],["m","Lamp or LED driver in the instrument cluster shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or cluster connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Glow plug light does not come on or stays dim","Check engine light on (GM after two trips)","Driver starts too early, possibly poor cold start"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P037B":["Glow Plug/Heater Indicator Control Circuit High","P037B is the counterpart of P037A: the control circuit of the wait-to-start lamp is too high, i.e. the line carries voltage although the engine module's driver is off, or the driver sees a short to battery voltage.",[["h","Lamp control wire chafed against battery or ignition positive"],["m","Fault in the instrument cluster feeding voltage back"],["m","Water damage in a connector bridging to a neighbouring pin"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Glow plug light stays on or flickers","Check engine light on (after two trips)","No recognisable preheat indication"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P037C":["Cold Start Camshaft Profile System Performance","To heat the catalyst quickly after a cold start, some engines select a particular camshaft profile; CARB counts altered valve timing among these cold-start measures and requires components of the cold-start strategy to have their own diagnostics. P037C checks whether the requested profile is actually engaged during this phase.",[["h","Engine module software (GM L3B, reprogramming)"],["m","Profile actuator (sliding cam pin) sticking or not switching"],["m","Actuator circuit (connector, wiring)"],["l","Wrong oil viscosity or low oil level, mechanism stiff when cold"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running or stalling when cold (for example on remote start)","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P037D":["Glow Plug Sense Circuit","To check the glow plugs, the engine module reads back the voltage or current of the glow plug supply through a sense circuit – CARB requires glow plug circuits to be monitored for correct current and voltage drop, and from model year 2010 the failure of a single plug to be detected. In the J2012 scheme P037D is the general code for this sense circuit, P037E and P037F its low and high counterparts.",[["h","Sense wire open or high resistance, connector corroded"],["m","Glow relay or glow plug control module giving an implausible feedback"],["m","Fuse or fusible link of the glow plug supply blown"],["l","Engine module internal"]],["Check engine light or glow plug light on","Possibly poor cold start if the cause also affects the glow plug supply","Further glow plug codes stored"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P037E":["Glow Plug Sense Circuit Low","P037E means the voltage read back from the glow plug supply is too low. GM's 2016/2018 diesels, whose glow plug output stages sit in the engine module, compare the feedback with the battery voltage while glowing: if it is lower by more than a calibrated, current-dependent threshold – excessive voltage drop or a short to ground of the sense line – a sample counts as faulty, and 60 of 120 samples in a 50 ms grid set the code, roughly 3 of 6 s.",[["h","High contact resistance in the glow plug supply (connector, ground, fusible link)"],["m","Measuring circuit or output stage in the engine module"],["m","Sense or supply wire shorted to ground"],["l","Weak battery or vehicle voltage at the edge of the enable range"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly hard starting or knocking when cold","Further glow plug codes"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P037F":["Glow Plug Sense Circuit High","P037F means the voltage read back from the glow plug supply is too high. GM's 2016/2018 diesels set it when the feedback lies above a threshold derived from the run/crank relay voltage, with a feedback above 5.0 V – for example voltage is reported although the plugs are not energised, or the sense line is shorted to positive.",[["h","Sense wire chafed against positive or bridged in the connector"],["m","Glow relay sticking or output stage shorted (supply stays on)"],["m","Measuring circuit in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Glow plugs possibly energised permanently (flat battery, plug damage)","Glow plug light implausible"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0380":["Glow Plug/Heater Circuit A","P0380 concerns glow plug circuit A – depending on the manufacturer the first group of plugs, glow relay 1 or feedback line A of the glow plug control module. Saab's 3.0 TiD (9-5, 2002) has two feedback lines from the control module; if line A at the engine module stays above 4.0 V for more than 3 s while glowing, the module is reporting a plug or circuit fault, checked once per drive cycle with the warning lamp on.",[["h","Glow plug(s) in circuit A burnt out"],["m","Glow plug control module or glow relay faulty"],["m","Wiring or connector fault (feedback line, plug connector, supply)"],["l","Missing battery or ignition supply of the control module (fuse)"]],["Hard starting when cold, long cranking","White or blue smoke and rough running after a cold start","Glow plug light or check engine light on or flashing"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0381":["Glow Plug/Heater Indicator Control Circuit/Open","P0381 monitors the circuit of the wait-to-start lamp for an open circuit or, more generally, for faults that keep the lamp dark when commanded – CARB names a burnt-out bulb as the example, with exemptions for LCD clusters and for engines that only release the starter after preheating. GM's 2015–2018 diesels detect an open circuit in the driver by a high load impedance (about 200 kΩ in 2015), in 0.2 s or 10 of 15 samples, with the check engine light after two trips; on the 2014 Duramax LML P0381 still covered all lamp circuit faults including a short to ground.",[["h","Control wire open or connector fault between engine module and cluster"],["m","Lamp or LED in the instrument cluster burnt out"],["l","Instrument cluster faulty or communication problem (Ford)"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Glow plug light does not come on at key-on","Check engine light on (GM after two trips)","Driver starts too early in the cold"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0382":["Glow Plug/Heater Circuit B","P0382 is the counterpart of P0380 for glow plug circuit B – depending on the manufacturer the second group of plugs, glow relay 2 or feedback line B of the glow plug control module. A code-specific manufacturer description exists from Saab: the 3.0 TiD V6 diesel in the 9-5 (2002) reads two feedback lines from its glow plug control module, and if line B stays above 4.0 V for more than 3 s while glowing, the module is reporting a fault in the second group of plugs; the check runs once per drive cycle, the warning lamp comes on and there is no further system reaction.",[["h","Glow plug(s) in circuit B burnt out"],["m","Glow plug control module or glow relay 2 faulty"],["m","Feedback line B, plug connector or busbar faulty"]],["Hard starting when cold, long cranking","White or blue smoke and rough running after a cold start","Glow plug light or check engine light on or flashing"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0383":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 Control Circuit Low","Many diesels have a separate glow plug control module that the engine module commands over a control line. P0383 means this line is too low.",[["h","Control line between engine module and glow plug module shorted to ground or open"],["m","Corroded connector at the glow plug module in the engine bay"],["m","Glow plug control module faulty (input pulls the line down)"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["No preheating, poor cold start","Smoke after a cold start","Warning lamp depending on the manufacturer"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0384":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 Control Circuit High","P0384 is the counterpart of P0383: the control line from the engine module to the glow plug control module is too high, i.e. shorted to positive.",[["h","Control line chafed against positive or bridged in the connector"],["m","Glow plug control module faulty internally (feeding back)"],["m","Ford: supply of the module outside 6.5–16.5 V or open (battery, alternator, fuse)"],["l","Engine module"]],["No or irregular preheating, hard starting when cold","Smoke after a cold start","Warning lamp depending on the manufacturer"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0385":["Crankshaft Position Sensor B Circuit","Some engines have a second crankshaft sensor or a second track on the tone wheel, which the engine module checks against sensor A or the camshaft. P0385 means its signal is missing or does not match.",[["h","Crankshaft position sensor B faulty"],["h","Connector or wiring open or corroded"],["l","Tone wheel or second track damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking","Tachometer flickers, rough running"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0386":["Crankshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0386 means the signal of the second crankshaft sensor or second tone wheel track is present but implausible. GM's 2004 Northstar 4.6 V8 sets it after 5 resynchronisations within 10 s, after two trips.",[["h","Interference pulses (wiring near the ignition, shielding)"],["m","Sensor dirty or air gap wrong"],["m","Tone wheel damaged"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Jerking, misfire","Longer cranking"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0387":["Crankshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Low","By its J2012 name P0387 means the signal of the second crankshaft sensor stays at a low level – typically a short to ground of the signal wire, a missing or shorted sensor supply, or with an inductive sensor an amplitude that is too small. No manufacturer document available to us uses P0387: the engines with a sensor B that we found (GM Northstar 4.6 and 3.8-litre V6, the Honda 3.5-litre in the Saturn Vue Redline) report its electrical faults under P0385 and implausible signals under P0386.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Sensor supply too low or shorted"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking","Rough running, no start if sensor A also fails"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0388":["Crankshaft Position Sensor B Circuit High","Some engines have a second crankshaft sensor or a second track on the tone wheel – GM's 3.4/3.5/3.8-litre V6 use the low resolution track of a dual Hall sensor, the Honda 3.5-litre in the Saturn Vue Redline a second sensor – which the engine module checks against sensor A or the camshaft. By its J2012 name P0388 means the signal of sensor B stays above its upper limit – with a Hall sensor typically an open signal wire held high by the module's pull-up, or a short to positive.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor B open or connector corroded or loose"],["m","Signal wire shorted to positive or reference voltage"],["m","Sensor ground open"],["m","Sensor B faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Longer cranking or starting problems","Possibly rough running or misfire"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0389":["Crankshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Intermittent","Some engines have a second crankshaft sensor or a second track on the tone wheel – GM's 3.4/3.5/3.8-litre V6 use the low resolution track of a dual Hall sensor, the Honda 3.5-litre in the Saturn Vue Redline a second sensor – which the engine module checks against sensor A or the camshaft. By its J2012 name P0389 means the signal of sensor B drops out for moments or becomes erratic – missing or extra edges – without staying permanently low or high.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor B connector"],["h","Wire break or chafing that appears with engine movement"],["m","Electrical interference (add-on devices, routing near ignition or relays)"],["m","Tone wheel damaged or dirty, air gap too large"]],["Check engine light on, possibly only after several trips","Occasional stalling or jerking","Sporadic starting problems"],["medium",[40,350]],"c"],"P038A":["Ignition I Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition I is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P038B":["Ignition I Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition I is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P038C":["Ignition I Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition I is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P038D":["Ignition J Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition J is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Ignition module or coil with integrated driver of this group faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the ignition module faulty"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P038E":["Ignition J Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition J is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Input of the ignition module or coil shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P038F":["Ignition J Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition J is one of the control signal lines from the engine module to an ignition module, ignition output stage or group of coils; which cylinders it serves is defined by the manufacturer, not by the code standard. The code concerns the logic-level control signal, not the coil's primary current (that has its own codes, P0351 ff and P2300 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Ignition module or coil faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver stage in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Misfire, jerking, rough idle","Loss of power, possibly cylinders shut off"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P0390":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Bank 2","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor; bank 2 is the side without cylinder 1, so the code only occurs on V and boxer engines with one exhaust cam sensor per bank. P0390 means no or too few exhaust cam pulses arrive from bank 2 while the crankshaft turns.",[["h","Connector or wiring of the bank 2 exhaust sensor open or corroded (signal, 5 V, ground)"],["h","Camshaft position sensor faulty"],["m","Oil contamination or leak at the sensor, sensor loose"],["m","Tone wheel on the phaser damaged or wrongly fitted after timing chain work"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (bank 2 exhaust cam timing in its base position)","Longer cranking"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0391":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor; bank 2 is the side without cylinder 1, so the code only occurs on V and boxer engines with one exhaust cam sensor per bank. P0391 means the bank 2 exhaust cam signal is present, but the number or position of its pulses does not fit the crankshaft.",[["h","Tone wheel on the bank 2 exhaust phaser damaged, loose or wrongly fitted"],["m","Interference or loose contact producing extra edges"],["m","Sensor loose, wrong O-ring or gap, chips on the magnet"],["m","Timing chain stretched or jumped"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (bank 2 exhaust cam timing in its base position)","Longer cranking"],["medium",[60,1200]],"c"],"P0392":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Low Bank 2","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor; bank 2 is the side without cylinder 1, so the code only occurs on V and boxer engines with one exhaust cam sensor per bank. P0392 means the bank 2 exhaust cam signal stays low without edges – typically a short to ground of the signal wire, a sensor output stuck low or a missing 5 V reference.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (output stuck low)"],["m","5 V reference missing (shorted by another sensor on the same circuit)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (bank 2 exhaust cam timing in its base position)","Longer cranking"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0393":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit High Bank 2","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor; bank 2 is the side without cylinder 1, so the code only occurs on V and boxer engines with one exhaust cam sensor per bank. P0393 means the bank 2 exhaust cam signal stays high without edges.",[["h","Signal wire open or connector off"],["m","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power (bank 2 exhaust cam timing in its base position)","Longer cranking"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0394":["Camshaft Position Sensor B Circuit Intermittent Bank 2","Camshaft position sensor B is the exhaust camshaft sensor; bank 2 is the side without cylinder 1, so the code only occurs on V and boxer engines with one exhaust cam sensor per bank. P0394 means the bank 2 exhaust cam signal now and then appears at a position where it is not expected relative to the crank reference – sporadic extra or missing edges.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor connector or harness"],["m","Interference (ignition wiring, add-on devices, ground problems)"],["m","Tone wheel or phaser damaged, or sensor loose"],["m","Sensor faulty, temperature-dependent"]],["Check engine light on","Sporadic jerking or stalling","Occasionally longer cranking"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P0395":["Cylinder 1 Pressure Sensor Circuit","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 1.",[["h","Connector at the pressure-sensing glow plug loose or corroded"],["m","Reference, signal or ground wire open or shorted"],["m","Sensor electronics in the glow plug faulty"],["l","Engine module (reference voltage or input)"]],["Check engine light on (often after two trips)","Usually barely noticeable – combustion control for the cylinder is switched off","Possibly rougher running, more noise or slightly higher consumption"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P0396":["Cylinder 1 Pressure Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor in the glow plug drifting (deposits, ageing)"],["m","Glow plug leaking or wrongly torqued (pressure transmitted wrongly)"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or ground"],["l","Actual compression pressure differs (valve, rings)"]],["Check engine light on","Combustion control for the cylinder off – slightly rough running, more noise","Possibly more smoke or consumption"],["medium",[120,500]],"u"],"P0397":["Cylinder 1 Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 1.",[["h","5 V reference wire open or shorted to ground"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor electronics of the glow plug faulty"],["l","Engine module supplies no reference"]],["Check engine light on (often after two trips)","Usually barely noticeable – combustion control for the cylinder is switched off","Possibly rougher running, more noise or slightly higher consumption"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P0398":["Cylinder 1 Pressure Sensor Circuit High","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 1.",[["h","Connector off or loose, signal wire open"],["m","Sensor ground wire open"],["m","Sensor in the glow plug faulty"],["l","Signal or reference wire shorted to positive"]],["Check engine light on (often after two trips)","Usually barely noticeable – combustion control for the cylinder is switched off","Possibly rougher running, more noise or slightly higher consumption"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P0399":["Cylinder 1 Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code con