"u"],"P03D7":["Cylinder 7 Pressure Too High","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 7.",[["m","Injector delivering too much or dripping"],["m","Sensor drifting upwards (diesel pressure-sensing glow plug)"],["l","Knock or pre-ignition on a petrol engine"]],["EPC or check engine light on","Misfire codes, possibly a cylinder shut off with noticeable loss of power","Rough running"],["medium",[80,800]],"u"],"P03D8":["Cylinder 7 Pressure Variation Low","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 7.",[["h","Pressure-sensing glow plug faulty or coked (glow rod stuck)"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or ground"],["l","Actual compression loss or misfire in the cylinder"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Possibly rough running or combustion noise because control is off"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P03D9":["Cylinder 7 Pressure Variation High","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 7.",[["h","Sensor in the glow plug faulty (signal noise)"],["m","Loose contact or interference on the signal wire"],["l","Combustion anomaly (dripping injector, harsh combustion)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Possibly rough running or combustion noise because control is off"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P03DA":["Cylinder 7 Combustion Performance","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 7.",[["h","Injector coked, leaking or delivering the wrong quantity"],["m","Compression loss (valve, rings)"],["m","Partial combustion or misfire (fuel quality, EGR distribution)"],["l","Pressure sensor drifting"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, vibration","Loss of power, more smoke and consumption"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P03DB":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Sensor Circuit","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","Connector at the pressure-sensing glow plug loose or corroded"],["m","Reference, signal or ground wire open or shorted"],["m","Sensor electronics in the glow plug faulty"],["l","Engine module (reference voltage or input)"]],["Check engine light on (often after two trips)","Usually barely noticeable – combustion control for the cylinder is switched off","Possibly rougher running, more noise or slightly higher consumption"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P03DC":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","Sensor in the glow plug drifting (deposits, ageing)"],["m","Glow plug leaking or wrongly torqued (pressure transmitted wrongly)"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or ground"],["l","Actual compression pressure differs (valve, rings)"]],["Check engine light on","Combustion control for the cylinder off – slightly rough running, more noise","Possibly more smoke or consumption"],["medium",[120,500]],"u"],"P03DD":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","5 V reference wire open or shorted to ground"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor electronics of the glow plug faulty"],["l","Engine module supplies no reference"]],["Check engine light on (often after two trips)","Usually barely noticeable – combustion control for the cylinder is switched off","Possibly rougher running, more noise or slightly higher consumption"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P03DE":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Sensor Circuit High","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","Connector off or loose, signal wire open"],["m","Sensor ground wire open"],["m","Sensor in the glow plug faulty"],["l","Signal or reference wire shorted to positive"]],["Check engine light on (often after two trips)","Usually barely noticeable – combustion control for the cylinder is switched off","Possibly rougher running, more noise or slightly higher consumption"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P03DF":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","Loose contact at the glow plug connector"],["m","Chafing or broken wire in the engine harness"],["m","Sensor electronics of the glow plug unstable with temperature"],["l","Interference from ground problems"]],["Check engine light on","Occasionally rough running","Often no symptoms, the code comes and goes"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P03E0":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Too Low","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["m","Compression loss (valve, piston rings, leaking glow plug seat)"],["m","Injector delivering too little or coked"],["m","Sensor drifting downwards (deposits)"],["l","Misfire or partial combustion"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, rough running, possibly misfire on this cylinder","More smoke"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P03E1":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Too High","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["m","Injector delivering too much or dripping"],["m","Sensor drifting upwards (diesel pressure-sensing glow plug)"],["l","Knock or pre-ignition on a petrol engine"]],["EPC or check engine light on","Misfire codes, possibly a cylinder shut off with noticeable loss of power","Rough running"],["medium",[80,800]],"u"],"P03E2":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Variation Low","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","Pressure-sensing glow plug faulty or coked (glow rod stuck)"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or ground"],["l","Actual compression loss or misfire in the cylinder"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Possibly rough running or combustion noise because control is off"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P03E3":["Cylinder 8 Pressure Variation High","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","Sensor in the glow plug faulty (signal noise)"],["m","Loose contact or interference on the signal wire"],["l","Combustion anomaly (dripping injector, harsh combustion)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Possibly rough running or combustion noise because control is off"],["medium",[100,450]],"u"],"P03E4":["Cylinder 8 Combustion Performance","Some diesels have a combustion pressure sensor in the glow plug; the engine module uses it to control injection for each cylinder. This code concerns cylinder 8.",[["h","Injector coked, leaking or delivering the wrong quantity"],["m","Compression loss (valve, rings)"],["m","Partial combustion or misfire (fuel quality, EGR distribution)"],["l","Pressure sensor drifting"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running, vibration","Loss of power, more smoke and consumption"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P03E5":["A Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Circuit Bank 1","On some engines the intake cam phaser (camshaft position actuator A) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. P03E5 is the general circuit code for this sensor: the module sees an open circuit or a signal outside its valid window on the supply, signal or ground line.",[["h","Connector or harness to position sensor C open or corroded"],["m","Position sensor C faulty"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed (also by another sensor on the same circuit)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or higher consumption"],["medium",[80,450]],"u"],"P03E6":["A Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Range/Performance Bank 1","On some engines the intake cam phaser (camshaft position actuator A) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. P03E6 means the sensor signal is electrically valid but implausible – for example the number or position of its edges per engine cycle does not match the crankshaft sensor, it jumps, or it lies outside the phaser's adjustment range.",[["h","Sensor dirty (metal debris on the magnet) or air gap wrong"],["m","Phaser or sensor target damaged or wrongly fitted (timing)"],["m","Loose contact or interference"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or higher consumption"],["medium",[80,800]],"u"],"P03E7":["A Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Circuit Low Bank 1","On some engines the intake cam phaser (camshaft position actuator A) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. P03E7 means the sensor signal stays below its lower limit – signal or reference wire shorted to ground, supply open, or the sensor shorted internally; if the 5 V reference is the cause, other sensors on the same circuit often set codes too.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed harness)"],["m","5 V supply open or shorted"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or higher consumption"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P03E8":["A Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Circuit High Bank 1","On some engines the intake cam phaser (camshaft position actuator A) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. P03E8 means the sensor signal stays above its upper limit – typically an open sensor ground, a signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage, or a faulty sensor.",[["h","Sensor ground open or connector corroded"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or vehicle voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or higher consumption"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P03E9":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator Position Sensor A Exceeded Learning Limit","Engines with cam profile switching move the intake camshaft between lift profiles with actuators – for example pins that shift a sliding cam – and position sensors report which profile is engaged. The engine module learns reference values of position sensor A, such as its end-position levels or the expected pulse position relative to the crankshaft and camshaft sensors; a Ford patent describes correcting this position for the cam phase.",[["h","Position sensor A or actuator with integrated sensor worn or faulty (signal drift)"],["m","Actuator pin or sliding piece stiff, guide worn, dirty oil"],["m","Actuator not relearned or wrongly fitted after replacement"],["l","Connector or wiring with contact resistance"]],["Check engine light on","Profile switching disabled: less power at high revs or higher consumption","Possibly further profile actuator or sensor codes"],["hard",[150,900]],"u"],"P03EA":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator Position Sensor B Exceeded Learning Limit","Engines with cam profile switching move the intake camshaft between lift profiles with actuators – for example pins that shift a sliding cam – and position sensors report which profile is engaged. The engine module learns reference values of position sensor B, such as its end-position levels or the expected pulse position relative to the crankshaft and camshaft sensors; a Ford patent describes correcting this position for the cam phase.",[["h","Position sensor B or actuator with integrated sensor worn or faulty (signal drift)"],["m","Actuator pin or sliding piece stiff, guide worn, dirty oil"],["m","Actuator not relearned or wrongly fitted after replacement"],["l","Connector or wiring with contact resistance"]],["Check engine light on","Profile switching disabled: less power at high revs or higher consumption","Possibly further profile actuator or sensor codes"],["hard",[150,900]],"u"],"P03EB":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator Position Sensor C Exceeded Learning Limit","Engines with cam profile switching move the intake camshaft between lift profiles with actuators – for example pins that shift a sliding cam – and position sensors report which profile is engaged. The engine module learns reference values of position sensor C, such as its end-position levels or the expected pulse position relative to the crankshaft and camshaft sensors; a Ford patent describes correcting this position for the cam phase.",[["h","Position sensor C or actuator with integrated sensor worn or faulty (signal drift)"],["m","Actuator pin or sliding piece stiff, guide worn, dirty oil"],["m","Actuator not relearned or wrongly fitted after replacement"],["l","Connector or wiring with contact resistance"]],["Check engine light on","Profile switching disabled: less power at high revs or higher consumption","Possibly further profile actuator or sensor codes"],["hard",[150,900]],"u"],"P03EC":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Circuit/Open Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the intake camshaft (camshaft A) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Actuator connector loose, corroded or pin pushed back (often after valve cover work)"],["m","Actuator solenoid open"],["m","Wire broken in the engine harness"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Lift switching and cylinder deactivation blocked, higher consumption","Possibly rough running if a cam piece stays in the wrong position"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P03ED":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Circuit Low Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the intake camshaft (camshaft A) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Shorted turns in the actuator solenoid"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Lift switching and cylinder deactivation blocked, higher consumption","Possibly rough running if a cam piece stays in the wrong position"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P03EE":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Circuit High Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the intake camshaft (camshaft A) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Short between supply and control pin in the connector (water, corrosion)"],["m","Internal short in the actuator"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Lift switching and cylinder deactivation blocked, higher consumption","Possibly rough running if a cam piece stays in the wrong position"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P03EF":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Performance/Stuck Off Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the intake camshaft (camshaft A) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Actuator faulty inside: pin extends uncommanded (weak return spring)"],["m","Actuator connectors swapped, or intake and exhaust actuators fitted to the wrong camshaft"],["m","Interference in the Hall feedback signal (ground, connector)"],["l","Cam piece sticking or shift groove worn"]],["Check engine light on","Ticking or rattling at the camshaft carrier","Misfire, rough idle or less power in the wrong lift profile"],["medium",[150,1500]],"u"],"P03F0":["A Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Stuck On Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the intake camshaft (camshaft A) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Pin sticking in the actuator (sludge, wear)"],["m","Return ramp of the shift groove damaged or cam piece sticking"],["m","Solenoid stays energized (control wire shorted to ground)"],["l","Hall feedback faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Ticking or rattling at the camshaft carrier","Misfire, rough idle or less power in the wrong lift profile"],["hard",[200,2500]],"u"],"P03F1":["B Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Circuit/Open Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the exhaust camshaft (camshaft B) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Actuator connector loose, corroded or pin pushed back (often after valve cover work)"],["m","Actuator solenoid open"],["m","Wire broken in the engine harness"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Lift switching and cylinder deactivation blocked, higher consumption","Possibly rough running if a cam piece stays in the wrong position"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P03F2":["B Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Circuit Low Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the exhaust camshaft (camshaft B) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Shorted turns in the actuator solenoid"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Lift switching and cylinder deactivation blocked, higher consumption","Possibly rough running if a cam piece stays in the wrong position"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P03F3":["B Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Circuit High Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the exhaust camshaft (camshaft B) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Short between supply and control pin in the connector (water, corrosion)"],["m","Internal short in the actuator"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Lift switching and cylinder deactivation blocked, higher consumption","Possibly rough running if a cam piece stays in the wrong position"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P03F4":["B Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Performance/Stuck Off Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the exhaust camshaft (camshaft B) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Actuator faulty inside: pin extends uncommanded (weak return spring)"],["m","Actuator connectors swapped, or intake and exhaust actuators fitted to the wrong camshaft"],["m","Interference in the Hall feedback signal (ground, connector)"],["l","Cam piece sticking or shift groove worn"]],["Check engine light on","Ticking or rattling at the camshaft carrier","Misfire, rough idle or less power in the wrong lift profile"],["medium",[150,1500]],"u"],"P03F5":["B Camshaft Profile Actuator C Control Stuck On Bank 1","With a sliding camshaft, the exhaust camshaft (camshaft B) carries movable cam pieces with several lift profiles; solenoid actuators shoot a pin into a groove in the piece and shift it axially. Actuator C is the third of these actuators – not a profile position.",[["h","Pin sticking in the actuator (sludge, wear)"],["m","Return ramp of the shift groove damaged or cam piece sticking"],["m","Solenoid stays energized (control wire shorted to ground)"],["l","Hall feedback faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Ticking or rattling at the camshaft carrier","Misfire, rough idle or less power in the wrong lift profile"],["hard",[200,2500]],"u"],"P03F6":["B Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Circuit Bank 1","On some engines the exhaust cam phaser (camshaft position actuator B) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. This is a different family from the sliding-cam position sensors (P2C05 ff).",[["h","Connector or harness to position sensor C open or corroded"],["m","Position sensor C faulty"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Exhaust cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or poorer emissions"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P03F7":["B Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Range/Performance Bank 1","On some engines the exhaust cam phaser (camshaft position actuator B) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. This is a different family from the sliding-cam position sensors (P2C05 ff).",[["h","Sensor signal implausible (drift, loose target or magnet)"],["m","Phaser stiff, actual position deviates"],["m","Loose contact or interference"],["l","Valve timing wrong after a repair"]],["Check engine light on","Exhaust cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or poorer emissions"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P03F8":["B Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Circuit Low Bank 1","On some engines the exhaust cam phaser (camshaft position actuator B) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. This is a different family from the sliding-cam position sensors (P2C05 ff).",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or 5 V supply open"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Exhaust cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or poorer emissions"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P03F9":["B Camshaft Position Actuator Position Sensor C Circuit High Bank 1","On some engines the exhaust cam phaser (camshaft position actuator B) has its own position sensors, which tell the engine module where the phaser actually is; sensor C is the third of them, bank 1 the side with cylinder 1. This is a different family from the sliding-cam position sensors (P2C05 ff).",[["h","Sensor ground open or signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector wet or corroded"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Exhaust cam phasing held in its base position, loss of power","Possibly rough idle or poorer emissions"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P03FA":["Ignition K Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition K is one of up to twelve control signal lines (A to L) from the engine module to an ignition coil with integrated output stage or to an ignition module – plausibly the 11th channel, but the code standard leaves the assignment to the manufacturer. The code concerns this logic-level signal, not the coil's primary circuit (coil K has its own codes, P0361 and P2330 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Coil with integrated output stage or ignition module faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the coil or module missing"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on, may flash with severe misfire","Misfire on the cylinder of this channel, rough running","Loss of power, cylinder possibly shut off"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P03FB":["Ignition K Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition K is one of up to twelve control signal lines (A to L) from the engine module to an ignition coil with integrated output stage or to an ignition module – plausibly the 11th channel, but the code standard leaves the assignment to the manufacturer. The code concerns this logic-level signal, not the coil's primary circuit (coil K has its own codes, P0361 and P2330 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafed harness)"],["m","Input of the coil output stage shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on, may flash with severe misfire","Misfire on the cylinder of this channel, rough running","Loss of power, cylinder possibly shut off"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P03FC":["Ignition K Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition K is one of up to twelve control signal lines (A to L) from the engine module to an ignition coil with integrated output stage or to an ignition module – plausibly the 11th channel, but the code standard leaves the assignment to the manufacturer. The code concerns this logic-level signal, not the coil's primary circuit (coil K has its own codes, P0361 and P2330 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to battery voltage or another supply"],["m","Coil output stage faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on, may flash with severe misfire","Misfire on the cylinder of this channel, rough running","Loss of power, cylinder possibly shut off"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P03FD":["Ignition L Control Signal Circuit/Open","Ignition L is one of up to twelve control signal lines (A to L) from the engine module to an ignition coil with integrated output stage or to an ignition module – plausibly the 12th channel, but the code standard leaves the assignment to the manufacturer. The code concerns this logic-level signal, not the coil's primary circuit (coil L has its own codes, P0362 and P2333 ff).",[["h","Control line or connector pin open (corrosion, pushed-back pin)"],["m","Coil with integrated output stage or ignition module faulty"],["m","Supply or ground of the coil or module missing"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on, may flash with severe misfire","Misfire on the cylinder of this channel, rough running","Loss of power, cylinder possibly shut off"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P03FE":["Ignition L Control Signal Circuit Low","Ignition L is one of up to twelve control signal lines (A to L) from the engine module to an ignition coil with integrated output stage or to an ignition module – plausibly the 12th channel, but the code standard leaves the assignment to the manufacturer. The code concerns this logic-level signal, not the coil's primary circuit (coil L has its own codes, P0362 and P2333 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to ground (chafed harness)"],["m","Input of the coil output stage shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on, may flash with severe misfire","Misfire on the cylinder of this channel, rough running","Loss of power, cylinder possibly shut off"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P03FF":["Ignition L Control Signal Circuit High","Ignition L is one of up to twelve control signal lines (A to L) from the engine module to an ignition coil with integrated output stage or to an ignition module – plausibly the 12th channel, but the code standard leaves the assignment to the manufacturer. The code concerns this logic-level signal, not the coil's primary circuit (coil L has its own codes, P0362 and P2333 ff).",[["h","Control line shorted to battery voltage or another supply"],["m","Coil output stage faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on, may flash with severe misfire","Misfire on the cylinder of this channel, rough running","Loss of power, cylinder possibly shut off"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P0400":["EGR A Flow","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P0400 is the general flow fault of EGR system A, used where the module does not classify the error as too little (P0401) or too much (P0402).",[["h","EGR passages or valve carboned up"],["m","EGR valve stiff or faulty"],["m","Leak in the intake or EGR pipe"],["l","MAP or MAF sensor inaccurate"]],["Check engine light on","Pinging under load or rough idle, depending on direction","Higher NOx, emission test failed"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0401":["EGR A Flow Insufficient Detected","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P0401 means too little exhaust is recirculated.",[["h","EGR passages, valve or cooler carboned up"],["h","EGR valve faulty or stuck closed"],["m","Ford: DPFE sensor or its hoses blocked, cracked or swapped"],["m","Diesel: air mass meter drifting or air leak after the turbo"]],["Check engine light on","Pinging or knocking under load (petrol)","Higher NOx, emission test failed"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0402":["EGR A Flow Excessive Detected","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P0402 means too much exhaust is recirculated, or exhaust flows although the valve should be closed – CARB explicitly requires a leaking valve to be detected.",[["h","EGR valve stuck open, dirt under the seat"],["m","Ford: DPFE sensor drifting or hose wrong"],["m","Diesel: air leak between air mass meter and turbo, MAF drift"],["l","Valve control opens unintentionally"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle or stalling (petrol)","Jerking at part load"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0403":["EGR A Control Circuit/Open","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P0403 concerns the electrical drive of the EGR valve – solenoid, stepper motor, DC motor or vacuum regulator.",[["h","Connector at the EGR valve corroded or loose"],["m","Broken control or supply wire"],["m","Coil or motor in the EGR valve burnt out"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off: pinging (petrol) or higher NOx","Usually no loss of power"],["easy",[30,600]],"c"],"P0404":["EGR A Control Circuit Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P0404 is the function check of the valve drive: on valves with a position sensor the module compares commanded and actual position, and a persistent deviation means the valve sticks, is coked or its drive is too weak (GM diesel 2018 above 10 %, GM petrol 2004 above 15 %, both MIL after two trips).",[["h","Valve coked or stiff"],["m","Valve drive (motor, gear) worn"],["m","Position sensor in the valve inaccurate"],["l","Supply voltage too low or ground fault"]],["Check engine light on","EGR deactivated","Rough idle or jerking if the valve hangs open"],["medium",[100,700]],"c"],"P0405":["EGR Sensor A Circuit Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR sensor A is usually the valve position sensor with a 5 V supply; on Ford until about 2011 it was the DPFE differential pressure sensor.",[["h","Wire or connector to the EGR valve open or shorted to ground"],["m","Position sensor in the EGR valve faulty (usually one unit with the valve)"],["m","Older Ford: DPFE sensor faulty"],["l","Shared 5 V reference shorted"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off","Diesel: possibly reduced power"],["easy",[30,600]],"c"],"P0406":["EGR Sensor A Circuit High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR sensor A is usually the valve position sensor; on Ford until about 2011 the DPFE differential pressure sensor.",[["h","Sensor ground or return wire open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or positive"],["m","Sensor in the EGR valve faulty"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off","Diesel: possibly reduced power"],["easy",[30,600]],"c"],"P0407":["EGR Sensor B Circuit Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. What EGR sensor B is depends on the manufacturer – on systems with a second valve or pressure sensor it is that second sensor.",[["h","Sensor wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded (near coolant or exhaust)"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR or EGR cooler protection possibly restricted"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0408":["EGR Sensor B Circuit High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. What EGR sensor B is depends on the manufacturer – on systems with a second valve or pressure sensor it is that second sensor.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector off"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded (near coolant or exhaust)"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR or EGR cooler protection possibly restricted"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0409":["EGR Sensor A Circuit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR sensor A is usually the valve position sensor (on older Fords the DPFE pressure sensor).",[["h","Connector or wire of the position sensor open or loose"],["m","EGR valve with integrated sensor faulty"],["l","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["easy",[30,600]],"c"],"P040A":["EGR Temperature Sensor A Circuit","EGR temperature sensor A measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Connector or wire at the sensor (heat, corrosion)"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P040B":["EGR Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","EGR temperature sensor A measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Sensor sluggish, tip sooted"],["m","Sensor drifting or faulty"],["m","No EGR flow (valve or cooler blocked), sensor sees no heat"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P040C":["EGR Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","EGR temperature sensor A measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed on the exhaust)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Water in the connector"],["l","Actual extreme gas temperature (EGR cooler faulty)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P040D":["EGR Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","EGR temperature sensor A measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Connector off, broken wire or open circuit"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["m","Ground wire open or short to positive"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P040E":["EGR Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","EGR temperature sensor A measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Loose contact in the connector or chafed wire"],["m","Sensor with an internal hairline crack"],["m","Corroded pins"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","No change in driving"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P040F":["EGR Temperature Sensor A/B Correlation","The two EGR temperature sensors measure the gas temperature in the EGR path, before and after the cooler – which is A and which is B depends on the maker. P040F compares sensors A and B: after a long stop (GM: at least 8 hours) both must read about ambient temperature.",[["h","One sensor drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at one sensor's connector"],["l","Connectors swapped after a repair"],["l","Unrecognised heat source (auxiliary heater)"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","No change in driving"],["easy",[50,300]],"c"],"P0410":["AIR System A","After a cold start the secondary air (AIR) pump blows fresh air behind the exhaust valves so that unburnt fuel burns off and the catalyst heats up faster; a shut-off valve opens the path and stops exhaust from flowing back. P0410 is the general function code for secondary air system A.",[["h","Secondary air pump failed (water ingress, seized)"],["h","Combination or shut-off valve carboned up or stuck"],["m","Vacuum hose or electric changeover valve leaking or faulty"],["m","Pump relay or fuse faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)","Pump silent after a cold start, or unusually loud"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P0411":["AIR System A Incorrect Flow Detected","After a cold start the secondary air (AIR) pump blows fresh air behind the exhaust valves so that unburnt fuel burns off and the catalyst heats up faster; a shut-off valve opens the path and stops exhaust from flowing back. P0411 means the measured air flow does not match the expected value.",[["h","Pump weak or failed (water, filter debris)"],["h","Combination valve partly stuck or check valve faulty"],["m","Hoses blocked, leaking or off; ducts in the head carboned up"],["l","Pressure sensor port blocked or sensor drifting (pressure-sensor systems)"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)","Pump silent after a cold start, or unusually loud"],["medium",[80,1000]],"c"],"P0412":["AIR System Switching Valve A Circuit","The switching valve is the electrically driven valve of this path – a solenoid valve, or on Pierburg/VW systems an electric changeover valve that applies vacuum to the pneumatic combination valve; valve A serves the first path or bank 1, valve B the second. P0412 is the general electrical fault in the control circuit of valve A, detected by comparing the driver's feedback with its commanded state.",[["h","Switching valve A faulty (coil open or shorted, water ingress)"],["h","Connector corroded or wire open or chafed"],["m","Valve supply missing (fuse, main relay)"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air inactive","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[40,350]],"c"],"P0413":["AIR System Switching Valve A Circuit Open","The switching valve is the electrically driven valve of this path – a solenoid valve, or on Pierburg/VW systems an electric changeover valve that applies vacuum to the pneumatic combination valve; valve A serves the first path or bank 1, valve B the second. P0413 means the control circuit of valve A is open: with the driver switched off the module does not see the expected supply or pull-up level.",[["h","Wire to the valve open or connector loose or corroded"],["h","Valve coil burnt out"],["m","Fuse of the valve supply blown"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air inactive","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0414":["AIR System Switching Valve A Circuit Shorted","The switching valve is the electrically driven valve of this path – a solenoid valve, or on Pierburg/VW systems an electric changeover valve that applies vacuum to the pneumatic combination valve; valve A serves the first path or bank 1, valve B the second. P0414 means the control circuit of valve A is shorted.",[["h","Valve coil with shorted turns (water ingress)"],["m","Wire chafed against ground or supply"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air inactive","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0415":["AIR System Switching Valve B Circuit","The switching valve is the electrically driven valve of this path – a solenoid valve, or on Pierburg/VW systems an electric changeover valve that applies vacuum to the pneumatic combination valve; valve A serves the first path or bank 1, valve B the second. P0415 is the general electrical fault in the control circuit of valve B, the second valve on two-valve or two-bank systems such as V engines or Subaru's turbo boxer.",[["h","Switching valve B faulty"],["h","Wire or connector to valve B faulty"],["m","Valve supply or fuse"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air inactive on the second bank","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[40,350]],"c"],"P0416":["AIR System Switching Valve B Circuit Open","The switching valve is the electrically driven valve of this path – a solenoid valve, or on Pierburg/VW systems an electric changeover valve that applies vacuum to the pneumatic combination valve; valve A serves the first path or bank 1, valve B the second. P0416 means the control circuit of valve B is open – the module does not see the expected level with its driver switched off.",[["h","Wire or connector to valve B open"],["h","Coil of valve B burnt out"],["m","Shared supply of both valves faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air inactive on bank 2","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0417":["AIR System Switching Valve B Circuit Shorted","The switching valve is the electrically driven valve of this path – a solenoid valve, or on Pierburg/VW systems an electric changeover valve that applies vacuum to the pneumatic combination valve; valve A serves the first path or bank 1, valve B the second. P0417 means the control circuit of valve B is shorted.",[["h","Coil of valve B with shorted turns (water ingress)"],["m","Wire chafed against ground or supply"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Secondary air inactive on bank 2","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0418":["AIR System Control A Circuit","After a cold start the secondary air (AIR) pump blows fresh air behind the exhaust valves so that unburnt fuel burns off and the catalyst heats up faster; a shut-off valve opens the path and stops exhaust from flowing back. Control A is the drive circuit of pump A – in practice the coil of the pump relay, which the module switches to ground; the old wording was Relay A.",[["h","Pump relay faulty (coil open, contacts burnt or welded)"],["h","Control wire or connector to the relay open or corroded"],["m","Fuse blown, often due to a seized pump"],["m","Pump stiff or blocked by water or debris"]],["Check engine light on","Pump does not run after a cold start – or runs constantly if the relay is welded","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[20,900]],"c"],"P0419":["AIR System Control B Circuit","After a cold start the secondary air (AIR) pump blows fresh air behind the exhaust valves so that unburnt fuel burns off and the catalyst heats up faster; a shut-off valve opens the path and stops exhaust from flowing back. Control B is the drive circuit of a second pump or pump relay, found only on systems with two pumps or relays, for example large V engines with one pump per bank.",[["h","Pump relay B faulty"],["h","Wire or connector to relay B faulty"],["m","Fuse or supply"],["l","Pump B blocked (consequential damage)"]],["Check engine light on","Second pump does not run","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["easy",[20,700]],"c"],"P041A":["EGR Temperature Sensor B Circuit","EGR temperature sensor B measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Connector or wire at the sensor"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P041B":["EGR Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","EGR temperature sensor B measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Sensor drifting or sluggish (soot)"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["m","No EGR flow (GM stuck test)"],["l","Comparison sensor (intake or charge air temperature) faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P041C":["EGR Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","EGR temperature sensor B measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture in the connector"],["l","Actual overtemperature (cooler or coolant problem)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P041D":["EGR Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","EGR temperature sensor B measures the gas temperature in the EGR path – for cooler monitoring, component protection and the EGR model. Whether it sits before or after the cooler depends on the maker: GM diesels have sensor A after and B before the cooler, Ford diesels sensor B after it.",[["h","Connector off or broken wire"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["m","Ground open or short to positive"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Substitute value used, EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P041E":["EGR Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR temperature sensor B is the second temperature sensor in the EGR path; on GM diesels it sits before the cooler, on Ford diesels after it.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor connector"],["m","Harness chafed or broken (heat at the EGR pipe)"],["m","Sensor cracked internally (thermal cycling)"],["l","Engine module or ground connection"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","Usually no noticeable symptom","Further EGR temperature or cooler checks suspended"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P041F":["AIR System Switching Valve A Circuit Low","After a cold start the secondary air pump blows fresh air into the exhaust; switching valve A is the electrically driven valve that opens this path. GM checks the valve circuit in three codes – P0412 open, P041F shorted to ground, P044F shorted to positive – while other makers use P0413/P0414 or P0412 for everything.",[["h","Control wire chafed against ground"],["m","Water or corrosion in the valve connector"],["m","Valve coil shorted to its housing"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on (after two trips)","No change in driving","Secondary air readiness not set, possibly P0411"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0420":["Catalyst System Efficiency Below Threshold Bank 1","The engine module checks whether the catalyst of bank 1 still converts enough. On petrol engines it estimates the oxygen storage capacity by comparing the sensors before and after the catalyst – a good catalyst smooths the rear signal.",[["h","Catalyst aged or poisoned (oil consumption, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Catalyst overheated by misfire or rich running"],["m","Rear oxygen sensor sluggish, dirty or unsuitable"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of or at the rear sensor"]],["Check engine light on, otherwise often no change in driving","Emission test failed (higher HC, CO, NOx)","Rattling or loss of power if the catalyst has broken up"],["medium",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0421":["Catalyst 1 Efficiency Below Threshold Bank 1","Catalyst 1 is the first catalyst in the flow direction of bank 1 (the side with cylinder 1) – the close-coupled or warm-up catalyst that lights off first after a cold start; the older wording was Warm Up Catalyst. P0421 means it no longer converts enough.",[["h","Close-coupled catalyst aged, overheated or coating detached"],["m","Misfire or rich running as prior damage"],["m","Sensor after the close-coupled catalyst sluggish or faulty"],["l","Engine module software (Mitsubishi: update for 2006 Eclipse V6)"]],["Check engine light on, otherwise often no change in driving","Emission test failed (higher HC, CO, NOx)","Rattling or loss of power if the catalyst has broken up"],["medium",[250,2000]],"c"],"P0423":["Heated Catalyst Efficiency Below Threshold Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of a support catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start (Emitec EMICAT, 12–48 V); it was tried on Californian petrol cars in the 1990s and is used today mainly on diesels, for example Audi's 3.0 TDI from 2014 and 48-volt diesel vans. P0423 means the heated catalyst of bank 1 (the side with cylinder 1) no longer converts enough; CARB requires it to be assessed like any other catalyst.",[["h","Heated catalyst aged or poisoned"],["m","Heater not working, catalyst active too late"],["m","Sensor used for the assessment faulty"],["m","Calibration (Ram: software update per bulletin)"]],["Check engine light on","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["hard",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0424":["Heated Catalyst Temperature Below Threshold Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of a support catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start (Emitec EMICAT, 12–48 V); it was tried on Californian petrol cars in the 1990s and is used today mainly on diesels, for example Audi's 3.0 TDI from 2014 and 48-volt diesel vans. P0424 implements CARB's heated-catalyst rule for bank 1 (the side with cylinder 1): the catalyst must reach its intended temperature within a time set by the manufacturer after the start, measured by a temperature sensor or modelled from heating power.",[["m","Heating element aged or partly faulty"],["m","Supply too weak (battery, 48 V system, relay, contact resistance)"],["m","Catalyst temperature sensor reads wrong"],["l","Engine module or calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0425":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 1 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor faulty (element or lead broken by heat or vibration)"],["h","Harness chafed or scorched at the exhaust, connector corroded"],["m","Connector loose or pins pushed back"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0426":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 1 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Soot on the sensor tip (diesel)"],["m","Sensor aged, characteristic drifted"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["m","Engine module software (plausibility limits)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[0,250]],"c"],"P0427":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 1 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 1 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0428":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 1 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 1 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor or wire open (broken by vibration or heat)"],["h","Connector off or loose, ground wire open"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0429":["Catalyst Heater Control Circuit/Open Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of a support catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start (Emitec EMICAT, 12–48 V); it was tried on Californian petrol cars in the 1990s and is used today mainly on diesels, for example Audi's 3.0 TDI from 2014 and 48-volt diesel vans. P0429 is the electrical check of the catalyst heater of bank 1 (the side with cylinder 1): the module drives it via a relay or power stage and detects an open control circuit or heating element from the driver feedback, as CARB requires for output circuits.",[["h","Wire or connector to the heating element open (heat, corrosion)"],["m","Heating element burnt out"],["m","Relay, fuse or power stage faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[50,2500]],"c"],"P042A":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 1 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor faulty (element or lead broken by heat or vibration)"],["h","Harness chafed or scorched at the exhaust, connector corroded"],["m","Connector loose or pins pushed back"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P042B":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance Bank 1 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 1 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Soot on the sensor tip (diesel)"],["m","Sensor aged, characteristic drifted"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["m","Engine module software (plausibility limits)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P042C":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 1 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 1 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P042D":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 1 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 1 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side with cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor or wire open (broken by vibration or heat)"],["h","Connector off or loose, ground wire open"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P042E":["EGR A Control Stuck Open","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P042E means the position-controlled EGR valve A is more open than commanded and cannot be closed.",[["h","Valve or disc coked, does not close"],["m","Actuator motor, gear or return spring faulty"],["m","Position sensor in the valve reads wrong"],["l","Weak supply to the drive or connector fault"]],["Check engine light on (GM after one trip)","Rough idle, stalling, starting problems","Loss of power, possibly limp mode"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P042F":["EGR A Control Stuck Closed","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P042F means EGR valve A does not follow the opening command: its actual position stays more closed than commanded although the actuator works with high opening duty.",[["h","Valve coked or seized in the closed position"],["m","Actuator motor or gear weak or faulty"],["m","Position sensor or connector gives a wrong value"],["l","Icing in winter (temporary)"]],["Check engine light on (GM after one trip)","Usually no driveability problems","Possibly pinging (petrol) or higher NOx"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0430":["Catalyst System Efficiency Below Threshold Bank 2","Like P0420, but for the catalyst of bank 2, so only on V and boxer engines with one catalyst per bank; Y-pipe systems with a shared catalyst report P0420 only. The module compares the oxygen sensors before and after the bank 2 catalyst – a good catalyst smooths the rear signal.",[["h","Catalyst aged or poisoned (oil consumption, silicone, coolant)"],["m","Catalyst overheated by misfire or rich running"],["m","Rear oxygen sensor sluggish, dirty or unsuitable"],["m","Exhaust leak ahead of or at the rear sensor"]],["Check engine light on, otherwise often no change in driving","Emission test failed (higher HC, CO, NOx)","Rattling or loss of power if the catalyst has broken up"],["medium",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0432":["Catalyst 2 Efficiency Below Threshold Bank 2","Catalyst 2 is the second catalyst in the flow direction of bank 2 (the side without cylinder 1) – the main or underfloor catalyst behind the close-coupled one; the older wording was Main Catalyst. P0432 means it no longer converts enough.",[["h","Main or underfloor catalyst aged or poisoned"],["m","Diesel: exhaust fuel injector coked, doses too little"],["m","Temperature or oxygen sensor after the catalyst wrong"],["l","Misfire or mixture fault as prior damage"]],["Check engine light on","Emission test problems","Diesel: possibly more frequent filter regenerations"],["medium",[400,2500]],"c"],"P0433":["Heated Catalyst Efficiency Below Threshold Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of a support catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start (Emitec EMICAT, 12–48 V); it was tried on Californian petrol cars in the 1990s and is used today mainly on diesels, for example Audi's 3.0 TDI from 2014 and 48-volt diesel vans. P0433 means the heated catalyst of bank 2 (the side without cylinder 1) no longer converts enough; CARB requires it to be assessed like any other catalyst.",[["h","Heated catalyst aged or poisoned"],["m","Heater not working, catalyst active too late"],["m","Sensor used for the assessment faulty"],["m","Calibration (Ram: software update per bulletin)"]],["Check engine light on","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["hard",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0434":["Heated Catalyst Temperature Below Threshold Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of a support catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start (Emitec EMICAT, 12–48 V); it was tried on Californian petrol cars in the 1990s and is used today mainly on diesels, for example Audi's 3.0 TDI from 2014 and 48-volt diesel vans. P0434 implements CARB's heated-catalyst rule for bank 2 (the side without cylinder 1): the catalyst must reach its intended temperature within a time set by the manufacturer after the start, measured by a temperature sensor or modelled from heating power.",[["m","Heating element aged or partly faulty"],["m","Supply too weak (battery, 48 V system, relay, contact resistance)"],["m","Catalyst temperature sensor reads wrong"],["l","Engine module or calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0435":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 2 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor faulty (element or lead broken by heat or vibration)"],["h","Harness chafed or scorched at the exhaust, connector corroded"],["m","Connector loose or pins pushed back"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0436":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 2 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Soot on the sensor tip (diesel)"],["m","Sensor aged, characteristic drifted"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["m","Engine module software (plausibility limits)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0437":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 2 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 2 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0438":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 2 Sensor 1","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 1 of bank 2 is the first catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor or wire open (broken by vibration or heat)"],["h","Connector off or loose, ground wire open"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0439":["Catalyst Heater Control Circuit/Open Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of a support catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start (Emitec EMICAT, 12–48 V); it was tried on Californian petrol cars in the 1990s and is used today mainly on diesels, for example Audi's 3.0 TDI from 2014 and 48-volt diesel vans. P0439 is the electrical check of the catalyst heater of bank 2 (the side without cylinder 1): the module drives it via a relay or power stage and detects an open control circuit or heating element from the driver feedback, as CARB requires for output circuits.",[["h","Wire or connector to the heating element open (heat, corrosion)"],["m","Heating element burnt out"],["m","Relay, fuse or power stage faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[50,2500]],"c"],"P043A":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 2 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor faulty (element or lead broken by heat or vibration)"],["h","Harness chafed or scorched at the exhaust, connector corroded"],["m","Connector loose or pins pushed back"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P043B":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance Bank 2 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 2 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Soot on the sensor tip (diesel)"],["m","Sensor aged, characteristic drifted"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["m","Engine module software (plausibility limits)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P043C":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 2 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 2 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P043D":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 2 Sensor 2","Some engines – mainly diesels – measure the temperature at or in the catalyst with a thermistor in a voltage divider instead of only modelling it; most makers report exhaust temperature sensors under P0544 ff, P2031 ff or P2080 ff instead. Sensor 2 of bank 2 is a second, further downstream catalyst temperature sensor on the side without cylinder 1.",[["h","Sensor or wire open (broken by vibration or heat)"],["h","Connector off or loose, ground wire open"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable change (substitute value from the model)","Diesel: filter regeneration distorted or blocked, possibly further codes such as P2459 or P0420"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P043E":["EVAP System Leak Detection Reference Orifice Low Flow","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Systems with a leak detection pump (Bosch DM-TL at VW, Audi, BMW and Maserati, Denso's canister pump module at Toyota/Lexus) first measure a reference value with the pump working only through a built-in 0.5 mm (0.020 in) orifice; it is the yardstick for the small-leak test that CARB requires.",[["h","Reference orifice or pump module dirty or blocked by liquid (overfilled tank, splash water)"],["m","Leak detection pump faulty (weak or blocking pump, changeover valve stuck)"],["l","Engine module software (false detection)"],["l","Pump connector or supply (voltage dip)"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP leak test does not run, readiness not set","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[0,600]],"c"],"P043F":["EVAP System Leak Detection Reference Orifice High Flow","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Systems with a leak detection pump (Bosch DM-TL at VW, Audi, BMW and Maserati, Denso's canister pump module at Toyota/Lexus) first measure a reference value with the pump working only through a built-in 0.5 mm (0.020 in) orifice; it is the yardstick for the small-leak test that CARB requires.",[["h","Leak detection pump faulty (weak pump, leaking changeover valve, liquid damage)"],["m","Purge valve does not seal"],["m","Pump supply or connector (contact resistance, corrosion)"],["l","Leaking hose between pump module and canister"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP leak test does not run, readiness not set","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[100,600]],"c"],"P0440":["EVAP System","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0440 is the general code for a fault in this system without further narrowing down – leak, purge flow or component.",[["h","Fuel cap leaking, loose or wrong"],["m","Purge valve leaking or sticking"],["m","Leaking or cracked EVAP hoses, canister or vent valve"],["l","Engine module software"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly slight fuel smell","Readiness or emission inspection not passed"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P0441":["EVAP System Incorrect Purge Flow","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0441 means the actual purge flow does not match the target – too little, or flow although the purge valve is closed.",[["h","Purge valve stuck open or not sealing"],["m","Purge valve stuck closed, purge line blocked, kinked or off"],["m","Leak detection pump with tolerance fault (VW)"],["l","Canister saturated or damaged, turbo purge check valve faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle or hard start after refuelling (valve stuck open)","Fuel smell"],["easy",[40,300]],"c"],"P0442":["EVAP System Leak Detected (small leak)","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0442 reports a small leak; what size that means depends on the maker.",[["h","Fuel cap seal worn, wrong or not clicked in"],["h","Cracked or porous EVAP hoses or leaking connections"],["m","Purge or vent valve does not seal"],["m","Leak detection pump itself leaking (VW/Audi)"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly slight fuel smell","Readiness or emission inspection not passed"],["medium",[15,400]],"c"],"P0443":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve A Circuit","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0443 is the electrical check of purge valve A, which the module switches to ground in pulses; it compares the driver's feedback voltage with the commanded state.",[["h","Purge valve connector corroded, loose or off"],["m","Purge valve coil open or shorted"],["m","Control wire broken or chafed, or supply missing (fuse, main relay)"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly fuel smell (no purging)","EVAP leak test does not run"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0444":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve A Circuit Open","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0444 belongs to the older scheme of circuit, open and shorted codes for purge valve A; Saab's Trionic T7 and VW use it, while GM reports an open circuit as P0443 and Ford covers everything in P0443.",[["h","Purge valve connector off or corroded"],["m","Valve coil open"],["m","Supply wire, fuse, main relay or control wire open"],["l","Control wire shorted to ground (Saab definition)"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly fuel smell","EVAP monitors do not run"],["easy",[20,200]],"c"],"P0445":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve A Circuit Shorted","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0445 is the short-circuit code of the older scheme for purge valve A, used by Saab's Trionic T7 and VW; GM reports shorts as P0458/P0459, Ford covers them in P0443.",[["h","Purge valve coil shorted (shorted turns)"],["m","Control wire shorted to positive (chafed)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly fuel smell","EVAP monitors do not run"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0446":["EVAP System Vent Control Circuit","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The vent valve is normally open and closes for the leak test.",[["h","Vent valve or filter blocked by dirt, dust or water (GM reading)"],["m","Vent valve electrically faulty, coil open or shorted (Ford reading)"],["m","Connector or wire to the vent valve corroded or open"],["m","Canister clogged with charcoal or liquid (overfilling)"]],["Check engine light on","Refuelling problems, nozzle clicks off early (blocked vent)","Hissing when opening the fuel cap (vacuum)"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0447":["EVAP System Vent Control Circuit Open","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0447 belongs to the older scheme for the vent valve circuit (P0446 circuit, P0447 open, P0448 shorted), used by Subaru (also in the Saab 9-2X), VW and Nissan; GM reports an open vent circuit as P0449, Ford as P0446.",[["h","Vent valve connector corroded or off (underbody, splash water)"],["m","Vent valve coil open (resistance outside 10–100 Ω)"],["m","Wire to the module or supply from the main relay open"],["l","Engine module or its connector"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP leak test does not run, readiness not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0448":["EVAP System Vent Control Circuit Shorted","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0448 is the short-circuit code of the older scheme for the vent valve.",[["h","Vent valve or vent path blocked by dust, dirt or water (Nissan reading)"],["m","Vent valve coil shorted (below 1 Ω)"],["m","Control wire shorted to positive"],["l","Module driver damaged"]],["Check engine light on","Refuelling problems if the vent is blocked","EVAP leak test does not run"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0449":["EVAP System Vent Valve Control Circuit/Open","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0449 belongs to the newer scheme for the vent valve circuit – P0449 open, P0498 low, P0499 high – used mainly by GM and also by VW.",[["h","Vent valve connector (usually at the canister or tank, underbody) corroded or loose"],["m","Vent valve coil open"],["m","Supply (fuse) or control wire open"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP leak test does not run, readiness not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P044A":["EGR Sensor C Circuit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. What EGR sensor C is depends on the maker: on Ford petrol engines from 2020 it is the DPFE sensor, which measures the pressure drop across an orifice in the EGR pipe (sensor A there is the valve position sensor); GM diesels assign it to the position sensor of a low-pressure EGR valve, but only within fault groups.",[["h","Connector or wire to sensor C open or corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed (with other sensor codes)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR readiness not set, EGR possibly switched off"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P044B":["EGR Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. What EGR sensor C is depends on the maker: on Ford petrol engines from 2020 it is the DPFE sensor, which measures the pressure drop across an orifice in the EGR pipe (sensor A there is the valve position sensor); GM diesels assign it to the position sensor of a low-pressure EGR valve, but only within fault groups.",[["h","Pressure hoses off, swapped or blocked (soot, condensate)"],["m","Sensor zero point drifted"],["m","Orifice or EGR pipe coked"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR readiness not set, EGR possibly switched off"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P044C":["EGR Sensor C Circuit Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. What EGR sensor C is depends on the maker: on Ford petrol engines from 2020 it is the DPFE sensor, which measures the pressure drop across an orifice in the EGR pipe (sensor A there is the valve position sensor); GM diesels assign it to the position sensor of a low-pressure EGR valve, but only within fault groups.",[["h","5 V supply of the sensor open or shorted to ground"],["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector corroded or off"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR readiness not set, EGR possibly switched off"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P044D":["EGR Sensor C Circuit High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. What EGR sensor C is depends on the maker: on Ford petrol engines from 2020 it is the DPFE sensor, which measures the pressure drop across an orifice in the EGR pipe (sensor A there is the valve position sensor); GM diesels assign it to the position sensor of a low-pressure EGR valve, but only within fault groups.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or positive"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR readiness not set, EGR possibly switched off"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P044E":["EGR Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. What EGR sensor C is depends on the maker: on Ford petrol engines from 2020 it is the DPFE sensor, which measures the pressure drop across an orifice in the EGR pipe (sensor A there is the valve position sensor); GM diesels assign it to the position sensor of a low-pressure EGR valve, but only within fault groups.",[["h","Loose contact in the connector or broken wire"],["m","Condensate in the sensor or hose (DPFE)"],["m","Sensor unstable internally"],["l","Interference or ground problems"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","Usually no driving symptom","Code returns after clearing"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P044F":["AIR System Switching Valve A Circuit High","After a cold start the secondary air pump blows fresh air into the exhaust; switching valve A is the electrically driven valve that opens this path. GM checks the valve circuit in three codes – P0412 open, P041F shorted to ground, P044F shorted to positive – while other makers use P0413/P0414 or P0412 for everything.",[["h","Valve coil shorted internally (low resistance)"],["m","Control wire chafed against a supply wire"],["m","Water in the connector bridging to the supply"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on (after two trips)","No change in driving","Secondary air readiness not set, possibly P0411"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0450":["EVAP System Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The tank pressure sensor measures small pressures around ambient for the leak tests.",[["h","Connector at the tank pressure sensor corroded or loose (underbody, splash water)"],["m","Tank pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Damaged 5 V, ground or signal wire"],["l","Engine module software"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[40,250]],"c"],"P0451":["EVAP System Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit Range/Performance","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The tank pressure sensor measures small pressures around ambient for the leak tests.",[["h","Tank pressure sensor offset shifted or sluggish"],["m","Vent line or canister blocked (pressure does not release)"],["m","Sensor hose or port leaking, kinked or blocked"],["l","Tank overfilled, fuel in the lines"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P0452":["EVAP System Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit Low","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The tank pressure sensor measures small pressures around ambient for the leak tests.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or connector corroded"],["m","Tank pressure sensor faulty internally"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted (shared reference)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[40,250]],"c"],"P0453":["EVAP System Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit High","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The tank pressure sensor measures small pressures around ambient for the leak tests.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector off"],["m","Tank pressure sensor faulty"],["l","Signal shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[40,250]],"c"],"P0454":["EVAP System Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit Intermittent","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The tank pressure sensor measures small pressures around ambient for the leak tests.",[["h","Loose contact at the connector to the tank pressure sensor"],["m","Chafed harness at the tank"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (noisy signal)"],["l","Fuel sloshing in an overfilled tank or refuelling misdetected"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[20,250]],"c"],"P0455":["EVAP System Leak Detected - Large Leak","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0455 means a large leak or that no vacuum builds up.",[["h","Fuel cap missing, not clicked in or seal faulty (or capless flap)"],["h","Hose off or cracked (canister, filler neck, purge line)"],["m","Vent valve stuck open or purge valve stuck closed"],["m","Canister or filler neck cracked or corroded"]],["Check engine light on (possibly a check fuel cap message)","Possibly fuel smell","EVAP readiness or emission inspection not passed"],["easy",[10,400]],"c"],"P0456":["EVAP System Leak Detected (very small leak)","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0456 reports a very small leak of 0.020 in (0.5 mm), as CARB requires.",[["h","Fuel cap seal worn or cap not clicked in"],["m","Purge or vent valve not sealing"],["m","Porous hoses or O-rings, hairline crack in canister or filler neck"],["l","Leak detection pump itself leaking (VW/Audi)"]],["Check engine light on (possibly a check fuel cap message)","Possibly fuel smell","EVAP readiness or emission inspection not passed"],["medium",[15,450]],"c"],"P0457":["EVAP System Leak Detected (fuel cap loose/off)","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0457 is a large leak attributed to the fuel cap: Ford detects refuelling and treats a vacuum that is not reached afterwards (about −2 kPa in 30 s) as an open cap.",[["h","Fuel cap not closed, not clicked in or forgotten"],["m","Fuel cap seal faulty or non-original cap"],["m","Filler neck sealing surface damaged or capless flap sticking"],["l","Vent valve stuck open"]],["Check fuel cap lamp or check engine light on","Fuel smell","EVAP readiness not set"],["easy",[0,60]],"c"],"P0458":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve A Circuit Low","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Purge valve A is switched to ground by the module and fed from the main relay.",[["h","Purge valve control wire chafed against ground"],["m","Purge valve coil shorted internally"],["m","Connector scorched or wet"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle or rich mixture possible (valve permanently open)","Starting problems after refuelling possible"],["easy",[25,200]],"c"],"P0459":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve A Circuit High","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Purge valve A is switched to ground by the module and fed from the main relay.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage or supply"],["m","Purge valve coil low-resistance (shorted turns)"],["m","Connector or harness damaged"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel smell when the canister is full","EVAP readiness not set"],["easy",[25,200]],"c"],"P045A":["EGR B Control Circuit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR B is a second EGR valve next to valve A – on diesels with two EGR circuits the low-pressure valve, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor.",[["h","Connector at EGR valve B corroded or loose"],["m","Harness chafed or heat-damaged"],["m","Actuator motor in valve B open"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Low-pressure EGR switched off, possibly higher NOx","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P045B":["EGR B Control Circuit Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR B is a second EGR valve next to valve A – on diesels with two EGR circuits the low-pressure valve, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor.",[["h","Valve B coked or stiff"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["m","Actuator motor worn internally"],["l","Engine module software or calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Low-pressure EGR switched off, possibly higher NOx","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P045C":["EGR B Control Circuit Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR B is a second EGR valve next to valve A – on diesels with two EGR circuits the low-pressure valve, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor.",[["h","Control wire chafed against ground (engine, exhaust)"],["m","Actuator motor winding shorted"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Low-pressure EGR switched off, possibly higher NOx","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P045D":["EGR B Control Circuit High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR B is a second EGR valve next to valve A – on diesels with two EGR circuits the low-pressure valve, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a supply wire"],["m","Actuator motor shorted internally (overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Low-pressure EGR switched off, possibly higher NOx","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P045E":["EGR B Control Stuck Open","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR B is a second EGR valve next to valve A – on diesels with two EGR circuits the low-pressure valve, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor.",[["h","Soot or condensate deposits in the low-pressure valve"],["m","Valve position sensor fault as the trigger"],["m","Actuator gear or return spring faulty"],["l","Foreign matter or filter debris in the low-pressure circuit"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, rough running, misfire","Possibly black smoke"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P045F":["EGR B Control Stuck Closed","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. EGR B is a second EGR valve next to valve A – on diesels with two EGR circuits the low-pressure valve, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor.",[["h","Low-pressure valve coked or stuck"],["m","Actuator motor or gear faulty"],["l","Icing or condensate in the low-pressure circuit"],["l","Position sensor reports a wrong position"]],["Check engine light on","Low-pressure EGR switched off, possibly higher NOx","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0460":["Fuel Level Sensor A Circuit","The fuel level sensor is a float on a resistor track in the tank unit. It is no emission part, but other monitors depend on it – the EVAP leak tests, for example, need at least 15 % fuel – so CARB requires it to be monitored and the usual exemption from the warning lamp does not apply.",[["h","Resistor track worn or float arm sticking"],["m","Wire or connector at the tank unit open or shorted (corrosion)"],["l","Instrument cluster or gateway sends a wrong value (if the level comes via the bus)"],["l","Frequent topping up or overfilling (plausibility test fails without a defect)"]],["Fuel gauge wrong, stuck or jumping","Range display and low-fuel warning wrong","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0461":["Fuel Level Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The fuel level sensor is a float on a resistor track in the tank unit. It is no emission part, but other monitors depend on it – the EVAP leak tests, for example, need at least 15 % fuel – so CARB requires it to be monitored and the usual exemption from the warning lamp does not apply.",[["h","Resistor track or wiper worn or dirty (sulphur deposits), arm sticking"],["m","Float deformed or soaked, or tank deformed"],["m","Overfilling or frequent topping up (no defect)"],["l","Contact resistance at connectors (occasional jumps)"]],["Fuel gauge wrong, stuck or jumping","Range display and low-fuel warning wrong","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0462":["Fuel Level Sensor A Circuit Low","The fuel level sensor is a float on a resistor track in the tank unit. It is no emission part, but other monitors depend on it – the EVAP leak tests, for example, need at least 15 % fuel – so CARB requires it to be monitored and the usual exemption from the warning lamp does not apply.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed at tank or underbody)"],["m","Sensor shorted or damaged internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the tank connector"],["l","Evaluating module faulty"]],["Fuel gauge wrong, stuck or jumping","Range display and low-fuel warning wrong","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0463":["Fuel Level Sensor A Circuit High","The fuel level sensor is a float on a resistor track in the tank unit. It is no emission part, but other monitors depend on it – the EVAP leak tests, for example, need at least 15 % fuel – so CARB requires it to be monitored and the usual exemption from the warning lamp does not apply.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, or tank unit connector off"],["h","Resistor track interrupted or worn (sulphur deposits, wiper lifting)"],["m","Corroded connector or pushed-back pin"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Fuel gauge wrong, stuck or jumping","Range display and low-fuel warning wrong","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0464":["Fuel Level Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The fuel level sensor is a float on a resistor track in the tank unit. It is no emission part, but other monitors depend on it – the EVAP leak tests, for example, need at least 15 % fuel – so CARB requires it to be monitored and the usual exemption from the warning lamp does not apply.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the tank connector or sensor terminal"],["h","Worn spots on the resistor track (wiper skipping)"],["m","Chafed wire with intermittent contact"],["l","Car parked on a steep slope or fuel sloshing after switch-off"]],["Check engine light on (GM after one trip)","Fuel gauge occasionally jumps","EVAP readiness not set"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0465":["EVAP Purge Flow Sensor Circuit","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. A purge flow sensor – a heated thermistor in the purge line between purge valve and intake manifold, as described in a Ford patent – measures purge flow directly for the CARB purge monitor.",[["h","Connector or wire at the purge flow sensor"],["m","Sensor (heater or thermistor) faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0466":["EVAP Purge Flow Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. A purge flow sensor – a heated thermistor in the purge line between purge valve and intake manifold, as described in a Ford patent – measures purge flow directly for the CARB purge monitor.",[["h","Sensor dirty or sluggish (charcoal dust, fuel deposits)"],["m","Purge line blocked or purge valve sticking"],["l","Hose leaking ahead of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0467":["EVAP Purge Flow Sensor Circuit Low","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. A purge flow sensor – a heated thermistor in the purge line between purge valve and intake manifold, as described in a Ford patent – measures purge flow directly for the CARB purge monitor.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Supply or reference open"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0468":["EVAP Purge Flow Sensor Circuit High","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. A purge flow sensor – a heated thermistor in the purge line between purge valve and intake manifold, as described in a Ford patent – measures purge flow directly for the CARB purge monitor.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Short to positive"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0469":["EVAP Purge Flow Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. A purge flow sensor – a heated thermistor in the purge line between purge valve and intake manifold, as described in a Ford patent – measures purge flow directly for the CARB purge monitor.",[["h","Loose contact at the connector"],["m","Wire broken by engine movement"],["l","Sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set, emission inspection not passed","No change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P046A":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor 1/2 Correlation Bank 1","Some engines measure the catalyst temperature with sensors instead of only modelling it. P046A compares catalyst temperature sensors 1 and 2 of bank 1 (the side with cylinder 1): both are electrically valid, but their readings do not match – for example they differ after a long stop, or the difference does not fit the operating state.",[["h","One of the two sensors drifting (soot on the tip, ageing)"],["m","Sensors fitted or connected the wrong way round"],["m","Contact resistance at one sensor's connector"],["l","Exhaust leak between the sensors"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Diesel: filter regeneration possibly changed"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P046B":["Catalyst Temperature Sensor 1/2 Correlation Bank 2","Some engines measure the catalyst temperature with sensors instead of only modelling it. P046B compares catalyst temperature sensors 1 and 2 of bank 2 (the side without cylinder 1): both are electrically valid, but their readings do not match – for example they differ after a long stop, or the difference does not fit the operating state.",[["h","One of the two sensors drifting (soot on the tip, ageing)"],["m","Sensors fitted or connected the wrong way round"],["m","Contact resistance at one sensor's connector"],["l","Exhaust leak between the sensors"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Diesel: filter regeneration possibly changed"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P046C":["EGR Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Which part EGR sensors A and B are depends on the maker: sensor A is usually the position sensor of EGR valve A; GM's Duramax diesels of 2017/18 use sensor B for the coolant temperature sensor at the inlet of the high-pressure EGR cooler, FCA for the position sensor of the low-pressure valve.",[["h","EGR valve coked and stiff, actual position does not follow"],["m","Position sensor worn or offset shifted"],["m","Contact resistance at connector or 5 V supply"],["l","Values not relearned after valve replacement"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off, possibly higher NOx","Rarely jerking or lack of power"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P046D":["EGR Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Which part EGR sensors A and B are depends on the maker: sensor A is usually the position sensor of EGR valve A; GM's Duramax diesels of 2017/18 use sensor B for the coolant temperature sensor at the inlet of the high-pressure EGR cooler, FCA for the position sensor of the low-pressure valve.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the EGR valve connector"],["m","Chafed wire with brief short to ground or positive"],["m","Position sensor in the valve worn"],["l","Disturbed 5 V reference"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","Usually no noticeable change","Occasional jerking when EGR switches off"],["easy",[30,600]],"c"],"P046E":["EGR Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Which part EGR sensors A and B are depends on the maker: sensor A is usually the position sensor of EGR valve A; GM's Duramax diesels of 2017/18 use sensor B for the coolant temperature sensor at the inlet of the high-pressure EGR cooler, FCA for the position sensor of the low-pressure valve.",[["h","Temperature sensor drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["l","Reference sensor (charge air temperature) inaccurate without its own code"],["l","Low coolant or air at the sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR cooler check suspended"],["easy",[40,300]],"c"],"P046F":["EGR Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Which part EGR sensors A and B are depends on the maker: sensor A is usually the position sensor of EGR valve A; GM's Duramax diesels of 2017/18 use sensor B for the coolant temperature sensor at the inlet of the high-pressure EGR cooler, FCA for the position sensor of the low-pressure valve.",[["h","Loose contact at the sensor connector"],["m","Chafed wire or vibration break"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0470":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor A Circuit","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector corroded or heat-damaged near the manifold"],["m","Broken or chafed signal or 5 V wire"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P0471":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Sensor drifting or faulty"],["h","Sensing tube or hose blocked or coked"],["m","Real high back pressure from a clogged particulate filter or catalyst"],["l","Leaking sensing tube or exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[60,500]],"c"],"P0472":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor A Circuit Low","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted (often shared with other sensors)"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector damaged by heat or corrosion"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0473":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor A Circuit High","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Signal wire open or sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector loose or corroded"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0474":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Loose contact at connector or wire near the hot manifold"],["m","Sensing tube partly blocked (sluggish, jumping values)"],["m","Engine module software (Ford 6.7, 2018)"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P0475":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A","The exhaust pressure control valve is a flap in the exhaust: on older Navistar/Ford diesels a warm-up flap that raises back pressure when cold, on Ram and others an exhaust brake, on VW's 2.0 TDI a flap that drives the low-pressure EGR, and on Audi and Bentley a sound flap. P0475 is the general circuit fault of valve A, monitored like any output as CARB requires.",[["h","Flap actuator (motor or electronics) faulty"],["m","Wire or connector at the exhaust damaged or corroded"],["m","Control wire open"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function: sound unchanged, rattling","Diesel: longer warm-up, exhaust brake weak or EGR faults"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0476":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Range/Performance","The exhaust pressure control valve is a flap in the exhaust: on older Navistar/Ford diesels a warm-up flap that raises back pressure when cold, on Ram and others an exhaust brake, on VW's 2.0 TDI a flap that drives the low-pressure EGR, and on Audi and Bentley a sound flap. P0476 is the function check: the valve does not reach its target position or the expected back pressure.",[["h","Flap stiff, coked or corroded"],["m","Linkage, cable or bracket misadjusted"],["m","Actuator (motor, hydraulic or vacuum) weak"],["m","Exhaust pressure sensor or sensing tube giving wrong feedback"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function: sound unchanged, rattling","Diesel: longer warm-up, exhaust brake weak or EGR faults"],["medium",[100,1000]],"c"],"P0477":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Low","The exhaust pressure control valve is a flap in the exhaust: on older Navistar/Ford diesels a warm-up flap that raises back pressure when cold, on Ram and others an exhaust brake, on VW's 2.0 TDI a flap that drives the low-pressure EGR, and on Audi and Bentley a sound flap. P0477 means the control circuit of valve A is low.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground"],["h","Flap actuator faulty internally"],["m","Flap seized by corrosion (motor blocked)"],["m","Chafed wire at the underbody"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function: sound unchanged, rattling","Diesel: longer warm-up, exhaust brake weak or EGR faults"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0478":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A High","The exhaust pressure control valve is a flap in the exhaust: on older Navistar/Ford diesels a warm-up flap that raises back pressure when cold, on Ram and others an exhaust brake, on VW's 2.0 TDI a flap that drives the low-pressure EGR, and on Audi and Bentley a sound flap. P0478 means the control circuit of valve A is high – a short to positive, an open ground, or a driver reporting a high state.",[["h","Flap actuator electronics faulty"],["m","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Connector damaged by moisture or heat"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function: sound unchanged, rattling","Diesel: longer warm-up, exhaust brake weak or EGR faults"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0479":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Intermittent","The exhaust pressure control valve is a flap in the exhaust: on older Navistar/Ford diesels a warm-up flap that raises back pressure when cold, on Ram and others an exhaust brake, on VW's 2.0 TDI a flap that drives the low-pressure EGR, and on Audi and Bentley a sound flap. P0479 means an intermittent circuit or function fault of valve A.",[["h","Loose contact in connector or wire"],["m","Flap motor with commutator wear"],["m","Moisture in the connector"]],["Check engine light on, possibly only after several events","Occasional flap noise or malfunction"],["medium",[0,800]],"c"],"P047A":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor B Circuit","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector corroded or heat-damaged near the manifold"],["m","Broken or chafed signal or 5 V wire"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P047B":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Sensor drifting or faulty"],["h","Sensing tube or hose blocked or coked"],["m","Real high back pressure from a clogged particulate filter or catalyst"],["l","Leaking sensing tube or exhaust leak ahead of the sensor"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[60,500]],"c"],"P047C":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor B Circuit Low","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted (often shared with other sensors)"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector damaged by heat or corrosion"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P047D":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor B Circuit High","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Signal wire open or sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector loose or corroded"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P047E":["Exhaust Pressure Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The exhaust pressure sensor measures the pressure ahead of the turbine or in the manifold (Ford: turbine upstream pressure, Cummins: exhaust manifold pressure, older Ford diesels: EBP). The module uses it to calculate EGR flow, to control the variable turbine and engine braking, and to protect against overpressure.",[["h","Loose contact at connector or wire near the hot manifold"],["m","Sensing tube partly blocked (sluggish, jumping values)"],["m","Engine module software (Ford 6.7, 2018)"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","EGR and turbo control on a substitute value, possibly reduced power","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without function"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P047F":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Stuck Open","The exhaust pressure control valve is a flap in the exhaust: on older Navistar/Ford diesels a warm-up flap that raises back pressure when cold, on Ram and others an exhaust brake, on VW's 2.0 TDI a flap that drives the low-pressure EGR, and on Audi and Bentley a sound flap. P047F means valve A stays open although it is commanded to close, detected by the flap's position sensor or by missing back pressure.",[["h","Flap shaft corroded or seized"],["m","Soot or carbon on the flap"],["m","Actuator or linkage faulty or unhooked"],["l","Position sensor reports wrongly"]],["Check engine light on","EGR flow too low (P0401), higher NOx","Exhaust brake or warm-up flap without effect, possibly louder exhaust"],["medium",[150,1000]],"c"],"P0480":["Fan 1 Control Circuit","The engine module switches the radiator fan either through a relay, whose coil it pulls to ground, or with a PWM signal to a fan control module. The circuit is an output that CARB requires to be checked continuously.",[["h","Fan relay (coil open) or fan control module faulty"],["h","Connector at fan or relay corroded, water ingress or pin pushed out"],["m","Control wire open or shorted to ground"],["m","Fan motor or fan assembly faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Radiator fan does not run, or runs constantly","Overheating at standstill or in traffic"],["medium",[20,600]],"c"],"P0481":["Fan 2 Control Circuit","The engine module switches the radiator fan either through a relay, whose coil it pulls to ground, or with a PWM signal to a fan control module. The circuit is an output that CARB requires to be checked continuously.",[["h","Fan relay 2 (or 2/3) faulty"],["m","Corrosion or water in relay or fan connector"],["m","Control wire open or shorted to ground"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","One fan stage or fan not working, or running constantly","Temperature rises at standstill"],["medium",[20,500]],"c"],"P0482":["Fan 3 Control Circuit","The engine module switches cooling fan stages through relays, pulling each relay coil to ground. P0482 concerns the control circuit of the third stage or relay: with the driver off the line should sit at battery voltage through the coil, and the internal pull-up level means an open circuit (Saab ME9: more than 1 s, 10–18 V, engine running).",[["h","Fan relay 3 faulty (coil open) or not seated properly"],["h","Control wire open or high-resistance, corroded or backed-out pins"],["m","Relay coil supply missing (fuse, supply wire)"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","One fan stage fails – temperature rises in traffic or when towing","Air conditioning weaker at standstill"],["medium",[30,350]],"c"],"P0483":["Fan Rationality Check","Two kinds of cooling fan carry these codes: electric fans switched by relays or driven by a PWM/LIN fan module with speed feedback, and engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch, whose speed a Hall sensor reports – GM calls them EV fans, meaning electro-viscous, not electric vehicle. P0483 checks the fan's function, not its circuit.",[["h","Viscous clutch worn or leaking, or its solenoid faulty – fan does not reach or leave target speed"],["m","Electric fan motor stiff or worn, or fan blocked"],["m","Fan speed sensor or wiring giving a wrong value"],["l","Radiator or condenser clogged, coolant low (Subaru logic)"]],["Check engine light on (after two trips)","Constant fan roar, or temperature rise at standstill","Air conditioning weak at low speed"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0484":["Fan Circuit Current High","P0484 monitors the current drawn in the fan's load circuit: if it exceeds a threshold – blocked or stiff motor, shorted turns, a short in the load circuit – the code sets. This is only possible where the fan runs on a current-measuring semiconductor driver or fan module; plain relay circuits do not measure load current.",[["h","Fan motor stiff (bearing damage) or blocked (foreign object, ice, bent blade)"],["m","Shorted turns or worn brushes in the fan motor"],["m","Short in the load circuit (pinched or chafed wire)"],["l","Fan control module faulty (wrong current measurement)"]],["Warning lamp (depending on the maker)","Fan does not run, runs only briefly or at reduced power","Humming or grinding at the fan, possibly blown fan fuse"],["medium",[120,700]],"c"],"P0485":["Fan Power/Ground Circuit","P0485 monitors the supply or ground path of the fan or its control module – not the control line. It is typical of fans with their own module (PWM or LIN), which can report undervoltage or a missing ground.",[["h","Fan or fan module ground loose, corroded or broken"],["h","Fan fuse blown or fuse holder overheated or corroded"],["m","Relay power contact burnt – voltage drop under load"],["m","Connector at the fan scorched, water ingress"]],["Warning lamp (depending on the maker)","Fan does not run or only intermittently","Overheating at standstill, weak air conditioning"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P0486":["EGR Sensor B Circuit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Which part EGR sensor B is depends on the maker: FCA uses it for the position sensor of the low-pressure EGR valve, GM's Duramax diesels for the coolant temperature sensor at the EGR cooler inlet.",[["h","Connector or harness to sensor B corroded or open (heat at the exhaust)"],["m","Sensor B faulty"],["l","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off, usually no noticeable change","Higher NOx"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0487":["EGR Throttle Control Circuit A/Open","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The EGR throttle is a regulating flap ahead of the intake manifold on diesels: by closing partly it lowers manifold pressure below exhaust back pressure so that enough exhaust is recirculated, and it also helps filter regeneration and shuts off air at engine stop.",[["h","Connector at the flap corroded or loose, wire open"],["m","Flap actuator motor faulty (winding open)"],["l","Pneumatic version: vacuum converter faulty"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode possible","Shaking when the engine is switched off"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0488":["EGR Throttle Control Circuit A Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The EGR throttle is a regulating flap ahead of the intake manifold on diesels: by closing partly it lowers manifold pressure below exhaust back pressure so that enough exhaust is recirculated, and it also helps filter regeneration and shuts off air at engine stop.",[["h","Flap coked by soot and oil mist (EGR plus crankcase ventilation)"],["m","Actuator gear or motor worn or broken"],["m","Position sensor in the flap faulty or implausible"],["l","Corrosion on flap or housing"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode possible","Shaking when the engine is switched off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0489":["EGR A Control Circuit Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P0489 means the drive circuit of EGR valve A – solenoid, stepper or DC motor – is shorted to ground.",[["h","Harness chafed at the exhaust with short to ground"],["m","EGR valve winding shorted to its housing"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off","Rough idle or stalling if the valve stays open"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P048A":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Stuck Closed","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve A is the first such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Flap or flap shaft corroded or coked (stiff)"],["m","Flap actuator (motor or gear) faulty or too weak"],["l","Linkage between actuator and flap broken or misadjusted"],["l","Position sensor reports wrongly"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[200,1200]],"c"],"P048B":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Position Sensor/Switch Circuit","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve A is the first such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Actuator with integrated position sensor faulty"],["h","Connector or wire at the underbody corroded, chafed or open"],["l","Engine module (sensor supply or input)"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P048C":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Range/Performance","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve A is the first such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Flap or shaft corroded or coked (stiff)"],["m","End positions not relearned after replacement"],["m","Position sensor in the actuator drifting"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P048D":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Low","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve A is the first such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed at the underbody)"],["h","Position sensor or actuator faulty internally"],["m","5 V sensor supply open or shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P048E":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Position Sensor/Switch Circuit High","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve A is the first such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Actuator electronics (position sensor) faulty"],["m","Signal or ground wire open"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Signal shorted to positive"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[150,600]],"c"],"P048F":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve A Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve A is the first such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the actuator connector"],["m","Wire broken by vibration or heat"],["m","Position sensor worn"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0490":["EGR A Control Circuit High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P0490 means the drive circuit of EGR valve A is shorted to battery voltage, or its output stage sees overcurrent.",[["h","Harness shorted to a supply wire (chafing)"],["m","EGR valve with shorted turns (low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off, usually no noticeable change","Higher NOx"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0491":["AIR System Insufficient Flow Bank 1","After a cold start the secondary air pump blows fresh air behind the exhaust valves so that unburnt fuel burns off and the catalyst heats up faster; CARB requires the air flow to be monitored per bank. P0491 means too little air reaches bank 1 (the side with cylinder 1).",[["h","Secondary air passages in the cylinder head coked"],["h","Combination valve stuck or stiff"],["m","Vacuum supply or changeover valve leaking, valve closes too early"],["m","Secondary air pump weak or water in the pump"]],["Check engine light on","No change in driving","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0492":["AIR System Insufficient Flow Bank 2","After a cold start the secondary air pump blows fresh air behind the exhaust valves so that unburnt fuel burns off and the catalyst heats up faster; CARB requires the air flow to be monitored per bank. P0492 means too little air reaches bank 2 (the side without cylinder 1).",[["h","Secondary air passages in the cylinder head coked"],["h","Combination valve stuck or stiff"],["m","Vacuum supply or changeover valve leaking, valve closes too early"],["m","Secondary air pump weak or water in the pump"]],["Check engine light on","No change in driving","Secondary air readiness not set (emission test)"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0493":["Fan Overspeed","Two kinds of cooling fan carry these codes: electric fans switched by relays or driven by a PWM/LIN fan module with speed feedback, and engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch, whose speed a Hall sensor reports – GM calls them EV fans, meaning electro-viscous, not electric vehicle. P0493 is documented only on GM petrol engines of 2004–06 with a viscous fan: the measured fan speed exceeds a limit that is hardly physically possible (about 6400 rpm), so it is effectively a plausibility check of the speed signal or a seized clutch at high engine speed.",[["h","Viscous clutch seized – fan runs rigidly with the belt drive"],["m","Interference or faulty signal from the fan speed sensor"],["m","Clutch solenoid or its control stuck at fully engaged"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Check engine light on (first trip)","Loud fan roar at higher revs","Higher consumption, slightly less power"],["medium",[150,600]],"u"],"P0494":["Fan 1 Underspeed","Two kinds of cooling fan carry these codes: electric fans switched by relays or driven by a PWM/LIN fan module with speed feedback, and engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch, whose speed a Hall sensor reports – GM calls them EV fans, meaning electro-viscous, not electric vehicle. P0494 means fan 1 turns slower than commanded, judged from the speed reported by a LIN fan module or the clutch's speed sensor.",[["h","Fan motor or fan module faulty or worn (brushes, bearings, electronics)"],["m","Fan slowed or blocked (water, snow, ice, foreign object, distorted shroud)"],["m","Viscous clutch slipping or low on fluid, clutch valve faulty"],["m","High-resistance supply or ground, connector problems"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Temperature rises at standstill or when towing","Air conditioning weak at standstill"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0495":["Fan 1 Overspeed","Two kinds of cooling fan carry these codes: electric fans switched by relays or driven by a PWM/LIN fan module with speed feedback, and engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch, whose speed a Hall sensor reports – GM calls them EV fans, meaning electro-viscous, not electric vehicle. P0495 mainly concerns viscous fans: with the fan commanded off it turns faster than the permitted drag speed, so the clutch does not release.",[["h","Viscous clutch does not release (gummed, overfilled, seized)"],["m","Clutch solenoid or its control stuck open (short to positive, valve sticking)"],["m","Wrong speed signal (sensor, wiring, interference)"],["l","Electric fan: module runs uncontrolled at full speed or relay sticking"]],["Check engine light on","Loud fan roar even with a cold engine or at idle","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up"],["medium",[150,800]],"c"],"P0496":["EVAP System High Purge Flow","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0496 means vapour flows into the intake although the module is not purging – usually a purge valve that leaks or sticks open.",[["h","Purge valve leaking or stuck open (charcoal dust in the seat)"],["m","Vent valve stuck closed or vent filter or line blocked"],["l","Vapour line kinked or blocked"],["l","Tank pressure sensor reading wrong"]],["Check engine light on","Hard or long starting right after refuelling","Rough idle, rich fuel trim"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0497":["EVAP System Low Purge Flow","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. P0497 is the counterpart of P0496: the module purges but sees too little or no purge flow, judged indirectly from fuel trim, idle control or tank vacuum – CARB requires missing purge flow to be detected.",[["h","Purge valve stuck closed, does not open"],["m","Purge line off, kinked or blocked (check valve sticking)"],["m","Canister or vent filter blocked (e.g. after overfilling)"],["l","Vent valve stuck closed"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel smell around the car","Fuel nozzle clicks off early"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P0498":["EVAP System Vent Valve Control Circuit Low","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The vent valve is normally open and switched to ground by the module; P0449 (open), P0498 and P0499 form the newer code scheme for its circuit, used mainly by GM and Saab models with GM engines, while Ford uses P0446.",[["h","Vent valve control wire chafed against ground (underbody, tank area)"],["m","Vent valve coil shorted internally"],["m","Corrosion or water in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Refuelling difficult, nozzle clicks off","Vacuum noise when opening the fuel cap"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P0499":["EVAP System Vent Valve Control Circuit High","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. The vent valve is normally open and switched to ground by the module (GM scheme P0449/P0498/P0499; Ford uses P0446).",[["h","Control wire touching battery positive or the valve supply (chafed harness)"],["m","Vent valve coil with shorted turns, driver switches off"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P049A":["EGR B Flow","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Circuit B is the second EGR path; on diesels with two circuits it is the low-pressure EGR, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor (GM diesels 2016–18, FCA's 2.2 and 3.0 diesels).",[["h","Low-pressure EGR valve sooted or sticking"],["m","Low-pressure EGR cooler or line blocked or leaking"],["m","Flap creating the pressure difference faulty"],["m","Sensors (air mass, pressure difference, position sensor B) implausible"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off, usually no noticeable change","Higher NOx"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P049B":["EGR B Flow Insufficient Detected","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Circuit B is the second EGR path; on diesels with two circuits it is the low-pressure EGR, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor (GM diesels 2016–18, FCA's 2.2 and 3.0 diesels).",[["h","Software or calibration (false trigger)"],["h","Low-pressure EGR valve sooted or stuck closed"],["m","Low-pressure EGR cooler or line blocked"],["l","Implausible sensors (air mass, pressure difference)"]],["Check engine light on","Sluggish acceleration (FCA bulletin)","Higher NOx"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P049C":["EGR B Flow Excessive Detected","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Circuit B is the second EGR path; on diesels with two circuits it is the low-pressure EGR, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor (GM diesels 2016–18, FCA's 2.2 and 3.0 diesels).",[["h","Low-pressure EGR valve stuck open or not sealing (soot)"],["m","Position sensor B implausible (valve seemingly closed)"],["l","Leak between exhaust side and intake"],["l","Air mass meter reading too low"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power, black smoke","Rough idle or misfire, in extreme cases stalling"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P049D":["EGR A Control Position Exceeded Learning Limit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P049D checks the learned zero point – the fully closed position – of EGR valve A.",[["h","Soot or deposits at the valve seat, valve does not close fully"],["m","Position sensor A faulty or characteristic shifted"],["m","New or cleaned valve not taught in, or wrong part"],["l","Position sensor circuit fault as the primary cause"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driveability effect","EGR possibly switched off, rough idle if the valve stays open"],["medium",[0,800]],"c"],"P049E":["EGR B Control Position Exceeded Learning Limit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Circuit B is the second EGR path; on diesels with two circuits it is the low-pressure EGR, which takes exhaust after the particulate filter and feeds it in before the turbo compressor (GM diesels 2016–18, FCA's 2.2 and 3.0 diesels).",[["h","Low-pressure EGR valve sooted, does not close fully"],["m","Position sensor of the low-pressure valve faulty or shifted"],["m","Valve replaced without teaching in"],["l","Harness or connector of the low-pressure valve"]],["Check engine light on","EGR switched off, usually no noticeable change","Higher NOx"],["hard",[0,1000]],"c"],"P049F":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Flap actuator faulty (commutator short)"],["m","Wire or connector at the actuator damaged"],["m","Control wire open"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[200,1000]],"c"],"P04A0":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Range/Performance","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Flap actuator worn, commutator dirty"],["m","Flap shaft stiff (corrosion, deposits)"],["m","Wiring or contacts"],["l","Basic setting not carried out"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[200,1500]],"c"],"P04A1":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Low","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground"],["h","Flap actuator shorted internally"],["m","Chafed wire at the underbody"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P04A2":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B High","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Control wire shorted to positive"],["m","Actuator electronics faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or wet"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P04A3":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Intermittent","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Commutator in the actuator partly shorted"],["m","Wire broken by vibration"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P04A4":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Stuck Open","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Flap or flap shaft corroded or coked (stiff)"],["m","Flap actuator (motor or gear) faulty or too weak"],["l","Linkage between actuator and flap broken or misadjusted"],["l","Position sensor reports wrongly"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[200,1500]],"c"],"P04A5":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Stuck Closed","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Flap or flap shaft corroded or coked (stiff)"],["m","Flap actuator (motor or gear) faulty or too weak"],["l","Linkage between actuator and flap broken or misadjusted"],["l","Position sensor reports wrongly"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[200,1500]],"c"],"P04A6":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Position Sensor/Switch Circuit","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Actuator with integrated position sensor faulty"],["h","Connector or wire at the underbody corroded, chafed or open"],["l","Engine module (sensor supply or input)"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P04A7":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Range/Performance","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Flap or shaft corroded or coked (stiff)"],["m","End positions not relearned after replacement"],["m","Position sensor in the actuator drifting"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P04A8":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Low","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed at the underbody)"],["h","Position sensor or actuator faulty internally"],["m","5 V sensor supply open or shorted"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P04A9":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Position Sensor/Switch Circuit High","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Actuator electronics (position sensor) faulty"],["m","Signal or ground wire open"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Signal shorted to positive"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[100,700]],"c"],"P04AA":["Exhaust Pressure Control Valve B Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","An exhaust pressure control valve is an electrically driven flap in the exhaust with a position sensor or end-position switch, usually inside the actuator. Valve B is the second such flap; Bentley's V8 assigns A to bank 1 and B to bank 2.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the actuator connector"],["m","Wire broken by vibration or heat"],["m","Position sensor worn"]],["Check engine light on","Flap without function, exhaust sound changed","VW/Audi TDI: EGR fault P0401 possible"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P04AB":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve B Circuit/Open","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Purge valve B is a second electric purge valve, usually for the high-load purge line of a turbo engine, which purges to the compressor inlet when there is boost in the manifold (Ford patent).",[["h","Purge valve B connector loose or corroded"],["m","Coil of purge valve B open"],["m","Supply (fuse, main relay) or control wire open"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P04AC":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve B Circuit Low","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Purge valve B is a second electric purge valve, usually for the high-load purge line of a turbo engine, which purges to the compressor inlet when there is boost in the manifold (Ford patent).",[["h","Control wire of purge valve B chafed against ground"],["m","Coil shorted internally"],["m","Moisture in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, rich mixture","Starting problems after refuelling"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P04AD":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve B Circuit High","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Purge valve B is a second electric purge valve, usually for the high-load purge line of a turbo engine, which purges to the compressor inlet when there is boost in the manifold (Ford patent).",[["h","Control wire touching battery positive"],["m","Coil shorted internally (driver overcurrent)"],["m","Corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P04AE":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve B Performance/Stuck Open","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Purge valve B is a second electric purge valve, usually for the high-load purge line of a turbo engine, which purges to the compressor inlet when there is boost in the manifold (Ford patent).",[["h","Engine module software (Subaru FA24: known false trigger)"],["m","Purge valve B leaking or stuck open (charcoal dust, wear)"],["l","Purge lines swapped after a repair"],["l","Tank pressure sensor misreading"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle or starting problems after refuelling","Often no change in driving (software false trigger)"],["medium",[80,350]],"u"],"P04AF":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve B Stuck Closed","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Purge valve B is a second electric purge valve, usually for the high-load purge line of a turbo engine, which purges to the compressor inlet when there is boost in the manifold (Ford patent).",[["h","Purge valve B stuck closed (gummed, dirt)"],["m","Line to the compressor inlet off, kinked or blocked, check valve sticking"],["l","Engine module software or false trigger"],["l","Canister or vent blocked"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel smell","EVAP readiness not set"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04B0":["Refueling Vapor Control Valve Circuit/Open","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Plug-in hybrids and range extenders with a sealed, pressurised tank have an electric valve in the vapour line between tank and canister.",[["h","Connector at the tank valve (tank, underbody) loose or corroded"],["m","Valve coil open"],["m","Supply or control wire open"],["l","Driver in the control module"]],["Check engine light on","Filler flap does not open after pressing the refuel button, or only after a delay","EVAP readiness not set"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P04B1":["Refueling Vapor Control Valve Circuit Low","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Plug-in hybrids and range extenders with a sealed, pressurised tank have an electric valve in the vapour line between tank and canister.",[["h","Control wire chafed against ground (underbody, tank area)"],["m","Valve coil shorted internally"],["m","Moisture in the connector"],["l","Driver in the control module"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly fuel smell","EVAP readiness not set"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P04B2":["Refueling Vapor Control Valve Circuit High","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Plug-in hybrids and range extenders with a sealed, pressurised tank have an electric valve in the vapour line between tank and canister.",[["h","Control wire touching battery positive"],["m","Valve coil with shorted turns (driver overcurrent)"],["m","Corrosion in the connector"],["l","Driver in the control module"]],["Check engine light on","Filler flap does not open after pressing the refuel button, or only after a delay","EVAP readiness not set"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P04B3":["Refueling Vapor Control Valve Performance/Stuck Open","The evaporative emission (EVAP) system stores fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister; a purge valve lets the engine draw it off, and a vent valve opens the canister to fresh air. Plug-in hybrids and range extenders with a sealed, pressurised tank have an electric valve in the vapour line between tank and canister.",[["h","Valve leaking or stuck open (dirt, wear)"],["m","Tank pressure sensor reading wrong"],["l","Line at the valve swapped or damaged"],["l","Control module software"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel smell","EVAP readiness not set"],["medium",[100,500]],"u"],"P04B4":["Refueling Vapor Control Valve Stuck Closed","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Valve mechanically stuck (dirt, fuel in the valve, ice)"],["m","Vapour line kinked or blocked, liquid separator blocked"],["l","Canister clogged (charcoal dust, liquid fuel)"],["l","Tank pressure sensor sluggish or wrong"]],["Check engine light on","Filler door does not open after pressing the button, or only after a long delay","Nozzle clicks off early when refuelling"],["medium",[150,600]],"u"],"P04B5":["Fuel Fill Door Stuck Open","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Door not fully latched after refuelling or opened by an accidental button press"],["m","Fuel cap tether blocking the plunger or latch"],["m","Plunger missing or loose, magnet or latch motor shifted or faulty"],["l","Status line of the door switch shorted to ground"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Fuel door open message in the instrument cluster","Door stays open or does not latch"],["easy",[0,250]],"u"],"P04B6":["Fuel Fill Door Stuck Closed","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Door latch, magnet or actuator blocked or faulty (ice, dirt, corrosion)"],["m","Door housing distorted after fitting, door jams"],["m","Tank cannot be depressurised (valve stuck closed, line blocked)"],["l","Software false trigger"]],["Filler door does not open despite pressing the button","Code often stored without warning lamp (Jeep)","Refuelling only via emergency release"],["easy",[0,300]],"u"],"P04B7":["Fuel Fill Door Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Loose contact in the connector, pins pushed back"],["m","Broken wire at the bend or grommet"],["m","Switch bouncing or worn, door seated loosely"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel door open message although the door is closed, or missing when it is open","Door may not open or only after a delay"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P04B8":["Fuel Fill Door Position Sensor/Switch Circuit","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Wire or connector at the door switch open (side panel, moisture)"],["m","Switch or sensor faulty internally"],["m","Ground or supply interrupted"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel door open message although the door is closed, or missing when it is open","Door may not open or only after a delay"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P04B9":["Fuel Fill Door Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Low","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground (chafed or pinched)"],["m","Switch shorted internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel door open message although the door is closed, or missing when it is open","Door may not open or only after a delay"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P04BA":["Fuel Fill Door Position Sensor/Switch Circuit High","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Signal or ground line open (pull-up drives it high)"],["m","Signal line shorted to positive"],["m","Switch faulty (high resistance)"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel door open message although the door is closed, or missing when it is open","Door may not open or only after a delay"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P04BB":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Control Circuit/Open","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04BB concerns the lock circuit: the module detects an open circuit in the output to the door actuator or its relay, which CARB requires for outputs.",[["h","Open control wire or connector at the door actuator"],["m","Actuator motor or solenoid open internally"],["m","Actuator supply, fuse or relay not working"],["l","Driver in the module"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P04BC":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Control Range/Performance","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04BC concerns the lock circuit: the actuator is driven but the expected reaction is missing – the door or lock sensor reports no change within the time window.",[["h","Actuator or door mechanically blocked (ice, dirt, bent door, cap tether)"],["m","Actuator worn or stiff"],["m","Lock or door sensor reporting wrongly"],["m","Door held or pressed by hand during actuation"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[0,250]],"u"],"P04BD":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Control Circuit Low","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04BD concerns the lock circuit: the voltage in the control circuit is below the expected range – typically the control line is shorted to ground.",[["h","Control line chafed against the body (short to ground)"],["m","Water or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Actuator shorted internally"],["l","Relay or module driver faulty"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P04BE":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Control Circuit High","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04BE concerns the lock circuit: the voltage in the control circuit is above the expected range – typically a short to positive, or with a low-side driver an open load circuit.",[["h","Control line shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Actuator load circuit open (seen as high with a low-side driver)"],["m","Relay sticking or faulty"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P04BF":["Fuel Fill Door Unlock Control Circuit/Open","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04BF concerns the unlock circuit: the module detects an open circuit in the output to the door actuator or its relay, which CARB requires for outputs.",[["h","Open control wire or connector at the door actuator"],["m","Actuator motor or solenoid open internally"],["m","Actuator supply, fuse or relay not working"],["l","Driver in the module"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P04C0":["Fuel Fill Door Unlock Control Range/Performance","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04C0 concerns the unlock circuit: the actuator is driven but the expected reaction is missing – the door or lock sensor reports no change within the time window.",[["h","Actuator or door mechanically blocked (ice, dirt, bent door, cap tether)"],["m","Actuator worn or stiff"],["m","Lock or door sensor reporting wrongly"],["m","Door held or pressed by hand during actuation"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[0,250]],"u"],"P04C1":["Fuel Fill Door Unlock Control Circuit Low","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04C1 concerns the unlock circuit: the voltage in the control circuit is below the expected range – typically the control line is shorted to ground.",[["h","Control line chafed against the body (short to ground)"],["m","Water or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Actuator shorted internally"],["l","Relay or module driver faulty"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P04C2":["Fuel Fill Door Unlock Control Circuit High","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04C2 concerns the unlock circuit: the voltage in the control circuit is above the expected range – typically a short to positive, or with a low-side driver an open load circuit.",[["h","Control line shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Actuator load circuit open (seen as high with a low-side driver)"],["m","Relay sticking or faulty"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Filler door does not open or lock as commanded","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P04C3":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Position Sensor/Switch Circuit","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). The lock position sensor in the actuator reports locked or unlocked – not to be confused with the door position sensor (open or closed, P04B7–P04BA).",[["h","Wire or connector to the lock sensor open or shorted"],["m","Switch in the actuator faulty (usually replaced with the actuator)"],["m","Water ingress"],["l","Module"]],["Filler door does not unlock or lock reliably","Refuelling system message","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04C4":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Range/Performance","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). The lock position sensor in the actuator reports locked or unlocked – not to be confused with the door position sensor (open or closed, P04B7–P04BA).",[["h","Actuator does not travel fully (blocked or worn)"],["m","Switch in the actuator worn or misadjusted"],["m","Door distorted, latch does not engage"],["l","Module"]],["Filler door does not unlock or lock reliably","Refuelling system message","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04C5":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Low","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). The lock position sensor in the actuator reports locked or unlocked – not to be confused with the door position sensor (open or closed, P04B7–P04BA).",[["h","Wire to the lock sensor shorted to ground"],["m","Switch in the actuator faulty"],["m","Water ingress"],["l","Module"]],["Filler door does not unlock or lock reliably","Refuelling system message","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04C6":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Position Sensor/Switch Circuit High","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). The lock position sensor in the actuator reports locked or unlocked – not to be confused with the door position sensor (open or closed, P04B7–P04BA).",[["h","Wire shorted to positive or open (pull-up)"],["m","Switch in the actuator faulty"],["m","Water ingress"],["l","Module"]],["Filler door does not unlock or lock reliably","Refuelling system message","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04C7":["Fuel Fill Door Lock Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). The lock position sensor in the actuator reports locked or unlocked – not to be confused with the door position sensor (open or closed, P04B7–P04BA).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector near the door"],["m","Broken wire at a bend"],["m","Switch contact bouncing"],["l","Module"]],["Filler door does not unlock or lock reliably","Refuelling system message","Possibly check engine light"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04C8":["Fuel Fill Door Open Request Sensor/Switch Circuit","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04C8 is the general circuit fault of the fuel button input.",[["h","Fuel button faulty"],["h","Wire or connector between button and module open or shorted"],["m","Button ground open"],["l","Module"]],["Fuel button has no effect, door does not open","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Refuelling only via emergency release"],["easy",[20,200]],"u"],"P04C9":["Fuel Fill Door Open Request Sensor/Switch Performance/Stuck Off","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04C9 means the button gives no or no plausible signal when it should be pressed, or the door is found open without a button press – as after use of the emergency release.",[["h","Fuel button faulty"],["h","Wire between button and module open"],["m","Door opened via the emergency release"],["m","Button ground open"]],["Fuel button has no effect, door does not open","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Refuelling only via emergency release"],["easy",[20,200]],"u"],"P04CA":["Fuel Fill Door Open Request Sensor/Switch Circuit Low","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04CA means the button line is below its valid range.",[["h","Button supply circuit open (Ford: supply circuit or splice)"],["h","Button line shorted to ground"],["m","Fuel button faulty"],["l","Module"]],["Fuel button has no effect, door does not open","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Refuelling only via emergency release"],["easy",[20,200]],"u"],"P04CB":["Fuel Fill Door Open Request Sensor/Switch Circuit High","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04CB means the button line is above its valid range.",[["h","Button line shorted to positive"],["m","Fuel button faulty"],["m","Connector or wiring damaged"],["l","Module"]],["Fuel button has no effect, door does not open","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Refuelling only via emergency release"],["easy",[20,200]],"u"],"P04CC":["Fuel Fill Door Open Request Sensor/Switch Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04CC means the button signal is sporadic or implausible – a bouncing button or a loose contact.",[["h","Loose contact between button and module"],["h","Fuel button worn or bouncing"],["m","Button ground poor"],["l","Module"]],["Fuel button has no effect, door does not open","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Refuelling only via emergency release"],["easy",[20,200]],"u"],"P04CD":["Fuel Fill Door Open Request Sensor/Switch Stuck On","On plug-in hybrids with a sealed, pressurised tank, pressing the fuel button (open request) starts a refuelling mode: the module opens the tank valve, releases the pressure into the charcoal canister and only then unlocks the filler door via an electric actuator (Ford patent). P04CD means the button is detected as pressed for an unusually long time.",[["h","Fuel button mechanically jammed or sticking"],["m","Button line shorted to ground"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the button"],["l","Module"]],["Fuel button has no effect, door does not open","Refuelling system message in the instrument cluster","Refuelling only via emergency release"],["easy",[20,200]],"u"],"P04CE":["EGR Temperature Sensor C Circuit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The code standard allows up to four EGR temperature sensors, A to D – for example before and after the cooler, or in the high- and low-pressure circuits; which is which depends on the maker.",[["h","Open or poor contact in signal or ground line"],["h","Connector or wire at the sensor corroded or worn by heat and vibration"],["m","Sensor faulty (element broken or sooted)"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving (EGR on a substitute value or off)","Possibly EGR flow or cooler codes (P0401, P2457)"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04CF":["EGR Temperature Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The code standard allows up to four EGR temperature sensors, A to D – for example before and after the cooler, or in the high- and low-pressure circuits; which is which depends on the maker.",[["h","Sensor drifting or sluggish (soot, ageing)"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","EGR cooler or valve problem changes the real temperature"],["l","Comparison sensor (coolant, intake air, exhaust) faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving (EGR on a substitute value or off)","Possibly EGR flow or cooler codes (P0401, P2457)"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04D0":["EGR Temperature Sensor C Circuit Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The code standard allows up to four EGR temperature sensors, A to D – for example before and after the cooler, or in the high- and low-pressure circuits; which is which depends on the maker.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground (chafed hot harness)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving (EGR on a substitute value or off)","Possibly EGR flow or cooler codes (P0401, P2457)"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04D1":["EGR Temperature Sensor C Circuit High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The code standard allows up to four EGR temperature sensors, A to D – for example before and after the cooler, or in the high- and low-pressure circuits; which is which depends on the maker.",[["h","Signal or ground line open, connector off or corroded"],["m","Sensor open internally (element broken)"],["l","Signal shorted to 5 V or positive"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving (EGR on a substitute value or off)","Possibly EGR flow or cooler codes (P0401, P2457)"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04D2":["EGR Temperature Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The code standard allows up to four EGR temperature sensors, A to D – for example before and after the cooler, or in the high- and low-pressure circuits; which is which depends on the maker.",[["h","Loose contact or fretting at the connector pins"],["m","Wire chafing intermittently (vibration, heat)"],["m","Sensor with an internal contact fault"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04D3":["Exhaust Brake Input Circuit","The exhaust brake input is the driver's request for the exhaust or engine brake – a button or stalk on diesel pick-ups and trucks. On GM's 6.6-litre L5P, for example, the button must be pressed again after every start.",[["h","Exhaust brake button faulty (contact, lighting board)"],["m","Open wire or connector between button and module"],["l","Fault in an intermediate module or bus message"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Exhaust brake cannot be switched on, button lamp off or wrong","Possibly check engine light","More load on the service brakes downhill"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P04D4":["Exhaust Brake Input Circuit Range/Performance","The exhaust brake input is the driver's request for the exhaust or engine brake – a button or stalk on diesel pick-ups and trucks. On GM's 6.6-litre L5P, for example, the button must be pressed again after every start.",[["h","Button mechanically stuck or contact dirty (permanently pressed)"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or ground (voltage between thresholds)"],["l","Wrongly retrofitted or coded button or lever"],["l","Engine module or software"]],["Exhaust brake cannot be switched on, button lamp off or wrong","Possibly check engine light","More load on the service brakes downhill"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P04D5":["Exhaust Brake Input Circuit Low","The exhaust brake input is the driver's request for the exhaust or engine brake – a button or stalk on diesel pick-ups and trucks. On GM's 6.6-litre L5P, for example, the button must be pressed again after every start.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground (chafed)"],["m","Button supply missing (fuse, open wire)"],["m","Button shorted internally or wet"],["l","Corroded connector pins"]],["Exhaust brake cannot be switched on, button lamp off or wrong","Possibly check engine light","More load on the service brakes downhill"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P04D6":["Exhaust Brake Input Circuit High","The exhaust brake input is the driver's request for the exhaust or engine brake – a button or stalk on diesel pick-ups and trucks. On GM's 6.6-litre L5P, for example, the button must be pressed again after every start.",[["h","Signal line shorted to supply voltage"],["m","Button ground or signal line open (pull-up logic)"],["m","Button faulty internally"],["l","Corroded or wet connector"]],["Exhaust brake cannot be switched on, button lamp off or wrong","Possibly check engine light","More load on the service brakes downhill"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P04D7":["Exhaust Brake Input Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The exhaust brake input is the driver's request for the exhaust or engine brake – a button or stalk on diesel pick-ups and trucks. On GM's 6.6-litre L5P, for example, the button must be pressed again after every start.",[["h","Loose contact at the button or module connector"],["m","Worn or bouncing button"],["m","Chafed wire with intermittent short"],["l","Moisture or corrosion"]],["Exhaust brake cannot be switched on, button lamp off or wrong","Possibly check engine light","More load on the service brakes downhill"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P04D8":["Excessive Time To Enter Closed Loop EGR Control","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Where EGR is closed-loop controlled – on flow, valve position or pressure difference – CARB requires the control to start within a time set by the maker after starting; otherwise a fault is due, and P04D8 is that code.",[["h","Engine reaches release temperature too late (thermostat stuck open, very short trips, cold weather)"],["m","Faulty input sensor blocks the release (EGR temperature, air mass, position)"],["m","EGR valve or actuator not ready (stuck, learned value missing)"],["l","Software or calibration"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driveability problems","Often together with coolant or sensor codes"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P04D9":["Closed Loop EGR Control At Limit - Flow Too Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P04D9 means the EGR control has used up its authority and still gets too little flow – CARB requires this to be detected.",[["h","EGR cooler, valve or pipes sooted or narrowed"],["m","EGR valve sticking or not opening fully"],["m","EGR rate mismeasured (temperature sensor contacts, air mass meter, pressure sensor)"],["l","Software or driving profile with long low-load operation"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable symptoms","Higher NOx, petrol engines may knock"],["medium",[150,1500]],"u"],"P04DA":["Closed Loop EGR Control At Limit - Flow Too High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P04DA is the counterpart of P04D9: the control cannot lower the flow far enough even at the end of its range.",[["h","EGR valve not closing (soot at the seat, stuck open)"],["m","Air mass meter reading too little fresh air (EGR rate calculated too high)"],["m","Position or EGR temperature sensor giving a wrong value"],["l","Intake or exhaust throttle wrong (pressure difference too large)"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle, jerking","Smoke or soot (diesel), possibly stalling at idle (petrol)"],["medium",[150,1200]],"u"],"P04DB":["Crankcase Ventilation System Disconnected","The crankcase ventilation (PCV) system routes blow-by gas from the crankcase into the intake; CARB requires a disconnection between crankcase and intake to be detected. GM's 2.0-litre LSY has a pressure sensor in the PCV hose: with the hose connected it must see a pressure drop that grows with air flow, plus pressure pulses from the pistons.",[["h","Ventilation hose off or quick connector not latched (often after intake work)"],["h","Hose cracked or porous, or nipple broken"],["m","PCV valve stuck closed or oil separator blocked or frozen (simulates a disconnection)"],["l","Pressure sensor in the hose giving a wrong signal"]],["Check engine light on","Hissing, false air, rough idle or lean running","Oil mist or smell in the engine bay"],["easy",[0,200]],"u"],"P04DC":["Fuel Fill Door Position Sensor/Switch Circuit Range/Performance","Plug-in and some full hybrids keep the fuel tank sealed while driving, separated from the charcoal canister by a valve. When the driver presses the fuel button, the system opens the valve, lets the pressure escape into the canister and only then unlocks the filler door.",[["h","Switch mechanically jammed or misadjusted (reports one state permanently)"],["m","Door or actuator damaged or missing"],["m","Signal or connector fault with a valid but wrong level"],["l","Fuel level sensor faulty (refuelling misdetected)"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel door open message although the door is closed, or missing when it is open","Door may not open or only after a delay"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P04DD":["Cold Start EGR A Flow Insufficient Detected","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P04DD monitors EGR A while a cold-start emission reduction strategy is active and commands EGR: if the flow does not react as intended, too little exhaust is recirculated in the warm-up phase.",[["h","EGR valve position sensor faulty (follow-on code)"],["m","EGR valve sticking or not opening when cold (soot, icing, condensate)"],["m","EGR path or cooler narrowed"],["l","Pressure, temperature or air mass sensor misreading"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Higher NOx after a cold start"],["medium",[150,1200]],"u"],"P04DE":["Cold Start EGR A Flow Excessive Detected","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P04DE is the counterpart of P04DD: during an active cold-start strategy the flow of EGR A is too high – the strategy calls for little or limited EGR, but the valve lets more through.",[["h","EGR valve not closing fully (soot, condensate, stuck open)"],["m","Position sensor reports a wrong position"],["l","Air mass or pressure difference misreading"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle after a cold start","Smoke or soot after a cold start (diesel), stalling possible"],["medium",[150,1200]],"u"],"P04DF":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve A Performance/Stuck Open","Purge valve A is the main EVAP purge valve between charcoal canister and intake manifold (on turbo engines the low-load path); its electrical circuit faults are P0443, P0458 and P0459. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code; the description follows GM's comparable check P0496, Subaru's valve B counterpart P04AE and CARB's purge flow requirement.",[["h","Purge valve A leaking or stuck open (charcoal dust or dirt on the seat)"],["m","Canister broken down, charcoal dust in the valve"],["l","Tank pressure sensor giving a wrong signal"],["l","Hoses swapped or wrongly routed"]],["Check engine light on","Hard starting or stumbling idle right after refuelling","Rough idle, rich fuel trim"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P04E0":["EVAP System Purge Control Valve A Stuck Closed","Purge valve A is the main EVAP purge valve between charcoal canister and intake manifold (on turbo engines the low-load path); its electrical circuit faults are P0443, P0458 and P0459. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code; the description follows GM's comparable check P0496, Subaru's valve B counterpart P04AE and CARB's purge flow requirement.",[["h","Purge valve A stuck closed (gummed, charcoal dust, spring or armature)"],["m","Purge line kinked or blocked"],["l","Canister or vent blocked"],["l","Tank pressure sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Fuel smell","Nozzle clicks off early when refuelling"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P04E1":["Crankcase Ventilation Hose Connection Sensor Circuit","The crankcase ventilation (PCV) system routes blow-by gas from the crankcase into the intake; CARB requires a disconnection between crankcase and intake to be detected. GM's 2.0-litre LSY has a pressure sensor in the PCV hose: with the hose connected it must see a pressure drop that grows with air flow, plus pressure pulses from the pistons.",[["h","Sensor connector not latched or corroded"],["m","Signal, 5 V reference or ground wire open"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Disconnection check P04DB does not run","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,250]],"u"],"P04E2":["Crankcase Ventilation Hose Connection Sensor Circuit Low","The crankcase ventilation (PCV) system routes blow-by gas from the crankcase into the intake; CARB requires a disconnection between crankcase and intake to be detected. GM's 2.0-litre LSY has a pressure sensor in the PCV hose: with the hose connected it must see a pressure drop that grows with air flow, plus pressure pulses from the pistons.",[["h","Signal line open or shorted to ground"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted (often shared with other sensors)"],["m","Connector not latched or corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Disconnection check P04DB does not run","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,250]],"u"],"P04E3":["Crankcase Ventilation Hose Connection Sensor Circuit High","The crankcase ventilation (PCV) system routes blow-by gas from the crankcase into the intake; CARB requires a disconnection between crankcase and intake to be detected. GM's 2.0-litre LSY has a pressure sensor in the PCV hose: with the hose connected it must see a pressure drop that grows with air flow, plus pressure pulses from the pistons.",[["h","Signal line shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor ground open"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Corroded or wet connector"]],["Check engine light on","Disconnection check P04DB does not run","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,250]],"u"],"P04E4":["Crankcase Ventilation Hose Connection Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The crankcase ventilation (PCV) system routes blow-by gas from the crankcase into the intake; CARB requires a disconnection between crankcase and intake to be detected. GM's 2.0-litre LSY has a pressure sensor in the PCV hose: with the hose connected it must see a pressure drop that grows with air flow, plus pressure pulses from the pistons.",[["h","Loose contact at the sensor connector"],["m","Chafed wire with intermittent short"],["m","Sensor faulty or measuring port blocked (oil sludge, ice)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Disconnection check P04DB does not run","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,250]],"u"],"P04E5":["EGR Temperature Sensor D Circuit","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. The code standard allows up to four EGR temperature sensors, A to D – for example before and after the cooler, or in the high- and low-pressure circuits; which is which depends on the maker.",[["h","Wire or connector fault"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving (EGR on a substitute value or off)","Possibly EGR flow or cooler codes (P0401, P2457)"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04E6":["EGR Temperature Sensor D Circuit Range/Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Sensor D is the fourth of up to four EGR temperature sensors, conceivable on engines with high- and low-pressure EGR and a sensor before and after each cooler; it is usually an NTC whose low voltage means hot.",[["h","Sensor drifting (soot or oil crust, ageing)"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or harness (heat, corrosion)"],["m","Real temperature deviation: EGR cooler or bypass faulty, valve sticking"],["l","Comparison sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR possibly reduced, higher NOx"],["medium",[60,300]],"u"],"P04E7":["EGR Temperature Sensor D Circuit Low","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Sensor D is the fourth of up to four EGR temperature sensors, conceivable on engines with high- and low-pressure EGR and a sensor before and after each cooler; it is usually an NTC whose low voltage means hot.",[["h","Signal line chafed against ground or the engine"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR possibly reduced, higher NOx"],["medium",[50,250]],"u"],"P04E8":["EGR Temperature Sensor D Circuit High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Sensor D is the fourth of up to four EGR temperature sensors, conceivable on engines with high- and low-pressure EGR and a sensor before and after each cooler; it is usually an NTC whose low voltage means hot.",[["h","Connector off or loose, or pin corroded"],["m","Sensor open internally (heat damage)"],["m","Sensor ground line open"],["l","Signal line shorted to positive"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","EGR possibly reduced, higher NOx"],["medium",[50,250]],"u"],"P04E9":["EGR Temperature Sensor D Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Sensor D is the fourth of up to four EGR temperature sensors, conceivable on engines with high- and low-pressure EGR and a sensor before and after each cooler; it is usually an NTC whose low voltage means hot.",[["h","Loose contact in connector or harness (vibration, heat)"],["m","Sensor with an internal hairline crack"],["m","Chafed harness"],["l","Module or ground connection"]],["Check engine light on, may come and go","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[40,250]],"u"],"P04EA":["EVAP System Small Leak Detected - Fuel Tank Side","Hybrids with a sealed fuel tank have a valve separating the tank from the charcoal canister and its vent side. CARB allows such sections to be leak-checked separately with their own codes; a Ford patent checks the tank side via tank pressure with the valve closed and the canister side with a leak check module.",[["h","Fuel cap or seal leaking or not closed properly (tether trapped)"],["m","Filler neck or tank vent line leaking"],["m","Sender or pump flange seal leaking"],["l","Tank valve or tank pressure sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly check fuel cap message or slight fuel smell","No change in driving"],["medium",[20,500]],"u"],"P04EB":["EVAP System Small Leak Detected - Fresh Air Side","Hybrids with a sealed fuel tank have a valve separating the tank from the charcoal canister and its vent side. CARB allows such sections to be leak-checked separately with their own codes; a Ford patent checks the tank side via tank pressure with the valve closed and the canister side with a leak check module.",[["h","Crack or leaking connection at the canister or vent lines"],["m","Vent valve or leak check module not sealing"],["m","Purge valve leaking"],["l","Rodent bite or chafing on lines under the car"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly check fuel cap message or slight fuel smell","No change in driving"],["medium",[50,450]],"u"],"P04EC":["EVAP System Large Leak Detected - Fuel Tank Side","Hybrids with a sealed fuel tank have a valve separating the tank from the charcoal canister and its vent side. CARB allows such sections to be leak-checked separately with their own codes; a Ford patent checks the tank side via tank pressure with the valve closed and the canister side with a leak check module.",[["h","Fuel cap not closed properly or tether trapped"],["m","Cap or filler neck sealing surface poor"],["m","Recirculation or vent line loose at the quick connector"],["l","Tank pressure sensor or valve faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Check fuel cap message, often right after refuelling","Possibly fuel smell"],["easy",[0,250]],"u"],"P04ED":["EVAP System Large Leak Detected - Fresh Air Side","Hybrids with a sealed fuel tank have a valve separating the tank from the charcoal canister and its vent side. CARB allows such sections to be leak-checked separately with their own codes; a Ford patent checks the tank side via tank pressure with the valve closed and the canister side with a leak check module.",[["h","Line at the canister or leak check module off or broken"],["m","Vent valve or leak check module not closing"],["m","Canister housing cracked"],["l","Purge valve stuck open"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly check fuel cap message or slight fuel smell","No change in driving"],["medium",[40,450]],"u"],"P04EE":["EVAP System Very Small Leak Detected - Fuel Tank Side","Hybrids with a sealed fuel tank have a valve separating the tank from the charcoal canister and its vent side. CARB allows such sections to be leak-checked separately with their own codes; a Ford patent checks the tank side via tank pressure with the valve closed and the canister side with a leak check module.",[["h","Fuel cap seal deformed or dirty, tether trapped"],["m","Purge valve not sealing (Ford hybrids) – plus software"],["m","Leaking quick connector or vent line at the tank"],["l","Hairline crack in tank lines or flange seal"]],["Check engine light on","Check fuel cap message","No change in driving"],["easy",[0,350]],"u"],"P04EF":["EVAP System Very Small Leak Detected - Fresh Air Side","Hybrids with a sealed fuel tank have a valve separating the tank from the charcoal canister and its vent side. CARB allows such sections to be leak-checked separately with their own codes; a Ford patent checks the tank side via tank pressure with the valve closed and the canister side with a leak check module.",[["h","Purge valve not sealing"],["m","Vent valve or leak check module leaking"],["m","Hairline crack or leaking connections at the canister"],["l","Module software (misjudgement)"]],["Check engine light on","EVAP readiness not set","No change in driving"],["medium",[40,350]],"u"],"P04F0":["EVAP System High Load Purge Line A Performance","Turbocharged engines also purge the charcoal canister under boost, through a high-load line to the compressor inlet via a venturi; check valves keep boost out of the EVAP system, and CARB requires this path to be monitored. P04F0 checks it: Ford (2020–23, replacing P144C) runs the test once per trip under boost with the vent closed and purge open, and sets the code if tank pressure rises faster than 0.5 or 1 inH2O/s or the vacuum rate is out of range, within 5–10 s.",[["h","Check valve in the purge line leaking, stuck open, fitted wrongly or missing"],["m","High-load purge line to the turbo or venturi off, cracked or blocked"],["m","Purge line pressure sensor wrongly connected or faulty (VW group)"],["l","Purge valve or venturi faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Possibly fuel smell, EVAP readiness not set"],["medium",[30,300]],"u"],"P04F1":["EVAP System Purge Line Performance","P04F1 is the function check of a purge line that is not the high-load path A – by the wording of another code list the low-pressure path to the intake manifold, counterpart of P04F0. The purge valve opens and the tank pressure or mixture reaction is missing or wrong: the line is blocked or off, or a check valve sticks.",[["h","Purge line to the intake manifold off, kinked or blocked"],["m","Check valve in the low-load path sticking"],["m","Purge valve not opening fully"],["l","Tank pressure sensor misreading"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly fuel smell","No change in driving"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P04F2":["Catalyst Heater Supply Voltage Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","48 V or high-voltage supply weak (battery, charge, cold, DC-DC converter)"],["m","Fuse, relay or power switch faulty"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at high-current connector or ground"],["l","Module or voltage measurement faulty"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P04F3":["Catalyst Heater Supply Voltage Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","48 V or high-voltage supply weak (battery, charge, cold, DC-DC converter)"],["m","Fuse, relay or power switch faulty"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at high-current connector or ground"],["l","Module or voltage measurement faulty"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P04F4":["Catalyst Heater Circuit Low Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Feed shorted to ground (chafed at the exhaust, heat)"],["m","Heating disc shorted to its housing (insulation damage)"],["m","Connector wet or corroded"],["l","Power switch or module faulty"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[100,2500]],"u"],"P04F5":["Catalyst Heater Circuit Low Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Feed shorted to ground (chafed at the exhaust, heat)"],["m","Heating disc shorted to its housing (insulation damage)"],["m","Connector wet or corroded"],["l","Power switch or module faulty"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[100,2500]],"u"],"P04F6":["Catalyst Heater Circuit High Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Wire shorted to the supply"],["m","Heating element or feed open (crack, electrode damage)"],["m","Relay or power switch sticking"],["l","Corroded connector"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[100,2500]],"u"],"P04F7":["Catalyst Heater Circuit High Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Wire shorted to the supply"],["m","Heating element or feed open (crack, electrode damage)"],["m","Relay or power switch sticking"],["l","Corroded connector"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[100,2500]],"u"],"P04F8":["Catalyst Heater Circuit Intermittent Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Loose contact at the heater or module connector"],["m","Wire broken near the exhaust"],["m","Corrosion at ground or connector"],["l","Internal contact point of the heating disc"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[50,800]],"u"],"P04F9":["Catalyst Heater Circuit Intermittent Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Loose contact at the heater or module connector"],["m","Wire broken near the exhaust"],["m","Corrosion at ground or connector"],["l","Internal contact point of the heating disc"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[50,800]],"u"],"P04FA":["EGR A Control Temperature Too High","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. P04FA is a protective check of the electrically driven EGR valve A (DC motor on an H-bridge with position sensor): the module detects overtemperature or thermal overload of the actuator, modelled from current, actuating work and position behaviour, and limits or switches off EGR.",[["h","EGR valve stiff from coking (motor working against resistance)"],["m","High thermal load (full load, poor valve cooling, low coolant)"],["m","Valve motor or H-bridge driver faulty (overcurrent)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light on","Rough idle or jerking if the valve hangs","Loss of power, possibly limp mode"],["medium",[150,700]],"u"],"P04FB":["Crankcase Ventilation Hose Connection Sensor Range/Performance","The crankcase ventilation hose connection sensor is a pressure sensor in the PCV line that measures pressure relative to atmosphere; it is the basis for detecting a disconnected hose (P04DB). P04FB is its plausibility check: GM's 2.0-litre LSY checks the zero point with the engine stopped (at least 10 s, coolant 70 °C or more) – a reading outside ±0.63 kPa or a frozen value sets the code in 128 of 160 samples, MIL after two trips.",[["h","Pressure sensor drifting (zero point error)"],["m","Sensor port or line blocked by oil or ice (value frozen)"],["m","Contact resistance in supply, ground or signal"],["l","Residual crankcase pressure after switch-off (blocked ventilation)"]],["Check engine light on","No change in driving"],["easy",[50,220]],"u"],"P04FC":["Crankcase Ventilation System Performance","The crankcase ventilation routes blow-by gas into the intake, and CARB requires it to be monitored. P04FC is a general function check: pressure or flow in the system deviates from the model.",[["h","Module software (FCA false trigger)"],["m","PCV valve or oil separator blocked or sticking"],["m","Ventilation or fresh-air hose leaking or loose, oil filler cap leaking"],["l","Increased blow-by (piston rings)"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly oil consumption or oil in the intake","Whistling from false air"],["medium",[0,600]],"u"],"P04FD":["EGR Actuator A Control Processor Performance","Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) routes part of the exhaust back into the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx. Some EGR actuators have their own electronics (smart actuators), with position control inside the actuator and communication with the engine module by bus or PWM.",[["h","Engine or actuator software (false trigger)"],["m","Electronics in the EGR actuator faulty (heat, moisture)"],["m","Actuator supply or ground unstable"],["l","Valve stiff, causing a control fault in the actuator"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power possible","Higher NOx"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P04FE":["Catalyst Heater Circuit Performance Bank 1","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Heating disc aged (resistance increased, cracks, electrode damage)"],["m","Supply drops under load (cold or weak 48 V battery, contact resistance)"],["l","Catalyst or exhaust temperature sensor implausible"],["l","Software or calibration"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["hard",[300,2500]],"u"],"P04FF":["Catalyst Heater Circuit Performance Bank 2","An electrically heated catalyst has a heating disc ahead of the catalyst so that it works soon after a cold start; Emitec's EMICAT runs on 12, 24 or 48 V and draws up to about 300 A. It is in series production on 48-volt mild-hybrid diesel vans since 2022.",[["h","Heating disc aged (resistance increased, cracks, electrode damage)"],["m","Supply drops under load (cold or weak 48 V battery, contact resistance)"],["l","Catalyst or exhaust temperature sensor implausible"],["l","Software or calibration"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Higher emissions after a cold start","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["hard",[300,2500]],"u"],"P0500":["Vehicle Speed Sensor A Circuit","The vehicle speed signal A comes, depending on the maker, from a sensor on the gearbox, the transmission output speed, the wheel speeds via ABS/ESP or a CAN message; CARB explicitly names it as an input to be monitored. P0500 is the general code for this signal.",[["h","ABS/ESP module or wheel speed sensor faulty (signal source)"],["h","Output speed sensor or vehicle speed sensor faulty or dirty (metal debris on the magnet)"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted, corrosion"],["m","CAN communication fault (message missing)"]],["Check engine light on","Speedometer dead or jumping","Cruise control not working"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0501":["Vehicle Speed Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The vehicle speed signal A comes, depending on the maker, from a sensor on the gearbox, the transmission output speed, the wheel speeds via ABS/ESP or a CAN message; CARB explicitly names it as an input to be monitored. P0501 means the signal is present but does not fit the driving state or lies outside the physically possible range.",[["h","Wheel speed sensor or tone ring damaged or dirty (ABS source)"],["m","Wrong tyre size, axle ratio or coding"],["m","Sensor with weak signal or damaged tone wheel"],["l","Interference from damaged shielding or ground"]],["Check engine light on","Speedometer dead or jumping","Cruise control not working"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0502":["Vehicle Speed Sensor A Circuit Low","The vehicle speed signal A comes, depending on the maker, from a sensor on the gearbox, the transmission output speed, the wheel speeds via ABS/ESP or a CAN message; CARB explicitly names it as an input to be monitored. Despite its wording, all makers found check plausibility: the car is clearly moving (load, torque, overrun), yet the signal stays at zero.",[["h","Speed or output speed sensor faulty"],["h","Wire open or shorted to ground, connector corroded"],["m","ABS module sends no speed signal"],["l","Tone wheel damaged or sensor dirty"]],["Check engine light on","Speedometer dead or jumping","Cruise control not working"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0503":["Vehicle Speed Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic/High","The vehicle speed signal A comes, depending on the maker, from a sensor on the gearbox, the transmission output speed, the wheel speeds via ABS/ESP or a CAN message; CARB explicitly names it as an input to be monitored. P0503 combines three fault patterns: sporadic signal loss, jumping values and a value that is too high.",[["h","Loose contact, chafed wire, corroded connector"],["m","Interference from damaged shielding or the ignition"],["m","Sensor with weakened magnet or damaged tone wheel"],["l","ABS/ESP fault (signal source)"]],["Check engine light on","Speedometer needle jumps","Cruise control drops out, jerky shifting"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0504":["Brake Switch A/B Correlation","The brake pedal switch has two contacts – A usually for the brake lights, B for cruise control and brake information – working in opposite sense, so the module expects opposite states on every brake application. P0504 means they disagree.",[["h","Brake light switch faulty (burnt or corroded contact)"],["h","Switch misadjusted or retaining clip or stop pad worn"],["m","Wire, connector or brake light fuse faulty"],["l","Moisture in fuse or junction boxes"]],["Cruise control cannot be switched on","Brake lights stay on or do not come on","Selector lever cannot be moved out of P"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0505":["Idle Control System","CARB requires the idle speed control to be monitored: petrol engines must flag a fault if the target idle speed is not reached within +200/−100 rpm, diesels within ±30 %. Older engines use a separate idle air control valve that bypasses the closed throttle; with electronic throttle the throttle itself controls idle and no separate valve exists.",[["h","Throttle body or idle valve dirty or gummed"],["m","False air (hoses, intake gasket, crankcase ventilation)"],["m","Idle valve faulty mechanically or electrically"],["l","Learned values not reset after cleaning"]],["Idle speed fluctuates, too high or too low","Engine stalls at idle or when coasting","Start-stop not working"],["medium",[40,600]],"c"],"P0506":["Idle Control System RPM - Lower Than Expected","CARB requires the idle speed control to be monitored: petrol engines must flag a fault if the target idle speed is not reached within +200/−100 rpm, diesels within ±30 %. Older engines use a separate idle air control valve that bypasses the closed throttle; with electronic throttle the throttle itself controls idle and no separate valve exists.",[["h","Deposits at the throttle or bypass channel, or gummed idle valve"],["m","Extra load: stiff accessory drive, gearbox or converter"],["m","Intake or exhaust restricted (air filter, catalyst)"],["m","Idle valve or throttle actuator faulty, wrong learned value"]],["Idle too low, engine shakes","Engine stalls when stopping or with A/C on","Check engine light after two trips"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0507":["Idle Control System RPM - Higher Than Expected","CARB requires the idle speed control to be monitored: petrol engines must flag a fault if the target idle speed is not reached within +200/−100 rpm, diesels within ±30 %. Older engines use a separate idle air control valve that bypasses the closed throttle; with electronic throttle the throttle itself controls idle and no separate valve exists.",[["h","False air (vacuum hose, intake gasket, brake booster)"],["m","Crankcase ventilation leaking or wrongly routed"],["m","Throttle not closing fully (deposits, ice, stop misadjusted)"],["m","Idle valve stuck open"]],["Idle speed too high","Automatic car creeps at standstill","Hissing from false air"],["medium",[20,500]],"c"],"P0508":["Idle Air Control System Circuit Low","CARB requires the idle speed control to be monitored: petrol engines must flag a fault if the target idle speed is not reached within +200/−100 rpm, diesels within ±30 %. Older engines use a separate idle air control valve that bypasses the closed throttle; with electronic throttle the throttle itself controls idle and no separate valve exists.",[["h","Idle valve coil faulty"],["m","Control wire shorted to ground or open"],["m","Supply missing (main relay, fuse)"],["m","Connector contact at valve or module"]],["Idle fluctuates or is wrong","Engine stalls at idle","Check engine light on at once"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0509":["Idle Air Control System Circuit High","CARB requires the idle speed control to be monitored: petrol engines must flag a fault if the target idle speed is not reached within +200/−100 rpm, diesels within ±30 %. Older engines use a separate idle air control valve that bypasses the closed throttle; with electronic throttle the throttle itself controls idle and no separate valve exists.",[["h","Control wire shorted to positive"],["m","Idle valve with shorted turns"],["m","Connector corroded or wet, interference"],["l","Engine module faulty"]],["Idle too high or fluctuating","Stalling at idle","Check engine light on at once"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P050A":["Cold Start Idle Control System Performance","After a cold start the engine module raises idle speed and air mass and retards the ignition so that much heat goes into the exhaust and the catalyst lights off quickly. CARB requires this cold-start strategy to be monitored with its own codes, separate from ordinary idle and ignition faults.",[["h","Throttle or idle valve dirty or coked, not enough air when cold"],["m","False air or vacuum leak"],["m","Fault in the throttle or idle valve circuit"],["l","Extra load at cold start (alternator, A/C), stiff engine"]],["Check engine light on","Idle after a cold start too low, too high or hunting","Occasional stalling shortly after a cold start"],["medium",[0,350]],"c"],"P050B":["Cold Start Ignition Timing Performance","After a cold start the engine module raises idle speed and air mass and retards the ignition so that much heat goes into the exhaust and the catalyst lights off quickly. CARB requires this cold-start strategy to be monitored with its own codes, separate from ordinary idle and ignition faults.",[["h","Engine module software or calibration"],["m","Idle control takes back the retard because speed would drop (false air, dirty throttle)"],["l","Knock control or knock sensor intervenes"],["l","Extra load or alternator at cold start"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable symptoms","Catalyst warms up more slowly after a cold start"],["medium",[0,250]],"c"],"P050C":["Cold Start Engine Coolant Temperature Performance","P050C is documented only on Bosch ME9 in the Saab 9-3 V6 2.8 turbo: a cold-start plausibility check of the coolant temperature sensor. After a long stop coolant and intake air must read about the same; the code sets if coolant is more than 10 °C below or 15 °C above intake air, once per trip with the warning lamp.",[["h","Coolant temperature sensor drifting, wrong characteristic"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion at the coolant or intake air sensor connector"],["m","Intake air temperature sensor reading wrong"],["l","Auxiliary heater or block heater not recognised, very short stop"]],["Check engine light on","Rough running after a cold start","Radiator fan runs with a cold engine"],["easy",[30,180]],"c"],"P050D":["Cold Start Rough Idle","GM's direct-injection petrol engines (from about 2016) split the injection into two pulses and retard the ignition after a cold start to heat the catalyst. P050D counts engine cycles with incomplete combustion via the crankshaft misfire detection during this strategy; if the rate reaches the catalyst-damaging misfire threshold, the code sets – once per trip, while the strategy is active (under 45 s running, coolant up to 66 °C).",[["h","Injector with poor spray pattern or deposits, or injector circuit fault"],["m","Spark plug or ignition coil weak"],["m","Engine module software or calibration"],["l","Poor fuel or low fuel pressure"]],["Check engine light on (depending on calibration)","Rough idle or jerking in the first seconds after a cold start","Usually unremarkable when warm"],["medium",[0,800]],"c"],"P050E":["Cold Start Engine Exhaust Temperature Too Low","After a cold start the engine module raises idle speed and air mass and retards the ignition so that much heat goes into the exhaust and the catalyst lights off quickly. CARB requires this cold-start strategy to be monitored with its own codes, separate from ordinary idle and ignition faults.",[["h","Idle or air control does not reach the cold-start target (dirty throttle, false air)"],["m","Ignition not retarded enough (knock or torque intervention)"],["m","Engine module software"],["l","Air mass meter reading wrong"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Higher cold-start emissions"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P050F":["Brake Assist Vacuum Too Low","GM (from about model year 2017/18) monitors the brake booster with a vacuum sensor: with the pedal at rest and high intake vacuum the booster must hold its vacuum; a loss over time is filtered as a drift ratio, and above a threshold P050F sets, MIL after two trips. It runs with the engine on, during start-stop auto-stops and after switch-off.",[["h","Leaking vacuum hose or check valve"],["m","Leaking diaphragm in the brake booster"],["m","Vacuum sensor or its seal faulty"],["l","Electric or mechanical vacuum pump weak (where fitted)"]],["Check engine light on","Hard brake pedal, especially after auto-stop","Hissing at the pedal, start-stop stops less often"],["medium",[20,600]],"c"],"P0510":["Closed Throttle Position Switch","Older engines with a mechanical throttle have a separate idle contact on the throttle or the combined throttle position sensor (Bosch Motronic throttle switch, Nissan and Toyota sensors with idle switch). The module uses it to recognise idle – for idle control, fuel cut-off on overrun and idle ignition timing – and as a substitute if the position sensor fails.",[["h","Idle switch or throttle sensor misadjusted"],["h","Switch contacts worn, dirty or corroded"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted"],["m","Throttle not closing (deposits, sticking cable, stop misadjusted)"]],["Idle too high or fluctuating","Engine stalls when coasting","No fuel cut-off on overrun (higher consumption)"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0511":["Idle Air Control Circuit","CARB requires the idle speed control to be monitored: petrol engines must flag a fault if the target idle speed is not reached within +200/−100 rpm, diesels within ±30 %. Older engines use a separate idle air control valve that bypasses the closed throttle; with electronic throttle the throttle itself controls idle and no separate valve exists.",[["h","Idle valve faulty (coil open or shorted)"],["m","Wire open or shorted"],["m","Connector corroded, pins pushed back"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Idle too high, too low or fluctuating","Engine stalls at idle","Check engine light on"],["medium",[50,350]],"c"],"P0512":["Starter Request Circuit","The engine module reads a start request – from the ignition switch, a start button via the body module, or start-stop – and drives the starter relay with it. P0512 flags a fault in this signal.",[["h","Ignition or start switch, or start button, faulty or sticking"],["m","Harness chafed, short to ground or positive"],["l","Selector or clutch switch in the start path"],["l","Starter relay sticking"]],["Engine does not start, starter does not crank","Starter keeps running after the start (noise)","Check engine light (Subaru after two trips)"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0513":["Incorrect Immobilizer Key","The engine module receives a secret code from the immobiliser or body module (Chrysler SKIM/SKREEM/RF hub, Saab CIM); if it does not match the stored code, P0513 sets and starting is prevented – Saab cuts fuel and blocks the starter. The most common trigger is a replaced or reflashed engine module that was not taught to the immobiliser (FCA, Saab), less often handshake timing on FCA hybrids or a key not programmed.",[["h","Engine module replaced or reflashed and not taught to the immobiliser"],["m","Key or transponder not programmed or faulty"],["m","Immobiliser, body or RF hub module replaced or faulty"],["l","Software fault in the handshake"]],["Engine does not start or stops after 1–2 s","Immobiliser or security lamp flashes","Starter does not crank (Saab)"],["hard",[80,600]],"c"],"P0514":["Battery Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The battery temperature sensor – usually an NTC in the battery tray or inside an intelligent battery sensor on the negative terminal – lets the module adjust the charging voltage (higher when cold, lower when warm) and supports battery management and start-stop. P0514 is the plausibility check of the temperature signal.",[["h","Engine module software (false trigger)"],["m","Battery sensor or NTC drifting"],["m","Corrosion at sensor or connector near the battery acid"],["l","Sensor fitted wrongly after a battery change"]],["Possibly check engine light","Charging voltage too high or too low","Start-stop not working"],["easy",[0,250]],"c"],"P0515":["Battery Temperature Sensor Circuit","The battery temperature sensor – usually an NTC in the battery tray or inside an intelligent battery sensor on the negative terminal – lets the module adjust the charging voltage (higher when cold, lower when warm) and supports battery management and start-stop. P0515 is its general circuit fault – open or short – without separating low and high; the charging voltage is then fixed or conservative.",[["h","Sensor or connector corroded (battery acid)"],["m","Wire open or chafed"],["m","Sensor not plugged in after a battery change"],["l","Module"]],["Possibly check engine light","Charging voltage not temperature-compensated","Start-stop or battery management limited"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0516":["Battery Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","The battery temperature sensor – usually an NTC in the battery tray or inside an intelligent battery sensor on the negative terminal – lets the module adjust the charging voltage (higher when cold, lower when warm) and supports battery management and start-stop. P0516 means the signal voltage stays below its limit for a minimum time; with an NTC in a voltage divider a low voltage means very hot, or more often a signal line shorted to ground or an internal short.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground (chafed at the battery tray)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or acid in the connector"],["l","Module"]],["Possibly check engine light","Charging voltage too low","Battery weak, starting problems in the cold"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0517":["Battery Temperature Sensor Circuit High","The battery temperature sensor – usually an NTC in the battery tray or inside an intelligent battery sensor on the negative terminal – lets the module adjust the charging voltage (higher when cold, lower when warm) and supports battery management and start-stop. P0517 means the signal voltage is above its limit, close to the reference; with an NTC that means very cold, or more often an open circuit – sensor unplugged, wire broken – or a short to positive.",[["h","Sensor unplugged or forgotten after a battery change"],["h","Wire or connector open or corroded"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Signal line shorted to positive"]],["Possibly check engine light","Charging voltage too high","Battery gassing or overcharged"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0518":["Idle Air Control Circuit Intermittent","Older engines without electronic throttle have a separate idle air control valve bypassing the closed throttle; CARB requires its output to be checked but not the fault types to be coded separately. P0518 means its circuit fails sporadically – the signal or current briefly leaves its valid range and returns.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the idle valve connector"],["m","Broken or chafed wire in the harness"],["m","Valve coil with shorted turns (temperature-dependent)"],["l","Driver in the engine module"]],["Fluctuating idle","Engine stalls when coasting or stopping","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["easy",[0,200]],"c"],"P0519":["Idle Air Control System Performance","P0519 means the idle control fails functionally with an electrically sound circuit – the actual idle speed deviates strongly despite fully used control. It is documented only on Subaru (also in the Saab 9-2X, electronic throttle, around 2005): idle at least 1000 rpm above target with the control already at zero for 2 or 5 s sets the code with the warning lamp at once, and a fail-safe cuts fuel to cylinders to stop the engine racing.",[["h","False air behind the throttle (intake, gasket, vacuum hose)"],["h","Throttle or idle valve stuck open (foreign body, coking)"],["m","Throttle actuator motor faulty (follow-on P2101)"],["m","Follow-on fault of other codes (sensors, pedal)"]],["Idle far above target, engine races","Jerking from cylinder cut-off in limp mode","Check engine light on"],["medium",[0,450]],"c"],"P051A":["Crankcase Pressure Sensor A Circuit","The crankcase pressure sensor measures pressure in the crankcase ventilation (PCV) against atmosphere; Ford's EcoBoost engines use it for the disconnection check P04DB, which needs a sound sensor. P051A is the sensor's general circuit fault.",[["h","Connector or supply, ground or SENT signal wire open or corroded"],["m","Sensor electronics faulty"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Disconnection check P04DB suspended"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P051B":["Crankcase Pressure Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The crankcase pressure sensor measures pressure in the crankcase ventilation (PCV) against atmosphere; Ford's EcoBoost engines use it for the disconnection check P04DB, which needs a sound sensor. P051B is its plausibility check.",[["h","Sensor zero point drifting"],["m","PCV or blow-by valve sticking, ventilation blocked or frozen (real overpressure)"],["m","Engine module software (known false trigger)"],["l","Increased blow-by from engine wear"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly oil leaks or oil mist (real overpressure)","Often no change in driving"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P051C":["Crankcase Pressure Sensor A Circuit Low","The crankcase pressure sensor measures pressure in the crankcase ventilation (PCV) against atmosphere; Ford's EcoBoost engines use it for the disconnection check P04DB, which needs a sound sensor. P051C means the signal of the analogue ratiometric sensor is too low – Ford sets it below 0.44 V for 5 s.",[["h","5 V reference or signal line open or shorted to ground"],["m","Connector corroded or oily"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","5 V reference pulled down by another sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Disconnection check P04DB suspended"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P051D":["Crankcase Pressure Sensor A Circuit High","The crankcase pressure sensor measures pressure in the crankcase ventilation (PCV) against atmosphere; Ford's EcoBoost engines use it for the disconnection check P04DB, which needs a sound sensor. P051D means the signal is too high – Ford sets it above 4.56 V for 5 s.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal line shorted to positive"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Disconnection check P04DB suspended"],["easy",[40,200]],"c"],"P051E":["Crankcase Pressure Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The crankcase pressure sensor measures pressure in the crankcase ventilation (PCV) against atmosphere; Ford's EcoBoost engines use it for the disconnection check P04DB, which needs a sound sensor. P051E means the signal jumps or briefly drops below or above its limits – physically impossible changes, usually from a loose contact.",[["h","Loose contact at the sensor connector"],["m","Chafed harness"],["m","Oil or water in the connector"],["l","Sensor faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no change in driving","Disconnection check P04DB suspended"],["easy",[0,200]],"c"],"P051F":["PCV Filter Restriction","The crankcase ventilation has an oil mist separator or filter element that can clog; the crankcase pressure sensor then sees rising pressure at a given flow. P051F reports this restriction.",[["h","Separator or filter clogged with oil, soot or sludge"],["m","Icing in the separator or hose on short winter trips"],["m","Oil change intervals exceeded (sludge)"],["l","Pressure sensor reading wrong"]],["Check engine light on","Oil leaks at seals or dipstick","Oil mist in the intake, higher oil consumption"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0520":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit","The oil pressure sensor matters for emissions control because modern engines use it as an input – for two-stage or regulated oil pumps, hydraulic cam phasers and GM's cylinder deactivation, whose lifters switch by oil pressure. P0520 is the general circuit fault, used differently: Ford petrol and diesel engines set it for the digital SENT sensor when it reports an internal fault, does not initialise or sends values out of range (lost communication is U0600), after 5 s.",[["h","Sensor or switch faulty internally"],["m","Connector corroded or oily (oil creeping through a leaking sensor)"],["m","Signal line open or shorted to ground"],["l","5 V reference or supply faulty (usually with other sensor codes)"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Oil pressure gauge or warning wrong","Cam phasing or cylinder deactivation possibly disabled"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0521":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch A Range/Performance","The oil pressure sensor matters for emissions control because modern engines use it as an input – for two-stage or regulated oil pumps, hydraulic cam phasers and GM's cylinder deactivation, whose lifters switch by oil pressure. P0521 is the plausibility check.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck (offset)"],["m","Oil pump control valve or two-stage pump stuck in the wrong stage"],["m","Wrong viscosity, low oil level or foaming oil"],["l","Worn oil pump or bearings"]],["Check engine light on","Oil pressure reading implausible or fluctuating","Cylinder deactivation or cam phasing possibly disabled"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0522":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch A Low","The oil pressure sensor matters for emissions control because modern engines use it as an input – for two-stage or regulated oil pumps, hydraulic cam phasers and GM's cylinder deactivation, whose lifters switch by oil pressure. P0522 means the signal voltage is below its limit: GM 2018 below 5 % of the 5 V reference, Ford petrol below 0.25 V, Ford diesel below 0.018 V, about 5 s.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground or open"],["m","5 V reference failed or shorted"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector corroded or oily"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Oil pressure gauge at zero or warning despite normal pressure","Cam phasing or cylinder deactivation possibly disabled"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0523":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch A High","The oil pressure sensor matters for emissions control because modern engines use it as an input – for two-stage or regulated oil pumps, hydraulic cam phasers and GM's cylinder deactivation, whose lifters switch by oil pressure. P0523 means the signal voltage is above its limit: GM 2018 above 95 % of the 5 V reference, GM 2006 above 4.89 V, Ford petrol above 4.75 V, Ford diesel above 4.992 V, about 5 s.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V reference or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Oil or moisture in the connector"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Oil pressure gauge at maximum","Cam phasing or cylinder deactivation possibly restricted"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P0524":["Engine Oil Pressure Too Low","P0524 is not a sensor fault but a condition: the measured oil pressure is below a minimum depending on speed, load and temperature (Ford diesel 30–90 kPa) for about 5 s, with the sensor itself plausible. Its purpose is engine protection; Ford petrol engines with mid-lock cam phasers drive them into the locked position.",[["h","Oil level too low, oil loss or consumption"],["m","Too thin oil, fuel dilution or clogged pick-up screen"],["m","Oil pump or pressure control valve faulty or stuck"],["l","Worn crankshaft or camshaft bearings"]],["Oil pressure warning and/or check engine light","Engine noise (lifters, timing chain)","Possible power reduction"],["hard",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0525":["Cruise Control Servo Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0525 concerns vehicles with a separate cruise control servo – a vacuum or electric actuator on the throttle cable driven by the engine module: the control circuit is electrically sound, but the response does not match the command. With electronic throttle the engine module controls the throttle itself, so the code hardly applies there.",[["m","Servo worn or sticking, cable misadjusted"],["m","Vacuum leak at hose or diaphragm (vacuum servo)"],["m","Wiring, connector or fuse to the servo"],["l","Brake or clutch switch interrupting the servo release"]],["Cruise control inoperative or switches off","Speed not held or fluctuating","Usually no check engine light"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P0526":["Fan Speed Sensor Circuit","Engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch (GM petrol SUVs and pick-ups 2004–2006, GM Duramax, Ford 6.7 L Power Stroke) have a Hall sensor reporting fan speed; the fan checks P0483, P0494 and P0495 need it. P0526 means no usable signal although the clutch is driven: GM Duramax below 500 rpm with fan duty at least 36 % for 2 s; the 6.6 L LML distinguishes a level stuck low or high; GM petrol V8s set it when no pulses arrive with the engine running.",[["h","Hall sensor at the fan clutch faulty"],["m","Wiring or connector (chafing at the shroud, corrosion)"],["m","Clutch not turning despite command"],["l","Engine module or supply"]],["Check engine light on","Fan permanently on or off, loud fan noise","Weak air conditioning at low speed, overheating under load"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0527":["Fan Speed Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","Engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch (GM petrol SUVs and pick-ups 2004–2006, GM Duramax, Ford 6.7 L Power Stroke) have a Hall sensor reporting fan speed; the fan checks P0483, P0494 and P0495 need it. P0527 means a signal is present but implausible – spikes, jumps or a speed that does not fit the clutch command and engine speed.",[["m","Sensor or trigger element damaged, wrong air gap"],["m","Interference or ground fault on the sensor line"],["m","Viscous clutch slipping or sticking (real speed off)"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Unsteady fan control","Fan permanently on"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0528":["Fan Speed Sensor Circuit No Signal","Engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch (GM petrol SUVs and pick-ups 2004–2006, GM Duramax, Ford 6.7 L Power Stroke) have a Hall sensor reporting fan speed; the fan checks P0483, P0494 and P0495 need it. P0528 means no pulses arrive from the sensor although the fan should be turning.",[["h","Sensor faulty"],["m","Wire open or connector off"],["m","Fan or clutch really not turning"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Fan permanently on or not at all","Tendency to overheat"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0529":["Fan Speed Sensor Circuit Intermittent","Engine-driven fans with an electronically controlled viscous clutch (GM petrol SUVs and pick-ups 2004–2006, GM Duramax, Ford 6.7 L Power Stroke) have a Hall sensor reporting fan speed; the fan checks P0483, P0494 and P0495 need it. P0529 means the speed signal drops out or jumps at times.",[["h","Loose contact at connector or wiring at the fan (vibration, chafing)"],["m","Sensor in the fan clutch intermittently faulty"],["l","Clutch itself faulty"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Fan running permanently or not at all","Loud fan noise"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P052A":["Cold Start A Camshaft Position Timing Over-Advanced Bank 1","Some engines shift the intake (A) camshaft right after a cold start so that more exhaust heat reaches the catalyst; CARB requires each commanded element of this cold-start strategy to be monitored under its own codes. Documented only by Ford (from 2008): during the first 60 s it checks the cam position for 5 s and sets a code above 11° deviation, plus a stuck-pin test (10° commanded, under 2° movement) and an averaged deviation index.",[["h","Cam phaser sticking or lock pin not releasing"],["h","Low oil level, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["m","Oil control valve sticking or screen clogged"],["l","Stretched timing chain"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms","Possibly rattle or rough idle on cold start"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P052B":["Cold Start A Camshaft Position Timing Over-Retarded Bank 1","Some engines shift the intake (A) camshaft right after a cold start so that more exhaust heat reaches the catalyst; CARB requires each commanded element of this cold-start strategy to be monitored under its own codes. Documented only by Ford (from 2008): during the first 60 s it checks the cam position for 5 s and sets a code above 11° deviation, plus a stuck-pin test (10° commanded, under 2° movement) and an averaged deviation index.",[["h","Cam phaser sticking or lock pin not releasing (stuck in base position)"],["h","Low oil level, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["m","Oil control valve sticking or screen clogged"],["l","Stretched timing chain"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms","Possibly rattle or rough idle on cold start"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P052C":["Cold Start A Camshaft Position Timing Over-Advanced Bank 2","Some engines shift the intake (A) camshaft right after a cold start so that more exhaust heat reaches the catalyst; CARB requires each commanded element of this cold-start strategy to be monitored under its own codes. Documented only by Ford (from 2008): during the first 60 s it checks the cam position for 5 s and sets a code above 11° deviation, plus a stuck-pin test (10° commanded, under 2° movement) and an averaged deviation index.",[["h","Cam phaser sticking or lock pin not releasing (bank 2)"],["h","Low oil level, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["m","Oil control valve sticking or screen clogged"],["l","Stretched timing chain"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms","Possibly rattle or rough idle on cold start"],["medium",[80,1400]],"c"],"P052D":["Cold Start A Camshaft Position Timing Over-Retarded Bank 2","Some engines shift the intake (A) camshaft right after a cold start so that more exhaust heat reaches the catalyst; CARB requires each commanded element of this cold-start strategy to be monitored under its own codes. Documented only by Ford (from 2008): during the first 60 s it checks the cam position for 5 s and sets a code above 11° deviation, plus a stuck-pin test (10° commanded, under 2° movement) and an averaged deviation index.",[["h","Cam phaser sticking or lock pin not releasing (bank 2)"],["h","Low oil level, wrong viscosity or sludged oil"],["m","Oil control valve sticking or screen clogged"],["l","Stretched timing chain"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms","Possibly rattle or rough idle on cold start"],["medium",[80,1400]],"c"],"P052E":["PCV Regulator Valve Performance","P052E is a function fault of the crankcase ventilation pressure regulating valve. Audi's 3.0 TFSI has a diaphragm valve whose torn diaphragm or elbow causes false air and whistling; Mercedes' M270/M274 have a switched part-load ventilation valve that blocks with oil carbon and lets oil into the intake – blue smoke, rough running, lack of power; Mercedes extended the emission warranty on the intake module.",[["h","Diaphragm of the regulating valve torn or fatigued"],["h","Part-load ventilation valve blocked (oil carbon, sludge)"],["m","Elbow or hose at the valve cracked, clamp leaking"],["m","Engine module software"]],["Check engine light on","Whistling or hissing in the engine bay","Rough idle, blue smoke or oil consumption, lack of power"],["hard",[80,1200]],"c"],"P052F":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 System Voltage","Diesel glow plugs are switched by a separate glow plug control module fed from the battery, with its own ground, a command from the engine module and a diagnostic line back. P052F means its supply voltage is out of range.",[["h","Main feed to the module open or high-resistance (fuse, connector, corroded ring terminal)"],["m","Poor ground of the glow plug module"],["m","System voltage too low or too high (weak battery, regulator fault)"],["l","Glow plug module faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","Hard cold start, long cranking, white smoke","Several glow plug codes at once"],["medium",[20,600]],"u"],"P0530":["A/C Refrigerant Pressure Sensor A Circuit","The engine module reads the high-side refrigerant pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal rising with pressure) to switch or regulate the compressor, raise the radiator fans at high pressure and pre-control the compressor load at idle. CARB therefore requires A/C components to be monitored where they alter fuel or ignition control or feed other diagnoses (from 2006–2008).",[["h","Connector or contacts corroded or loose"],["h","Pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Wire open or chafed"],["l","5 V reference shorted by another sensor"]],["Air conditioning not cooling (compressor off)","Radiator fan running permanently","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0531":["A/C Refrigerant Pressure Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module reads the high-side refrigerant pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal rising with pressure) to switch or regulate the compressor, raise the radiator fans at high pressure and pre-control the compressor load at idle. CARB therefore requires A/C components to be monitored where they alter fuel or ignition control or feed other diagnoses (from 2006–2008).",[["h","Pressure sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","Refrigerant charge wrong (too little or too much)"],["m","Compressor or control valve delivering poorly"],["l","Condenser dirty or fan weak"]],["Weak or no cooling","Compressor cycling unusually","Usually no check engine light"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0532":["A/C Refrigerant Pressure Sensor A Circuit Low","The engine module reads the high-side refrigerant pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal rising with pressure) to switch or regulate the compressor, raise the radiator fans at high pressure and pre-control the compressor load at idle. CARB therefore requires A/C components to be monitored where they alter fuel or ignition control or feed other diagnoses (from 2006–2008).",[["h","Signal line open or shorted to ground"],["h","Pressure sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded or loose"],["m","5 V reference shorted or failed"]],["Air conditioning not cooling (compressor off)","Radiator fan running permanently","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0533":["A/C Refrigerant Pressure Sensor A Circuit High","The engine module reads the high-side refrigerant pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal rising with pressure) to switch or regulate the compressor, raise the radiator fans at high pressure and pre-control the compressor load at idle. CARB therefore requires A/C components to be monitored where they alter fuel or ignition control or feed other diagnoses (from 2006–2008).",[["h","Signal line shorted to positive (e.g. 5 V reference)"],["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Pressure sensor faulty internally"],["l","Extreme overpressure (overcharge, blocked condenser, fan off)"]],["Air conditioning not cooling (compressor off)","Radiator fan running permanently","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0534":["A/C Refrigerant Charge Loss","P0534 reports too little refrigerant (a leak), not a sensor circuit: the module compares pressure or temperature in the circuit with a model, and the compressor is locked out for protection. FCA's 2020 Ram EcoDiesel sets it with the warning lamp; a software update fixed false setting.",[["h","Refrigerant leak (condenser, joints, compressor shaft seal, service ports)"],["m","Pressure or temperature sensor offset (misjudgement)"],["m","Software or calibration"],["l","Traction battery chiller faulty (electric and hybrid only)"]],["Air conditioning weak or not cooling","Compressor not engaging","Check engine light or high-voltage service message (electric)"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0535":["A/C Evaporator Temperature Sensor Circuit","The evaporator temperature sensor, an NTC thermistor behind the evaporator, prevents icing: near 0–3 °C the compressor is switched off or reduced. Usually the climate module reads it and reports body codes; P0535–P0539 only arise where the engine module controls the compressor and reads the sensor.",[["h","Sensor faulty"],["h","Connector corroded by condensate"],["m","Wire open or shorted"],["l","Engine module"]],["Air conditioning not or weakly cooling","Evaporator icing, airflow decreasing","Compressor cycling unusually"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0536":["A/C Evaporator Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The evaporator temperature sensor, an NTC thermistor behind the evaporator, prevents icing: near 0–3 °C the compressor is switched off or reduced. Usually the climate module reads it and reports body codes; P0535–P0539 only arise where the engine module controls the compressor and reads the sensor.",[["h","Sensor drifting"],["m","Sensor not seated correctly in the evaporator"],["m","Refrigerant shortage or compressor fault"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Fluctuating or weak cooling","Evaporator icing","Compressor switching off too early"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0537":["A/C Evaporator Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","The evaporator temperature sensor, an NTC thermistor behind the evaporator, prevents icing: near 0–3 °C the compressor is switched off or reduced. Usually the climate module reads it and reports body codes; P0535–P0539 only arise where the engine module controls the compressor and reads the sensor.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground"],["h","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Condensate in the connector (leakage to ground)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Air conditioning not cooling","Possible icing","Compressor permanently on or locked out"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0538":["A/C Evaporator Temperature Sensor Circuit High","The evaporator temperature sensor, an NTC thermistor behind the evaporator, prevents icing: near 0–3 °C the compressor is switched off or reduced. Usually the climate module reads it and reports body codes; P0535–P0539 only arise where the engine module controls the compressor and reads the sensor.",[["h","Connector off or corroded, wire open"],["h","Sensor open internally"],["m","Ground line open"],["l","Short to positive"]],["Air conditioning not cooling (compressor locked out)","Vents blowing warm","Usually no check engine light"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0539":["A/C Evaporator Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The evaporator temperature sensor, an NTC thermistor behind the evaporator, prevents icing: near 0–3 °C the compressor is switched off or reduced. Usually the climate module reads it and reports body codes; P0535–P0539 only arise where the engine module controls the compressor and reads the sensor.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the connector"],["m","Broken wire from movement or chafing"],["m","Sensor with internal intermittent contact"]],["Air conditioning failing at times","Cooling fluctuates","Usually no check engine light"],["medium",[20,400]],"c"],"P053A":["PCV Heater A Control Circuit/Open","The PCV heater is an electric element of a few ohms (Saab: 6–10 Ω) at the junction of the ventilation hose and the intake; it stops water in the blow-by gas from freezing and blocking the ventilation in frost. The module switches it to ground.",[["h","Heating element burnt out"],["h","Connector or wire open, corrosion"],["m","Fuse of the ignition supply blown"],["l","Engine module"]],["Often none, except in frost","Check engine light (depending on the maker)","In frost: oil leaks or loss of power from frozen ventilation"],["easy",[30,250]],"c"],"P053B":["PCV Heater A Control Circuit Low","The PCV heater is an electric element of a few ohms (Saab: 6–10 Ω) at the junction of the ventilation hose and the intake; it stops water in the blow-by gas from freezing and blocking the ventilation in frost. The module switches it to ground.",[["h","Control line chafed against ground"],["m","Heating element shorted to its housing"],["l","Engine module driver shorted"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Heater permanently on","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[0,250]],"c"],"P053C":["PCV Heater A Control Circuit High","The PCV heater is an electric element of a few ohms (Saab: 6–10 Ω) at the junction of the ventilation hose and the intake; it stops water in the blow-by gas from freezing and blocking the ventilation in frost. The module switches it to ground.",[["h","Control line shorted to 12 V"],["m","Heating element shorted internally (very low resistance, overcurrent)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Often none, except in frost","Check engine light (depending on the maker)","In frost: oil leaks or loss of power from frozen ventilation"],["easy",[0,250]],"c"],"P053D":["PCV Heater A Performance","The PCV heater is an electric element of a few ohms (Saab: 6–10 Ω) at the junction of the ventilation hose and the intake; it stops water in the blow-by gas from freezing and blocking the ventilation in frost. The module switches it to ground.",[["h","Heating element aged (resistance outside 6–10 Ω, Saab reference)"],["m","Increased contact resistance at the connector"],["m","Partial short in the element (overcurrent, driver overheating)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Often none, except in frost","Check engine light (depending on the maker)","In frost: oil leaks or loss of power from frozen ventilation"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P053E":["Crankcase Pressure Too High","P053E is the system code for crankcase pressure above an operating-point limit while the sensor itself is plausible. Normally the crankcase runs at a slight vacuum (GM: −1 to −5 inH2O).",[["h","Ventilation or oil separator blocked or frozen"],["m","Pressure regulating or PCV valve faulty"],["m","Increased blow-by (piston rings, turbo oil side)"],["l","Leaking vacuum reservoir or pump in the valve cover"]],["Check engine light on","Oil leaks at seals, dipstick pushed out","Higher oil consumption, blue smoke"],["hard",[50,2500]],"u"],"P053F":["Cold Start Fuel Pressure Performance Bank 1","Petrol direct-injection engines often inject twice after a cold start, once late in the compression stroke, to heat the catalyst; this needs rail pressure within a window. P053F reports that the window was not reached often enough.",[["h","High-pressure pump weak or volume control valve sluggish"],["m","Low supply pressure (lift pump, filter)"],["m","Rail pressure sensor drifting"],["l","Pump follower or cam lobe worn"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms","Possibly longer cranking or rough cold idle"],["hard",[100,1200]],"u"],"P0540":["Intake Air Heater A Circuit","Some diesels – typically Cummins engines in Ram pick-ups and commercial vehicles, and Iveco – heat the intake air with a grid heater in the intake manifold instead of or alongside glow plugs. It draws very high current through a relay (on recent Ram a solid-state relay) and is switched by the engine module before and after starting; CARB requires cold-start aids to be monitored.",[["h","Heater relay faulty (contacts welded or burnt, solid-state relay shorted)"],["m","Heating grid burnt out"],["m","Power cable or ring terminal corroded or overheated, control connector"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Check engine light on","Hard cold start, white smoke, rough cold idle","With welded relay: battery drained, heater stays on"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0541":["Intake Air Heater A Circuit Low","Some diesels – typically Cummins engines in Ram pick-ups and commercial vehicles, and Iveco – heat the intake air with a grid heater in the intake manifold instead of or alongside glow plugs. It draws very high current through a relay (on recent Ram a solid-state relay) and is switched by the engine module before and after starting; CARB requires cold-start aids to be monitored.",[["h","Relay control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Relay coil open or solid-state relay faulty"],["m","Connector at relay or module corroded"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Check engine light on","Hard start in the cold","White smoke after cold start"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0542":["Intake Air Heater A Circuit High","Some diesels – typically Cummins engines in Ram pick-ups and commercial vehicles, and Iveco – heat the intake air with a grid heater in the intake manifold instead of or alongside glow plugs. It draws very high current through a relay (on recent Ram a solid-state relay) and is switched by the engine module before and after starting; CARB requires cold-start aids to be monitored.",[["h","Heater relay faulty (solid-state relay shorted, contacts stuck)"],["m","Control line shorted to positive, harness chafed on a power cable"],["m","Power circuit overheated, cable scorched"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Check engine light on","Battery drained when heater stays on","Burning smell or scorched wiring at the relay"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0543":["Intake Air Heater A Circuit Open","Some diesels – typically Cummins engines in Ram pick-ups and commercial vehicles, and Iveco – heat the intake air with a grid heater in the intake manifold instead of or alongside glow plugs. It draws very high current through a relay (on recent Ram a solid-state relay) and is switched by the engine module before and after starting; CARB requires cold-start aids to be monitored.",[["h","Heating grid burnt out"],["m","Relay coil or contact open"],["m","Broken cable, loose nut or ring terminal at the grid"],["l","Fusible link in the heater circuit"]],["Check engine light on","Starting problems in the cold","White smoke or knocking while warming up"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0544":["Exhaust Gas Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 1 Sensor 1","The exhaust gas temperature sensor is a thermistor in a voltage divider with the module's pull-up resistor; the first one (sensor 1) sits before the turbocharger, the oxidation catalyst or after it depending on the maker. Diesels use it for particulate filter regeneration and component protection; CARB names it explicitly as a monitored input.",[["h","Sensor aged or drifting, soot on the tip"],["m","Cable or connector damaged by heat or vibration"],["l","Exhaust leak before the sensor"],["l","Exhaust temperature really deviating (injection fault)"]],["Check engine light on","Particulate filter regeneration missing, filter warning","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0545":["Exhaust Gas Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 1 Sensor 1","The exhaust gas temperature sensor is a thermistor in a voltage divider with the module's pull-up resistor; the first one (sensor 1) sits before the turbocharger, the oxidation catalyst or after it depending on the maker. Diesels use it for particulate filter regeneration and component protection; CARB names it explicitly as a monitored input.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground (chafed on the exhaust or bracket)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Connector damp or corroded"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Particulate filter regeneration missing, filter warning","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0546":["Exhaust Gas Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 1 Sensor 1","The exhaust gas temperature sensor is a thermistor in a voltage divider with the module's pull-up resistor; the first one (sensor 1) sits before the turbocharger, the oxidation catalyst or after it depending on the maker. Diesels use it for particulate filter regeneration and component protection; CARB names it explicitly as a monitored input.",[["h","Sensor element or internal lead open (vibration, heat)"],["h","Connector loose or corroded, broken wire"],["l","Signal line shorted to positive"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Particulate filter regeneration missing, filter warning","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[60,300]],"c"],"P0547":["Exhaust Gas Temperature Sensor Circuit Bank 2 Sensor 1","The exhaust gas temperature sensor is a thermistor in a voltage divider with the module's pull-up resistor; the first one (sensor 1) sits before the turbocharger, the oxidation catalyst or after it depending on the maker. Diesels use it for particulate filter regeneration and component protection; CARB names it explicitly as a monitored input.",[["h","Sensor faulty or aged"],["h","Cable or connector damaged by heat or vibration"],["l","Exhaust leak before the sensor"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Particulate filter regeneration missing, filter warning","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0548":["Exhaust Gas Temperature Sensor Circuit Low Bank 2 Sensor 1","The exhaust gas temperature sensor is a thermistor in a voltage divider with the module's pull-up resistor; the first one (sensor 1) sits before the turbocharger, the oxidation catalyst or after it depending on the maker. Diesels use it for particulate filter regeneration and component protection; CARB names it explicitly as a monitored input.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Connector damp or corroded"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Particulate filter regeneration missing, filter warning","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P0549":["Exhaust Gas Temperature Sensor Circuit High Bank 2 Sensor 1","The exhaust gas temperature sensor is a thermistor in a voltage divider with the module's pull-up resistor; the first one (sensor 1) sits before the turbocharger, the oxidation catalyst or after it depending on the maker. Diesels use it for particulate filter regeneration and component protection; CARB names it explicitly as a monitored input.",[["h","Sensor or internal lead open"],["h","Connector loose, broken wire"],["l","Short to positive"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Particulate filter regeneration missing, filter warning","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[60,350]],"c"],"P054A":["Cold Start B Camshaft Position Timing Over-Advanced Bank 1","Camshaft B is the exhaust camshaft. During catalyst heating after a cold start some engines set it to a specific position; CARB requires such commanded elements to be monitored with their own codes, separate from the normal cam codes.",[["h","Oil pressure building too slowly when cold (oil level, viscosity, pick-up, pump)"],["m","Exhaust oil control valve sticking or screens clogged"],["m","Module software (threshold too tight)"],["l","Cam phaser defective, locking pin"]],["Check engine light on, usually without noticeable symptom","Brief rattle on cold start","Rough idle while warming up"],["medium",[0,900]],"c"],"P054B":["Cold Start B Camshaft Position Timing Over-Retarded Bank 1","Camshaft B is the exhaust camshaft. During catalyst heating after a cold start some engines set it to a specific position; CARB requires such commanded elements to be monitored with their own codes, separate from the normal cam codes.",[["h","Oil pressure building too slowly when cold (oil level, viscosity, pick-up, pump)"],["m","Exhaust oil control valve sticking or screens clogged"],["m","Module software (threshold too tight)"],["l","Cam phaser defective, locking pin"]],["Check engine light on, usually without noticeable symptom","Brief rattle on cold start","Rough idle while warming up"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P054C":["Cold Start B Camshaft Position Timing Over-Advanced Bank 2","Camshaft B is the exhaust camshaft. During catalyst heating after a cold start some engines set it to a specific position; CARB requires such commanded elements to be monitored with their own codes, separate from the normal cam codes.",[["h","Oil pressure building too slowly when cold (oil level, viscosity, pick-up, pump)"],["m","Exhaust oil control valve bank 2 sticking or screens clogged"],["m","Module software (threshold too tight)"],["l","Cam phaser bank 2 defective, locking pin"]],["Check engine light on, usually without noticeable symptom","Brief rattle on cold start","Rough idle while warming up"],["medium",[0,1000]],"c"],"P054D":["Cold Start B Camshaft Position Timing Over-Retarded Bank 2","Camshaft B is the exhaust camshaft. During catalyst heating after a cold start some engines set it to a specific position; CARB requires such commanded elements to be monitored with their own codes, separate from the normal cam codes.",[["h","Oil pressure building too slowly when cold (oil level, viscosity, pick-up, pump)"],["m","Exhaust oil control valve bank 2 sticking or screens clogged"],["m","Module software (threshold too tight)"],["l","Cam phaser bank 2 defective, locking pin"]],["Check engine light on, usually without noticeable symptom","Brief rattle on cold start","Rough idle while warming up"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P054E":["Idle Control System Fuel Quantity Lower Than Expected","Diesel only. At controlled idle the module compares the fuel quantity the idle governor needs for target speed with a model value; CARB sets the limit from 2013 at ±50 % of what a sound engine needs.",[["h","Injectors delivering more than commanded (drift, coking, leaking needle)"],["m","Oil entering combustion (turbo seal, crankcase ventilation)"],["m","Rail pressure actually higher than measured"],["m","Wrong injector correction codes after replacement"]],["Check engine light on","Rough or fluctuating idle","Blue or white smoke, knocking at idle"],["hard",[150,2500]],"u"],"P054F":["Idle Control System Fuel Quantity Higher Than Expected","Diesel only. At controlled idle the module compares the fuel quantity the idle governor needs for target speed with a model value; CARB sets the limit from 2013 at ±50 % of what a sound engine needs.",[["h","Injectors delivering too little (coked, high return flow)"],["m","Rail pressure actually lower, fuel supply (filter, air)"],["m","Mechanical losses: compression, seizing accessories"],["l","EGR valve stuck open or intake blocked"]],["Check engine light on","Idle fluctuating or dropping","Smoke, higher consumption"],["hard",[150,2500]],"u"],"P0550":["Power Steering Pressure Sensor/Switch Circuit","Hydraulic power steering has a pressure switch or sensor on the high-pressure line; when the wheel is turned at standstill, pump pressure and engine load rise sharply, so the engine module raises idle air in advance to stop the engine stalling. That makes it an input to idle control under CARB.",[["h","Connector or wire open or corroded (splash zone at the pump)"],["h","Sensor or switch faulty, often also leaking"],["m","5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Idle drops when steering at standstill, engine may stall","Raised idle (substitute value)"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0551":["Power Steering Pressure Sensor/Switch Circuit Range/Performance","Hydraulic power steering has a pressure switch or sensor on the high-pressure line; when the wheel is turned at standstill, pump pressure and engine load rise sharply, so the engine module raises idle air in advance to stop the engine stalling. That makes it an input to idle control under CARB.",[["h","Pressure switch stuck closed or faulty internally"],["m","Signal line shorted to ground (ground means active)"],["l","Permanently high pressure (steering valve sticking, kinked line)"],["l","Vehicle speed signal faulty (Saab depends on P0501)"]],["Permanently slightly raised idle","No load compensation when steering","Check engine light depending on the maker (Saab: none)"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0552":["Power Steering Pressure Sensor/Switch Circuit Low","Hydraulic power steering has a pressure switch or sensor on the high-pressure line; when the wheel is turned at standstill, pump pressure and engine load rise sharply, so the engine module raises idle air in advance to stop the engine stalling. That makes it an input to idle control under CARB.",[["h","Sensor faulty (Tundra 2007–08 series part)"],["m","5 V supply open or signal shorted to ground"],["m","Connector corroded or oily (leaking sensor)"],["l","Engine module 5 V reference"]],["Check engine light on (Toyota)","Idle drops when steering at standstill","Possible fluid leak at the sensor"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0553":["Power Steering Pressure Sensor/Switch Circuit High","Hydraulic power steering has a pressure switch or sensor on the high-pressure line; when the wheel is turned at standstill, pump pressure and engine load rise sharply, so the engine module raises idle air in advance to stop the engine stalling. That makes it an input to idle control under CARB.",[["h","Sensor faulty (Tundra 2007–08 series part)"],["m","Sensor ground or signal line open"],["l","Signal shorted to voltage"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on (Toyota)","Permanently raised idle or no load pre-control","Possible fluid leak at the sensor"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P0554":["Power Steering Pressure Sensor/Switch Circuit Intermittent","Hydraulic power steering has a pressure switch or sensor on the high-pressure line; when the wheel is turned at standstill, pump pressure and engine load rise sharply, so the engine module raises idle air in advance to stop the engine stalling. That makes it an input to idle control under CARB.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at connector or wire"],["m","Low fluid or air in the system (pressure pulsation)"],["m","Pressure switch worn (contact bounce)"],["l","Steering pump delivering unsteady pressure"]],["Idle speed jumping or fluctuating","Check engine light depending on the maker","Steering pump noise (air, low fluid)"],["easy",[20,250]],"c"],"P0555":["Brake Booster Pressure Sensor Circuit","The brake booster pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal, ground) measures the vacuum in the brake booster. On direct-injection, turbo, stop-start and hybrid engines intake vacuum is scarce, so the engine module watches it and creates vacuum when needed – closing the throttle, suppressing engine stop or running an electric vacuum pump.",[["h","Connector or wire to the sensor open or corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","5 V reference disturbed (short at another sensor on the same reference)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light or brake booster warning","Stop-start inactive","Often no change in driving"],["medium",[40,250]],"c"],"P0556":["Brake Booster Pressure Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The brake booster pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal, ground) measures the vacuum in the brake booster. On direct-injection, turbo, stop-start and hybrid engines intake vacuum is scarce, so the engine module watches it and creates vacuum when needed – closing the throttle, suppressing engine stop or running an electric vacuum pump.",[["h","Leaking or kinked vacuum line or faulty check valve"],["m","Sensor stuck or drifting"],["m","Electric vacuum pump or its relay weak (hybrid, Porsche, turbo DI)"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light and/or brake booster warning","Stop-start not working","Hard brake pedal with real vacuum loss"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0557":["Brake Booster Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","The brake booster pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal, ground) measures the vacuum in the brake booster. On direct-injection, turbo, stop-start and hybrid engines intake vacuum is scarce, so the engine module watches it and creates vacuum when needed – closing the throttle, suppressing engine stop or running an electric vacuum pump.",[["h","Signal line open or shorted to ground, connector loose or corroded"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light or brake booster warning","Stop-start inactive","Often no change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0558":["Brake Booster Pressure Sensor Circuit High","The brake booster pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal, ground) measures the vacuum in the brake booster. On direct-injection, turbo, stop-start and hybrid engines intake vacuum is scarce, so the engine module watches it and creates vacuum when needed – closing the throttle, suppressing engine stop or running an electric vacuum pump.",[["h","Sensor ground open (connector, wire)"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V reference or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light or brake booster warning","Stop-start inactive","Often no change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0559":["Brake Booster Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The brake booster pressure sensor (5 V reference, signal, ground) measures the vacuum in the brake booster. On direct-injection, turbo, stop-start and hybrid engines intake vacuum is scarce, so the engine module watches it and creates vacuum when needed – closing the throttle, suppressing engine stop or running an electric vacuum pump.",[["h","Loose contact at the sensor connector (pin tension, corrosion)"],["m","Broken or chafed wire moving with the engine"],["l","Sensor with internal dropouts"]],["Warning comes and goes","Stop-start inactive at times","Check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["medium",[20,250]],"c"],"P055A":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch B Circuit","Oil pressure sensor B is a second sensor, usually in the oil feed to the cam phasers or valve lift switching – on GM's 2.5 L (2014 Impala and Malibu) in the camshaft cover – while sensor A measures the main gallery. Documented only by GM (type B, two trips); Ford uses no sensor B.",[["h","Connector or harness to sensor B open, corroded or oily"],["m","Sensor B open internally"],["m","Shared 5 V reference or ground disturbed"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing or valve lift switching possibly restricted","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[40,250]],"u"],"P055B":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch B Range/Performance","Oil pressure sensor B is a second sensor, usually in the oil feed to the cam phasers or valve lift switching – on GM's 2.5 L (2014 Impala and Malibu) in the camshaft cover – while sensor A measures the main gallery. Documented only by GM (type B, two trips); Ford uses no sensor B.",[["h","Sensor B drifting or stuck"],["m","Sensor A wrong (comparison fails because of A)"],["m","Real pressure difference: clogged screen, oil level or quality"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing or valve lift switching possibly restricted","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P055C":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch B Low","Oil pressure sensor B is a second sensor, usually in the oil feed to the cam phasers or valve lift switching – on GM's 2.5 L (2014 Impala and Malibu) in the camshaft cover – while sensor A measures the main gallery. Documented only by GM (type B, two trips); Ford uses no sensor B.",[["h","5 V supply or signal open, connector loose or corroded"],["m","Signal shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Sensor B faulty internally"],["l","Module's 5 V reference disturbed"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing or valve lift switching possibly restricted","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[40,250]],"u"],"P055D":["Engine Oil Pressure Sensor/Switch B High","Oil pressure sensor B is a second sensor, usually in the oil feed to the cam phasers or valve lift switching – on GM's 2.5 L (2014 Impala and Malibu) in the camshaft cover – while sensor A measures the main gallery. Documented only by GM (type B, two trips); Ford uses no sensor B.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or 12 V"],["m","Sensor B faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Cam phasing or valve lift switching possibly restricted","Often no noticeable symptoms"],["medium",[40,250]],"u"],"P055E":["Oxygen Sensor Control Module System Voltage","Some engines use wide-band or NOx sensors with their own evaluation module that reports over CAN. That module measures its supply; outside its working range it sets P055E, and lambda control and the sensor diagnoses are suspended because no valid values exist.",[["h","Fuse, relay or supply line to the sensor module"],["m","Poor module ground"],["m","Battery or alternator (under- or overvoltage)"],["l","Sensor module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Lambda control in open loop, possibly higher consumption","Further lambda or communication codes"],["medium",[20,400]],"u"],"P055F":["Engine Oil Pressure Out of Range","P055F is a condition code: the measured oil pressure is above the maximum expected for speed and oil temperature – Ford, which documents it, calls it 'too high' and pairs it with P0524 (too low). Ford checks continuously for 5 s, only with plausible oil pressure, oil temperature and coolant sensors, as engine protection.",[["h","Oil pump control valve or variable pump stuck at high pressure"],["m","Pump relief valve stuck"],["m","Wrong viscosity, very cold oil"],["l","Sensor A reading high within range"]],["Check engine light or oil pressure warning","Possible engine protection or power limit","Possible oil filter or seal leaks"],["hard",[80,1200]],"u"],"P0560":["System Voltage","The module measures its own supply voltage. CARB allows other monitors to be suspended below 11 V, and above a maker's threshold only if the voltage itself is diagnosed – that is what these codes record.",[["h","Battery weak or faulty"],["h","Alternator or regulator (under- or overcharging)"],["m","Jump start or charger at 24 V"],["m","Corroded or loose battery terminals or ground strap"]],["Charge warning","Transmission limp mode (Saab)","Several modules report codes, starting problems"],["easy",[0,600]],"u"],"P0561":["System Voltage Unstable","The module measures its own supply voltage. CARB allows other monitors to be suspended below 11 V, and above a maker's threshold only if the voltage itself is diagnosed – that is what these codes record.",[["h","Fuse, relay or line of the ignition supply (voltage drop)"],["m","Loose or corroded connector or ground at the module"],["m","Voltage regulator fluctuating"],["m","Battery terminal loose"]],["Further voltage or communication codes","Transmission warning or check engine light (GM transmission module)","Sporadic dropouts of electrical functions"],["medium",[0,300]],"c"],"P0562":["System Voltage Low","The module measures its own supply voltage. CARB allows other monitors to be suspended below 11 V, and above a maker's threshold only if the voltage itself is diagnosed – that is what these codes record.",[["h","Alternator or regulator delivering too little"],["h","Battery faulty (shorted cell)"],["m","Corroded terminals, ground straps or alternator cable"],["l","High quiescent current or many consumers at idle"]],["Battery or charge warning lamp","Starting problems, dim lights","Codes in several modules"],["easy",[0,600]],"c"],"P0563":["System Voltage High","The module measures its own supply voltage. CARB allows other monitors to be suspended below 11 V, and above a maker's threshold only if the voltage itself is diagnosed – that is what these codes record.",[["h","Regulator or alternator overcharging"],["m","24 V jump start or fast charger"],["m","Missing battery or poor battery terminal (voltage spikes)"],["l","Alternator control line from the module (smart charging)"]],["Charge warning","Bulbs burning out, flickering lights","Several modules switch off or report faults"],["medium",[0,700]],"c"],"P0564":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input A Circuit","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Cruise or steering wheel switch faulty or dirty (moisture, worn contacts)"],["m","Clockspring or steering wheel connector with contact resistance"],["m","Supply or signal line to the body module open or shorted"],["l","Body module"]],["Cruise control cannot be switched on or switches off","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0565":["Cruise Control On Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","On/off button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Cruise control inoperative, indicator permanently on or off","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0566":["Cruise Control Off Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","On/off button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Cruise control cannot be switched on or off","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0567":["Cruise Control Resume Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Resume button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Stored speed cannot be resumed","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0568":["Cruise Control Set Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Set button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Cruise control cannot be set","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0569":["Cruise Control Coast Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Coast (minus) button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Speed cannot be lowered with cruise control","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P056A":["Cruise Control Increase Distance Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Distance button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Following distance cannot be increased","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P056B":["Cruise Control Decrease Distance Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Distance button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Following distance cannot be decreased","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P056C":["Cruise Control Cancel Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Cancel button stuck or faulty"],["m","Moisture or wear in the steering wheel switch"],["m","Contact resistance in clockspring or steering wheel connector"],["l","Body module or cruise module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or cancels immediately","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,400]],"u"],"P056D":["Reductant Control Module System Voltage","Diesels with SCR may have a separate module for AdBlue/DEF dosing (pump, heaters, dosing valve) talking to the engine module over CAN. It measures its supply; outside its window no safe dosing is possible, so dosing and SCR diagnoses are suspended and NOx rises, and if it persists the SCR warnings and power limits apply.",[["h","Fuse, relay or supply line of the DEF module"],["m","Corrosion at module connector or ground (underbody, road salt)"],["m","System under- or overvoltage"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Check engine light and DEF warning","Possible countdown to start inhibit or power limit","Further SCR codes"],["medium",[30,900]],"u"],"P056E":["Cold Start Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Performance","CARB requires every element commanded by the cold-start strategy to respond measurably in the commanded direction, with its own codes separate from the general monitoring of the same part. During catalyst heating the boost control is positioned deliberately – diesels close the variable turbine for load and heat, petrol engines open the wastegate so heat reaches the catalyst.",[["h","Variable turbine or wastegate actuator stiff (coking, cold)"],["m","Module software or calibration"],["m","Vacuum or boost hose leaking"],["l","Position or boost sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Hard cold start or rough warm-up","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["medium",[0,1500]],"u"],"P056F":["Cold Start Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Performance","CARB requires every element commanded by the cold-start strategy to respond measurably in the commanded direction, with its own codes separate from the general monitoring of the same part. During catalyst heating the boost control is positioned deliberately – diesels close the variable turbine for load and heat, petrol engines open the wastegate so heat reaches the catalyst.",[["h","Charger B actuator stiff or faulty"],["m","Vacuum or charge air line of charger B leaking"],["m","Software or calibration"],["l","Boost or position sensor B"]],["Check engine light on","Rough cold running","Otherwise usually unremarkable"],["hard",[0,2000]],"u"],"P0570":["Cruise Control Accelerate Signal","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["m","Accelerate button faulty (conventional systems)"],["m","Ford adaptive cruise: CAN fault or software of radar module or PCM"],["m","Contact problem in steering wheel switch or clockspring"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Cruise control does not accelerate, adaptive cruise unavailable","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0571":["Brake Switch A Circuit","Brake switch A is the brake light switch or its signal to the engine module. The module needs it to cancel cruise control and for brake-throttle override, stop-start, idle control and the shift interlock.",[["h","Brake light switch misadjusted"],["h","Brake light switch faulty (worn contact)"],["m","Connector, wire or brake light fuse"],["l","Engine or body module"]],["Cruise control cannot be set or switches off","Brake lights not working or permanently on","Possibly ABS/ESC warning (Hyundai/Kia)"],["easy",[15,120]],"c"],"P0572":["Brake Switch A Circuit Low","Brake switch A is the brake light switch or its signal to the engine module. The module needs it to cancel cruise control and for brake-throttle override, stop-start, idle control and the shift interlock.",[["h","Brake light switch faulty or misadjusted (not switching)"],["m","Brake light or switch supply fuse blown"],["m","Signal line open or shorted to ground, connector"],["l","Body or engine module"]],["Cruise control not working","Brake lights not lit (depending on cause)","Wrench lamp (Ford), no check engine light"],["easy",[15,120]],"c"],"P0573":["Brake Switch A Circuit High","Brake switch A is the brake light switch or its signal to the engine module. The module needs it to cancel cruise control and for brake-throttle override, stop-start, idle control and the shift interlock.",[["h","Brake light switch stuck closed or misadjusted (pedal stop buffer missing)"],["m","Brake pedal not returning fully (floor mat, return spring)"],["m","Signal line shorted to positive"],["l","Body or engine module"]],["Brake lights permanently on","Cruise control unusable","Shift interlock disturbed"],["easy",[15,120]],"c"],"P0574":["Cruise Control System Vehicle Speed Too High","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["m","Driving situation (long descent, towing) where speed cannot be held"],["m","Implausible speed signal (speed sensor, ABS wheel speed)"],["l","Software or cruise logic"]],["Cruise control switches off at high speed","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[0,300]],"c"],"P0575":["Cruise Control Input Circuit","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["m","Body module faulty or software (invalid message)"],["m","CAN disturbance (connectors, wires, termination)"],["m","Steering wheel switch fault (B3794 in the body module)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Cruise control inoperative, possibly further U codes","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["hard",[80,900]],"c"],"P0576":["Cruise Control Input Circuit Low","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Switch shorted internally (moisture)"],["l","Module pull-up or reference faulty"]],["Cruise control inoperative","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0577":["Cruise Control Input Circuit High","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Signal or switch ground open (clockspring)"],["m","Signal shorted to positive"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Cruise control inoperative","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0578":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input A Circuit Stuck","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Button mechanically stuck"],["m","Moisture or dirt producing a constant value"],["l","Clockspring or connector"]],["Cruise control reacting unexpectedly","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,400]],"c"],"P0579":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input A Circuit Range/Performance","Cruise control buttons are almost always a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage; the module reading it (GM and Saab from about 2010: the body module; FCA: steering column module or PCM) sends the button state to the engine module over CAN. CARB names cruise control as the example of an emission-neutral diagnosis – it is simply switched off – so GM lists all these codes as type C and Ford as non-MIL: no warning lamp, the engine runs normally.",[["h","Oxidised or dirty contacts at the steering wheel connector"],["m","Switch contact for signal 1 not closing"],["m","Clockspring faulty"],["l","Wiring or module"]],["Cruise control cannot be switched on at times","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light, engine runs normally"],["medium",[0,500]],"c"],"P057A":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit/Open","The brake pedal position sensor is an analogue 5 V sensor, usually Hall-type, giving the pedal position continuously instead of a switch – for brake lights, cruise control, stop-start, brake-throttle override and regenerative braking. GM (from about 2012) and Stellantis read it in the engine module, Saab's 9-4X and 9-5 in the body module; its learned rest position (about 1 V) must be relearned after replacement.",[["h","Connector at the sensor loose or corroded, pins pushed back"],["m","5 V reference, signal or ground line open"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Engine or body module"]],["Check engine light on","Cruise control and stop-start inoperative","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[40,250]],"u"],"P057B":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The brake pedal position sensor is an analogue 5 V sensor, usually Hall-type, giving the pedal position continuously instead of a switch – for brake lights, cruise control, stop-start, brake-throttle override and regenerative braking. GM (from about 2012) and Stellantis read it in the engine module, Saab's 9-4X and 9-5 in the body module; its learned rest position (about 1 V) must be relearned after replacement.",[["h","Sensor misinstalled or not learned after replacement"],["m","Contact fault on 5 V reference or signal causes wrong rest position learning"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (sticking)"],["l","Pedal mechanism stiff, floor mat"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power when accelerating","Cruise control and stop-start inoperative"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P057C":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit Low","The brake pedal position sensor A is an analogue 5 V sensor giving pedal travel continuously; its rest position (about 1 V) is learned. GM reads it in the engine module from about 2012 and uses it as an enabling condition of transmission monitors – hence type A, warning lamp after one trip, unlike cruise control codes.",[["h","Connector or terminals at sensor or inline connector (GM X205) loose, pushed back or corroded"],["m","Signal or 5 V reference shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","5 V reference pulled down by another sensor"]],["Check engine light on (GM, one trip)","Cruise control, brake light or stability functions restricted","Reduced power when accelerating"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P057D":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit High","The brake pedal position sensor A is an analogue 5 V sensor giving pedal travel continuously; its rest position (about 1 V) is learned. GM reads it in the engine module from about 2012 and uses it as an enabling condition of transmission monitors – hence type A, warning lamp after one trip, unlike cruise control codes.",[["h","Ground or signal line open, connector X205 with poor contact"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Signal shorted to voltage"],["l","Sensor misinstalled or misadjusted"]],["Check engine light on (GM, one trip)","Cruise unavailable, brake light or stability messages","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P057E":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The brake pedal position sensor A is an analogue 5 V sensor giving pedal travel continuously; its rest position (about 1 V) is learned. GM reads it in the engine module from about 2012 and uses it as an enabling condition of transmission monitors – hence type A, warning lamp after one trip, unlike cruise control codes.",[["h","Loose contact or fretting corrosion at sensor or inline connector"],["m","Broken or chafed wire near the pedal"],["m","Sensor worn internally (signal jumping)"],["l","Interference on the 5 V reference"]],["Check engine light on (GM)","Sporadic power reduction when accelerating","Cruise control cutting out sporadically"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P057F":["Battery A State of Charge Performance","P057F means the state of charge of battery A behaves implausibly or leaves its expected range – a performance code, not a circuit fault. Its documented use is on plug-in hybrids of the VW group: the 2025 Porsche Panamera E-Hybrid and Bentley Continental and Flying Spur set it in the high-voltage battery controller, which requests the warning lamp; both causes were software.",[["h","Software of the battery controller (state of charge miscalculated)"],["m","State of charge not relearned after deep discharge, long standstill or disconnection"],["l","Battery ageing or cell imbalance"],["l","Faulty current or voltage measurement in the battery system"]],["Check engine light on","Hybrid function restricted (less electric range or power)","State of charge display jumping"],["medium",[80,300]],"u"],"P0580":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input A Circuit Low","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground (steering wheel wiring, clockspring)"],["m","Button block faulty (water, dirt)"],["m","Steering wheel or clockspring connector corroded or off"],["l","Evaluating module"]],["Cruise control cannot be switched on, indicator missing","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P0581":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input A Circuit High","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Clockspring dirty or faulty"],["m","Signal line shorted to positive or ground open"],["m","Button block faulty"],["m","Corroded steering wheel connector"]],["Cruise control inoperative, on the Jeep JK possibly airbag warning","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0582":["Cruise Control Vacuum Control Circuit/Open","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Connector at the servo loose or corroded"],["h","Vacuum valve coil in the servo open"],["m","Wire between servo and engine module open"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0583":["Cruise Control Vacuum Control Circuit Low","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Vacuum control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Vacuum valve coil shorted"],["m","Servo supply missing (P0595)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0584":["Cruise Control Vacuum Control Circuit High","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Vacuum control line shorted to positive"],["m","Servo or vacuum valve faulty internally"],["m","Damp or corroded connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0585":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input A/B Correlation","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Button stuck mechanically (trim, dirt)"],["m","Increased contact resistance on one line (clockspring or column module connector)"],["m","Button block faulty internally"],["l","Clockspring faulty"]],["Cruise control not engaging or cutting out, button sticking","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["easy",[0,400]],"c"],"P0586":["Cruise Control Vent Control Circuit/Open","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Servo connector or terminals"],["h","Vent valve in the servo faulty"],["m","Vent control line open or shorted to ground"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0587":["Cruise Control Vent Control Circuit Low","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Vent control line shorted to ground"],["m","Valve coil shorted"],["m","Servo supply missing (P0595)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0588":["Cruise Control Vent Control Circuit High","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Vent control line shorted to positive"],["m","Vent valve or servo faulty"],["m","Corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0589":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input B Circuit","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Increased contact resistance shifting button voltages"],["m","Button block aged or damp"],["m","Clockspring faulty"],["l","Module input"]],["Cruise or distance control unavailable","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P058A":["Battery Monitor Module Performance","The battery monitor module (intelligent battery sensor) sits on the negative terminal of the 12 V battery: a shunt measures current, electronics also measure voltage and temperature and calculate state of charge, health and cranking ability, reporting over LIN. Energy management uses the data for alternator control, idle boost and stop-start release, which is why the engine module diagnoses it.",[["h","Battery sensor faulty internally"],["m","Poor supply or ground at the sensor (loose terminal, corroded connector)"],["m","Sensor not relearned after battery replacement or disconnection"],["l","Module or engine module software"]],["Check engine light on","Stop-start not available","No driveability problems"],["easy",[60,250]],"u"],"P058B":["Battery Monitor Module Current Monitoring Performance","The battery monitor module (intelligent battery sensor) sits on the negative terminal of the 12 V battery: a shunt measures current, electronics also measure voltage and temperature and calculate state of charge, health and cranking ability, reporting over LIN. Energy management uses the data for alternator control, idle boost and stop-start release, which is why the engine module diagnoses it.",[["h","Ground ring touching the shunt or loose"],["m","Fretting corrosion in the sensor connector, loose ground splice"],["m","Battery sensor faulty"],["l","Retrofitted loads grounded directly at the negative post"]],["Check engine light, often sporadic","Stop-start inactive","Wrong state of charge display or battery warning"],["easy",[0,300]],"u"],"P058C":["Battery Monitor Module Temperature Monitoring Performance","The battery monitor module (intelligent battery sensor) sits on the negative terminal of the 12 V battery: a shunt measures current, electronics also measure voltage and temperature and calculate state of charge, health and cranking ability, reporting over LIN. Energy management uses the data for alternator control, idle boost and stop-start release, which is why the engine module diagnoses it.",[["h","Battery sensor faulty internally (temperature)"],["m","Sensor supply interrupted (fuse, connector)"],["l","Heat from contact resistance at the terminal"],["l","Software"]],["Check engine light on","Battery warning light (FCA case)","Stop-start inactive"],["easy",[5,250]],"u"],"P058D":["Battery Monitor Module Voltage Monitoring Performance","The battery monitor module (intelligent battery sensor) sits on the negative terminal of the 12 V battery: a shunt measures current, electronics also measure voltage and temperature and calculate state of charge, health and cranking ability, reporting over LIN. Energy management uses the data for alternator control, idle boost and stop-start release, which is why the engine module diagnoses it.",[["h","Loose or corroded terminal, ground ring or sensor connector"],["m","Poor ground, loose splice, fault at the dual battery isolation module"],["m","Battery sensor faulty"],["l","Weak main or auxiliary battery"]],["Check engine light on","Stop-start inactive, battery or charge warning","Rarely burning smell in the engine bay (GM)"],["easy",[0,300]],"u"],"P058E":["Battery Monitor Module Temperature Too High","The battery monitor module (intelligent battery sensor) sits on the negative terminal of the 12 V battery: a shunt measures current, electronics also measure voltage and temperature and calculate state of charge, health and cranking ability, reporting over LIN. Energy management uses the data for alternator control, idle boost and stop-start release, which is why the engine module diagnoses it.",[["h","Battery sensor faulty internally"],["m","High contact resistance at terminal or ground ring heating the sensor"],["l","Missing battery heat shield after repair"]],["Check engine light on","Stop-start inactive","Possibly discoloured or scorched terminal"],["easy",[60,250]],"u"],"P058F":["Battery Monitor Module Temperature Too Low","The battery monitor module (intelligent battery sensor) sits on the negative terminal of the 12 V battery: a shunt measures current, electronics also measure voltage and temperature and calculate state of charge, health and cranking ability, reporting over LIN. Energy management uses the data for alternator control, idle boost and stop-start release, which is why the engine module diagnoses it.",[["h","Battery sensor faulty internally (temperature element open)"],["l","Sensor software or initialisation after power loss"],["l","Supply or connector problem at the sensor"]],["Check engine light on","Stop-start not available","No driveability problems"],["easy",[60,250]],"u"],"P0590":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input B Circuit Stuck","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Button stuck mechanically (trim, dirt, spilled drink)"],["m","Short in the button block's resistor ladder"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the switch"],["l","Module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged, button noticeably sticking","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["easy",[0,350]],"c"],"P0591":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input B Circuit Range/Performance","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Oxidised steering wheel connector at clockspring or column module"],["h","Clockspring dirty or faulty"],["m","Button block aged"],["l","Column module or PCM"]],["Cruise control working sporadically or not at all","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["medium",[0,500]],"c"],"P0592":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input B Circuit Low","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Line B shorted to ground (steering wheel, clockspring)"],["m","Button block faulty or damp"],["m","Corroded connector"],["l","Module"]],["Cruise control and distance or mode buttons inoperative","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P0593":["Cruise Control Multi-Function Input B Circuit High","Cruise control buttons form a resistor ladder on one or two signal lines, each button giving its own voltage window. FCA uses two lines with offset windows – signal 1 is input A, signal 2 input B – and both must report the same button; GM evaluates the ratio to the reference.",[["h","Clockspring dirty or faulty"],["m","Line B shorted to positive or ground open"],["m","Button block or connector"],["l","Module"]],["Cruise control inoperative, possibly airbag warning (Jeep JK)","Cruise control locked out","No check engine light"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0594":["Cruise Control Servo Control Circuit/Open","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Brake light switch (cruise contact) faulty or misadjusted"],["m","Supply line open or shorted to ground"],["m","Servo connector or servo"],["l","Engine module output"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Usually no check engine light, code stored only","Possibly brake light problem as well (shared switch)"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0595":["Cruise Control Servo Control Circuit Low","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Supply line from brake switch to servo shorted to ground"],["m","Brake light switch (cruise contact)"],["m","Servo shorted internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0596":["Cruise Control Servo Control Circuit High","Older engines without electronic throttle use a vacuum cruise servo on the throttle cable: a vacuum valve (closed when unpowered) opens the throttle with intake vacuum, a vent valve (open when unpowered) holds or releases it, and a dump valve fed through the brake switch vents it when braking. Where the engine module drives these valves, it checks each circuit and switches cruise off; CARB treats this as emission-neutral, usually without warning lamp.",[["h","Servo supply line shorted to positive"],["m","Brake light switch sticking"],["m","Servo faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or switches off","Cruise indicator goes out or flashes (depending on vehicle)","Usually no check engine light, code stored only"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0597":["Thermostat Heater Control Circuit/Open","A map-controlled thermostat is a wax thermostat with a built-in heating resistor (Valeo: about 10 Ω). Unheated it opens late, for hot running at part load; the engine module heats it by map under full load or towing so it opens earlier.",[["h","Heating resistor in the thermostat open"],["m","Connector at the thermostat loose or corroded, coolant in the connector"],["m","Broken wire or missing supply (fuse, relay)"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Check engine light on (after two trips)","Coolant temperature higher than usual under load","Audi/VW: coolant warning and engine speed limit possible"],["medium",[120,600]],"c"],"P0598":["Thermostat Heater Control Circuit Low","A map-controlled thermostat is a wax thermostat with a built-in heating resistor (Valeo: about 10 Ω). Unheated it opens late, for hot running at part load; the engine module heats it by map under full load or towing so it opens earlier.",[["h","Internal short in thermostat or heater, coolant in the connector"],["m","Control line shorted to ground (chafing)"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Check engine light on","Engine slow to reach operating temperature (possibly P0128)","Weak cabin heating in winter, higher consumption"],["medium",[120,600]],"c"],"P0599":["Thermostat Heater Control Circuit High","A map-controlled thermostat is a wax thermostat with a built-in heating resistor (Valeo: about 10 Ω). Unheated it opens late, for hot running at part load; the engine module heats it by map under full load or towing so it opens earlier.",[["h","Heating resistor in the thermostat shorted"],["m","Control line shorted to positive"],["m","Coolant or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Check engine light on","Higher coolant temperature under load","Coolant warning and engine speed limit (Audi)"],["medium",[120,600]],"c"],"P059A":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Position Sensor Circuit","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. A separately evaluated position sensor, as these titles assume, is described in Ford patents (Hall sensor on the motor or sensor on the linkage).",[["h","Position sensor in the actuator faulty (replaced as a unit)"],["h","Actuator connector corroded or with water ingress"],["m","Wire open in the front bumper harness (accident, rodents)"],["l","Engine module or reference voltage"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter stays in one position (usually open) or does not react","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up in winter"],["medium",[30,600]],"u"],"P059B":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Position Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. A separately evaluated position sensor, as these titles assume, is described in Ford patents (Hall sensor on the motor or sensor on the linkage).",[["h","Louvres blocked (ice, snow, debris, bent bracket) – sensor correct, position wrong"],["m","Position sensor in the actuator drifting or worn"],["m","Basic setting not learned after replacement"],["m","Shaft or linkage between motor and louvres broken"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter stays in one position (usually open) or does not react","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up in winter"],["medium",[50,600]],"u"],"P059C":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Position Sensor Circuit High","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. A separately evaluated position sensor, as these titles assume, is described in Ford patents (Hall sensor on the motor or sensor on the linkage).",[["h","Signal shorted to supply or ground line open"],["h","Connector corroded or with water ingress"],["m","Position sensor in the actuator faulty internally"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter stays in one position (usually open) or does not react","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up in winter"],["medium",[30,600]],"u"],"P059D":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Position Sensor Circuit Low","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. A separately evaluated position sensor, as these titles assume, is described in Ford patents (Hall sensor on the motor or sensor on the linkage).",[["h","Signal shorted to ground or 5 V reference open"],["m","Bumper harness chafed or damaged (accident, parking knock)"],["m","Position sensor in the actuator faulty"],["l","Engine module or reference regulator"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter stays in one position (usually open) or does not react","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up in winter"],["medium",[30,600]],"u"],"P059E":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. A separately evaluated position sensor, as these titles assume, is described in Ford patents (Hall sensor on the motor or sensor on the linkage).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the actuator connector"],["m","Broken wire in the front bumper harness"],["m","Position sensor in the actuator (wear, electronics)"],["l","Moisture after washing or rain"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter stays in one position (usually open) or does not react","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up in winter"],["medium",[30,600]],"u"],"P059F":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Performance/Stuck Off","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. P059F compares commanded and reached position.",[["h","Louvres blocked by ice, snow or debris"],["m","Bent bracket or bumper part blocking the louvres"],["m","Actuator, gear or shaft faulty (shutter stays closed)"],["m","Louvres unhooked or rubbing on horn or intake duct"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter not opening: engine temperature rises in traffic, fan running often","Often after frost, gone after thawing"],["medium",[0,700]],"u"],"P05A0":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Stuck On","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. P05A0 is the counterpart of P059F: at Toyota failure type 72 means the actuator stuck open, 74 slipping; VW, Audi and Mazda use it for sticking in general (Mazda after up to three clearing cycles).",[["h","Debris or ice holding the louvres open or blocking them"],["m","Engine module or actuator software setting the code too early"],["m","Actuator slipping or gear worn"],["l","Louvres or linkage broken"]],["Check engine light on","Grille shutter warning (Toyota, Audi), speed limit (Audi)","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up"],["medium",[0,700]],"u"],"P05A1":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Position Sensor Minimum/Maximum Stop Performance","The active grille shutter (A = upper or only shutter, B = second) closes louvres to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and requires its components to be monitored. P05A1 checks whether the shutter reaches its end stops (fully closed and open), which serve as reference for the position sensor in the self-learning run.",[["h","Louvres not reaching the stop (ice, debris, bent louvres or bracket)"],["m","Actuator or gear weak, linkage worn"],["m","Misassembled or not learned after replacement"],["l","Position sensor in the actuator faulty"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Shutter not opening or closing fully","Possibly higher engine temperature or consumption"],["medium",[0,600]],"u"],"P05A2":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Control Circuit/Open","An active grille shutter closes louvres in the grille to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and treats the actuator as an output component. Smart actuators (Ford 3.0 L diesel, Toyota) check their own coil and supply and report over LIN; simpler ones (VW 1.8 T) have their own supply and a control line from the engine module.",[["h","Actuator motor coil open or shorted"],["m","Connector corroded or damaged (front bumper, splash water, stone chips)"],["m","Front harness damaged (accident, rodents)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter not moving (usually open)","Slightly higher consumption, longer warm-up"],["medium",[80,450]],"u"],"P05A3":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Control Circuit Range/Performance","An active grille shutter closes louvres in the grille to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for cooling; CARB names it as an off-cycle CO2 technology and treats the actuator as an output component. Smart actuators (Ford 3.0 L diesel, Toyota) check their own coil and supply and report over LIN; simpler ones (VW 1.8 T) have their own supply and a control line from the engine module.",[["h","Shutter blocked (ice, snow, dirt, accident damage)"],["h","Actuator with faulty software or defective"],["m","Control line or connector with contact resistance"],["l","Insufficient supply or ground at the actuator"]],["Check engine light on","Shutter not moving or moving incompletely, often also P05A0","Slightly higher consumption"],["medium",[80,450]],"u"],"P05A4":["Active Grille Air Shutter A Control Circuit High","An active grille shutter closes louvres in the grille to cut drag and speed warm-up and opens them for 