measuring wrong)"],["l","Supply shorted to another circuit"]],["Check engine light on","Lower shutter inoperative","Possibly P0563 or further voltage codes"],["medium",[20,450]],"u"],"P05B8":["Restraint Deployment Signal Circuit/Open","After a crash the restraint control module tells the engine module that airbags or belt tensioners have fired; the engine module then cuts the fuel pump (Ford: until the ignition is cycled) and possibly the engine. Depending on the maker the message travels over CAN and/or a dedicated wire with a defined level or voltage drop.",[["h","Signal wire or connector between restraint and engine module open (e.g. after accident repair or seat removal)"],["m","Corroded or spread pin at either module"],["m","Replacement module not configured"],["l","Restraint module output stage"]],["Code stored, driving usually normal","Possibly message that fuel cut-off is unavailable","No check engine light"],["medium",[80,300]],"u"],"P05B9":["Restraint Deployment Signal Circuit Low","After a crash the restraint control module tells the engine module that airbags or belt tensioners have fired; the engine module then cuts the fuel pump (Ford: until the ignition is cycled) and possibly the engine. Depending on the maker the message travels over CAN and/or a dedicated wire with a defined level or voltage drop.",[["h","Signal wire chafed to ground (harness under seat or console)"],["m","Water in the connector (footwell)"],["l","Restraint module output stuck low"],["l","Engine module input"]],["Code stored","Possibly fuel cut-off warning","No check engine light"],["medium",[80,300]],"u"],"P05BA":["Restraint Deployment Signal Circuit High","After a crash the restraint control module tells the engine module that airbags or belt tensioners have fired; the engine module then cuts the fuel pump (Ford: until the ignition is cycled) and possibly the engine. Depending on the maker the message travels over CAN and/or a dedicated wire with a defined level or voltage drop.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to battery or ignition voltage"],["m","Open circuit with pull-up in the engine module"],["m","Retrofitted loads or harness damage after accident repair"],["l","Restraint or engine module"]],["Code stored","Vehicle drives normally","No check engine light"],["medium",[80,300]],"u"],"P05BB":["Restraints Deployed","After a crash the restraint control module tells the engine module that airbags or belt tensioners have fired; the engine module then cuts the fuel pump (Ford: until the ignition is cycled) and possibly the engine. Depending on the maker the message travels over CAN and/or a dedicated wire with a defined level or voltage drop.",[["h","Real accident with airbag or belt tensioner deployment"],["l","Deployment after a hard underbody hit (e.g. off-road)"],["l","False message from wiring or module fault"],["l","Used restraint module with stored crash fitted"]],["Engine stops or will not start (fuel cut-off)","Airbag warning light","Doors unlocked, fuel cut-off message"],["hard",[500,3000]],"u"],"P05BC":["Stop-Start Mode Switch Circuit/Open","The stop-start off switch lets the driver disable automatic engine stop for the current drive. It is read directly or via another module over CAN – on Stellantis' 2025 Ram 1500 it sits in the electronic shifter module.",[["h","Open circuit in switch, connector or wire"],["m","Switch faulty (contact or resistor ladder)"],["m","Connector not refitted after console or dashboard work"],["l","Reading module or software"]],["Stop-start cannot be switched off or stays disabled","Switch indicator not working","Usually no check engine light"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P05BD":["Stop-Start Mode Switch Circuit Range/Performance","The stop-start off switch lets the driver disable automatic engine stop for the current drive. It is read directly or via another module over CAN – on Stellantis' 2025 Ram 1500 it sits in the electronic shifter module.",[["h","Engine module software (Stellantis: fixed by update)"],["m","Sticking or bouncing stop-start button in the shifter module"],["l","Very frequent pressing by occupants"],["l","Shifter module faulty"]],["Usually no noticeable symptoms","Stop-start button not reacting or status jumping","Code stored in the engine module"],["easy",[0,250]],"u"],"P05BE":["Stop-Start Mode Switch Circuit Low","The stop-start off switch lets the driver disable automatic engine stop for the current drive. It is read directly or via another module over CAN – on Stellantis' 2025 Ram 1500 it sits in the electronic shifter module.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Switch shorted internally (moisture, spilled drink)"],["l","5 V reference of the reading module pulled down"],["l","Module input"]],["Stop-start cannot be switched off or stays disabled","Switch indicator not working","Usually no check engine light"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P05BF":["Stop-Start Mode Switch Circuit High","The stop-start off switch lets the driver disable automatic engine stop for the current drive. It is read directly or via another module over CAN – on Stellantis' 2025 Ram 1500 it sits in the electronic shifter module.",[["h","Signal line shorted to positive"],["m","Switch ground open (signal pulled up)"],["m","Switch faulty"],["l","Module input"]],["Stop-start cannot be switched off or stays disabled","Switch indicator not working","Usually no check engine light"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P05C0":["Active Grille Air Shutter Module A Over Temperature","Shutters cut drag and speed warm-up when closed and open for cooling; CARB names them as an off-cycle CO2 technology and treats the actuator as an output component. Smart actuators report over LIN (loss of communication: U0285); a fault usually costs fuel and warm-up time, a closed shutter can make the engine run hot under load.",[["h","Actuator faulty internally (driver overheating)"],["m","Shutter stiff or blocked (ice, dirt, bent louvres) causing continuous current"],["l","High heat load at the mounting point (radiator, traffic, towing)"]],["Shutter inoperative at times","Check engine light possible","Possibly also a performance code"],["medium",[100,500]],"u"],"P05C1":["Active Grille Air Shutter Module B Over Temperature","Shutter B is the second active grille shutter – at GM, Ford and Stellantis the lower one in the bumper. Shutters cut drag and speed warm-up when closed and open for cooling; CARB names them as an off-cycle CO2 technology and treats the actuator as an output component.",[["h","Actuator faulty internally (driver overheating)"],["m","Shutter stiff or blocked (ice, dirt, bent louvres) causing continuous current"],["l","High heat load at the mounting point (radiator, traffic, towing)"]],["Lower shutter inoperative at times","Check engine light possible","Possibly also a performance code"],["medium",[100,500]],"u"],"P05C2":["Engine Coolant Pressure Sensor Circuit","An engine coolant pressure sensor (5 V reference, ratiometric signal) measures pressure in the cooling circuit; in a sealed circuit pressure rises with temperature, so deviations reveal leaks or a loose cap – described in Navistar and GE patents for commercial and rail engines. No manufacturer document available to us uses P05C2–P05C6 for passenger cars; CARB requires input components to be checked for circuit faults, range and plausibility.",[["h","Connector corroded or loose, wire open (coolant and heat zone)"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","5 V reference disturbed"],["l","Module or software"]],["Check engine light or warning (depending on the maker)","Coolant pressure or leak warning unavailable","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P05C3":["Engine Coolant Pressure Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","An engine coolant pressure sensor (5 V reference, ratiometric signal) measures pressure in the cooling circuit; in a sealed circuit pressure rises with temperature, so deviations reveal leaks or a loose cap – described in Navistar and GE patents for commercial and rail engines. No manufacturer document available to us uses P05C2–P05C6 for passenger cars; CARB requires input components to be checked for circuit faults, range and plausibility.",[["h","Sensor drifting or stuck (fouled pressure port, scale)"],["m","Real pressure fault: leak, faulty radiator or expansion tank cap"],["m","Air in the cooling system after filling"],["l","Combustion gas in the coolant (head gasket)"]],["Check engine light or warning (depending on the maker)","Coolant loss or smell with a real leak","Possibly rising temperature"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P05C4":["Engine Coolant Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","An engine coolant pressure sensor (5 V reference, ratiometric signal) measures pressure in the cooling circuit; in a sealed circuit pressure rises with temperature, so deviations reveal leaks or a loose cap – described in Navistar and GE patents for commercial and rail engines. No manufacturer document available to us uses P05C2–P05C6 for passenger cars; CARB requires input components to be checked for circuit faults, range and plausibility.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground or open"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"]],["Check engine light or warning (depending on the maker)","Coolant pressure or leak warning unavailable","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P05C5":["Engine Coolant Pressure Sensor Circuit High","An engine coolant pressure sensor (5 V reference, ratiometric signal) measures pressure in the cooling circuit; in a sealed circuit pressure rises with temperature, so deviations reveal leaks or a loose cap – described in Navistar and GE patents for commercial and rail engines. No manufacturer document available to us uses P05C2–P05C6 for passenger cars; CARB requires input components to be checked for circuit faults, range and plausibility.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Moisture or coolant in the connector"]],["Check engine light or warning (depending on the maker)","Coolant pressure or leak warning unavailable","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P05C6":["Engine Coolant Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","An engine coolant pressure sensor (5 V reference, ratiometric signal) measures pressure in the cooling circuit; in a sealed circuit pressure rises with temperature, so deviations reveal leaks or a loose cap – described in Navistar and GE patents for commercial and rail engines. No manufacturer document available to us uses P05C2–P05C6 for passenger cars; CARB requires input components to be checked for circuit faults, range and plausibility.",[["h","Loose contact or connector corrosion"],["m","Chafed wire (engine movement, heat)"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Air or vapour bubbles at the sensor"]],["Check engine light or warning (depending on the maker)","Coolant pressure or leak warning unavailable","Usually no change in driving"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P05C7":["Economy Mode Switch Circuit/Open","The economy mode switch selects a fuel-saving mode (flatter pedal map, earlier upshifts, sometimes reduced A/C). It is usually a resistor ladder with 5 V reference read by the engine module; CARB names a driver-selectable eco mode as an off-cycle technology whose use must be counted.",[["h","Wire or connector to the switch open"],["m","Switch faulty (resistor ladder or contact)"],["m","Connector loose after console work"],["l","Module input"]],["Eco mode not selectable","Eco indicator not working","Vehicle runs in normal mode"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P05C8":["Economy Mode Switch Circuit Range/Performance","The economy mode switch selects a fuel-saving mode (flatter pedal map, earlier upshifts, sometimes reduced A/C). It is usually a resistor ladder with 5 V reference read by the engine module; CARB names a driver-selectable eco mode as an off-cycle technology whose use must be counted.",[["h","Switch stuck (permanently pressed)"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion shifting the voltage step"],["l","Software"],["l","Module input"]],["Eco mode switching unintentionally or not at all","Indicator flickering or off","Usually no check engine light"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P05C9":["Economy Mode Switch Circuit Low","The economy mode switch selects a fuel-saving mode (flatter pedal map, earlier upshifts, sometimes reduced A/C). It is usually a resistor ladder with 5 V reference read by the engine module; CARB names a driver-selectable eco mode as an off-cycle technology whose use must be counted.",[["h","Signal or reference line shorted to ground"],["m","Switch shorted internally"],["l","Shared 5 V reference loaded by another sensor"],["l","Module"]],["Eco mode not selectable","Eco indicator not working","Vehicle runs in normal mode"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P05CA":["Economy Mode Switch Circuit High","The economy mode switch selects a fuel-saving mode (flatter pedal map, earlier upshifts, sometimes reduced A/C). It is usually a resistor ladder with 5 V reference read by the engine module; CARB names a driver-selectable eco mode as an off-cycle technology whose use must be counted.",[["h","Switch ground open"],["m","Signal line shorted to positive"],["m","Switch faulty"],["l","Module"]],["Eco mode not selectable","Eco indicator not working","Vehicle runs in normal mode"],["easy",[30,200]],"u"],"P05CB":["Vehicle Hardware Incompatibility","P05CB means a control module has found that installed hardware does not match the vehicle configuration – a wrong variant or part number, an unprogrammed replacement module, or messages from another vehicle type. No admissible manufacturer document available to us uses P05CB.",[["h","Replacement module or battery with wrong part number or for another variant"],["h","Module not programmed or configured after replacement"],["m","Faulty module software level"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Power limitation possible","Wrench or powertrain warning lamp","Further functions possibly disabled"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P05CC":["Cold Start A Camshaft Position Timing Performance Bank 1","During catalyst heating after a cold start the engine module commands a specific cam phaser position for more residual gas and exhaust heat; CARB requires this to be monitored under codes separate from the normal cam codes. These 'performance' codes are GM's current form (since about 2015/16; older GM engines and Ford use the direction codes P052A ff and P054A ff): fault when commanded and actual position differ by more than 4° or 6° once the target is stable.",[["h","Insufficient cold oil supply: wrong viscosity, low level, worn oil"],["m","Oil control valve sticking or screen clogged"],["m","Phaser worn or lock pin not releasing; on electric phasers a damaged phaser motor"],["l","Timing chain stretched or skipped"]],["Check engine light on (GM mostly after two trips)","Often no noticeable symptoms, possibly rough cold idle","Rattle from the timing drive (GM electric phaser)"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P05CD":["Cold Start A Camshaft Position Timing Performance Bank 2","During catalyst heating after a cold start the engine module commands a specific cam phaser position for more residual gas and exhaust heat; CARB requires this to be monitored under codes separate from the normal cam codes. These 'performance' codes are GM's current form (since about 2015/16; older GM engines and Ford use the direction codes P052A ff and P054A ff): fault when commanded and actual position differ by more than 4° or 6° once the target is stable.",[["h","Insufficient cold oil supply: wrong viscosity, low level, worn oil"],["m","Oil control valve bank 2 sticking or screen clogged"],["m","Phaser worn or lock pin not releasing; on electric phasers a damaged phaser motor"],["l","Timing chain stretched or skipped"]],["Check engine light on (GM mostly after two trips)","Often no noticeable symptoms, possibly rough cold idle","Higher cold-start emissions"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P05CE":["Cold Start B Camshaft Position Timing Performance Bank 1","During catalyst heating after a cold start the engine module commands a specific cam phaser position for more residual gas and exhaust heat; CARB requires this to be monitored under codes separate from the normal cam codes. These 'performance' codes are GM's current form (since about 2015/16; older GM engines and Ford use the direction codes P052A ff and P054A ff): fault when commanded and actual position differ by more than 4° or 6° once the target is stable.",[["h","Insufficient cold oil supply: wrong viscosity, low level, worn oil"],["m","Oil control valve sticking or screen clogged"],["m","Phaser worn or lock pin not releasing; on electric phasers a damaged phaser motor"],["l","Timing chain stretched or skipped"]],["Check engine light on (GM mostly after two trips)","Often no noticeable symptoms, possibly rough cold idle","Higher cold-start emissions"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P05CF":["Cold Start B Camshaft Position Timing Performance Bank 2","During catalyst heating after a cold start the engine module commands a specific cam phaser position for more residual gas and exhaust heat; CARB requires this to be monitored under codes separate from the normal cam codes. These 'performance' codes are GM's current form (since about 2015/16; older GM engines and Ford use the direction codes P052A ff and P054A ff): fault when commanded and actual position differ by more than 4° or 6° once the target is stable.",[["h","Insufficient cold oil supply: wrong viscosity, low level, worn oil"],["m","Oil control valve bank 2 sticking or screen clogged"],["m","Phaser worn or lock pin not releasing; on electric phasers a damaged phaser motor"],["l","Timing chain stretched or skipped"]],["Check engine light on (GM mostly after two trips)","Often no noticeable symptoms, possibly rough cold idle","Higher cold-start emissions"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P05D0":["Driver Mode Select Switch A Circuit","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Connector at the switch or control panel corroded or loose"],["m","Switch faulty, e.g. liquid in the centre console"],["m","Wire to the reading module open"],["l","Reading module"]],["Drive mode cannot be changed, vehicle stays in normal mode","Mode display missing or jumping back","Check engine light (GM after two trips)"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D1":["Driver Mode Select Switch A Circuit Low","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Signal line to the body module shorted to ground (chafing in the console)"],["m","Switch shorted internally (moisture, spilled drink)"],["m","Connector at the switch damaged"],["l","Body module input"]],["Drive mode cannot be changed, vehicle stays in normal mode","Mode display missing or jumping back","Check engine light (GM after two trips)"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D2":["Driver Mode Select Switch A Circuit High","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Signal or ground line open, connector off at the switch"],["m","Switch faulty (track interrupted)"],["l","Signal line shorted to positive"],["l","Body module"]],["Drive mode cannot be changed, vehicle stays in normal mode","Mode display missing or jumping back","Check engine light (GM after two trips)"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D3":["Driver Mode Select Switch A Range/Performance","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Corroded connector or increased contact resistance"],["m","Switch worn or detent unclear (knob between two positions)"],["m","Moisture in the switch"],["l","Ground offset at the switch"]],["Selected mode not taken over or jumping","Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Mode display missing"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D4":["Driver Mode Select Switch B Circuit","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Connector or wire at switch B open or corroded"],["m","Switch B faulty"],["l","Reading module"]],["Additional drive modes not selectable","Check engine light possible","Vehicle stays in its basic mode"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D5":["Driver Mode Select Switch B Circuit Low","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Signal line B shorted to ground"],["m","Switch B shorted or damp"],["l","Module"]],["Mode selection via switch B inoperative","Check engine light possible","Vehicle stays in its basic mode"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D6":["Driver Mode Select Switch B Circuit High","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Connector off or wire open"],["m","Switch B faulty"],["l","Short to positive"]],["Mode selection via switch B inoperative","Check engine light possible","Vehicle stays in its basic mode"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D7":["Driver Mode Select Switch B Range/Performance","The driver mode select switch (tour, sport, eco, snow, off-road, towing) changes engine and transmission maps such as shift points, pedal map and cylinder deactivation, so CARB requires it to be monitored as an input. GM reads switch A as an eight-position resistor ladder in the body module and sets P05D1–P05D3 in its 2025 tables when a condition is present for 4 s within 5 s, type B with warning lamp after two trips.",[["h","Contact resistance at the connector"],["m","Switch B worn or damp"],["l","Ground offset"]],["Mode via switch B not or wrongly taken over","Check engine light possible","Mode display jumping"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P05D8":["PCV Regulator Valve Control Circuit/Open","Some turbo engines have an electrically controlled crankcase ventilation valve that decides when and how much blow-by gas is drawn into the intake (part-load versus full-load path). The engine module monitors its driver output; CARB requires output components to be checked for circuit faults and the PCV system to be monitored.",[["h","Harness at the valve connector chafed or severed"],["m","Connector not latched or corroded after engine cover or intake work"],["m","Valve coil open"],["l","Engine module driver"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable symptoms","If the valve sticks: oil mist, blue smoke, rough idle"],["medium",[60,500]],"u"],"P05D9":["PCV Regulator Valve Control Circuit Low","Some turbo engines have an electrically controlled crankcase ventilation valve that decides when and how much blow-by gas is drawn into the intake (part-load versus full-load path). The engine module monitors its driver output; CARB requires output components to be checked for circuit faults and the PCV system to be monitored.",[["h","Damaged harness shorted to ground"],["m","Valve shorted internally"],["l","Moisture or oil in the connector"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable symptoms","If the valve sticks: oil mist, blue smoke, rough idle"],["medium",[60,500]],"u"],"P05DA":["PCV Regulator Valve Control Circuit High","Some turbo engines have an electrically controlled crankcase ventilation valve that decides when and how much blow-by gas is drawn into the intake (part-load versus full-load path). The engine module monitors its driver output; CARB requires output components to be checked for circuit faults and the PCV system to be monitored.",[["h","Control line shorted to positive"],["m","Valve coil low-resistance (shorted turns)"],["l","Faulty harness repair (swapped wires)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light on","Often no noticeable symptoms","If the valve sticks: oil mist, blue smoke, rough idle"],["medium",[60,500]],"u"],"P05DB":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit/Open","Brake-by-wire and hybrid systems use a dual-channel pedal travel sensor; channel B runs opposite to A at Toyota (3.6–4.4 V at rest, falling when pressed), so a common fault of both channels shows up. It feeds brake assist and regenerative blending; CARB requires inputs used by other diagnoses to be monitored.",[["h","Harness or connector at the pedal sensor (open, contact fault)"],["m","Pedal sensor assembly faulty"],["l","Retrofitted wiring near the pedal (e.g. trailer brake controller)"],["l","Brake or hybrid module"]],["Brake or regeneration warning light","Check engine light","Regeneration or brake assist reduced, pedal feel changed"],["hard",[80,600]],"u"],"P05DC":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Brake-by-wire and hybrid systems use a dual-channel pedal travel sensor; channel B runs opposite to A at Toyota (3.6–4.4 V at rest, falling when pressed), so a common fault of both channels shows up. It feeds brake assist and regenerative blending; CARB requires inputs used by other diagnoses to be monitored.",[["h","Pedal sensor misadjusted, misassembled or not calibrated"],["m","Contact resistance on signal line B"],["m","Sensor worn internally"],["l","Brake module"]],["Brake or regeneration warning light","Check engine light","Regeneration or brake assist reduced, pedal feel changed"],["hard",[80,600]],"u"],"P05DD":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit Low","Brake-by-wire and hybrid systems use a dual-channel pedal travel sensor; channel B runs opposite to A at Toyota (3.6–4.4 V at rest, falling when pressed), so a common fault of both channels shows up. It feeds brake assist and regenerative blending; CARB requires inputs used by other diagnoses to be monitored.",[["h","Signal line B shorted to ground (harness at the pedal box)"],["m","5 V reference shorted to ground (also sensor A code)"],["m","Pedal sensor faulty"],["l","Brake or hybrid module"]],["Brake or regeneration warning light","Check engine light","Regeneration or brake assist reduced, pedal feel changed"],["hard",[80,600]],"u"],"P05DE":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit High","Brake-by-wire and hybrid systems use a dual-channel pedal travel sensor; channel B runs opposite to A at Toyota (3.6–4.4 V at rest, falling when pressed), so a common fault of both channels shows up. It feeds brake assist and regenerative blending; CARB requires inputs used by other diagnoses to be monitored.",[["h","Signal line B or connector open"],["m","Shared ground or 5 V reference open (sensor A reports too)"],["m","Pedal sensor faulty"],["l","Signal line B shorted to positive"]],["Brake or regeneration warning light","Check engine light","Regeneration or brake assist reduced, pedal feel changed"],["hard",[80,600]],"u"],"P05DF":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Brake-by-wire and hybrid systems use a dual-channel pedal travel sensor; channel B runs opposite to A at Toyota (3.6–4.4 V at rest, falling when pressed), so a common fault of both channels shows up. It feeds brake assist and regenerative blending; CARB requires inputs used by other diagnoses to be monitored.",[["h","Loose contact or fretting corrosion at the sensor connector"],["m","Broken or chafed wire in the moving pedal area"],["m","Sensor track or Hall element worn"],["l","Interference on the shared 5 V reference"]],["Sporadic brake warning and check engine light","Regeneration cutting out at times","Code often only stored as history"],["hard",[80,600]],"u"],"P05E0":["Brake Pedal Position Sensor A/B Correlation","Brake-by-wire and hybrid systems measure brake pedal travel with a two-channel sensor; the channels often run in opposite directions (Toyota RAV4 hybrid 2024–25: one rises from about 1 V, the other falls from about 4 V). P05E0 means both channels are electrically fine but no longer match each other.",[["h","Pedal travel sensor faulty internally (channels drift apart)"],["m","Contact resistance or corrosion in the connector or harness of one channel"],["m","Sensor not calibrated after replacement or pedal work"],["m","Foot resting lightly on the brake while accelerating (GM)"]],["Check engine light or service warning","Brake, ABS or stability warning lamp","Hard brake pedal, reduced brake assist"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P05E1":["A/C Refrigerant Pressure Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The refrigerant pressure sensor (three wires, 5 V reference, signal rising with pressure) tells the engine module the A/C pressure; it uses it to switch the radiator fan, to block the compressor at too high or too low pressure and to pre-control idle load. CARB requires such inputs to be checked for circuit faults and plausibility, but they need not light the lamp if they do not affect emissions.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor connector (front end, splash water)"],["m","Chafed or broken wire (5 V, signal, ground)"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (signal jumps)"],["l","Shared 5 V reference disturbed by another sensor"]],["A/C intermittently not cooling (compressor switched off)","Radiator fan runs unnecessarily or irregularly","Idle speed fluctuates when A/C engages"],["medium",[40,250]],"u"],"P05E2":["A/C Refrigerant Pressure Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The refrigerant pressure sensor (three wires, 5 V reference, signal rising with pressure) tells the engine module the A/C pressure; it uses it to switch the radiator fan, to block the compressor at too high or too low pressure and to pre-control idle load. CARB requires such inputs to be checked for circuit faults and plausibility, but they need not light the lamp if they do not affect emissions.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the sensor B connector"],["m","Chafed or broken harness"],["m","Sensor B faulty"],["l","5 V reference or control module"]],["A/C or heat pump intermittently switched off","Heating or cooling output fluctuates","Warning lamp depending on the maker"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P05E3":["Park Brake Sensor/Switch Circuit","The engine module reads the park brake status, directly or via another module, because strategies depend on it: a parked diesel filter regeneration only runs with the park brake applied (Navistar), idle shutdown and PTO logic use it, and Ford delays an automatic restart while the electric park brake moves. CARB requires such inputs to be monitored.",[["h","Broken wire or corroded connector at the park brake switch or sensor"],["m","Switch or pressure sensor faulty"],["l","Faulty diode in the switch circuit (Volvo)"],["l","Vehicle or brake module delivers a wrong status"]],["Check engine light or OBD warning (heavy-duty)","Park brake warning in the cluster","Parked regeneration cannot be started"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P05E4":["Park Brake Sensor/Switch Circuit Low","The engine module reads the park brake status, directly or via another module, because strategies depend on it: a parked diesel filter regeneration only runs with the park brake applied (Navistar), idle shutdown and PTO logic use it, and Ford delays an automatic restart while the electric park brake moves. CARB requires such inputs to be monitored.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed)"],["m","Sensor 5 V supply missing or shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light or OBD warning (heavy-duty)","Park brake warning in the cluster","Parked regeneration cannot be started"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P05E5":["Park Brake Sensor/Switch Circuit High","The engine module reads the park brake status, directly or via another module, because strategies depend on it: a parked diesel filter regeneration only runs with the park brake applied (Navistar), idle shutdown and PTO logic use it, and Ford delays an automatic restart while the electric park brake moves. CARB requires such inputs to be monitored.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire"],["m","Connector at the switch or sensor loose or corroded"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Signal wire shorted to voltage"]],["Check engine light or OBD warning (heavy-duty)","Park brake warning in the cluster","Parked regeneration cannot be started"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P05E6":["Park Brake Sensor/Switch Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module reads the park brake status, directly or via another module, because strategies depend on it: a parked diesel filter regeneration only runs with the park brake applied (Navistar), idle shutdown and PTO logic use it, and Ford delays an automatic restart while the electric park brake moves. CARB requires such inputs to be monitored.",[["h","New pressure sensor and old switch report differently (switch point, delayed change)"],["m","Faulty diode in the switch circuit"],["m","Low air pressure or leak in the spring brake circuit"],["m","Switch or sensor sticking or faulty"]],["Check engine light or OBD warning (heavy-duty)","Park brake warning in the cluster","Parked regeneration cannot be started"],["medium",[50,400]],"u"],"P05E7":["Park Brake Sensor/Switch Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The engine module reads the park brake status, directly or via another module, because strategies depend on it: a parked diesel filter regeneration only runs with the park brake applied (Navistar), idle shutdown and PTO logic use it, and Ford delays an automatic restart while the electric park brake moves. CARB requires such inputs to be monitored.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at connector or splice"],["m","Chafed wire with intermittent short"],["m","Switch bouncing or worn"],["l","Module connector"]],["Intermittent check engine or park brake warning","Running parked regeneration aborts","Stop-start occasionally blocked"],["medium",[30,250]],"u"],"P05E8":["Engine Oil Bypass Valve Control Circuit/Open","The engine oil bypass valve is an electrically switched solenoid that, according to patents (Hyundai/Kia), routes oil around the oil cooler when cold so the engine warms up faster with less friction, and through the cooler once the oil is hot. It is not the purely mechanical oil filter bypass.",[["h","Connector at the valve loose, corroded or spread"],["m","Valve solenoid coil open"],["m","Broken wire in the harness"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["Check engine light (if rated as emission related)","Oil too hot under load or slow warm-up, depending on rest position","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P05E9":["Engine Oil Bypass Valve Control Circuit Low","The engine oil bypass valve is an electrically switched solenoid that, according to patents (Hyundai/Kia), routes oil around the oil cooler when cold so the engine warms up faster with less friction, and through the cooler once the oil is hot. It is not the purely mechanical oil filter bypass.",[["h","Control wire chafing against ground (block, bracket)"],["m","Shorted turns in the valve"],["m","Moisture or oil in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the module"]],["Check engine light (if rated as emission related)","Oil too hot under load or slow warm-up, depending on rest position","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P05EA":["Engine Oil Bypass Valve Control Circuit High","The engine oil bypass valve is an electrically switched solenoid that, according to patents (Hyundai/Kia), routes oil around the oil cooler when cold so the engine warms up faster with less friction, and through the cooler once the oil is hot. It is not the purely mechanical oil filter bypass.",[["h","Control wire touching battery or ignition voltage"],["m","Valve coil shorted (overcurrent at the driver)"],["m","Connector or wiring damage"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light (if rated as emission related)","Oil too hot under load or slow warm-up, depending on rest position","Usually no noticeable driving symptoms"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P05EB":["Cold Start SCR NOx Catalyst Inlet Temperature Too Low","After a cold start a diesel heats its exhaust actively – retarded and post injection, intake or exhaust throttling, higher idle, EGR cooler bypass – so the SCR catalyst quickly reaches the temperature at which urea dosing can start (around 180–200 °C). P05EB sets when the measured temperature at the SCR inlet stays below the expected or modelled value within a set time after the start: the heating strategy is not working.",[["h","Engine module software or calibration (false trigger)"],["m","Exhaust temperature sensor before the SCR reads low or slow"],["m","Heating measure not effective: throttle, EGR cooler bypass or turbo vanes sticking"],["l","Post or retarded injection not effective (injectors)"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driving symptoms","Higher NOx emissions after cold start (not noticeable)"],["hard",[100,800]],"u"],"P05EC":["Cold Start Injection Timing Performance","To heat the exhaust after a cold start, a diesel shifts its start of injection, usually later, plus post injections. CARB requires faults of such elements during the cold start strategy to be reported under codes separate from normal operation – hence P05EC.",[["h","Engine module software or internal fault (request and execution differ)"],["m","Cold start mode blocked by other faults (coolant or fuel temperature, rail pressure)"],["l","Injector driver or injector circuit"],["l","Weak battery during cold start"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driving symptoms","Possibly more white smoke or rougher running after cold start"],["hard",[100,600]],"u"],"P05ED":["Reductant Heater Control Module Supply Voltage","Some diesels use a separate control module or power stage for the DEF heaters in tank, pump and lines; without heating, frozen fluid cannot thaw in winter and dosing stays blocked. P05ED reports that this module's supply voltage is outside its allowed window – a blown main fuse, an open feed or ground, or system voltage too low or too high.",[["h","Main fuse or feed wire to the heater module faulty, connector corroded (splash zone)"],["m","Poor module ground"],["m","Weak battery or charging fault (system voltage too low or too high)"],["l","Heater control module faulty internally"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","In cold weather: DEF frozen, dosing blocked, further heater codes"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P05EE":["Reductant Pump Supply Voltage Circuit/Open","The electric supply pump in the diesel exhaust fluid (DEF, AdBlue) tank module builds the dosing pressure for the SCR catalyst. Its supply voltage is monitored separately from the pump control circuit (P208A ff); CARB requires SCR components and their outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Connector at the pump or tank module corroded or loose"],["m","Open wire in the harness to the tank"],["m","Fuse or relay of the pump supply faulty"],["l","Pump or tank module open internally"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for low DEF pressure or SCR efficiency"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P05EF":["Reductant Pump Supply Voltage Circuit Low","The electric supply pump in the diesel exhaust fluid (DEF, AdBlue) tank module builds the dosing pressure for the SCR catalyst. Its supply voltage is monitored separately from the pump control circuit (P208A ff); CARB requires SCR components and their outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Supply line shorted to ground (chafed against the frame)"],["m","High contact resistance or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Pump shorted internally or stiff (high current)"],["l","Weak battery or charging system"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for low DEF pressure or SCR efficiency"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P05F0":["Reductant Pump Supply Voltage Circuit High","The electric supply pump in the diesel exhaust fluid (DEF, AdBlue) tank module builds the dosing pressure for the SCR catalyst. Its supply voltage is monitored separately from the pump control circuit (P208A ff); CARB requires SCR components and their outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Pump supply shorted to battery positive in the harness"],["m","Overvoltage from the charging system or a jump start"],["m","Moisture or corrosion causing leakage current in the connector"],["l","Relay or driver does not switch off"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for low DEF pressure or SCR efficiency"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P05F1":["Reductant Pump Supply Voltage Circuit Stuck On","The electric supply pump in the diesel exhaust fluid (DEF, AdBlue) tank module builds the dosing pressure for the SCR catalyst. Its supply voltage is monitored separately from the pump control circuit (P208A ff); CARB requires SCR components and their outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Pump supply relay stuck (welded contacts)"],["m","Semiconductor driver shorted"],["m","External voltage or short to positive in the harness"],["l","Software or calibration fault"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for low DEF pressure or SCR efficiency"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P05F2":["Reductant Pump Supply Voltage Circuit Stuck Off","The electric supply pump in the diesel exhaust fluid (DEF, AdBlue) tank module builds the dosing pressure for the SCR catalyst. Its supply voltage is monitored separately from the pump control circuit (P208A ff); CARB requires SCR components and their outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Relay does not pull in or fuse blown"],["m","Open in the relay control or load circuit"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"],["l","Electronics of the tank module faulty (no feedback)"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for low DEF pressure or SCR efficiency"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P05F3":["Exhaust Aftertreatment Power Relay Control Circuit/Open","A power relay, switched by the engine module through an output driver, feeds aftertreatment consumers such as the DEF pump, tank and line heaters and possibly NOx sensors or the SCR module. CARB requires such outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Relay missing, coil open or not seated properly"],["m","Corroded connector or relay socket"],["m","Harness damaged"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for heaters, pump or NOx sensor supply"],["easy",[20,400]],"u"],"P05F4":["Exhaust Aftertreatment Power Relay Control Circuit Low","A power relay, switched by the engine module through an output driver, feeds aftertreatment consumers such as the DEF pump, tank and line heaters and possibly NOx sensors or the SCR module. CARB requires such outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Control line shorted to ground"],["m","Corroded connector or relay socket"],["m","Harness damaged"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for heaters, pump or NOx sensor supply"],["easy",[20,400]],"u"],"P05F5":["Exhaust Aftertreatment Power Relay Control Circuit High","A power relay, switched by the engine module through an output driver, feeds aftertreatment consumers such as the DEF pump, tank and line heaters and possibly NOx sensors or the SCR module. CARB requires such outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Control line shorted to battery positive"],["m","Corroded connector or relay socket"],["m","Harness damaged"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for heaters, pump or NOx sensor supply"],["easy",[20,400]],"u"],"P05F6":["Exhaust Aftertreatment Power Relay Control Circuit Stuck On","A power relay, switched by the engine module through an output driver, feeds aftertreatment consumers such as the DEF pump, tank and line heaters and possibly NOx sensors or the SCR module. CARB requires such outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Relay contacts welded or sticking"],["m","External voltage on the load side (short to positive)"],["m","Corroded connector or relay socket"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for heaters, pump or NOx sensor supply"],["easy",[20,400]],"u"],"P05F7":["Exhaust Aftertreatment Power Relay Control Circuit Stuck Off","A power relay, switched by the engine module through an output driver, feeds aftertreatment consumers such as the DEF pump, tank and line heaters and possibly NOx sensors or the SCR module. CARB requires such outputs to be checked for circuit faults and function.",[["h","Relay contacts burnt or coil faulty, relay does not close"],["m","Fuse on the load side blown"],["m","Corroded connector or relay socket"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","DEF/AdBlue warning, possibly countdown to no-restart","Follow-up codes for heaters, pump or NOx sensor supply"],["easy",[20,400]],"u"],"P05F8":["Reductant Heater Control Module Performance","P05F8 is the function check of the DEF heater control module: its feedback does not match the command – heating current or power, temperature rise, or an internal module fault. The code is documented for Chrysler's 3.0 L EcoDiesel in the 2014 Ram 1500 and Jeep Grand Cherokee: several bulletins list it together with the heater codes P20BA, P20BE and P20C2, and the fix in each case was only reprogramming the engine module – a diagnostic that was too sensitive rather than a hardware defect.",[["h","Engine module software (Chrysler 3.0 L 2014: diagnostic revised by reflash)"],["m","Heater control module faulty internally"],["m","Heater element or line in tank or hose with abnormal current"],["l","Unstable module supply or ground"]],["Check engine light on","Usually no driveability issue","DEF warning in cold weather, possible heater codes P20BA/P20BE/P20C2"],["medium",[100,900]],"u"],"P05F9":["Stop-Start Status Signal Circuit","A discrete stop-start status line tells the engine module (or another module) whether stop-start is active, inhibited or has just stopped the engine. On most current cars this status travels over CAN instead, so a hard-wired line and these codes are rare; no manufacturer document available to us uses P05F9–P05FB.",[["h","Open status line or connector problem"],["m","Sending module without supply or faulty"],["l","Wrong software or variant coding (stop-start set without hardware)"]],["Stop-start does not work","Stop-start indicator or message in the cluster","Check engine light only with some makers"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P05FA":["Stop-Start Status Signal Circuit Low","A discrete stop-start status line tells the engine module (or another module) whether stop-start is active, inhibited or has just stopped the engine. On most current cars this status travels over CAN instead, so a hard-wired line and these codes are rare; no manufacturer document available to us uses P05F9–P05FB.",[["h","Status line shorted to ground"],["m","Sending module pulls the line to ground permanently (faulty output)"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Stop-start does not work","Stop-start indicator or message in the cluster"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P05FB":["Stop-Start Status Signal Circuit High","A discrete stop-start status line tells the engine module (or another module) whether stop-start is active, inhibited or has just stopped the engine. On most current cars this status travels over CAN instead, so a hard-wired line and these codes are rare; no manufacturer document available to us uses P05F9–P05FB.",[["h","Open status line or connector off"],["m","Sending module faulty or without ground"],["l","Short to voltage"]],["Stop-start does not work","Stop-start indicator or message in the cluster"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P05FC":["Cold Start Fuel Pressure Performance Bank 2","During a cold start, direct injection petrol engines usually raise rail pressure for finer atomisation with multiple injections while the catalyst heats up. P05FC monitors whether the actual rail pressure of bank 2 stays within the target window during this phase – only engines with separate rails and pressure sensors per bank (V or boxer engines with two high-pressure circuits) can set it.",[["h","High-pressure pump or volume control valve of bank 2 weak or sticking"],["m","Low supply pressure (low-pressure pump, filter)"],["m","Rail pressure sensor of bank 2 inaccurate"],["m","Engine module software"]],["Check engine light on","Stumbling, hesitation or rough idle during warm-up","Poorer cold start"],["hard",[100,1200]],"u"],"P05FD":["Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Temperature Too High","Electric variable-geometry turbo actuators use a DC motor with gearing, driven by an H-bridge in the engine module or in the actuator itself. Constant high load – sooted or stiff vanes, running against a stop, an internal motor short – drives up current and temperature.",[["h","Vane mechanism sooted or stiff, actuator works constantly against resistance"],["m","Electric actuator faulty internally (shorted turns, bearing)"],["l","High ambient heat at the actuator (missing heat shield, exhaust leak)"],["l","Engine module or driver"]],["Check engine light on","Loss of power or limp mode","Possibly underboost or overboost codes (P0299/P0234)"],["hard",[300,2500]],"u"],"P05FE":["Active Grille Air Shutter C Performance/Stuck Off","Active grille shutters close the air inlet when little cooling is needed, to reduce drag and speed up warm-up. P05FE is the performance check of a third shutter C – target and actual position do not match, as P059F does for shutter A and P05AE for shutter B.",[["h","Debris or ice blocking the louvres"],["m","Louvres unhooked or broken (impact)"],["m","Shutter C actuator faulty"],["l","Connector, wiring or supply corroded"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Higher coolant temperature under load or slower warm-up","Slightly higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P05FF":["Brake Pressure Sensor/Brake Pedal Position Sensor Correlation","Stop-start decides when to stop and restart the engine from the brake: Ford stops it when the pedal switch is on and master cylinder pressure is high enough, and restarts when pressure falls below a filtered reference. P05FF means brake pressure (from the sensor in the ABS unit, sent over the network) and brake pedal position do not agree – for example pedal pressed without pressure build-up, or pressure without pedal.",[["h","Brake pedal position sensor or switch misadjusted or faulty"],["m","Master cylinder pressure sensor in the ABS unit faulty"],["m","Network communication or software between ABS module and engine module"],["l","Air in the brake system or hydraulic fault (long pedal travel without pressure)"]],["Stop-start disabled","No check engine light at Ford, possibly wrench or service warning","Possible related codes P0504 or brake pressure codes"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P0600":["Serial Communication Link","P0600 means a serial data link that the module relies on has failed. At Ford it is the internal link between the main processor and the monitoring processor ('quizzer') of the electronic throttle safety concept: if data cannot be exchanged or check bytes are invalid, the code sets within about a second (diesel: 5 s), usually with the check engine and wrench lamps and a throttle failure mode such as an RPM limit.",[["h","Internal module fault in the processor-to-processor link"],["m","Faulty module software (update available)"],["m","Open or short in a dedicated serial line between engine and transmission module (e.g. Subaru)"],["m","Low voltage, voltage dips or a poor ground or supply at the module"]],["Check engine light, at Ford also the wrench lamp","Reduced power or RPM limit (throttle failure mode)","Harsh shifts or transmission limp mode where the line links engine and gearbox"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0601":["Internal Control Module Memory Checksum Error","The module continuously calculates a checksum over its program and calibration memory (flash) and compares it with the stored value; on newer units the flash error correction also reports bit errors it cannot repair. A mismatch means the stored software is corrupted.",[["h","Interrupted or faulty reprogramming"],["m","Faulty module software (update available)"],["m","Defective flash memory in the module"],["l","Low voltage, voltage dips or a poor ground or supply at the module"]],["Check engine light on","Often no change in driving","Possibly no start or limp mode if the software is badly damaged"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0602":["Control Module Programming Error","P0602 reports that the module is not, or not completely, programmed for this vehicle. GM checks at power-up and then every second whether a service replacement module still carries no calibration, and sets the code in the first trip; in brake modules a missing calibration set or a sensor ID that does not match also counts.",[["h","Module replaced and not (completely) programmed"],["h","Programming interrupted by a voltage dip or lost connection"],["m","Wrong or incompatible software, calibration or configuration block"],["l","Internal memory defect in the module"]],["Check engine light or service lamp","Engine may not start or runs in limp mode","Reduced engine power"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0603":["Internal Control Module Keep Alive Memory (KAM) Error","Keep alive memory holds learned values – fuel trim, idle and shift adaptation, readiness status – while the engine is off, as long as the module has constant battery power; CARB defines it as memory that is lost when the battery or fuse is disconnected. The module stores a checksum of this memory at shutdown and compares it at the next start (Ford, GM Europe); GM calls the check 'long term memory reset', and the Ford diesel watches the supply of its engine-off timer chip (below 0.25 V).",[["h","Battery disconnected, deeply discharged or replaced"],["m","Interrupted constant power feed to the module (fuse, wire, corrosion)"],["l","Main relay drops out too early, memory not saved completely at shutdown"],["l","Internal memory defect (EEPROM/NVM)"]],["Check engine light on (depending on the maker)","Rough idle or changed shifting until values are relearned","Readiness monitors reset"],["easy",[0,1200]],"c"],"P0604":["Internal Control Module Random Access Memory (RAM) Error","P0604 means the module's working memory (RAM) failed its self-test: a value written to a cell does not read back correctly, a built-in hardware test fails, error correction reports faults, or protected memory is written without permission. GM's engine module scans the whole RAM within 30 s of start-up and then keeps checking in the background, setting the code after repeated failures with the lamp in the first trip; Ford tests RAM during initialisation, the GM gateway with error correction at start (two trips).",[["h","Internal hardware defect of the module (RAM)"],["m","Low voltage, voltage dips or a poor ground or supply at the module"],["l","Faulty module software (update available)"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the module or at its connector"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or transmission limp mode","Intermittent stalling or starting problems"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0605":["Internal Control Module Read Only Memory (ROM) Error","P0605 reports an error in the module's program memory (ROM, today flash), found by a checksum or error-correction check at start-up or in the background. Ford is the main user: its engine and transmission modules compare a ROM checksum and set P0605 with the check engine light; the diesel transmission module tests once per trip at start.",[["h","Program memory corrupted by an interrupted reflash"],["m","Internal flash or ROM defect"],["m","Faulty module software (update available)"],["l","Low voltage, voltage dips or a poor ground or supply at the module"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly starting problems","Transmission limp mode if the transmission module is affected"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0606":["Control Module Processor","P0606 means the module doubts the integrity of its own processor. Checks include a question-and-answer game between main and monitoring processor (GM: new seed via SPI within 450 ms; Ford: 'quizzer' level 3), arithmetic unit and register tests, stack overflow, the order in which safety-critical software runs, and the internal voltage supply (Ford diesel: 5 V rail outside 4.7–5.3 V).",[["h","Faulty module software (update available)"],["m","Low voltage, voltage dips or a poor ground or supply at the module"],["m","Internal hardware defect (processor, voltage regulator, output stage chip)"],["l","Short on a 5 V sensor reference loading the internal supply"]],["Check engine light, at Ford also the wrench lamp","Limp mode with reduced power or RPM limit","Poor throttle response"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0607":["Control Module Performance","P0607 is the general 'module not working correctly' code, used when no specific memory or processor part is named. Ford sets it for wrong instruction execution or too many processor resets in engine and transmission modules; the Ford diesel after five communication errors with its monitoring chip within 20 s (a complete loss of that link is P0600).",[["h","Faulty module software (update available)"],["m","Internal module defect (processor, clock, monitoring chip)"],["m","Low voltage, voltage dips or a poor ground or supply at the module"],["l","Electrical interference, e.g. from a faulty alternator or ignition"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode or reduced power","Brief stumbling or stalling (module reset)"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0608":["Control Module VSS Output A","Many engine or powertrain modules turn the raw wheel or output-shaft speed into a cleaned-up vehicle speed signal and pass it on through their own driver – a square-wave signal whose frequency rises with speed – to the instrument cluster, cruise control, radio or steering. P0608 concerns output A: the module compares the commanded driver state with the actual line state and reports a short to ground or voltage, an open circuit or an internal driver fault, as CARB requires for output components.",[["h","Short or open in the output line A to the receiving module"],["m","Corroded or spread connector pins at the module or receiver"],["m","Faulty receiving module pulling the line to ground"],["l","Internal driver fault in the module"]],["Speedometer dead or jumping","Cruise control not working","Odometer stops counting"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0609":["Control Module VSS Output B","Many engine or powertrain modules turn the raw wheel or output-shaft speed into a cleaned-up vehicle speed signal and pass it on through their own driver – a square-wave signal whose frequency rises with speed – to the instrument cluster, cruise control, radio or steering. P0609 concerns output B: the module compares the commanded driver state with the actual line state and reports a short to ground or voltage, an open circuit or an internal driver fault, as CARB requires for output components.",[["h","Short or open in the output line B to the receiving module"],["m","Corroded or spread connector pins at the module or receiver"],["m","Faulty receiving module pulling the line to ground"],["l","Internal driver fault in the module"]],["Speed-dependent function of the receiver on output B disturbed","Cruise control or add-on device without speed signal","Often no noticeable driving symptom"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P060A":["Internal Control Module Monitoring Processor Performance","Electronic throttle systems use three levels: level 1 runs the engine, level 2 recalculates key values independently on the same processor, and level 3 is a separate monitoring processor that quizzes the main one and can shut down the throttle outputs. P060A means this monitoring processor (level 3) or its cooperation with the main processor is faulty: the question-and-answer game fails, an operating system call ends with an unrecoverable error, or the self-test of the monitoring module fails (Ford diesel: 5 s).",[["h","Faulty module software (update available)"],["m","Unstable supply voltage or poor ground at the module (weak battery, voltage spikes)"],["m","Internal hardware defect (monitoring processor, memory)"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the module or connector"]],["Check engine light, at Ford also the wrench lamp","Reduced power or limp mode","Often no driving symptoms"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P060B":["Internal Control Module A/D Processing Performance","The analog-to-digital converter turns every analog sensor voltage into a number, so a faulty converter can falsify many values at once. The module checks it by timing its start-up calibration (GM 6.6 L diesel: 0.30 s or longer is a fault), by converting fixed internal reference voltages (about 4.73–4.83 V must be read), by grounding a test channel that must then read near 0 V (Ford diesel: over 249 mV is a fault) and by comparing with a second converter.",[["h","Faulty module software (update available)"],["m","Unstable supply voltage or poor ground at the module (weak battery, voltage spikes)"],["m","Internal defect of the converter or its chip"],["l","Shorted sensor input pulling the converter reference"]],["Check engine light, at Ford also the wrench lamp","Reduced power or limp mode","Several sensor or misfire codes at the same time"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P060C":["Internal Control Module Main Processor Performance","P060C reports a fault in the main processor that actually runs the engine, found either by the monitoring processor (Ford 'quizzer', level 3) or by duplicate storage and recalculation (GM). Typical findings are corrupted RAM or ROM data, arithmetic unit errors, memory address violations, a wrong program sequence or internal supply or temperature out of range (Ford diesel: internal voltage outside 1.3–3.3 V, 5 s).",[["h","Faulty module software (update available)"],["m","Unstable supply voltage or poor ground at the module (weak battery, voltage spikes)"],["m","Internal hardware defect (memory, processor)"],["l","Overheating or moisture in the module"]],["Check engine light on","Reduced power or limp mode","Driver assistance such as adaptive cruise unavailable"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P060D":["Internal Control Module Accelerator Pedal Position Performance","The accelerator pedal has two sensor tracks. With P060D the module's own cross-check of the pedal value fails: two processors (GM: main and 'motor' processor) calculate pedal position independently and must agree, and an active test briefly pulls one track to ground – the other must not follow, otherwise the tracks are shorted together.",[["h","Short between the two pedal sensor signal lines or moisture in the pedal connector"],["m","Faulty pedal sensor (tracks drifting)"],["m","Faulty module software (update available)"],["l","Internal module fault (converter, processor)"]],["Reduced power, engine barely responds to the pedal","Check engine light or wrench lamp","Cruise control disabled"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P060E":["Internal Control Module Throttle Position Performance","P060E is the throttle counterpart of P060D. The throttle body also has two position sensor tracks, and the module checks whether it can still detect a short between them: during an active test one track is influenced briefly, and the second processor (GM 'motor processor') confirms that the test actually ran.",[["h","Short between the throttle sensor signal lines or moisture in the throttle connector"],["m","Faulty throttle body (sensor)"],["m","Internal module fault (test circuit, second processor)"],["l","Faulty module software (update available)"]],["Reduced power","Check engine light (GM) or service lamp (GM Europe)","Hesitant or jerky throttle response"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P060F":["Internal Control Module Coolant Temperature Performance","Coolant temperature feeds the torque model of the engine (friction, cold start), which the level 2 monitoring of the electronic throttle concept recalculates. P060F means the module's internal cross-check of this value failed: the temperature used by the main software and the value captured or recalculated by the monitoring level or a second converter path do not agree.",[["m","Internal module fault in a converter or processor path"],["m","Faulty module software (update available)"],["l","Loose contact at the coolant temperature sensor that jumps between samples"],["l","Unstable supply voltage or poor ground at the module (weak battery, voltage spikes)"]],["Check engine light or service lamp","Possibly reduced power","Radiator fan runs constantly (substitute value)"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0610":["Control Module Vehicle Options Error","The module checks its vehicle-specific configuration for completeness and validity: at Ford the vehicle identification block or as-built data with tyre circumference, axle ratio, transmission and options; at GM the programmed vehicle options. Ford 2012 sets P0610 when one or more fields of that block are configured wrongly, the Ford diesel checks continuously within 0.5 s; GM and Saab V6 engines set it in the first trip, also for incomplete programming.",[["h","Module not or wrongly configured after replacement (used part, missing as-built data)"],["m","Interrupted or faulty reprogramming"],["m","Changed tyre size or axle ratio without updating the data"],["l","Internal memory fault of the module"]],["Check engine light (depending on maker and variant)","Wrong speedometer reading or shift points","Optional functions such as cruise control missing"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P0611":["Fuel Injector Control Module Performance","P0611 concerns systems with a separate injector control module or injector driver stage. On GM's 6.6 L Duramax LB7/LLY the injector driver unit reports a microcontroller, monitoring or converter fault or a timeout to the engine module over CAN; the code sets after 30 of 50 checks at 125 ms with battery voltage of 10–18 V, and the lamp follows after two trips.",[["h","Faulty voltage converter or driver stage in the injector module (Ford 6.0 L)"],["h","Low battery voltage, weak batteries or poor ground"],["m","CAN or supply line between engine and injector module disturbed"],["l","Faulty module software (update available)"]],["Hard or no start, especially when cold","Rough running, misfires","Lack of power"],["hard",[300,2000]],"c"],"P0612":["Fuel Injector Control Module Relay Control","Some diesels have a separate injector driver module that is powered through a relay switched by the engine module – GM's 6.6 L Duramax LB7/LLY with its injector driver unit, Ford's 6.0 L Power Stroke with its injector control module. P0612 means the commanded relay state and the voltage measured back at the output do not match.",[["h","Injector module relay faulty (coil open, contacts welded)"],["m","Relay control or feedback wire open or corroded, fuse"],["m","Low battery voltage or ground problems"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Engine does not start or stalls while driving","Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Battery drain if the relay does not drop out"],["medium",[20,900]],"c"],"P0613":["TCM Processor","P0613 reports an internal processor or calculation fault in the transmission control module. It is usually set by the transmission module itself – the scan tool shows which module stored it; on some architectures the engine module reports it on the transmission module's behalf.",[["h","Transmission module software (self-diagnosis triggers falsely)"],["m","Voltage dip, poor ground or supply at the transmission module"],["m","Interrupted or faulty programming"],["l","Internal hardware defect (moisture, overheating)"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Transmission limp mode (fixed gear, harsh shifts)","Implausible shifting"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P0614":["ECM/TCM Incompatible","P0614 means the software or calibration variant of the engine module does not match the fitted transmission module or transmission type, or the transmission module is not registered. CARB allows modules with several calibration variants (for example manual and automatic) to share one calibration ID only if a wrong variant turns on the lamp – exactly this check.",[["h","Engine or transmission module replaced and not matched or registered"],["m","Used module with the wrong variant (different gearbox or engine)"],["m","Software update on only one of the two modules"],["l","Internal engine module fault"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly transmission limp mode or limited shifting","Sometimes no other symptoms"],["medium",[80,400]],"u"],"P0615":["Starter Relay A Circuit","The engine module switches the starter relay coil through its own driver – GM petrol engines with 12 V stop-start use a low-side driver, the Saab 9-4X switches 12 V to the coil. CARB requires output components to be checked for open and short circuits.",[["h","Starter relay faulty (coil open)"],["m","Control wire open, harness chafed or pinched, connector corroded"],["m","Relay ground, fuse or supply faulty"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Engine does not crank","Stop-start not working","Intermittent starting problems"],["easy",[15,300]],"c"],"P0616":["Starter Relay A Circuit Low","The engine module switches the starter relay coil through its own driver – GM petrol engines with 12 V stop-start use a low-side driver, the Saab 9-4X switches 12 V to the coil. CARB requires output components to be checked for open and short circuits.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Relay coil shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the relay or fuse box"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Engine does not crank","Stop-start disabled","Possibly blown fuse in the relay circuit"],["easy",[15,300]],"c"],"P0617":["Starter Relay A Circuit High","The engine module switches the starter relay coil through its own driver – GM petrol engines with 12 V stop-start use a low-side driver, the Saab 9-4X switches 12 V to the coil. CARB requires output components to be checked for open and short circuits.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a battery feed (harness damage)"],["m","Relay faulty internally (contact to coil, welded)"],["l","Voltage fed in by retrofitted electrics (remote start, alarm)"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Engine does not crank, or the starter keeps running","Stop-start not working","Battery drained"],["easy",[15,400]],"c"],"P0618":["Alternative Fuel Control Module KAM Error","CARB treats gas-fuelled vehicles (CNG, LPG, also the gas mode of bi-fuel cars) like petrol cars up to model year 2018 and under an approved manufacturer plan afterwards. P0618 is meant for vehicles with a separate control module for the alternative fuel: it reports an error in that module's keep alive memory, which holds learned values while the ignition is off and loses them when power is cut – the gas counterpart of P0603.",[["h","Power interruption at the gas module (battery disconnected, fuse, loose contact)"],["m","Poor supply, ground or corrosion at the module (underbody mounting)"],["m","Software or programming fault of the gas module"],["l","Internal memory defect"]],["Check engine or gas system warning light","Switch to petrol, gas mode blocked (bi-fuel)","Rough idle until values are relearned"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P0619":["Alternative Fuel Control Module RAM/ROM Error","CARB treats gas-fuelled vehicles (CNG, LPG, also the gas mode of bi-fuel cars) like petrol cars up to model year 2018 and under an approved manufacturer plan afterwards. P0619 means the separate alternative fuel module reports an error in its working memory (RAM, read/write test) or program memory (ROM/flash, checksum) – one code for both, while the engine module uses P0604 and P0605.",[["h","Interrupted or faulty programming of the gas module"],["m","Voltage dips, supply, ground or corrosion"],["m","Internal hardware defect (memory chip)"],["l","Manufacturer software fault"]],["Check engine or gas system warning light","No gas operation, switch to petrol","Starting problems on dedicated gas engines"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P061A":["Internal Control Module Torque Performance","Electronic throttle systems run a second, independent calculation on the same processor (level 2, at Ford 'IPC – independent plausibility check') that recomputes driver demand, permitted and actual torque and their inputs, and compares them with the main software. P061A is the result of the torque comparison: the commanded or estimated torque exceeds the permitted torque for the current operating point (Ford diesel 2023: within 0.1 s).",[["h","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["m","Contradictory inputs (pedal, throttle, air mass, cruise request) produce an implausible torque demand"],["m","Voltage dips, poor ground or supply at the module"],["l","Internal defect of the engine module"]],["Reduced power or limp mode, poor throttle response","Wrench, service or check engine lamp depending on the maker","Cruise control disabled"],["hard",[80,1200]],"c"],"P061B":["Internal Control Module Torque Calculation Performance","Electronic throttle systems run a second, independent calculation on the same processor (level 2, at Ford 'IPC – independent plausibility check') that recomputes driver demand, permitted and actual torque and their inputs, and compares them with the main software. P061B means the torque calculation itself is inconsistent: main software and monitoring compute the torque differently.",[["h","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["m","Dirty throttle or air path, idle adaptation at its limit (petrol)"],["m","Voltage dips, poor ground or supply at the module"],["l","Injector quantity deviation pushing the zero-fuel calibration out of range (diesel)"]],["Check engine light and wrench lamp","Reduced power or limp mode","Fluctuating idle (Ford petrol with dirty throttle)"],["hard",[80,1200]],"c"],"P061C":["Internal Control Module Engine RPM Performance","Electronic throttle systems run a second, independent calculation on the same processor (level 2, at Ford 'IPC – independent plausibility check') that recomputes driver demand, permitted and actual torque and their inputs, and compares them with the main software. P061C means the two internal engine speed calculations from the crankshaft signal disagree.",[["h","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["m","Disturbed crankshaft signal (sensor, reluctor, shielding, connector, interference)"],["m","Voltage dips, poor ground or supply at the module"],["l","Internal defect of the engine module"]],["Check engine, wrench or service lamp","Reduced power or RPM limit","Stumbling or brief misfires with a disturbed signal"],["hard",[80,1200]],"c"],"P061D":["Internal Control Module Engine Air Mass Performance","Electronic throttle systems run a second, independent calculation on the same processor (level 2, at Ford 'IPC – independent plausibility check') that recomputes driver demand, permitted and actual torque and their inputs, and compares them with the main software. P061D means the independent recalculation of air mass or engine load disagrees with the main software's value.",[["m","Implausible air mass or load signal (dirty MAF, vacuum leak after the sensor)"],["h","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["l","Dirty throttle, adaptation at its limit"],["m","Voltage dips, poor ground or supply at the module"]],["Check engine and wrench lamp (Ford) or service lamp","Reduced power, poor throttle response","Often air flow or mixture codes as well"],["hard",[80,1200]],"c"],"P061E":["Internal Control Module Brake Signal Performance","Electronic throttle systems run a second, independent calculation on the same processor (level 2, at Ford 'IPC – independent plausibility check') that recomputes driver demand, permitted and actual torque and their inputs, and compares them with the main software. Torque monitoring needs a reliable brake signal: brake applied means zero driver demand, cruise is cancelled, and in pedal limp mode the engine returns to idle.",[["m","Brake light switch misadjusted or faulty (both outputs do not switch together)"],["h","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["m","Faulty brake data from the ABS module or CAN disturbance"],["l","Connector or wiring fault at the brake switch"]],["Wrench lamp (Ford), no check engine light","Cruise control not working","Possibly reduced power"],["medium",[30,900]],"c"],"P061F":["Internal Control Module Throttle Actuator Controller Performance","P061F monitors the throttle position controller inside the engine module and its communication with the main processor. At Ford the main processor sends the target throttle angle to this controller (generation 2: a 312.5 Hz duty cycle signal, 10–92 % for 0–82°), which drives the throttle motor via an H-bridge; the monitoring checks that the target is implemented and reported back correctly.",[["m","Internal fault in the throttle controller of the engine module"],["m","Throttle stiff, sooted or iced, controller at its limit"],["m","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["l","Throttle motor connector or wiring resistance"]],["Wrench lamp (Ford), check engine light on generation 2","Throttle in default position, raised idle, barely any response","RPM limit"],["hard",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0620":["Generator Control Circuit","The alternator regulator is linked to the engine module by an L (turn-on) line, an F (field) line reporting field load as a duty cycle, or on newer cars a LIN bus. P0620 is the general control circuit code.",[["h","Connector or wire between module and alternator (corrosion, break, chafing)"],["h","Alternator or regulator faulty, including its LIN interface"],["m","Battery flat or faulty, jump start or charger connected"],["l","Loose or worn drive belt"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Battery not or incorrectly charged, starting problems","Stop-start not working"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0621":["Generator Lamp/L Terminal Circuit","The alternator regulator is linked to the engine module by an L (turn-on) line, an F (field) line reporting field load as a duty cycle, or on newer cars a LIN bus. P0621 concerns the L line: the engine module applies voltage through a high-side driver and so switches the regulator's field control on and off.",[["h","L line or alternator connector open or shorted"],["m","Regulator or alternator faulty"],["m","Ground problems between engine and body"],["l","Driver in the module faulty"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Battery not or incorrectly charged, starting problems","Raised or fluctuating idle"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0622":["Generator Field/F Terminal Circuit","The alternator regulator is linked to the engine module by an L (turn-on) line, an F (field) line reporting field load as a duty cycle, or on newer cars a LIN bus. P0622 checks the F feedback: its duty cycle equals the field on-time, from which the module calculates alternator torque and raises idle under load.",[["h","F line or alternator connector open or shorted"],["h","Regulator or alternator faulty (field driver)"],["m","Alternator not charging (belt, brushes), duty cycle implausible"],["l","Module or software"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Check engine light possible on GM from model year 2023","Idle fluctuates or drops when large loads switch on"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0623":["Generator Lamp Control Circuit","P0623 concerns the control of the battery warning lamp by a module's output driver, not the alternator itself. Traditionally the regulator switches the lamp through terminal L; where a module switches it instead – GM's Saab 9-4X turns the lamp on 15 s after a charging code – it can monitor its lamp output for open or short circuits.",[["h","Wire or connector to the lamp or instrument cluster"],["m","Bulb/LED or instrument cluster faulty"],["l","Output driver in the module"],["l","Ground fault"]],["Battery lamp never lights (not even at bulb check) or stays on","Real charging faults may not be displayed","Otherwise usually no driving symptoms"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0624":["Fuel Cap Lamp Control Circuit","Despite its neighbours, P0624 has nothing to do with the alternator: it is the control circuit of the 'check fuel cap' warning lamp of the evaporative system. Ford's evaporative monitor lights such a lamp when, after refuelling, the tank cannot be pulled into vacuum – a gross leak or missing cap (P0457) – and turns it off once the cap is secured; not every vehicle has this lamp.",[["h","Wire or connector to the warning lamp or instrument cluster"],["m","Bulb/LED or instrument cluster faulty"],["l","Output driver in the engine or body module"]],["Fuel cap lamp never lights or stays on","A loose cap is no longer indicated","No driving symptoms"],["easy",[0,300]],"c"],"P0625":["Generator Field/F Terminal Circuit Low","The alternator regulator is linked to the engine module by an L (turn-on) line, an F (field) line reporting field load as a duty cycle, or on newer cars a LIN bus. P0625 means the F feedback is too low: with the engine running the regulator reports (almost) no field duty cycle – typically an open or grounded F line, or the alternator is not charging.",[["h","F line open or shorted to ground, connector corroded"],["h","Regulator or alternator faulty or not charging (brushes, field winding)"],["m","Alternator B+ feed missing or high resistance"],["l","Engine module"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Battery not or incorrectly charged, starting problems","Raised or fluctuating idle"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P0626":["Generator Field/F Terminal Circuit High","The alternator regulator is linked to the engine module by an L (turn-on) line, an F (field) line reporting field load as a duty cycle, or on newer cars a LIN bus. P0626 means the F feedback is too high – a duty cycle near 100 % when little or no field current is expected, typically a short to battery voltage.",[["h","F line shorted to battery voltage (chafing), connector"],["h","Regulator faulty (field permanently on, possibly overcharging)"],["m","Engine module software (Ram 6.7 L 2019)"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Battery or check engine lamp (Chrysler/Ford partly with MIL)","Raised idle","Possibly overvoltage or overcharging with a regulator fault"],["medium",[0,600]],"c"],"P0627":["Fuel Pump A Control Circuit/Open","What 'fuel pump A control circuit' means differs by maker: at GM petrol engines it is the control line from the engine module to the fuel pump relay (high-side driver), at GM's LML diesel the lift pump, at Ford petrol engines with a pump driver module the secondary circuit from that module to the pump (reported as a duty cycle on a monitor line), and at Ford's 6.7 L diesel (F-650/750) the high or low side of the pump relay. P0627 is the open or general circuit fault.",[["h","Fuel pump relay or pump driver module faulty"],["h","Wire or connector to relay or pump open or corroded (tank, underbody)"],["m","Fuel pump itself (winding open, overheated)"],["m","Fuse or ground point"]],["Engine does not start or stalls","Lack of power or stumbling under load","Pump not audible with ignition on"],["medium",[20,700]],"c"],"P0628":["Fuel Pump A Control Circuit Low","What 'fuel pump A control circuit' means differs by maker: at GM petrol engines it is the control line from the engine module to the fuel pump relay (high-side driver), at GM's LML diesel the lift pump, at Ford petrol engines with a pump driver module the secondary circuit from that module to the pump (reported as a duty cycle on a monitor line), and at Ford's 6.7 L diesel (F-650/750) the high or low side of the pump relay. P0628 means a short to ground: GM detects 0.5 Ω or less to ground with the driver on (8 of 10 samples at 250 ms; two trips in 2018, first trip in 2025); the driver then shuts off, the relay does not pull in and the pump stops.",[["h","Control wire to relay or pump shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Relay coil shorted"],["m","Pump or pump module shorted internally (Ford high side)"],["l","Driver in the engine module faulty"]],["No start or stalling","Pump does not run","Blown fuse"],["medium",[20,700]],"c"],"P0629":["Fuel Pump A Control Circuit High","What 'fuel pump A control circuit' means differs by maker: at GM petrol engines it is the control line from the engine module to the fuel pump relay (high-side driver), at GM's LML diesel the lift pump, at Ford petrol engines with a pump driver module the secondary circuit from that module to the pump (reported as a duty cycle on a monitor line), and at Ford's 6.7 L diesel (F-650/750) the high or low side of the pump relay. P0629 means a short to battery voltage: GM detects voltage at the output while the driver is off (8 of 10 samples at 250 ms, two trips in 2018); because the driver hardware cannot always separate open circuit and short to voltage, P0627 may set as well.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a battery feed"],["m","Relay contacts welded or relay faulty internally"],["l","Moisture in the relay or fuse box"],["l","Driver in the engine module shorted"]],["Pump runs constantly or after switch-off","Battery drained","Possibly no start"],["medium",[20,500]],"c"],"P062A":["Fuel Pump A Control Circuit Range/Performance","P062A is a plausibility check of the fuel pump control circuit, not a plain short or open. On Ford's 2023 diesels (F-250 to F-550 and 3.0 L) the lift pump module reports its status as a duty cycle; outside 78–82 % – the expected 'good' signal – the code sets after 0.5 s.",[["h","Pump driver module faulty or reporting an error status"],["m","Engine module software (false trigger)"],["m","Contact problems or resistance in signal or supply wires"],["l","Pump stiff or overloaded"]],["Check engine light on","Lack of power or stumbling","Delayed start"],["medium",[0,600]],"c"],"P062B":["Internal Control Module Fuel Injector Control Performance","P062B monitors the injector driver stage inside the engine module. On direct injection engines a separate driver chip generates the boost voltage for opening the injectors and is configured by the main processor via SPI; GM checks initialisation, boost voltage (petrol: 90 V or more or 40 V or less), self-test, data download and communication, and sets the code in the first trip – while it is active, single injector circuit codes are suppressed.",[["m","Short to ground on an injector high-voltage or control wire, or a faulty injector"],["m","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["m","Undervoltage or supply problem (battery, main relay)"],["m","Faulty driver chip in the engine module"]],["Check engine light (GM in the first trip)","Stumbling, misfires, loss of power","Starting problems or stalling"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P062C":["Internal Control Module Vehicle Speed Performance","Electronic throttle systems run a second, independent calculation on the same processor (level 2, at Ford 'IPC – independent plausibility check') that recomputes driver demand, permitted and actual torque and their inputs, and compares them with the main software. Vehicle speed feeds torque monitoring through cruise control, speed limiter and gear-dependent limits.",[["h","Engine module software or calibration fault (update available)"],["m","Disturbed vehicle or output speed signal (sensor, reluctor, ABS CAN data)"],["l","Wrong tyre size or axle ratio configured in the module"],["l","Internal defect of the engine module"]],["Wrench lamp (Ford), check engine light only on older Ford","Cruise control not working","Possibly reduced power"],["medium",[50,1000]],"c"],"P062D":["Fuel Injector Driver Circuit Performance Bank 1","Direct-injection injectors (petrol and common-rail diesel) need a high boost voltage generated in the module to open fast; the module then holds them with pulsed battery voltage. P062D monitors this boost voltage or the shared injector supply of bank 1.",[["h","Short in an injector high-side wire or injector collapsing the boost voltage"],["m","Module internal (boost converter, driver chip)"],["m","System undervoltage or poor ground"],["m","Harness or injector connector (moisture, oil)"]],["Check engine light (GM in the first trip)","Misfires, rough running, stumbling","Loss of power or limp mode"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P062E":["Fuel Injector Driver Circuit Performance Bank 2","Direct-injection injectors (petrol and common-rail diesel) need a high boost voltage generated in the module to open fast; the module then holds them with pulsed battery voltage. P062E is the bank 2 counterpart of P062D for V engines with separate driver circuits per bank.",[["h","Short in an injector wire or injector of bank 2"],["m","Engine module or driver chip faulty internally"],["m","Connector or harness under the bank 2 valve cover"],["l","Undervoltage or poor ground"]],["Check engine light on","Misfires on one side of the engine","Loss of power or limp mode"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P062F":["Internal Control Module EEPROM Error","Learned values, adaptations, counters and configuration are saved to non-volatile memory (EEPROM or flash-emulated NVM) when the module shuts down. P062F means this memory failed a check: GM and GM Europe test at start-up whether the last write at shutdown completed, GM's 6.6 L diesel checks a checksum per data block and whether sectors can be erased, Ford's diesel reports read, write or erase errors (5 s).",[["h","Shutdown interrupted (battery disconnected, voltage dip, main relay drops out too early)"],["m","Weak battery, poor ground or supply at the module"],["m","Software fault or incomplete programming"],["l","Memory defect in the module (write cycles, ageing)"]],["Check engine or service lamp (depending on the maker, sometimes after two trips)","Learned values lost: rough idle or harsh shifts until relearned","Often no driveability symptoms"],["medium",[0,1200]],"c"],"P0630":["VIN Not Programmed or Incompatible ECM/PCM","Since model year 2005 CARB requires exactly one module to report the VIN to a scan tool, and reprogramming the VIN must erase all emission-related diagnostic data. P0630 means the engine module holds no valid VIN or one that does not fit.",[["h","New replacement module without written VIN"],["m","Used module from another vehicle (VIN does not match)"],["m","Interrupted programming or missing configuration data from the body module (Ford)"],["l","Internal memory fault"]],["Check engine light (some Ford trucks without)","Scan tool shows no or a wrong VIN","Ford: wrong tyre or axle data, speedometer errors"],["medium",[60,250]],"c"],"P0631":["VIN Not Programmed or Incompatible TCM","P0631 is the transmission module counterpart of P0630: the transmission control module holds no VIN, an invalid one, or one that does not match the vehicle – usually compared with the VIN of the engine or body module. Typical is a replaced transmission module or mechatronic unit (on dual-clutch and automatic gearboxes with integrated control) that was not VIN-programmed, or a used part.",[["h","Transmission module or mechatronic unit replaced, VIN not written"],["m","Used transmission module from another vehicle"],["m","Interrupted transmission module programming"],["l","CAN communication fault during the VIN comparison"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Scan tool shows no or a wrong VIN in the transmission module","Transmission adaptation possibly impossible"],["medium",[60,250]],"c"],"P0632":["Odometer Not Programmed ECM/PCM","Engine modules increasingly keep their own copy of the odometer reading or take it over from the instrument cluster or body module; for model years 2019–2021 onward CARB requires mileage in the module's operating data records and explicitly mentions the case 'odometer is lost'. P0632 means the engine module holds no valid odometer value.",[["h","Engine module replaced, mileage not transferred"],["m","Several modules (engine, body, cluster) replaced or programmed at the same time"],["l","No communication to the cluster or body module during takeover"],["l","Internal memory fault (EEPROM/NVM)"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Scan tool shows no or a different mileage in the engine module","Implausible service or oil change displays"],["medium",[60,250]],"c"],"P0633":["Immobilizer Key Not Programmed ECM/PCM","The immobiliser couples the engine module to the immobiliser control module (GM body module, Chrysler SKIM/WCM) through a secret key and a challenge-response exchange – 'key' here means these security data, not the ignition key. P0633 means the engine module has not learned this secret key yet.",[["h","Engine module replaced, immobiliser not learned"],["m","Learn procedure aborted or learn mode not exited (no start for another reason)"],["m","Communication fault to the immobiliser module"],["l","Engine module software fault (false setting)"]],["Engine does not start or dies immediately","Security or immobiliser lamp on or flashing","Code present after engine module replacement until learned"],["medium",[80,400]],"c"],"P0634":["Control Module Internal Temperature A Too High","The module measures its own circuit board temperature with an internal sensor and sets P0634 when it overheats. In practice this concerns mainly transmission modules mounted in or on the gearbox, such as GM's 6T70/6T75 and 6L units with the module in the valve body, surrounded by transmission fluid.",[["h","Transmission fluid too hot: cooler clogged, lines kinked, air flow blocked, wrong level"],["m","Engine overheating"],["m","Charging system overvoltage (18 V or more)"],["m","Faulty internal temperature sensor or valve body module"]],["Transmission limp mode (fixed gear), harsh shifts","Manual shifting disabled","Transmission warning, check engine light depending on the maker"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0635":["Power Steering Control Circuit","P0635–P0637 concern the control or signal circuit between engine module and power steering – depending on the maker the drive of an electro-hydraulic pump or the steering load signal the engine module uses to raise idle. CARB requires power steering components to be monitored only if another diagnostic uses them, so these codes are rare and maker-specific.",[["h","Wire or connector between engine module and steering module or pump open or corroded"],["m","Software or calibration fault in the steering module (Mercedes)"],["m","Steering module or electro-hydraulic pump faulty"],["m","Undervoltage from weak battery, alternator or poor ground"]],["Power steering warning, heavy steering","Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Idle drops when steering"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0636":["Power Steering Control Circuit Low","P0635–P0637 concern the control or signal circuit between engine module and power steering – depending on the maker the drive of an electro-hydraulic pump or the steering load signal the engine module uses to raise idle. CARB requires power steering components to be monitored only if another diagnostic uses them, so these codes are rare and maker-specific.",[["h","Signal or control wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Undervoltage from battery, alternator or ground"],["m","Steering module or pump module faulty internally"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Power steering warning, heavy steering","Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Idle drops when steering"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0637":["Power Steering Control Circuit High","P0635–P0637 concern the control or signal circuit between engine module and power steering – depending on the maker the drive of an electro-hydraulic pump or the steering load signal the engine module uses to raise idle. CARB requires power steering components to be monitored only if another diagnostic uses them, so these codes are rare and maker-specific.",[["h","Control or signal wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Steering module or pump module faulty internally"],["l","Overvoltage from the alternator regulator"],["l","Engine module faulty"]],["Power steering warning","Heavy steering or pump running constantly","Check engine light possible"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0638":["Throttle Actuator Control Range/Performance Bank 1","The electronic throttle motor positions the throttle plate; P0638 means the actuator does not follow its command on bank 1 (or the only throttle): target and actual angle from the two position sensors differ, or the motor needs too much duty cycle to hold the position. Subaru (also sold as Saab 9-2X) sets it at more than 3.5° deviation for 250 ms or over 95 % duty cycle for 2 s, lights the lamp at once and lets the spring set the plate to about 6° – raised idle, almost no power.",[["h","Throttle dirty, sooted or stiff"],["m","Icing from condensation in frost"],["m","Throttle motor or gears worn"],["m","Resistance in the motor control wires"]],["Check engine or electronic throttle light","Limp mode: much reduced power, raised idle","Delayed or jerky throttle response"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0639":["Throttle Actuator Control Range/Performance Bank 2","P0639 is the bank 2 counterpart of P0638 and only applies to engines with two separate electronic throttles, one per cylinder bank (some V8, V10 and V12 engines with separate intake paths). The module compares target and actual angle and the duty cycle the motor needs; if the plate does not follow, it is de-energised and springs to its rest position, and power is limited.",[["h","Bank 2 throttle dirty or stiff"],["m","Bank 2 actuator motor or gears faulty"],["l","Icing"],["l","Wiring or module"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode, loss of power","Rough idle from uneven bank filling"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P063A":["Generator Voltage Sense Circuit A","Some charging systems measure the actual battery or alternator voltage through a separate sense line (regulator terminal S or an input of the module) to compensate voltage drops in the charging cable; without it the system reports a charging fault. No manufacturer document available to us uses P063A–P063D, so these descriptions follow the circuit principle and CARB, under which a charging-system input needs the check engine light only if it affects emissions or other diagnostics.",[["h","Sense wire, fuse or connector between battery and alternator or module open or corroded"],["m","Alternator or regulator faulty"],["m","Contact resistance at battery terminals or grounds"],["l","Battery faulty"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Charging voltage too high or too low (flat or overcharged battery)","Flickering lights"],["medium",[20,600]],"c"],"P063B":["Generator Voltage Sense Circuit A Range/Performance","Some charging systems measure the actual battery or alternator voltage through a separate sense line (regulator terminal S or an input of the module) to compensate voltage drops in the charging cable; without it the system reports a charging fault. No manufacturer document available to us uses P063A–P063D, so these descriptions follow the circuit principle and CARB, under which a charging-system input needs the check engine light only if it affects emissions or other diagnostics.",[["h","Sense wire, fuse or connector between battery and alternator or module open or corroded"],["m","Alternator or regulator faulty"],["m","Contact resistance at battery terminals or grounds"],["l","Battery faulty"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Charging voltage too high or too low (flat or overcharged battery)","Flickering lights"],["medium",[20,600]],"c"],"P063C":["Generator Voltage Sense Circuit A Low","Some charging systems measure the actual battery or alternator voltage through a separate sense line (regulator terminal S or an input of the module) to compensate voltage drops in the charging cable; without it the system reports a charging fault. No manufacturer document available to us uses P063A–P063D, so these descriptions follow the circuit principle and CARB, under which a charging-system input needs the check engine light only if it affects emissions or other diagnostics.",[["h","Sense wire, fuse or connector between battery and alternator or module open or corroded"],["m","Alternator or regulator faulty"],["m","Contact resistance at battery terminals or grounds"],["l","Battery faulty"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Charging voltage too high or too low (flat or overcharged battery)","Flickering lights"],["medium",[20,600]],"c"],"P063D":["Generator Voltage Sense Circuit A High","Some charging systems measure the actual battery or alternator voltage through a separate sense line (regulator terminal S or an input of the module) to compensate voltage drops in the charging cable; without it the system reports a charging fault. No manufacturer document available to us uses P063A–P063D, so these descriptions follow the circuit principle and CARB, under which a charging-system input needs the check engine light only if it affects emissions or other diagnostics.",[["h","Sense wire, fuse or connector between battery and alternator or module open or corroded"],["m","Alternator or regulator faulty"],["m","Contact resistance at battery terminals or grounds"],["l","Battery faulty"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Charging voltage too high or too low (flat or overcharged battery)","Flickering lights"],["medium",[20,600]],"c"],"P063E":["Auto Configuration Throttle Input Not Present Bank 1","Some modules configure themselves at start-up by detecting which inputs are present. P063E means the module expects a throttle position input for bank 1 but does not detect it.",[["m","Throttle sensor or unit not connected, connector or wire open"],["m","Wrong module software or variant for the vehicle (module from another model)"],["l","Faulty throttle unit"],["l","Internal module fault"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode, reduced power","Rough idle, poor throttle response"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P063F":["Auto Configuration Engine Coolant Temperature Input Not Present","Some modules configure themselves at start-up by detecting which inputs are present. P063F means the module expects a coolant temperature input but does not detect it during this self-configuration.",[["m","Coolant temperature sensor not connected, connector or wire open"],["m","Wrong module software or variant"],["l","Faulty sensor"],["l","Internal module fault"]],["Check engine light","Radiator fan runs constantly","Temperature gauge not working"],["easy",[30,400]],"c"],"P0640":["Intake Air Heater Control Circuit","Some diesels (for example GM's 6.6 L Duramax LML and the Cummins in Ram trucks) warm the intake air with a heater grid switched by a relay or semiconductor switch. P0640 reports a fault in its control circuit.",[["h","Heater relay or switching module faulty"],["m","Control wire or connector open or corroded"],["m","Heater grid burnt out or shorted to ground"],["m","Heater main cable or fuse loose or faulty"]],["Check engine light","Poor cold start in low temperatures","White smoke, rough running after cold start"],["medium",[60,500]],"c"],"P0641":["Sensor Reference Voltage A Circuit/Open","The engine module feeds several sensors (pedal, throttle, pressure and position sensors) from shared, regulated 5 V reference circuits; 'A' is circuit 1. A short on one sensor or its wire pulls down every sensor on that circuit and triggers many follow-up codes – so look for the one faulty sensor or chafed wire, for example by unplugging the sensors one at a time.",[["h","Short to ground or voltage at one of the supplied sensors or its wire"],["m","A sensor signal wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Harness damage (chafing, rodent bite, water in connector)"],["l","Module internal (reference regulator)"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode with reduced power","Many codes from several sensors at once"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0642":["Sensor Reference Voltage A Circuit Low","The engine module feeds several sensors (pedal, throttle, pressure and position sensors) from shared, regulated 5 V reference circuits; 'A' is circuit 1. A short on one sensor or its wire pulls down every sensor on that circuit and triggers many follow-up codes – so look for the one faulty sensor or chafed wire, for example by unplugging the sensors one at a time.",[["h","5 V wire or sensor shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in a supplied sensor (e.g. pressure sensor)"],["m","Chafed harness or water ingress"],["l","Module reference regulator faulty"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode with reduced power","Many codes from several sensors at once"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0643":["Sensor Reference Voltage A Circuit High","The engine module feeds several sensors (pedal, throttle, pressure and position sensors) from shared, regulated 5 V reference circuits; 'A' is circuit 1. A short on one sensor or its wire pulls down every sensor on that circuit and triggers many follow-up codes – so look for the one faulty sensor or chafed wire, for example by unplugging the sensors one at a time.",[["h","5 V wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Signal wire of a supplied sensor shorted to voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode with reduced power","Several 'high' sensor codes"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0644":["Driver Display Serial Communication Circuit","P0644 concerns a dedicated serial line on which the engine module sends values or warnings to a driver display or information display – not the general vehicle CAN bus. The module reports missing or invalid communication on that line (open, short, display not answering); CARB requires digital outputs to be checked for communication faults.",[["h","Wire or connector between module and receiver open, corroded or shorted"],["m","Display or instrument cluster faulty or without supply (fuse, ground)"],["l","Low system voltage during cranking"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["Warning message or lamp (depending on the maker)","Information in the driver display missing, frozen or jumping","Display stays dark"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0645":["A/C Clutch Relay Control Circuit","The engine module switches the A/C compressor clutch relay through a low-side driver once the climate control requests cooling. P0645 is the general circuit fault of the relay coil control, usually an open circuit.",[["h","A/C clutch relay faulty (coil open)"],["h","Control wire or connector open, terminals spread"],["m","Relay or clutch supply fuse blown (e.g. shorted clutch coil)"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["A/C not cooling (compressor does not engage)","Compressor runs constantly","Check engine light usually off, code stored only"],["easy",[15,250]],"c"],"P0646":["A/C Clutch Relay Control Circuit Low","The engine module switches the A/C compressor clutch relay through a low-side driver once the climate control requests cooling. P0646 means the voltage at the control output is too low while the driver is off – the control wire is shorted to ground, so the relay can pull in permanently and the compressor keeps running, which can ice up the evaporator.",[["h","Control wire between relay and module shorted to ground (chafing)"],["m","Relay faulty internally (coil, ground)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["A/C compressor runs constantly, even with A/C off","Evaporator ices up, cooling drops","With an open circuit: no cooling"],["easy",[15,250]],"c"],"P0647":["A/C Clutch Relay Control Circuit High","The engine module switches the A/C compressor clutch relay through a low-side driver once the climate control requests cooling. P0647 means the voltage at the control output stays too high while the driver switches on (it should pull to ground) – the control wire is shorted to voltage; the driver shuts off for protection and the relay does not pull in, so there is no cooling.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a supply wire (chafing)"],["m","Relay with internal short (coil bypassed, very low resistance)"],["l","Wrong relay fitted"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["A/C not cooling","Compressor clutch never engages","Code stored, check engine light usually off"],["easy",[15,250]],"c"],"P0648":["Immobilizer Lamp Control Circuit","The engine module switches this hard-wired output with its own driver and checks the circuit electrically (open, short to ground or voltage) – it cannot see whether the lamp lights; on CAN-based cars such faults appear as U codes. P0648 concerns the immobiliser or security lamp where the engine module drives it directly by wire – on GM and Saab systems this lamp shows the immobiliser learn mode and faults.",[["h","Wire or connector between module and receiver open, corroded or shorted"],["m","Bulb/LED or instrument cluster faulty"],["m","Instrument cluster (board, connector) faulty"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["Immobiliser lamp off, permanently on or flashing wrongly","Code stored","Engine start normally not affected"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0649":["Speed Control Lamp Control Circuit","The engine module switches this hard-wired output with its own driver and checks the circuit electrically (open, short to ground or voltage) – it cannot see whether the lamp lights; on CAN-based cars such faults appear as U codes. P0649 concerns the cruise control ('speed control') indicator where the engine module drives it by wire.",[["h","Wire or connector between module and receiver open, corroded or shorted"],["m","Bulb/LED or instrument cluster faulty"],["m","Instrument cluster faulty"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["Cruise control indicator not working or stays on","Cruise control possibly disabled (depending on the maker)","Code stored"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P064A":["Fuel Pump Control Module A","Demand-controlled fuel systems drive the pump through a separate pump control module; the engine module sets the target and reads its status back. P064A means this module reports an internal fault.",[["h","Pump control module faulty internally (output stage, electronics)"],["m","Output stage overheating from a stiff or worn pump or poor module cooling"],["m","Supply or ground fault or corrosion at the module (often on frame or tank)"],["l","Disturbed monitor line, so a wrong duty cycle is read"]],["Check engine light (GM) or stored code only (Ford petrol)","Hard start, stumbling or lack of power from low fuel pressure","Stalling or no start if the pump is switched off"],["medium",[150,600]],"c"],"P064B":["PTO Control Module","A power take-off (PTO) drives equipment on trucks and pick-ups – tipper, crane, hydraulic pump or winch – from the engine or transmission. A PTO control module, or the PTO function in the engine module, manages switching, working speed and release conditions; P064B reports a fault of this module or an implausible PTO request.",[["h","Engine or PTO module software or calibration (implausible torque or speed request)"],["m","PTO equipment installed or programmed incorrectly by the upfitter"],["m","Harness, connector or supply of the PTO module or switches"],["l","PTO control module faulty"]],["PTO switches off or cannot be engaged","Engine speed hunts with PTO active or stays below about 1100 rpm (Chrysler)","Warning lamp or stored code; driving usually normal"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P064C":["Glow Plug Control Module 1","On diesels a glow plug control module switches the glow plugs (at Ford also the DEF heaters) with semiconductor switches, diagnoses itself and reports to the engine module. P064C means this module reports an internal fault.",[["h","Glow plug module faulty internally (switches, memory)"],["m","Insufficient supply or ground (battery, corrosion, main fuse)"],["m","Short in a glow plug or wire overloading a driver"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the connector of the engine-mounted module"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in cold weather","White smoke and rough running after a cold start"],["medium",[150,600]],"c"],"P064D":["Internal Control Module O2 Sensor Processor Performance Bank 1","Wide-band oxygen sensors need a pump current control; inside the engine module a dedicated chip (ASIC, for example Bosch's CJ125 family) regulates it, measures lambda, diagnoses sensor wiring and heater, and talks to the main processor via SPI. P064D means this chip for bank 1 sensor 1 does not work correctly or its messages are invalid.",[["h","Engine module (lambda chip) faulty"],["m","Wiring or sensor fault pushing the chip into an invalid state (short to battery voltage, water in connector)"],["m","Faulty wide-band sensor (e.g. heater-to-signal short)"],["l","Voltage dips or software"]],["Check engine light","Higher fuel consumption, rough running (open loop)","Failed emissions test"],["hard",[150,1200]],"c"],"P064E":["Internal Control Module O2 Sensor Processor Performance Bank 2","Wide-band oxygen sensors need a pump current control; inside the engine module a dedicated chip (ASIC, for example Bosch's CJ125 family) regulates it, measures lambda, diagnoses sensor wiring and heater, and talks to the main processor via SPI. P064E is the bank 2 counterpart of P064D and only occurs on V and boxer engines with two sensors before the catalysts.",[["h","Engine module (lambda chip bank 2) faulty"],["m","Wiring or connector fault of the bank 2 sensor 1"],["m","Bank 2 wide-band sensor faulty"],["l","Voltage dips or software"]],["Check engine light","Higher fuel consumption, rough running","Failed emissions test"],["hard",[150,1200]],"c"],"P064F":["Unauthorized Software/Calibration Detected","P064F means the module has found that its software or calibration is not the version released by the manufacturer, or fails an authenticity check (signature, checksum, security keys). CARB requires manufacturers to protect calibrations against changes without special tools and to report calibration IDs and checksums.",[["h","Unapproved or tuned software (chip tuning, programmer)"],["m","Faulty or interrupted reflash, outdated software"],["m","Used or replaced module without learning the security keys, wrong configuration"],["l","Module faulty internally (key storage)"]],["Check engine light (GM in the first trip)","Possibly follow-up codes in other modules or communication problems","Driving often normal"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0650":["MIL Control Circuit/Open","The engine module switches the check engine light (MIL) through a low-side driver – or sends the command over CAN – and monitors the circuit: with the lamp off the line must carry supply voltage, with it on nearly 0 V. CARB requires the MIL to light for a bulb check at ignition on with the engine off (at least 15 s since model year 2005).",[["h","Wire or connector between engine module and instrument cluster open or corroded"],["h","MIL bulb burnt out or LED/instrument cluster faulty"],["m","MIL supply in the cluster missing (fuse, ignition feed)"],["m","Short in the circuit (at GM reported as P263A/P263B)"]],["MIL does not light at the bulb check","MIL stays on (with a short or combined code)","Code only visible with a scan tool, engine runs normally"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0651":["Sensor Reference Voltage B Circuit/Open","The engine module feeds several sensors (camshaft, pedal, pressure sensors) from separate regulated 5 V reference circuits; 'B' is circuit 2. A short at one sensor pulls the whole reference down, so every sensor on it reports at once – unplug them one by one until the reference returns to 4.8–5.2 V.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor (camshaft, pedal, pressure)"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A sensor signal wire shorted to voltage"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once (camshaft, pedal, pressure)","Limp mode, reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0652":["Sensor Reference Voltage B Circuit Low","The engine module feeds several sensors (camshaft, pedal, pressure sensors) from separate regulated 5 V reference circuits; 'B' is circuit 2. A short at one sensor pulls the whole reference down, so every sensor on it reports at once – unplug them one by one until the reference returns to 4.8–5.2 V.",[["h","5 V reference wire shorted to ground (chafing)"],["h","Sensor on the reference circuit shorted internally"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in a connector"],["l","Module"]],["Check engine light","Several 'low' sensor codes at once","Limp mode or reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0653":["Sensor Reference Voltage B Circuit High","The engine module feeds several sensors (camshaft, pedal, pressure sensors) from separate regulated 5 V reference circuits; 'B' is circuit 2. A short at one sensor pulls the whole reference down, so every sensor on it reports at once – unplug them one by one until the reference returns to 4.8–5.2 V.",[["h","Reference or sensor signal wire shorted to a 12 V line"],["m","Faulty sensor feeding in external voltage"],["l","Module voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several 'high' sensor codes","Limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0654":["Engine RPM Output Circuit/Open","The engine module switches this hard-wired output with its own driver and checks the circuit electrically (open, short to ground or voltage) – it cannot see whether the lamp lights; on CAN-based cars such faults appear as U codes. P0654 concerns the engine speed output: the module turns the crankshaft signal into a cleaned-up square wave for the tachometer and other modules – Saab's 1999 9-3, for example, sends two pulses per revolution, about 30 Hz at idle.",[["h","Wire between module and tachometer or receiver open, connector corroded"],["m","Output wire shorted"],["m","Instrument cluster or receiving module faulty (input stage)"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["Tachometer reads zero or jumps","Functions using the speed signal disturbed (accessories, cruise control, shift indicator)","Check engine light depending on the maker"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0655":["Engine Hot Lamp Output Control Circuit","The engine module switches this hard-wired output with its own driver and checks the circuit electrically (open, short to ground or voltage) – it cannot see whether the lamp lights; on CAN-based cars such faults appear as U codes. P0655 concerns the engine hot or coolant warning lamp that the engine module switches by wire from the coolant temperature signal.",[["h","Wire or connector between module and receiver open, corroded or shorted"],["m","Bulb/LED or instrument cluster faulty"],["m","Lamp supply missing (fuse)"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"]],["Temperature warning lamp does not light at bulb check or when overheating","Warning lamp stays on without overheating","Code stored, check engine light depending on the maker"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0656":["Fuel Level Output Circuit","The engine module switches this hard-wired output with its own driver and checks the circuit electrically (open, short to ground or voltage) – it cannot see whether the lamp lights; on CAN-based cars such faults appear as U codes. Where the engine module reads the fuel level sender itself – it needs the level for the evaporative leak test – it passes the level on to the fuel gauge.",[["h","Wire or connector between module and instrument cluster open or shorted"],["m","Instrument cluster (fuel gauge input) faulty"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"],["l","Fuel level sender fault as root cause"]],["Fuel gauge reads empty, full or jumps","Low fuel warning wrong","Range display wrong"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P0657":["Actuator Supply Voltage A Circuit/Open","Actuator supply A is a high-side switch feeding several actuators together; its use differs by maker (Ford transmission solenoids, GM shared high-side driver, GM Europe 2.0 CDTI internal 5 V supply). P0657 is the open-circuit code; it is documented at Ford: the high-side switch of the 5R110W and 6R80 automatics feeds all shift solenoids, and the 2023 diesels split them into two supplies (P0657 and P2669), checked continuously.",[["h","Open or connector fault at the transmission pass-through connector or wire to the valve body"],["m","Damaged harness"],["m","Internal high-side switch in the engine or transmission module"],["l","Internal transmission harness"]],["Transmission limp mode (fixed gear)","Harsh shifts, no upshift","Check engine light (depending on the variant)"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0658":["Actuator Supply Voltage A Circuit Low","Actuator supply A is a high-side switch feeding several actuators together; its use differs by maker (Ford transmission solenoids, GM shared high-side driver, GM Europe 2.0 CDTI internal 5 V supply). P0658 means the supply output is too low with the driver on – a short to ground (0.5 Ω or less).",[["h","Actuator supply wire shorted to ground"],["m","Actuator (solenoid) shorted internally"],["m","Transmission harness or connector (also open, in transmission modules)"],["m","Module internal (main cause on the Saab diesel)"]],["Transmission limp mode or fixed gear","Several actuators not working","Check engine light (GM from 2017)"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0659":["Actuator Supply Voltage A Circuit High","Actuator supply A is a high-side switch feeding several actuators together; its use differs by maker (Ford transmission solenoids, GM shared high-side driver, GM Europe 2.0 CDTI internal 5 V supply). P0659 means a short to voltage on the supply output, checked with the driver off – the output should then be dead (GM: 0.5 Ω or less to the supply, 20 of 25 samples; lamp after two trips in 2017–18, none in 2016).",[["h","Actuator supply wire shorted to battery or ignition voltage"],["m","Harness chafed against a 12 V line"],["m","Module (driver shorted; internal on the Saab diesel)"],["l","External voltage fed back through a faulty actuator"]],["Check engine light (GM from 2017)","Transmission or actuator limp mode","Actuators cannot be switched off"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P065A":["Generator System Performance","Demand-controlled (smart) charging lets the module vary alternator voltage; the charging system is not an emission component, so these codes usually come without the check engine light. P065A is the function check of the whole charging strategy.",[["h","Electrical fault in another module (lighting, wipers, blower) disabling smart charging"],["m","Loose or corroded B+ or high-current connection"],["m","Engine module software"],["l","Alternator or regulator faulty"]],["Battery warning lamp (in some cases)","Fluctuating charging voltage reading","No check engine light (Chrysler)"],["medium",[0,400]],"c"],"P065B":["Generator A Control Circuit Range/Performance","Demand-controlled (smart) charging lets the module vary alternator voltage; the charging system is not an emission component, so these codes usually come without the check engine light. P065B is the plausibility check of the control channel to the alternator regulator (LIN or a dedicated smart-charge line): command and feedback do not match, or the signal is out of range (too low or too high).",[["h","LIN or control wire between alternator and module (connector, corrosion, open)"],["m","Weak battery, jump start or external charging"],["m","Alternator regulator faulty"],["m","Ground or B+ connections"]],["Battery warning lamp on","Stop-start not working","Battery drains"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P065C":["Generator A Mechanical Performance","Demand-controlled (smart) charging lets the module vary alternator voltage; the charging system is not an emission component, so these codes usually come without the check engine light. P065C points to the alternator's mechanical drive: the alternator does not deliver as commanded or its feedback does not match engine speed, typically because the belt slips.",[["h","Drive belt slipping (tension, wear, oil)"],["m","Weak belt tensioner"],["m","Alternator overrunning pulley faulty"],["l","Alternator bearing or rotor stiff"]],["Battery warning lamp","Stop-start disabled","Belt squeal"],["easy",[30,400]],"c"],"P065D":["Reductant System Malfunction Lamp Control Circuit","The engine module switches this hard-wired output with its own driver and checks the circuit electrically (open, short to ground or voltage) – it cannot see whether the lamp lights; on CAN-based cars such faults appear as U codes. On SCR diesels a separate lamp or symbol warns of low DEF/AdBlue level, poor quality or system faults and announces speed or start restrictions (inducement).",[["h","Wire or connector between module and receiver open, corroded or shorted"],["m","Bulb/LED or instrument cluster faulty"],["m","Lamp supply missing (fuse)"],["l","Driver in the engine or SCR module faulty"]],["AdBlue warning lamp does not light at ignition on, or stays on","No early warning of low AdBlue (risk of speed limit or no-restart without notice)","Check engine light possible (depending on the maker)"],["medium",[60,500]],"c"],"P065E":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Performance Bank 1","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P065E is the function check for bank 1 (or the only valve): the valve does not follow its command.",[["h","Valve shaft or linkage stiff or sooted (oil mist from crankcase ventilation or EGR)"],["m","Plastic lever or ball joint between actuator and shaft broken or detached"],["m","Actuator motor or solenoid faulty (weak, gears)"],["l","Manifold leak or, on vacuum-operated actuators, a leak in the vacuum line"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Torque gap in one rev band","Rattling or clicking from the intake manifold"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P065F":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Performance Bank 2","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P065F is the function check of the bank 2 valve: it does not follow its command, detected via a position sensor or the intake pressure pulsation (GM patent).",[["h","Bank 2 valve or shaft sooted or stiff"],["m","Linkage between actuator and valve broken or unhooked"],["m","Bank 2 actuator faulty internally"],["l","Manifold or vacuum leak"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Torque gap in one rev band","Noise from the intake manifold"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0660":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Control Circuit/Open Bank 1","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P0660 means the control circuit of the bank 1 (or only) valve is open.",[["h","Actuator connector loose, corroded or spread"],["h","Actuator coil or motor open internally"],["m","Broken or chafed wire (control or supply)"],["l","Supply (fuse, main relay) to the actuator missing"]],["Check engine light (GM after two trips; Ford without)","Less power in the upper or middle rev range","Actuator does not click at ignition on or when switching"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P0661":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Control Circuit Low Bank 1","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P0661 means the bank 1 control circuit voltage is too low with the driver off – a short to ground (0.5 Ω or less), which bypasses the driver and keeps the valve energised; GM also includes an open circuit (200 kΩ or more) here, so P0660 may set alongside.",[["h","Low-side control wire shorted to ground by chafing on engine or manifold"],["m","Actuator shorted internally to housing or ground"],["m","Open circuit (connector, coil) – included under P0661 at GM"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light after two trips (GM)","Torque or power gap in one rev band","Possibly blown fuse, further codes of the same supply circuit"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P0662":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Control Circuit High Bank 1","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P0662 means a short to voltage in the bank 1 control circuit: with the driver on, the low-side line should be pulled to ground, but the voltage stays high (0.5 Ω or less to positive); the driver protects itself and the valve is not actuated.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery or supply voltage (harness)"],["m","Actuator winding shorted (very low resistance, overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector between supply and control pin"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Loss of power in part of the rev range","Actuator does not click when switching"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P0663":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Control Circuit/Open Bank 2","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P0663 means the control circuit of the bank 2 valve is open.",[["h","Actuator connector loose, corroded or spread"],["h","Bank 2 actuator coil open"],["m","Broken wire in the harness"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Loss of power in part of the rev range","Actuator does not click when switching"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P0664":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Control Circuit Low Bank 2","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P0664 means the bank 2 control circuit voltage is too low with the driver off – typically a short to ground; with GM-style logic an open circuit is included.",[["h","Bank 2 control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Bank 2 actuator shorted internally to ground"],["m","Open circuit (included with GM-style logic)"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Loss of power in part of the rev range","Actuator does not click when switching"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P0665":["Intake Manifold Tuning Valve Control Circuit High Bank 2","The intake manifold tuning valve switches the manifold length or plenum connection with engine speed (more torque low, more power high); it is not the runner or swirl flap (IMRC, P2004 ff). P0665 means the bank 2 control line stays high with the driver on – a short to voltage or overcurrent, after which the driver shuts off.",[["h","Bank 2 control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Actuator winding shorted (overcurrent)"],["m","Corrosion or moisture in the connector"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Loss of power in part of the rev range","Actuator does not click when switching"],["easy",[20,350]],"c"],"P0666":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Circuit","Control modules measure their own circuit board temperature with an internal sensor to protect output stages (overtemperature is P0634) and, in GM transmission modules, as a substitute for fluid temperature. In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics the sensor sits in the transmission module inside the valve body, with no external wiring – so the repair is practically always the module or valve body assembly.",[["h","Module faulty internally (temperature sensor or measuring circuit)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or valve body"],["l","Supply or ground problems of the module"]],["Warning or check engine light","Changed shift quality (substitute value)","Possibly transmission fluid temperature codes"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P0667":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Range/Performance","Control modules measure their own circuit board temperature with an internal sensor to protect output stages (overtemperature is P0634) and, in GM transmission modules, as a substitute for fluid temperature. In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics the sensor sits in the transmission module inside the valve body, with no external wiring – so the repair is practically always the module or valve body assembly.",[["h","Transmission module or valve body (internal sensor) faulty"],["m","Transmission fluid temperature sensor reads wrong (comparison partner)"],["l","Unusual conditions (long standing in extreme cold or heat, fluid level or condition)"]],["Engine or transmission warning lamp","Harsher or delayed shifts (substitute temperature)","Possibly P0711 as well"],["hard",[400,1500]],"c"],"P0668":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","Control modules measure their own circuit board temperature with an internal sensor to protect output stages (overtemperature is P0634) and, in GM transmission modules, as a substitute for fluid temperature. In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics the sensor sits in the transmission module inside the valve body, with no external wiring – so the repair is practically always the module or valve body assembly.",[["h","Module faulty internally (sensor or measuring circuit shorted)"],["m","Moisture or fluid in the electronics of the valve body"],["l","Module supply problem"]],["Warning lamp","Changed shift quality (substitute value)","Scan tool shows an implausibly low module temperature"],["hard",[400,1500]],"c"],"P0669":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","Control modules measure their own circuit board temperature with an internal sensor to protect output stages (overtemperature is P0634) and, in GM transmission modules, as a substitute for fluid temperature. In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics the sensor sits in the transmission module inside the valve body, with no external wiring – so the repair is practically always the module or valve body assembly.",[["h","Module faulty internally (sensor open)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the electronics"],["l","Module supply or software"]],["Warning lamp","Changed shift quality (substitute value)","Scan tool shows an implausibly high module temperature"],["hard",[400,1500]],"c"],"P066A":["Cylinder 1 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 1 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground (block, bracket)"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P066B":["Cylinder 1 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 1 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector (leakage between pins)"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P066C":["Cylinder 2 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 2 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground (block, bracket)"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P066D":["Cylinder 2 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 2 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector (leakage between pins)"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P066E":["Cylinder 3 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 3 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground (block, bracket)"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P066F":["Cylinder 3 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 3 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector (leakage between pins)"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0670":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 Control Circuit/Open","In today's J2012 meaning P0670 is the electrical check of the activation line from the engine module to glow plug control module 1 for an open circuit; a short to ground is P0383, a short to voltage P0384, and serial communication between the modules P0683/P0684. On Bosch systems (Saab 2.2 TiD and EDC16) the engine module switches this line, detects an open load with the output off and a short to voltage with it on, and also compares the glow module's diagnostic feedback, which must switch inversely – Saab reports all line faults under P0670.",[["h","Wire or connector between engine and glow module open or corroded (bent pin, contact tension)"],["m","Glow module faulty (input open) or without supply or ground (fuse, terminal 30)"],["m","Control line shorted (makers reporting all line faults under P0670, e.g. Saab)"],["l","Output driver in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine light on","Glow plug lamp behaves implausibly (stays off or on)","Hard or no cold start in frost, since no plug heats"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P0671":["Cylinder 1 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 1 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle, knocking and white or blue smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0672":["Cylinder 2 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 2 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle, knocking and white or blue smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0673":["Cylinder 3 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 3 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle, knocking and white or blue smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0674":["Cylinder 4 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 4 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle, knocking and white or blue smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0675":["Cylinder 5 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 5 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM 3.0 L 2020)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle, knocking and white or blue smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0676":["Cylinder 6 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single faulty plug to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 6 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0677":["Cylinder 7 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single faulty plug to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 7 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0678":["Cylinder 8 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single faulty plug to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 8 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0679":["Cylinder 9 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single faulty plug to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 9 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P067A":["Cylinder 4 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 4 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P067B":["Cylinder 4 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 4 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P067C":["Cylinder 5 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 5 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P067D":["Cylinder 5 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 5 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P067E":["Cylinder 6 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 6 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P067F":["Cylinder 6 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 6 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0680":["Cylinder 10 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single faulty plug to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 10 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0681":["Cylinder 11 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single faulty plug to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 11 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0682":["Cylinder 12 Glow Plug Circuit/Open","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single faulty plug to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 12 burnt out (element open)"],["m","Plug connector loose or corroded, terminal nut loose, wire broken"],["m","Glow module supply or ground (several cylinder codes at once)"],["l","Glow module or driver faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine light (GM also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in low temperatures","Rough idle and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0683":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 to PCM Communication Circuit","Glow plug module 1 reports to the engine module over a feedback wire (Bosch-type systems expect it inverse to the activation line) or the bus; GM and Ford report a lost bus link as U0106, Saab as P0380/P1380, and no manufacturer document available to us uses P0683/P0684 themselves. P0683 means communication or feedback from the glow module to the engine module is missing or the line is open or shorted.",[["h","Communication or feedback wire between glow and engine module open, chafed or connector corroded"],["m","Glow module without supply or ground (fuse, main cable) – does not answer"],["m","Glow module faulty internally"],["l","Glow or engine module software or programming"]],["Check engine light (glow plug lamp may flash or stay on)","Harder cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0684":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 to PCM Communication Circuit Range/Performance","Glow plug module 1 reports to the engine module over a feedback wire (Bosch-type systems expect it inverse to the activation line) or the bus; GM and Ford report a lost bus link as U0106, Saab as P0380/P1380, and no manufacturer document available to us uses P0683/P0684 themselves. P0684 means communication exists but is implausible: wrong level or duty cycle, implausible content or a feedback that does not match the activation state – as in the Saab logic, where the feedback must be the inverse of the command.",[["h","Contact resistance or corrosion at connectors or pins of the communication line (signal distorted)"],["m","Supply or ground problem at the glow module (voltage dip at high glow currents)"],["m","Glow module faulty, or software/configuration not matching the engine module"],["l","Engine module input stage faulty"]],["Check engine light (glow plug lamp may flash or stay on)","Harder cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0685":["ECM/PCM Power Relay Control Circuit/Open","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P0685 means the relay control circuit is open: GM measures with the driver off and flags 200 kΩ or more to ground.",[["h","Open or corroded control wire between relay coil and module or connector"],["h","Relay coil open or relay badly seated"],["m","Relay coil supply missing (fuse, battery positive)"],["l","Weak battery or voltage dip"]],["Check engine light","Engine does not start or stalls","No communication with the engine module (Saab)"],["medium",[20,350]],"c"],"P0686":["ECM/PCM Power Relay Control Circuit Low","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P0686 means the control line is shorted to ground: with the driver off it should carry battery voltage through the coil, but it sits at ground (0.5 Ω or less).",[["h","Control wire between relay and module chafed to ground"],["m","Relay shorted internally or water in the relay carrier"],["l","Module driver shorted internally"]],["Check engine light","Relay stays on after ignition off, battery drains","Possibly P068B as well"],["medium",[20,350]],"c"],"P0687":["ECM/PCM Power Relay Control Circuit High","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P0687 means the control line is shorted to voltage: when the driver switches on and should pull it to ground, it stays high (0.5 Ω or less to positive) or the driver goes into overcurrent protection, so the relay does not pull in – no supply to the loads, no start or stalling.",[["h","Control wire shorted to positive (harness, external voltage)"],["m","Relay coil shorted (very low resistance)"],["l","Module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light","Engine does not start or stalls","Relay does not click"],["medium",[20,350]],"c"],"P0688":["ECM/PCM Power Relay Sense Circuit/Open","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P0688 means the expected switched voltage does not arrive on the sense line – the line is open, a fuse in the monitored path has blown or the contact does not close.",[["h","Terminal in the sense or ASD circuit connector bent, pushed back or spread"],["m","Blown fuse in the monitored load circuit (e.g. shorted ignition coil)"],["m","Relay contact does not close"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light","Engine cranks but intermittently does not start (Stellantis)","Engine stalls while driving"],["medium",[10,350]],"c"],"P0689":["ECM/PCM Power Relay Sense Circuit Low","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P0689 means the sense voltage is too low although the relay is commanded on – the contact does not close or has high resistance, the sense line is open or grounded, or a fuse in the load circuit has blown.",[["h","Relay contact burnt or not closing, relay faulty"],["m","Fuse in the relay load or sense path blown"],["m","High resistance or corrosion in relay carrier, connector or wire"],["l","Weak battery"]],["Check engine light (depending on the maker)","Starting problems or no start","Engine stalls or stumbles"],["easy",[10,250]],"c"],"P068A":["ECM/PCM Power Relay De-Energized Performance Too Early","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P068A means the relay dropped out before the module released it.",[["h","Engine module software (false report)"],["m","Battery disconnected during or right after switch-off, or severe voltage dip"],["m","Open, high resistance or short to ground in the relay coil or contact supply"],["l","Relay faulty (drops out with vibration or heat)"]],["Check engine light (Ford, VW, Stellantis)","Often no noticeable symptoms","Lost learned values, rough idle after restart"],["medium",[0,300]],"c"],"P068B":["ECM/PCM Power Relay De-Energized Performance Too Late","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P068B is the counterpart of P068A: after ignition off and the module's switch-off command the relay voltage remains – the relay drops out late or not at all.",[["h","Relay contact sticking or welded"],["m","Relay control wire shorted to ground (coil stays energised)"],["m","Relay output or load line shorted to voltage"],["l","Module driver or software"]],["Relay or module stays active after ignition off (fan or pump keeps running)","Battery drains","Possibly check engine light (depending on the maker)"],["easy",[10,250]],"c"],"P068C":["Cylinder 7 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 7 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P068D":["Cylinder 7 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 7 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P068E":["Cylinder 8 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 8 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P068F":["Cylinder 8 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 8 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0690":["ECM/PCM Power Relay Sense Circuit High","The engine module switches its main relay coil to ground (control circuit), reads the switched voltage back on a sense line and keeps the relay on briefly after ignition off to save learned values. P0690 means the sense voltage is too high: voltage is present although the relay has been off for 2 s – a welded or sticking contact, or external voltage fed in through a short – or, on some systems, overvoltage above 18 V with the relay on.",[["h","Relay contact sticking or welded"],["m","Short to voltage on the sense or load line (external feed from another component)"],["m","Control wire shorted to ground (relay permanently on)"],["l","Overvoltage from the alternator regulator (where the 18 V limit applies)"]],["Check engine light","Module, fan or pumps keep running after ignition off","Battery drains overnight"],["easy",[10,250]],"c"],"P0691":["Fan 1 Control Circuit Low","The engine module switches the radiator fan relays (or the input of a PWM fan module) through output drivers and checks each control circuit electrically. GM sets a short to ground or voltage at 0.5 Ω or less after 50 of 63 samples, with the lamp after two trips; GM Europe's 2011 Saab 9-5 covers P0691–P0696 with relay assignments, the Saab 9-4X's PWM fan module reacts within 1 s.",[["h","Control wire to fan relay or module shorted to ground (chafing)"],["h","Water or corrosion in fan or relay connector"],["m","Fan relay faulty (coil shorted or low-resistance)"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Fan runs constantly or not at all, depending on circuit design","Rising coolant temperature in traffic or with A/C on"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0692":["Fan 1 Control Circuit High","The engine module switches the radiator fan relays (or the input of a PWM fan module) through output drivers and checks each control circuit electrically. GM sets a short to ground or voltage at 0.5 Ω or less after 50 of 63 samples, with the lamp after two trips; GM Europe's 2011 Saab 9-5 covers P0691–P0696 with relay assignments, the Saab 9-4X's PWM fan module reacts within 1 s.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery or ignition voltage"],["m","Relay coil open or wrong relay fitted"],["m","PWM fan module feeding voltage back onto the control line"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Fan does not run","Rising coolant temperature in traffic or with A/C on"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0693":["Fan 2 Control Circuit Low","The engine module switches the radiator fan relays (or the input of a PWM fan module) through output drivers and checks each control circuit electrically. GM sets a short to ground or voltage at 0.5 Ω or less after 50 of 63 samples, with the lamp after two trips; GM Europe's 2011 Saab 9-5 covers P0691–P0696 with relay assignments, the Saab 9-4X's PWM fan module reacts within 1 s.",[["h","Control wire to fan relay or module shorted to ground (chafing)"],["h","Water or corrosion in fan or relay connector"],["m","Fan relay faulty (coil shorted or low-resistance)"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Fan runs constantly or not at all, depending on circuit design","Rising coolant temperature in traffic or with A/C on"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0694":["Fan 2 Control Circuit High","The engine module switches the radiator fan relays (or the input of a PWM fan module) through output drivers and checks each control circuit electrically. GM sets a short to ground or voltage at 0.5 Ω or less after 50 of 63 samples, with the lamp after two trips; GM Europe's 2011 Saab 9-5 covers P0691–P0696 with relay assignments, the Saab 9-4X's PWM fan module reacts within 1 s.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery or ignition voltage"],["m","Relay coil open or wrong relay fitted"],["m","PWM fan module feeding voltage back onto the control line"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Fan does not run","Rising coolant temperature in traffic or with A/C on"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0695":["Fan 3 Control Circuit Low","The engine module switches the radiator fan relays (or the input of a PWM fan module) through output drivers and checks each control circuit electrically. GM sets a short to ground or voltage at 0.5 Ω or less after 50 of 63 samples, with the lamp after two trips; GM Europe's 2011 Saab 9-5 covers P0691–P0696 with relay assignments, the Saab 9-4X's PWM fan module reacts within 1 s.",[["h","Control wire to fan relay or module shorted to ground (chafing)"],["h","Water or corrosion in fan or relay connector"],["m","Fan relay faulty (coil shorted or low-resistance)"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Fan runs constantly or not at all, depending on circuit design","Rising coolant temperature in traffic or with A/C on"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0696":["Fan 3 Control Circuit High","The engine module switches the radiator fan relays (or the input of a PWM fan module) through output drivers and checks each control circuit electrically. GM sets a short to ground or voltage at 0.5 Ω or less after 50 of 63 samples, with the lamp after two trips; GM Europe's 2011 Saab 9-5 covers P0691–P0696 with relay assignments, the Saab 9-4X's PWM fan module reacts within 1 s.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery or ignition voltage"],["m","Relay coil open or wrong relay fitted"],["m","PWM fan module feeding voltage back onto the control line"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after two trips)","Fan does not run","Rising coolant temperature in traffic or with A/C on"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0697":["Sensor Reference Voltage C Circuit/Open","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. Which sensors hang on circuit C depends on the maker – pedal sensor 2 is common to all: GM 2016–2025 feeds only pedal sensor 2 from it, the Saab 9-4X also the MAP sensor, the Saab 9-5 2.0 L turbo also throttle, crankshaft and A/C pressure sensors, and Bentley's 2026 Bentayga V8 lists its own set.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once (pedal, throttle, pressure)","Electronic throttle limp mode, reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0698":["Sensor Reference Voltage C Circuit Low","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. Which sensors hang on circuit C depends on the maker – pedal sensor 2 is common to all: GM 2016–2025 feeds only pedal sensor 2 from it, the Saab 9-4X also the MAP sensor, the Saab 9-5 2.0 L turbo also throttle, crankshaft and A/C pressure sensors, and Bentley's 2026 Bentayga V8 lists its own set.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once (pedal, throttle, pressure)","Electronic throttle limp mode, reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0699":["Sensor Reference Voltage C Circuit High","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. Which sensors hang on circuit C depends on the maker – pedal sensor 2 is common to all: GM 2016–2025 feeds only pedal sensor 2 from it, the Saab 9-4X also the MAP sensor, the Saab 9-5 2.0 L turbo also throttle, crankshaft and A/C pressure sensors, and Bentley's 2026 Bentayga V8 lists its own set.",[["h","Reference or sensor signal wire shorted to 12 V"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once (pedal, throttle, pressure)","Electronic throttle limp mode, reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P069A":["Cylinder 9 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 9 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P069B":["Cylinder 9 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 9 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P069C":["Cylinder 10 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 10 shorted internally (element against body)"],["m","Plug wire insulation damaged and shorted to ground"],["l","Wrong plug type (resistance or voltage rating)"],["l","Driver in the glow or engine module faulty, or software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start, especially in frost","Rough running and white smoke right after a cold start"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P069D":["Cylinder 10 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Modern diesel glow systems switch each glow plug through its own power semiconductor (in a glow plug module or the engine module), so current and faults can be measured per plug; a faulty channel is switched off. CARB requires glow plugs to be checked for correct current and voltage drop and, since model year 2010, a single plug outside specification to be detected and named.",[["h","Cylinder 10 plug wire touching battery positive (chafing in the harness)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the module or plug connector"],["m","Driver in the glow or engine module shorted (permanently on)"],["l","Glow module software (GM bulletins)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Plug permanently powered, possible battery drain","Plug burns through, then an open-circuit code"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P069E":["Fuel Pump Control Module Requested MIL Illumination","P069E is not a component fault but a notice in the engine module: the separate fuel pump control module has detected and stored an emission-related fault of its own and requests the check engine light – over serial data or, on older systems, a request line. GM uses it (2014–2019 tables) and lists P069E itself as 'type A, no MIL': the lamp is lit because of the fault in the pump module, not because of P069E.",[["h","Fault in the fuel pump circuit (pump, wiring, ground) stored in the pump module"],["m","Fuel pressure sensor or its wiring or 5 V reference"],["m","Pump module faulty internally or overheated (corrosion, moisture)"],["l","Insufficient supply or ground of the pump module"]],["Check engine light on","Possibly hard start, stumbling or lack of power from fuel supply problems","Further codes stored in the fuel pump module"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P069F":["Throttle Actuator Control Lamp Control Circuit","P069F concerns the hard-wired control circuit of the electronic throttle warning lamp (called 'reduced power', wrench or EPC lamp depending on the maker), which lights when the throttle control detects a fault and limits power. The module monitors this output electrically for open circuit and shorts; it cannot see whether the lamp lights.",[["h","Open or short in the lamp wire between engine module and cluster"],["m","Lamp/LED or its driver in the instrument cluster faulty"],["m","Connector at cluster or module corroded or loose"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Throttle warning lamp does not light at bulb check, or stays on","No warning when the engine goes into throttle limp mode","Code stored, engine runs normally"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P06A0":["Variable A/C Compressor Control Circuit","Variable-displacement A/C compressors change their swash-plate stroke via an electric control valve: a PWM current sets the crankcase pressure and thus the output. Where the engine module drives this valve (Ford's 'EVDC' compressors 2013–2019), it monitors the circuit; where the climate control module drives it (Hyundai, VW/Audi, GM), faults appear in that module instead.",[["h","Compressor control valve faulty (coil open)"],["h","Connector at the compressor corroded or loose, wire open"],["m","Low or incorrect refrigerant charge misleading the diagnosis"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["A/C cools weakly or not at all","Code stored, check engine light usually off","Compressor stays at minimum output"],["medium",[30,900]],"c"],"P06A1":["Variable A/C Compressor Control Circuit Low","Variable-displacement A/C compressors change their swash-plate stroke via an electric control valve: a PWM current sets the crankcase pressure and thus the output. Where the engine module drives this valve (Ford's 'EVDC' compressors 2013–2019), it monitors the circuit; where the climate control module drives it (Hyundai, VW/Audi, GM), faults appear in that module instead.",[["h","Valve control wire shorted to ground (chafing at compressor or engine)"],["m","Shorted turns in the control valve"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the compressor connector"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["A/C cools weakly or not at all","Code stored"],["medium",[30,900]],"c"],"P06A2":["Variable A/C Compressor Control Circuit High","Variable-displacement A/C compressors change their swash-plate stroke via an electric control valve: a PWM current sets the crankcase pressure and thus the output. Where the engine module drives this valve (Ford's 'EVDC' compressors 2013–2019), it monitors the circuit; where the climate control module drives it (Hyundai, VW/Audi, GM), faults appear in that module instead.",[["h","Valve control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Valve coil open (seen as 'high' with low-side drivers)"],["m","Connector or harness at the compressor damaged"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["A/C cools weakly or not at all","Code stored"],["medium",[30,900]],"c"],"P06A3":["Sensor Reference Voltage D Circuit/Open","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. On GM engines (2016–2025) circuit D feeds pedal sensor 1 and both throttle position sensors, on the Saab 9-4X the throttle and pedal sensor 1 – a fault therefore usually means electronic throttle limp mode.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once (pedal, throttle, pressure)","Electronic throttle limp mode, reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P06A4":["Sensor Reference Voltage D Circuit Low","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. On GM engines (2016–2025) circuit D feeds pedal sensor 1 and both throttle position sensors, on the Saab 9-4X the throttle and pedal sensor 1 – a fault therefore usually means electronic throttle limp mode.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once (pedal, throttle, pressure)","Electronic throttle limp mode, reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P06A5":["Sensor Reference Voltage D Circuit High","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. On GM engines (2016–2025) circuit D feeds pedal sensor 1 and both throttle position sensors, on the Saab 9-4X the throttle and pedal sensor 1 – a fault therefore usually means electronic throttle limp mode.",[["h","Reference or sensor signal wire shorted to 12 V"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once (pedal, throttle, pressure)","Electronic throttle limp mode, reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P06A6":["Sensor Reference Voltage A Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06A6 is the range/performance check of reference circuit A: the voltage is outside a narrow window without being split into low and high.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Fuel pressure or crankshaft sensor codes as well","Reduced power or starting problems"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P06A7":["Sensor Reference Voltage B Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down, so all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06A7 is the range/performance check of reference circuit B, without separate low and high.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator"]],["Check engine light","Camshaft sensor codes as well","Starting problems or reduced power"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P06A8":["Sensor Reference Voltage C Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06A8 is the range/performance check of reference circuit C, without separate low and high codes.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P06A9":["Sensor Reference Voltage D Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06A9 is the range/performance check of reference circuit D.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P06AA":["Control Module Internal Temperature B Too High","Control modules can carry a second internal temperature sensor (B). In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics, sensor A measures the circuit board (substrate) temperature, sensor B the 'power-up temperature' used as a reference and substitute; both sit inside the transmission module in the valve body, so repairs mean module or valve body.",[["h","Transmission fluid too hot (cooler, lines, level)"],["m","Charging system overvoltage"],["m","Faulty internal sensor or module"],["l","High sustained load (towing, heat)"]],["Transmission limp mode possible","Transmission warning","Often transmission overtemperature as well"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P06AB":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor B Circuit","Control modules can carry a second internal temperature sensor (B). In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics, sensor A measures the circuit board (substrate) temperature, sensor B the 'power-up temperature' used as a reference and substitute; both sit inside the transmission module in the valve body, so repairs mean module or valve body.",[["h","Transmission module or valve body (internal sensor) faulty"],["m","Moisture or fluid in the module electronics"],["l","Module supply or software"]],["Warning or check engine light","Changed shift quality (substitute value)","Implausible module temperature in the scan tool"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P06AC":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor B Range/Performance","Control modules can carry a second internal temperature sensor (B). In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics, sensor A measures the circuit board (substrate) temperature, sensor B the 'power-up temperature' used as a reference and substitute; both sit inside the transmission module in the valve body, so repairs mean module or valve body.",[["h","Transmission module or valve body (internal sensor) faulty"],["m","Transmission fluid temperature sensor reads wrong"],["l","Module supply or software"]],["Warning or check engine light","Changed shift quality (substitute value)","Implausible module temperature in the scan tool"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P06AD":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","Control modules can carry a second internal temperature sensor (B). In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics, sensor A measures the circuit board (substrate) temperature, sensor B the 'power-up temperature' used as a reference and substitute; both sit inside the transmission module in the valve body, so repairs mean module or valve body.",[["h","Transmission module or valve body (internal sensor) faulty"],["m","Moisture or fluid in the module electronics"],["l","Module supply or software"]],["Warning or check engine light","Changed shift quality (substitute value)","Implausible module temperature in the scan tool"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P06AE":["Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","Control modules can carry a second internal temperature sensor (B). In GM's 6T70/6T75 and related automatics, sensor A measures the circuit board (substrate) temperature, sensor B the 'power-up temperature' used as a reference and substitute; both sit inside the transmission module in the valve body, so repairs mean module or valve body.",[["h","Transmission module or valve body (internal sensor) faulty"],["m","Moisture or fluid in the module electronics"],["l","Module supply or software"]],["Warning or check engine light","Changed shift quality (substitute value)","Implausible module temperature in the scan tool"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P06AF":["Torque Management System Forced Engine Shutdown","GM uses P06AF for a cross-check between two control modules: the monitored module (engine or transmission module, each with main and secondary processor) sends a fixed answer sequence over CAN only if both its processors work together correctly; the monitoring module (engine, transmission or hybrid control module) sets P06AF if the pattern is wrong or missing. In all GM tables from 2016 to 2025 it is type A – lamp in the first trip – and the torque management may shut the engine down for safety.",[["h","Software fault in engine, transmission or hybrid module"],["m","Processor fault in one of the modules"],["m","CAN communication disturbance between the modules"],["l","Supply interruption or poor ground of a module"]],["Check engine light","Engine shuts off or does not start","Reduced power"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P06B0":["Sensor Power Supply A Circuit/Open","Despite the similar name, 'sensor power supply' is not the 5 V sensor reference (P0641 ff). It is documented in GM hybrid drive motor control modules: a 15 V supply that excites the motor position sensor (resolver) and powers the gate drivers of the inverter transistors, kept separate from the 5 V reference and the 12 V supply.",[["h","Blown fuse or open feed to the module"],["m","Internal supply circuit of the motor control module faulty"],["m","Connector or wiring fault"],["l","Short in a supplied component (resolver, gate driver)"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Electric drive or hybrid mode limited or disabled","Further motor position or inverter codes"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P06B1":["Sensor Power Supply A Circuit Low","Despite the similar name, 'sensor power supply' is not the 5 V sensor reference (P0641 ff). It is documented in GM hybrid drive motor control modules: a 15 V supply that excites the motor position sensor (resolver) and powers the gate drivers of the inverter transistors, kept separate from the 5 V reference and the 12 V supply.",[["h","Blown supply fuse (e.g. 175 A or 7.5 A at GM eAssist)"],["m","Supplied component or wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal supply circuit of the motor control module faulty"],["l","Low 12 V system voltage"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Electric drive or hybrid mode limited or disabled","Further motor position or inverter codes"],["hard",[50,1500]],"c"],"P06B2":["Sensor Power Supply A Circuit High","Despite the similar name, 'sensor power supply' is not the 5 V sensor reference (P0641 ff). It is documented in GM hybrid drive motor control modules: a 15 V supply that excites the motor position sensor (resolver) and powers the gate drivers of the inverter transistors, kept separate from the 5 V reference and the 12 V supply.",[["h","Internal supply regulator of the motor control module faulty"],["m","Overvoltage in the 12 V system (DC/DC converter, charging)"],["m","Supply wire shorted to a higher voltage"],["l","Connector or wiring fault"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Electric drive or hybrid mode limited or disabled","Further motor position or inverter codes"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P06B3":["Sensor Power Supply B Circuit/Open","Despite the similar name, 'sensor power supply' is not the 5 V sensor reference (P0641 ff). It is documented in GM hybrid drive motor control modules: a 15 V supply that excites the motor position sensor (resolver) and powers the gate drivers of the inverter transistors, kept separate from the 5 V reference and the 12 V supply.",[["h","Blown fuse or open feed to the module"],["m","Internal supply circuit of the motor control module faulty"],["m","Connector or wiring fault"],["l","Short in a supplied component"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Electric drive or hybrid mode limited or disabled","Further motor position or inverter codes"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P06B4":["Sensor Power Supply B Circuit Low","Despite the similar name, 'sensor power supply' is not the 5 V sensor reference (P0641 ff). It is documented in GM hybrid drive motor control modules: a 15 V supply that excites the motor position sensor (resolver) and powers the gate drivers of the inverter transistors, kept separate from the 5 V reference and the 12 V supply.",[["h","Blown supply fuse"],["m","Supplied component or wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal supply circuit faulty"],["l","Low 12 V system voltage"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Electric drive or hybrid mode limited or disabled","Further motor position or inverter codes"],["hard",[50,1500]],"c"],"P06B5":["Sensor Power Supply B Circuit High","Despite the similar name, 'sensor power supply' is not the 5 V sensor reference (P0641 ff). It is documented in GM hybrid drive motor control modules: a 15 V supply that excites the motor position sensor (resolver) and powers the gate drivers of the inverter transistors, kept separate from the 5 V reference and the 12 V supply.",[["h","Internal supply regulator faulty"],["m","Overvoltage in the 12 V system"],["m","Supply wire shorted to a higher voltage"],["l","Connector or wiring fault"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Electric drive or hybrid mode limited or disabled","Further motor position or inverter codes"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P06B6":["Internal Control Module Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor Processor 1 Performance","To detect an open knock sensor circuit, GM's engine modules feed a 20 kHz test signal into the sensor circuit and check whether it returns. P06B6 checks the internal test circuit or knock signal processor for sensor 1: it sets when the returned 20 kHz component lies between the open-circuit thresholds, so the module cannot decide 'connected' or 'open'.",[["h","Engine module software (GM bulletin)"],["m","Internal knock processor or test circuit faulty"],["m","Knock sensor wiring with high resistance or intermittent contact"],["l","Knock sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light","Retarded ignition, less power and higher consumption","Often no other symptoms"],["medium",[0,1200]],"c"],"P06B7":["Internal Control Module Knock/Combustion Vibration Sensor Processor 2 Performance","To detect an open knock sensor circuit, GM's engine modules feed a 20 kHz test signal into the sensor circuit and check whether it returns. P06B7 checks the internal test circuit or knock signal processor for sensor 2: it sets when the returned 20 kHz component lies between the open-circuit thresholds.",[["h","Engine module software (GM bulletin)"],["m","Internal knock processor or test circuit faulty"],["m","Knock sensor wiring with high resistance or intermittent contact"],["l","Knock sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light","Retarded ignition, less power and higher consumption","Often no other symptoms"],["medium",[0,1200]],"c"],"P06B8":["Internal Control Module Non-Volatile Random Access Memory (NVRAM) Error","Non-volatile RAM keeps its content without power; among other things it stores the permanent fault codes CARB requires, which cannot be cleared with a scan tool. Ford sets P06B8 when the checksum of this permanent-code memory is wrong; a common companion is P068A (main relay dropped out too early, memory write incomplete).",[["h","Interrupted shutdown (battery disconnected, voltage dip, main relay too early)"],["m","Engine module software"],["m","Weak battery or poor ground"],["l","Memory defect in the module"]],["Check engine light (depending on the vehicle)","Often no driving symptoms","Possibly P068A as well"],["medium",[0,1200]],"c"],"P06B9":["Cylinder 1 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 1 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06BA":["Cylinder 2 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 2 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06BB":["Cylinder 3 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 3 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06BC":["Cylinder 4 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 4 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06BD":["Cylinder 5 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 5 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06BE":["Cylinder 6 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 6 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06BF":["Cylinder 7 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 7 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06C0":["Cylinder 8 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 8 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06C1":["Cylinder 9 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 9 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06C2":["Cylinder 10 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 10 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06C3":["Cylinder 11 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 11 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06C4":["Cylinder 12 Glow Plug Circuit Range/Performance","The glow module calculates each plug's resistance from voltage and current while glowing. 'Range/performance' means the circuit is electrically intact but the resistance or current lies outside the specified window – typically an aged, high-resistance or wrong plug or contact resistance at the connector.",[["h","Glow plug cylinder 12 aged or high-resistance"],["m","Contact resistance at plug connector or terminal nut"],["m","Wrong plug type or a plug with a manufacturing fault"],["l","Engine or glow module software (Ram 6.7 L from 2025)"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start in frost","Rough running and white smoke after a cold start"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P06C5":["Cylinder 1 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 1 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06C6":["Cylinder 2 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 2 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06C7":["Cylinder 3 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 3 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06C8":["Cylinder 4 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 4 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06C9":["Cylinder 5 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 5 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06CA":["Cylinder 6 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 6 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06CB":["Cylinder 7 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 7 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06CC":["Cylinder 8 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 8 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06CD":["Cylinder 9 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 9 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06CE":["Cylinder 10 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 10 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06CF":["Cylinder 11 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 11 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06D0":["Cylinder 12 Glow Plug Incorrect","A glow plug has no identification; the control unit recognises its type only from its electrical behaviour – cold resistance at switch-on and how resistance rises during heating (R = U/I). A Beru patent describes how steel and ceramic plugs are told apart this way so the right heating profile is used and a wrong or mixed type is reported.",[["h","Wrong glow plug type fitted in cylinder 12 (steel/ceramic, voltage rating)"],["m","Plug resistance drifted (ageing, damage)"],["m","Glow module software or check during cranking (false report)"],["l","Contact resistance at the plug connector"]],["Check engine and glow plug lamp","Harder cold start or white smoke","Possibly plug burnt out (low-voltage plug on 11 V control)"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P06D1":["Internal Control Module Ignition Coil Control Performance","On ignition systems where the coils are driven through a separate driver chip inside the engine module, P06D1 reports that this internal coil control does not work correctly. Ford checks the communication with the coil driver chip continuously and sets the code within 1 s at 200–3200 rpm, positive torque and 11–16 V.",[["h","Coil driver chip or internal communication in the engine module faulty"],["m","Engine module software"],["m","Supply or ground problem at the module"],["l","Shorted coil overloading the driver"]],["Check engine light","Misfires or rough running","Possibly no start"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P06D2":["Sensor Reference Voltage E Circuit/Open","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D2 is the general code for circuit E.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06D3":["Sensor Reference Voltage E Circuit Low","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D3 means reference E is too low – typically a short to ground.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["m","Engine module internal fault (e.g. leaking capacitor, Ram 2023)"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06D4":["Sensor Reference Voltage E Circuit High","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D4 means reference E is too high – often a sensor signal wire shorted to 12 V.",[["h","Reference or sensor signal wire shorted to 12 V"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06D5":["Sensor Reference Voltage E Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D5 is the range/performance check of reference circuit E.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06D6":["Sensor Reference Voltage F Circuit/Open","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D6 is the general code for circuit F.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06D7":["Sensor Reference Voltage F Circuit Low","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D7 means reference F is too low – typically a short to ground.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06D8":["Sensor Reference Voltage F Circuit High","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D8 means reference F is too high – a short to voltage, often via a sensor signal wire.",[["h","Reference or sensor signal wire shorted to 12 V"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06D9":["Sensor Reference Voltage F Circuit Range/Performance","The engine module feeds sensors from several regulated 5 V reference circuits; a short at one sensor pulls the whole circuit down and all its sensors report at once – unplug them one by one to find the culprit. P06D9 is the range/performance check of reference circuit F.",[["h","Short in the wire or sensor of a connected sensor"],["m","Chafing or water in harness or connector"],["m","A connected sensor faulty internally"],["l","Module internal voltage regulator or components"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at once","Reduced power or limp mode"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06DA":["Engine Oil Pressure Control Circuit/Open","A switchable or variable oil pump saves fuel by lowering its output at part load; a solenoid valve sets the stage. GM and Ford design it so the pump runs at full output, i.e.",[["h","Connector at the control valve loose or corroded (often at the pump in the oil pan)"],["m","Valve coil open or valve broken"],["m","Supply (main relay) or control wire broken"],["l","Engine module software"]],["Check engine light after two trips","Usually no noticeable symptom, oil pressure permanently high","Slightly higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[60,450]],"u"],"P06DB":["Engine Oil Pressure Control Circuit Low","A switchable or variable oil pump saves fuel by lowering its output at part load; a solenoid valve sets the stage. GM and Ford design it so the pump runs at full output, i.e.",[["h","Control wire chafing against ground (engine block, oil pan)"],["m","Control valve shorted internally"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Engine module driver stage"]],["Check engine light at once (GM after one trip)","Low oil pressure at high engine speed, possibly oil pressure warning","Possibly reduced power"],["medium",[60,450]],"u"],"P06DC":["Engine Oil Pressure Control Circuit High","A switchable or variable oil pump saves fuel by lowering its output at part load; a solenoid valve sets the stage. GM and Ford design it so the pump runs at full output, i.e.",[["h","Control wire shorted to positive"],["m","Valve coil shorted (looks like a short to positive at the driver)"],["l","Engine module"]],["Check engine light after two trips","Usually no noticeable symptom, oil pressure permanently high","Slightly higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[60,450]],"u"],"P06DD":["Engine Oil Pressure Control Circuit Performance/Stuck Off","A switchable oil pump changes its output via a solenoid valve; unpowered it delivers high pressure. To test it, the module switches the pump between stages (GM up to 15 times, with oil at 50–110 °C and 1350–2500 rpm, expecting more than 100 kPa difference) and watches the oil pressure sensor; Ford checks each switch-over of a two-stage pump within 1 s, or a variable pump over 10 s, from 30 s after start.",[["h","Control valve stuck or broken, screen clogged"],["m","Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludge"],["m","Mechanical pump or drive damage (tensioner, drive gear, pump face)"],["l","Valve not seated correctly (retaining clip)"]],["Check engine light after two trips","Oil pressure stays high – or, with pump damage, low or fluctuating","Possibly oil pressure warning"],["hard",[100,3500]],"u"],"P06DE":["Engine Oil Pressure Control Circuit Stuck On","A switchable oil pump changes its output via a solenoid valve; powered it runs at the low stage. To test it, the module switches the pump between stages (GM up to 15 times, with oil at 50–110 °C and 1350–2500 rpm, expecting more than 100 kPa difference) and watches the oil pressure sensor; Ford checks each switch-over of a two-stage pump within 1 s, or a variable pump over 10 s, from 30 s after start.",[["h","Control valve stuck in the powered position"],["m","Low oil level, diluted or sludged oil, aeration"],["m","Pump wear or internal leakage"],["l","Engine module software (FCA 3.6 L)"]],["Check engine light at once","Oil pressure warning at higher engine speed possible","Possibly reduced power or raised idle"],["hard",[100,3500]],"u"],"P06DF":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 Memory Checksum Error","A diesel glow plug control module has its own microcontroller. On power-up it forms a checksum over its memory and compares it with the stored value; P06DF means they differ, so the module's program or data cannot be trusted.",[["h","Glow plug control module faulty internally (memory, controller)"],["m","Failed or interrupted programming of the module"],["l","Voltage dip or spike at power-up (weak battery, poor ground)"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in module or connector"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking","White smoke and knocking after cold start"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P06E0":["Cylinder 11 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Diesel glow systems switch each plug through its own power semiconductor and switch off a faulty channel; CARB requires single-plug detection from model year 2010. P06E0 means the circuit to the cylinder 11 glow plug is shorted to ground or draws overcurrent.",[["h","Cylinder 11 glow plug shorted internally"],["m","Plug wire chafed through to ground"],["m","Burnt connector or terminal nut, tracking through oil or moisture"],["l","Driver stage in the glow module defective"]],["Check engine light (GM: also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in frost","Rough cold idle, white smoke after cold start"],["medium",[80,800]],"u"],"P06E1":["Cylinder 11 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Diesel glow systems switch each plug through its own power semiconductor and switch off a faulty channel; CARB requires single-plug detection from model year 2010. P06E1 means the circuit to the cylinder 11 glow plug is shorted to positive: voltage is present although the driver is off, or no current flows when it is on.",[["h","Plug wire shorted to supply in the harness"],["m","Driver stage in the glow module shorted (permanently on)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion bridging to a supply pin"],["l","Glow module software"]],["Check engine light (GM: also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in frost","Rough cold idle, white smoke after cold start"],["medium",[80,800]],"u"],"P06E2":["Cylinder 12 Glow Plug Control Circuit Low","Diesel glow systems switch each plug through its own power semiconductor and switch off a faulty channel; CARB requires single-plug detection from model year 2010. P06E2 means the circuit to the cylinder 12 glow plug is shorted to ground or draws overcurrent.",[["h","Cylinder 12 glow plug shorted internally"],["m","Plug wire chafed through to ground"],["m","Burnt connector or terminal nut, tracking through oil or moisture"],["l","Driver stage in the glow module defective"]],["Check engine light (GM: also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in frost","Rough cold idle, white smoke after cold start"],["medium",[80,800]],"u"],"P06E3":["Cylinder 12 Glow Plug Control Circuit High","Diesel glow systems switch each plug through its own power semiconductor and switch off a faulty channel; CARB requires single-plug detection from model year 2010. P06E3 means the circuit to the cylinder 12 glow plug is shorted to positive: voltage is present although the driver is off, or no current flows when it is on.",[["h","Plug wire shorted to supply in the harness"],["m","Driver stage in the glow module shorted (permanently on)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion bridging to a supply pin"],["l","Glow module software"]],["Check engine light (GM: also glow plug lamp)","Harder cold start in frost","Rough cold idle, white smoke after cold start"],["medium",[80,800]],"u"],"P06E4":["Control Module Wake-up Circuit Performance","Control modules are woken by a dedicated wake-up line or a network wake-up – at ignition on, when a charging cable is plugged in, or on a timer for engine-off diagnostics and battery management. P06E4 means this wake-up signal is implausible: the module wakes too late or not at all, stays awake, or the line state does not match the network status.",[["h","Module software (false setting, permanent code does not clear)"],["m","Wake-up line or connector open or shorted"],["m","Weak 12 V battery, voltage dips at wake-up"],["l","Engine or hybrid control module faulty"]],["Check engine light","Permanent code blocks emission inspection","Possibly battery drain (module stays awake)"],["medium",[80,400]],"u"],"P06E5":["Glow Plug Control Module 1 Performance","P06E5 means glow plug control module 1 does not respond correctly to the glow command: its outputs do not switch or its internal drivers report faults or overheating. Ford's 2023 6.7 L and 3.0 L Power Stroke list P06E5 among the glow module checks, run continuously, with a fault after about 1 s when internal drivers for glow plugs, DEF heaters or relays fail to switch or overheat for more than 1 s – Ford does not assign each threshold to a single code.",[["h","Glow plug control module faulty internally (drivers do not switch)"],["m","Poor supply or ground of the module (main feed, fuse, ground)"],["m","Module overheating (mounting location, short at a plug)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking","White smoke and rough running after cold start"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P06E6":["Sensor Power Supply C Circuit/Open","'Sensor power supply' is a separate J2012 family next to the 5 V sensor reference (P0697 ff): an additional supply from the module for sensors or drivers – in GM hybrids a 15 V supply for resolvers and inverter gate drivers, there as supplies A and B. Supply C is a third such circuit.",[["h","Open circuit or connector problem in the supply circuit"],["m","Short in the supply wire or a supplied sensor"],["m","Supply regulator inside the module faulty"],["l","Vehicle under- or overvoltage"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at the same time","Limp mode or reduced power possible"],["medium",[100,1200]],"u"],"P06E7":["Sensor Power Supply C Circuit Low","'Sensor power supply' is a separate J2012 family next to the 5 V sensor reference (P0697 ff): an additional supply from the module for sensors or drivers – in GM hybrids a 15 V supply for resolvers and inverter gate drivers, there as supplies A and B. Supply C is a third such circuit.",[["h","Short to ground in the supply wire or a supplied sensor"],["m","Supply regulator inside the module faulty"],["l","Connector problem in the supply circuit"],["l","Vehicle undervoltage"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at the same time","Limp mode or reduced power possible"],["medium",[100,1200]],"u"],"P06E8":["Sensor Power Supply C Circuit High","'Sensor power supply' is a separate J2012 family next to the 5 V sensor reference (P0697 ff): an additional supply from the module for sensors or drivers – in GM hybrids a 15 V supply for resolvers and inverter gate drivers, there as supplies A and B. Supply C is a third such circuit.",[["h","Short to positive in the supply wire or a supplied sensor"],["m","Supply regulator inside the module faulty"],["l","Vehicle overvoltage (alternator)"]],["Check engine light","Several sensor codes at the same time","Limp mode or reduced power possible"],["medium",[100,1200]],"u"],"P06E9":["Engine Starter Performance","On cars with stop-start or keyless start the engine module operates the starter relay itself and watches the start: if cranking is commanded but the engine does not turn, turns too slowly or the start is aborted, it sets P06E9. CARB counts an engine start from 150 rpm below warm idle.",[["h","Starter worn or solenoid contacts intermittently open"],["h","Weak battery, voltage drop on main cables or ground"],["m","Starter relay or its control"],["m","Engine module software"]],["Intermittent no-start or only clicking","Stop-start not working","Check engine light depending on manufacturer (FCA: yes)"],["medium",[50,700]],"u"],"P06EA":["NOx Sensor Processor Performance Bank 1 Sensor 1","A modern NOx sensor is a smart sensor: a ceramic sensing element with pump cells and heater plus its own control unit with a microcontroller, which regulates the pump currents, calculates NOx and oxygen and sends them to the engine module over CAN. P06EA means this control unit of sensor 1 (before the SCR catalyst) reports an internal fault – its self-tests of RAM and ROM or EEPROM checksums fail – so the electronics, not the sensing element, are at fault.",[["h","Faulty electronics in the NOx sensor control unit"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in control unit or connector (underbody location)"],["m","Under- or overvoltage or dips in the sensor supply"],["l","Unsuitable replacement sensor or software"]],["Check engine light or emissions warning","Message to check AdBlue/emission system, possibly countdown to start lock","Follow-up codes for SCR efficiency or NOx plausibility"],["medium",[250,900]],"u"],"P06EB":["NOx Sensor Processor Performance Bank 1 Sensor 2","A modern NOx sensor is a smart sensor: a ceramic sensing element with pump cells and heater plus its own control unit with a microcontroller, which regulates the pump currents, calculates NOx and oxygen and sends them to the engine module over CAN. P06EB means this control unit of sensor 2 (after the SCR catalyst) reports an internal fault – its self-tests of RAM and ROM or EEPROM checksums fail – so the electronics, not the sensing element, are at fault.",[["h","Faulty electronics in the NOx sensor control unit"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in control unit or connector (underbody location)"],["m","Under- or overvoltage or dips in the sensor supply"],["l","Unsuitable replacement sensor or software"]],["Check engine light or emissions warning","Message to check AdBlue/emission system, possibly countdown to start lock","Follow-up codes for SCR efficiency or NOx plausibility"],["medium",[250,900]],"u"],"P06EC":["Fuel Injector Control Module Requested MIL Illumination","P06EC is a notice code like P0700: a separate fuel injector control module has stored an emission-related fault of its own and asks the engine module over CAN to switch on the check engine light. The actual fault is stored in that module and must be read there; simple OBD readers show only P06EC.",[["h","Emission-related fault in the injector control module or its injectors/sensors (code stored there)"],["m","Supply, ground or relay problem of the module"],["m","Internal module fault (memory, processor)"],["l","Software level of engine or injector module"]],["Check engine light on","Generic scanner shows only P06EC – details in the injector module","Depending on the fault, power loss, misfire or limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P06ED":["Engine RPM Output Circuit Low","The engine module turns the crankshaft signal into a cleaned-up square wave for the tachometer, transmission or body-builder modules – Saab's 1999 9-3, for example, sends two pulses per revolution, about 30 Hz at idle. P06ED means this output is too low, typically shorted to ground, so it can no longer switch high.",[["h","Speed output wire chafing against ground"],["m","Receiver (instrument, add-on device) shorted internally"],["m","Aftermarket tachometer or accessory wired wrongly"],["l","Engine module driver stage"]],["Tachometer at zero or jumping","Other modules without speed signal","Engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P06EE":["Engine RPM Output Circuit High","The engine module turns the crankshaft signal into a cleaned-up square wave for the tachometer, transmission or body-builder modules – Saab's 1999 9-3, for example, sends two pulses per revolution, about 30 Hz at idle. P06EE means this output is too high, typically shorted to supply voltage, so it can no longer switch to ground; the driver may shut down for protection.",[["h","Output wire shorted to 12 V"],["m","Wrongly connected accessory (speed pick-up)"],["m","Receiving device faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["Tachometer shows nothing or full scale","Other modules without speed signal","Engine runs normally"],["medium",[40,300]],"u"],"P06EF":["Engine Restart Performance","P06EF concerns the automatic restart after the system itself has stopped the engine – stop-start or hybrid. The restart, by starter, reinforced stop-start starter or belt starter-generator, takes too long or fails.",[["h","Starter or starter-generator worn, contacts intermittently open"],["m","Engine module software"],["m","Weak stop-start battery (AGM/EFB) or battery sensor"],["l","Fuel pressure or ignition at restart"]],["Engine does not restart or restarts late after stop-start","Stop-start disabled","Check engine light (FCA)"],["medium",[50,900]],"u"],"P06F0":["Reductant Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit","Some SCR diesels have a separate reductant control module that drives the DEF pump, heaters and dosing valve – Ford calls it the pump motor controller (fault above 130 °C under P20FF), GM's L5P an SCR power module. It measures its own board temperature with an internal sensor to protect itself from heat.",[["h","Reductant control module or pump unit faulty (sensor is on the board)"],["m","Water ingress or corrosion in the module (DEF crystals, underbody location)"],["m","Supply or ground problems at the module"],["l","Module software or calibration"]],["Check engine light or SCR warning","Message to check AdBlue system, possibly countdown to start lock","DEF heating or pump inactive (no thawing in frost)"],["hard",[400,1800]],"u"],"P06F1":["Reductant Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Range/Performance","Some SCR diesels have a separate reductant control module that drives the DEF pump, heaters and dosing valve – Ford calls it the pump motor controller (fault above 130 °C under P20FF), GM's L5P an SCR power module. It measures its own board temperature with an internal sensor to protect itself from heat.",[["h","Reductant control module or pump unit faulty (sensor is on the board)"],["m","Water ingress or corrosion in the module (DEF crystals, underbody location)"],["m","Supply or ground problems at the module"],["l","Module software or calibration"]],["Check engine light or SCR warning","Message to check AdBlue system, possibly countdown to start lock","DEF heating or pump inactive (no thawing in frost)"],["hard",[400,1800]],"u"],"P06F2":["Reductant Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","Some SCR diesels have a separate reductant control module that drives the DEF pump, heaters and dosing valve – Ford calls it the pump motor controller (fault above 130 °C under P20FF), GM's L5P an SCR power module. It measures its own board temperature with an internal sensor to protect itself from heat.",[["h","Reductant control module or pump unit faulty (sensor is on the board)"],["m","Water ingress or corrosion in the module (DEF crystals, underbody location)"],["m","Supply or ground problems at the module"],["l","Module software or calibration"]],["Check engine light or SCR warning","Message to check AdBlue system, possibly countdown to start lock","DEF heating or pump inactive (no thawing in frost)"],["hard",[400,1800]],"u"],"P06F3":["Reductant Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit High","Some SCR diesels have a separate reductant control module that drives the DEF pump, heaters and dosing valve – Ford calls it the pump motor controller (fault above 130 °C under P20FF), GM's L5P an SCR power module. It measures its own board temperature with an internal sensor to protect itself from heat.",[["h","Reductant control module or pump unit faulty (sensor is on the board)"],["m","Water ingress or corrosion in the module (DEF crystals, underbody location)"],["m","Supply or ground problems at the module"],["l","Module software or calibration"]],["Check engine light or SCR warning","Message to check AdBlue system, possibly countdown to start lock","DEF heating or pump inactive (no thawing in frost)"],["hard",[400,1800]],"u"],"P06F4":["Stop-Start Mode Lamp Control Circuit","When the engine module switches a lamp through its own wire, it checks the output driver electrically like other lamp outputs: with the lamp off the line must carry supply voltage, with it on nearly 0 V – GM's MIL driver, for example, flags an open circuit at 200 kΩ or more. It cannot see whether the bulb lights, and on cars that send the lamp command over CAN such faults appear as U codes.",[["h","Wire or connector to the indicator open or shorted"],["m","Indicator or instrument cluster faulty"],["l","Engine module driver stage"]],["Stop-start indicator not working or permanently lit","Usually no check engine light"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P06F5":["Glow Plug Control Module 2","SAE J2012 provides a complete code family for a second glow plug control module, mirroring module 1 code for code. P06F5 is the general or internal fault of module 2, the counterpart of P064C.",[["h","Glow plug control module 2 faulty internally"],["m","Poor supply or ground of module 2"],["l","Module software or configuration"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P06F6":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 Control Circuit/Open","P06F6 means the command line from the engine module to glow plug control module 2 is open or has a general circuit fault – the counterpart of P0670 for module 1. The engine module's output driver detects an open load while the driver is off.",[["h","Line from engine module to glow module 2 open, connector loose or corroded"],["m","Input of glow module 2 faulty or module not connected"],["l","Engine module output driver faulty"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06F7":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 Control Circuit Low","P06F7 means the command line from the engine module to glow plug control module 2 is too low, typically shorted to ground – the counterpart of P0383 for module 1. The engine module's output driver detects the short by its current when switched on or by the line voltage when switched off.",[["h","Command line to glow module 2 chafed through to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector bridging to ground"],["l","Input of glow module 2 or engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06F8":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 Control Circuit High","P06F8 means the command line from the engine module to glow plug control module 2 is too high, typically shorted to positive – the counterpart of P0384 for module 1. Note that Ford's 2023 diesels use P0384 differently, for the supply voltage of the glow module (below 6.5 V or above 16.5 V); in J2012 that is P052F, and for module 2 P06F9.",[["h","Command line shorted to positive or supply"],["m","Corrosion in the connector bridging to a B+ pin"],["l","Engine module driver shorted"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06F9":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 System Voltage","P06F9 means the supply voltage of glow plug control module 2 is out of range – the counterpart of P052F for module 1. For module 1, Ford's 2023 Power Stroke flags below 6.5 V, above 16.5 V or an open supply after about 1 s; GM's L5P checks the same under P163C (below 6.0 V with ignition above 9 V, or implausible against ignition voltage, 4 s).",[["h","Main feed to glow module 2 open or high-resistance (fuse, fusible link, B+ wire)"],["m","Weak battery or discharged auxiliary battery"],["m","Module ground corroded"],["l","Charging voltage too high"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[30,500]],"u"],"P06FA":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 Performance","P06FA means glow plug control module 2 does not respond correctly to the glow command – the counterpart of P06E5 for module 1. For module 1, Ford's 2023 Power Stroke flags internal drivers that fail to switch or overheat for more than 1 s; GM's L5P files the same check under P064C (under 3.2 A with 0 V at the plug for 3.5 s, two trips).",[["h","Glow module 2 faulty internally (drivers do not switch)"],["m","Poor supply or ground of module 2"],["m","Module overheating"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P06FB":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 Memory Checksum Error","P06FB means glow plug control module 2 has found a memory checksum error – the counterpart of P06DF for module 1. The module forms a checksum over its memory at power-up and compares it with the stored value.",[["h","Glow module 2 faulty internally (memory)"],["m","Interrupted programming or faulty software"],["l","Voltage dip at power-up"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[100,700]],"u"],"P06FC":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 to PCM Communication Circuit","P06FC means the feedback or communication from glow plug control module 2 to the engine module is missing, or the line is open or shorted – the counterpart of P0683 for module 1. Depending on design the module reports over a single feedback wire (Bosch-type systems expect it inverse to the command line) or over the bus; GM and Ford report a lost bus link as U0106 rather than P0683.",[["h","Feedback or bus line open or shorted, connector corroded"],["m","Glow module 2 without supply (does not transmit)"],["l","Glow module 2 or engine module interface faulty"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06FD":["Glow Plug Control Module 2 to PCM Communication Circuit Range/Performance","P06FD means signals or messages from glow plug control module 2 arrive at the engine module but are implausible – wrong duty cycle or pattern, feedback not matching the command, or single messages timing out. It is the counterpart of P0684 for module 1; Saab's Bosch-based diesels, for example, expect the feedback signal to be inverse to the command line.",[["h","Loose contact or contact resistance in the feedback or bus line"],["m","Ground offset between module and engine module from glow currents"],["l","Software mismatch between modules or module faulty"]],["Check engine light, possibly glow plug/wait-to-start lamp","Hard cold start, long cranking in frost","Rough cold idle and white smoke, often one cylinder bank only"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P06FE":["Cold Start Diesel Intake Air Flow Control Performance","'Diesel intake air flow control' is the diesel's electric intake throttle with position sensor (P02E0 ff). During cold start many diesels close it partly: the extra load raises exhaust temperature so the catalysts light off sooner.",[["h","Intake throttle coked with soot and oil (EGR, crankcase ventilation)"],["m","Throttle motor or gear stiff, position sensor faulty"],["l","Ice or condensate in the intake in frost"],["l","Contact resistance in connector or wiring, low voltage"]],["Check engine light, usually after two trips","Hardly any change in driveability","Possibly more smoke or smell while warming up"],["medium",[80,700]],"u"],"P06FF":["Economy Mode Lamp Control Circuit","When the engine module switches a lamp through its own wire, it checks the output driver electrically like other lamp outputs: with the lamp off the line must carry supply voltage, with it on nearly 0 V – GM's MIL driver, for example, flags an open circuit at 200 kΩ or more. It cannot see whether the bulb lights, and on cars that send the lamp command over CAN such faults appear as U codes.",[["h","Wire or connector to the lamp open or shorted"],["m","Lamp or instrument cluster faulty"],["l","Engine module"]],["ECO indicator not working or permanently lit","No effect on driving"],["easy",[30,250]],"u"],"P0700":["Transmission Control System (MIL Request)","P0700 is a notice code: the separate transmission control module has stored an emission-related fault of its own and asks the engine module to switch on the check engine light. The actual fault – a sensor, solenoid, ratio or speed sensor code – is stored in the transmission module and must be read with a tool that can address it; simple OBD readers often show only P0700.",[["h","Actual fault stored in the transmission module (sensor, solenoid, ratio, speed sensor)"],["m","Wrong transmission fluid level or condition causing slip or ratio codes"],["m","Harness or connector at the transmission corroded or damaged"],["l","Low system voltage or weak battery"]],["Check engine light, possibly transmission warning","Depending on the transmission fault: limp mode, harsh or delayed shifts","Often no symptoms if the fault was intermittent"],["medium",[50,1500]],"c"],"P0701":["Transmission Control System Range/Performance","P0701 means the transmission control system works outside its expected range or its function is implausible – without a circuit fault. What exactly is checked is up to the manufacturer; no GM, Ford, Saab or bulletin document available to us describes the strategy.",[["m","Transmission control faulty internally or software"],["m","Implausible input signals (speed or range sensor) without own circuit fault"],["m","Wrong fluid level or quality – hydraulics do not respond"],["l","Harness or connector with contact resistance"]],["Transmission warning (at Ford no check engine light)","Limp mode with fixed gear or missing gears","Harsh or irregular shifts"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0702":["Transmission Control System Electrical","P0702 reports an electrical fault in the transmission control system as a whole – typically the supply of the transmission actuators (solenoid supply, transmission relay, supply switch in the module) or a circuit fault not assigned to a single component. No manufacturer document available to us describes it in detail; Ford's engine module list for 6.8 L E- and F-Series trucks stores it without the check engine light.",[["h","Transmission actuator supply interrupted (fuse, relay, ground)"],["m","Harness or connector at transmission or module damaged, corroded or oil-soaked"],["m","Transmission module output stage or supply switch faulty"],["l","Low or fluctuating system voltage"]],["Transmission limp mode with fixed gear","Transmission warning; check engine light depending on the maker","No or harsh shifts, further solenoid codes"],["medium",[50,1200]],"c"],"P0703":["Brake Switch B Circuit","Brake switch A is usually the brake light switch, B a second, redundant contact or signal used to cancel cruise control, release the converter clutch, check 'brake before throttle' and for stop-start. Since about 2014 GM receives it as a CAN message from the body module and checks its integrity (rolling counter, protection value: 3 of 10 faulty in 2014, 9 of 17 in 2025) without the check engine light; cruise control is disabled.",[["h","Brake light or double switch worn, corroded or misadjusted"],["m","Wiring or connector at the pedal damaged, fuse blown"],["m","CAN message from the body module disturbed (GM: software, bus fault)"],["l","Aftermarket LED lamps or trailer module distorting the circuit"]],["Cruise control cannot be engaged or drops out","Brake light stays on or does not work","Selector cannot be moved out of P (shift lock)"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0704":["Clutch Switch Input Circuit","Only on cars with a clutch pedal: the clutch switch tells the engine module that the clutch is depressed, used to cancel cruise control, smooth load changes when shifting and for stop-start. The check is a plausibility test over driving events rather than a voltage check: Saab's Trionic T8 expects a change of state every time the car pulls away from 3 to 50 km/h (four misses in a row), the EDC16 diesel at gear changes (25 in a row, above 35 km/h and 1500 rpm), and Ford compares its two clutch switches continuously.",[["h","Clutch switch faulty or misadjusted (does not switch or too late)"],["m","Connector or wiring at the pedal box damaged or corroded"],["m","Floor mat or pedal stop shifted, switch bracket broken"],["l","Driving style (pulling away or shifting without fully depressing the clutch)"]],["Cruise control not working or not cancelling when declutching","Jerks or rev hang when shifting","Stop-start inactive"],["easy",[20,120]],"c"],"P0705":["Transmission Range Sensor A Circuit (PRNDL Input)","The transmission range sensor (selector or multifunction switch, PRNDL) reports the selected range; it may be a multi-contact switch with a bit pattern per position, an analog sensor with two opposing voltages adding up to 5 V, or a contactless PWM sensor. Its second channel has its own codes (P2801–P2805).",[["h","Range sensor worn, dirty or with oil or water ingress"],["m","Sensor misadjusted or selector cable out of adjustment"],["m","Harness or connector at the transmission damaged, short to ground"],["l","Transmission or engine module input faulty"]],["Check engine and/or transmission warning lamp","Transmission limp mode, fixed gear, harsh shifts","Starting problems in P/N or start interlock"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0706":["Transmission Range Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The transmission range sensor (selector or multifunction switch, PRNDL) reports the selected range; it may be a multi-contact switch with a bit pattern per position, an analog sensor with two opposing voltages adding up to 5 V, or a contactless PWM sensor. Its second channel has its own codes (P2801–P2805).",[["h","Range sensor or transmission module with integrated sensor faulty"],["m","Selector cable or sensor misadjusted (position between two ranges)"],["m","Contact resistance in sensor supply or ground"],["l","Moisture or oil in harness or connector"]],["Check engine and/or transmission warning lamp","Transmission limp mode, fixed gear, harsh shifts","Starting problems in P/N or start interlock"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P0707":["Transmission Range Sensor A Circuit Low","The transmission range sensor (selector or multifunction switch, PRNDL) reports the selected range; it may be a multi-contact switch with a bit pattern per position, an analog sensor with two opposing voltages adding up to 5 V, or a contactless PWM sensor. Its second channel has its own codes (P2801–P2805).",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafing at the transmission case)"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Sensor supply missing (5 V reference, fuse)"],["l","Connector corroded or oil in the connector"]],["Check engine and/or transmission warning lamp","Transmission limp mode, fixed gear, harsh shifts","Starting problems in P/N or start interlock"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0708":["Transmission Range Sensor A Circuit High","The transmission range sensor (selector or multifunction switch, PRNDL) reports the selected range; it may be a multi-contact switch with a bit pattern per position, an analog sensor with two opposing voltages adding up to 5 V, or a contactless PWM sensor. Its second channel has its own codes (P2801–P2805).",[["h","Sensor connector off or signal/ground wire open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Poor sensor or transmission ground"]],["Check engine and/or transmission warning lamp","Transmission limp mode, fixed gear, harsh shifts","Starting problems in P/N or start interlock"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0709":["Transmission Range Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The transmission range sensor (selector or multifunction switch, PRNDL) reports the selected range; it may be a multi-contact switch with a bit pattern per position, an analog sensor with two opposing voltages adding up to 5 V, or a contactless PWM sensor. Its second channel has its own codes (P2801–P2805).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion in the range sensor connector"],["m","Worn sliding contacts in the selector switch"],["m","Harness chafing on transmission or bracket, breaking when moved"],["l","Misadjusted sensor or selector cable (position at the limit)"]],["Intermittent transmission warning or check engine light","Gear indication flickers, brief shift jolts","Occasional starting problems in P/N"],["medium",[50,450]],"c"],"P070A":["Transmission Fluid Level Sensor Circuit","Fluid level sensors in the transmission sump (capacitive or voltage-based) report the oil level to the transmission module; measurements are only valid under defined conditions – an Allison patent names fluid temperature, vehicle at rest, low input speed and a tilt correction. Such sensors are mainly found in heavy automatic transmissions.",[["h","Fluid level sensor faulty"],["m","Wire or connector to the sensor (often through the sump or transmission connector) open or oil-soaked"],["l","Transmission module input faulty"]],["Transmission warning or 'check fluid level' message","Fluid level display missing","Usually no driveability problems"],["medium",[100,500]],"c"],"P070B":["Transmission Fluid Level Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","Fluid level sensors in the transmission sump (capacitive or voltage-based) report the oil level to the transmission module; measurements are only valid under defined conditions – an Allison patent names fluid temperature, vehicle at rest, low input speed and a tilt correction. Such sensors are mainly found in heavy automatic transmissions.",[["h","Fluid level sensor drifting or dirty (wear debris, sludge)"],["m","Fluid level actually wrong (over- or underfilled) or wrong fluid"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wire"],["l","Faulty tilt or temperature compensation (fluid temperature sensor fault)"]],["Warning or message about transmission fluid level","Fluid level display implausible or unavailable","Usually no driveability problems"],["medium",[100,500]],"c"],"P070C":["Transmission Fluid Level Sensor Circuit Low","Fluid level sensors in the transmission sump report the oil level to the transmission module as a voltage; an Allison patent converts it to millimetres and only accepts readings with the vehicle at rest, warm fluid, low input speed and a tilt correction. Such sensors are mainly found in heavy automatic transmissions.",[["h","Signal wire to the level sensor shorted to ground (chafing, transmission connector)"],["m","Level sensor faulty internally"],["m","Transmission connector soaked in oil or corroded"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Fluid level display missing or warning to check transmission fluid","Usually no driveability problems","Transmission warning lamp depending on manufacturer"],["medium",[100,500]],"c"],"P070D":["Transmission Fluid Level Sensor Circuit High","Fluid level sensors in the transmission sump report the oil level to the transmission module as a voltage; an Allison patent converts it to millimetres and only accepts readings with the vehicle at rest, warm fluid, low input speed and a tilt correction. Such sensors are mainly found in heavy automatic transmissions.",[["h","Wire or connector to the level sensor open"],["m","Sensor faulty (open)"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Fluid level display missing or warning to check transmission fluid","Usually no driveability problems","Transmission warning lamp depending on manufacturer"],["medium",[100,500]],"c"],"P070E":["Transmission Fluid Level Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Fluid level sensors in the transmission sump report the oil level to the transmission module as a voltage; an Allison patent converts it to millimetres and only accepts readings with the vehicle at rest, warm fluid, low input speed and a tilt correction. Such sensors are mainly found in heavy automatic transmissions.",[["h","Loose contact in transmission connector or harness"],["m","Sensor with internal contact fault"],["l","Contaminated or foaming fluid at the sensor"]],["Fluid level display jumps or drops out at times","Intermittent warning message"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P070F":["Transmission Fluid Level Too Low","P070F is not a circuit fault but a condition: a working fluid level sensor reports a level below the target band. In the Allison patent the module compares the measured height in millimetres with a tilt-dependent band and reports at least a quart too little or too much – only with the vehicle at rest, fluid above about 30–40 °C and low input speed.",[["h","Leak (pan gasket, shaft seals, cooler lines)"],["m","Underfilled after fluid change or repair"],["l","Level sensor reading wrong (drift)"]],["Warning: transmission fluid level low","Slipping gears, delayed engagement","Transmission overheating"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0710":["Transmission Fluid Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The transmission fluid temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as the fluid warms; the module feeds it with 5 V through a resistor. A low signal therefore means very hot or a short to ground, a high signal very cold or an open circuit.",[["h","Fluid temperature sensor faulty (often part of the internal harness or valve body)"],["m","Transmission connector leaking or corroded, oil in the connector"],["m","Wire open or shorted"],["l","Transmission or engine module faulty"]],["Harsh or late shifts","Torque converter clutch applies late or not at all","Check engine or transmission lamp depending on manufacturer"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0711":["Transmission Fluid Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0711 means the fluid temperature signal is electrically valid but implausible. Manufacturers check three things: the fluid does not warm up as expected (GM 2025 still below 15 °C after the calibrated warm-up time, Saab's AF40 below 20 °C after 10 minutes of driving), the value is stuck (Ford: within ±2 °F for about 2.5 minutes below 70 or above 225 °F), or it jumps unrealistically.",[["h","Fluid temperature sensor drifting or stuck"],["m","High contact resistance in connector or harness (oil, corrosion)"],["m","Very cold weather or short trips (fluid does not reach warm-up threshold)"],["l","Outdated module software"]],["Harsh or late shifts","Torque converter clutch applies late or not at all","Check engine or transmission lamp depending on manufacturer"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0712":["Transmission Fluid Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The transmission fluid temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as the fluid warms; the module feeds it with 5 V through a resistor. A low signal therefore means very hot or a short to ground, a high signal very cold or an open circuit.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (internal harness, chafing)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["l","Transmission actually severely overheated"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Transmission in hot mode (early converter clutch, changed shift points)","Harsh shifts","Extremely high fluid temperature shown"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0713":["Transmission Fluid Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","The transmission fluid temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as the fluid warms; the module feeds it with 5 V through a resistor. A low signal therefore means very hot or a short to ground, a high signal very cold or an open circuit.",[["h","Transmission connector loose or corroded, wire open"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Fluid temperature shown around −40 °C","Converter clutch stays open, late upshifts","Higher fuel consumption"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0714":["Transmission Fluid Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The transmission fluid temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor whose resistance falls as the fluid warms; the module feeds it with 5 V through a resistor. A low signal therefore means very hot or a short to ground, a high signal very cold or an open circuit.",[["h","Loose contact in transmission connector (oil, corrosion, spread pins)"],["m","Internal harness chafing from movement or vibration"],["m","Sensor with internal contact fault"],["l","Module software (false warning)"]],["Harsh shifts at times","Jumping fluid temperature display","Check engine or transmission lamp depending on manufacturer"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0715":["Input/Turbine Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit","The input or turbine shaft speed sensor – a Hall sensor with square-wave output or an inductive sensor with sine output – reports the speed behind the torque converter. With output and engine speed it gives the actual gear ratio, converter slip and shift timing.",[["h","Speed sensor faulty (Hall IC or coil open or shorted)"],["h","Connector or harness corroded, chafed or loose pins (with internal sensors also the pass-through connector)"],["m","Metal debris on the sensor magnet or damaged reluctor wheel"],["l","Transmission module or its sensor supply faulty"]],["Check engine and/or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode with fixed gear, harsh shifts","Torque converter clutch does not apply"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0716":["Input/Turbine Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The input or turbine shaft speed sensor – a Hall sensor with square-wave output or an inductive sensor with sine output – reports the speed behind the torque converter. With output and engine speed it gives the actual gear ratio, converter slip and shift timing.",[["h","Loose contact or chafing in the sensor circuit"],["m","Sensor faulty, wrong air gap or metal debris"],["m","Reluctor wheel or clutch drum damaged"],["l","Sensor wires swapped after repair or interference"]],["Check engine and/or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode with fixed gear (6T70: reverse and fifth only)","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0717":["Input/Turbine Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit No Signal","The input or turbine shaft speed sensor – a Hall sensor with square-wave output or an inductive sensor with sine output – reports the speed behind the torque converter. With output and engine speed it gives the actual gear ratio, converter slip and shift timing.",[["h","Speed sensor faulty (Hall IC or coil open or shorted)"],["h","Connector or harness corroded, chafed or loose pins (with internal sensors also the pass-through connector)"],["m","Metal debris on the sensor magnet or damaged reluctor wheel"],["l","Transmission module or its sensor supply faulty"]],["Check engine and/or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode with fixed gear, harsh shifts","Torque converter clutch does not apply"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0718":["Input/Turbine Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The input or turbine shaft speed sensor – a Hall sensor with square-wave output or an inductive sensor with sine output – reports the speed behind the torque converter. With output and engine speed it gives the actual gear ratio, converter slip and shift timing.",[["h","Loose contact, corroded connector or chafing"],["m","Electrical interference (shielding, ground, added consumers)"],["m","Metal debris on the sensor or loose mounting"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (temperature-dependent)"]],["Occasional harsh or unexpected shifts","Warning lamp","Converter clutch applies and releases unevenly"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0719":["Brake Switch B Circuit Low","Brake switch B is the brake signal to the transmission module or a second brake switch channel, used to release the converter clutch, adapt shift strategy on grades and cancel cruise control. Low and high describe the signal voltage, and what that means depends on polarity: GM's converter clutch brake switch is closed at rest (12 V), while Saab's Trionic 8 sees 12 V when braking.",[["h","Brake light switch faulty or misadjusted"],["m","Wire to the module open (GM) or shorted to ground (Saab T8)"],["m","Connector at the pedal box corroded"],["l","Brake light circuit fuse blown"]],["Cruise control cannot be activated or switches off","Unusual shifting, converter clutch behaviour changed","No downshift on uphill grades (Subaru)"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P071A":["Transmission Mode Switch A Circuit","A transmission mode switch selects an alternative shift schedule such as sport, winter or tow/haul; J2012 does not define which button is A or B. No manufacturer document available to us uses P071A–P071F: GM monitors its analog drive mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, 200 of 250 samples, two trips), which shows how such buttons are read as coded voltages.",[["h","Mode switch A faulty (selector lever or console)"],["m","Wiring or connector in the console damaged (spilled drinks, chafing)"],["l","Resistor coding drifted (analog buttons)"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Mode A cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal shift schedule"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P071B":["Transmission Mode Switch A Circuit Low","A transmission mode switch selects an alternative shift schedule such as sport, winter or tow/haul; J2012 does not define which button is A or B. No manufacturer document available to us uses P071A–P071F: GM monitors its analog drive mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, 200 of 250 samples, two trips), which shows how such buttons are read as coded voltages.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Button stuck pressed (switching to ground)"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the button"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Mode A cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal shift schedule"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P071C":["Transmission Mode Switch A Circuit High","A transmission mode switch selects an alternative shift schedule such as sport, winter or tow/haul; J2012 does not define which button is A or B. No manufacturer document available to us uses P071A–P071F: GM monitors its analog drive mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, 200 of 250 samples, two trips), which shows how such buttons are read as coded voltages.",[["h","Wire or connector to the button open"],["m","Button open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Mode A cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal shift schedule"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P071D":["Transmission Mode Switch B Circuit","A transmission mode switch selects an alternative shift schedule such as sport, winter or tow/haul; J2012 does not define which button is A or B. No manufacturer document available to us uses P071A–P071F: GM monitors its analog drive mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, 200 of 250 samples, two trips), which shows how such buttons are read as coded voltages.",[["h","Mode switch B faulty (selector lever or console)"],["m","Wiring or connector in the console damaged (spilled drinks, chafing)"],["l","Resistor coding drifted (analog buttons)"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Mode B cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal shift schedule"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P071E":["Transmission Mode Switch B Circuit Low","A transmission mode switch selects an alternative shift schedule such as sport, winter or tow/haul; J2012 does not define which button is A or B. No manufacturer document available to us uses P071A–P071F: GM monitors its analog drive mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, 200 of 250 samples, two trips), which shows how such buttons are read as coded voltages.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Button stuck pressed (switching to ground)"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the button"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Mode B cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal shift schedule"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P071F":["Transmission Mode Switch B Circuit High","A transmission mode switch selects an alternative shift schedule such as sport, winter or tow/haul; J2012 does not define which button is A or B. No manufacturer document available to us uses P071A–P071F: GM monitors its analog drive mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, 200 of 250 samples, two trips), which shows how such buttons are read as coded voltages.",[["h","Wire or connector to the button open"],["m","Button open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Mode B cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal shift schedule"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0720":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit","The output shaft speed sensor (Hall or inductive) reports the speed of the transmission output; it gives the gear ratio together with input speed and on many vehicles also the road speed for speedometer and cruise control. P0720 is the general circuit code.",[["h","Speed sensor faulty (Hall IC or coil open or shorted)"],["h","Connector or harness corroded, chafed or loose pins (with internal sensors also the pass-through connector)"],["m","Metal debris on the sensor magnet or damaged reluctor wheel"],["l","Transmission module or its sensor supply faulty"]],["Speedometer drops out or jumps","Warning lamp, limp mode or fixed gear","Harsh shifts, cruise control not working"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0721":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The output shaft speed sensor (Hall or inductive) reports the speed of the transmission output; it gives the gear ratio together with input speed and on many vehicles also the road speed for speedometer and cruise control. P0721 means the output speed is present but implausible: too high (Ford 6R80 above 7450 rpm), not matching wheel or input speed in the engaged gear (ZF 6HP26 more than 200 or 250 rpm apart, Ford's F21 more than 15 % for 10 s), or jumping (Allison 1000: 500 rpm within 0.15 s).",[["h","Output speed sensor faulty or dirty (metal debris)"],["m","Contact problems or interference in the sensor circuit"],["m","Wrong tyre size or axle ratio, faulty wheel speed sensor"],["l","Reluctor wheel damaged or sensor mounted wrongly"]],["Speedometer jumps","Warning lamp","Wrong shifts, limp mode"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0722":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit No Signal","The output shaft speed sensor (Hall or inductive) reports the speed of the transmission output; it gives the gear ratio together with input speed and on many vehicles also the road speed for speedometer and cruise control. P0722 means the output speed signal is missing while the vehicle is driving.",[["h","Speed sensor faulty (Hall IC or coil open or shorted)"],["h","Connector or harness corroded, chafed or loose pins (with internal sensors also the pass-through connector)"],["m","Metal debris on the sensor magnet or damaged reluctor wheel"],["l","Transmission module or its sensor supply faulty"]],["Speedometer reads 0","Limp mode or fixed gear","Warning lamp (MIL, transmission)"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0723":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The output shaft speed sensor (Hall or inductive) reports the speed of the transmission output; it gives the gear ratio together with input speed and on many vehicles also the road speed for speedometer and cruise control. P0723 means the output speed drops or jumps briefly.",[["h","Loose contact, corroded connector or chafing"],["m","Electrical interference (shielding, ground, added consumers)"],["m","Metal debris on the sensor or loose mounting"],["m","Sensor faulty internally (temperature-dependent)"]],["Speedometer needle twitches or drops briefly","Occasional wrong shifts or downshift jolts","Warning lamp"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0724":["Brake Switch B Circuit High","Brake switch B is the brake signal to the transmission module or a second brake switch channel, used to release the converter clutch, adapt shift strategy on grades and cancel cruise control. Low and high describe the signal voltage, and what that means depends on polarity: GM's converter clutch brake switch is closed at rest (12 V), while Saab's Trionic 8 sees 12 V when braking.",[["h","Brake light switch sticking or misadjusted"],["m","Signal wire shorted to positive (Saab T8) or switch permanently closed (GM)"],["l","Retrofitted trailer or LED lights feeding back"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Cruise control cannot be activated or switches off","Unusual shifting, converter clutch behaviour changed","No downshift on uphill grades (Subaru)"],["easy",[20,150]],"c"],"P0725":["Engine Speed Input Circuit","The transmission module needs engine speed for converter slip, shift quality and to check the turbine speed. Today it almost always arrives over CAN from the engine module with a validity flag; only GM's 2007 Allison 1000 tables show a separate engine speed sensor read by the transmission module.",[["h","Crankshaft or camshaft sensor fault in the engine module (speed invalid there)"],["m","CAN fault between engine and transmission module (wiring, termination, connector)"],["l","Engine module software or fault"],["l","Transmission module faulty"]],["Transmission warning lamp, possibly check engine light","Torque converter clutch locked out","Harsh shifts or limp mode"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0726":["Engine Speed Input Circuit Range/Performance","The transmission module needs engine speed for converter slip, shift quality and to check the turbine speed. Today it almost always arrives over CAN from the engine module with a validity flag; only GM's 2007 Allison 1000 tables show a separate engine speed sensor read by the transmission module.",[["h","Engine speed sensor at the transmission (Allison) dirty or faulty, loose contact"],["m","Interference (shielding, ground)"],["m","Wrong bus value from the engine module (crankshaft sensor problem)"],["l","Transmission module"]],["Transmission warning lamp, possibly check engine light","Torque converter clutch locked out","Harsh shifts or limp mode"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0727":["Engine Speed Input Circuit No Signal","The transmission module needs engine speed for converter slip, shift quality and to check the turbine speed. Today it almost always arrives over CAN from the engine module with a validity flag; only GM's 2007 Allison 1000 tables show a separate engine speed sensor read by the transmission module.",[["h","Engine speed wire or connector open, or own sensor (Allison) faulty"],["m","CAN communication between engine and transmission module disturbed"],["m","Crankshaft sensor fault in the engine module"],["l","Transmission module faulty"]],["Transmission warning lamp, possibly check engine light","Torque converter clutch locked out","Harsh shifts or limp mode"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0728":["Engine Speed Input Circuit Intermittent","The transmission module needs engine speed for converter slip, shift quality and to check the turbine speed. Today it almost always arrives over CAN from the engine module with a validity flag; only GM's 2007 Allison 1000 tables show a separate engine speed sensor read by the transmission module.",[["h","Loose contact in signal or CAN circuit"],["m","Sensor (if fitted) dirty or loose"],["m","Intermittent crankshaft sensor fault in the engine module"],["l","Transmission module"]],["Occasional transmission warning lamp","Harsh shift now and then","Converter clutch releases occasionally"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0729":["Gear 6 Incorrect Ratio","The transmission module divides input (turbine) speed by output speed and compares the result with the ratio of the commanded gear; a deviation means slip – a clutch or brake not holding, too little pressure – or a wrong gear. Thresholds: Ford more than 30 rpm off for 1.8 s three times (diesel 2023: over 20 % for 12 s), GM's 2006 tables and the Allison 1000 converter slip of 200–230 rpm for 2.25 s after one trip, Saab's Aisin boxes over 20 % twelve times without MIL.",[["h","Fluid level too low or fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Clutch for sixth gear worn (linings, piston seal)"],["m","Sticking shift or pressure valve, leaking valve body gasket, blocked passages"],["m","GM manual transmissions only: gear position sensor faulty or not learned"]],["Slipping or flare in sixth gear, engine speed higher than expected","Harsh or delayed shifts","Limp mode (Aisin: second gear and reverse only), transmission warning"],["hard",[150,3500]],"c"],"P072A":["Stuck in Neutral","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum A motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P072B":["Stuck in Reverse","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum B motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P072C":["Stuck in Gear 1","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum A motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P072D":["Stuck in Gear 2","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum B motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P072E":["Stuck in Gear 3","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum A motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P072F":["Stuck in Gear 4","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum B motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P0730":["Incorrect Gear Ratio","P0730 reports a wrong transmission ratio without assigning it to a gear. Two uses are documented.",[["h","Fluid level or quality (CVT fluid or ATF)"],["h","Shift solenoid or pressure control valve sticking or faulty"],["m","Clutch or brake wear, or worn variator belt, chain or pulleys"],["l","Faulty speed sensor signal"]],["Slipping, engine speed rises without drive","Wrong gear or missing upper gears, limp mode","Check engine light (GM) or transmission warning"],["hard",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0731":["Gear 1 Incorrect Ratio","The transmission module divides input (turbine) speed by output speed and compares the result with the ratio of the commanded gear; a deviation means slip – a clutch or brake not holding, too little pressure – or a wrong gear. Thresholds: Ford more than 30 rpm off for 1.8 s three times (diesel 2023: over 20 % for 12 s), GM's 2006 tables and the Allison 1000 converter slip of 200–230 rpm for 2.25 s after one trip, Saab's Aisin boxes over 20 % twelve times without MIL.",[["h","Fluid level too low or fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Clutch or brake for first gear worn (linings, piston seal)"],["m","Sticking shift or pressure valve, leaking valve body gasket, blocked passages"],["m","GM manual transmissions only: gear position sensor faulty or not learned"]],["Slipping or flare in first gear, engine speed higher than expected","Harsh or delayed shifts","Limp mode (Aisin: second gear and reverse only), transmission warning"],["hard",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0732":["Gear 2 Incorrect Ratio","The transmission module divides input (turbine) speed by output speed and compares the result with the ratio of the commanded gear; a deviation means slip – a clutch or brake not holding, too little pressure – or a wrong gear. Thresholds: Ford more than 30 rpm off for 1.8 s three times (diesel 2023: over 20 % for 12 s), GM's 2006 tables and the Allison 1000 converter slip of 200–230 rpm for 2.25 s after one trip, Saab's Aisin boxes over 20 % twelve times without MIL.",[["h","Fluid level too low or fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Clutch or brake for second gear worn (linings, piston seal)"],["m","Sticking shift or pressure valve, leaking valve body gasket, blocked passages"],["m","GM manual transmissions only: gear position sensor faulty or not learned"]],["Slipping or flare in second gear, engine speed higher than expected","Harsh or delayed shifts","Limp mode (Aisin: second gear and reverse only), transmission warning"],["hard",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0733":["Gear 3 Incorrect Ratio","The transmission module divides input (turbine) speed by output speed and compares the result with the ratio of the commanded gear; a deviation means slip – a clutch or brake not holding, too little pressure – or a wrong gear. Thresholds: Ford more than 30 rpm off for 1.8 s three times (diesel 2023: over 20 % for 12 s), GM's 2006 tables and the Allison 1000 converter slip of 200–230 rpm for 2.25 s after one trip, Saab's Aisin boxes over 20 % twelve times without MIL.",[["h","Fluid level too low or fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Clutch or brake for third gear worn (linings, piston seal)"],["m","Sticking shift or pressure valve, leaking valve body gasket, blocked passages"],["m","GM manual transmissions only: gear position sensor faulty or not learned"]],["Slipping or flare in third gear, engine speed higher than expected","Harsh or delayed shifts","Limp mode (Aisin: second gear and reverse only), transmission warning"],["hard",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0734":["Gear 4 Incorrect Ratio","The transmission module divides input (turbine) speed by output speed and compares the result with the ratio of the commanded gear; a deviation means slip – a clutch or brake not holding, too little pressure – or a wrong gear. Thresholds: Ford more than 30 rpm off for 1.8 s three times (diesel 2023: over 20 % for 12 s), GM's 2006 tables and the Allison 1000 converter slip of 200–230 rpm for 2.25 s after one trip, Saab's Aisin boxes over 20 % twelve times without MIL.",[["h","Fluid level too low or fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Clutch or brake for fourth gear worn (linings, piston seal)"],["m","Sticking shift or pressure valve, leaking valve body gasket, blocked passages"],["m","GM manual transmissions only: gear position sensor faulty or not learned"]],["Slipping or flare in fourth gear, engine speed higher than expected","Harsh or delayed shifts","Limp mode (Aisin: second gear and reverse only), transmission warning"],["hard",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0735":["Gear 5 Incorrect Ratio","The transmission module divides input (turbine) speed by output speed and compares the result with the ratio of the commanded gear; a deviation means slip – a clutch or brake not holding, too little pressure – or a wrong gear. Thresholds: Ford more than 30 rpm off for 1.8 s three times (diesel 2023: over 20 % for 12 s), GM's 2006 tables and the Allison 1000 converter slip of 200–230 rpm for 2.25 s after one trip, Saab's Aisin boxes over 20 % twelve times without MIL.",[["h","Fluid level too low or fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Clutch or brake for fifth gear worn (linings, piston seal)"],["m","Sticking shift or pressure valve, leaking valve body gasket, blocked passages"],["m","GM manual transmissions only: gear position sensor faulty or not learned"]],["Slipping or flare in fifth gear, engine speed higher than expected","Harsh or delayed shifts","Limp mode (Aisin: second gear and reverse only), transmission warning"],["hard",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0736":["Reverse Incorrect Ratio","The transmission module divides input (turbine) speed by output speed and compares the result with the ratio of the commanded gear; a deviation means slip – a clutch or brake not holding, too little pressure – or a wrong gear. Thresholds: Ford more than 30 rpm off for 1.8 s three times (diesel 2023: over 20 % for 12 s), GM's 2006 tables and the Allison 1000 converter slip of 200–230 rpm for 2.25 s after one trip, Saab's Aisin boxes over 20 % twelve times without MIL.",[["h","Fluid level or condition"],["h","Reverse clutch or low/reverse brake worn"],["m","Valve body, shift valve or gasket"],["l","Oil pump or pump cover"]],["Delayed or slipping engagement of reverse","No reverse uphill or under load","Transmission warning, limp mode, check engine light depending on manufacturer"],["hard",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0737":["TCM Engine Speed Output Circuit","Some transmission modules pass an engine speed signal on to other devices such as a tachometer over a wire of their own. CARB requires output circuits to be checked.",[["h","Wire to the receiver open or shorted"],["m","Receiver (e.g. instrument cluster) shorted internally"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Driver stage in the transmission module faulty"]],["Tachometer or dependent display not working or wrong","Warning lamp depending on manufacturer","Usually no effect on driving"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0738":["TCM Engine Speed Output Circuit Low","Some transmission modules pass an engine speed signal on to other devices such as a tachometer over a wire of their own. CARB requires output circuits to be checked.",[["h","Output wire shorted to ground"],["m","Receiver shorted internally"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Driver stage in the transmission module faulty"]],["Tachometer at zero or jumping","Warning lamp depending on manufacturer","Usually no effect on driving"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P0739":["TCM Engine Speed Output Circuit High","Some transmission modules pass an engine speed signal on to other devices such as a tachometer over a wire of their own. CARB requires output circuits to be checked.",[["h","Output wire shorted to positive or open"],["m","Receiver faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Driver stage in the transmission module faulty"]],["Tachometer shows nothing or full scale","Warning lamp depending on manufacturer","Usually no effect on driving"],["medium",[50,300]],"c"],"P073A":["Stuck in Gear 5","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum A motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P073B":["Stuck in Gear 6","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum B motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P073C":["Stuck in Gear 7","Ford's DPS6 dry dual-clutch transmission moves its gears with two electrically driven shift drums whose position is read by Hall sensors: drum A shifts 1st, 3rd and 5th, drum B reverse, 2nd, 4th and 6th. Ford's 2012 summary lists 'stuck in gear' codes for each drum: the module commands the drum out of a gear, but the gear stays engaged.",[["m","Shift fork or synchronizer for this gear sticking"],["m","Shift drum B motor, Hall position sensing or driver faulty"],["l","Transmission module"],["l","On planetary automatics: clutch or brake not releasing (sticking valve)"]],["Transmission stays in one gear, no further shifts","Limp mode, transmission warning","Clunking or blocking when shifting"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P073D":["Unable to Engage Neutral","P073D means neutral is commanded but not reached or not held. GM uses it on vehicles with electronic gear selection (shift-by-wire, e.g.",[["h","Ford 6F35: forward or direct clutch dragging (balance piston, plates, seal)"],["m","Shift-by-wire: range actuator or park module, range sensor or its calibration"],["m","Valve body (manual valve, missing check ball, sticking solenoid)"],["l","CAN communication or control module"]],["Vehicle creeps in N or engine is loaded when shifting to N","N selection not accepted, warning message","Harsh engagement N to D/R (6F35)"],["hard",[150,3500]],"c"],"P073E":["Unable to Engage Reverse","P073E means reverse is commanded but not reached. Two uses are documented.",[["h","Range actuator or park module centred wrongly or faulty (shift-by-wire)"],["m","Module software (e.g. after an over-the-air update)"],["m","Range sensor reports the wrong actual range"],["m","Reverse clutch or brake, valve or shift fork (DCT)"]],["No reverse, or R not accepted","Transmission warning, check engine light after two trips (GM)","Display shows R but the vehicle does not reverse"],["hard",[50,5000]],"c"],"P073F":["Unable to Engage Gear 1","'Unable to engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – the opposite of 'stuck in gear', where a gear cannot be disengaged. Documented users are dual-clutch transmissions, whose shift forks are moved hydraulically and watched by travel sensors: GM's 8-speed in the 2020–23 Corvette, Maserati's MC20 and – for the odd gears only – Acura's RLX Sport Hybrid, whose 'odd gear monitor' is part of an emission recall with permanent codes.",[["h","Shift fork valve or solenoid in the mechatronic unit sticking (dirt, wear)"],["m","System pressure unstable or too low (pressure valve, pump, fluid level)"],["m","Shift fork, sleeve, synchronizer or dogs damaged"],["m","Learned shift fork or clutch values lost or wrong"]],["Gear 1 skipped or not engaged","Transmission warning or check engine light, possibly limp mode with one sub-gearbox locked out","Jerking, delayed pulling away or shifting"],["hard",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0740":["Torque Converter Clutch Circuit/Open","The torque converter clutch (lock-up) connects pump and turbine mechanically; a solenoid – on/off, pulse-width modulated or proportional – moves a control valve in the valve body. Slip is engine speed minus turbine speed.",[["h","Open or corroded harness or transmission pass-through connector"],["m","Converter clutch solenoid coil open"],["m","Internal transmission harness or mechatronic unit"],["l","Driver stage in the module faulty"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","No lock-up: engine speed about 100–300 rpm higher when cruising","Higher fuel consumption, hotter transmission fluid"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P0741":["Torque Converter Clutch Circuit Performance/Stuck Off","P0741 means the converter clutch does not lock although commanded – slip stays too high while the circuit is electrically fine. Thresholds: GM's T43 at least 130 rpm for 5 s twice, the 6T70 50 rpm for 5 s three times, Saab's Aisin AF40 over 100 rpm for 2 s, all after two trips; Ford over 100–200 rpm or a speed ratio below 0.93 over five lock-up events; Subaru engine speed more than 9/8 of target for 10.2 s.",[["h","Fluid level too low, old or burnt fluid"],["h","Converter clutch control valve worn or sticking, bore worn"],["m","Lock-up clutch in the converter worn or glazed"],["m","Solenoid mechanically sluggish (electrically fine)"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Higher engine speed when cruising, 'rubber band' feel","Higher consumption, rising fluid temperature"],["hard",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0742":["Torque Converter Clutch Circuit Stuck On","P0742 means the converter clutch stays applied although commanded off – there is almost no slip. GM's T43 flags −50 to +13 rpm for 1.5 s six times and the 6T70 −20 to +30 rpm for 2.5 s, both after one trip; Allison and Saab's Aisin AF40 after two trips, the AF40 also when the engine stalls without slip.",[["h","Converter clutch control valve sticking in the applied position"],["m","Solenoid stuck open (or short to ground with low-side control)"],["m","Lock-up clutch seized in the converter"],["m","Wrong fluid level or contaminated fluid"]],["Engine stalls when stopping or selecting D/R","Jerking or bucking at low speed","Check engine light or transmission warning"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P0743":["Torque Converter Clutch Circuit Electrical","The torque converter clutch (lock-up) connects pump and turbine mechanically; a solenoid – on/off, pulse-width modulated or proportional – moves a control valve in the valve body. Slip is engine speed minus turbine speed.",[["h","Transmission harness or pass-through connector (corrosion, chafing, oil in connector)"],["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Internal harness or mechatronic unit"],["l","Driver stage in the transmission or engine module"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","No lock-up: engine speed about 100–300 rpm higher when cruising","Limp mode on some makes (Saab/Aisin)"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0744":["Torque Converter Clutch Circuit Intermittent","The torque converter clutch (lock-up) connects pump and turbine mechanically; a solenoid – on/off, pulse-width modulated or proportional – moves a control valve in the valve body. Slip is engine speed minus turbine speed.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector or harness"],["m","Ford: control wire shorted to positive"],["m","Solenoid with temperature-dependent winding fault"],["l","Nissan CVT: converter or valve body mechanical"]],["Check engine light","Lock-up missing or jerky at times","Engine speed jumps when cruising"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0745":["Pressure Control Solenoid A","Pressure control solenoid A sets line or clutch pressure by current; most are 'normally high', so with no current – as in limp mode – maximum pressure applies. 'A' is usually the line pressure valve, on GM clutch-to-clutch transmissions (6T, 8L, 9T) the pressure valve of clutch 1.",[["h","Pressure control solenoid (coil) faulty"],["h","Transmission harness or connector"],["m","Contaminated fluid, valve sluggish"],["l","Transmission module"]],["Harsh shifts or engagement (maximum pressure)","Limp mode in a fixed gear","Transmission warning or check engine light"],["medium",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0746":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Performance/Stuck Off","Pressure control solenoid A sets line or clutch pressure by current; most are 'normally high', so with no current – as in limp mode – maximum pressure applies. 'A' is usually the line pressure valve, on GM clutch-to-clutch transmissions (6T, 8L, 9T) the pressure valve of clutch 1.",[["h","Pressure valve or spool worn or dirty"],["h","Fluid level too low, burnt fluid, clogged filter"],["m","Clutch plates or piston seals of the affected clutch worn"],["m","Line pressure too low (pump, main regulator)"]],["Slipping or engine flare on shifts","Gear not reached, limp mode in a fixed gear","Check engine light or transmission warning"],["hard",[400,4000]],"c"],"P0747":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Stuck On","Pressure control solenoid A sets line or clutch pressure by current; most are 'normally high', so with no current – as in limp mode – maximum pressure applies. 'A' is usually the line pressure valve, on GM clutch-to-clutch transmissions (6T, 8L, 9T) the pressure valve of clutch 1.",[["h","Pressure or clutch valve or spool sticking (dirt, debris)"],["m","Valve body worn"],["m","Software or false setting"],["m","Wrong or contaminated fluid"]],["Harsh, abrupt shifts","Transmission ties up, engine speed drops on shifts","Limp mode or default gear"],["hard",[400,3500]],"c"],"P0748":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Electrical","Pressure control solenoid A sets line or clutch pressure by current; most are 'normally high', so with no current – as in limp mode – maximum pressure applies. 'A' is usually the line pressure valve, on GM clutch-to-clutch transmissions (6T, 8L, 9T) the pressure valve of clutch 1.",[["h","Harness or transmission connector (corrosion, oil, chafing)"],["h","Pressure control solenoid faulty (resistance out of spec)"],["m","Internal harness, electronic module or mechatronic unit"],["l","System voltage too low"]],["Harsh shifts or engagement (maximum pressure)","Limp mode in a fixed gear","Transmission warning or check engine light"],["medium",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0749":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Intermittent","Pressure control solenoid A sets line or clutch pressure by current; most are 'normally high', so with no current – as in limp mode – maximum pressure applies. 'A' is usually the line pressure valve, on GM clutch-to-clutch transmissions (6T, 8L, 9T) the pressure valve of clutch 1.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed harness (intermittent short)"],["m","Valve with temperature-dependent winding fault"],["l","Voltage dips or poor ground"]],["Harsh shifts at times","Occasional limp mode, normal again after restart","Warning lamp (sometimes only stored)"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P074A":["Unable to Engage Gear 2","'Unable to engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – the opposite of 'stuck in gear', where a gear cannot be disengaged. Documented users are dual-clutch transmissions, whose shift forks are moved hydraulically and watched by travel sensors: GM's 8-speed in the 2020–23 Corvette, Maserati's MC20 and – for the odd gears only – Acura's RLX Sport Hybrid, whose 'odd gear monitor' is part of an emission recall with permanent codes.",[["h","Shift fork valve or solenoid in the mechatronic unit sticking (dirt, wear)"],["m","System pressure unstable or too low (pressure valve, pump, fluid level)"],["m","Shift fork, sleeve, synchronizer or dogs damaged"],["m","Learned shift fork or clutch values lost or wrong"]],["Gear 2 skipped or not engaged","Transmission warning or check engine light, possibly limp mode with one sub-gearbox locked out","Jerking, delayed pulling away or shifting"],["hard",[100,6000]],"c"],"P074B":["Unable to Engage Gear 3","'Unable to engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – the opposite of 'stuck in gear', where a gear cannot be disengaged. Documented users are dual-clutch transmissions, whose shift forks are moved hydraulically and watched by travel sensors: GM's 8-speed in the 2020–23 Corvette, Maserati's MC20 and – for the odd gears only – Acura's RLX Sport Hybrid, whose 'odd gear monitor' is part of an emission recall with permanent codes.",[["h","Shift fork valve or solenoid in the mechatronic unit sticking (dirt, wear)"],["m","System pressure unstable or too low (pressure valve, pump, fluid level)"],["m","Shift fork, sleeve, synchronizer or dogs damaged"],["m","Learned shift fork or clutch values lost or wrong"]],["Gear 3 skipped or not engaged","Transmission warning or check engine light, possibly limp mode with one sub-gearbox locked out","Jerking, delayed pulling away or shifting"],["hard",[100,6000]],"c"],"P074C":["Unable to Engage Gear 4","'Unable to engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – the opposite of 'stuck in gear', where a gear cannot be disengaged. Documented users are dual-clutch transmissions, whose shift forks are moved hydraulically and watched by travel sensors: GM's 8-speed in the 2020–23 Corvette, Maserati's MC20 and – for the odd gears only – Acura's RLX Sport Hybrid, whose 'odd gear monitor' is part of an emission recall with permanent codes.",[["h","Shift fork valve or solenoid in the mechatronic unit sticking (dirt, wear)"],["m","System pressure unstable or too low (pressure valve, pump, fluid level)"],["m","Shift fork, sleeve, synchronizer or dogs damaged"],["m","Learned shift fork or clutch values lost or wrong"]],["Gear 4 skipped or not engaged","Transmission warning or check engine light, possibly limp mode with one sub-gearbox locked out","Jerking, delayed pulling away or shifting"],["hard",[100,6000]],"c"],"P074D":["Unable to Engage Gear 5","'Unable to engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – the opposite of 'stuck in gear', where a gear cannot be disengaged. Documented users are dual-clutch transmissions, whose shift forks are moved hydraulically and watched by travel sensors: GM's 8-speed in the 2020–23 Corvette, Maserati's MC20 and – for the odd gears only – Acura's RLX Sport Hybrid, whose 'odd gear monitor' is part of an emission recall with permanent codes.",[["h","Shift fork valve or solenoid in the mechatronic unit sticking (dirt, wear)"],["m","System pressure unstable or too low (pressure valve, pump, fluid level)"],["m","Shift fork, sleeve, synchronizer or dogs damaged"],["m","Learned shift fork or clutch values lost or wrong"]],["Gear 5 skipped or not engaged","Transmission warning or check engine light, possibly limp mode with one sub-gearbox locked out","Jerking, delayed pulling away or shifting"],["hard",[100,6000]],"c"],"P074E":["Unable to Engage Gear 6","'Unable to engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – the opposite of 'stuck in gear', where a gear cannot be disengaged. Documented users are dual-clutch transmissions, whose shift forks are moved hydraulically and watched by travel sensors: GM's 8-speed in the 2020–23 Corvette, Maserati's MC20 and – for the odd gears only – Acura's RLX Sport Hybrid, whose 'odd gear monitor' is part of an emission recall with permanent codes.",[["h","Shift fork valve or solenoid in the mechatronic unit sticking (dirt, wear)"],["m","System pressure unstable or too low (pressure valve, pump, fluid level)"],["m","Shift fork, sleeve, synchronizer or dogs damaged"],["m","Learned shift fork or clutch values lost or wrong"]],["Gear 6 skipped or not engaged","Transmission warning or check engine light, possibly limp mode with one sub-gearbox locked out","Jerking, delayed pulling away or shifting"],["hard",[100,6000]],"c"],"P074F":["Unable to Engage Gear 7","'Unable to engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – the opposite of 'stuck in gear', where a gear cannot be disengaged. Documented users are dual-clutch transmissions, whose shift forks are moved hydraulically and watched by travel sensors: GM's 8-speed in the 2020–23 Corvette, Maserati's MC20 and – for the odd gears only – Acura's RLX Sport Hybrid, whose 'odd gear monitor' is part of an emission recall with permanent codes.",[["h","Shift fork valve or solenoid in the mechatronic unit sticking (dirt, wear)"],["m","System pressure unstable or too low (pressure valve, pump, fluid level)"],["m","Shift fork, sleeve, synchronizer or dogs damaged"],["m","Learned shift fork or clutch values lost or wrong"]],["Gear 7 skipped or not engaged","Transmission warning or check engine light, possibly limp mode with one sub-gearbox locked out","Jerking, delayed pulling away or shifting"],["hard",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0750":["Shift Solenoid A","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, newer GM units have separate low/high codes, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's 9-5: A = valve S2).",[["h","Open or corroded wire or transmission pass-through connector"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Internal harness or electronic module in the transmission"],["l","Module driver faulty or valve supply missing"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no upshifts","Harsh shifts from maximum line pressure"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0751":["Shift Solenoid A Performance/Stuck Off","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; together the solenoid states select the gear, and the module checks the result by gear ratio, slip or a pressure switch – the pattern of wrong gears shows which solenoid hangs off or on. P0751 means solenoid A is commanded on but valve, clutch or gear do not follow.",[["h","Shift spool in the valve body sticking (dirt, debris)"],["m","Internal leak or clutch fault in the circuit"],["m","Solenoid mechanically sluggish or screen clogged"],["m","Fluid level or quality wrong"]],["Gears missing or skipped","Pulling away in the wrong gear, weak acceleration","Limp mode or gear lock-out"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0752":["Shift Solenoid A Stuck On","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; together the solenoid states select the gear, and the module checks the result by gear ratio, slip or a pressure switch – the pattern of wrong gears shows which solenoid hangs off or on. P0752 means solenoid A is commanded off but its clutch stays applied.",[["h","Shift spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Solenoid stuck open or leaking"],["m","Clutch not releasing (burnt or stuck)"],["m","Contaminated or wrong fluid"]],["Wrong, often too low gear, engine revs high","Tie-up or jolt on shifts","Limp mode (GM: reverse, 1st, 3rd only)"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0753":["Shift Solenoid A Electrical","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, newer GM units have separate low/high codes, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's 9-5: A = valve S2).",[["h","Open or corroded wire or transmission pass-through connector"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Internal harness or electronic module in the transmission"],["l","Module driver faulty or valve supply missing"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no upshifts","Harsh shifts from maximum line pressure"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0754":["Shift Solenoid A Intermittent","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, newer GM units have separate low/high codes, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's 9-5: A = valve S2).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed harness, dependent on temperature or vibration"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or jolt at times, then normal again","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0755":["Shift Solenoid B","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, newer GM units have separate low/high codes, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's 9-5: A = valve S2).",[["h","Open or corroded wire or transmission pass-through connector"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Internal harness or electronic module in the transmission"],["l","Module driver faulty or valve supply missing"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no upshifts","Harsh shifts from maximum line pressure"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0756":["Shift Solenoid B Performance/Stuck Off","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; together the solenoid states select the gear, and the module checks the result by gear ratio, slip or a pressure switch – the pattern of wrong gears shows which solenoid hangs off or on. P0756 means solenoid B is commanded on but its clutch or valve does not follow.",[["h","Shift spool in the valve body sticking (dirt, debris)"],["m","Internal leak or clutch fault in the circuit"],["m","Solenoid mechanically sluggish or screen clogged"],["m","Fluid level or quality wrong"]],["Gears missing or skipped","Pulling away in the wrong gear, weak acceleration","Limp mode or gear lock-out"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0757":["Shift Solenoid B Stuck On","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; together the solenoid states select the gear, and the module checks the result by gear ratio, slip or a pressure switch – the pattern of wrong gears shows which solenoid hangs off or on. P0757 means solenoid B is commanded off but its clutch stays applied.",[["h","Shift spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Solenoid stuck open or leaking"],["m","Clutch not releasing (burnt or stuck)"],["m","Contaminated or wrong fluid"]],["No upshift beyond second gear, high engine speed","Tie-up on shifts","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0758":["Shift Solenoid B Electrical","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, newer GM units have separate low/high codes, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's 9-5: A = valve S2).",[["h","Open or corroded wire or transmission pass-through connector"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Internal harness or electronic module in the transmission"],["l","Module driver faulty or valve supply missing"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no upshifts","Harsh shifts from maximum line pressure"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0759":["Shift Solenoid B Intermittent","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, newer GM units have separate low/high codes, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's 9-5: A = valve S2).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed harness, dependent on temperature or vibration"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or jolt at times, then normal again","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P075A":["Shift Solenoid G","Shift solenoid G only exists on transmissions with seven or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 and 8F35 use solenoids A–F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes, the Allison 1000 uses three; the only documented user of the G block is Hyundai/Genesis' 8-speed, which assigns P075A and P075D to its on/off solenoids.",[["h","On/off solenoid faulty"],["m","Electronic module or internal harness"],["m","Connector or wiring"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or shift problems","With shift-by-wire possibly a park lock fault"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P075B":["Shift Solenoid G Performance/Stuck Off","Shift solenoid G only exists on transmissions with seven or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 and 8F35 use solenoids A–F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes, the Allison 1000 uses three; the only documented user of the G block is Hyundai/Genesis' 8-speed, which assigns P075A and P075D to its on/off solenoids.",[["h","Shift spool in the valve body sticking (dirt, debris)"],["m","Internal leak or clutch fault in the circuit"],["m","Solenoid mechanically sluggish or screen clogged"],["m","Fluid level or quality wrong"]],["Gears missing or skipped","Pulling away in the wrong gear, weak acceleration","Limp mode or gear lock-out"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P075C":["Shift Solenoid G Stuck On","Shift solenoid G only exists on transmissions with seven or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 and 8F35 use solenoids A–F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes, the Allison 1000 uses three; the only documented user of the G block is Hyundai/Genesis' 8-speed, which assigns P075A and P075D to its on/off solenoids.",[["h","Shift spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Solenoid stuck open or leaking"],["m","Clutch not releasing (burnt or stuck)"],["m","Contaminated or wrong fluid"]],["Wrong gear, tie-up","Limp mode","Check engine light"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P075D":["Shift Solenoid G Electrical","Shift solenoid G only exists on transmissions with seven or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 and 8F35 use solenoids A–F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes, the Allison 1000 uses three; the only documented user of the G block is Hyundai/Genesis' 8-speed, which assigns P075A and P075D to its on/off solenoids.",[["h","Solenoid faulty"],["m","Electronic module or internal harness"],["m","Connector or wiring"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or shift fault","Possibly park lock or shift-by-wire fault"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P075E":["Shift Solenoid G Intermittent","Shift solenoid G only exists on transmissions with seven or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 and 8F35 use solenoids A–F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes, the Allison 1000 uses three; the only documented user of the G block is Hyundai/Genesis' 8-speed, which assigns P075A and P075D to its on/off solenoids.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed harness, dependent on temperature or vibration"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or jolt at times, then normal again","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P075F":["Transmission Fluid Level Too High","P075F is not a circuit fault but a condition: a working fluid level sensor reports a level above the target band – the counterpart of P070F. In the Allison patent the transmission module reports 'too high, drain a quart or more', measuring only with the vehicle at rest, warm fluid and low input speed.",[["h","Overfilled at fluid change or repair (wrong check temperature or procedure)"],["l","Level sensor drifting (reads too high)"],["l","Measured under poor conditions (slope, cold fluid)"]],["Transmission fluid level warning","Fluid escaping at the transmission vent","Foaming fluid, unsteady or harsh shifts"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0760":["Shift Solenoid C","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's Aisin 9-5: C = valve S4, D = valve S5).",[["h","Open or corroded wire, connector or internal harness"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["l","Solenoid supply (high-side switch) missing"],["l","Module driver faulty"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear (Ford: fifth with maximum pressure)","Second and sixth gear missing (Ford CB26)"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0761":["Shift Solenoid C Performance/Stuck Off","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; the module checks whether its clutch follows the command by gear ratio, shift time or a pressure switch. P0761 means solenoid C is commanded on but its clutch does not follow.",[["h","Clutch or brake of this circuit leaking or worn (piston seal, linings)"],["m","Shift spool or proportional valve sticking (dirt, debris)"],["m","Fluid level or quality"],["l","Pressure switch faulty (Allison, Aisin)"]],["Gears missing or skipped","Slip or engine flare on shifts","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0762":["Shift Solenoid C Stuck On","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; the module checks whether its clutch follows the command by gear ratio, shift time or a pressure switch. P0762 means solenoid C is commanded off but its clutch stays applied.",[["h","Shift spool or proportional valve sticking in the applied position"],["m","Clutch not releasing (stuck or burnt)"],["m","Solenoid stuck open or leaking"],["l","Control wire shorted to ground (permanently powered)"]],["Tie-up or jolt on shifts","Delayed shifts, engine revs high","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0763":["Shift Solenoid C Electrical","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's Aisin 9-5: C = valve S4, D = valve S5).",[["h","Open or corroded wire, connector or internal harness"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["l","Solenoid supply (high-side switch) missing"],["l","Module driver faulty"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear (Ford: fifth with maximum pressure)","Harsh shifts"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0764":["Shift Solenoid C Intermittent","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's Aisin 9-5: C = valve S4, D = valve S5).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed harness, dependent on temperature or vibration"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or jolt at times, then normal again","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0765":["Shift Solenoid D","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's Aisin 9-5: C = valve S4, D = valve S5).",[["h","Open or corroded wire, connector or internal harness"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["l","Solenoid supply (high-side switch) missing"],["l","Module driver faulty"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear (Ford: fifth with maximum pressure)","No reverse or engine braking in first, or missing 4th–6th gears (Ford)"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0766":["Shift Solenoid D Performance/Stuck Off","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; the module checks whether its clutch follows the command by gear ratio, shift time or a pressure switch. P0766 means solenoid D is commanded on but its clutch does not follow.",[["h","Clutch or brake of this circuit leaking or worn (piston seal, linings)"],["m","Shift spool or proportional valve sticking (dirt, debris)"],["m","Fluid level or quality"],["l","Pressure switch faulty (Allison, Aisin)"]],["Upper gears (4th–6th) missing, or no reverse or engine braking in first (Ford)","Slip or engine flare on shifts","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0767":["Shift Solenoid D Stuck On","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body; the module checks whether its clutch follows the command by gear ratio, shift time or a pressure switch. P0767 means solenoid D is commanded off but its clutch stays applied.",[["h","Shift spool or proportional valve sticking in the applied position"],["m","Clutch not releasing (stuck or burnt)"],["m","Solenoid stuck open or leaking"],["l","Control wire shorted to ground (permanently powered)"]],["Tie-up or jolt on shifts","Delayed shifts, engine revs high","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0768":["Shift Solenoid D Electrical","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's Aisin 9-5: C = valve S4, D = valve S5).",[["h","Open or corroded wire, connector or internal harness"],["m","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["l","Solenoid supply (high-side switch) missing"],["l","Module driver faulty"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear (Ford: fifth with maximum pressure)","Harsh shifts"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0769":["Shift Solenoid D Intermittent","A shift solenoid is an electrically switched valve in the valve body. Manufacturers use these codes differently: Ford reports an open circuit with the base code and shorts separately, and the letter is not tied to a valve number (Saab's Aisin 9-5: C = valve S4, D = valve S5).",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed harness, dependent on temperature or vibration"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or jolt at times, then normal again","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P076A":["Shift Solenoid H","Shift solenoid H exists only on transmissions with eight or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 ends at F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes; the only documented user of the H block is the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire, where it concerns the on/off solenoid of the park lock rather than a shift valve.",[["h","Park lock on/off solenoid or electronic module faulty"],["m","Park position switch or actuator (shift-by-wire)"],["m","Connector or wiring"],["l","Transmission module"]],["Check engine light","Park lock or range selection fault, shift-by-wire warning","Limp mode"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P076B":["Shift Solenoid H Performance/Stuck Off","Shift solenoid H exists only on transmissions with eight or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 ends at F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes; the only documented user of the H block is the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire, where it concerns the on/off solenoid of the park lock rather than a shift valve.",[["h","Clutch or brake of this circuit leaking or worn (piston seal, linings)"],["m","Shift spool or proportional valve sticking (dirt, debris)"],["m","Fluid level or quality"],["l","Pressure switch faulty (Allison, Aisin)"]],["Gears missing or skipped","Slip or engine flare on shifts","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P076C":["Shift Solenoid H Stuck On","Shift solenoid H exists only on transmissions with eight or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 ends at F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes; the only documented user of the H block is the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire, where it concerns the on/off solenoid of the park lock rather than a shift valve.",[["h","Shift spool or proportional valve sticking in the applied position"],["m","Clutch not releasing (stuck or burnt)"],["m","Solenoid stuck open or leaking"],["l","Control wire shorted to ground (permanently powered)"]],["Tie-up or jolt on shifts","Delayed shifts, engine revs high","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P076D":["Shift Solenoid H Electrical","Shift solenoid H exists only on transmissions with eight or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 ends at F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes; the only documented user of the H block is the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire, where it concerns the on/off solenoid of the park lock rather than a shift valve.",[["h","Park lock on/off solenoid or electronic module"],["m","Connector or wiring"],["l","Transmission module"]],["Check engine light","Park lock or shift-by-wire warning","Limp mode"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P076E":["Shift Solenoid H Intermittent","Shift solenoid H exists only on transmissions with eight or more solenoids. Ford's 10R80 ends at F, GM's 9- and 10-speeds report clutch pressure valve codes; the only documented user of the H block is the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire, where it concerns the on/off solenoid of the park lock rather than a shift valve.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed harness, dependent on temperature or vibration"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or jolt at times, then normal again","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P076F":["Gear 7 Incorrect Ratio","P076F means the ratio in seventh gear is wrong: input speed divided by output speed does not match the commanded gear, so a clutch slips or another gear is engaged. It only exists on transmissions with seven or more gears.",[["h","Hydraulic leak at the seventh gear clutch (Ford 10R80: clutch cylinder sleeve)"],["m","Sticking valves in the valve body or solenoid"],["m","Fluid level or condition"],["m","Gear position sensor implausible or not learned (GM manual only)"]],["Slipping or flaring engine speed in seventh gear","Harsh or delayed shifts","Check engine light, transmission warning, possibly limp mode"],["hard",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0770":["Shift Solenoid E","What shift solenoid E does depends on the transmission. On Ford's 6R80 and 6F35/6F55 it is an on/off valve that moves a multiplex valve, so solenoid D feeds either the low/reverse brake or the 4-5-6 clutch; on the 6R140 and 5R110W it is a proportional clutch valve with separate short codes (P0985/P0986).",[["h","Open or corroded wire or transmission connector"],["m","Solenoid coil faulty"],["m","Transmission module or electronic module faulty"],["l","Solenoid supply missing"]],["Transmission warning or check engine light","Limp mode in a fixed gear","Harsh shifts"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0771":["Shift Solenoid E Performance/Stuck Off","What shift solenoid E does depends on the transmission. On Ford's 6R80 and 6F35/6F55 it is an on/off valve that moves a multiplex valve, so solenoid D feeds either the low/reverse brake or the 4-5-6 clutch; on the 6R140 and 5R110W it is a proportional clutch valve with separate short codes (P0985/P0986).",[["h","Valve body or multiplex valve sticking (debris, dirt)"],["h","Clutch, piston seal or clutch cylinder leaking or worn"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid contaminated"],["m","Solenoid E mechanically stuck"]],["Slipping or flare on shifts","Missing gear (e.g. 4th–6th)","Limp mode"],["hard",[400,4500]],"c"],"P0772":["Shift Solenoid E Stuck On","What shift solenoid E does depends on the transmission. On Ford's 6R80 and 6F35/6F55 it is an on/off valve that moves a multiplex valve, so solenoid D feeds either the low/reverse brake or the 4-5-6 clutch; on the 6R140 and 5R110W it is a proportional clutch valve with separate short codes (P0985/P0986).",[["h","Multiplex or shift spool sticking"],["m","Clutch plates stuck or burnt, piston hanging"],["m","Solenoid E stuck open"],["m","Contaminated fluid"]],["Harsh shift jolt, transmission ties up","Limp mode in a fixed gear","Check engine light"],["hard",[400,4500]],"c"],"P0773":["Shift Solenoid E Electrical","What shift solenoid E does depends on the transmission. On Ford's 6R80 and 6F35/6F55 it is an on/off valve that moves a multiplex valve, so solenoid D feeds either the low/reverse brake or the 4-5-6 clutch; on the 6R140 and 5R110W it is a proportional clutch valve with separate short codes (P0985/P0986).",[["h","Open or corroded wire or transmission connector"],["m","Solenoid coil faulty"],["m","Transmission module or electronic module faulty"],["l","Solenoid supply missing"]],["Transmission warning or check engine light","Limp mode in a fixed gear","Harsh shifts"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P0774":["Shift Solenoid E Intermittent","What shift solenoid E does depends on the transmission. On Ford's 6R80 and 6F35/6F55 it is an on/off valve that moves a multiplex valve, so solenoid D feeds either the low/reverse brake or the 4-5-6 clutch; on the 6R140 and 5R110W it is a proportional clutch valve with separate short codes (P0985/P0986).",[["h","Loose contact or corroded connector"],["m","Chafed harness"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or harsh shifts at times","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P0775":["Pressure Control Solenoid B","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0775 is the general code for valve B.",[["h","Valve body or pressure valve B worn or dirty"],["m","Line pressure wrong (load signal, main regulator)"],["m","Fluid condition"],["l","Circuit or coil"]],["Harsh or slipping shifts","Limp mode","Check engine light"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P0776":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Performance/Stuck Off","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0776 means the valve is electrically fine but pressure stays too low.",[["h","Clutch, return spring or piston seal of the assigned clutch faulty"],["h","Valve body or pressure valve dirty or worn"],["m","CVT belt or pulley damaged (CVT)"],["m","Fluid level low, fluid worn, filter clogged"]],["Slipping or missing gears","CVT: hesitation, lack of power","Limp mode"],["hard",[400,5000]],"c"],"P0777":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Stuck On","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0777 means the valve is electrically fine but pressure stays applied – the off-going clutch does not release.",[["h","Spool or pressure valve sticking"],["m","Clutch stuck or piston hanging"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris after clutch damage)"],["l","Software or false setting"]],["Harsh shift jolt, transmission ties up","Limp mode in a fixed gear","Check engine light"],["hard",[400,5000]],"c"],"P0778":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Electrical","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0778 is the electrical code for valve B.",[["h","Harness or connector shorted or open"],["h","Pressure valve B coil faulty – often only replaceable with the valve body"],["m","Transmission module faulty"],["l","System voltage"]],["Harsh engagement jolt","Weak pulling away and acceleration","Limp mode (Saab: second and reverse only)"],["medium",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0779":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Intermittent","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. In J2012 P0779 is an intermittent fault of valve B.",[["h","Connector or wire open or loose contact"],["m","Short to positive in the harness"],["m","Valve coil faulty"]],["Harsh engagement at times","Weak acceleration, no lock-up","Check engine light"],["medium",[100,2000]],"c"],"P077A":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit Loss of Direction Signal","Some transmissions use an output shaft speed sensor that also reports direction of rotation: in a GM patent two offset Hall elements send one pulse per tooth whose width encodes forward or reverse. The direction is needed to detect rolling back on a hill (a Hyundai patent raises idle speed then), for hill hold, stop-start and the forward/reverse change.",[["h","Output speed sensor faulty (one Hall element or direction channel failed)"],["m","Contact resistance in connector or wiring distorts the pulse"],["m","Wrong replacement sensor without direction function"],["l","Reluctor wheel damaged or air gap too large"]],["Transmission warning or check engine light depending on manufacturer","Hill hold, auto hold or stop-start unavailable","Rolling back on a hill not caught"],["medium",[150,500]],"c"],"P077B":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit Direction Error","Some transmissions use an output shaft speed sensor that also reports direction of rotation: in a GM patent two offset Hall elements send one pulse per tooth whose width encodes forward or reverse. The direction is needed to detect rolling back on a hill (a Hyundai patent raises idle speed then), for hill hold, stop-start and the forward/reverse change.",[["h","Output speed sensor faulty (direction evaluation disturbed)"],["m","Wrong or incorrectly fitted sensor (orientation to the wheel)"],["m","Reluctor wheel damaged, metal debris on the sensor"],["l","Faulty reference (input or intermediate shaft sensor)"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Hill hold off","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[150,500]],"c"],"P077C":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit Low","GM's transmission modules from 2019 to 2025 (9- and 10-speeds) also read the active output speed sensor's line voltage as an analog value. P077C means this voltage is below 0.25 V – a short to ground of under about 0.5 Ω between signal and module ground – for more than 0.05 s with the system above 10 V; GM rates it after one trip.",[["h","Signal or supply wire shorted to ground (chafing at the case)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector, corrosion"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light at once (GM)","Limp mode or fixed gear","Speedometer or cruise control failed"],["medium",[120,450]],"u"],"P077D":["Output Shaft Speed Sensor Circuit High","GM's transmission modules from 2019 to 2025 (9- and 10-speeds) also read the active output speed sensor's line voltage as an analog value. P077D is the mirror of P077C: the voltage is above 4.75 V – a short to the module supply of under about 0.5 Ω – for more than 0.05 s, after one trip.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to sensor supply or battery voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["m","Ground fault at the sensor, corroded connector"],["l","Module input"]],["Check engine light at once (GM)","Limp mode","Speedometer or cruise control failed"],["medium",[120,450]],"u"],"P077E":["Transmission Fluid Temperature Measurement System Multiple Sensor Correlation","P077E is a plausibility check between several fluid temperature readings – for example sensors A and B, sump and valve body, or the module's internal temperature. If they differ by more than allowed after a settling time, such as a long stop, the code sets, without being able to blame a single sensor.",[["h","One temperature sensor drifting (aged NTC)"],["m","Contact resistance at a sensor connector or wire"],["l","Fluid level too low (sensor not wetted)"],["l","Module or software"]],["Transmission warning, possibly check engine light","Converter clutch applies late or not at all","Harsh cold shifts"],["medium",[100,600]],"u"],"P077F":["Reverse 2 Incorrect Ratio","P077F checks the ratio in a second reverse gear: input speed divided by output speed lies outside the tolerance band around the target ratio – a slipping clutch, a wrong gear or a sensor fault. Only transmissions with two reverse ratios need it, such as Mercedes' 7G-Tronic (−3.42 and −2.23, with the second reverse kept in limp mode) and its 5G-Tronic predecessor; GM's 9- and 10-speeds, Ford's 10R and ZF's 8HP have one.",[["h","Slipping clutch or brake in the second reverse power path"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid worn"],["m","Solenoid or pressure regulator sticking"],["l","Faulty input or output speed sensor"]],["Reverse slips or engages late","Limp mode","Transmission warning or check engine light"],["hard",[200,3500]],"u"],"P0780":["Shift Error","Shift codes are a functional check of the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded shift with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, is too short or abrupt, does not happen, flares or ties up. Ford does not use P0780–P0784 (its shift completion check feeds the solenoid codes), nor do GM's 2018–2025 tables.",[["h","Fluid level wrong, fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Valve body, shift or pressure valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutches or bands (mechanical damage)"],["m","Module software or shift adaption"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing shifts","Engine flare during shifts","Warning lamp"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P0781":["1-2 Shift","Shift codes are a functional check of the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded shift with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, is too short or abrupt, does not happen, flares or ties up. Ford does not use P0780–P0784 (its shift completion check feeds the solenoid codes), nor do GM's 2018–2025 tables.",[["h","Fluid level wrong, fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Valve body, shift or pressure valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutches or bands"],["m","Module software or shift adaption"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing 1-2 shift","Engine flare during the shift","Warning lamp"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P0782":["2-3 Shift","Shift codes are a functional check of the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded shift with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, is too short or abrupt, does not happen, flares or ties up. Ford does not use P0780–P0784 (its shift completion check feeds the solenoid codes), nor do GM's 2018–2025 tables.",[["h","Fluid level wrong, fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Valve body, shift or pressure valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutches or bands"],["m","Module software or shift adaption"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing 2-3 shift","Engine flare during the shift","Warning lamp"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P0783":["3-4 Shift","Shift codes are a functional check of the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded shift with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, is too short or abrupt, does not happen, flares or ties up. Ford does not use P0780–P0784 (its shift completion check feeds the solenoid codes), nor do GM's 2018–2025 tables.",[["h","Fluid level wrong, fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Valve body, shift or pressure valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutches or bands"],["m","Module software or shift adaption"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing 3-4 shift","Engine flare during the shift","Warning lamp"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P0784":["4-5 Shift","Shift codes are a functional check of the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded shift with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, is too short or abrupt, does not happen, flares or ties up. Ford does not use P0780–P0784 (its shift completion check feeds the solenoid codes), nor do GM's 2018–2025 tables.",[["h","Fluid level wrong, fluid worn or contaminated"],["h","Valve body, shift or pressure valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutches or bands"],["m","Module software or shift adaption"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing 4-5 shift","Engine flare during the shift","Warning lamp"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P0785":["Shift Timing Solenoid A","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). P0785 is the general circuit code for solenoid A.",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Connector or wire at the transmission (oil, corrosion)"],["m","Electronic module or transmission module driver"]],["Warning lamp","Limp mode","Harsh 3-2 downshift (GM) or rough shifts (Aisin)"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0786":["Shift Timing Solenoid A Range/Performance","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). P0786 means the circuit is intact but the solenoid does not reach its target current.",[["h","Valve worn or coil resistance drifting"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["l","Unstable system voltage"]],["Rough shifts","Warning lamp","Limp mode"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0787":["Shift Timing Solenoid A Low","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). P0787 means current or voltage in the circuit is too low – an open circuit or short to ground.",[["h","Wire open or shorted to ground"],["h","Solenoid coil open"],["l","Module driver stage"]],["Warning lamp","Limp mode (GM: fixed third gear)","Harsh shifts from maximum pressure"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0788":["Shift Timing Solenoid A High","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). P0788 means the circuit is shorted to positive or draws overcurrent.",[["h","Short to positive in the harness"],["m","Winding short in the solenoid"],["l","Module driver stage"]],["Warning lamp","Limp mode","Shift jolts"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0789":["Shift Timing Solenoid A Intermittent","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). P0789 is an intermittent fault in the circuit of solenoid A.",[["h","Loose contact at connector or harness"],["m","Coil with thermal dropouts"]],["Shift jolts at times","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P078A":["Shift Timing Solenoid B","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). P078A is the general circuit code for solenoid B.",[["h","Solenoid faulty"],["m","Electronic module or transmission module"],["l","Connector or wire"]],["Warning lamp","Reverse cannot be engaged (Genesis shift-by-wire)","Limp mode"],["medium",[200,1000]],"c"],"P078B":["Shift Timing Solenoid B Range/Performance","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). Only Hyundai/Genesis is documented using solenoid B, and only with the base code P078A.",[["h","Valve or coil faulty"],["m","Connector"]],["Warning lamp","Shift problems"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P078C":["Shift Timing Solenoid B Low","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). Only Hyundai/Genesis is documented using solenoid B, and only with the base code P078A.",[["h","Wire open or shorted to ground"],["h","Coil open"]],["Warning lamp","Limp mode"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P078D":["Shift Timing Solenoid B High","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). Only Hyundai/Genesis is documented using solenoid B, and only with the base code P078A.",[["h","Short to positive"],["m","Winding short"]],["Warning lamp","Limp mode"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P078E":["Shift Timing Solenoid B Intermittent","'Shift timing solenoid' means different parts: on GM's 4L60-E the 3-2 downshift solenoid that times the band apply on that downshift, on Aisin five-speeds (GM, Saab) the linear valve SLS that sets clutch pressure during the shift, and on the 2017–18 Genesis G80/G90 with shift-by-wire the on/off solenoids that engage drive (A) and reverse (B). Only Hyundai/Genesis is documented using solenoid B, and only with the base code P078A.",[["h","Loose contact"],["l","Coil with thermal dropouts"]],["Occasional warning lamp","Shift jolts at times"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P078F":["Transmission Mode Switch C Circuit","P078F is the circuit fault of a third transmission mode switch (C), after A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F): its state is invalid or implausible. GM's transmission modules of 2023 and 2025 list the number only as a blocking condition for another check, without a description of their own; GM reports its drive mode selector today under P05D0–P05D3 (8-step resistor ladder, two trips).",[["h","Switch or button faulty or sticking"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the centre console"],["l","Control module"]],["Drive mode (sport, winter, tow) cannot be selected","Mode indicator missing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[40,250]],"u"],"P0790":["Normal/Performance Switch Circuit","P0790 is the circuit fault of the normal/performance (sport or power program) selector of an automatic transmission – the older single code that preceded the mode switch codes P071A ff; the early J2012 list still called it 'circuit malfunction'. The switch is usually a button to ground or a resistor ladder; a fault is an invalid level, a permanently pressed button or an open circuit.",[["h","Button faulty or sticking"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted"],["m","Liquid in the console"],["l","Control module"]],["Sport or performance program unavailable or always on","Mode indicator missing or flashing"],["easy",[40,250]],"c"],"P0791":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit","The intermediate shaft speed sensor measures a shaft between input and output – a countershaft on Toyota's U760 six-speed, the intermediate shaft behind the overdrive set on Ford's 5R110W. Together with turbine and output speed it lets the module check each gear stage and control clutch slip.",[["h","Intermediate shaft speed sensor faulty"],["m","Connector or wire at the transmission (oil, corrosion, chafing)"],["m","Reluctor wheel damaged or debris on the sensor"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or fixed gear","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["medium",[150,600]],"c"],"P0792":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The intermediate shaft speed sensor measures a shaft between input and output – a countershaft on Toyota's U760 six-speed, the intermediate shaft behind the overdrive set on Ford's 5R110W. Together with turbine and output speed it lets the module check each gear stage and control clutch slip.",[["h","Sensor delivering faulty pulses (air gap, reluctor wheel)"],["m","Interference or loose contact"],["l","Mechanical slip interpreted as a sensor fault"],["l","Wrong sensor fitted"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Shift problems","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[150,600]],"c"],"P0793":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit No Signal","The intermediate shaft speed sensor measures a shaft between input and output – a countershaft on Toyota's U760 six-speed, the intermediate shaft behind the overdrive set on Ford's 5R110W. Together with turbine and output speed it lets the module check each gear stage and control clutch slip.",[["h","Sensor faulty (internal open circuit)"],["m","Wire open or connector loose"],["l","Reluctor wheel or air gap"],["l","Transmission module"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode","Harsh shifts"],["medium",[150,600]],"c"],"P0794":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The intermediate shaft speed sensor measures a shaft between input and output – a countershaft on Toyota's U760 six-speed, the intermediate shaft behind the overdrive set on Ford's 5R110W. Together with turbine and output speed it lets the module check each gear stage and control clutch slip.",[["h","Loose contact at the connector or broken wire"],["m","Sensor thermally unstable"],["l","Interference (ground, shielding)"]],["Occasional transmission warning","Harsh shift now and then","Limp mode, gone after restart"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0795":["Pressure Control Solenoid C","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0795 is the general code for valve C.",[["h","Valve body or pressure valve C"],["m","Fluid or line pressure"],["l","Circuit or coil"]],["Shift problems","Limp mode","Check engine light"],["hard",[200,3000]],"c"],"P0796":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Performance/Stuck Off","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0796 means valve C is electrically fine but pressure stays too low.",[["h","Clutch, return spring or piston seal of the assigned clutch faulty"],["h","Valve body or pressure valve dirty or worn"],["m","Fluid level low, fluid worn, filter clogged"],["m","Software false setting"]],["Slipping or missing upper gears","Limp mode","Check engine light"],["hard",[300,5000]],"c"],"P0797":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Stuck On","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0797 means valve C is electrically fine but pressure stays applied – the off-going clutch does not release.",[["h","Spool or pressure valve sticking"],["m","Clutch stuck or piston hanging"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris after clutch damage)"],["l","Software or false setting"]],["Harsh shift jolt, transmission ties up","Limp mode in a fixed gear","Check engine light"],["hard",[300,5000]],"c"],"P0798":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Electrical","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0798 is the electrical code for valve C.",[["h","Clutch valve SLC2 coil faulty"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted"],["l","Transmission module damaged (e.g. after a short)"]],["Limp mode (second gear and reverse only)","Harsh shifts","Transmission warning or check engine light"],["medium",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0799":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Intermittent","Pressure control solenoids are current-controlled proportional valves, and what B and C mean depends on the design: on GM's clutch-to-clutch automatics they are the pressure valves of clutches 2 and 3, on GM's CVT B is primary pulley pressure and C line pressure, on Jatco CVTs B is the secondary pressure valve, on Saab's AF40 B and C are clutch valves SLC1/SLC2. P0799 is an intermittent electrical fault of valve C.",[["h","Loose contact or corroded connector"],["m","Chafed harness"],["l","Coil with heat-dependent winding fault"]],["Limp mode or harsh shifts at times","Warning lamp comes and goes"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P079A":["Transmission Friction Element A Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch A plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch A or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P079B":["Transmission Friction Element B Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch B plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch B or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P079C":["Transmission Friction Element C Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch C plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch C or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P079D":["Transmission Friction Element D Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch D plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch D or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P079E":["Transmission Friction Element E Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch E plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch E or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P079F":["Transmission Friction Element F Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch F plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch F or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P07A0":["Transmission Friction Element G Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch G plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch G or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P07A1":["Transmission Friction Element H Slip Detected","The 'friction element slip' codes monitor a clutch that is commanded locked in a power-split hybrid transmission: the module calculates its slip from electric motor, engine and output speeds (target 0 rpm) and flags slip above a threshold for a minimum time, with sufficient line pressure and clutch torque; from 2016 GM also flags a calculated friction energy threshold at once. The letter matches the manufacturer's clutch number.",[["h","Clutch H plates worn or glazed"],["m","Too little line pressure: low or aged fluid, filter, auxiliary oil pump or blocked pressure orifice"],["m","Pressure or shift valve of clutch H or valve body sticking"],["l","Module software (false setting)"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Limp mode with reduced power or restricted drive mode","Slipping, engine speed rises without drive"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P07A2":["Transmission Friction Element A Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. P07A2 concerns clutch A.",[["h","Clutch A plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07A3":["Transmission Friction Element A Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. GM's hybrid transmissions use only the stuck-on codes: when a clutch should release, the module expects slip to build and flags it if slip stays near zero – the Two-Mode SUVs (2008–13) at 80 rpm or less for 4.5 s with more than 320–350 Nm on the clutch, after two trips; the Volt Gen 1 and ELR when clutch 1 shows no slip while released ('applied or welded'), three attempts, after one trip; Volt Gen 2, Malibu Hybrid and CT6 plug-in by comparing actual slip with the target release profile, after one trip.",[["h","Clutch A stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07A4":["Transmission Friction Element B Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. P07A4 concerns clutch B.",[["h","Clutch B plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07A5":["Transmission Friction Element B Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. GM's hybrid transmissions use only the stuck-on codes: when a clutch should release, the module expects slip to build and flags it if slip stays near zero – the Two-Mode at 50 rpm or less for 3.2 s with high clutch torque, after two trips; Volt Gen 1 and ELR when clutch 2 shows no slip while released, after one trip; Volt Gen 2 and CT6 by comparison with the release profile, after one trip; the 2016 Malibu Hybrid without MIL, from 2018 with.",[["h","Clutch B stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07A6":["Transmission Friction Element C Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code: GM's hybrids report clutch C only as slip (P079C) or stuck on (P07A7), Ford's DPS6 dual-clutch has only clutches A and B.",[["h","Clutch C plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07A7":["Transmission Friction Element C Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. GM's hybrid transmissions use only the stuck-on codes: when a clutch should release, the module expects slip to build and flags it if slip stays near zero – the Two-Mode at 80 rpm or less for 4.5 s with more than 140–200 Nm, after two trips; Volt Gen 1 and ELR when the target is above 360 rpm but actual slip stays below 100 rpm, after one trip.",[["h","Clutch C stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07A8":["Transmission Friction Element D Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code: GM's hybrids report clutch D only as slip (P079D) or stuck on (P07A9), Ford's DPS6 dual-clutch has only clutches A and B.",[["h","Clutch D plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07A9":["Transmission Friction Element D Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. GM's hybrid transmissions use only the stuck-on codes: when a clutch should release, the module expects slip to build and flags it if slip stays near zero – the Two-Mode SUVs (2008–13) in two cases: in mode 1 at very low slip with strong deceleration of slip and over 700 Nm for 3.2 s, in mode 2 at 80 rpm or less with over 180 Nm for 4.5 s, after two trips.",[["h","Clutch D stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07AA":["Transmission Friction Element E Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code: GM's hybrids report clutch E only as slip (P079E) or stuck on (P07AB), only the Cadillac CT6 plug-in has a clutch 5.",[["h","Clutch E plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07AB":["Transmission Friction Element E Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. GM's hybrid transmissions use only the stuck-on codes: when a clutch should release, the module expects slip to build and flags it if slip stays near zero – here only the Cadillac CT6 plug-in, whose transmission has a fifth clutch: it compares actual slip with the target release profile or detects missing slip while releasing, 2 s, after one trip.",[["h","Clutch E stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07AC":["Transmission Friction Element F Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code: the documented hybrids have at most five monitored clutches.",[["h","Clutch F plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07AD":["Transmission Friction Element F Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code; the documented hybrids have at most five monitored clutches.",[["h","Clutch F stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07AE":["Transmission Friction Element G Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code: the documented hybrids have at most five monitored clutches.",[["h","Clutch G plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07AF":["Transmission Friction Element G Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code; the documented hybrids have at most five monitored clutches.",[["h","Clutch G stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07B0":["Transmission Friction Element H Performance/Stuck Off","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code: the documented hybrids have at most five monitored clutches.",[["h","Clutch H plates worn or piston seal leaking"],["m","Too little apply pressure (fluid, pump, valve)"],["m","Clutch actuator or valve sticking (DCT: electric actuator)"],["l","Module software or lost adaption"]],["Check engine light or transmission warning","Gear missing or slipping, weak acceleration","Limp mode"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07B1":["Transmission Friction Element H Stuck On","Each friction element (clutch or brake) has three functional codes: slip while commanded locked (P079A ff), performance/stuck off – it does not apply or applies too weakly – and stuck on – it does not release. No manufacturer document available to us uses this code; the documented hybrids have at most five monitored clutches.",[["h","Clutch H stuck, welded or piston hanging"],["m","Valve or spool sticking in the applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid (debris)"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light or 'service hybrid system' message","Jolt or tie-up on mode or gear changes","Engine stalls or drags when stopping"],["hard",[200,4500]],"c"],"P07B2":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit/Open","With electronic gear selection the park position sensor/switch reports the park request or position. On GM's shift-by-wire selectors (2017 LaCrosse, XT4/XT5, Bolt EV, later SUVs and pickups) it is the P button, read by two redundant analog sensors A and B with separate 'released' and 'pressed' voltage zones.",[["h","Wire or connector to the selector open"],["m","Connector not latched"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light, 'service transmission' or 'unable to shift' message","Park cannot be selected by button, or the car will not shift out of P","P button illumination off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P07B3":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit Low","With electronic gear selection the park position sensor/switch reports the park request or position. On GM's shift-by-wire selectors (2017 LaCrosse, XT4/XT5, Bolt EV, later SUVs and pickups) it is the P button, read by two redundant analog sensors A and B with separate 'released' and 'pressed' voltage zones.",[["h","Signal shorted to ground or supply wire open"],["m","Selector knob not fully seated or connector not latched"],["m","Sensor in the P button faulty"],["l","Selector or control module"]],["Check engine light, 'service transmission' or 'unable to shift' message","Park cannot be selected by button, or the car will not shift out of P","P button illumination off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P07B4":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit High","With electronic gear selection the park position sensor/switch reports the park request or position. On GM's shift-by-wire selectors (2017 LaCrosse, XT4/XT5, Bolt EV, later SUVs and pickups) it is the P button, read by two redundant analog sensors A and B with separate 'released' and 'pressed' voltage zones.",[["h","Signal shorted to positive or ground open"],["m","Selector knob not fully seated or connector not latched"],["m","Sensor in the P button faulty"],["l","Selector or control module"]],["Check engine light, 'service transmission' or 'unable to shift' message","Park cannot be selected by button, or the car will not shift out of P","P button illumination off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P07B5":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit Performance/Low","P07B5 means something different at each manufacturer. At GM it covers the P button of the shift-by-wire selector: sensor A reads in the band between its 'released' and 'pressed' zones (53.2–73.7 % of 5 V, ICCM 45–58 %), after two trips.",[["h","P button sensor faulty or worn"],["m","Knob not seated, button sticking"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light, 'service transmission' or 'unable to shift' message","Park cannot be selected by button, or the car will not shift out of P","P button illumination off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P07B6":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit Performance High","With electronic gear selection the park position sensor/switch reports the park request or position. On GM's shift-by-wire selectors (2017 LaCrosse, XT4/XT5, Bolt EV, later SUVs and pickups) it is the P button, read by two redundant analog sensors A and B with separate 'released' and 'pressed' voltage zones.",[["h","P button sensor faulty or worn"],["m","Knob not seated, button sticking"],["m","Contact resistance in the connector"],["l","Module software"]],["Check engine light, 'service transmission' or 'unable to shift' message","Park cannot be selected by button, or the car will not shift out of P","P button illumination off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P07B7":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","With electronic gear selection the park position sensor/switch reports the park request or position. On GM's shift-by-wire selectors (2017 LaCrosse, XT4/XT5, Bolt EV, later SUVs and pickups) it is the P button, read by two redundant analog sensors A and B with separate 'released' and 'pressed' voltage zones.",[["h","Loose contact in the selector connector or harness"],["m","Sensor with intermittent fault"],["l","Off-centre pressing or a hand resting on the button"]],["Check engine light, 'service transmission' or 'unable to shift' message","Park cannot be selected by button, or the car will not shift out of P","P button illumination off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P07B8":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch B Circuit/Open","With electronic gear selection the park position sensor/switch reports the park request or position. On GM's shift-by-wire selectors (2017 LaCrosse, XT4/XT5, Bolt EV, later SUVs and pickups) it is the P button, read by two redundant analog sensors A and B with separate 'released' and 'pressed' voltage zones.",[["h","Wire or connector to the selector open"],["m","Connector not latched"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["Check engine light, 'service transmission' or 'unable to shift' message","Park cannot be selected by button, or the car will not shift out of P","P button illumination off"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P07B9":["Transmission Park Position Sensor/Switch B Circuit Low","With electronic gear selection the park position sensor/switch reports the park request or position. On GM's shift-by-wire selectors (2017 LaCrosse, XT4/XT5, Bolt EV, later SUVs and pickups) it is the P button, read by two redundant analog sensors A and B with separate 'released' and 'pressed' voltage zones.",[["h","Signal short