n circuit or broken wire inside the sensor"],["m","Corroded connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after one trip)","Limp mode, harsh shifts, no converter lock-up","Adaption frozen"],["medium",[120,600]],"u"],"P07C5":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit Low","These low/high codes are a purely electrical check of the sensor's signal voltage at the module and run even with the vehicle at rest – unlike the circuit, range, no-signal and intermittent codes, which evaluate speed while driving. Low means a short to ground, high a short to supply or, depending on the circuit, an open.",[["h","Signal or supply wire shorted to ground (chafing at the case)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after one trip)","Limp mode, harsh shifts, no converter lock-up","Adaption frozen"],["medium",[120,600]],"u"],"P07C6":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor A Circuit High","These low/high codes are a purely electrical check of the sensor's signal voltage at the module and run even with the vehicle at rest – unlike the circuit, range, no-signal and intermittent codes, which evaluate speed while driving. Low means a short to ground, high a short to supply or, depending on the circuit, an open.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to supply"],["m","Open circuit or broken wire inside the sensor"],["m","Corroded connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after one trip)","Limp mode, harsh shifts, no converter lock-up","Adaption frozen"],["medium",[120,600]],"u"],"P07C7":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor B Circuit Low","These low/high codes are a purely electrical check of the sensor's signal voltage at the module and run even with the vehicle at rest – unlike the circuit, range, no-signal and intermittent codes, which evaluate speed while driving. Low means a short to ground, high a short to supply or, depending on the circuit, an open.",[["h","Signal or supply wire shorted to ground (chafing at the case)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after one trip)","Limp mode, harsh shifts, no converter lock-up","Adaption frozen"],["medium",[120,600]],"u"],"P07C8":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor B Circuit High","These low/high codes are a purely electrical check of the sensor's signal voltage at the module and run even with the vehicle at rest – unlike the circuit, range, no-signal and intermittent codes, which evaluate speed while driving. Low means a short to ground, high a short to supply or, depending on the circuit, an open.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to supply"],["m","Open circuit or broken wire inside the sensor"],["m","Corroded connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after one trip)","Limp mode, harsh shifts, no converter lock-up","Adaption frozen"],["medium",[120,600]],"u"],"P07C9":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor C Circuit Low","These low/high codes are a purely electrical check of the sensor's signal voltage at the module and run even with the vehicle at rest – unlike the circuit, range, no-signal and intermittent codes, which evaluate speed while driving. Low means a short to ground, high a short to supply or, depending on the circuit, an open.",[["h","Signal or supply wire shorted to ground (chafing at the case)"],["m","Sensor shorted internally"],["m","Oil or moisture in the connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after one trip)","Limp mode, harsh shifts, no converter lock-up","Adaption frozen"],["medium",[120,600]],"u"],"P07CA":["Intermediate Shaft Speed Sensor C Circuit High","These low/high codes are a purely electrical check of the sensor's signal voltage at the module and run even with the vehicle at rest – unlike the circuit, range, no-signal and intermittent codes, which evaluate speed while driving. Low means a short to ground, high a short to supply or, depending on the circuit, an open.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to supply"],["m","Open circuit or broken wire inside the sensor"],["m","Corroded connector"],["l","Module input faulty"]],["Check engine light (GM after one trip)","Limp mode, harsh shifts, no converter lock-up","Adaption frozen"],["medium",[120,600]],"u"],"P07CB":["Transmission Fluid Thermostat Performance","Many automatics have a thermostat in the fluid cooler circuit or a coolant-to-oil heat exchanger: a BMW patent describes a wax thermostat that bypasses the cooler until about 76 °C, a Ford patent a heat exchanger that warms transmission fluid from the coolant after a cold start. P07CB means this thermostat does not work as expected: fluid warms up too slowly (stuck open) or overheats (stuck closed).",[["h","Transmission fluid thermostat stuck open or closed"],["m","Fluid temperature sensor drifting"],["m","Cooler lines or heat exchanger blocked or leaking"],["l","Very cold weather or short trips"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp","Converter clutch applies late, higher consumption","Fluid overheating warning"],["medium",[80,500]],"u"],"P07CC":["Transmission Mode Switch C Circuit Low","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Button stuck pressed (switching to ground)"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the button"],["l","Control module"]],["Mode C cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07CD":["Transmission Mode Switch C Circuit High","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Wire or connector to the button open"],["m","Button open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Mode C cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07CE":["Transmission Mode Switch D Circuit","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Switch or button faulty or sticking"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the console"],["l","Control module"]],["Mode D cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07CF":["Transmission Mode Switch D Circuit Low","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Button stuck pressed (switching to ground)"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the button"],["l","Control module"]],["Mode D cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07D0":["Transmission Mode Switch D Circuit High","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Wire or connector to the button open"],["m","Button open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Mode D cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07D1":["Transmission Mode Switch E Circuit","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Switch or button faulty or sticking"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the console"],["l","Control module"]],["Mode E cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07D2":["Transmission Mode Switch E Circuit Low","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Button stuck pressed (switching to ground)"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the button"],["l","Control module"]],["Mode E cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07D3":["Transmission Mode Switch E Circuit High","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter or tow/haul; the J2012 family runs from A (P071A–P071C) and B (P071D–P071F) to C (base code P078F), D and E. No manufacturer document available to us uses switches C to E; GM reads its analog mode switch under its own codes (low below 21–29 % of 5 V, high from 94 %, two trips) and newer drive mode selectors under P05D0–P05D3.",[["h","Wire or connector to the button open"],["m","Button open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Mode E cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or flashing","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[30,300]],"u"],"P07D4":["Transmission Mode Switch F Circuit","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter, eco or tow/haul; J2012 numbers them A to F without saying which button is which. Switch F is the sixth and last of the family after A (P071A–P071C), B (P071D–P071F), C (P078F, P07CC, P07CD), D (P07CE–P07D0) and E (P07D1–P07D3).",[["h","Switch or button faulty or sticking"],["m","Wire or connector open or shorted"],["m","Moisture or dirt in the switch"],["l","Control module input"]],["Mode F program cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or wrong","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[20,250]],"u"],"P07D5":["Transmission Mode Switch F Circuit Low","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter, eco or tow/haul; J2012 numbers them A to F without saying which button is which. Switch F is the sixth and last of the family after A (P071A–P071C), B (P071D–P071F), C (P078F, P07CC, P07CD), D (P07CE–P07D0) and E (P07D1–P07D3).",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground (chafed harness)"],["m","Button stuck pressed (switching to ground)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the button or connector"],["l","Control module input"]],["Mode F program cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or wrong","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[20,250]],"u"],"P07D6":["Transmission Mode Switch F Circuit High","Transmission mode switches select alternative shift programs such as sport, winter, eco or tow/haul; J2012 numbers them A to F without saying which button is which. Switch F is the sixth and last of the family after A (P071A–P071C), B (P071D–P071F), C (P078F, P07CC, P07CD), D (P07CE–P07D0) and E (P07D1–P07D3).",[["h","Wire or connector to the switch open"],["m","Switch open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to positive"]],["Mode F program cannot be selected or stays active","Mode indicator missing or wrong","Transmission stays on the normal program"],["easy",[20,250]],"u"],"P07D7":["Stuck in Gear 8","P07D7 means gear 8 cannot be disengaged: the module commands a shift out of 8th, but the transmission stays in it. It is the counterpart to P07D8, where gear 8 cannot be engaged.",[["h","Shift fork actuator or shift valve sticking (contamination)"],["m","System pressure low or unstable (mechatronic pressure valve)"],["m","Shift fork, synchronizer or gear damaged"],["l","Fluid or filter contaminated"]],["Transmission stays in gear 8 and will not downshift","Weak take-off after stopping","Transmission warning, limp mode"],["hard",[200,9000]],"u"],"P07D8":["Unable to Engage Gear 8","'Unable to Engage' means the transmission does not reach the commanded gear – here 8th. Documented users are eight-speed dual-clutch gearboxes with hydraulically moved, position-monitored shift forks.",[["h","Sticking solenoid or shift valve in the mechatronic unit"],["m","System pressure too low or unstable"],["m","Adaptives lost or wrong after software or parts work"],["m","Shift fork or synchronizer damaged"]],["Gear 8 skipped or never reached, higher rpm at cruise","Check engine and transmission warning lamp","Jerky shifts, possibly loss of one half of a dual-clutch gearbox"],["hard",[150,9000]],"u"],"P07D9":["Gear 8 Incorrect Ratio","A ratio code compares input or turbine speed divided by output speed with the ratio of the commanded gear; a slipping clutch or the wrong gear trips it. Ford's summaries describe the check (over 30 rpm off for more than 1.8 s, three times) for gears 1–6 only; its 10-speed bulletins show gears 7–10 as P076F, P07D9, P07F6 and P07F7.",[["h","Clutch slipping or hydraulic leak (Ford 10R: migrating sleeve in the CDF clutch cylinder)"],["h","Shift adaptives wrong or not learned"],["m","Sticking valve in the valve body"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid degraded"]],["Slipping or flaring engine speed in gear 8","Harsh or delayed shifts","Check engine or transmission warning lamp"],["hard",[150,4500]],"u"],"P07DA":["6-7 Shift","Shift codes check the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded change with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, does not happen, flares or binds up two clutches. They continue the series P0781 '1-2 Shift' to P0784 '4-5 Shift'.",[["m","Shift or clutch valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutch or hydraulic leak on the clutches involved"],["m","Shift adaptives wrong"],["l","Fluid level or condition"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing 6-7 shift","Engine speed flares during the shift","Transmission warning, possibly MIL"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07DB":["7-8 Shift","Shift codes check the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded change with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, does not happen, flares or binds up two clutches. They continue the series P0781 '1-2 Shift' to P0784 '4-5 Shift'.",[["m","Shift or clutch valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutch or hydraulic leak on the clutches involved"],["m","Shift adaptives wrong"],["l","Fluid level or condition"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing 7-8 shift","Engine speed flares during the shift","Transmission warning, possibly MIL"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07DC":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 1","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 1","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07DD":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 2","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 2","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07DE":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 3","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 3","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07DF":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 4","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 4","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07E0":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 5","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 5","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07E1":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 6","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 6","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07E2":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 7","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 7","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07E3":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 8","The series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA and P07FD for 9 and 10) covers a shift that leaves the starting gear but lands in a gear other than the commanded target – a skipped or overshot gear. A module can see this after the shift because the measured ratio matches the wrong gear.",[["h","Shift or pressure control valve sticking in the valve body"],["m","Clutch or brake pack worn or leaking"],["m","Fluid low or contaminated"],["m","Wrong adaptives or software"]],["Wrong or skipped gear when shifting out of gear 8","Harsh shift or engine speed jump","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P07E4":["Unable to Engage Park","With shift-by-wire or park-by-wire, P, R, N and D are requested electrically (buttons, rotary selector, automatic park on shutdown) and an actuator moves the selector shaft or the park pawl; the module compares the requested with the range actually reached. P07E4 means park is not reached or not held within the allowed time.",[["h","Manual park release cable misrouted or actuated"],["m","Range actuator misaligned or its coupling offset"],["m","Control module software"],["m","Park lock actuator or its ground faulty"]],["Park cannot be selected, 'Park not available' message","Electric parking brake applies automatically","Wrench lamp (Ford) or MIL (GM, Ram)"],["medium",[100,1800]],"u"],"P07E5":["Unable to Engage Drive","With shift-by-wire or park-by-wire, P, R, N and D are requested electrically (buttons, rotary selector, automatic park on shutdown) and an actuator moves the selector shaft or the park pawl; the module compares the requested with the range actually reached. P07E5 means drive is requested but not reached in time.",[["h","Range control actuator misaligned or faulty"],["m","Software or after-effect of an over-the-air update"],["m","Range sensor signal wrong"],["l","Forward clutch or line pressure missing"]],["No drive in D","'Service Transmission' message, MIL","Range falls back to N or P"],["medium",[100,1500]],"u"],"P07E6":["Stuck in Park","With shift-by-wire or park-by-wire, P, R, N and D are requested electrically (buttons, rotary selector, automatic park on shutdown) and an actuator moves the selector shaft or the park pawl; the module compares the requested with the range actually reached. P07E6 means park cannot be left: the pawl stays engaged or its position feedback keeps reporting P.",[["h","Park lock actuator failed internally"],["m","High resistance at the park motor ground"],["m","Control module software"],["l","Low system voltage"]],["Vehicle stays in P and cannot be driven away","P flashes on the selector, P in the cluster dark","Wrench lamp or 'Service vehicle soon'"],["hard",[200,3000]],"u"],"P07E7":["Stuck in Drive","With shift-by-wire or park-by-wire, P, R, N and D are requested electrically (buttons, rotary selector, automatic park on shutdown) and an actuator moves the selector shaft or the park pawl; the module compares the requested with the range actually reached. P07E7 means drive cannot be left: after a request for P, N or R the transmission stays in D.",[["m","Range actuator or selector shaft sticking"],["m","Range sensor wrongly reporting D"],["l","Selector sensor or software"]],["Range stays in D despite the selector request","P or N cannot be reached, warning message","Warning lamp (depends on manufacturer)"],["medium",[150,1800]],"u"],"P07E8":["Transmission Range Control A Circuit/Open","Despite its name, 'Transmission Range Control A' is not the range actuator motor – that has its own codes, P272A and P272B – but the sensor circuits of an electric shift-by-wire selector. The only documented user is Mitsubishi's 2022 service manual for a CVT with electric shift (very likely the 2022–23 Outlander): the lever carries eight position sensors whose on/off pattern encodes R, N, home, D and M.",[["h","Wire or connector between selector and shift module open"],["m","Position sensor in the selector faulty"],["l","Shift module input faulty"]],["Electric shift or check engine warning","Usually no noticeable restriction","Further sensor faults lead to P07EC/P07ED"],["medium",[150,900]],"u"],"P07E9":["Transmission Range Control A Circuit Performance","Despite its name, 'Transmission Range Control A' is not the range actuator motor – that has its own codes, P272A and P272B – but the sensor circuits of an electric shift-by-wire selector. The only documented user is Mitsubishi's 2022 service manual for a CVT with electric shift (very likely the 2022–23 Outlander): the lever carries eight position sensors whose on/off pattern encodes R, N, home, D and M.",[["h","Corroded or damp connector, contact resistance"],["m","Position sensor drifting"],["l","Shift module input"]],["Electric shift or check engine warning","Usually no noticeable restriction","Further sensor faults lead to P07EC/P07ED"],["medium",[150,900]],"u"],"P07EA":["Transmission Range Control A Circuit Low","Despite its name, 'Transmission Range Control A' is not the range actuator motor – that has its own codes, P272A and P272B – but the sensor circuits of an electric shift-by-wire selector. The only documented user is Mitsubishi's 2022 service manual for a CVT with electric shift (very likely the 2022–23 Outlander): the lever carries eight position sensors whose on/off pattern encodes R, N, home, D and M.",[["h","Selector sensor wire shorted to ground (chafed harness, connector)"],["m","Sensor supply in the selector open"],["m","Selector or module faulty"]],["Electric shift or check engine warning","Usually no noticeable restriction","Further sensor faults lead to P07EC/P07ED"],["medium",[150,900]],"u"],"P07EB":["Transmission Range Control A Circuit High","Despite its name, 'Transmission Range Control A' is not the range actuator motor – that has its own codes, P272A and P272B – but the sensor circuits of an electric shift-by-wire selector. The only documented user is Mitsubishi's 2022 service manual for a CVT with electric shift (very likely the 2022–23 Outlander): the lever carries eight position sensors whose on/off pattern encodes R, N, home, D and M.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or 12 V supply"],["m","Selector faulty internally"],["l","Shift module"]],["Electric shift or check engine warning","Usually no noticeable restriction","Further sensor faults lead to P07EC/P07ED"],["medium",[150,900]],"u"],"P07EC":["Transmission Range Multi-Function Select Circuit","'Transmission Range Multi-Function Select' is the multi-bit code of the shift-by-wire selector position: each position produces a fixed pattern of sensor states, and the module checks the pattern for logic errors. Mitsubishi's 2022 CVT with electric shift uses eight sensors (R: 1–2 on, N toward R: 1–5, home: all, N toward D: 4–8, D/M: 7–8).",[["h","One position sensor in the selector faulty"],["m","One sensor wire open or shorted"],["l","Dirt or sticking mechanism in the selector"]],["Yellow shift warning or message","Selected range taken over with a delay of a second or more","No MIL (Mitsubishi)"],["medium",[250,900]],"u"],"P07ED":["Transmission Range Multi-Function Select Circuit Stuck","'Transmission Range Multi-Function Select' is the multi-bit code of the shift-by-wire selector position: each position produces a fixed pattern of sensor states, and the module checks the pattern for logic errors. Mitsubishi's 2022 CVT with electric shift uses eight sensors (R: 1–2 on, N toward R: 1–5, home: all, N toward D: 4–8, D/M: 7–8).",[["h","Selector sensor board faulty"],["m","Selector connector corroded or loose (several pins)"],["l","Liquid spilled into the center console"]],["No range can be selected, vehicle in neutral","MIL and red or yellow shift warning","Vehicle cannot be driven (tow)"],["medium",[300,1000]],"u"],"P07EE":["Transmission Range Multi-Function Select Circuit Range/Performance","'Transmission Range Multi-Function Select' is the multi-bit code of the shift-by-wire selector position: each position produces a fixed pattern of sensor states, and the module checks the pattern for logic errors. Mitsubishi's 2022 CVT with electric shift uses eight sensors (R: 1–2 on, N toward R: 1–5, home: all, N toward D: 4–8, D/M: 7–8).",[["h","Electric shift module faulty (hardware or software)"],["m","Outdated module software"],["l","Selector or harness"]],["Vehicle goes to neutral, shifting blocked","MIL and shift warning","Generic scan tool shows P0914"],["hard",[150,1200]],"u"],"P07EF":["Transmission Range Multi-Function Select Circuit Low","'Transmission Range Multi-Function Select' is the multi-bit code of the shift-by-wire selector position: each position produces a fixed pattern of sensor states, and the module checks the pattern for logic errors. Mitsubishi's 2022 CVT with electric shift uses eight sensors (R: 1–2 on, N toward R: 1–5, home: all, N toward D: 4–8, D/M: 7–8).",[["h","Selector plug disconnected or its supply or ground open"],["m","Selector sensor board faulty"],["m","Electric shift module"]],["Vehicle in neutral, no range selectable","MIL and shift warning","Fault appears right after switching the ignition on"],["medium",[100,1000]],"u"],"P07F0":["Transmission Range Multi-Function Select Circuit High","'Transmission Range Multi-Function Select' is the multi-bit code of the shift-by-wire selector position: each position produces a fixed pattern of sensor states, and the module checks the pattern for logic errors. Mitsubishi's 2022 CVT with electric shift uses eight sensors (R: 1–2 on, N toward R: 1–5, home: all, N toward D: 4–8, D/M: 7–8).",[["m","Selector signal wires shorted to the supply"],["m","Selector faulty"],["l","Shift module"]],["Shift warning or MIL (depends on manufacturer)","Range selection blocked or neutral"],["medium",[150,1000]],"u"],"P07F1":["Input/Turbine Shaft Speed Sensor C Circuit","P07F1 is the general circuit code of a third input or turbine shaft speed sensor, after sensor A (P0715) and B (P2765); low and high for sensor C are P07C3 and P07C4. No production use could be found in GM tables from 2015 to 2025, Ford's summaries of 2012 and 2020–23, Mitsubishi's code index or manufacturer bulletins; such a sensor would make sense on transmissions with three input shafts or a hybrid input.",[["m","Sensor wire or connector open"],["m","Speed sensor faulty"],["l","Control module or software"]],["Check engine lamp (expected)","Harsh shifts, converter clutch stays open","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[100,500]],"u"],"P07F2":["Transmission Range Control Module Communication Circuit","The transmission range control module is the separate shift-by-wire control unit that reads the selector and drives the range actuator. P07F2 reports a fault in its communication.",[["h","Shift-by-wire module faulty or its software"],["m","Communication wire or connector"],["l","Weak battery during start-up"]],["Shift warning","Usually no restriction; neutral if the transmission is affected too","U codes in other modules possible"],["hard",[100,1200]],"u"],"P07F3":["Transmission Range Control Module Communication Circuit Low","The transmission range control module is the separate shift-by-wire control unit that reads the selector and drives the range actuator. P07F3 is the electrical low check of its communication line: the line is shorted to ground and stays at a low level.",[["m","Communication wire shorted to ground"],["l","Module output stage faulty"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Shift warning","Possible shift lock or neutral (depends on manufacturer)"],["hard",[100,800]],"u"],"P07F4":["Transmission Range Control Module Communication Circuit High","The transmission range control module is the separate shift-by-wire control unit that reads the selector and drives the range actuator. P07F4 is the electrical high check of its communication line: the line is shorted to the supply and stays at a high level.",[["m","Communication wire shorted to the supply"],["l","Module faulty"],["l","Connector fault"]],["Shift warning","Possible shift lock or neutral (depends on manufacturer)"],["hard",[100,800]],"u"],"P07F5":["Transmission Range Control Module System Voltage Low","The transmission range control module is the separate shift-by-wire control unit that reads the selector and drives the range actuator. P07F5 means its supply voltage is too low.",[["h","Weak battery or charging system"],["m","Fuse, supply or ground wire of the module with high resistance"],["l","High actuator motor current on a weak supply"]],["Shift warning","Range or park lock reacts late or not at all","Often together with battery voltage codes"],["easy",[0,300]],"u"],"P07F6":["Gear 9 Incorrect Ratio","A ratio code compares input or turbine speed divided by output speed with the ratio of the commanded gear; a slipping clutch or the wrong gear trips it. Ford's summaries describe the check (over 30 rpm off for more than 1.8 s, three times) for gears 1–6 only; its 10-speed bulletins show gears 7–10 as P076F, P07D9, P07F6 and P07F7.",[["h","Clutch slipping or hydraulic leak (Ford 10R: migrating sleeve in the CDF clutch cylinder)"],["h","Shift adaptives wrong or not learned"],["m","Sticking valve in the valve body"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid degraded"]],["Slipping or flaring engine speed in gear 9","Harsh or delayed shifts","Check engine or transmission warning lamp"],["hard",[150,4500]],"u"],"P07F7":["Gear 10 Incorrect Ratio","A ratio code compares input or turbine speed divided by output speed with the ratio of the commanded gear; a slipping clutch or the wrong gear trips it. Ford's summaries describe the check (over 30 rpm off for more than 1.8 s, three times) for gears 1–6 only; its 10-speed bulletins show gears 7–10 as P076F, P07D9, P07F6 and P07F7.",[["h","Clutch slipping or hydraulic leak (Ford 10R: migrating sleeve in the CDF clutch cylinder)"],["h","Shift adaptives wrong or not learned"],["m","Sticking valve in the valve body"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid degraded"]],["Slipping or flaring engine speed in gear 10","Harsh or delayed shifts","Check engine or transmission warning lamp"],["hard",[300,4500]],"u"],"P07F8":["Stuck in Gear 9","P07F8 means gear 9 cannot be disengaged: the transmission stays in 9th despite the command to leave it. No manufacturer document available to us uses it.",[["h","Shift fork actuator or shift valve sticking (contamination)"],["m","System pressure low or unstable (mechatronic pressure valve)"],["m","Shift fork, synchronizer or gear damaged"],["l","Fluid or filter contaminated"]],["Transmission stays in gear 9 and will not downshift","Weak take-off after stopping","Transmission warning, limp mode"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07F9":["Unable to Engage Gear 9","P07F9 means the transmission does not reach the commanded 9th gear – the counterpart to P07F8, where 9th cannot be left. No manufacturer document available to us uses it.",[["h","Sticking solenoid or shift valve in the mechatronic unit"],["m","System pressure too low or unstable"],["m","Adaptives lost or wrong after software or parts work"],["m","Shift fork or synchronizer damaged"]],["Gear 9 skipped or never reached, higher rpm at cruise","Check engine and transmission warning lamp","Jerky shifts, possibly loss of one half of a dual-clutch gearbox"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07FA":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 9","P07FA belongs to the series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA for 9, P07FD for 10): when leaving 9th gear, the transmission ends up in a gear other than the commanded target, or the shift out of 9th goes wrong. No manufacturer document available to us uses it.",[["m","Shift or clutch valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutch or hydraulic leak on the clutches involved"],["m","Shift adaptives wrong"],["l","Fluid level or condition"]],["Wrong gear or harsh downshift out of 9th","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07FB":["Stuck in Gear 10","P07FB means gear 10 cannot be disengaged: the transmission stays in 10th despite the command to leave it. No manufacturer document available to us uses it.",[["h","Shift fork actuator or shift valve sticking (contamination)"],["m","System pressure low or unstable (mechatronic pressure valve)"],["m","Shift fork, synchronizer or gear damaged"],["l","Fluid or filter contaminated"]],["Transmission stays in gear 10 and will not downshift","Weak take-off after stopping","Transmission warning, limp mode"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07FC":["Unable to Engage Gear 10","P07FC means the transmission does not reach the commanded 10th gear – the counterpart to P07FB, where 10th cannot be left. No manufacturer document available to us uses it.",[["h","Sticking solenoid or shift valve in the mechatronic unit"],["m","System pressure too low or unstable"],["m","Adaptives lost or wrong after software or parts work"],["m","Shift fork or synchronizer damaged"]],["Gear 10 skipped or never reached, higher rpm at cruise","Check engine and transmission warning lamp","Jerky shifts, possibly loss of one half of a dual-clutch gearbox"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07FD":["Incorrect Shift from Gear 10","P07FD closes the series 'Incorrect Shift from Gear N' (P07DC–P07E3 for gears 1–8, P07FA for 9): when leaving 10th gear, the transmission ends up in a gear other than the commanded target, or the shift out of 10th goes wrong. No manufacturer document available to us uses it.",[["m","Shift or clutch valve sticking"],["m","Worn clutch or hydraulic leak on the clutches involved"],["m","Shift adaptives wrong"],["l","Fluid level or condition"]],["Wrong gear or harsh downshift out of 10th","Transmission warning, possibly limp mode"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07FE":["Stuck in Gear Not Specified","P07FE is the catch-all of the 'Stuck in Gear' family: the transmission stays in a gear it was commanded to leave, but the module cannot or does not name the gear. The gear-specific codes are P072B–P073C for reverse and gears 1–7, P07D7 for 8th, P07F8 for 9th and P07FB for 10th.",[["m","Shift actuator or shift valve sticking"],["m","System pressure low or unstable"],["m","Mechanical damage in the gear train"],["l","Position or speed sensor signal wrong"]],["Transmission no longer shifts and stays in one gear","Limp mode, transmission warning"],["hard",[150,4000]],"u"],"P07FF":["Cold Start Transmission Performance","P07FF means the transmission does not work as expected during the cold-start phase, shortly after starting with cold, thick fluid – for example a gear preselection or a clutch or valve response outside the expected window. The only documented user is Mercedes-Benz: its 2024 bulletin for the eight-speed dual-clutch gearbox 8G-DCT in the compact models (A-Class, CLA, GLA, GLB) names P07FF together with P0921, with reverse not available, a possible limp-home mode and the MIL; after switching off and on everything works again, and the fix was new control unit software.",[["h","Transmission control module software misjudging the cold phase"],["m","Shift valve or hydraulics too slow with cold fluid"],["l","Wrong or aged fluid, low level"],["l","Fluid temperature sensor reading wrong"]],["Reverse not available (message in the cluster)","Check engine lamp on","Limp-home mode shortly after a cold start"],["medium",[80,300]],"u"],"P0800":["Transfer Case Control System (MIL Request)","P0800 works like P0700 for the transmission: the transfer case control module has detected a fault of its own that it rates as emissions-relevant and asks the engine control module to turn on the MIL. The engine module only stores P0800; the actual fault – often C, U or manufacturer codes – sits in the transfer case module and needs a scan tool that addresses it.",[["h","Fault in the transfer case system stored in its module (shift motor, sensor, clutch)"],["m","Wiring or connector at the transfer case (terminal tension, corrosion)"],["m","Transfer case module software"],["l","Transfer case module faulty"]],["Check engine lamp on","'Service 4WD' or similar warning","All-wheel drive or low range cannot be selected"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0801":["Reverse Inhibit Control Circuit/Open","On GM manual gearboxes (Tremec T56/TR6060 in Corvette, Camaro, CTS-V and ATS-V) the engine module drives a solenoid that blocks reverse while the car moves forward. P0801 means its control circuit is open.",[["h","Connector at the reverse inhibit solenoid loose or corroded"],["m","Solenoid coil open"],["m","Harness between engine module and gearbox damaged"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Reverse hard or impossible to engage","Reverse no longer blocked","Service note in the driver display, usually no MIL"],["medium",[30,350]],"c"],"P0802":["Transmission Control System MIL Request Circuit/Open","On older vehicles with a separate transmission module and no MIL message on the CAN bus, the transmission module signals 'MIL on' over its own wire to the engine module. P0802 checks this wire for an open circuit.",[["h","MIL request wire or connector between the modules open or corroded"],["m","Transmission module without power or ground (fuse, relay)"],["l","Module output or input faulty"],["l","Wrong or retrofitted transmission module without this line"]],["Check engine lamp on","Transmission faults no longer light the MIL","Usually no change in driving"],["medium",[30,400]],"c"],"P0803":["Upshift/Skip Shift Solenoid Control Circuit","GM's skip-shift (CAGS) on manual gearboxes such as the T56 and TR6060 uses an engine-module solenoid in the shifter housing that, at light load and low speed blocks the way from 1st into 2nd and 3rd so the driver shifts 1 to 4 – a measure for fuel economy ratings; the 1-4 lamp (P0804) shows it. P0803 means the solenoid control circuit is open.",[["h","Solenoid unplugged or replaced by a skip-shift eliminator with the wrong resistance"],["h","Connector at the gearbox loose or corroded"],["m","Solenoid coil open"],["m","Harness damaged"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips","1-4 lockout no longer works (1-2 always possible)","Otherwise no change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P0804":["Upshift/Skip Shift Lamp Control Circuit","With GM's skip-shift (CAGS) on manual gearboxes, a '1-4' lamp in the cluster tells the driver that the lockout is active and to shift from 1st directly into 4th. P0804 means the control circuit of this lamp is faulty – open, shorted, or the lamp does not follow its command.",[["h","Bulb or LED in the cluster faulty"],["m","Wire or connector between engine module and cluster open or shorted"],["m","Instrument cluster board faulty"],["l","Engine module output faulty"]],["1-4 lamp never lights or stays on","Possibly check engine lamp","Lockout works without the hint"],["easy",[10,300]],"c"],"P0805":["Clutch Position Sensor A Circuit","The J2012 family 'Clutch Position Sensor A' (P0805 circuit, P0806 range/performance, P0807 low, P0808 high, P0809 intermittent, P080A not learned) covers the position of a clutch – the pedal of a manual gearbox, the clutch actuator of an automated or dual-clutch gearbox, or a clutch inside an automatic. P0805 is the general circuit fault.",[["h","Clutch position sensor faulty (pedal or actuator)"],["m","Connector or harness open, corroded or shorted"],["m","Transmission module software (Nissan)"],["m","Control unit internal fault (Ford DPS6: clutch motor driver)"]],["Check engine lamp on","Idle stop disabled ('Idle Stop System Fault')","Jerky driving or harsh clutch engagement (dual clutch)"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0806":["Clutch Position Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The 'Clutch Position Sensor A' family (P0805 circuit to P0809, P080A not learned) means different parts by vehicle generation. GM used P0806–P080A until model year 2018 for the analog clutch pedal sensor of its manual cars, then the newer pedal sensor codes P08A8 ff; Saab's 2012 9-3 does the same.",[["h","Pedal sensor or Hall sensors of the clutch motor faulty"],["m","Pedal sensor misadjusted or not relearned after clutch work"],["m","Harness or connector with contact resistance"],["m","Clutch actuator motor faulty (DPS6)"]],["Check engine lamp on","Cruise control and start-stop not working","Engine starts only with the pedal fully pressed, or not at all"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P0807":["Clutch Position Sensor A Circuit Low","The 'Clutch Position Sensor A' family (P0805 circuit to P0809, P080A not learned) means different parts by vehicle generation. GM used P0806–P080A until model year 2018 for the analog clutch pedal sensor of its manual cars, then the newer pedal sensor codes P08A8 ff; Saab's 2012 9-3 does the same.",[["h","Signal wire open or shorted to ground"],["m","5 V reference of the sensor missing"],["m","Pedal sensor faulty internally"],["m","Connector corroded or pins loose"]],["Check engine lamp on","Cruise control not working","Start-stop disabled, possibly no engine start"],["easy",[50,300]],"c"],"P0808":["Clutch Position Sensor A Circuit High","The 'Clutch Position Sensor A' family (P0805 circuit to P0809, P080A not learned) means different parts by vehicle generation. GM used P0806–P080A until model year 2018 for the analog clutch pedal sensor of its manual cars, then the newer pedal sensor codes P08A8 ff; Saab's 2012 9-3 does the same.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Pedal sensor faulty"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"]],["Check engine lamp on","Cruise control not working","Start-stop disabled"],["easy",[50,300]],"c"],"P0809":["Clutch Position Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The 'Clutch Position Sensor A' family (P0805 circuit to P0809, P080A not learned) means different parts by vehicle generation. GM used P0806–P080A until model year 2018 for the analog clutch pedal sensor of its manual cars, then the newer pedal sensor codes P08A8 ff; Saab's 2012 9-3 does the same.",[["h","Clutch A actuator motor or its Hall sensors faulty"],["m","Loose contact at the actuator connector"],["l","Transmission module Hall evaluation faulty"],["l","Supply voltage dips"]],["Check engine lamp on","Jerky or judder take-off","Limp mode, odd gears missing"],["hard",[300,1200]],"c"],"P080A":["Clutch Position Not Learned","The 'Clutch Position Sensor A' family (P0805 circuit to P0809, P080A not learned) means different parts by vehicle generation. GM used P0806–P080A until model year 2018 for the analog clutch pedal sensor of its manual cars, then the newer pedal sensor codes P08A8 ff; Saab's 2012 9-3 does the same.",[["h","Relearn not done after sensor, clutch or module replacement"],["m","Clutch badly worn or misadjusted"],["m","Pedal sensor misadjusted or wrongly fitted"],["m","Engine module reprogrammed or replaced"]],["Check engine lamp on","Start-stop, cruise control or hill start assist disabled","Engine may start only with the pedal at the stop"],["easy",[50,250]],"c"],"P080B":["Upshift/Skip Shift Solenoid Control Circuit Range/Performance","GM's skip-shift (CAGS) on manual gearboxes such as the T56 and TR6060 uses an engine-module solenoid that, at light load, blocks the way from 1st into 2nd so the driver shifts 1 to 4; the 1-4 lamp shows it (P0804). The engine module drives the solenoid with a low-side driver; P0803 is the open circuit.",[["m","Solenoid coil with shorted turns or resistance out of range"],["m","Retrofitted skip-shift eliminator with the wrong value"],["m","Corroded connector at the gearbox (contact resistance)"],["l","Control module or software"]],["Check engine lamp (depends on manufacturer)","1-4 lockout works not at all or only sometimes","Otherwise no change in driving"],["medium",[30,250]],"c"],"P080C":["Upshift/Skip Shift Solenoid Control Circuit Low","GM's skip-shift (CAGS) on manual gearboxes such as the T56 and TR6060 uses an engine-module solenoid that, at light load, blocks the way from 1st into 2nd so the driver shifts 1 to 4; the 1-4 lamp shows it (P0804). The engine module drives the solenoid with a low-side driver; P0803 is the open circuit.",[["h","Control wire chafed through to ground at gearbox or frame"],["m","Solenoid coil shorted to its housing"],["m","Connector corroded or water ingress"],["m","Older GM modules: circuit open or solenoid unplugged"]],["Check engine lamp","1-4 lockout permanently active or not working","1-4 lamp possibly unrelated to the lockout"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P080D":["Upshift/Skip Shift Solenoid Control Circuit High","GM's skip-shift (CAGS) on manual gearboxes such as the T56 and TR6060 uses an engine-module solenoid that, at light load, blocks the way from 1st into 2nd so the driver shifts 1 to 4; the 1-4 lamp shows it (P0804). The engine module drives the solenoid with a low-side driver; P0803 is the open circuit.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage (damaged harness)"],["m","Solenoid coil with shorted turns (resistance near zero)"],["m","Substitute resistor ('eliminator') with too low a value"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips","1-4 lockout not working","Otherwise no change in driving"],["medium",[30,300]],"c"],"P0810":["Clutch Position Control Error","P0810 reports a control error of an electronically or hydraulically operated clutch: commanded and actual position differ too much or for too long. It is documented on Stellantis plug-in hybrids – Jeep Wrangler 4xe and Grand Cherokee 4xe with the 8P75PH transmission – where it concerns the K0 disconnect clutch between engine and electric motor.",[["h","Control unit software with too tight parameters"],["m","Clutch actuator or hydraulics (pressure, valve) faulty"],["m","Clutch position sensor inaccurate"],["l","Wrong or aged fluid, worn clutch"]],["Check engine lamp on","Transmission warning message","Loss of drive until the next key cycle"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0811":["Excessive Clutch A Slippage","P0811 means clutch A slips too much while it should be closed – on a dual-clutch gearbox clutch 1 with the odd gears. The module compares engine speed with the input shaft speed of the closed clutch; thresholds are not published.",[["h","Transmission module calculating wrong slip (speed capture)"],["m","Clutch worn or overheated"],["m","Fluid burnt or wrong (wet clutch)"],["m","Clutch pressure or actuator too low"]],["Transmission warning, limp mode","Engine flares, poor acceleration","Judder on take-off"],["hard",[400,3500]],"c"],"P0812":["Reverse Input Circuit","P0812 concerns the input 'reverse engaged' at the engine or transmission module – the reverse or back-up lamp switch on a manual gearbox, or the R contact of a selector. Modules check it for open or stuck states and against a second source such as a gear position sensor or the direction from speed sensors.",[["h","Reverse or back-up lamp switch faulty or misadjusted"],["h","Connector loose or wire broken (e.g. after clutch work)"],["m","Back-up lamp circuit fuse blown"],["l","Gear position sensor reporting the wrong position"]],["Back-up lamps never or always on","Reversing camera or parking aid does not switch on","Usually no check engine lamp"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0813":["Reverse Output Circuit","P0813 concerns a signal 'reverse active' that a module puts out – usually the transmission module, on manual cars the engine or body module – for example to the back-up lamp relay, the reversing camera or the body module. Like other driver outputs it is checked by comparing commanded and actual state, which reveals an open circuit or a short.",[["h","Wire from module to relay or body module open or shorted"],["m","Back-up lamp relay or connected load faulty"],["m","Connector corroded"],["l","Module output stage faulty"]],["Back-up lamps not working","Reversing camera or parking beeper does not activate","Stored code without driving effects"],["medium",[40,300]],"c"],"P0814":["Transmission Range Display Circuit","P0814 concerns the drive or data circuit of the range display – the PRNDL or PRNDM display or lighting on the selector, sometimes in the instrument cluster. It is documented on the 2017–22 Jeep Compass with the nine-speed automatic: the electronic shift module stores P0814 with the MIL on, the selector lighting flickers or goes dark, and in a 2020 bulletin the lever could be moved out of park during cranking so that the engine stalled.",[["h","Shift module software (Jeep Compass)"],["m","Connector or wire to the selector display or cluster"],["m","Display LED or selector module faulty"],["l","Range sensor or transmission module delivering an invalid range"]],["Range display on the selector flickers or stays dark","Check engine lamp (Jeep)","Lever can leave P during cranking, engine stalls (Jeep)"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0815":["Upshift Switch Circuit","Tap-up and tap-down switches let the driver choose gears on an automatic (selector gate, buttons or paddles). GM, Ford and Saab all treat a shift request that appears where it is impossible – in park or neutral, at Ford also reverse – or that stays on implausibly long as a stuck switch.",[["h","Tap switch or paddle sticking or faulty"],["m","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["m","Selector assembly (Hall sensor, resistor network) faulty"],["l","Clockspring or steering wheel wiring (paddles)"]],["Manual up shifting not working","Transmission stays in the normal automatic mode","No check engine lamp"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0816":["Downshift Switch Circuit","Tap-up and tap-down switches let the driver choose gears on an automatic (selector gate, buttons or paddles). GM, Ford and Saab all treat a shift request that appears where it is impossible – in park or neutral, at Ford also reverse – or that stays on implausibly long as a stuck switch.",[["h","Downshift switch or paddle sticking or faulty"],["m","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["m","Selector assembly faulty"],["l","Body or transmission module"]],["Manual down shifting not working","Transmission stays in the normal automatic mode","No check engine lamp"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0817":["Starter Disable Circuit/Open","The starter disable line carries the signal 'park or neutral – start allowed' from the transmission module, or its built-in range switch, to the body or engine module, which only then releases the starter. On Saab's 2010 9-5 automatic the line is open in a drive range (0 V at the body module) and closed in P or N (12 V).",[["h","Starter disable wire to the body module open or shorted"],["m","Connector corroded or pins pushed back"],["m","Transmission module with built-in range switch faulty"],["l","Body module faulty"]],["Engine does not crank in P or N","Start possible in only one of the two ranges","No check engine lamp"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0818":["Driveline Disconnect Switch Input Circuit","P0818 concerns a switch input that reports whether the driveline is disconnected – on four-wheel-drive vehicles typically the neutral position of the transfer case or an axle disconnect, on hybrids a disconnect clutch between engine and transmission, as a Ford patent uses the term. It matters for OBD because in transfer case neutral the engine runs without load and wheel speed does not match the ratio, so misfire, ratio and vehicle speed monitors must know about it; CARB requires monitoring only then.",[["h","Neutral or disconnect switch at the transfer case or axle faulty"],["h","Underbody wiring or connector corroded or damaged"],["m","Transfer case actuator does not reach its end position"],["l","Control module or software"]],["All-wheel drive or neutral cannot be selected","'Service 4WD' or similar warning","Possibly check engine lamp (depends on manufacturer)"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0819":["Up and Down Shift Switch to Transmission Range Correlation","P0819 is the plausibility check between the tap-up and tap-down switches and the range sensor: a shift request may only appear in the manual gate or in drive. If a tap switch reports in P, R or N, or taps arrive without the manual gate being detected, the correlation is broken.",[["h","Tap switch sticking or faulty"],["m","Range sensor or selector sensor misadjusted or faulty"],["m","Selector cable misadjusted"],["m","Selector wiring or connectors"]],["Tap or manual mode not working","Range display partly wrong","No check engine lamp"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P081A":["Starter Disable Circuit Low","The starter disable line carries the signal 'park or neutral – start allowed' from the transmission module, or its built-in range switch, to the body or engine module, which only then releases the starter. On Saab's 2010 9-5 automatic the line is open in a drive range (0 V at the body module) and closed in P or N (12 V).",[["h","P/N line to the engine module shorted to ground"],["h","Line or connector open"],["m","Connector oxidized or pins pushed back"],["l","Transmission module output stage faulty"]],["Engine does not start or only sometimes","Starter does not crank in P or N","No check engine lamp"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P081B":["Starter Disable Circuit High","The starter disable line carries the signal 'park or neutral – start allowed' from the transmission module, or its built-in range switch, to the body or engine module, which only then releases the starter. On Saab's 2010 9-5 automatic the line is open in a drive range (0 V at the body module) and closed in P or N (12 V).",[["h","P/N line shorted to positive (chafed against a supply wire)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Transmission module output stage faulty"]],["Engine does not start or starts unreliably","Start release in the wrong range possible (safety risk)","No check engine lamp"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P081C":["Park Input Circuit","P081C concerns the input 'park position' – the P contact of the selector or shift module, or the park pawl feedback – which controls switching off the ignition, key release, starter release and the 'not in park' warning. It is documented on the 2017–22 Jeep Compass with the nine-speed automatic: a 2022 bulletin lists P081C with the MIL on, with 'Vehicle not in Park' shown although P is engaged; the car cannot be switched off, modules stay awake and the battery drains.",[["h","Shift module software (Jeep Compass)"],["m","P contact or selector switch faulty or misadjusted"],["m","Selector cable misadjusted"],["m","Wire or connector"]],["'Not in park' message although P is engaged","Ignition cannot be switched off, battery drains","Check engine lamp (Jeep)"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P081D":["Neutral Input Circuit","P081D concerns the input 'neutral' – the N contact of the selector or range switch, on manual cars a neutral switch or sensor for start-stop. Neutral serves for starter release, idle control without load, start-stop release and enabling other diagnostics.",[["h","Neutral contact, range switch or neutral sensor faulty or misadjusted"],["m","Selector or shift cable misadjusted"],["m","Wire or connector"],["l","Control module or software"]],["Engine does not start in N (only in P)","Start-stop not working","Range display does not show N"],["medium",[60,450]],"c"],"P081E":["Excessive Clutch B Slippage","P081E is the counterpart to P0811 for clutch B – on a dual-clutch gearbox clutch 2 with the even gears and reverse, which Audi calls 'clutch 2'. The module compares engine speed with the input shaft speed of the closed clutch and flags slip above its limit.",[["h","Transmission module speed capture faulty"],["m","Clutch 2 worn or overheated"],["m","Fluid or pressure"],["l","Input speed sensor B faulty"]],["Transmission warning, limp mode","Reverse or even gears not available","Engine flares on gear changes"],["hard",[400,3500]],"c"],"P081F":["Brake Transmission Shift Interlock (BTSI) Signal Correlation","The brake transmission shift interlock (BTSI) is a solenoid or actuator that locks the selector in park until the brake pedal is pressed, as US rule FMVSS 114 requires. 'Signal correlation' is the plausibility check between the interlock command or feedback and its input signals – brake switch and selector position: for example the lock released without a brake signal, or still active although the brake is pressed.",[["h","Brake lamp switch misadjusted or faulty"],["m","Interlock solenoid or locking mechanism sticking"],["m","Wire or connector to the selector"],["l","Selector module, control unit or software"]],["Selector cannot be moved out of P","Selector moves out of P without the brake","Warning about brake or range"],["medium",[50,500]],"u"],"P0820":["Gear Lever X-Y Position Sensor Circuit","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Wire or connector between selector and module open or shorted"],["h","Position sensor in the selector assembly faulty"],["m","Sensor supply (5 V reference) or ground faulty"],["l","Transmission, hybrid or selector module"]],["Range cannot be selected or only partly","Vehicle stays in P or N, or limp mode","Transmission warning, range display flashes or missing"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0821":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 1 Circuit","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","X position sensor in the selector assembly faulty"],["h","Signal wire or connector open or corroded"],["m","Liquid or dirt in the center console"],["l","Control module"]],["Transmission warning in the cluster","Ranges partly not selectable","With both sensors failed the vehicle stays in P or N"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0822":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 1 Circuit","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Y position sensor in the selector assembly faulty"],["h","Signal wire or connector open or corroded"],["m","Liquid or dirt in the center console"],["l","Control module"]],["Transmission warning in the cluster","Manual gate or reverse not selectable","With both sensors failed the vehicle stays in P or N"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0823":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 1 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Loose contact at the selector connector"],["m","Chafed or broken wire in the harness to the console"],["m","Sensor worn (potentiometer track) or Hall sensor disturbed"],["l","Interference or ground fault"]],["Transmission warning comes and goes","Selected range occasionally not accepted","Occasional limp-mode reaction"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0824":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 1 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Loose contact at the selector connector"],["m","Chafed or broken wire in the harness to the console"],["m","Sensor worn (potentiometer track) or Hall sensor disturbed"],["l","Interference or ground fault"]],["Occasional transmission warning","Gate change occasionally not recognized","Occasional limp mode"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0825":["Gear Lever Push-Pull Switch (Shift Anticipate)","P0825 concerns a switch on the selector that reports pushing or pulling the lever – sequential + and – shifting – or the start of a shift movement, hence 'shift anticipate'. Its purpose: the control unit of an automated manual gearbox can prepare clutch and engine torque before the actual gear change is detected; a LuK patent describes such shift-intent detection, which reports intent when the lever signal exceeds a filtered, load-dependent reference and cancels on a reversal.",[["h","Switch in the selector faulty or sticking"],["m","Wire or connector to the selector assembly"],["m","Liquid or dirt in the shift gate"],["l","Control module"]],["Delayed or jerky gear changes (automated manual)","Sequential tapping not working","Transmission warning in the cluster"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0826":["Up and Down Shift Switch Circuit","Many selectors read tap-up and tap-down over one shared wire with voltage steps or a resistor network; P0826–P0828 cover this shared circuit, P0815/P0816 the single directions. P0826 is the general fault of the shared circuit.",[["h","Wire or connector between selector and module open or shorted"],["h","Selector assembly (Hall switch, resistor network, module) faulty"],["m","Transmission module software (Ram)"],["l","Clockspring or steering wheel wiring (paddles)"]],["Tap or manual shifting not working","Transmission runs only in automatic mode","Transmission warning; lever locked in P/N (Saab 9-3)"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0827":["Up and Down Shift Switch Circuit Low","Many selectors read tap-up and tap-down over one shared wire with voltage steps or a resistor network; P0826–P0828 cover this shared circuit, P0815/P0816 the single directions. P0827 means the shared circuit voltage is too low – a short to ground, depending on the design an open on a pulled-up input, or a switch held closed.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Tap switch held closed or selector assembly faulty"],["m","Transmission software (Ram 2025)"],["l","Control module faulty"]],["Tap or manual shifting not working","MIL (Ram)","N shown although in P, engine cannot be switched off (Ram)"],["medium",[0,600]],"c"],"P0828":["Up and Down Shift Switch Circuit High","Many selectors read tap-up and tap-down over one shared wire with voltage steps or a resistor network; P0826–P0828 cover this shared circuit, P0815/P0816 the single directions. P0828 means the shared circuit voltage is too high – a short to positive, or on ground-switching switches with a pull-up also an open.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["m","Signal wire or selector ground open (pulled-up input)"],["m","Selector assembly faulty"],["l","Control module"]],["Tap or manual shifting not working","Transmission warning in the cluster","No effect on driving in automatic mode"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0829":["5-6 Shift","Shift codes check the gear change itself: the module compares the commanded change with the speed trace and flags a shift that takes too long, does not happen, flares or binds up two clutches. P0829 covers the change between 5th and 6th gear in either direction; J2012 placed it here because P0785 ff were already taken, between P0781 '1-2 Shift' to P0784 '4-5 Shift' and P07DA/P07DB for 6-7 and 7-8.",[["h","Fluid low or aged"],["m","Shift or clutch pressure valve for 5-6 sticking"],["m","Clutch or brake of 6th gear worn"],["m","Adaptives or software"]],["Harsh, delayed or missing 5-6 shift","Engine speed flares during the shift","Limp mode in a fixed gear, possibly MIL"],["hard",[150,3500]],"u"],"P082A":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 1 Circuit Range/Performance","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Selector assembly faulty (Hall sensor, magnet, worn mechanism)"],["m","Connector or wire with resistance or interference (frequency error)"],["m","Lever mechanically blocked (object in the console)"],["l","Engine or selector module software"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips (GM)","'Shift to park' message or transmission warning","Range selection restricted; with both sensors failed the vehicle goes to park"],["medium",[200,900]],"c"],"P082B":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 1 Circuit Low","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply open"],["m","Sensor in the selector assembly faulty"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips (GM)","'Shift to park' message or transmission warning","Range selection restricted; with both sensors failed the vehicle goes to park"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P082C":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 1 Circuit High","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["m","Signal or ground wire open (pulled-up input)"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips (GM)","'Shift to park' message or transmission warning","Range selection restricted; with both sensors failed the vehicle goes to park"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P082D":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 1 Circuit Range/Performance","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Selector assembly faulty (Hall sensor, magnet, worn mechanism)"],["m","Connector or wire with resistance or interference (frequency error)"],["m","Lever mechanically blocked (object in the console)"],["l","Engine or selector module software"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips (GM)","'Shift to park' message or transmission warning","Range selection restricted; with both sensors failed the vehicle goes to park"],["medium",[200,900]],"c"],"P082E":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 1 Circuit Low","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply open"],["m","Sensor in the selector assembly faulty"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips (GM)","'Shift to park' message or transmission warning","Range selection restricted; with both sensors failed the vehicle goes to park"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P082F":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 1 Circuit High","Gear lever X and Y position sensors detect a selector's position in two directions: on an H-gate shift and select direction (a LuK patent uses two sensors about 90° apart), on a shift-by-wire lever its two axes. P0820 covers the whole X-Y circuit, P0821–P0824 and P082A–P082F sensor 1, P089A–P08A3 a redundant sensor 2.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["m","Signal or ground wire open (pulled-up input)"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Control module"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips (GM)","'Shift to park' message or transmission warning","Range selection restricted; with both sensors failed the vehicle goes to park"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0830":["Clutch Pedal Switch A Circuit","Clutch pedal switches A and B are digital inputs of manual gearboxes: they release the starter only with the pedal pressed, switch off cruise control and tell start-stop whether the clutch is open. Which switch is A or B is up to each manufacturer – FCA calls the pedal-up switch A.",[["h","Switch faulty or misadjusted"],["m","Foot resting on the clutch pedal keeps the switch actuated"],["m","Software misjudgment (reprogramming)"],["l","Wiring, connector or remote-start bypass"]],["Cruise control switches off or cannot be activated","Start-stop disabled","Engine may not crank (starter interlock)"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0831":["Clutch Pedal Switch A Circuit Low","Clutch pedal switches A and B are digital inputs of manual gearboxes: they release the starter only with the pedal pressed, switch off cruise control and tell start-stop whether the clutch is open. Which switch is A or B is up to each manufacturer – FCA calls the pedal-up switch A.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Switch supply (fuse) open"],["m","Switch faulty internally"],["l","Connector corroded"]],["Cruise control switches off or cannot be activated","Start-stop disabled","Engine may not crank (starter interlock)"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0832":["Clutch Pedal Switch A Circuit High","Clutch pedal switches A and B are digital inputs of manual gearboxes: they release the starter only with the pedal pressed, switch off cruise control and tell start-stop whether the clutch is open. Which switch is A or B is up to each manufacturer – FCA calls the pedal-up switch A.",[["h","Signal shorted to positive"],["m","Switch ground open"],["m","Switch stuck closed"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Cruise control switches off or cannot be activated","Start-stop disabled"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0833":["Clutch Pedal Switch B Circuit","Clutch pedal switches A and B are digital inputs of manual gearboxes: they release the starter only with the pedal pressed, switch off cruise control and tell start-stop whether the clutch is open. Which switch is A or B is up to each manufacturer – FCA calls the pedal-up switch A.",[["h","Switch misadjusted (no longer actuated or released)"],["m","Switch faulty"],["m","Wire or connector open"],["l","Pedal stop or bumper worn"]],["Cruise control switches off or cannot be activated","Start-stop disabled","Engine may not crank (starter interlock)"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0834":["Clutch Pedal Switch B Circuit Low","Clutch pedal switches A and B are digital inputs of manual gearboxes: they release the starter only with the pedal pressed, switch off cruise control and tell start-stop whether the clutch is open. Which switch is A or B is up to each manufacturer – FCA calls the pedal-up switch A.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Switch supply open"],["m","Switch faulty"],["l","Connector corrosion"]],["Cruise control switches off or cannot be activated","Start-stop disabled","Engine may not crank (starter interlock)"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0835":["Clutch Pedal Switch B Circuit High","Clutch pedal switches A and B are digital inputs of manual gearboxes: they release the starter only with the pedal pressed, switch off cruise control and tell start-stop whether the clutch is open. Which switch is A or B is up to each manufacturer – FCA calls the pedal-up switch A.",[["h","Signal shorted to positive"],["m","Switch ground open"],["m","Switch stuck"],["l","Control module input faulty"]],["Cruise control switches off or cannot be activated","Start-stop disabled"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0836":["Four Wheel Drive (4WD) Switch Circuit","The four-wheel-drive switch tells the engine module which transfer case range is engaged. 4WD low changes the ratio of engine to road speed by about 2.7:1 on GM trucks, so GM uses 'not in 4WD low' as an enable condition for idle speed and clutch pedal checks; that is why CARB requires monitoring such inputs.",[["h","Position switch or sensor at the transfer case faulty"],["h","Wire or connector at the transfer case corroded or torn"],["m","Selector switch in the cabin faulty"],["l","Control module or software"]],["4WD indicator flashes or stays off","4WD range cannot be selected or display wrong","Possibly check engine lamp (depends on manufacturer)"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0837":["Four Wheel Drive (4WD) Switch Circuit Range/Performance","The four-wheel-drive switch tells the engine module which transfer case range is engaged. 4WD low changes the ratio of engine to road speed by about 2.7:1 on GM trucks, so GM uses 'not in 4WD low' as an enable condition for idle speed and clutch pedal checks; that is why CARB requires monitoring such inputs.",[["h","Shift linkage or transfer case not fully reaching the position"],["m","Position sensor out of tolerance (drifting resistance)"],["m","Contact resistance at the connector"],["l","Mechanical fault in the transfer case (range jumps out)"]],["4WD indicator flashes or stays off","4WD range cannot be selected or display wrong","Possibly check engine lamp (depends on manufacturer)"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0838":["Four Wheel Drive (4WD) Switch Circuit Low","The four-wheel-drive switch tells the engine module which transfer case range is engaged. 4WD low changes the ratio of engine to road speed by about 2.7:1 on GM trucks, so GM uses 'not in 4WD low' as an enable condition for idle speed and clutch pedal checks; that is why CARB requires monitoring such inputs.",[["h","Signal wire chafed through to ground"],["m","Switch or sensor shorted internally"],["m","Water or corrosion in the connector (leakage to ground)"],["l","Control module"]],["4WD indicator flashes or stays off","4WD range cannot be selected or display wrong","Possibly check engine lamp (depends on manufacturer)"],["medium",[40,350]],"c"],"P0839":["Four Wheel Drive (4WD) Switch Circuit High","The four-wheel-drive switch tells the engine module which transfer case range is engaged. 4WD low changes the ratio of engine to road speed by about 2.7:1 on GM trucks, so GM uses 'not in 4WD low' as an enable condition for idle speed and clutch pedal checks; that is why CARB requires monitoring such inputs.",[["h","Switch signal or ground wire open, connector unplugged"],["m","Signal wire shorted to positive"],["m","Switch or sensor open internally"],["l","Control module"]],["4WD indicator flashes or stays off","4WD range cannot be selected or display wrong","Possibly check engine lamp (depends on manufacturer)"],["medium",[40,350]],"c"],"P083A":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch G Circuit","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch G is one of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) that SAE J2012 provides for automatic gearboxes. No maker could be found that uses the letter G: GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Allison stop at A to E.",[["h","Pressure switch or sensor in the valve body faulty or stuck"],["m","Harness or connector at the gearbox: fluid wicking, corrosion"],["m","Sticking valve in the valve body"],["m","Fluid level or condition"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with substitute gear or limited ratio","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P083B":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch G Circuit Range/Performance","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch G is one of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) that SAE J2012 provides for automatic gearboxes. No maker could be found that uses the letter G: GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Allison stop at A to E.",[["h","Dirty or worn valve body, sticking valve"],["h","Pressure sensor or switch drifting"],["m","Mechanical damage: CVT belt or pulleys, pump, clutch"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid aged"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with substitute gear or limited ratio","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["hard",[150,2500]],"c"],"P083C":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch G Circuit Low","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch G is one of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) that SAE J2012 provides for automatic gearboxes. No maker could be found that uses the letter G: GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Allison stop at A to E.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground, or open on duty-cycle sensors"],["h","Sensor faulty or switch stuck closed"],["m","5 V supply missing"],["m","Valve stuck applied, so the switch reports pressure correctly"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with substitute gear or limited ratio","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P083D":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch G Circuit High","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch G is one of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) that SAE J2012 provides for automatic gearboxes. No maker could be found that uses the letter G: GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Allison stop at A to E.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector fault"],["h","Sensor faulty or switch stuck open"],["m","Short to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Too little pressure, switch does not close"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with substitute gear or limited ratio","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P083E":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch G Circuit Intermittent","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch G is one of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) that SAE J2012 provides for automatic gearboxes. No maker could be found that uses the letter G: GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Allison stop at A to E.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the gearbox connector"],["m","Chafed spot in the harness"],["m","Dirt at sensor or valve causing signal jumps"],["l","Sensor faulty internally"]],["Warning lamp coming and going","Occasional shift shocks","Brief limp mode"],["medium",[120,900]],"c"],"P083F":["Clutch Pedal Switch A/B Correlation","Manual cars often carry two clutch pedal switches: one near the top of the pedal travel for cruise control and start-stop, one near the floor that releases the starter. Pressing the pedal must switch them in a sensible order; P083F means their states contradict each other.",[["h","One clutch switch faulty or corroded"],["m","Switch misadjusted or not seated after clutch or pedal work"],["m","Open circuit or contact resistance in harness or connector"],["l","Pedal does not fully return (stop, hydraulics)"]],["Start-stop not working","Engine does not crank although the clutch is pressed","Cruise control cannot be set"],["easy",[30,200]],"c"],"P0840":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Pressure switch or sensor A faulty or stuck"],["m","Sticking shift valve or dirt in the valve body"],["m","Harness or connector at the gearbox: fluid wicking, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid contaminated"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with a fixed gear, harsh or missing shifts","Torque converter clutch stays open, higher fuel use"],["hard",[150,900]],"c"],"P0841":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit Range/Performance","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Dirty or sticking valve, worn valve body"],["h","Pressure sensor or switch drifting or sticking"],["m","Fluid level low, fluid aged or filter clogged"],["m","Mechanical damage: thrust washer, CVT belt or pulley, pump"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Shift shock, slipping or delayed engagement","CVT: ratio and power limited"],["hard",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0842":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit Low","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or, on GM duty-cycle sensors, open"],["h","Switch stuck closed or sensor faulty internally"],["m","5 V supply of the sensor missing"],["m","Shift valve stuck in the applied position, so the switch reports pressure correctly"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with a fixed gear, harsh or missing shifts","CVT: ratio and power limited"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P0843":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit High","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector soaked with fluid"],["h","Switch stuck open or sensor faulty"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Too little pressure at the switch: low fluid level or sticking valve"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with a fixed gear, harsh or missing shifts","Torque converter clutch stays open, higher fuel use"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P0844":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch A Circuit Intermittent","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the gearbox connector or control unit"],["m","Dirt in valve or sensor causing signal jumps"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Chafed spot in the harness"]],["Warning lamp coming and going","Occasional shift shocks","Brief limp mode"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0845":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch B Circuit","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Pressure switch or sensor B faulty or stuck"],["m","Sticking shift valve or dirt in the valve body"],["m","Harness or connector at the gearbox: fluid wicking, corrosion, chafing"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid contaminated"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with a fixed gear, harsh or missing shifts"],["hard",[150,900]],"c"],"P0846":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch B Circuit Range/Performance","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Dirty or sticking valve, worn valve body"],["h","Pressure sensor or switch drifting or sticking"],["m","Fluid level low, fluid aged or filter clogged"],["m","Mechanical damage: thrust washer, CVT belt or pulley, pump"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Slipping or shift shock","CVT: ratio and power limited"],["hard",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0847":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch B Circuit Low","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or, on GM duty-cycle sensors, open"],["h","Switch stuck closed or sensor faulty internally"],["m","5 V supply of the sensor missing"],["m","Shift valve stuck in the applied position, so the switch reports pressure correctly"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with a fixed gear, harsh or missing shifts","CVT: ratio and power limited"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P0848":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch B Circuit High","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector soaked with fluid"],["h","Switch stuck open or sensor faulty"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Too little pressure at the switch: low fluid level or sticking valve"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with a fixed gear, harsh or missing shifts"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P0849":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch B Circuit Intermittent","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch A and B are inputs inside the automatic gearbox. A switch only reports pressure present or absent, a sensor reports a value.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the gearbox connector or control unit"],["m","Dirt in valve or sensor causing signal jumps"],["m","Sensor faulty internally"],["l","Chafed spot in the harness"]],["Warning lamp coming and going","Occasional shift shocks","Brief limp mode"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P084A":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch H Circuit","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch H is the last of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) in SAE J2012; the letter is the maker's choice. Documented uses are rare: Mitsubishi sets P084A on its Jatco CVT8 (F1CJC/W1CJC) and Ford P084B on the 2020–23 Explorer 3.3 full hybrid.",[["h","Valve body with faulty primary or secondary pressure sensor"],["h","CVT belt or pulleys damaged, slip and debris in the fluid"],["m","Harness or connector"],["l","Control module software or module"]],["Warning lamp on","Judder or slipping when pulling away","CVT limp mode with limited ratio"],["hard",[800,5000]],"c"],"P084B":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch H Circuit Range/Performance","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch H is the last of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) in SAE J2012; the letter is the maker's choice. Documented uses are rare: Mitsubishi sets P084A on its Jatco CVT8 (F1CJC/W1CJC) and Ford P084B on the 2020–23 Explorer 3.3 full hybrid.",[["h","Control module software with too tight thresholds"],["h","Dirty or worn valve body, sticking valve"],["h","Pressure sensor or switch drifting"],["m","Fluid level low or fluid aged"]],["MIL or wrench lamp on","Shift quality changes until the adaptation has relearned","Limp mode with substitute gear or limited ratio"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P084C":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch H Circuit Low","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch H is the last of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) in SAE J2012; the letter is the maker's choice. Documented uses are rare: Mitsubishi sets P084A on its Jatco CVT8 (F1CJC/W1CJC) and Ford P084B on the 2020–23 Explorer 3.3 full hybrid.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground, or open on duty-cycle sensors"],["h","Sensor faulty or switch stuck closed"],["m","5 V supply missing"],["m","Valve stuck applied, so the switch reports pressure correctly"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with substitute gear or limited ratio","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P084D":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch H Circuit High","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch H is the last of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) in SAE J2012; the letter is the maker's choice. Documented uses are rare: Mitsubishi sets P084A on its Jatco CVT8 (F1CJC/W1CJC) and Ford P084B on the 2020–23 Explorer 3.3 full hybrid.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open, connector fault"],["h","Sensor faulty or switch stuck open"],["m","Short to 5 V or battery voltage"],["m","Too little pressure, switch does not close"]],["Check engine or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with substitute gear or limited ratio","Harsh or delayed shifts"],["hard",[120,900]],"c"],"P084E":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch H Circuit Intermittent","Transmission fluid pressure sensor/switch H is the last of up to eight pressure inputs (A to H) in SAE J2012; the letter is the maker's choice. Documented uses are rare: Mitsubishi sets P084A on its Jatco CVT8 (F1CJC/W1CJC) and Ford P084B on the 2020–23 Explorer 3.3 full hybrid.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at the gearbox connector"],["m","Chafed spot in the harness"],["m","Dirt at sensor or valve causing signal jumps"],["l","Sensor faulty internally"]],["Warning lamp coming and going","Occasional shift shocks","Brief limp mode"],["medium",[120,900]],"c"],"P084F":["Park/Neutral Switch Output Circuit","P084F concerns an output: a gearbox, hybrid or selector module passes the park/neutral status as a switched signal to other modules, for example for start release. The module watches its own driver for open circuits and shorts.",[["h","P/N output line or connector open or shorted"],["m","Module software misjudging the output"],["l","Output driver in the module faulty"],["l","Receiving module pulls the line wrongly"]],["Engine or ready mode does not start in P/N","12 V battery drains while parked","Warning about gear or park position"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0850":["Park/Neutral Switch Input Circuit","The park/neutral input tells the engine control module whether the automatic is in P or N, or a manual gearbox in neutral. It releases the starter, prepares idle control for the load of an engaged gear and enables cruise control and other monitors.",[["h","Range or inhibitor switch faulty or misadjusted"],["m","Selector cable misadjusted or stretched"],["m","Wiring or connector between switch, gearbox module and engine module"],["l","Module input or software"]],["Engine does not crank in P or N","Engine cranks in a drive range (safety risk)","Rough or wrong idle"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0851":["Park/Neutral Switch Input Circuit Low","The park/neutral input tells the engine control module whether the automatic is in P or N, or a manual gearbox in neutral. It releases the starter, prepares idle control for the load of an engaged gear and enables cruise control and other monitors.",[["h","Inhibitor or neutral switch faulty or misadjusted, stays closed"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Selector cable misadjusted"],["l","Gearbox module status wrong where it is the reference"]],["MIL on (GM diesel, Subaru after two trips)","Cruise control not working","Rough idle when stopping"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0852":["Park/Neutral Switch Input Circuit High","The park/neutral input tells the engine control module whether the automatic is in P or N, or a manual gearbox in neutral. It releases the starter, prepares idle control for the load of an engaged gear and enables cruise control and other monitors.",[["h","Signal or ground wire or connector open"],["h","P/N or neutral switch does not close"],["m","Selector cable or inhibitor misadjusted"],["l","Signal wire shorted to voltage"]],["Engine does not crank in P/N (automatic)","High or rough idle","Cruise control blocked"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0853":["Drive Switch Input Circuit","The drive switch input reports to the engine or gearbox module that range D is selected, usually through its own contact in the range switch or selector next to the park/neutral contact. No maker documents these codes; GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Saab service data were searched.",[["h","Range switch or D contact faulty or misadjusted"],["m","Wire or connector open or corroded"],["m","Selector cable misadjusted"],["l","Module input or software"]],["Fault stored, MIL depending on maker","Gear indicator wrong","Cruise control not working"],["medium",[80,450]],"c"],"P0854":["Drive Switch Input Circuit Low","The drive switch input reports to the engine or gearbox module that range D is selected, usually through its own contact in the range switch or selector next to the park/neutral contact. No maker documents these codes; GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Saab service data were searched.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Contact stuck closed"],["l","Module input"]],["Fault stored, MIL depending on maker","Gear indicator wrong","Cruise control not working"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0855":["Drive Switch Input Circuit High","The drive switch input reports to the engine or gearbox module that range D is selected, usually through its own contact in the range switch or selector next to the park/neutral contact. No maker documents these codes; GM's tables up to 2025, Ford's summaries and Saab service data were searched.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open"],["m","Contact worn or misadjusted, does not close"],["l","Short to voltage"]],["Fault stored, MIL depending on maker","Gear indicator wrong","Cruise control not working"],["medium",[60,400]],"c"],"P0856":["Traction Control Input Signal","The traction control input carries the request of the ABS/stability module to the engine module to cut torque when wheels spin. Older cars used a wire with a voltage or duty-cycle signal, hence the low and high variants; today it is a CAN message, where pure loss of communication sets U0121 or U0415 instead.",[["h","Fault in the engine module that prevents torque reduction, often with other engine codes"],["m","Software of engine or brake module, module faulty"],["m","CAN fault, connector or ground at the brake or engine module"],["l","Low supply voltage"]],["Traction or stability control lamp on, system switched off","No torque reduction when wheels spin","Usually no check engine lamp"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0857":["Traction Control Input Signal Range/Performance","The traction control input carries the request of the ABS/stability module to the engine module to cut torque when wheels spin. Older cars used a wire with a voltage or duty-cycle signal, hence the low and high variants; today it is a CAN message, where pure loss of communication sets U0121 or U0415 instead.",[["m","Software or calibration of brake or engine module"],["m","Loose contact or corrosion between brake module and engine module"],["m","Wheel speed fault in the ABS causing false slip detection"],["l","Brake module faulty"]],["Traction or stability control lamp on, system switched off","Traction control intervenes unexpectedly or not at all","Engine power briefly reduced"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0858":["Traction Control Input Signal Low","The traction control input carries the request of the ABS/stability module to the engine module to cut torque when wheels spin. Older cars used a wire with a voltage or duty-cycle signal, hence the low and high variants; today it is a CAN message, where pure loss of communication sets U0121 or U0415 instead.",[["h","Signal wire from brake module to engine module shorted to ground"],["m","Open circuit or connector fault"],["m","Brake module without power or ground, or faulty"],["l","Input of the engine module faulty"]],["Traction or stability control lamp on, system switched off","No torque reduction when wheels spin"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P0859":["Traction Control Input Signal High","The traction control input carries the request of the ABS/stability module to the engine module to cut torque when wheels spin. Older cars used a wire with a voltage or duty-cycle signal, hence the low and high variants; today it is a CAN message, where pure loss of communication sets U0121 or U0415 instead.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Open circuit with pull-up in the engine module"],["m","Ground fault of the brake module"],["l","Module faulty"]],["Traction or stability control lamp on, system switched off","No torque reduction when wheels spin"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P085A":["Gear Shift Control Module B Communication Circuit","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["h","Wire or connector between selector module and gearbox module: open, chafed, moisture in the console"],["m","Power or ground of the selector module"],["m","Software of selector or gearbox module"],["m","Selector module faulty"]],["Selector or gearbox warning in the instrument cluster","Range cannot be selected or only with delay, car stays in P/N","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P085B":["Gear Shift Control Module B Communication Circuit Low","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["h","Communication line shorted to ground"],["m","Power or ground of the selector module"],["m","Software of selector or gearbox module"],["m","Selector module faulty"]],["Selector or gearbox warning in the instrument cluster","Range cannot be selected or only with delay, car stays in P/N","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P085C":["Gear Shift Control Module B Communication Circuit High","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["h","Communication line shorted to voltage"],["m","Open line with pull-up in the receiver"],["m","Software of selector or gearbox module"],["m","Selector module faulty"]],["Selector or gearbox warning in the instrument cluster","Range cannot be selected or only with delay, car stays in P/N","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P085D":["Gear Shift Control Module A Performance","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["h","Selector module software (Audi update J587)"],["m","Selector module faulty: position sensor, P button"],["m","Supply voltage dips at the module"],["l","Connector or wiring at the lever"]],["Selector lever fault shown in the cluster","No range selectable, car stays in P","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P085E":["Gear Shift Control Module B Performance","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["m","Selector module software"],["m","Module B faulty"],["m","Supply voltage"],["l","Connector or wiring"]],["Selector or gearbox warning in the instrument cluster","Range cannot be selected or only with delay, car stays in P/N","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P085F":["Up and Down Shift Switch Circuit Range/Performance","Many tip switches for manual gear selection share one signal line, with a voltage step for up, down and rest (family P0826 to P0828). P085F means the signal is electrically valid but sits between the allowed windows, changes implausibly or does not fit the selector position.",[["h","Gearbox module software (Ram 2025 heavy duty)"],["m","Contact resistance or moisture in switch or connector"],["m","Tip switch or selector faulty"],["l","Wiring damage between switch and gearbox module"]],["Check engine lamp on","Limp mode with substitute gear","Wrong range shown (N instead of P)"],["medium",[50,600]],"u"],"P0860":["Gear Shift Control Module A Communication Circuit","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["h","Wire or connector between selector module and gearbox module: open, chafed, moisture in the console"],["m","Power or ground of the selector module"],["m","Software of selector or gearbox module"],["m","Selector module faulty"]],["Selector or gearbox warning in the instrument cluster","Range cannot be selected or only with delay, car stays in P/N","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0861":["Gear Shift Control Module A Communication Circuit Low","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["h","Communication line shorted to ground"],["m","Power or ground of the selector module"],["m","Software of selector or gearbox module"],["m","Selector module faulty"]],["Selector or gearbox warning in the instrument cluster","Range cannot be selected or only with delay, car stays in P/N","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0862":["Gear Shift Control Module A Communication Circuit High","Gear shift control modules A and B are selector modules of shift-by-wire systems, such as Audi's selector lever J587 or Stellantis' rotary shift module. The codes cover a dedicated link to the gearbox module and the module's plausibility.",[["h","Communication line shorted to voltage"],["m","Open line with pull-up in the receiver"],["m","Software of selector or gearbox module"],["m","Selector module faulty"]],["Selector or gearbox warning in the instrument cluster","Range cannot be selected or only with delay, car stays in P/N","Gearbox limp mode"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0863":["TCM Communication Circuit","The TCM communication circuit is a direct link between gearbox and engine module outside the CAN bus. Subaru's 2005 WRX, sold as Saab 9-2X, uses a single 'AT diagnosis' wire: about once a second the gearbox module sends a frame with a start pulse of 40 ms low and 20 ms high, carrying among other things its request for the MIL.",[["h","Wire or connector between engine and gearbox module open or corroded"],["m","Gearbox module without power or ground"],["m","Gearbox module faulty"],["l","Input of the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine lamp on","Gearbox warning or limp mode possible","Gearbox faults not passed to the engine module"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0864":["TCM Communication Circuit Range/Performance","The TCM communication circuit is a direct link between gearbox and engine module outside the CAN bus. Subaru's 2005 WRX, sold as Saab 9-2X, uses a single 'AT diagnosis' wire: about once a second the gearbox module sends a frame with a start pulse of 40 ms low and 20 ms high, carrying among other things its request for the MIL.",[["h","Gearbox module faulty or its software"],["m","Interference from retrofitted radio or phone with poor ground"],["m","Loose contact in wire or connector between the modules"],["l","Low supply voltage"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips","Shift problems or limp mode possible","Often no noticeable effect"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0865":["TCM Communication Circuit Low","The TCM communication circuit is a direct link between gearbox and engine module outside the CAN bus. Subaru's 2005 WRX, sold as Saab 9-2X, uses a single 'AT diagnosis' wire: about once a second the gearbox module sends a frame with a start pulse of 40 ms low and 20 ms high, carrying among other things its request for the MIL.",[["h","Line between the modules shorted to ground"],["m","Gearbox module faulty, holding the line low"],["m","Poor contact at engine or gearbox module connector"],["l","Pull-up in the engine module faulty"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips","Gearbox faults not passed to the engine module","Shift problems possible"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0866":["TCM Communication Circuit High","The TCM communication circuit is a direct link between gearbox and engine module outside the CAN bus. Subaru's 2005 WRX, sold as Saab 9-2X, uses a single 'AT diagnosis' wire: about once a second the gearbox module sends a frame with a start pulse of 40 ms low and 20 ms high, carrying among other things its request for the MIL.",[["h","Gearbox module without power or ground, or faulty"],["m","Line between the modules open"],["m","Line shorted to voltage"],["m","Poor contact at a module connector"]],["Check engine lamp after two trips","Gearbox in limp mode with fixed gear if its module has no power","Gearbox faults not passed to the engine module"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0867":["Transmission Fluid Pressure","P0867 to P0869 cover the system or line pressure of the gearbox as a whole: a pressure sensor measures it and the control module compares it with the pressure it commands. Faults of the sensor circuit itself have their own codes (Chrysler 68RFE P0932 ff), the single switches A to H have theirs (P0840 ff).",[["h","Fluid level extremely low or leak"],["m","Fluid pump or its drive faulty"],["m","Pressure regulator or relief valve sticking"],["l","System pressure sensor faulty"]],["Transmission warning lamp or MIL on","Weak or no drive","Limp mode, car may be stranded"],["hard",[150,6000]],"c"],"P0868":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Low","P0867 to P0869 cover the system or line pressure of the gearbox as a whole: a pressure sensor measures it and the control module compares it with the pressure it commands. Faults of the sensor circuit itself have their own codes (Chrysler 68RFE P0932 ff), the single switches A to H have theirs (P0840 ff).",[["h","Fluid level low, leak or clogged filter"],["h","Pressure control valve or solenoid sticking from dirt"],["m","Internal leakage or mechanical damage: thrust washer, seal rings, pump"],["l","Software calibration of the target pressure"]],["Transmission warning lamp or MIL on","Slipping, delayed engagement","Limp mode"],["hard",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0869":["Transmission Fluid Pressure High","P0867 to P0869 cover the system or line pressure of the gearbox as a whole: a pressure sensor measures it and the control module compares it with the pressure it commands. Faults of the sensor circuit itself have their own codes (Chrysler 68RFE P0932 ff), the single switches A to H have theirs (P0840 ff).",[["h","Pressure control valve or solenoid stuck from dirt"],["m","Software or calibration"],["m","Line pressure sensor reading high"],["l","Pressure control solenoid without power, gearbox falls back to maximum pressure"]],["Transmission warning lamp or MIL on","Harsh shifts and engagement shocks","Limp mode"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P086A":["Neutral Position Sensor A Circuit/Open","Neutral position sensor A is a position sensor, usually a three-wire Hall sensor, on the shift shaft of a manual gearbox. It tells the engine module that neutral is selected, which start-stop needs before it may switch the engine off.",[["h","Wire or connector to the sensor open or chafed"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["m","Corroded connector low on the gearbox"],["l","Supply output of the control module"]],["Start-stop not working","Warning about the start system","MIL possible depending on maker"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P086B":["Neutral Position Sensor A Range/Performance","Neutral position sensor A is a position sensor, usually a three-wire Hall sensor, on the shift shaft of a manual gearbox. It tells the engine module that neutral is selected, which start-stop needs before it may switch the engine off.",[["h","Sensor or magnet target loose, misaligned or covered with metal debris"],["m","Learned value missing or wrong after gearbox, clutch or sensor work"],["m","Sensor drifting"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector"]],["Start-stop not working or behaving oddly","Warning about start-stop or start system"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P086C":["Neutral Position Sensor A Circuit Low","Neutral position sensor A is a three-wire Hall sensor on the shift shaft of a manual gearbox that reports neutral to the engine module; start-stop may only switch the engine off in neutral. Audi uses it as sender G701 on the Q3 and A3, GM on the 2019 Corvette ZR1 manual, where its faults suspend the gear ratio checks.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground or supply wire open or chafed"],["m","Neutral position sensor faulty"],["m","Corrosion or water in the connector"],["l","5 V reference of the module disturbed"]],["Start-stop not working","Start system warning in the cluster (Audi)","No noticeable change in driving"],["medium",[60,350]],"u"],"P086D":["Neutral Position Sensor A Circuit High","Neutral position sensor A is a three-wire Hall sensor on the shift shaft of a manual gearbox that reports neutral to the engine module; start-stop may only switch the engine off in neutral. Audi uses it as sender G701 on the Q3 and A3, GM on the 2019 Corvette ZR1 manual, where its faults suspend the gear ratio checks.",[["h","Wire to the neutral sender broken or laid too short (Audi G701)"],["m","Connector loose or corroded"],["m","Sensor open internally"],["l","Ground wire open"]],["Start system fault message (Audi)","Start-stop not working","Fault appears after gear changes"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P086E":["Neutral Position Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Neutral position sensor A is a three-wire Hall sensor on the shift shaft of a manual gearbox that reports neutral to the engine module; start-stop may only switch the engine off in neutral. Audi uses it as sender G701 on the Q3 and A3, GM on the 2019 Corvette ZR1 manual, where its faults suspend the gear ratio checks.",[["h","Wire at the neutral sender strained or cracked (Audi G701)"],["m","Loose contact or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Sensor or magnet target loose"]],["Start-stop works only at times","Start system warning comes and goes"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P086F":["Neutral Position Sensor A Not Learned","Neutral position sensor A is a three-wire Hall sensor on the shift shaft of a manual gearbox that reports neutral to the engine module; start-stop may only switch the engine off in neutral. Audi uses it as sender G701 on the Q3 and A3, GM on the 2019 Corvette ZR1 manual, where its faults suspend the gear ratio checks.",[["h","Learn procedure not done after replacing sensor, gearbox or module"],["m","Learn run aborted: lever strained, linkage misadjusted"],["m","Software update deleted learned values"],["l","Sensor fitted outside the learnable range or faulty"]],["Start-stop not working","Cruise control cannot be set (FCA)","Fault appears after a workshop visit"],["easy",[30,150]],"u"],"P0870":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch C Circuit","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Pressure switch C faulty (sticking, seal leaking)"],["m","Sticking shift valve or worn valve body bore"],["m","Wiring or connector at the gearbox"],["l","Control module software (Ram 68RFE)"]],["MIL or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode with a fixed gear","Harsh or missing shifts"],["hard",[100,900]],"c"],"P0871":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch C Circuit Range/Performance","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Worn valve body bores or shift valve plugs"],["m","Mechanical damage: thrust washer, clutch seals"],["m","Pressure switch faulty (lip seal)"],["m","Dirt or sticking valve (dual clutch)"]],["MIL or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode, often in 2nd or 3rd gear","Harsh engagement, neutral feeling in 4th gear"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0872":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch C Circuit Low","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Pressure switch stuck closed or shorted internally"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground inside the gearbox"],["m","Clutch circuit does not exhaust: dirt, sticking valve"],["l","Gearbox module"]],["Code stored in the gearbox module; on GM without MIL, on Allison with MIL","Harsh shifts, possibly substitute gear"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P0873":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch C Circuit High","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Signal wire or connector open (gearbox pass-through, harness)"],["m","Pressure switch does not close (diaphragm, seal)"],["m","Clutch pressure does not build: low fluid, leak, regulator valve"],["l","Gearbox module input"]],["Code stored in the gearbox module; on GM without MIL, on Allison with MIL","Shift shock or slip in gears using clutch C"],["hard",[200,1500]],"c"],"P0874":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch C Circuit Intermittent","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at connector or harness"],["m","Dirt or sticking valve (dual clutch)"],["m","Sensor or switch with dropouts"],["l","Low fluid level, pressure drops in corners"]],["Warning lamp coming and going","Occasional harsh shift or limp mode"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0875":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch D Circuit","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Control valve body with pressure switch faulty (Nissan CVT)"],["m","Reverse pressure switch stuck closed (Allison)"],["m","Range sensor misadjusted"],["l","Wiring or connector"]],["MIL or transmission warning lamp on","Judder or reduced power (Nissan)","Reverse indication or shifting disturbed (Allison)"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P0876":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch D Circuit Range/Performance","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Valve body worn or dirty, valve sticking"],["m","Pressure switch faulty"],["m","Mechanical damage: thrust washer, clutch"],["l","Fluid level or condition"]],["MIL or transmission warning lamp on","Limp mode, harsh engagement"],["hard",[200,3500]],"c"],"P0877":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch D Circuit Low","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Pressure switch stuck closed or shorted internally"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground inside the gearbox"],["m","Clutch circuit does not exhaust: dirt, sticking valve"],["l","Range sensor wrong (Allison comparison)"]],["Code stored in the gearbox module; on GM without MIL, on Allison with MIL","Harsh shifts, possibly substitute gear"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P0878":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch D Circuit High","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Signal wire or connector open (gearbox pass-through, harness)"],["m","Pressure switch does not close (diaphragm, seal)"],["m","Clutch pressure does not build: low fluid, leak, regulator valve"],["l","Gearbox module input"]],["Code stored in the gearbox module; on GM without MIL, on Allison with MIL","Slip or shift shock in gears 1 to 4 (GM) or in reverse (Allison)"],["hard",[200,1500]],"c"],"P0879":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch D Circuit Intermittent","Transmission fluid pressure switches C and D report whether a clutch circuit is pressurised. They are more widespread than A and B: GM's 6L80/6L90 and 6T40/6T70 six-speeds put switch C on the 2-6 brake and D on the 1-2-3-4 clutch, Allison uses them for its third shift valve and its reverse switch, Ram's RFE gearboxes for the clutch pressure switches in the solenoid block.",[["h","Loose contact or corrosion at connector or harness"],["m","Dirt or sticking valve (dual clutch)"],["m","Sensor or switch with dropouts"]],["Warning lamp coming and going","Occasional harsh shift or limp mode"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P087A":["Clutch Position Sensor B Circuit","Clutch position sensor B reports the position of the second clutch or its actuator, the counterpart of sensor A (P0805 ff). The only documented user is Ford's dry DPS6 dual clutch in Fiesta and Focus: clutch B, for reverse and the even gears, is moved by a three-phase motor whose Hall sensors give its position, and the gearbox module drives it through an internal motor driver.",[["h","Gearbox module faulty internally (motor driver)"],["m","Water or corrosion at the gearbox module connector"],["m","Clutch B motor or its wiring faulty"],["l","Weak supply to the gearbox module"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Jerky take-off or shifts","Reverse or even gears missing, limp mode"],["hard",[400,1300]],"u"],"P087B":["Clutch Position Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Clutch position sensor B reports the position of the second clutch or its actuator, the counterpart of sensor A (P0805 ff). The only documented user is Ford's dry DPS6 dual clutch in Fiesta and Focus: clutch B, for reverse and the even gears, is moved by a three-phase motor whose Hall sensors give its position, and the gearbox module drives it through an internal motor driver.",[["h","Hall sensor in the clutch B motor faulty"],["m","Wiring or connector between motor and gearbox module"],["m","Input of the gearbox module faulty"],["l","Water ingress or corrosion"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Jerky take-off or shifts","Gears skipped, vibration"],["hard",[300,1300]],"u"],"P087C":["Clutch Position Sensor B Circuit Low","Clutch position sensor B reports the position of the second clutch or its actuator, the counterpart of sensor A (P0805 ff). The only documented user is Ford's dry DPS6 dual clutch in Fiesta and Focus: clutch B, for reverse and the even gears, is moved by a three-phase motor whose Hall sensors give its position, and the gearbox module drives it through an internal motor driver.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","5 V supply to the sensor open"],["m","Sensor faulty"],["l","Module reference disturbed"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Jerky take-off or shifts","Reverse or even gears missing, limp mode"],["hard",[200,1200]],"u"],"P087D":["Clutch Position Sensor B Circuit High","Clutch position sensor B reports the position of the second clutch or its actuator, the counterpart of sensor A (P0805 ff). The only documented user is Ford's dry DPS6 dual clutch in Fiesta and Focus: clutch B, for reverse and the even gears, is moved by a three-phase motor whose Hall sensors give its position, and the gearbox module drives it through an internal motor driver.",[["h","Signal or ground wire open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Sensor faulty"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Jerky take-off or shifts","Reverse or even gears missing, limp mode"],["hard",[200,1200]],"u"],"P087E":["Clutch Position Sensor B Circuit Intermittent","Clutch position sensor B reports the position of the second clutch or its actuator, the counterpart of sensor A (P0805 ff). The only documented user is Ford's dry DPS6 dual clutch in Fiesta and Focus: clutch B, for reverse and the even gears, is moved by a three-phase motor whose Hall sensors give its position, and the gearbox module drives it through an internal motor driver.",[["h","Hall sensor in the clutch B motor dropping out"],["m","Loose contact at motor or module connector"],["l","Motor sluggish or slipping mechanically"],["l","Evaluation in the gearbox module faulty"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Sporadic judder when pulling away or shifting","Delayed gear changes"],["hard",[300,1300]],"u"],"P0880":["TCM Power Input Signal","The TCM power input codes watch the supply voltage of the gearbox module or of its solenoid supply. Without a proper supply all solenoids go dead and the gearbox falls back to its hydraulic emergency gear with maximum line pressure: a fixed gear, harsh engagement, no shifting.",[["h","Harness or connector between module and valve body damaged or corroded"],["m","TCU fuse or transmission relay faulty"],["m","Weak battery, charging fault, poor ground"],["l","Gearbox module faulty"]],["MIL on","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no shifting","Harsh engagement into D or R"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0881":["TCM Power Input Signal Range/Performance","The TCM power input codes watch the supply voltage of the gearbox module or of its solenoid supply. Without a proper supply all solenoids go dead and the gearbox falls back to its hydraulic emergency gear with maximum line pressure: a fixed gear, harsh engagement, no shifting.",[["h","Contact resistance in supply or ground, voltage drop under load"],["m","Alternator or battery problem"],["m","Relay with worn contacts"],["l","Internal measurement of the module faulty"]],["MIL on","Occasional harsh shifts","Limp mode possible"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0882":["TCM Power Input Signal Low","The TCM power input codes watch the supply voltage of the gearbox module or of its solenoid supply. Without a proper supply all solenoids go dead and the gearbox falls back to its hydraulic emergency gear with maximum line pressure: a fixed gear, harsh engagement, no shifting.",[["h","Weak battery or alternator not charging"],["h","Transmission relay, fuse or fuse box module faulty (Chrysler)"],["m","Corroded ground or supply wire, connector at the module"],["l","Module faulty"]],["MIL on","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no shifting","Torque converter clutch stays open"],["medium",[30,800]],"c"],"P0883":["TCM Power Input Signal High","The TCM power input codes watch the supply voltage of the gearbox module or of its solenoid supply. Without a proper supply all solenoids go dead and the gearbox falls back to its hydraulic emergency gear with maximum line pressure: a fixed gear, harsh engagement, no shifting.",[["h","Alternator voltage regulator faulty (overcharging)"],["m","Jump start with 24 V or charger in boost mode"],["m","Poor battery ground, regulator measures wrongly"],["l","Module measurement faulty"]],["MIL on","Charging warning or unusually bright lights","Overvoltage codes in other modules"],["medium",[100,700]],"c"],"P0884":["TCM Power Input Signal Intermittent","The TCM power input codes watch the supply voltage of the gearbox module or of its solenoid supply. Without a proper supply all solenoids go dead and the gearbox falls back to its hydraulic emergency gear with maximum line pressure: a fixed gear, harsh engagement, no shifting.",[["h","Loose or unlatched connector at fuse box or module"],["m","Ignition switch or relay with contact dropouts"],["m","Battery terminal or ground loose"],["l","Internal module reset"]],["Gearbox jerks or briefly enters limp mode while driving","Engine may stall with a combined powertrain module","Warning lamps flicker"],["medium",[0,500]],"c"],"P0885":["TCM Power Relay Control Circuit/Open","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Relay faulty (coil open) or missing"],["h","Wire between relay and module open, connector or fuse box contact"],["m","Fuse of the relay coil blown"],["m","Module software (FCA 948TE reflash)"]],["MIL on","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no shifting","Harsh engagement into D or R"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0886":["TCM Power Relay Control Circuit Low","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Control wire chafed to ground"],["m","Relay coil shorted"],["l","Module driver faulty"]],["MIL on","Limp mode possible","Relay does not click or stays pulled in"],["medium",[20,400]],"c"],"P0887":["TCM Power Relay Control Circuit High","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Gearbox module software (FCA 948TE)"],["m","Control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Relay faulty"],["l","Gearbox module faulty"]],["MIL on","Shift problems or limp mode"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0888":["TCM Power Relay Sense Circuit","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Relay contacts burnt or corroded"],["m","Fuse in the load circuit"],["m","Wire between relay output and module"],["l","Module"]],["MIL on","Limp mode in a fixed gear, no shifting","Harsh engagement into D or R"],["easy",[20,400]],"c"],"P0889":["TCM Power Relay Sense Circuit Range/Performance","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Relay contact with high resistance"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in relay or socket"],["m","Ground or supply problem"]],["MIL or gearbox warning","Occasional limp mode"],["easy",[20,300]],"c"],"P088A":["Transmission Fluid Filter Deteriorated","Allison's 'Prognostics' in its fourth and fifth generation controls (1000 to 4000 series, buses and trucks, from 2009) has no fluid quality sensor: the gearbox module calculates. No car maker is known to use these codes.",[["h","Filter change interval reached"],["m","Heavy contamination from clutch wear"],["m","Filter changed without resetting the monitor"],["l","Faulty main valve body (Allison serial range)"]],["Wrench or TRANS SERVICE indicator flashing","Usually no change in driving behaviour"],["easy",[60,250]],"c"],"P088B":["Transmission Fluid Filter Very Deteriorated","Allison's 'Prognostics' in its fourth and fifth generation controls (1000 to 4000 series, buses and trucks, from 2009) has no fluid quality sensor: the gearbox module calculates. No car maker is known to use these codes.",[["h","Filter change overdue"],["m","Monitor not reset after the change"],["m","Increased internal wear debris"],["l","Faulty main valve body (Allison)"]],["CHECK TRANS lamp on","Message to service the transmission filter","Rarely a gear limit"],["easy",[60,300]],"c"],"P088C":["Reverse Inhibit Control Circuit Low","The reverse inhibit solenoid on GM's six-speed manual gearboxes (Corvette, Camaro, CTS-V, ATS-V) blocks reverse while driving forward and is released by the engine module. A low-side driver with built-in diagnosis watches the control line: above 9 V and 250 rpm, 20 faults in 25 samples of 250 ms (about 6 s) set the code.",[["h","Wire between engine module and gearbox chafed to ground (tunnel, near exhaust)"],["m","Solenoid coil shorted to ground"],["m","Water or corrosion in the gearbox connector"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Reverse cannot be engaged or is no longer blocked","Service message (GM from 2019), no MIL"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P088D":["Reverse Inhibit Control Circuit High","The reverse inhibit solenoid on GM's six-speed manual gearboxes (Corvette, Camaro, CTS-V, ATS-V) blocks reverse while driving forward and is released by the engine module. A low-side driver with built-in diagnosis watches the control line: above 9 V and 250 rpm, 20 faults in 25 samples of 250 ms (about 6 s) set the code.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a supply wire in the harness"],["m","Solenoid coil low-resistance or shorted (driver overcurrent)"],["m","Moisture or corrosion between supply and control pin"],["l","Engine module driver faulty"]],["Reverse cannot be engaged (lockout not released) or is not blocked","Service message (GM from 2019), no MIL"],["medium",[30,350]],"u"],"P088E":["Planetary Gear Set Failure","P088E means the module has found that a planetary gear set no longer keeps the fixed speed relation between its sun, carrier and ring gear, for example through broken planet gears, seized planet pins or stripped teeth. No maker's table or bulletin documents the code.",[["h","Planet gears or pins seized or broken (lubrication, overheating)"],["m","Tooth damage on sun or ring gear"],["m","Speed sensor (engine, motor-generator, output) reading wrong"],["l","Low or worn fluid"]],["Grinding or whining from the gearbox","Power loss, limp mode (hybrids drive electrically only)","Possibly no drive"],["hard",[1500,6000]],"u"],"P0890":["TCM Power Relay Sense Circuit Low","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Relay contacts frozen or corroded (Subaru ignition relay)"],["h","Outdated engine module software (Subaru)"],["m","Fuse or wire between relay and gearbox module"],["l","Weak battery"]],["Starter does not crank after a short trip in frost (Subaru)","MIL on, P0700 in the engine module","False oil level or other warnings"],["easy",[30,300]],"c"],"P0891":["TCM Power Relay Sense Circuit High","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Relay contact welded or sticking"],["m","Sense or output line shorted to voltage"],["l","Wrong relay fitted"]],["MIL on","Battery drains overnight","Module stays awake after key-off"],["easy",[20,250]],"c"],"P0892":["TCM Power Relay Sense Circuit Intermittent","Some gearboxes draw their power through a relay, such as the transmission control relay in Chrysler's fuse box that feeds the solenoids. P0885 to P0887 cover the coil control of that relay, P0888 to P0892 its sense line, which reads back the switched output and compares it with the command.",[["h","Loose contact in relay socket or connector"],["m","Relay with worn contacts"],["m","Wire broken by vibration"]],["Sporadic gearbox warning","Brief limp mode or shift shock"],["medium",[20,300]],"c"],"P0893":["Multiple Gears Engaged","P0893 means more shift elements are applied than the commanded gear allows, so the gearbox binds up. It is detected through speeds, when the ratio matches no valid gear and input speed collapses, or through commanded clutch capacities: a GM patent flags capacities above their limits for longer than roughly 200 to 500 ms and reacts by downshifting or falling back to the hydraulic limp mode.",[["h","Gearbox module software or calibration"],["m","Shift or pressure valve sticking (dirt), control valve body faulty"],["l","Clutch plates stuck or welded"],["l","Speed sensor false signal"]],["Gearbox or engine warning lamp","Sudden limp mode, harsh jolts or judder","Strong braking or locking feeling during a shift"],["hard",[150,4500]],"c"],"P0894":["Transmission Component Slipping","P0894 reports slip of a gearbox component, found by comparing speeds. GM's 4L60-E applies it to the torque converter clutch: with lock-up commanded, slip between 100 and 550 rpm for 12.75 s on the third attempt in 4th gear sets it, type B with MIL after two trips; the module first freezes adaptation and raises line pressure.",[["h","Low, wrong or worn fluid"],["h","Converter clutch PWM solenoid cracked or regulator bore worn (GM 4L60-E)"],["m","Worn clutch plates or converter clutch lining"],["m","Pump not priming, cracked suction filter (Allison)"]],["Engine speed rises without matching acceleration, mostly when warm","Shudder during lock-up","Harsh shifts from maximum pressure, limp mode possible"],["hard",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0895":["Shift Time Too Short","Shift time is the duration of the speed transition from the old to the new ratio, measured from input and output speed. Gearbox modules adapt fill time and pressure to keep it in a calibrated window; the code sets when adaptation is at its limit and the time is still outside.",[["h","Pressure control valve stuck towards high pressure or line pressure too high"],["m","Shift adaptation faulty or reset, wrong software"],["m","Valve body: sticking spool, faulty damper or accumulator"],["l","Wrong fluid or level"]],["Harsh, jerky shifts","Transmission warning lamp","Limp mode possible"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0896":["Shift Time Too Long","Shift time is the duration of the speed transition from the old to the new ratio, measured from input and output speed. Gearbox modules adapt fill time and pressure to keep it in a calibrated window; the code sets when adaptation is at its limit and the time is still outside.",[["h","Low or worn fluid, low pressure (pump, filter)"],["h","Worn clutch plates or band"],["m","Pressure or shift valve sticking, leaking piston seals"],["l","Lost or reset adaptation, software"]],["Delayed shifts, engine flares between gears","Transmission warning lamp","Limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0897":["Transmission Fluid Deteriorated","Allison's 'Prognostics' in its fourth and fifth generation controls (1000 to 4000 series, buses and trucks, from 2009) has no fluid quality sensor: the gearbox module calculates. No car maker is known to use these codes.",[["h","Fluid change interval exceeded"],["m","Fluid changed without resetting the oil life monitor"],["m","Heavy duty (heat, stop-and-go, retarder) shortens calculated life"],["l","Prognostics disabled afterwards (Allison special case)"]],["CHECK TRANS lamp on","TRANS SERVICE or wrench indicator after each start beforehand","Driving usually normal"],["medium",[100,400]],"c"],"P0898":["Transmission Control System MIL Request Circuit Low","The MIL request circuit is a dedicated wire over which the gearbox module asks the engine module to switch on the MIL. Today that request travels over CAN and shows as P0700 in the engine module; older Subarus, also sold as Saab 9-2X, used a single serial wire with its own codes P0864 to P0866.",[["h","MIL request wire shorted to ground"],["m","Engine or gearbox module connector corroded"],["l","Gearbox module output faulty"]],["MIL on","Gearbox faults may not be indicated correctly"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P0899":["Transmission Control System MIL Request Circuit High","The MIL request circuit is a dedicated wire over which the gearbox module asks the engine module to switch on the MIL. Today that request travels over CAN and shows as P0700 in the engine module; older Subarus, also sold as Saab 9-2X, used a single serial wire with its own codes P0864 to P0866.",[["h","Wire open (pull-up in the engine module)"],["m","Short to voltage"],["m","Gearbox module without power or ground"],["l","Gearbox module faulty"]],["MIL on","Gearbox faults may not be reported","Limp mode as well if the gearbox module has no power"],["medium",[50,400]],"c"],"P089A":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 2 Circuit","Gear lever position sensor 2 is the redundant second sensor of an electronic selector lever, next to sensor 1 (P0821 ff), for the X or Y axis. The only documented user is GM's electronic range selection with PWM Hall sensors, as in the Cadillac XT4, and only for range, low and high.",[["h","Wire or connector at the selector module open"],["m","Sensor 2 in the selector faulty"],["l","Spilled liquid in the centre console"]],["Gearbox or selector warning in the cluster","Range selection delayed or limited","Usually still drivable, sensor 1 takes over"],["medium",[250,900]],"u"],"P089B":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 2 Circuit Range/Performance","Gear lever position sensor 2 is the redundant second sensor of an electronic selector lever, next to sensor 1 (P0821 ff), for the X or Y axis. The only documented user is GM's electronic range selection with PWM Hall sensors, as in the Cadillac XT4, and only for range, low and high.",[["h","Sensor 2 in the selector assembly faulty or drifting"],["m","Selector mechanism worn or bent"],["m","Loose contact or interference on the PWM line"],["l","Liquid in the console"]],["MIL after two trips","Range selection delayed or limited","Only P available if both sensors fail"],["medium",[250,900]],"u"],"P089C":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 2 Circuit Low","Gear lever position sensor 2 is the redundant second sensor of an electronic selector lever, next to sensor 1 (P0821 ff), for the X or Y axis. The only documented user is GM's electronic range selection with PWM Hall sensors, as in the Cadillac XT4, and only for range, low and high.",[["h","Sensor 2 signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor 2 faulty"],["m","Supply of the selector sensor open"],["l","Connector corrosion from spilled drinks"]],["MIL after two trips","Gearbox or selector warning in the cluster","Usually still drivable, sensor 1 takes over"],["medium",[200,900]],"u"],"P089D":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 2 Circuit High","Gear lever position sensor 2 is the redundant second sensor of an electronic selector lever, next to sensor 1 (P0821 ff), for the X or Y axis. The only documented user is GM's electronic range selection with PWM Hall sensors, as in the Cadillac XT4, and only for range, low and high.",[["h","Signal wire open (pull-up) or shorted to voltage"],["m","Sensor 2 faulty"],["m","Sensor ground open"]],["MIL after two trips","Gearbox or selector warning in the cluster","Usually still drivable, sensor 1 takes over"],["medium",[200,900]],"u"],"P089E":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 2 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Gear lever position sensor 2 is the redundant second sensor of an electronic selector lever, next to sensor 1 (P0821 ff), for the X or Y axis. The only documented user is GM's electronic range selection with PWM Hall sensors, as in the Cadillac XT4, and only for range, low and high.",[["h","Loose contact at the selector connector"],["m","Sensor 2 with internal dropouts"],["l","Wire chafed in the console"]],["Selector warning at times","Range selection hesitates sporadically"],["medium",[100,900]],"u"],"P089F":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 2 Circuit","Gear lever position sensor 2 is the redundant second sensor of an electronic selector lever, next to sensor 1 (P0821 ff), for the X or Y axis. The only documented user is GM's electronic range selection with PWM Hall sensors, as in the Cadillac XT4, and only for range, low and high.",[["h","Wire or connector at the selector module open"],["m","Sensor 2 faulty"],["l","Corrosion from liquid in the console"]],["Gearbox or selector warning in the cluster","Range selection delayed or limited","Usually still drivable, sensor 1 takes over"],["medium",[250,900]],"u"],"P08A0":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 2 Circuit Range/Performance","Sensor 2 is the redundant second Hall sensor that reports the Y-axis travel of an electronic selector lever as a PWM duty cycle. GM uses it in its electronic range selection (ECM and ICCM tables 2019–2025, Cadillac XT4 among them).",[["h","Sensor 2 in the selector assembly drifting or faulty"],["m","Selector mechanism worn, bent or binding, lever leaves its nominal path"],["m","Loose contact or interference on the PWM signal line"],["l","Spilled liquid in the centre console"]],["Check engine light after two drives","Shift lever message or occasional unaccepted range selection","Usually still drivable, sensor 1 takes over"],["medium",[150,900]],"u"],"P08A1":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 2 Circuit Low","The selector lever's Y-axis sensor 2 is one of two redundant Hall sensors that send the lever travel as a PWM duty cycle. GM documents P08A1 for its electronic range selection: the code sets when the measured duty cycle of Y sensor 2 drops below 17 % (2019 ECM and ICCM tables) or below 5 % (Cadillac XT4 2019, ECM tables 2023–2025), checked 3 of 4 samples at 25 ms or 10 of 12 in later years.",[["h","Sensor 2 signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply open or connector backed out"],["m","Sensor 2 in the selector assembly without output"],["l","Connector corrosion from liquid in the console"]],["Check engine light after two drives","Shifter warning message in the cluster","Normally no change in driving, sensor 1 takes over"],["medium",[100,900]],"u"],"P08A2":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 2 Circuit High","Y sensor 2 is the backup Hall sensor of an electronic selector lever and reports the lever's Y-axis travel as a PWM duty cycle. GM uses P08A2 in its electronic range selection and sets it when the duty cycle of Y sensor 2 rises above 88 % (2019 ECM and ICCM tables) or above 95 % (Cadillac XT4 2019, ECM tables 2023–2025); 3 failures in 4 samples at 25 ms, in later calibrations 10 of 12.",[["h","Sensor 2 signal wire shorted to supply voltage"],["m","Sensor ground open or high resistance"],["m","Internal fault of sensor 2 in the selector assembly"]],["Check engine light after two drives","Shifter warning message in the cluster","Range selection normally still works via sensor 1"],["medium",[100,900]],"u"],"P08A3":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 2 Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","This code covers short dropouts or jumps in the signal of the second, redundant Y-axis sensor of an electronic selector lever. No manufacturer documents P08A3 publicly.",[["h","Loose or fretting contact at the selector connector"],["m","Harness chafed by console or lever movement"],["l","Sensor 2 with intermittent internal fault"]],["Stored fault that comes and goes, often without driver complaint","Occasional shifter warning message","Possibly check engine light, depending on the manufacturer"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P08A4":["Gear Lever X Position Sensor 1/2 Correlation","An electronic selector lever with redundant sensing measures the X axis with two sensors. P08A4 means the two X readings no longer agree although each may be within its own limits.",[["h","One of the two X sensors drifting"],["m","Magnet carrier or lever mechanism shifted or worn"],["m","Contact resistance on one sensor's signal path"]],["Shifter warning message","Range selection refused or vehicle held in neutral or park","Check engine light, depending on the manufacturer"],["medium",[150,1000]],"u"],"P08A5":["Gear Lever Y Position Sensor 1/2 Correlation","P08A5 is the Y-axis twin of P08A4: the two redundant sensors that measure the selector lever's Y travel disagree beyond a calibrated limit. No manufacturer documents P08A5 publicly.",[["h","One of the two Y sensors drifting"],["m","Magnet carrier or lever mechanism shifted or worn"],["m","Contact resistance on one sensor's signal path"]],["Shifter warning message","Range selection refused or vehicle held in neutral or park","Check engine light, depending on the manufacturer"],["medium",[150,1000]],"u"],"P08A6":["Gear Lever Position Sensor System - Multiple Sensor Correlation","Here the module compares the combined gear lever position information with other evidence and finds no consistent picture. Two makers document it.",[["h","Tyre size changed without relearning the top-gear ratio (FCA manual)"],["m","Control module software needing an update"],["l","Modified transfer case gearing, fault only in 4Low"],["m","Internal fault or connector problem of the electronic shift module"]],["Check engine light","Start-stop and gear indicator disabled, clutch overheat or service transmission message","On shift-by-wire, no gear can be selected"],["medium",[50,1500]],"u"],"P08A7":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit/Open","Sensor A is the analog or PWM sensor that reports how far the clutch pedal of a manual car is pressed; the engine controller needs it for start-stop, cruise control, gear detection and starter release. P08A7 is the general circuit or open-wire fault of that sensor.",[["h","Loose connector or broken wire at the pedal sensor (pedal box wiring flexes with every clutch stroke)"],["m","Internal fault in clutch pedal position sensor A"],["m","Missing 5 V reference or sensor ground"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the engine control module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Start-stop and cruise control unavailable","Engine may not crank if the starter release relies on the sensor"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P08A8":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P08A8 flags a clutch pedal sensor whose signal is electrically valid but contradicts how the car is actually driving. GM uses it on manual cars since model year 2018 (before: P0806), e.g.",[["h","Sensor loose or misaligned on the pedal bracket, so its reading no longer matches pedal travel"],["m","Worn or drifting sensor track giving an offset signal"],["m","Driver resting a foot on the clutch pedal, or a floor mat holding it down"],["l","Corroded connector adding resistance to signal or ground"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Start-stop and cruise control switched off","Rev-matching or hill-start functions may be disabled"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P08A9":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit Low","The sensor is designed with dead bands at both ends, so a signal near 0 V can only come from a fault. GM sets P08A9 when the signal stays below 4 % of the 5 V reference (low voltage or open circuit) for 400 of 500 samples taken every 12.5 ms, with the engine not cranking and system voltage above 9 V (10 V in some 2018 tables); Type A, MIL after one trip.",[["h","Signal wire chafed through and shorted to ground in the pedal area"],["h","Open signal wire or missing 5 V reference (connector off, broken wire)"],["m","Failed clutch pedal position sensor A"],["l","Shared 5 V reference pulled down by another sensor on the same circuit"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp on after one trip","Start-stop and cruise control unavailable","Engine may refuse to crank on vehicles that release the starter via the sensor"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P08AA":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit High","P08AA is the mirror image of P08A9: the pedal sensor signal climbs into the upper dead band that a healthy sensor never reaches. GM's threshold is above 96 % of the 5 V reference for 400 of 500 samples at 12.5 ms, engine not cranking, system voltage above 9 V; Type A, MIL after one trip.",[["h","Open sensor ground wire or corroded ground pin"],["m","Signal wire shorted to the 5 V reference or to battery voltage"],["m","Failed clutch pedal position sensor A"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the engine control module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp on after one trip","Start-stop and cruise control disabled","Clutch status shown incorrectly in scan tool data"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P08AB":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P08AB is meant for a clutch pedal sensor A signal that jumps, drops out briefly or changes faster than a foot could move the pedal, without staying out of range long enough for the low or high codes. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Loose or worn terminals in the pedal sensor connector"],["h","Wire breaking inside its insulation where the harness flexes with the pedal"],["m","Worn sensor track causing signal dropouts at certain pedal positions"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the footwell connector"]],["Start-stop or cruise control cutting out intermittently","Warning lamp that comes and goes","Erratic clutch pedal value in scan tool data while the pedal is held still"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P08AC":["Clutch Pedal Position Not Learned","The engine controller has to know at which pedal position the clutch is fully released or disengaged. GM teaches the fully applied position at the factory and then adapts it over time to clutch wear.",[["h","Relearn not performed after replacing the sensor, clutch parts or engine control module"],["m","Learned position drifted out of the allowed window (worn clutch, wrong pedal stop, air in hydraulics)"],["m","Learn procedure aborted, for example by low battery voltage"],["l","Faulty or misaligned clutch pedal sensor preventing a valid learn"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Start-stop, cruise control or automatic parking brake release not working as expected"],["medium",[50,250]],"u"],"P08AD":["Neutral Position Sensor B Circuit/Open","A neutral position sensor tells the engine module whether a manual gearbox is in neutral, which start-stop, remote start and rev matching rely on. Sensor B is a second sensor or channel next to sensor A (P086A ff).",[["h","Wire to the sensor broken or stretched by shift shaft movement"],["m","Connector at the gearbox loose or corroded"],["m","Sensor B open internally"]],["Start-stop no longer switches the engine off","Start system warning in the cluster","Driving normally unaffected"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P08AE":["Neutral Position Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","Sensor B is the second neutral sensor or channel on the shift shaft of a manual gearbox, complementing sensor A (P086A ff). P08AE means its signal is electrically present but implausible: it stays outside the expected window, does not change when gears are engaged, or reports neutral while the gear ratio says otherwise.",[["h","Sensor B or its magnet misaligned after gearbox work"],["m","Sensor B drifting or faulty"],["l","Worn shift shaft or detent giving an unclear neutral position"]],["Start-stop unavailable","Start system or gear indicator message in the cluster","No noticeable change while driving"],["medium",[60,400]],"u"],"P08AF":["Neutral Position Sensor B Circuit Low","The signal of neutral position sensor B, the second neutral sensor or channel of a manual gearbox, has fallen below its lower electrical limit. Typical reasons are a signal wire shorted to ground, a missing sensor supply or a sensor without output.",[["h","Signal wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply open"],["m","Sensor B without output"],["l","Water or corrosion in the connector at the gearbox"]],["Start-stop no longer works","Start system warning in the cluster","Fault stored without driving complaint"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P08B0":["Neutral Position Sensor B Circuit High","P08B0 reports that the signal of neutral position sensor B on a manual gearbox is above its upper electrical limit. With a three-wire Hall sensor this usually points to an open signal or ground wire or a short to voltage.",[["h","Signal or ground wire pulled apart, harness too short"],["m","Signal wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Sensor B open internally"]],["Start-stop does not switch the engine off","Start system warning in the cluster","Fault may come and go with gear changes"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P08B1":["Neutral Position Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Neutral position sensor B, the second neutral sensor or channel on a manual gearbox's shift shaft, delivers a signal that drops out or jumps for short moments. No manufacturer documents P08B1 publicly.",[["h","Wire strained or partly broken by gearbox movement"],["m","Loose or fretting connector contact"],["l","Sensor B with intermittent internal fault"]],["Start-stop works only now and then","Occasional start system message","Fault returns after clearing without visible cause"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P08B2":["Neutral Position Sensor A/B Correlation","On a manual gearbox with two neutral sensors or two channels, the engine module compares A and B. P08B2 means they disagree: one reports neutral, the other a gear, or their values differ beyond a calibrated limit, while each may look fine electrically.",[["h","One of the two neutral sensors drifting or faulty"],["m","Magnet on the shift shaft shifted or sensor misaligned after repair"],["m","Neutral position not relearned after component replacement"]],["Start-stop unavailable","Start system warning in the cluster","Gear indicator may be blank or wrong"],["medium",[60,450]],"u"],"P08B3":["Neutral Position Sensor B Not Learned","Analog neutral sensors report a position value, and the control module must learn which value means neutral to cover part and fitting tolerances. P08B3 says no valid learned value exists for sensor B, typically after replacing the sensor, gearbox or module, after a software update, or after an aborted learn.",[["h","Learn procedure not done after sensor, gearbox or module replacement"],["m","Learn run aborted, lever unsteady or linkage misadjusted"],["m","Learned values erased by software update or module reset"],["l","Sensor mounted outside the learnable range or faulty"]],["Start-stop unavailable","Cruise control may be inoperative","Appears right after a repair or software update"],["easy",[30,150]],"u"],"P08B4":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit/Open","Some manual cars read the clutch pedal with two independent sensors so a single failure can be detected; Ford's 2020–23 gasoline OBD summary describes such a dual PWM layout. Sensor B is the second channel.",[["h","Loose connector or broken wire on the sensor B channel"],["m","Internal fault in clutch pedal position sensor B (often a shared housing with sensor A)"],["m","Missing supply or ground for sensor B"],["l","Faulty sensor B input in the engine control module"]],["Warning lamp on","Start-stop disabled","Correlation code P08B9 may be stored as well"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P08B5":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P08B5 covers a second clutch pedal sensor whose signal lies within its electrical limits but is implausible on its own, for example stuck at one value while the pedal moves or not matching the driving state. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Sensor B drifting or worn so its value no longer follows pedal travel"],["m","Sensor loose or misaligned on the pedal bracket"],["m","Corrosion adding resistance in the sensor B circuit"]],["Warning lamp on","Start-stop or cruise control unavailable"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P08B6":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit Low","P08B6 means the signal of the second clutch pedal sensor has dropped below its valid range. Ford lists it in the 2020–23 gasoline OBD summary under dual PWM clutch sensors, together with P08A9/P08AA for sensor A, P08B7 for sensor B high and P08B9 for the A/B comparison; monitoring runs continuously and needs less than one second to flag a fault.",[["h","Sensor B signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Lost supply voltage to sensor B (open wire, connector off)"],["m","Failed clutch pedal position sensor B"]],["Warning lamp on","Start-stop disabled","P08B9 correlation code may set alongside"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P08B7":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit High","P08B7 is set when the second clutch pedal sensor reports a value above its valid range. Ford uses it on vehicles with two PWM clutch pedal sensors, as listed in its 2020–23 gasoline OBD summary next to P08B6 (sensor B low), P08A9/P08AA (sensor A) and P08B9 (A/B correlation); the check is continuous and decides within one second.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire of sensor B, leaving the input at its pull-up level"],["m","Sensor B signal shorted to a voltage source"],["m","Failed clutch pedal position sensor B"]],["Warning lamp on","Start-stop disabled","Sensor B reading pinned at maximum in scan tool data"],["easy",[50,300]],"u"],"P08B8":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P08B8 is intended for a second clutch pedal sensor whose signal drops out briefly or jumps without staying out of range. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Loose or worn terminals in the sensor B connector"],["m","Wire fatiguing where the harness flexes with pedal movement"],["m","Sensor B producing glitches internally"]],["Start-stop dropping out intermittently","Warning lamp that comes and goes"],["medium",[50,300]],"u"],"P08B9":["Clutch Pedal Position Sensor A/B Correlation","P08B9 is stored when two independent measurements of the clutch pedal disagree. Ford uses it in two ways per its 2020–23 gasoline OBD summary: with dual PWM sensors it compares sensor A with sensor B and decides within a second; with a single analog sensor plus two clutch switches it covers an in-range rationality fault or any switch-related mismatch, the switch part being judged over about 40 clutch cycles at different speeds.",[["h","One of the two sensors drifting or worn so the channels no longer agree"],["m","Sticking or misadjusted clutch switch on systems with one sensor plus switches"],["m","Corroded connector or wiring adding resistance to one channel"],["l","Sensor assembly or pedal stop misaligned after repairs"]],["Warning lamp on","Start-stop disabled","Cruise control unavailable"],["medium",[50,350]],"u"],"P08BA":["Pressure Control Solenoid L","Base code for the twelfth pressure control solenoid of an automatic transmission; which valve carries the letter L is up to the manufacturer. Toyota is the one documented user: bulletin T-SB-0094-24 for the 2024 Tacoma and Tacoma Hybrid lists it as P08BA71, actuator stuck, next to the stuck faults of solenoids A, C, D, G and H and a torque converter clutch stuck off.",[["h","Solenoid or its control valve sticking inside the valve body"],["m","Internal transmission fault from production, debris spreading through the hydraulics"],["m","Contaminated or wrong transmission fluid"],["l","Connector or harness fault at the solenoid"]],["Check engine light on","Harsh, delayed or missing shifts","Transmission may hold a substitute gear"],["shop_only",[300,9000]],"u"],"P08BB":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Performance/Stuck Off","Set when pressure control solenoid L is commanded but the pressure or shift element it serves does not respond, i.e. the valve acts as if it were stuck in its off state.",[["h","Valve spool held closed by debris or varnish"],["m","Low fluid level or weak supply pressure to the valve"],["m","Leaking seal or circuit downstream of the valve"]],["Check engine light on","Missing, slipping or delayed shifts","Possible substitute gear operation"],["shop_only",[200,4000]],"u"],"P08BC":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Stuck On","Set when pressure control solenoid L keeps applying pressure although the control module has commanded it off, so the element it feeds stays loaded. No manufacturer publishes this code.",[["h","Valve spool held open by debris"],["m","Worn bore in the valve body letting pressure through"],["l","Output stage or wiring keeping the solenoid energised"]],["Check engine light on","Harsh engagement or binding during shifts","Transmission locked in a fallback gear"],["shop_only",[200,4000]],"u"],"P08BD":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Electrical","Collective code for an electrical fault of pressure control solenoid L that the module does not break down further into open, low or high. No manufacturer documents it publicly; Toyota, the one known user of letter L, has only published the stuck fault P08BA71.",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Corroded connector or damaged internal harness"],["l","Faulty output stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine light on","Restricted gears or fallback gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08BE":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Intermittent","Reports a fault of pressure control solenoid L that comes and goes, typically a circuit that drops out briefly or a valve response that is only occasionally implausible. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and in earlier packages the intermittent codes of the other pressure control solenoids also proved unused.",[["h","Loose or spread terminal at the solenoid or transmission connector"],["m","Chafed wire touching ground intermittently"],["l","Debris making the valve hang only now and then"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Occasional harsh or flaring shift"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08BF":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Control Circuit/Open","Points to an open control circuit of pressure control solenoid L: the module sees no load where the coil should be. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; Toyota, the only known user of letter L, has published just P08BA71.",[["h","Connector loose, unplugged or corroded"],["m","Broken wire in the internal or external harness"],["m","Solenoid coil burnt open"]],["Check engine light on","Transmission in fallback gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08C0":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Control Circuit Range/Performance","Set when the control circuit of pressure control solenoid L is electrically intact but its behaviour is implausible, for example the current through the coil does not match the commanded value. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; Toyota, the only documented user of letter L, publishes only P08BA71.",[["h","Coil resistance out of specification"],["m","High contact resistance at connector pins"],["l","Unstable supply voltage to the transmission module"]],["Check engine light on","Uneven shift quality"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08C1":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Control Circuit Low","Reports a control circuit voltage of pressure control solenoid L that is too low, usually a short to ground. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; Toyota, the one documented user of letter L, has published only P08BA71.",[["h","Control wire chafed through to ground"],["m","Shorted turns in the solenoid coil"],["l","Fluid or moisture in the connector"]],["Check engine light on","Transmission in fallback gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08C2":["Pressure Control Solenoid L Control Circuit High","Reports a control circuit voltage of pressure control solenoid L that is too high, typically a short to battery voltage or to a neighbouring output. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; Toyota, the one documented user of letter L, has published only P08BA71.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Short between adjacent solenoid wires in the internal harness"],["l","Defective output stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine light on","Transmission in fallback gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08C3":["Pressure Control Solenoid M","Base code for the thirteenth pressure control solenoid; the letter is assigned by the manufacturer. GM uses letter M in the Tremec eight-speed dual clutch of the Corvette (RPO M1L/M1M): its 2023–2025 TCM tables name the part synchronizer actuation valve 1, which serves the odd-gear shaft, while valves 2 to 5 report under P27B9 onwards.",[["h","Valve sticking from debris in the valve body"],["m","Fault in the solenoid wiring or connector"],["l","Worn or failed solenoid"]],["Check engine light on","Rough or missing shifts in odd gears","Possible fallback mode"],["shop_only",[200,4000]],"u"],"P08C4":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Performance/Stuck Off","GM sets this code on the Corvette's Tremec dual clutch when synchronizer actuation valve 1, the valve for the odd-gear shaft, is hydraulically stuck off. The test runs inside a synchronizer recovery or integrity routine, started after a shift-related fault or when the stored gear does not match the sensed gears at start-up: the shift rods are moved slightly, and if a rod travels 100 µm or less where the valve should move it, a current profile check of the valve has to fail three times before the fault is confirmed.",[["h","Valve stuck closed by debris in the control valve body"],["m","Debris from a dislodged snap ring in the direct clutch assembly (bulletin 23-NA-197)"],["l","Low system pressure or fluid level"]],["Check engine light on","Odd gears not engaged or engaged late","Noise from the transmission area"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"u"],"P08C5":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Stuck On","GM uses this code on the Corvette's Tremec dual clutch when synchronizer actuation valve 1, serving the odd-gear shaft, keeps pressure on although it is commanded off. The check runs whenever the selector pilot valve changes position: a valve stuck on keeps pushing a shift rod, so after the switch the pressure is routed elsewhere.",[["h","Valve held open by debris in the control valve body"],["m","Debris from a dislodged snap ring in the direct clutch assembly (bulletin 23-NA-197)"],["l","Worn valve bore in the control valve body"]],["Check engine light on","Clunks or unwanted gear engagement on the odd shaft","Noise from the transmission area"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"u"],"P08C6":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Electrical","Collective code for an electrical fault of pressure control solenoid M without a finer split into open, low or high. No manufacturer documents it publicly.",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Damaged connector or internal harness"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine light on","Restricted shifting or fallback mode"],["hard",[100,2500]],"u"],"P08C7":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Intermittent","Reports an on-and-off fault of pressure control solenoid M, such as brief circuit dropouts or occasional implausible responses. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; GM, which uses letter M for synchronizer actuation valve 1 of the Corvette dual clutch, lists no intermittent code for it, and earlier packages found the intermittent codes of the other pressure control solenoids unused as well.",[["h","Loose terminal at the solenoid or transmission connector"],["m","Chafed wire with intermittent contact"],["l","Debris causing occasional valve hang-ups"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Sporadic rough shifts"],["hard",[100,2500]],"u"],"P08C8":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Control Circuit/Open","GM sets this code on the Corvette's Tremec dual clutch for an open circuit at synchronizer actuation valve 1, the valve for the odd-gear shaft. The open-load test runs with the low-side driver switched off: an intact coil pulls the sense pin to battery voltage, so if the driver chip can hold it between about 1.7 and 3 V (each ±0.5 V) with a very small current, the circuit is considered open.",[["h","Connector at the control valve body loose or corroded"],["m","Broken wire in the internal harness"],["m","Solenoid coil open"]],["Check engine light on immediately","Fallback mode with restricted gears"],["hard",[100,2500]],"u"],"P08C9":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Control Circuit Range/Performance","Meant for a control circuit of pressure control solenoid M that is not open or shorted but behaves implausibly, for instance a coil current that does not follow the command. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Coil resistance out of specification"],["m","High contact resistance at connector pins"],["l","Unstable supply voltage to the transmission module"]],["Check engine light on","Uneven shift quality"],["hard",[100,2500]],"u"],"P08CA":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Control Circuit Low","GM sets this code on the Corvette's Tremec dual clutch for a short to ground in the circuit of synchronizer actuation valve 1, which serves the odd-gear shaft. The test runs while the low-side driver is switched on: the sense pin should then sit at a defined level, and if the driver chip reports it below about 1.7 V (±0.5 V), a ground short is assumed.",[["h","Control wire chafed through to ground"],["m","Shorted turns in the solenoid coil"],["l","Fluid in the transmission connector"]],["Check engine light on immediately","Fallback mode with restricted gears"],["hard",[100,2500]],"u"],"P08CB":["Pressure Control Solenoid M Control Circuit High","GM uses this code on the Corvette's Tremec dual clutch for synchronizer actuation valve 1 (odd-gear shaft) in two situations. First, a short to battery voltage: with the low-side driver on, a sense voltage above 0.7, 0.9 or 1.1 V (±0.1 V), depending on supply voltage, counts as a shorted load, confirmed after 150 ms.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Short to an adjacent solenoid wire in the internal harness"],["l","Defective driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine light on immediately","Fallback mode with restricted gears","Code may appear right after starting"],["hard",[100,2500]],"u"],"P08CC":["Shift Solenoid J","Base code for the tenth on/off shift solenoid of an automatic transmission; which valve gets the letter J is decided by the manufacturer. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Shift solenoid J faulty (coil or valve)"],["m","Contaminated or degraded transmission fluid restricting the valve"],["m","Connector or harness fault between TCM and solenoid"],["l","Transmission control module output fault"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Harsh, delayed or missing shifts","Transmission may hold a substitute gear"],["hard",[150,2500]],"u"],"P08CD":["Shift Solenoid J Performance/Stuck Off","Set when shift solenoid J is switched on but the gear or clutch it controls does not respond, so the valve behaves as if stuck in its off position. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Solenoid or its shift valve held in the off position by debris or varnish"],["m","Low fluid level or weak supply pressure to the valve"],["m","Worn clutch pack or leaking seal in the circuit served by the valve"],["l","Solenoid coil too weak to move the valve"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Engine flare or slipping during or after a shift","A gear is missing or skipped"],["shop_only",[200,3500]],"u"],"P08CE":["Shift Solenoid J Stuck On","Set when shift solenoid J is switched off but its clutch or shift valve stays applied, so the valve behaves as if stuck in its on position. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Solenoid or its shift valve held in the applied position by debris or varnish"],["m","Contaminated transmission fluid after internal wear"],["l","Control circuit shorted so the solenoid stays energised"],["l","TCM output driver does not switch off"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Wrong gear or harsh engagement, possible binding feel","Transmission goes into limp mode"],["shop_only",[200,3500]],"u"],"P08CF":["Shift Solenoid J Electrical","General electrical fault of shift solenoid J, used when a manufacturer does not split the circuit fault into open, low, high or range codes. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted internally"],["m","Damaged harness, corroded or loose connector at the transmission"],["m","Fault in the internal transmission harness or solenoid body"],["l","TCM output driver fault"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Harsh or missing shifts","Transmission may lock into a limp-home gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08D0":["Shift Solenoid J Intermittent","Reserved for a shift solenoid J fault that comes and goes instead of being present continuously, typically brief circuit dropouts or erratic valve response. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and the intermittent codes of the earlier shift solenoids, such as P0754 for solenoid A, are not used in the GM and Ford sources checked either; those makers report dropouts through their open, short or functional codes.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector losing contact under vibration or heat"],["m","Chafed harness with intermittent short or break"],["m","Valve sticking now and then due to contaminated fluid"],["l","Solenoid coil failing only when hot"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Occasional harsh or delayed shifts","Sporadic limp mode that clears after restart"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08D1":["Shift Solenoid J Control Circuit Range/Performance","Set when the control circuit of shift solenoid J is electrically intact but its measured values, usually solenoid current or driver feedback, lie outside the expected window for the commanded state. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Solenoid coil resistance out of specification"],["m","High resistance at connector pins or internal harness"],["l","Unstable supply voltage to the TCM or solenoid feed"],["l","Faulty current sensing in the TCM output stage"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Harsh or irregular shifts","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08D2":["Shift Solenoid J Control Circuit Low","Set when the TCM sees too little voltage on the control line of shift solenoid J, typically because the line or the coil is shorted to ground. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; J would be a tenth on/off valve, which none of the known transmission sources assigns.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Solenoid coil with internal short"],["m","Damaged internal transmission harness or lead frame"],["l","Faulty output stage in the TCM"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Transmission holds a substitute gear","Harsh or missing shifts"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08D3":["Shift Solenoid J Control Circuit High","Logged when the control line of shift solenoid J reads higher than the commanded state allows, pointing to a short to battery voltage or, on some designs, an open coil. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a voltage-carrying conductor"],["m","Open solenoid coil (on systems that report opens as high)"],["l","Moisture in the transmission connector bridging pins"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Wrong gear or harsh engagement","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08D4":["Shift Solenoid J Control Circuit/Open","Dedicated code for an open control circuit of shift solenoid J: the TCM drives the valve but no current flows through wire, connector or coil. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Broken control wire or loose terminal"],["m","Burnt-out solenoid coil"],["m","Transmission connector not fully seated after service"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","One gear missing or transmission stuck in one gear","Harsh shifts"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P08D5":["Shift Solenoid K","Base code for shift solenoid K, the eleventh on/off shift valve in SAE's lettering. Which valve carries the letter is up to the manufacturer, and none documents K publicly: Ford's gasoline OBD summary lists shift solenoids only up to SS E, Hyundai's bulletin 21-AT-016HG for the Genesis 8-speed ends at shift control solenoid F, and GM's 2016 to 2025 tables contain no code of the K group.",[["h","Shift solenoid K faulty (coil or valve)"],["m","Harness or connector fault between TCM and solenoid"],["m","Degraded or contaminated transmission fluid"],["l","TCM output fault"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Harsh, delayed or missing shifts","Transmission may hold a substitute gear"],["hard",[150,2500]],"u"],"P08D6":["Shift Solenoid K Performance/Stuck Off","Indicates that shift solenoid K was commanded on but the gear or clutch behind it never engaged, as if the valve stayed in its off position. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Valve or shift spool held in rest position by debris or varnish"],["m","Low fluid level or weak line pressure"],["m","Leaking seal or worn clutch pack in the circuit fed by the valve"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Engine flare or slipping during shifts","A gear is skipped or missing"],["shop_only",[200,3500]],"u"],"P08D7":["Shift Solenoid K Stuck On","Stored when shift solenoid K is switched off but the circuit it feeds stays pressurised, so a clutch remains applied. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Valve or shift spool jammed in applied position"],["m","Metal debris in the fluid after internal wear"],["l","TCM output or shorted wire keeps the solenoid energised"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Wrong gear when pulling away","Binding or harsh engagement"],["shop_only",[200,3500]],"u"],"P08D8":["Shift Solenoid K Electrical","Collective electrical fault for shift solenoid K, used by makers that do not split circuit faults into low, high, open or range codes. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Solenoid coil open or shorted"],["m","Damaged harness or corroded connector"],["l","TCM output driver fault"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Harsh or missing shifts","Transmission may lock into a limp-home gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08D9":["Shift Solenoid K Intermittent","Meant for a shift solenoid K fault that appears and vanishes, such as a connector losing contact over bumps or a coil dropping out when hot. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and none of the sources checked even uses the intermittent code of solenoid A, P0754; GM and Ford catch brief interruptions with their open, short and functional codes instead.",[["h","Loose or fretted terminal in the transmission connector"],["m","Chafed wire that touches only under vibration"],["l","Solenoid coil dropping out at high fluid temperature"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Occasional harsh or late shift","Sporadic limp mode that clears after restart"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08DA":["Shift Solenoid K Control Circuit Range/Performance","Points to a control circuit of shift solenoid K that is neither open nor shorted, yet whose measured values, usually coil current, do not fit the commanded state. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and the analogue for solenoid A, P0972, is not used in the GM or Ford sources either.",[["h","Solenoid coil resistance out of specification"],["m","Corroded or loose pins adding resistance"],["l","TCM output stage no longer delivers correct current"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Delayed or soft shifts","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08DB":["Shift Solenoid K Control Circuit Low","The TCM's output stage reports a voltage on the shift solenoid K control circuit below what the commanded state requires, the classic sign of a control wire rubbing through to ground or a coil with an internal short. No manufacturer documents this K code publicly.",[["h","Control wire rubbed through to ground"],["m","Internally shorted solenoid coil"],["l","TCM output stage fault"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Transmission stays in a limp-home gear","Reduced acceleration due to missing gears"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08DC":["Shift Solenoid K Control Circuit High","The monitored control line of shift solenoid K sits closer to supply voltage than it should, for instance because it touches a powered wire or, depending on the driver design, because the coil is open. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Control wire touching a powered conductor"],["m","Open coil on systems that read opens as high"],["l","TCM low-side driver no longer pulls the line down"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Wrong gear or harsh engagement","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08DD":["Shift Solenoid K Control Circuit/Open","Signals that the control circuit of shift solenoid K is open, so commanding the valve produces no current: a broken wire, a pushed-out terminal, a burnt coil or a failed internal transmission harness. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Broken wire or pushed-out terminal"],["m","Failed internal transmission harness"],["m","Burnt-out solenoid coil"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Some gears no longer available","Limp mode with fixed gear"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P08DE":["Shift Solenoid L","Base code for shift solenoid L, which in SAE's lettering would be the twelfth on/off shift valve of an automatic transmission. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Shift solenoid L faulty"],["m","Wiring or connector fault on the solenoid circuit"],["m","Debris in the valve body affecting the valve"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Harsh or erratic shifting","Possible substitute gear"],["hard",[150,2500]],"u"],"P08DF":["Shift Solenoid L Performance/Stuck Off","Functional fault of shift solenoid L: the TCM switches the valve on, but speed sensor data show that the intended clutch did not take up, so the valve acts as if stuck off. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Shift spool jammed by debris"],["m","Low line pressure or low fluid level"],["m","Leak in the clutch circuit fed by the valve"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Engine flare between gears","Gear missing or skipped"],["shop_only",[200,3500]],"u"],"P08E0":["Shift Solenoid L Stuck On","Functional fault of shift solenoid L in the opposite direction: although switched off, the valve keeps its circuit applied, leaving engaged a clutch that should be released. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Shift spool seized in applied position"],["m","Contaminated fluid after clutch or bearing wear"],["l","TCM output stage does not switch off"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Drivetrain binding or harsh shifts","Limp mode"],["shop_only",[200,3500]],"u"],"P08E1":["Shift Solenoid L Electrical","General electrical malfunction of shift solenoid L, intended for transmissions whose TCM does not distinguish or separately report low, high and open circuit faults. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Solenoid L coil failed electrically"],["m","Harness or connector damage on the solenoid circuit"],["l","TCM output fault"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Harsh shifts or fixed gear","Reduced driving performance"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08E2":["Shift Solenoid L Intermittent","Reserved for an unstable fault of shift solenoid L that is detected, disappears and may return, typically from a loose terminal, a chafed wire that only touches under vibration or a heat-sensitive coil. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Loose terminal or poor pin tension in the connector"],["m","Insulation chafed at a bracket or edge"],["l","Coil that fails intermittently when hot"]],["Intermittent warning light","Occasional harsh shift","Limp mode that disappears after restart"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08E3":["Shift Solenoid L Control Circuit Range/Performance","Plausibility fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid L: the circuit is not open or shorted, but voltage or current during actuation is implausible for the commanded state. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and the matching code for solenoid A, P0972, is likewise absent from the GM and Ford documents checked.",[["h","Coil resistance drifting with temperature"],["m","Corroded pins adding resistance"],["l","TCM output stage losing drive strength"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Soft or delayed shifts","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08E4":["Shift Solenoid L Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid L. Usually it means a short to ground on the control wire, less often a coil with shorted windings that pulls the voltage down.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Coil with shorted windings"],["l","Fluid or moisture in the connector creating a path to ground"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Transmission locked in a substitute gear","Weak acceleration"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08E5":["Shift Solenoid L Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid L, set when the line shows battery voltage or a floating high level while the TCM expects otherwise. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Control wire chafed against a powered conductor"],["m","Open coil on systems that read opens as high"],["l","TCM output stage fault"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Wrong gear selected","Harsh engagement or limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P08E6":["Shift Solenoid L Control Circuit/Open","Open circuit in the control path of shift solenoid L: harness, connector, internal transmission wiring or the coil itself no longer carry current, so the valve cannot be energised. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Break in the harness or internal transmission wiring"],["m","Open solenoid coil"],["l","Connector unlatched or terminal backed out"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gear range missing or fixed gear","Harsh shifts"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P08E7":["Drive Motor A Clutch Enable Circuit","Covers the enable line with which a drive motor control module activates the actuator of a clutch that couples or uncouples electric drive motor A from the wheels. The code sets when the module sees an electrical fault on that line, typically an open circuit or an implausible state.",[["h","Enable wire open or connector at the clutch actuator not seated"],["m","Internal electronics fault in the disconnect clutch actuator"],["m","Corroded or water-damaged connector near the drive unit"],["l","Faulty enable driver in the drive motor control module"]],["Check engine light or hybrid system warning on","No electric drive or assist from the affected axle","All-wheel-drive or electric-only modes unavailable"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P08E8":["Drive Motor A Clutch Enable Circuit Low","Set when the voltage on the enable line of the drive motor A disconnect clutch actuator stays below the expected range, usually because the wire or the actuator input is shorted to ground. The control module can then no longer switch the actuator on, so the clutch cannot be engaged or released on command.",[["h","Enable wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short at the actuator enable input"],["m","Water ingress in the actuator connector"],["l","Faulty output stage in the drive motor control module"]],["Check engine light or hybrid system warning on","Electric drive axle stays disconnected","Reduced performance, no electric launch or all-wheel drive"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P08E9":["Drive Motor A Clutch Enable Circuit High","Set when the enable line of the drive motor A disconnect clutch actuator reads higher than it should, for example because it is shorted to a supply voltage or the line floats due to an open ground. The module then cannot reliably switch the actuator off or confirm its state.",[["h","Enable wire shorted to a supply circuit"],["m","Open ground or reference circuit of the actuator"],["m","Internal fault in the disconnect clutch actuator"],["l","Faulty output stage in the drive motor control module"]],["Check engine light or hybrid system warning on","Electric drive on the affected axle switched off","Possible clunk when the system tries to couple the motor"],["hard",[150,1500]],"u"],"P08EA":["Drive Motor A Clutch Pawl Position Circuit","Concerns the sensor or switch that reports where the pawl of the drive motor A disconnect clutch sits, i.e. whether the form-locking clutch is engaged or released.",[["h","Faulty disconnect clutch position switch or sensor"],["m","Open signal or reference wire to the position switch"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the drive unit"],["l","Faulty input stage in the drive motor control module"]],["Check engine light or hybrid system warning on","Electric drive or all-wheel drive unavailable","Reduced acceleration in hybrid modes"],["hard",[150,1200]],"u"],"P08EB":["Drive Motor A Clutch Pawl Position Circuit Low","Set when the pawl position signal of the drive motor A disconnect clutch stays below its valid range, which points to a short to ground in the signal wire, a missing sensor supply or a sensor that has failed low. The module then cannot tell whether the pawl has engaged.",[["h","Position signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Missing supply voltage to the position switch"],["m","Position switch or sensor failed low"]],["Check engine light or hybrid system warning on","Electric drive axle disabled","Hybrid modes with electric assist not selectable"],["hard",[150,1200]],"u"],"P08EC":["Drive Motor A Clutch Pawl Position Circuit High","Set when the pawl position signal of the drive motor A disconnect clutch stays above its valid range. Typical reasons are an open signal or ground wire, which lets the input float high, or a short to a supply voltage.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire of the position switch"],["m","Signal wire shorted to a supply circuit"],["m","Position switch or sensor failed high"]],["Check engine light or hybrid system warning on","No electric drive from the affected axle","All-wheel drive or electric launch missing"],["hard",[150,1200]],"u"],"P08ED":["Drive Motor A Clutch Pawl Position Performance","A plausibility fault: the pawl position reported for the drive motor A disconnect clutch does not match what the control module commanded, or the clutch did not reach the expected position, even though the circuit itself is electrically intact. GM documents the code for the 2024 Corvette E-Ray, whose front electric drive unit uses a clutch pawl and a disconnect clutch actuator; bulletin 24-NA-165 says it is stored in the drive motor control module, lights the MIL and is usually caused by the disconnect clutch switch, which is replaced as an external part.",[["h","Faulty drive motor disconnect clutch switch (GM E-Ray bulletin)"],["m","Disconnect clutch actuator not moving the pawl fully"],["l","Worn or binding pawl or clutch teeth inside the drive unit"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp on","Electric front-axle drive unavailable or intermittent","Hybrid modes restricted"],["hard",[200,1500]],"u"],"P0900":["Clutch A Actuator Control Circuit/Open","Watches the drive circuit of the actuator that opens and closes clutch A in an automated or dual-clutch transmission. The code sets when the module detects an open circuit, so it cannot energise the actuator.",[["h","Failed clutch A actuator motor (open winding or internal fault)"],["m","Loose connector or broken wire between TCM and actuator"],["l","Faulty motor driver inside the TCM"]],["Check engine light on","Erratic or missed shifts, vibration on take-off","Transmission in limp mode, possibly no drive"],["medium",[150,900]],"c"],"P0901":["Clutch A Actuator Control Circuit Range/Performance","Set when the clutch A actuator circuit is electrically present but does not behave as expected, for instance when the measured current or response deviates too far from the target. Usage differs by maker.",[["h","Weak or worn clutch A actuator motor (dual-clutch units)"],["m","High resistance in the transmission harness or sub-harness"],["l","TCM fault or outdated TCM software"]],["Check engine light on","Shudder, harsh engagement or missed shifts","Failsafe mode with a held gear"],["medium",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0902":["Clutch A Actuator Control Circuit Low","Set when the control circuit of the clutch A actuator shows a low state, in practice a short to ground on a motor or control line. Ford defines it exactly this way for the DPS6 PowerShift transmission: the TCM tests the three-phase clutch A motor for open circuit (P0900), short to ground (P0902) and short to power (P0903), next to hall sensor and sequence checks.",[["h","Clutch A actuator motor with internal short to ground"],["m","Failed TCM output stage (Ford program 14M02 lists this code)"],["m","Chafed motor wire touching ground"]],["Check engine light on","No drive or only some gears available","Jerky take-off and shift faults"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P0903":["Clutch A Actuator Control Circuit High","Set when the control circuit of the clutch A actuator shows a high state, meaning a motor or control line is shorted to battery voltage. Ford names P0903 as the short-to-power test of the clutch A motor in the DPS6 PowerShift gearbox of the Fiesta and Focus, part of a set with P0900 for an open circuit and P0902 for a short to ground.",[["h","Motor or control wire shorted to a supply line"],["m","Internal fault in the clutch A actuator motor"],["l","Faulty motor driver in the TCM"]],["Check engine light on","Limp mode, gears missing or no drive","Harsh or delayed engagement"],["medium",[150,1200]],"c"],"P0904":["Gate Select Position Circuit A","Circuit A of the gate select position sensor tells the control module of an automated manual gearbox where the shift mechanism stands in the select direction, i.e. which gate (for example 1-2, 3-4, 5-6 or reverse) is lined up.",[["h","Loose, corroded or water-damaged connector at the gate select sensor or actuator unit"],["m","Chafed or broken sensor wiring near the gearbox"],["m","Faulty gate select position sensor (often integrated in the shift and select actuator)"],["l","Faulty sensor input or reference supply in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox stops shifting or stays in one gear","Neutral indicator flashes or vehicle will not pull away"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0905":["Gate Select Position Circuit A Range/Performance","Plausibility fault of the gate select position signal on circuit A in an automated manual gearbox. The signal is electrically within limits, but it does not fit what the control module expects: typical checks compare the measured select travel with the learned gate positions and the commanded target gate.",[["h","Worn or drifting gate select sensor, main and redundant tracks disagree"],["m","High resistance at connector pins distorting the sensor voltage"],["m","Gate positions not relearned after actuator, clutch or TCM replacement"],["l","Loose or displaced magnet or sensor mounting on the select shaft"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox drops to neutral or refuses certain gears","Slow or jerky gear changes in fallback mode"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0906":["Gate Select Position Circuit A Low","Set when the gate select position signal on circuit A of an automated manual gearbox stays below its lowest valid value for longer than a calibrated time. Common causes are a short to ground on the signal line, a missing sensor supply, or an internal sensor defect; with many sensor designs a broken signal wire also reads low.",[["h","Signal wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor reference supply interrupted or shorted"],["m","Internal defect of the gate select sensor"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","No gear changes possible or gearbox stays in neutral","Warning message about the transmission on the dashboard"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0907":["Gate Select Position Circuit A High","Set when the gate select position signal on circuit A of an automated manual gearbox stays above its highest valid value for longer than a calibrated time. Typical causes are a short of the signal line to the sensor supply or to battery voltage, a broken sensor ground, or an internal sensor fault.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to sensor supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open sensor ground or corroded ground pin"],["m","Internal fault of the gate select sensor"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox stays in one gear or in neutral","Engine may not start because neutral cannot be confirmed"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0908":["Gate Select Position Circuit A Intermittent","Points to a gate select position signal on circuit A that drops out or jumps for short moments in an automated manual gearbox, too briefly or too irregularly for a steady low or high fault. Loose terminals, a damaged harness that moves with the engine, or moisture in the connector at the shift and select actuator are the usual reasons.",[["h","Loose or spread terminals at the sensor or actuator connector"],["m","Harness damage that only opens or shorts under engine movement"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector near the gearbox"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Occasional drop to neutral or refused shift","Fault often disappears after restarting the engine"],["medium",[50,1200]],"c"],"P0909":["Gate Select Control Error","Set when the control module of an automated manual gearbox commands the select actuator to a new gate, but the measured select position does not reach the target within the allowed time and tolerance. Sensor and actuator circuits may be electrically fine; the fault lies in the closed loop as a whole.",[["h","Stiff or jammed select shaft, shift head or internal selector mechanism"],["m","Worn select motor (brushes, gears) with too little force"],["m","Select sensor offset or gate positions not relearned"],["l","Weak battery or voltage drop on the actuator supply"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Certain gears cannot be engaged or gearbox stays in neutral","Clicking or whirring from the actuator without a gear change"],["hard",[150,2000]],"c"],"P090A":["Clutch B Actuator Control Circuit/Open","Watches the drive circuit of the actuator for clutch B, the second clutch of a dual-clutch transmission, and sets when the module finds an open circuit. Ford uses it on the DPS6 PowerShift of the Fiesta and Focus: the clutch B electric motor is tested for driver faults (P087A), hall sensor faults (P087B), sequence errors (P087E), an open circuit (P090A), a short to ground (P090C) and a short to power (P090D).",[["h","Failed clutch B actuator motor"],["m","Loose connector or broken wire to the clutch B motor"],["l","Faulty motor driver inside the TCM"]],["Check engine light on","Even gears or reverse missing, erratic shifts","Limp mode or no drive"],["medium",[150,900]],"u"],"P090B":["Clutch B Actuator Control Circuit Performance","Set when the clutch B actuator circuit is electrically connected but the actuator does not respond as commanded, for example too slowly or with an unexpected current draw. Ford uses P090B on the DPS6 PowerShift transmission.",[["h","Worn or sluggish clutch B actuator motor"],["m","TCM fault (Ford program 14M02 lists this code)"],["m","Damaged harness between TCM and clutch B motor"]],["Check engine light on","Shudder or slipping in even gears and reverse","Delayed or missed shifts, possible limp mode"],["medium",[150,1500]],"u"],"P090C":["Clutch B Actuator Control Circuit Low","Set when the control circuit of the clutch B actuator shows a low state, which on the documented application means a short to ground. Ford defines P090C as the short-to-ground check of the clutch B motor in the DPS6 PowerShift gearbox, paired with P090A for an open circuit and P090D for a short to power.",[["h","Clutch B actuator motor with internal short to ground"],["m","Failed TCM output stage (Ford program 14M02 lists this code)"],["m","Chafed motor wire touching ground"]],["Check engine light on","Even gears or reverse unavailable","Limp mode or no drive"],["medium",[150,1200]],"u"],"P090D":["Clutch B Actuator Control Circuit High","Set when the control circuit of the clutch B actuator shows a high state, i.e. a motor or control line is shorted to battery voltage.",[["h","Failed clutch B actuator motor (Ford program 20B23)"],["m","Motor or control wire shorted to a supply line"],["l","Faulty motor driver in the TCM"]],["Check engine light on","Erratic or missed shifts and vibration","Limp mode, possibly no drive"],["medium",[150,900]],"u"],"P0910":["Gate Select Actuator Circuit/Open","Concerns the drive circuit of the gate select actuator, the motor or valve that moves the shift mechanism of an automated manual gearbox sideways from gate to gate. The code sets when the control module finds this output open or electrically faulty, for example when no current flows although the driver stage is switched on.",[["h","Open winding or worn brushes in the select motor"],["m","Broken actuator wire or terminal pushed out of the connector"],["m","Corroded actuator connector near the gearbox"],["l","Faulty actuator driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox cannot change between gates, only some gears available","Vehicle may stay in neutral and not drive off"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0911":["Gate Select Actuator Circuit Range/Performance","Plausibility fault in the drive circuit of the gate select actuator of an automated manual gearbox. The circuit is neither open nor shorted, but the current, voltage or response measured on it does not fit the command, for example a current that is too high or too low for the requested movement.",[["h","Worn select motor drawing abnormal current"],["m","Stiff select mechanism overloading the actuator"],["m","High resistance in actuator wiring or connector"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Slow, hesitant or aborted gear changes","Gearbox enters fallback mode with limited gears"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0912":["Gate Select Actuator Circuit Low","Set when the drive circuit of the gate select actuator of an automated manual gearbox shows a voltage that is too low for the current switching state, which usually means a short to ground on a motor or control line, or an internal short in the actuator. Many controllers then switch the affected output off to protect the driver stage.",[["h","Actuator wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in the select motor, e.g. from brush debris"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","No gate change possible, gearbox stays in current gear or neutral","Vehicle may not pull away"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0913":["Gate Select Actuator Circuit High","Set when the drive circuit of the gate select actuator of an automated manual gearbox shows a voltage that is too high for the current switching state. Usual causes are a short of a motor or control line to battery voltage, or a driver stage in the module that no longer switches off; depending on the circuit design an open load can also read high.",[["h","Actuator wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Open motor winding read as high voltage by the driver stage"],["l","Driver stage in the transmission control module stuck on"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox stops changing gates or holds one gear","Actuator may run noisily or get hot"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0914":["Gear Shift Position Circuit A","Circuit A of the gear shift position sensor reports how far the shift mechanism of an automated manual gearbox has moved in the shift direction, i.e. whether a gear is engaged, in neutral or in between.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector at the shift sensor or actuator unit"],["m","Damaged sensor wiring near the gearbox"],["m","Faulty gear shift position sensor"],["l","Faulty sensor input or reference supply in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox stops shifting or stays in neutral","Engine may not start because neutral cannot be confirmed"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0915":["Gear Shift Position Circuit A Range/Performance","Plausibility fault of the gear shift position signal on circuit A in an automated manual gearbox. The voltage stays within its electrical limits, but the reading does not fit: the signal does not change while the shift actuator moves, or the engaged position does not match the learned gear positions.",[["h","Worn or drifting shift position sensor, redundant tracks disagree"],["m","Bent sensor bracket or displaced magnet on the shift rod"],["m","Gear positions not relearned after actuator or TCM replacement"],["l","High resistance at connector pins distorting the signal"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox drops to neutral while driving","Gears refused or engaged only after a delay"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0916":["Gear Shift Position Circuit A Low","Set when the gear shift position signal on circuit A of an automated manual gearbox stays below its lowest valid value for longer than a calibrated time. A short to ground on the signal line, a lost sensor supply or an internal sensor defect are the usual causes; depending on the sensor design a broken signal wire reads low as well.",[["h","Signal wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor reference supply interrupted or shorted"],["m","Internal defect of the shift position sensor"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","No gear changes possible or gearbox stays in neutral","Engine may not start because neutral cannot be confirmed"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0917":["Gear Shift Position Circuit A High","Set when the gear shift position signal on circuit A of an automated manual gearbox stays above its highest valid value for longer than a calibrated time. The signal line may be shorted to the sensor supply or to battery voltage, the sensor ground may be open, or the sensor itself may be faulty.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to sensor supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open sensor ground or corroded ground pin"],["m","Internal fault of the shift position sensor"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox stays in one gear or in neutral","Warning message about the transmission on the dashboard"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0918":["Gear Shift Position Circuit A Intermittent","Flags a gear shift position signal on circuit A that drops out or jumps for short moments in an automated manual gearbox. The module sees implausible steps or brief losses that are too short or too irregular for a lasting low or high fault.",[["h","Connector at the shift sensor or actuator unit losing contact under vibration"],["m","Chafed or partially broken sensor wire near the gearbox"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Shift position sensor with an internal contact or track fault"]],["Warning light comes and goes","Gear changes abort now and then or the gearbox drops to neutral","Fault often appears on rough roads or in wet weather"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0919":["Gear Shift Position Control Error","Set when the control module of an automated manual gearbox drives the shift actuator towards a gear, but the measured shift position does not reach the target within the allowed time and tolerance. Sensor and actuator circuits can be electrically intact; the fault concerns the closed loop as a whole.",[["h","Stiff or jammed shift rail, shift fork or synchroniser"],["m","Worn shift motor or leaking shift cylinder that no longer delivers full force"],["m","Low pressure in the hydraulic supply on hydraulically actuated units"],["l","Shift positions not relearned after actuator or module replacement"]],["Transmission warning light on","Gear does not engage or engages only after a delay","Gearbox remains in neutral or locks in one gear"],["hard",[150,2500]],"c"],"P091A":["Gear Shift Position Circuit B","Circuit B is a second channel reporting the shift position of an automated manual gearbox, for instance a redundant sensor track or a separate sensor on another shift rail. This general circuit code sets on an electrical fault on that signal that matches none of the more specific B variants.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector at the shift position sensor"],["m","Damaged signal, supply or ground wire of sensor circuit B"],["m","Faulty shift position sensor B"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the transmission control module"]],["Transmission or engine warning light on","Slower or restricted gear changes in fallback mode","Gearbox may hold a gear or stay in neutral"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P091B":["Gear Shift Position Circuit B Range/Performance","Plausibility fault of the shift position signal on circuit B in an automated manual gearbox. The voltage stays within its electrical limits, yet the value does not fit: it disagrees with circuit A, does not change while the shift actuator moves, or does not match the learned gear positions.",[["h","Worn or drifting shift position sensor, channels A and B disagree"],["m","Bent sensor bracket or displaced magnet on the shift rail"],["m","Gear positions not relearned after actuator or module replacement"],["l","Increased contact resistance in the sensor circuit"]],["Transmission warning light on","Harsh or aborted gear changes","Fallback mode with fewer available gears"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P091C":["Gear Shift Position Circuit B Low","Set when the shift position signal on circuit B of an automated manual gearbox stays below its lowest valid value for longer than a calibrated time. Usually the signal line is shorted to ground, the sensor supply is missing, or the sensor has failed internally.",[["h","Signal wire of circuit B shorted to ground"],["m","Open or shorted sensor supply line"],["m","Faulty shift position sensor B"]],["Transmission or engine warning light on","Restricted gear changes or a held gear","Engine start may be blocked if neutral cannot be confirmed"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P091D":["Gear Shift Position Circuit B High","Set when the shift position signal on circuit B of an automated manual gearbox stays above its highest valid value for longer than a calibrated time. The signal line may be shorted to sensor supply or battery voltage, the sensor ground may be open, or the sensor may be faulty.",[["h","Signal wire of circuit B shorted to supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open sensor ground"],["m","Faulty shift position sensor B"]],["Transmission or engine warning light on","Restricted gear changes or a held gear","Engine start may be blocked if neutral cannot be confirmed"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P0920":["Gear Shift Forward Actuator Circuit/Open","Concerns the drive circuit that moves the shift mechanism of an automated manual gearbox in the forward shift direction. Depending on design this is one of two solenoid valves on a double-acting shift cylinder or one rotation direction of an electric shift motor.",[["h","Loose, corroded or unplugged connector at the shift actuator or valve"],["m","Broken drive wire between module and actuator"],["m","Open solenoid coil or motor winding in the actuator"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Transmission warning light on","Some gears cannot be selected, the gearbox skips them or stays in neutral","Vehicle may only move off in a limited set of gears"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0921":["Gear Shift Forward Actuator Circuit Range/Performance","Plausibility fault in the drive circuit of the forward shift actuator of an automated manual gearbox. The circuit is neither open nor shorted, but current, voltage or the resulting movement do not fit the command, for example a current that is too high for the requested stroke or a shift position that hardly changes.",[["h","Sticking shift valve, worn shift motor or stiff shift linkage"],["m","Solenoid or motor resistance out of specification"],["m","Increased contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["l","Insufficient hydraulic pressure on hydraulic units"]],["Transmission warning light on","Slow, harsh or aborted gear changes in one direction","Fallback mode with fewer available gears"],["hard",[150,2000]],"c"],"P0922":["Gear Shift Forward Actuator Circuit Low","Set when the drive circuit of the forward shift actuator of an automated manual gearbox shows a voltage that is too low for the current switching state. This usually points to a short to ground on a valve or motor line, or to an internal short in the actuator.",[["h","Drive wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in solenoid coil or shift motor"],["l","Water or corrosion bridging pins in the connector"]],["Transmission warning light on","Certain gears can no longer be engaged","Gearbox may stay in neutral or hold one gear"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0923":["Gear Shift Forward Actuator Circuit High","Set when the drive circuit of the forward shift actuator of an automated manual gearbox shows a voltage that is too high for the current switching state. Typical reasons are a valve or motor line shorted to battery voltage, or a driver stage in the module that no longer switches off; depending on circuit design an open load can also read high.",[["h","Drive wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Driver stage in the control module stuck on"],["l","Open coil or winding read as high on some circuit designs"]],["Transmission warning light on","Gearbox locks in one gear or refuses to shift","Possible grinding when a gear is pushed unintentionally"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0924":["Gear Shift Reverse Actuator Circuit/Open","Concerns the drive circuit that moves the shift mechanism of an automated manual gearbox in the reverse shift direction, i.e. opposite to the forward actuator.",[["h","Loose, corroded or unplugged connector at the shift actuator or valve"],["m","Broken drive wire between module and actuator"],["m","Open solenoid coil or motor winding in the actuator"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Transmission warning light on","Some gears cannot be selected or the gearbox stays in neutral","Gear changes out of the current gear may fail"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0925":["Gear Shift Reverse Actuator Circuit Range/Performance","Plausibility fault in the drive circuit of the reverse shift actuator of an automated manual gearbox, the actuator that moves the shift mechanism opposite to the forward direction. The circuit is neither open nor shorted, but current, voltage or the resulting shift movement do not match the command.",[["h","Sticking shift valve, worn shift motor or stiff shift linkage"],["m","Solenoid or motor resistance out of specification"],["m","Increased contact resistance in connector or wiring"],["l","Insufficient hydraulic pressure on hydraulic units"]],["Transmission warning light on","Slow, harsh or aborted gear changes in one direction","Fallback mode with fewer available gears"],["hard",[150,2000]],"c"],"P0926":["Gear Shift Reverse Actuator Circuit Low","Set when the drive circuit of the reverse shift actuator of an automated manual gearbox, the one moving the shift mechanism opposite to the forward direction, shows a voltage that is too low for the current switching state. This usually means a valve or motor line shorted to ground or an internal short in the actuator; the module often switches the output off to protect itself.",[["h","Drive wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in solenoid coil or shift motor"],["l","Water or corrosion bridging pins in the connector"]],["Transmission warning light on","Certain gears can no longer be engaged","Gearbox may stay in neutral or hold one gear"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0927":["Gear Shift Reverse Actuator Circuit High","Set when the drive circuit of the reverse shift actuator of an automated manual gearbox shows a voltage that is too high for the current switching state. Common reasons are a valve or motor line shorted to battery voltage or a driver stage that no longer switches off; on some circuit designs an open load also reads high.",[["h","Drive wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Driver stage in the control module stuck on"],["l","Open coil or winding read as high on some circuit designs"]],["Transmission warning light on","Gearbox locks in one gear or refuses to shift","Possible grinding when a gear is pushed unintentionally"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0928":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit A/Open","The module that drives the shift lock (brake transmission shift interlock, BTSI) sees no continuity on control circuit A, so it cannot reliably release the selector from Park. FCA/Chrysler uses the code as P0928-00 BTSI Control: bulletin 08-072-14 (2013-14 Ram 1500, 2014 Durango with 8-speed) and 21-034-15 (2015 Chrysler 300 rotary shifter) name it when the selector will not leave Park or the knob turns without the brake applied; the cure there is a new electronic shifter.",[["h","Internal fault in the electronic shifter module, often logged together with P0607 (documented by FCA)"],["m","Shift lock solenoid coil open"],["m","Broken wire, poorly crimped or pushed-out terminal on the shift lock circuit"]],["Selector lever will not move out of Park even with the brake pressed","Problem may disappear after the ignition is switched off and the modules go to sleep","Rotary or electronic shifter can be moved without the brake applied"],["medium",[60,900]],"c"],"P0929":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit A Range/Performance","Set when the shift lock actuator on circuit A is electrically present but does not respond as commanded, for example it fails to reach the locked or released position. Ford stores it in the gear shift module (GSM): SSM 51057 and 53039 list P0929:77 (commanded position not reachable) on Mach-E, Escape, Bronco Sport, Maverick and Transit, fixed by GSM software or replacement; TSB 25-2045 adds P0929:00, :07 and :9A on 2021-24 F-150 together with a selector that will not leave or enter Park, and checks pin 3 of connector C211.",[["h","Gear shift module software that misjudges the actuator position (Ford reprogramming bulletins)"],["m","Worn or binding lock actuator inside the shifter assembly"],["m","Poor pin fit or damaged pin at the shifter connector"]],["Wrench or transmission warning lamp on","Selector will not leave Park or will not go back into Park","Clicking from the shifter or a selector that does not stow or unstow"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P092A":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit B/Open","Reports an open on control circuit B of the shift lock. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and none says which lock is B.",[["h","Broken wire or loose terminal on circuit B at the shifter"],["m","Open coil in the second lock solenoid"],["l","Shifter connector not fully seated after console work"]],["Selector lever locked in one position although the brake is pressed","Lock no longer engages, lever moves freely without brake","Warning message about the shifter or transmission"],["medium",[40,700]],"c"],"P092B":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit B Range/Performance","Set when the actuator on shift lock circuit B is wired correctly but does not reach or report the commanded lock or release state. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Lock pin or actuator binding inside the shifter assembly"],["m","Shifter module software misjudging the actuator response"],["l","Weak battery or poor supply leaving the solenoid too little force"]],["Lever releases late or only after several tries","Lever stays locked in one position","Transmission or wrench warning lamp on"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P092C":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit B Low","The voltage on shift lock control circuit B is lower than expected, usually because the wire or the solenoid has a path to ground. No manufacturer documents this code publicly or names the lock behind circuit B.",[["h","Circuit B wire chafed to ground near the shifter"],["m","Shorted turns in the second lock solenoid"],["l","Liquid spilled into the shifter causing leakage to ground"]],["Lever locked in a position despite brake applied","Lock does not engage, lever moves without brake","Warning message about the shifter"],["medium",[40,700]],"c"],"P092D":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit B High","The module sees a higher voltage on shift lock circuit B than the commanded state allows, such as a short to supply or, with a low-side driver, an open coil. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Circuit B wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Poor crimp or loose terminal at the second lock solenoid"],["m","Open coil in the second lock solenoid"]],["Lever will not release from its locked position","Fault appears intermittently with vibration","Warning message about the shifter or transmission"],["medium",[40,700]],"c"],"P0930":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit A Low","The voltage on control circuit A of the shift lock solenoid stays below the expected level, typically a short to ground or a driver output pulled low. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Shift lock control wire chafed through to ground in the console or under the carpet"],["m","Shorted turns in the shift lock solenoid coil"],["l","Spilled liquid in the shifter area causing leakage to ground"]],["Selector lever stuck in Park although the brake is pressed","Selector can be moved out of Park without pressing the brake","Shift lock override slot needed to move the lever"],["medium",[40,600]],"c"],"P0931":["Gear Shift Lock Solenoid/Actuator Control Circuit A High","The control module reads a voltage on shift lock circuit A that is higher than the commanded state allows. FCA documents the code directly: in recall 16V-249 the Jefferson North plant found 2016 Jeep Grand Cherokee (WK) vehicles with active or stored P0931-00 whose selector would not release from Park.",[["h","Poorly crimped or loose terminal on the shift lock solenoid harness (FCA recall 16V-249)"],["m","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Open shift lock solenoid coil seen as high voltage by a low-side driver"]],["Selector lever will not release from Park","Lever may lock in Park or Neutral unexpectedly after stopping","Fault comes and goes with vibration or temperature"],["medium",[40,700]],"c"],"P0932":["Hydraulic Pressure Sensor Circuit","General electrical fault in the circuit of the sensor that reports hydraulic pressure in the gearbox, usually line pressure in an automatic or system pressure in an automated manual or dual-clutch unit. The module sees a signal it cannot use at all, without a clear low or high pattern.",[["h","Fault in the solenoid pack or valve body module that carries the pressure sensor (open solenoid, internal lead frame)"],["m","Poor crimp or solder joint at the sensor connector terminals"],["m","5 V reference or sensor ground interrupted"],["l","Transmission or powertrain control module fault"]],["Warning light or transmission warning on","Harsh engagements and firm shifts from maximum line pressure","Possible limp mode with limited gears"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0933":["Hydraulic Pressure Sensor Range/Performance","The pressure reading is electrically valid but does not fit the operating state. Chrysler RFE, RLE and TES transmissions set it when measured line pressure is more than 25 psi (172 kPa) above the commanded value while staying below the highest pressure ever used in that gear; Sonnax notes that a maximum-pressure condition from an open pressure control solenoid circuit can trigger it and mislead toward the sensor.",[["h","Open circuit or fault in the line pressure control solenoid causing real excess pressure"],["m","Pressure sensor drifting or reading offset"],["m","Worn or sticking valves in the valve body"],["l","Wrong fluid level or degraded fluid"]],["Harsh or firm engagements and shifts","Warning light on","Shift quality does not adapt and may stay rough"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0934":["Hydraulic Pressure Sensor Circuit Low","The pressure sensor signal sits below its valid window. Chrysler RFE transmissions feed the line pressure sensor with a 5 V reference and expect 0.35 to 4.75 V; P0934 sets when the signal stays at or below 0.35 V for 0.18 s, monitored continuously with the engine running.",[["h","Sensor signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","5 V reference open or shorted"],["m","Pressure sensor or the solenoid pack carrying it failed"],["l","Control module fault"]],["Warning light on","Harsh shifts due to default maximum pressure","Possible limp mode"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0935":["Hydraulic Pressure Sensor Circuit High","The pressure sensor signal sits above its valid window. On Chrysler RFE transmissions the line pressure sensor normally reads between 0.35 and 4.75 V; P0935 sets when the signal stays at or above 4.75 V for 0.18 s with the engine running.",[["h","Sensor ground open or poor terminal contact"],["m","Signal wire shorted to 5 V reference or battery voltage"],["m","Pressure sensor or solenoid pack failed internally"],["l","Control module fault"]],["Warning light on","Harsh shifts and engagements","Possible limp mode"],["medium",[100,900]],"c"],"P0936":["Hydraulic Pressure Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The hydraulic pressure signal drops out or jumps for short periods, too briefly or irregularly for a lasting low or high fault. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Poor crimp or solder joint at the sensor connector"],["m","Chafed harness making intermittent contact"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["l","Sensor with an internal intermittent fault"]],["Warning light comes and goes","Occasional harsh or erratic shifts"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P0937":["Hydraulic Oil Temperature Sensor Circuit","General electrical fault in the circuit of the temperature sensor for the hydraulic fluid of an automated gearbox, without a clear low or high pattern. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Connector at the temperature sensor loose, corroded or oil-soaked"],["m","Damaged sensor wiring near the gearbox or cooler lines"],["m","Temperature sensor failed"]],["Gearbox warning light on","Rougher shifts or clutch engagement, especially when cold"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0938":["Hydraulic Oil Temperature Sensor Range/Performance","By J2012 wording the hydraulic oil temperature reading is electrically valid but implausible, for example frozen, rising too slowly after a cold start or disagreeing with other temperature values. Ferrari uses P0938 on its DCT for the radiator outlet and gearbox inlet oil temperature sensor, but describes it in its bulletin as no signal, an electrical fault rather than a plausibility check; the code is read in the NCR gearbox ECU.",[["h","Temperature sensor drifting or reacting sluggishly"],["m","Signal lost through connector or wiring fault (as in Ferrari's no-signal case)"],["l","Real temperature anomaly from a cooler or thermostat fault"]],["Gearbox warning light on","Shift and clutch quality inconsistent with operating temperature"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0939":["Hydraulic Oil Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","The hydraulic oil temperature sensor signal sits below its valid window. With the usual NTC thermistor, a low voltage corresponds to an implausibly hot reading and typically comes from a signal wire shorted to ground or a sensor shorted internally.",[["h","Sensor signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Temperature sensor shorted internally"],["l","Control module input fault"]],["Gearbox warning or overheat message","Reduced performance or altered shift behaviour"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P093A":["Hydraulic Accumulator Solenoid Circuit/Open","The control circuit of the solenoid that opens or locks a hydraulic accumulator in the gearbox is open. Such accumulators store pressurised fluid, for example to keep clutches applied during engine stop-start, as GM's patents on a stop-start accumulator and an accumulator with solenoid valve describe.",[["h","Accumulator solenoid coil open"],["m","Connector unplugged or wire broken between module and solenoid"],["m","Fault in the internal transmission harness or lead frame"],["l","Output driver in the control module failed"]],["Warning light on (after repeated detection)","Engine stop-start no longer active","Jolt or slip when driving off after an engine restart"],["hard",[150,1200]],"u"],"P093B":["Hydraulic Accumulator Solenoid Circuit Low","The control circuit of the hydraulic accumulator solenoid reads low, usually a control wire shorted to ground or a coil with very low resistance. No manufacturer documents P093B publicly.",[["h","Solenoid control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Solenoid coil shorted internally"],["l","Output driver in the control module failed"]],["Warning light on (after repeated detection)","Engine stop-start no longer active","Harsh engagement after restart"],["hard",[150,1200]],"u"],"P093C":["Hydraulic Accumulator Solenoid Circuit High","The control circuit of the hydraulic accumulator solenoid reads high, typically a control wire shorted to battery voltage so the driver cannot switch the valve as commanded. No manufacturer documents P093C publicly.",[["h","Solenoid control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Damaged internal transmission harness or lead frame"],["l","Output driver in the control module failed"]],["Warning light on (after repeated detection)","Engine stop-start no longer active","Jolt when pulling away after a restart"],["hard",[150,1200]],"u"],"P093D":["Hydraulic Accumulator Solenoid Performance","The accumulator solenoid circuit is electrically sound, but the accumulator system does not deliver the expected hydraulic result. No manufacturer documents P093D publicly.",[["h","Accumulator solenoid stuck open (drains) or closed (no fill)"],["m","Accumulator piston or bore leaking"],["m","Leakage at clutch seals or a worn valve body"],["l","Missing or misplaced check ball after a rebuild"]],["Engine stop-start disabled","Lurch or bang when the engine restarts","Firm shifts with adaptive learning frozen"],["shop_only",[250,2500]],"u"],"P0940":["Hydraulic Oil Temperature Sensor Circuit High","The hydraulic oil temperature sensor signal sits above its valid window. With an NTC thermistor this matches an extremely cold reading, typically from an open circuit, an unplugged connector, a broken ground or a short to a voltage source.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire, or connector unplugged"],["m","Temperature sensor open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to a voltage source"]],["Gearbox warning light on","Shifts or clutch engagements feel rough once warm"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0941":["Hydraulic Oil Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent","The hydraulic oil temperature signal jumps or drops out briefly, too short or irregular for a lasting low or high fault. Since fluid temperature changes slowly, a sudden step is a clear sign of an electrical problem rather than a real temperature change.",[["h","Loose contact at the sensor connector under vibration or heat"],["m","Chafed wiring near hot gearbox or cooler parts"],["l","Sensor with an intermittent internal fault"]],["Warning light comes and goes","Shift quality varies unpredictably"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0942":["Hydraulic Pressure Unit","General fault of the unit that generates and stores hydraulic pressure for an automated gearbox or its auxiliary oil supply: motor-driven pump, accumulator and pressure control. Toyota sets P0942 on the 2001 to 2005 MR2 Spyder sequential gearbox together with the SMT warning light and a buzzing pump, or with gears that can no longer be changed; fluid seeping from the reservoir cap or the seal of the hydraulic power unit is a known cause, fixed with new seal rings.",[["h","Fluid loss at the reservoir cap or hydraulic power unit seals"],["m","Worn pump motor or faulty pump relay"],["m","Damaged wiring, connectors or power supply of the pump"],["l","Transmission control module fault"]],["Transmission or gearbox warning light on","Pump buzzing or running for long periods","Gears cannot be changed or shifts are delayed"],["hard",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0943":["Hydraulic Pressure Unit Cycling Period Too Short","In a hydraulic pressure unit the pump charges an accumulator up to a cut-out pressure and switches off until pressure falls to the cut-in point. By J2012 wording this code means the pump switches on again too soon, so the interval between charging cycles is shorter than expected.",[["h","Accumulator has lost its gas precharge"],["h","Internal leakage at valves, seals or actuator cylinders"],["m","Faulty pressure switch or pressure sensor giving wrong cut-in point"],["l","Low or aerated hydraulic fluid"]],["Pump can be heard switching on unusually often","Warning light on","Slower or harsher gear changes"],["hard",[200,2500]],"c"],"P0944":["Hydraulic Pressure Unit Loss of Pressure","The pump runs but the expected hydraulic pressure does not build or collapses. Stellantis names it Loss of Hydraulic Pump Prime: a 2023 STAR case lists it with P0868 and a slow engagement after the vehicle has stood, both set as the only transmission codes with the MIL on, and the fix was a new transmission; an FCA defect file from 2015 also shows it beside line pressure low.",[["h","Pump loses prime (air drawn in through filter, seal or strainer pipe)"],["m","Blocked oil strainer or suction tube"],["m","Worn or failed pump (mechanical or electric)"],["l","Leaking check valve or internal leak in the valve body"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Slow engagement after the vehicle has been parked","Auto stop-start not available or engine restarts at once"],["shop_only",[150,6000]],"c"],"P0945":["Hydraulic Pump Relay/Control Circuit/Open","The control module drives the motor of the hydraulic pump through a relay or a direct output. This code reports a general circuit fault or an open in that control path, so the pump cannot be switched reliably.",[["h","Pump relay coil open or relay not seated"],["m","Open control wire or loose terminal between module and relay"],["l","Failed output stage in the control module"]],["Warning light on","Pump does not run, pressure is not built","Gears cannot be changed or auto stop-start disabled"],["medium",[30,500]],"c"],"P0946":["Hydraulic Pump Relay/Control Circuit Range/Performance","The pump control circuit is electrically intact, but its behaviour does not match the command, for example a pump that keeps running after being switched off, or one that switches but is not followed by the expected feedback. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Relay contacts welded or sticking"],["m","Pump supply or control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["l","Faulty pump state feedback or control module"]],["Pump keeps running after switch-off","Warning light on","Auto stop-start not available"],["medium",[30,500]],"c"],"P0947":["Hydraulic Pump Relay/Control Circuit Low","The voltage on the control line of the hydraulic pump relay or driver is lower than the module expects for the commanded state. Usually the line is shorted to ground or the relay coil is shorted, so the output may be overloaded and the pump runs when it should not, or the driver shuts down.",[["h","Relay control wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Shorted relay coil"],["l","Damaged output stage in the control module"]],["Warning light on","Pump runs continuously or not at all","Gearbox in limp mode or stop-start disabled"],["medium",[30,500]],"c"],"P0948":["Hydraulic Pump Relay/Control Circuit High","The control line of the hydraulic pump relay or driver shows a higher voltage than expected, typically because it is shorted to battery voltage or the relay coil is open on a low-side driver. The pump then cannot be switched on, or the module sees it as active when it is not.",[["h","Relay control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Open relay coil seen as high level by the driver"],["l","Control module fault"]],["Warning light on","Pump does not start","Gear changes not possible or stop-start disabled"],["medium",[30,500]],"c"],"P0949":["Auto Shift Manual Adaptive Learning Not Complete","Status code for an automatically shifted manual gearbox: the control unit has not yet finished learning its adaptation values, above all the clutch touch point and the end positions of the shift actuators. Such learning runs after a new control unit, clutch or actuator, after a software update and sometimes after the battery was disconnected; Hyundai and Kia patents show how the touch point is found and corrected from shaft speed changes.",[["h","Relearn not run after clutch, actuator or control unit replacement"],["m","Adaptation values erased by software update or battery disconnection"],["m","Learning aborted by low voltage or worn clutch outside the learn window"]],["Jerky launch or rough shifts","Gearbox warning or message to perform teach-in","Restricted gear selection until learning completes"],["medium",[50,250]],"c"],"P094A":["Hydraulic Pump Driver Performance","Concerns the electronic driver that powers the motor of a hydraulic or electric oil pump, often a separate pump controller. The driver reports, or the module infers, that it cannot deliver the commanded speed or current although the circuit is not open or shorted.",[["h","Internal fault of the pump controller or driver stage"],["m","Pump motor drawing excess current (worn bearings, fluid in motor)"],["m","High resistance in supply or ground of the driver"]],["Warning light on","Auto stop-start disabled","Delayed engagement or slower shifts"],["hard",[200,1500]],"u"],"P094B":["Hydraulic Pump Stuck On","The hydraulic pump keeps running although the module has switched it off, or it never reaches its cut-out point. Causes are a welded relay or shorted driver, or a pump that cannot reach cut-out pressure because of leakage.",[["h","Pump relay contacts welded closed"],["m","Leakage or lost accumulator charge keeps pressure below cut-out"],["m","Shorted driver stage or supply wire shorted to battery voltage"]],["Pump audible after the vehicle is switched off","Discharged battery","Warning light on"],["medium",[30,1500]],"u"],"P094C":["Hydraulic Pump Performance/Stuck Off","The pump is commanded on but does not deliver: it does not turn, turns too slowly or produces too little pressure. The module judges this from pressure or speed feedback.",[["h","Air in the pump after service or a suction leak"],["m","Seized or failed pump motor"],["m","Blocked strainer or leaking check valve"],["l","Supply or relay fault stopping the pump"]],["Warning light on","Engine restarts at once or stop-start not available","Delayed engagement or lost drive"],["hard",[100,1800]],"u"],"P094D":["Hydraulic Pump Over Temperature","The hydraulic or electric oil pump, or its integrated controller, has become too hot. The temperature is usually measured on the controller board and the pump is derated or switched off to protect it.",[["h","Pump running too long because of leakage or a stuck relay"],["m","Defective pump controller board"],["l","Very hot transmission fluid or blocked cooling"]],["Warning light on","Stop-start temporarily disabled","Burning smell near the pump in severe cases"],["hard",[200,1500]],"u"],"P094E":["Hydraulic Pump Supply Voltage Circuit/Open","Fault in the power feed of the hydraulic or electric oil pump: fuse, supply wire, connector or relay output. The module sees missing or implausible supply at the pump or its controller.",[["h","Blown pump fuse"],["m","Open or corroded supply wire or connector"],["l","Weak battery or poor main ground lowering pump supply voltage"]],["Warning light on","Pump does not run","Stop-start disabled or gearbox in limp mode"],["medium",[10,600]],"u"],"P0950":["Auto Shift Manual Control Circuit","General electrical fault in the circuit that carries the driver's manual up and down requests to the transmission control, as used by tip-shift gates on automatics and by automated manual gearboxes. A Chrysler patent shows the classic design: a transverse gate beside the drive positions with a proximity switch that signals upshift on one side and downshift on the other.",[["h","Failed up/down switch or sensor in the selector lever or paddles"],["m","Damaged wiring or corroded connector at the shifter (e.g. after spilled liquid)"],["l","Clockspring or steering column wiring fault on paddle-shift vehicles"]],["Manual or tip shifting does not respond","Gear display or warning message","Automatic mode still works normally"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0951":["Auto Shift Manual Control Circuit Range/Performance","Plausibility fault in the circuit that reports the driver's manual upshift and downshift requests, typically the tip gate of an automatic selector lever or steering wheel paddles. The signal is electrically valid but makes no sense, for example both requests active together or a request held far longer than any tap.",[["h","Sticking or worn up/down tip switch, request signal stays active"],["m","Displaced magnet or damaged Hall sensor board in the selector lever"],["m","Spilled liquid or dirt in the shifter causing cross-talk between contacts"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission or shifter control module"]],["Tip shifting or paddles do not respond or respond erratically","Unwanted gear changes in manual mode","Warning message, automatic mode keeps working"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0952":["Auto Shift Manual Control Circuit Low","The control unit sees the voltage on the manual up/down request circuit below its expected window, typically a signal wire shorted to ground or a switch stuck in the grounded state. In many tip gates the moving contact switches to ground, as a Nissan patent shows, so a short to ground can mimic a permanently held request; a Volkswagen patent describes checking the Hall tip sensors for shorts and breaks while the lever is in P or R.",[["h","Signal wire of the up/down circuit chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Tip switch stuck in the closed position"],["m","Moisture or spilled drink in the shifter creating a leakage path to ground"],["l","Low supply voltage to a Hall-type tip sensor"]],["Manual tip shifting inoperative","Gear indicator shows no manual gear or a warning message","Normal automatic driving in D still possible"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0953":["Auto Shift Manual Control Circuit High","Voltage on the manual up/down request circuit lies above its expected window. Common reasons are a signal wire shorted to battery voltage or, where the input is pulled up internally, a broken wire or a switch that never closes.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to a voltage-carrying wire in the shifter harness"],["m","Broken wire or unplugged connector on a pulled-up input"],["m","Missing ground of the tip switch or Hall sensor"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Tip gate or paddles have no effect","Warning or message about manual mode","Automatic shifting continues normally"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0954":["Auto Shift Manual Control Circuit Intermittent","The manual up/down request signal drops out or flickers for short moments, so a single tap may arrive twice, late or not at all. Unlike P0952 and P0953 the voltage is not stuck outside its window; it jumps between states without a matching lever movement.",[["h","Loose or spread pins in the selector lever connector"],["m","Harness broken internally where it moves with the lever or steering column"],["m","Worn or oxidised tip switch contacts"]],["Manual shifts occasionally ignored or executed twice","Fault comes and goes, often over bumps","Manual mode temporarily switched off"],["medium",[30,450]],"c"],"P0955":["Auto Shift Manual Mode Circuit","Fault in the circuit that tells the transmission control whether the driver has moved the selector lever from D into the manual gate. A Nissan patent shows a dedicated manual mode switch with two contacts on separate inputs, so a break or a short to ground can be told apart; a Hyundai patent uses a microswitch on the lever.",[["h","Failed manual mode switch or microswitch in the selector lever"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the selector lever assembly"],["m","Damaged wiring between selector lever and transmission control module"]],["Manual mode cannot be selected or is not displayed","Check engine or transmission warning light on","Driving in D remains possible"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0956":["Auto Shift Manual Mode Circuit Range/Performance","The manual mode signal is electrically valid but contradicts other information, for example manual mode reported while the range sensor reads P, R or N, or the mode switch never changing although the up/down requests arrive. A Hyundai patent uses a microswitch with two opposing contacts and treats identical or missing signals on both inputs as a defect; a Nissan patent likewise compares two contacts of the manual mode switch.",[["h","Mode switch or its actuating foot misaligned on the lever"],["m","Worn switch contacts giving contradictory states on both inputs"],["m","Transmission range sensor out of adjustment, disagrees with the mode signal"],["l","Worn selector lever bushings allowing side play"]],["Manual mode drops out or will not engage","Display shows manual mode while lever is in D, or the reverse","Warning lamp depending on manufacturer"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0957":["Auto Shift Manual Mode Circuit Low","Voltage in the manual mode circuit, the signal that reports the selector lever in the manual gate, is below the expected range. Mostly the signal line has a short to ground or the switch contact hangs in its grounded position.",[["h","Mode signal wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Mode switch stuck in the grounded position"],["m","Liquid or dirt in the selector lever housing"]],["Manual mode not available or permanently indicated","Warning lamp depending on manufacturer","Automatic driving in D unaffected"],["medium",[40,600]],"c"],"P0958":["Auto Shift Manual Mode Circuit High","The manual mode signal from the selector lever reads above its expected range. Possible causes are a short to supply voltage or, with an internally pulled-up input, an open circuit or missing switch ground that keeps the line high.",[["h","Broken mode signal wire or disconnected shifter plug"],["m","Missing ground at the mode switch"],["m","Mode signal shorted to a voltage-carrying wire"]],["Manual gate has no effect, gearbox stays in automatic mode","Warning lamp depending on manufacturer","No manual gear shown on the display"],["medium",[40,600]],"c"],"P0959":["Auto Shift Manual Mode Circuit Intermittent","The signal reporting the selector lever in the manual gate drops out or toggles briefly while the lever is not moved, so the transmission control switches between manual and automatic mode on its own. The voltage is not stuck out of range as with P0957 or P0958 but jumps.",[["h","Loose pins in the selector lever connector"],["m","Worn or bouncing mode microswitch"],["m","Harness damaged where it moves with the lever"]],["Manual mode switches on or off by itself","Gear display flickers between D and manual gear","Fault often appears on rough roads"],["medium",[30,600]],"c"],"P095A":["Gear Shift Position Circuit C","Circuit C is a third channel reporting the shift position inside an automated manual gearbox, for example a further sensor track or a sensor on another shift rail or selector shaft. This general circuit code sets on an electrical fault of that signal that fits none of the more specific C variants.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector at shift position sensor C"],["m","Damaged signal, supply or ground wire of sensor circuit C"],["m","Faulty shift position sensor C"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the transmission control module"]],["Transmission or engine warning light on","Slower or restricted gear changes in fallback mode","Gearbox may hold a gear or stay in neutral"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P095B":["Gear Shift Position Circuit C Range/Performance","Plausibility fault of the shift position signal on circuit C of an automated manual gearbox. The voltage is electrically acceptable, but the value does not fit: it contradicts the other position channels, stays put while the shift actuator moves, or misses the learned gear positions.",[["h","Worn or drifting shift position sensor C disagreeing with the other channels"],["m","Bent sensor bracket or displaced magnet on the shift rail"],["m","Gear positions not relearned after actuator or module replacement"],["l","Increased contact resistance in the sensor circuit"]],["Transmission warning light on","Harsh or aborted gear changes","Fallback mode with fewer available gears"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P095C":["Gear Shift Position Circuit C Low","The signal of shift position sensor C in an automated manual gearbox lies below its permitted voltage window. Typical reasons are a signal wire shorted to ground, a lost sensor supply or a sensor with an internal fault pulling its output down.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor C shorted to ground"],["m","Open or shorted 5 V supply of the sensor"],["m","Internally failed shift position sensor C"]],["Transmission or engine warning light on","Fallback mode with restricted gears","Gearbox may refuse to leave neutral"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P095D":["Gear Shift Position Circuit C High","Shift position sensor C of an automated manual gearbox delivers a voltage above its permitted window. This usually points to a signal wire shorted to the supply, a broken sensor ground or, with a pulled-up input, an interrupted signal line.",[["h","Signal wire of sensor C shorted to the 5 V supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open ground circuit of the sensor"],["m","Internally failed shift position sensor C"]],["Transmission or engine warning light on","Fallback mode with restricted gears","Jerky or aborted gear changes"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P0960":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Control Circuit/Open","Open-circuit fault in the driver circuit of pressure control solenoid A: the module measures an impedance far above the coil value, as if a wire, pin or winding were broken. Which solenoid carries the letter A depends on the gearbox.",[["h","Open solenoid coil inside the valve body or solenoid assembly"],["m","Loose, corroded or pushed-back pin at the transmission connector or pass-through"],["m","Broken wire between module and solenoid"],["l","Faulty output stage in the transmission control module"]],["Transmission warning or MIL on, fault message in the cluster","Very firm or harsh shifts due to maximum line pressure","Transmission stays in a fixed limp gear"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0961":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Control Circuit Range/Performance","Performance fault of pressure control solenoid A: the circuit is electrically intact, but the solenoid does not behave as commanded. On GM's six-speeds, where A is the line pressure solenoid, the Saab 9-4X 6T70 sets it when measured and commanded current differ for 5 s, GM's 2018 table after 4.4 of 5 s; both Type B, two trips.",[["h","Solenoid with internal electrical or mechanical fault, current no longer follows the command"],["m","Debris or varnish in the fluid sticking the solenoid or regulator valve"],["m","High resistance at connector or pass-through distorting the current"],["l","Low fluid level causing pressure errors (CVT pulley monitor)"]],["Harsh or erratic shifts","MIL or transmission warning after the second drive","Slipping or ratio flare on CVT applications"],["hard",[200,2500]],"c"],"P0962":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Control Circuit Low","Pressure control solenoid A circuit low: the driver sees a short to ground. Ford tests its line pressure (EPC) solenoid this way; a short there opens a high-side switch that cuts power to the shift solenoids and leaves park, reverse, neutral and one fixed forward gear at maximum line pressure.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and touching ground"],["m","Internally shorted solenoid coil"],["m","Transmission fluid or water in the connector bridging pins"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["MIL and transmission warning on immediately","Limp mode with only one forward gear and reverse","Harsh engagements, poor acceleration from standstill"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0963":["Pressure Control Solenoid A Control Circuit High","Pressure control solenoid A circuit high: the driver detects a short to voltage, and on GM's six-speeds also an open circuit, since those units have no separate open code. The Saab 9-4X 6T70 (line pressure solenoid) sets it after 5 s as Type B, GM's 2018 table after 4.4 of 5 s over two trips; the module then commands maximum line pressure and freezes adaptations.",[["h","Open coil or high-resistance connection (reported as high on GM six-speeds)"],["m","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Damaged or contaminated connector at the transmission"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["Firm or harsh shifts at maximum line pressure","Warning light, on some vehicles only after two drives or not at all","Shift adaptation no longer improves"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0964":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Control Circuit/Open","Open circuit in the driver of pressure control solenoid B. What B controls differs by gearbox: GM's 8-speed uses it for clutch 2 (CB12345R), the 9-speed for CB29 and the 10-speed for CB128910R, Allison 1000 for pressure control solenoid 2, and GM's dual-clutch gearbox for the even-clutch pressure valve.",[["h","Open solenoid coil"],["m","Poor contact at the transmission connector or internal harness"],["m","Broken control wire"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["MIL and transmission warning on","Certain gears missing or limp mode","Dual-clutch gearbox drives on one sub-gearbox only"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0965":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Control Circuit Range/Performance","Performance fault of pressure control solenoid B: wiring is intact, but current or resulting pressure does not match the command. In the Saab 9-4X 6T70, solenoid 2 feeds the 3-5-reverse clutch; a current mismatch for 5 s sets a Type C code without MIL and freezes that clutch's adaptations, and GM's 2016 six-speed table also lists it without MIL.",[["h","Worn or internally faulty solenoid, current no longer matches the command"],["m","Debris sticking the solenoid or its regulator valve"],["m","Internal leak in the CVT primary pulley circuit"],["l","High resistance in connector or pass-through"]],["Rough or delayed 3rd, 5th or reverse engagement","CVT ratio fluctuates, engine speed flares","Often no warning light on six-speed applications"],["hard",[200,2500]],"c"],"P0966":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Control Circuit Low","Pressure control solenoid B circuit low, meaning a short to ground in the driver circuit. On GM's six-speeds and the Saab 9-4X 6T70, B is clutch pressure solenoid 2 for the 3-5-reverse clutch; a short lasting 0.3 s (GM 2018 table) or 1 s (Saab) sets a Type A code with a fixed limp mode: reverse and 5th gear, maximum line pressure, converter clutch off.",[["h","Chafed control wire touching ground"],["m","Internally shorted solenoid in the valve body assembly"],["m","Fluid or moisture in the connector"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["MIL on immediately","Limp mode with reverse and one forward gear","No torque converter lock-up, higher fuel consumption"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0967":["Pressure Control Solenoid B Control Circuit High","Pressure control solenoid B circuit high: a short to voltage, and on GM six-speeds also an open, since they have no P0964. For the Saab 9-4X 6T70, where B is clutch pressure solenoid 2 for the 3-5-reverse clutch, the module needs 1 s, stores a Type A code and limits the transmission to reverse and 3rd gear at maximum line pressure; GM's 2018 six-speed table uses 0.3 s and one trip.",[["h","Open coil or poor connection (reported as high on GM six-speeds)"],["m","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Damaged or contaminated transmission connector"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["MIL on, limp mode in a fixed gear","Harsh engagements at maximum line pressure","Dual-clutch gearbox loses the even gears"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0968":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Control Circuit/Open","Open circuit in the driver of pressure control solenoid C. GM's 8-speed assigns C to clutch 3 (C13567), the 9-speed to CB38 and the 10-speed to C23457910; an impedance above 200 kOhm for 0.3 s counts as open and sets a Type A code after one trip.",[["h","Open solenoid coil"],["m","Poor contact at the transmission connector or internal harness"],["m","Broken control wire"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["MIL and transmission warning on","Gears that need clutch C are unavailable","Limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0969":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Control Circuit Range/Performance","Performance fault of pressure control solenoid C. Publicly documented only for GM's front-drive six-speeds: on the Saab 9-4X 6T70, clutch pressure solenoid 3 feeds the low/reverse and 4-5-6 clutches through the R1/4-5-6 regulator valve.",[["h","Solenoid 3 with internal fault, current no longer matches the command"],["m","Debris sticking the solenoid or the R1/4-5-6 regulator valve"],["l","High resistance in the internal connection to the module"]],["Usually no warning light","Rougher shifts into 4th, 5th, 6th or reverse over time","Shift adaptation stops learning"],["hard",[200,2500]],"c"],"P0970":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Control Circuit Low","Pressure control solenoid C circuit low, a short to ground in the driver. On GM's six-speeds C is the C456/CBR1 solenoid; on the Saab 9-4X 6T70 it is clutch pressure solenoid 3 for the low/reverse and 4-5-6 clutches.",[["h","Internally shorted solenoid in the control solenoid valve assembly"],["m","Chafed control wire touching ground"],["m","Fluid or moisture in the connector"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["MIL on immediately","Limp mode in reverse and one forward gear","Weak acceleration from standstill"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0971":["Pressure Control Solenoid C Control Circuit High","Pressure control solenoid C circuit high: a short to voltage, and on GM six-speeds also an open, as there is no separate open code there. The Saab 9-4X 6T70 (clutch pressure solenoid 3, low/reverse and 4-5-6 clutches) and GM's 2018 six-speed table set it after 1 s or 0.3 s as Type A, with limp mode in reverse and 5th gear at maximum line pressure.",[["h","Open coil or poor connection (reported as high on GM six-speeds)"],["m","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Damaged or loose transmission connector"],["l","Faulty output stage in the control module"]],["MIL on, limp mode in a fixed gear","Harsh engagements at maximum line pressure","Fault comes and goes with temperature or vibration"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0972":["Shift Solenoid A Control Circuit Range/Performance","Range/performance fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid A: the circuit is not plainly shorted, yet its electrical behaviour does not fit what the module commands. Today's large makers rarely use the code.",[["h","Open or high-resistance coil of shift solenoid A (Allison-type usage)"],["m","Loose or corroded terminal at the transmission connector or internal harness"],["m","Chafed or broken wire between module and solenoid"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Transmission falls back to a fixed default gear","Missing or abrupt shifts"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0973":["Shift Solenoid A Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid A, usually a short to ground. Ford lists it as SSA short to ground beside P0750 (open) for the 5R110W, 6R80, 6F and 6R140, checked by a driver feedback line within 0.5 to 5 s.",[["h","Control wire to solenoid A chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in solenoid A or the control solenoid assembly"],["m","Fluid or metal debris bridging pins at the transmission connector"],["l","Faulty low-side driver in the transmission or powertrain module"]],["MIL on after the first faulty trip on many vehicles","Limp mode with only a few gears, often reverse plus one forward gear","Harsh engagements due to maximum line pressure"],["hard",[100,1600]],"c"],"P0974":["Shift Solenoid A Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid A. What it covers depends on the maker.",[["h","Open coil or broken wire where the maker reports opens under the high code (GM six-speed)"],["m","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Spread or corroded pins at the transmission connector"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["MIL on, on GM six-speeds only after a second faulty trip","First gear locked out, start-off in a higher gear","Missing or wrong shifts, possibly limp mode"],["hard",[100,1600]],"c"],"P0975":["Shift Solenoid B Control Circuit Range/Performance","Range/performance fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid B. Three documented uses differ widely.",[["h","Poorly crimped terminals causing intermittent high resistance (FCA 9-speed recall)"],["m","Open or high-resistance coil of shift solenoid B"],["m","Outdated transmission software that misreads harness resistance"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Unexpected shift to neutral or loss of drive (FCA 9-speed)","Fixed default gear or harsh shifts"],["hard",[0,1500]],"c"],"P0976":["Shift Solenoid B Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault, normally a ground short, in the control circuit of shift solenoid B. On GM six-speeds SS B is the mode 3 multiplex valve; the 2016 table sets P0976 after 1.2 of 1.5 s without lighting the MIL, while the Saab 9-4X 6T70 version needs 2 s, is Type B and raises line pressure.",[["h","Solenoid B control wire shorted to ground or housing"],["m","Internal short in solenoid B or its valve body assembly"],["m","Moisture or debris bridging pins at the connector"],["l","Faulty driver in the transmission control module"]],["Warning light or stored code, on some GM six-speeds without MIL","Firm shifts due to raised line pressure","Gears missing or limp mode"],["hard",[100,1600]],"c"],"P0977":["Shift Solenoid B Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid B. GM six-speeds put an open or a power short of the mode 3 multiplex valve here, 1.2 of 1.5 s; the 2016 table sets it without MIL, the 2019 table with MIL after one trip.",[["h","Open solenoid B coil or wire on GM six-speeds, where opens report as high"],["m","Control wire shorted to battery or a switched supply"],["m","Damaged pins or seals at the transmission pass-through connector"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["Limp mode with only reverse and one high forward gear","Weak acceleration from rest","MIL or transmission warning, depending on calibration"],["hard",[100,1600]],"c"],"P0978":["Shift Solenoid C Control Circuit Range/Performance","Range/performance fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid C. GM's 2007 Allison 1000 table used it for an open SS3 circuit, but only while the commanded gear is not reverse trim, 5th or 6th; the table even prints the label Circuit Low beside an open-circuit test, an apparent typo.",[["h","Intermittent high resistance at crimped harness terminals (FCA 9-speed)"],["m","Open coil of shift solenoid C (Allison-type usage)"],["m","Transmission software that predates the manufacturer's fix"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["MIL or transmission warning on","Sudden neutral or default gear while driving","Erratic or missing shifts"],["hard",[0,1500]],"c"],"P0979":["Shift Solenoid C Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault, usually a short to ground, in the control circuit of shift solenoid C. GM uses it mainly on the Allison 1000 six-speed of its heavy pickups and chassis cabs: in 2016 a ground short of 0.22 ohm or less, stricter than the 0.42 ohm for SS1 and SS2, or an open of 200 kOhm or more counts after 10 samples (250 ms, Type A).",[["h","Solenoid C control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short of solenoid C inside the valve body"],["m","Open coil on GM 2016 Allison calibrations, which report opens under this code"],["l","Faulty low-side driver in the control module"]],["MIL on after the first faulty trip","Transmission holds a default gear","Rough or delayed shifts involving the affected clutch"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P097A":["Shift Solenoid A Control Circuit/Open","Open-circuit fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid A, a code that later J2012 editions added; older lists still show P097A as reserved. GM is the only maker found that documents it: the 2022 to 2025 tables for the Allison 1000 six-speed in its heavy-duty trucks call it Shift Solenoid 1 Control Circuit Open.",[["h","Open coil of shift solenoid 1/A"],["h","Unplugged, corroded or pushed-out pin at the transmission connector"],["m","Broken wire in the internal or external transmission harness"],["l","Faulty low-side driver in the control module"]],["MIL on after one faulty trip","Transmission in a fixed default gear","Shifts involving solenoid A missing"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P097B":["Shift Solenoid B Control Circuit/Open","Open-circuit fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid B. Public use is again limited to GM's Allison 1000 six-speed in heavy-duty trucks, where the 2022 to 2025 tables label it Shift Solenoid 2 Control Circuit Open.",[["h","Open coil of shift solenoid 2/B"],["h","Loose or corroded terminal on the solenoid B circuit"],["m","Wire broken by vibration or heat near the transmission"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["MIL on after one faulty trip","Default gear operation, some ranges unavailable","Harsh or missing shifts"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P097C":["Shift Solenoid C Control Circuit/Open","Open-circuit fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid C. GM documents it for the Allison 1000 six-speed in its 2022 to 2025 heavy-duty tables as Shift Solenoid 3 Control Circuit Open, with the same intrusive low-side test as SS1 and SS2: three samples, 120 ms, battery 9 to 18 V, MIL after one trip.",[["h","Open coil of shift solenoid 3/C"],["h","Loose, wet or corroded pin in the transmission connector"],["m","Broken wire between module and solenoid C"],["l","Faulty low-side driver in the control module"]],["MIL on after one faulty trip","Upper gears unavailable or fixed default gear","Possible additional pressure switch code"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P097D":["Shift Solenoid D Control Circuit/Open","Open-circuit fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid D. No maker documents this code publicly.",[["h","Open coil of shift solenoid D"],["m","Disconnected or corroded terminal in the solenoid D circuit"],["m","Broken wire in the transmission harness"],["l","Faulty driver in the control module"]],["Warning light or MIL on","Missing gears that depend on solenoid D","Limp mode in a fixed gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P097E":["Shift Solenoid E Control Circuit/Open","Open-circuit fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid E. No manufacturer was found that documents this code.",[["h","Open coil of shift solenoid E"],["m","Poor contact at the solenoid E terminal or lead frame"],["m","Broken signal wire to solenoid E"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["Warning light or MIL on","Gears using solenoid E unavailable","Default gear operation"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P097F":["Shift Solenoid F Control Circuit/Open","Open-circuit fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid F. It is the only code in its family for letter F, since J2012 has no range/performance, low or high codes for F in this block.",[["h","Open coil of shift solenoid F"],["m","Loose or corroded terminal at the valve body connector"],["m","Broken wire between module and solenoid F"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Warning light or MIL on","Gears that need solenoid F unavailable","Limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P0980":["Shift Solenoid C Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid C, meaning a short to supply. GM documents it for the Allison 1000 six-speed: the 2007 table wants four consecutive power-short samples (100 ms, Type A) and skips the check in reverse trim, 5th and 6th gear; later tables (2016, 2018) look for 0.39 ohm or less to a supply over three counts within 75 ms, and from 2022 within 60 ms, MIL after one trip.",[["h","Solenoid C control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Shorted turns in solenoid C lowering its resistance"],["m","Adjacent pins bridged by fluid or debris in the connector"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["MIL on after the first faulty trip","Solenoid C stuck energized, wrong gear or default gear","Harsh shifts"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0981":["Shift Solenoid D Control Circuit Range/Performance","Range/performance fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid D. GM's Allison tables end at solenoid C, and Ford checks SSD only with P0765, P0982 and P0983, so the large North American users skip it.",[["h","Weak terminal crimps causing intermittent resistance (FCA recall S55)"],["m","Transmission software not yet updated by the recall"],["m","Solenoid D coil resistance out of range"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["Unexpected shift to neutral while driving (FCA 9-speed)","MIL or transmission warning on","Default gear or harsh shifts"],["hard",[0,1500]],"c"],"P0982":["Shift Solenoid D Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault, in practice a short to ground, in the control circuit of shift solenoid D. Ford is the documented user: its summaries list SSD with P0765 open, P0982 short to ground and P0983 short to power, checked continuously through the driver feedback within 0.5 to 5 s.",[["h","Solenoid D control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in solenoid D or the main control assembly"],["m","Damaged internal lead frame or connector inside the transmission"],["l","Faulty driver in the powertrain or transmission module"]],["MIL on and default gear operation","No engine braking in first or no reverse, depending on gearbox","Harsh shifts into upper gears"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0983":["Shift Solenoid D Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid D. Ford defines it narrowly as SSD short to power; the open circuit stays with P0765.",[["h","Solenoid D control wire shorted to a supply"],["m","Solenoid D with shorted windings"],["m","Conductive contamination between neighbouring pins"],["l","Faulty smart driver or output stage in the module"]],["MIL on, default gear","Clunk or tie-up feel if the low/reverse clutch applies unintentionally","Upper gears blocked"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0984":["Shift Solenoid E Control Circuit Range/Performance","Range/performance fault in the drive circuit of shift solenoid E. Ford checks SSE with P0770, P0985 and P0986 only, GM's documented tables never reach letter E, and even Ferrari's DCT list, which reuses most of its neighbours, skips P0984.",[["h","Range sensor harness with weak crimps (FCA 9-speed recall)"],["m","TCM software below the recall level"],["m","Internal transmission fault when the code returns after the update"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["Sudden neutral or loss of drive (FCA 9-speed)","MIL or transmission warning on","Limp mode"],["hard",[0,1500]],"c"],"P0985":["Shift Solenoid E Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault, usually a short to ground, in the control circuit of shift solenoid E. Ford lists it for SSE on the 5R110W and 6R140 and in its 2020–2023 gasoline and 2023 diesel summaries, beside P0770 for an open; for the 6R80 and 6F, whose SSE is the on/off multiplex solenoid, the 2012 text names only P0770.",[["h","Solenoid E control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short of solenoid E"],["m","Water or fluid in the transmission connector"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the transmission module"]],["MIL on after the fault is confirmed","All solenoids off, fixed fail-safe gear (Saab/Aisin)","Missing upper gears on Ford gearboxes"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0986":["Shift Solenoid E Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid E. Ford means a short to power on SSE; an open sets P0770 and a ground short P0985.",[["h","Solenoid E control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Open coil or wire where the maker reads it as high (Saab/Aisin)"],["m","Bent or corroded terminals at the solenoid E connector"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the control module"]],["MIL on, transmission in fail-safe gear","Tie-up or harsh shift if the controlled clutch stays applied","Adaptation frozen, shift quality worsens"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0987":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch E Circuit","General circuit fault of transmission fluid pressure sensor or switch E. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Failed pressure sensor or switch E"],["m","Open or shorted signal wire or poor connector contact"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Warning light depending on manufacturer","Harsher or less consistent shifts","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0988":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch E Circuit Range/Performance","Range/performance fault of transmission fluid pressure sensor or switch E: the signal is electrically valid but does not fit the commanded clutch state or the pressure expected from other inputs. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Damaged or delaminated switch film, switch reacts late or not at all"],["m","Debris in the fluid holding the switch or its regulator valve"],["m","Low fluid level or real pressure deviation in the clutch circuit"]],["Often no noticeable symptom at first","Shift quality worsens because adaptation stops","Warning light depending on manufacturer"],["hard",[200,1500]],"c"],"P0989":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch E Circuit Low","Pressure switch E reads low, meaning no pressure, when it should show pressure. GM's 6T70/6T75 family uses E for switch 5 on the R1/4-5-6 clutch regulator valve; the normally closed switch sees pressure while both the low/reverse and the 4-5-6 clutch are released.",[["h","Damaged or delaminated film in switch 5 of the valve body module"],["m","Debris keeping pressure away from the switch"],["m","R1/4-5-6 clutch regulator valve sticking"],["l","Low fluid level"]],["No warning light, code stored only","Shifts into 4th to 6th or reverse gradually less smooth","Occasional limp mode if the fault persists"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P0990":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch E Circuit High","Pressure switch E reads high, meaning pressure, when it should be vented. GM's 6T70/6T75 family uses E for switch 5 on the R1/4-5-6 clutch regulator valve; applying the low/reverse or 4-5-6 clutch moves that valve and should exhaust the switch.",[["h","Switch 5 stuck open by a damaged film or debris"],["m","R1/4-5-6 regulator valve not moving, switch stays pressurised"],["l","Signal circuit shorted to voltage or open inside the module"]],["No warning light, code stored only","Intermittent occurrence without noticeable symptoms","Shift quality declines over time"],["hard",[300,1500]],"c"],"P0991":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch E Circuit Intermittent","The module has seen the signal of the fifth transmission pressure sensor or switch drop out or jump briefly, without a steady low or high reading. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Loose or corroded contact at the valve body module connector"],["m","Debris or damaged diaphragm film making the switch contact flicker"],["m","Chafed internal harness that shorts or opens under vibration"],["l","Fault in the transmission control module input stage"]],["Stored fault, often without warning light","Occasional harsh or flared shift","Shift adaptation frozen"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0992":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch F Circuit","General circuit fault of a sixth transmission pressure sensor or switch, labelled F. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and no gearbox with a switch F was found.",[["h","Open or damaged signal wire or connector to the switch"],["m","Pressure switch or sensor failed inside the valve body module"],["l","Transmission control module input fault"]],["Warning light depending on manufacturer calibration","Harsh or delayed shifts","Possible limp mode with a fixed gear"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0993":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch F Circuit Range/Performance","Pressure sensor or switch F is electrically intact, but its reading does not match what the module expects from the commanded clutch state. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Sticking clutch regulator valve feeding the switch"],["m","Worn or delaminated switch film, debris in the switch"],["m","Low or contaminated transmission fluid"]],["Harsh or flared shifts","Shift adaptation frozen","Warning light depending on calibration"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0994":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch F Circuit Low","The signal of pressure sensor or switch F stays low. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Switch stuck closed by debris or damaged film"],["l","Clutch circuit not reaching pressure (valve or seal leak)"]],["Harsh or delayed shifts","Shift adaptation frozen","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0995":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch F Circuit High","The signal of pressure sensor or switch F stays high: with a normally closed switch the contacts stay open, which looks like permanent pressure, or the wire is open or shorted to voltage. No manufacturer documents this use publicly.",[["h","Open signal wire or unplugged connector"],["m","Signal wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Switch stuck open inside the valve body module"]],["Harsh or delayed shifts","Shift adaptation frozen","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0996":["Transmission Fluid Pressure Sensor/Switch F Circuit Intermittent","The signal of pressure sensor or switch F drops out or flickers for short moments without staying low or high. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and no transmission with a sixth pressure switch was found: GM's six-speeds end at switch 5 (E), Mitsubishi's 2019 six-speed uses letters B, D and E.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector contact"],["m","Debris or delaminated film making the switch chatter"],["m","Chafed harness shorting or opening under vibration"]],["Stored fault, often without warning light","Occasional rough or flared shift"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0997":["Shift Solenoid F Control Circuit Range/Performance","The drive circuit of shift solenoid F works electrically but its measured current or voltage does not fit the command. No manufacturer was found to document this code.",[["h","Solenoid coil resistance out of specification"],["m","High-resistance connector or harness contact"],["l","Weak driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Warning light depending on calibration","Missing or harsh shifts into gears using this solenoid","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0998":["Shift Solenoid F Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault, typically a short to ground, in the control circuit of shift solenoid F. Two makers document it.",[["h","Solenoid or valve body (control valve) internal fault"],["m","Control wire shorted to ground in harness or connector"],["l","Transmission control module driver fault"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or loss of a gear range","Harsh engagement or flare when shifting"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P0999":["Shift Solenoid F Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid F, from a short to voltage or, where the driver uses a pull-up, an open coil or wire. Nissan's bulletin NTB15-037 for the 2012–2017 Versa, Versa Note and Sentra with the RE0F11A CVT names P0999 together with P0998 as shift solenoid F and calls for replacing the control valve rather than the whole CVT when diagnosis confirms the fault.",[["h","Open solenoid coil inside the valve body"],["m","Open control wire or short to voltage"],["m","Connector pin pushed out or corroded"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or loss of a gear range","Harsh shifts"],["hard",[150,1800]],"c"],"P099A":["Shift Solenoid G Control Circuit Range/Performance","The drive circuit of shift solenoid G is electrically intact, but current or voltage feedback does not fit the command. No manufacturer was found to document this code.",[["h","Solenoid coil resistance out of specification"],["m","High-resistance connector or harness contact"],["l","Weak driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Warning light depending on calibration","Harsh or missing shifts involving this solenoid","Possible limp mode"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P099B":["Shift Solenoid G Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault, usually a short to ground, in the control circuit of shift solenoid G. Mitsubishi's 2019 service manual for a six-speed automatic uses it for the low-reverse brake shift solenoid: with transmission relay voltage at 2 V or more and the solenoid commanded on, the module sets P099B if the actual voltage stays below 3.7 V for 0.5 s.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground in harness or connector"],["m","Shorted solenoid inside the valve body"],["l","Transmission control module driver fault"]],["Check engine light","No reverse or no engine braking in first gear (Mitsubishi)","Limp mode"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P099C":["Shift Solenoid G Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid G. Mitsubishi's 2019 six-speed uses P099C for an open circuit of the low-reverse brake shift solenoid: with the solenoid commanded off and transmission relay voltage of 2 V or more, a reading of 3.7 V or more for 0.5 s sets the code.",[["h","Open control wire or connector between module and valve body"],["m","Open solenoid coil inside the valve body"],["l","Control wire shorted to voltage"]],["Check engine light","No reverse or harsh reverse engagement (Mitsubishi)","Limp mode"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P099D":["Shift Solenoid H Control Circuit Range/Performance","Electrical feedback from the drive circuit of shift solenoid H does not fit the command, although no hard short or open is seen. No manufacturer was found to document this code.",[["h","Solenoid coil resistance out of specification"],["m","High-resistance connector or harness contact"],["l","Weak driver stage in the transmission control module"]],["Warning light depending on calibration","Harsh or missing shifts involving this solenoid"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P099E":["Shift Solenoid H Control Circuit Low","Low-voltage fault, typically a short to ground, in the control circuit of shift solenoid H. No manufacturer was found to document this code.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Shorted solenoid coil"],["l","Transmission control module driver fault"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or missing gears"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P099F":["Shift Solenoid H Control Circuit High","High-voltage fault in the control circuit of shift solenoid H, from a short to voltage or, on pull-up driver designs, an open coil or wire. No manufacturer was found to document this code.",[["h","Open control wire or connector"],["m","Control wire shorted to voltage"],["m","Open solenoid coil"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or missing gears"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P09A0":["Shift Solenoid G Control Circuit/Open","Open circuit or general circuit fault in the drive of shift solenoid G. It is a late addition to J2012: older code lists still show P09A0 to P09FF as reserved.",[["h","Broken control wire or unplugged connector"],["m","Open solenoid coil"],["l","Pin backed out of the connector housing"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or missing gears"],["hard",[100,1800]],"u"],"P09A1":["Shift Solenoid H Control Circuit/Open","Open circuit or general circuit fault in the drive of shift solenoid H, the eighth shift solenoid. Like P09A0 it is a late J2012 addition that older code lists still show as reserved.",[["h","Broken control wire or unplugged connector"],["m","Open solenoid coil"],["l","Pin backed out of the connector housing"]],["Check engine light","Limp mode or missing gears"],["hard",[100,1800]],"u"],"P09A2":["Gate Select Position Circuit B","General electrical fault on the second gate select position signal (circuit B) of an automated manual gearbox. The published designs from Isuzu (one select potentiometer) and Aisin AI (one select stroke sensor) show only a single sensor, so it is not documented whether B is a redundant second track of that sensor or a separate sensor.",[["h","Loose, corroded or wet connector at the select sensor or actuator unit"],["m","Chafed or broken signal wire of circuit B near the gearbox"],["m","Faulty select sensor, often integrated in the shift and select actuator"],["l","Faulty input stage for circuit B in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox shifts slowly or locks out certain gears","Gearbox stays in one gear or neutral"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P09A3":["Gate Select Position Circuit B Range/Performance","Plausibility fault of gate select circuit B: the signal is electrically valid but does not match what the module expects. That could be the reading of circuit A, the learned gate positions, or the target gate the module has commanded, as in the Aisin AI select control that compares actual and target gate.",[["h","Worn or drifting select sensor, signal B no longer agrees with the reference"],["m","Gate positions not relearned after actuator, clutch or module replacement"],["m","Contact resistance at connector pins distorting signal B"],["l","Sluggish or worn select mechanism so the measured gate lags the commanded one"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Wrong gate selected or gears refused","Slow, jerky shifting in fallback mode"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P09A4":["Gate Select Position Circuit B Low","Set when the gate select signal on circuit B stays below its lowest valid value for longer than a calibrated time. Likely causes are a signal line shorted to ground, a lost sensor supply or an internal sensor short; with potentiometer sensors like the select position sensor in an Isuzu patent, an open signal wire often reads low too.",[["h","Signal wire of circuit B chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor reference supply interrupted or shorted"],["m","Internal short or broken wiper in the select sensor"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gear changes blocked or limited to a few gears","Transmission warning message in the instrument cluster"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P09A5":["Gate Select Position Circuit B High","Indicates that the gate select signal on circuit B has stayed above its highest valid value for longer than allowed. Typical reasons are a short of the signal wire to the sensor supply or battery voltage, an open sensor ground, or a defective sensor.",[["h","Signal wire of circuit B shorted to sensor supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open sensor ground or corroded ground pin"],["m","Internal fault of the select sensor"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox stays in one gear or in neutral","Limp-home mode with very limited gear choice"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P09A6":["Gate Select Position Circuit C","Electrical fault on a third gate select position signal (circuit C) that fits none of the more specific C codes. Public AMT designs show far fewer channels: Aisin AI and Isuzu use one select sensor, and a Mannesmann Sachs patent combines motor encoders with an extra neutral position sensor.",[["h","Loose, corroded or wet connector at the select sensor or actuator unit"],["m","Damaged signal wire of circuit C in the gearbox harness"],["m","Faulty sensor supplying signal C"],["l","Faulty input stage for circuit C in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Delayed or missing gear changes","Vehicle reluctant to pull away"],["hard",[100,1500]],"u"],"P09A7":["Gate Select Position Circuit C Range/Performance","Rationality fault on gate select circuit C: the value is electrically valid, yet it disagrees with the other select channels, the learned gate positions or the commanded movement. A Mannesmann Sachs patent shows why several select signals can exist: when one sensor fails, the remaining ones still allow controlled positioning before an emergency mode based on stored drive times takes over.",[["h","Drifting or worn sensor so signal C disagrees with the other channels"],["m","Select positions not relearned after repair work on the actuator or gearbox"],["m","Loose sensor mounting or magnet shifting the measured position"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector of circuit C"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Slower or hesitant gear changes","Some gears no longer offered"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P09A8":["Gate Select Position Circuit C Low","The module stores this code when the gate select signal on circuit C remains below its valid range for a calibrated time. Common triggers are a signal wire shorted to ground, a missing sensor supply or an internal sensor fault; depending on the sensor type, a broken signal wire can read low as well.",[["h","Signal wire of circuit C shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply for circuit C missing or pulled down"],["m","Internal short in the sensor delivering signal C"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Gearbox holds one gear or drops to neutral","Transmission warning message in the instrument cluster"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P09A9":["Gate Select Position Circuit C High","Logged when the gate select signal on circuit C sits above its highest plausible value for longer than a calibrated time. Usual suspects are a short to the sensor supply or battery voltage, an interrupted sensor ground, or a sensor with an internal fault.",[["h","Signal wire of circuit C shorted to supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open or corroded sensor ground"],["m","Internal fault of the sensor delivering signal C"],["l","Faulty input stage in the transmission control module"]],["Check engine or transmission warning light on","Limp-home mode, often in a single low gear","Gear changes refused while driving"],["hard",[80,1500]],"u"],"P0A00":["Motor Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit","General circuit fault on temperature sensor A in the separate coolant loop that cools the inverter and other drive electronics of a hybrid or electric vehicle. The module flags it when the sensor signal cannot be used at all, without a more specific low, high or plausibility verdict; those cases have their own codes P0A01 to P0A04.",[["h","Corroded or loose connector at the sensor in the inverter coolant loop"],["m","Temperature sensor element failed"],["m","Damaged signal or reference wire between sensor and hybrid control module"],["l","Fault in the input stage of the hybrid or inverter control module"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning light","Reduced electric drive power","Inverter coolant pump or radiator fan running constantly as a precaution"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P0A01":["Motor Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The sensor delivers a value inside its electrical range, but it does not fit the other temperatures in the vehicle. GM uses this code on the 2013 Two-Mode hybrid Tahoe, Yukon, Escalade, Silverado and Sierra as a cold-start test: after the vehicle has stood for more than 28,800 s (8 h), the power electronics coolant temperature at power-up may differ from engine coolant temperature by no more than 30 °C, provided engine coolant and intake air agree within 15.75 °C.",[["h","Sensor characteristic drifted, reading offset against the other temperature sensors"],["m","Added resistance at a corroded connector shifting the reading"],["m","Low coolant or air pocket at the sensor in the electronics loop"],["l","Fault actually in the engine coolant or intake air sensor used for comparison"]],["Check engine light after two trips","Often no noticeable change in driving behaviour","Electric drive power limited when the electronics are hot"],["medium",[80,900]],"u"],"P0A02":["Motor Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The signal voltage of the coolant temperature sensor in the electronics loop sits below its valid window. Because the sensor is an NTC thermistor, a low voltage means an implausibly hot reading, typically a short to ground.",[["h","Signal wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in the thermistor"],["m","Moisture or coolant in the connector bridging signal to ground"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Displayed electronics temperature extremely high although the system is cool","Cooling fans and pump running at full output, drive power limited"],["medium",[60,900]],"c"],"P0A03":["Motor Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","The sensor signal voltage is above its valid window, which for an NTC thermistor reads as an impossibly cold coolant temperature. Typical reasons are an open circuit in the sensor or its wiring, or a short to a voltage source.",[["h","Connector unplugged, loose or with a pushed-back terminal"],["m","Broken signal or ground wire of the sensor"],["m","Thermistor element open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to a voltage supply"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Electronics temperature shown far below ambient","Reduced electric drive power as a safeguard"],["medium",[60,900]],"c"],"P0A04":["Motor Electronics Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","The temperature signal from the drive electronics coolant loop jumps or drops out for short moments, then returns to normal. Coolant temperature changes slowly, so a sudden step that the physics cannot explain points to a loose contact rather than a real temperature change.",[["h","Loose or fretting terminal at the sensor or module connector"],["m","Partly broken wire that opens under vibration or heat"],["l","Sensor with an internal intermittent contact"]],["Warning light that appears and disappears","Short, unexplained drops in electric drive power","Stored code with no steady fault on re-test"],["medium",[60,800]],"c"],"P0A05":["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Circuit/Open","The electric pump that circulates coolant through the inverter, converter and drive motor loop is driven by a control line from the hybrid module. P0A05 means that line shows an open circuit or a fault the driver cannot classify further.",[["h","Pump connector loose or corroded, often from road splash at the low-mounted pump"],["m","Broken control wire between module and pump"],["m","Failed electronics or open winding inside the pump"],["l","Blown pump fuse or missing supply, so the control input sees no load"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Electric drive power reduced, especially in warm weather or under load","Follow-up inverter overtemperature codes such as P0A93"],["medium",[100,800]],"c"],"P0A06":["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Circuit Low","The control line of pump A in the drive electronics coolant loop sits at a voltage below what the module expects for the commanded state, usually because the line or the pump input is shorted to ground. Depending on the driver design, the pump then runs uncontrolled or not at all.",[["h","Control wire chafed against the body and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short at the pump's control input"],["m","Water or coolant in the pump connector"]],["Hybrid system or check engine light","Electronics temperature rising quickly, power limited","Pump silent when commanded on, or running continuously"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P0A07":["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump A Control Circuit High","The module measures a voltage on the pump A control line that is higher than the commanded state allows, typically because the line touches a supply wire or the pump input has failed internally. The pump in the inverter and motor cooling loop may then be stuck on or never receive a valid command.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a battery or ignition supply wire"],["m","Pump control electronics failed, pulling the input high"],["l","Output driver in the control module damaged"]],["Hybrid system or check engine light","Pump running all the time, even with the vehicle off","Possible power limitation if cooling cannot be confirmed"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P0A08":["DC/DC Converter Status Circuit","General fault on the status line through which the DC/DC converter reports its condition to the hybrid control module. The converter replaces the alternator: it steps the traction battery voltage down to charge the 12 V battery.",[["h","Internal fault in the DC/DC converter reported by its self-diagnosis"],["m","Damaged or corroded status line or connector between converter and hybrid control module"],["l","Converter overheating due to a fault in the inverter/converter coolant loop"],["l","Faulty input stage in the hybrid control module"]],["Hybrid system, battery or master warning light on","12 V battery is no longer charged and voltage drops while driving","Electrical consumers fail and the hybrid system may finally shut down"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0A09":["DC/DC Converter Status Circuit Low","The status signal that the DC/DC converter sends to the hybrid control module stays below its expected range, typically from a short to ground, an open line read as low, or a converter that pulls its status output down because of an internal fault. Mitsubishi uses P0A09 on the 2012-2013 i-MiEV for a DC-DC converter fault; its bulletins tie it to a failed on-board charger that blows the high-voltage fuse feeding the converter, after which the 12 V battery and drive battery stop charging.",[["h","Blown high-voltage fuse feeding the converter, e.g. after an on-board charger failure (Mitsubishi i-MiEV)"],["m","DC/DC converter internal fault pulling the status output low"],["m","Status line shorted to ground or chafed harness"],["l","Faulty input stage in the hybrid/EV control module"]],["Power unit, battery or hybrid warning light on","12 V battery not charged, voltage drops","On EVs the drive battery may also stop charging"],["hard",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0A0A":["High Voltage System Interlock Circuit A","The high-voltage interlock loop (HVIL) is a low-voltage signal loop routed through every HV connector, the service disconnect and component covers. If it opens, the control module opens the main contactors so no one can touch live parts.",[["h","HV connector or service disconnect not fully seated after repair"],["m","Spread, bent or corroded interlock pins or damaged connector seals"],["m","Interlock wiring chafed or cut"],["l","Battery pack control module software fault (Chrysler Pacifica Hybrid 2024-2025)"]],["Vehicle will not start or go into ready mode","Check engine and hybrid system warning on","High-voltage system switched off while the 12 V system still works"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0A0B":["High Voltage System Interlock Circuit A Performance","The interlock loop A responds electrically, but its behaviour does not match what the module expects – for example a loop resistance outside the window, a signal that does not follow the commanded test pattern, or two modules on the same loop disagreeing. Mercedes-Benz logs P0A0B00 in the battery management system, the DC converter and the on-board charger of model line 243 when the vehicle will not start; its listed causes include oxidised interlock pins in the low-voltage connectors, twisted or chafed harnesses and bent pins on HV components.",[["h","Oxidised interlock pins in low-voltage connectors raising loop resistance"],["m","Improperly connected HV connector or bent interlock pins on an HV component"],["m","Twisted or chafed interlock harness rubbing on the body"],["l","Faulty interlock driver or receiver in a control module"]],["Vehicle cannot be started","High-voltage system warning in the instrument cluster","Several modules store interlock codes at the same time"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0A0C":["High Voltage System Interlock Circuit A Low","The voltage sensed on interlock loop A is too low while the module drives the loop – typically an opened loop or a short to ground. GM documents it codegenau: in its 2016 HPC2 table P0A0C sets when the sensed interlock voltage stays below 30 % of the 5 V test signal in 2 of 2 samples (12.5 ms each) with the 12 V supply above 10.2 V; it is Type A, so the MIL lights after one trip.",[["h","HV connector, cover or service disconnect open, interrupting the loop"],["m","Interlock wire shorted to ground, e.g. chafed at a module or the body"],["m","Corroded or pushed-back interlock pins"],["l","Faulty interlock source or sense stage in the battery or hybrid module"]],["Check engine light (GM Type A) and hybrid/EV warning","No ready mode, electric drive unavailable","Charging may be blocked"],["hard",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0A0D":["High Voltage System Interlock Circuit A High","The voltage sensed on interlock loop A is higher than expected – most often a short to a voltage source, or the loop not being pulled down when the module expects it. GM checks both states: in the 2016 HPC2 table P0A0D sets when the sensed voltage exceeds 24 % of 5 V with the source switched off (5 of 8 samples) or 44 % with it switched on (4 of 6 samples), Type A with MIL after one trip.",[["h","Service/safety plug not fully inserted or with poor contact (Hyundai Sonata Hybrid)"],["m","Interlock wire shorted to a 12 V or reference voltage"],["m","Damaged interlock harness or connector after body or battery work"],["l","Faulty interlock stage in the battery or hybrid control module"]],["No ready mode or ready light blinking","Check engine and charging warning lights on","Vehicle cannot be started"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0A0E":["High Voltage System Interlock Circuit A Intermittent","The interlock loop A signal drops out or jumps for short moments and then recovers, so the module sees an unstable loop rather than a lasting open or short. Typical are connectors with loose or spread contacts, harness sections that move with the body or drivetrain, and moisture in seals.",[["h","Loose or spread interlock contacts in an HV or low-voltage connector"],["m","Harness section with a broken strand that opens under vibration"],["m","Moisture entering through a damaged connector seal"],["l","Electrical interference on the interlock line or a weak module input"]],["Hybrid/EV warning that appears and disappears","Occasional loss of ready mode or sudden shutdown of electric drive","Intermittent no-start, often after rough roads or rain"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0A0F":["Engine Failed to Start","The control module commanded an engine start – by the hybrid system or an automatic start/stop restart – but the combustion engine did not reach a self-sustaining run within the allowed attempts. The code describes the result, not the cause.",[["h","Vacuum leak, e.g. separated purge VSV/PCV hose at the throttle body (Toyota Prius)"],["m","Shorted sensor harness pulling down the engine ECU 5 V reference (Kia Niro)"],["m","Fuel delivery or ignition fault preventing combustion"],["l","Failed automatic restart of a mild hybrid with a cold engine (Audi A6/A7 2019)"]],["Check engine light and hybrid or drive system warning","Engine does not start, or runs briefly and stops","Reduced power because the vehicle runs on the battery only"],["medium",[50,800]],"c"],"P0A10":["DC/DC Converter Status Circuit High","The status signal from the DC/DC converter to the hybrid control module sits above its valid range, typically because the line is shorted to a voltage source, its ground or pull-down path is open, or the converter drives the output high after an internal failure. The converter supplies the 12 V system from the traction battery, and its self-diagnosis reports problems over this status path.",[["m","Status line shorted to 12 V or another supply"],["m","Open ground or signal return at the converter connector"],["m","DC/DC converter internal fault driving the status output high"],["l","Faulty input stage in the hybrid control module"]],["Hybrid system or battery warning light on","12 V battery voltage falls while driving","Possible shutdown of the hybrid system if the 12 V battery runs flat"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0A11":["DC/DC Converter Enable Circuit/Open","General or open-circuit fault on the enable line with which the hybrid or battery control module tells the DC/DC converter that it may deliver power to the 12 V system. In typical designs a high enable level permits converter output, and a CAN message carries the same permission in parallel, so the two can be cross-checked.",[["h","Broken enable wire or unplugged connector between control module and converter"],["m","Corroded or pushed-out terminal at the converter enable pin"],["l","Faulty enable output driver in the control module"],["l","Faulty enable input inside the DC/DC converter"]],["Battery or charging warning light on","12 V battery is not charged and runs down","Vehicle may lose motive power after a while"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0A12":["DC/DC Converter Enable Circuit Low","The enable line to the DC/DC converter reads low when the control module is commanding it on. Because a low enable level usually means converter output is not permitted, the converter stays off and the 12 V system loses its charging source.",[["h","Enable wire shorted to ground"],["m","Weak or failed enable output driver in the control module"],["l","Converter enable input internally shorted"]],["Charging or battery warning light on","12 V voltage drops, electrical consumers misbehave","Hybrid system may shut down when the 12 V battery is flat"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0A13":["DC/DC Converter Enable Circuit High","The enable line to the DC/DC converter reads high when the control module expects it low, for example while the converter should be switched off. The usual reasons are a short to battery voltage on the enable wire, a stuck output driver, or a missing pull-down.",[["h","Enable wire shorted to 12 V or another supply"],["m","Enable output driver in the control module stuck on"],["l","Open pull-down or ground reference at the converter input"]],["Hybrid system or battery warning light on","12 V battery drains while the vehicle is parked","Converter-related functions disabled as a precaution"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0A14":["Engine Mount A Control Circuit/Open","Some vehicles use switchable or active engine mounts that the powertrain or a mount control unit changes between soft and firm, or drives with an actuator against vibration. P0A14 reports an open or undefined fault in the control circuit of mount A.",[["h","Mount actuator connector loose, corroded or unplugged after engine work"],["m","Open coil in the mount actuator or switching solenoid"],["m","Wire broken where the harness flexes with engine movement"],["l","Fault in the engine or mount control unit output"]],["More vibration at idle or during cylinder deactivation","Warning light, depending on manufacturer","Shudder that feels like a torque converter problem"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0A15":["Engine Mount A Control Circuit Low","The control circuit of switchable or active engine mount A shows a voltage or current below the expected level, which usually points to a short to ground on the line or inside the actuator. Ford uses P0A15 in its 2020 to 2023 gasoline OBD summary for the active mounts: the low-level driver reports the fault and the PCM matures it after about 5 seconds; the partner mount B has P0AB7.",[["h","Control wire chafed on the engine or subframe and shorted to ground"],["m","Shorted coil in the mount actuator or solenoid, often after fluid leakage"],["l","Moisture in the mount connector"]],["Noticeable vibration at idle or low speed","Warning light, depending on manufacturer","Cylinder deactivation may be suppressed"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0A16":["Engine Mount A Control Circuit High","The module sees a higher voltage on the control circuit of engine mount A than it should for the commanded state, usually a short to a supply line or a failed actuator input. The mount can then no longer be switched or driven as intended.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a battery or ignition supply"],["m","Mount actuator or solenoid failed with an open low side"],["l","Driver stage in the engine or mount control unit damaged"]],["Harsh idle or more engine movement on take-off","Warning light, depending on manufacturer","Vibration during cylinder deactivation"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0A17":["Motor Torque Sensor Circuit","Some electric drive units measure the torque on the motor or generator shaft directly, for example with a magnetostrictive sensor inside a hollow motor shaft as described in Honda patents for hybrid drive systems. The control unit uses that value to check the torque it commands.",[["h","Loose or corroded sensor connector at the drive unit"],["m","Damaged sensor wiring between drive unit and control module"],["m","Torque sensor or its excitation electronics failed, often only replaceable with the drive unit"],["l","Input stage of the hybrid or motor control module faulty"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Electric drive power limited or electric drive disabled","Higher fuel consumption because the engine runs more often"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A18":["Motor Torque Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The motor torque sensor delivers an electrically valid signal, but the torque it reports does not match what the control unit expects from the commanded motor current, speed and operating state. Typical checks compare measured and estimated torque or look for an offset when no torque is applied.",[["h","Sensor offset or gain drifted, reading off against the torque model"],["m","Electrical interference on the signal from damaged shielding or ground"],["m","Missing or outdated calibration after module or drive unit replacement"],["l","Mechanical fault in the drive unit that changes the real torque"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Reduced or uneven electric assistance","Engine runs more often, fuel use rises"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A19":["Motor Torque Sensor Circuit Low","The control unit watches the voltage of a torque sensor that measures load directly on the traction motor shaft, such as the magnetostrictive sensor in a hollow motor shaft shown in a Honda patent. P0A19 sets when the signal stays below its valid window for a calibrated time, typical of a short to ground, a broken supply or an open signal line pulled low.",[["h","Sensor signal or supply line shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor reference or excitation supply missing"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the drive unit"],["l","Torque sensor electronics failed, often replaced with the drive unit"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Reduced electric drive power or electric drive disabled","Engine runs more often, higher fuel consumption"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A1A":["Generator Control Module","P0A1A reports a fault of the electronics that control the generator, either inside a hybrid inverter or in a belt starter-generator. Toyota lists P0A1A for the 2010-2015 Prius and 2012-2017 Prius V together with P0A94, P324E and P3004 as a malfunction inside the inverter assembly with converter; the repair can mean replacing the MG ECU, IPM transistors or the whole inverter.",[["h","Internal failure of the inverter or generator control electronics"],["m","Control software fault, fixed by an update where the maker provides one"],["l","Supply, ground or bus connection to the module faulty"]],["MIL and hybrid or charging warning on","Battery no longer charged, start-stop or hybrid drive unavailable","Vehicle may not reach ready state"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0A1B":["Drive Motor A Control Module Performance","P0A1B flags a self-test failure of the processor that controls drive motor A, usually inside the inverter. In GM hybrid tables (2016) it sets when the processor fails an inhibit path test at power-down, three or more times, proving it could not take the system to a safe state; in 2011 tables internal SPI, RAM or cache faults in 28 of 32 samples count.",[["h","Control software fault, several makers resolve it by reprogramming"],["m","Internal processor or memory fault in the inverter control board"],["l","Weak 12 V supply during start-up or shutdown"]],["Loss of drive warning and MIL","Vehicle cannot be driven electrically or not at all","Fault may vanish after a full sleep cycle while the code stays stored"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0A1C":["Drive Motor B Control Module Performance","P0A1C is the counterpart of P0A1B for the second electric machine, drive motor B, and reports that its control processor is no longer trustworthy. GM hybrid tables use it for the motor control processor B: in 2016 calibrations the code sets when an inhibit path test at power-down fails three or more times, in 2011 calibrations internal processor integrity faults such as SPI, RAM or cache errors are counted.",[["h","Internal processor or memory fault on the motor B control board"],["m","Calibration or software fault in the inverter"],["l","Unstable 12 V supply or ground to the inverter electronics"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Reduced drive power or no electric drive"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0A1D":["Hybrid/EV Powertrain Control Module A","The hybrid powertrain control module coordinates engine, motors and battery. P0A1D says this module has reported an internal failure or is no longer working reliably.",[["h","Internal fault of the hybrid control module"],["m","Code stored by a scan tool communication error, not by a real defect"],["l","Supply, ground or network fault at the module"]],["MIL and hybrid warning on","Engine power reduced","Hybrid functions limited or unavailable"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A1E":["Starter/Generator Control Module","Mild and full hybrids often start the engine and recharge the battery with a belt-driven starter-generator that has its own control electronics. P0A1E means that controller has reported a fault or works incorrectly.",[["h","Loose wiring connection at the starter-generator"],["h","Starter-generator software fault, fixed by update"],["m","Starter-generator with integrated electronics failed"]],["Wrench lamp or MIL on, electrical system warning","Auto start-stop disabled","Electric-only driving unavailable"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0A1F":["Battery Energy Control Module A Performance","The battery energy control module supervises cell voltages, current and temperatures of the high-voltage pack. P0A1F reports that this module failed its own integrity checks.",[["h","Module software fault, fixed by a new calibration"],["m","Internal hardware fault of the battery control module"],["l","Unstable 12 V supply causing module resets"]],["MIL or service engine soon light on","Battery or hybrid warning, limited electric operation","Start-stop may be disabled"],["shop_only",[50,3000]],"c"],"P0A20":["Motor Torque Sensor Circuit High","This code covers the upper electrical limit of a torque sensor fitted to the traction motor shaft, for example the magnetostrictive design inside a hollow output shaft described in a Honda patent. The control unit sets P0A20 when the sensor voltage remains above the valid range for a calibrated time, which points to a short to the supply voltage, an open ground return or a sensor output stuck high.",[["h","Signal line shorted to sensor supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open sensor ground return"],["l","Sensor electronics output stuck high"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Electric drive torque limited","Reduced acceleration in electric mode"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A21":["Motor Torque Sensor Circuit Intermittent","Here the torque sensor on the traction motor shaft delivers a signal that drops out or jumps for short moments instead of failing permanently. Such shaft sensors, as in the Honda patent with a sensor inside a hollow motor shaft, sit in an area with strong electromagnetic fields and vibration, so loose terminals, chafed wires and interference are the usual suspects.",[["h","Loose or fretted terminal in the sensor connector"],["m","Chafed sensor wiring that shorts briefly under vibration"],["l","Damaged shield letting inverter noise into the signal"]],["Hybrid warning that appears and disappears","Brief loss of electric assist"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A22":["Generator Torque Sensor Circuit","In series or power-split hybrids a separate electric machine runs as generator, driven by the combustion engine. A Honda patent claims a torque sensor inside the hollow shaft of either the traction motor or this generator.",[["h","Sensor connector at the generator unplugged, loose or corroded"],["m","Open circuit in the sensor harness"],["m","Generator torque sensor or its electronics failed"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Reduced battery charging by the engine","Limited system power"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A23":["Generator Torque Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The generator torque sensor sends an electrically valid voltage, but the torque it reports does not fit the generator current, speed and engine load the control unit knows. Typical checks compare the measured value with a torque model or look for an offset while the generator is idle.",[["h","Sensor zero point or gain drifted"],["m","Electromagnetic interference from the generator or inverter"],["l","Contact resistance in the connector skewing the signal"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Generator output limited, battery charges slowly"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A24":["Generator Torque Sensor Circuit Low","P0A24 is stored when the voltage from the generator torque sensor stays under its lower diagnostic limit for a calibrated time. Electrically this means the line is pulled toward ground, the sensor supply is missing or the signal wire is broken.",[["h","Signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply voltage missing"],["l","Sensor electronics failed with output stuck low"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Reduced charging and system power"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A25":["Generator Torque Sensor Circuit High","With P0A25 the control unit sees the generator torque sensor signal above its permitted upper limit for longer than the calibrated debounce time. The common electrical explanations are a short from the signal line to the supply or battery voltage, an interrupted sensor ground, or a sensor output stuck at its maximum.",[["h","Signal line shorted to supply or battery voltage"],["m","Open sensor ground"],["l","Sensor output stuck high"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Generator output limited"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A26":["Generator Torque Sensor Circuit Intermittent","P0A26 points to short interruptions or jumps in the generator torque sensor signal rather than a constant fault. The control unit counts implausible signal changes or brief out-of-range events and sets the code once a calibrated limit is exceeded.",[["h","Loose or fretted terminal in the sensor connector"],["m","Chafed wiring near the generator housing"],["l","Shield or ground damage letting inverter noise in"]],["Hybrid warning that comes and goes","Brief drop in charging or system power"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A27":["Hybrid/EV Battery Power Off Circuit","P0A27 covers the circuit that tells the high-voltage battery to drop off the bus, i.e. the command path that opens the main contactors at key-off, after a fault or after a crash signal.",[["h","Contactor or its driver does not release when power-off is commanded"],["m","Internal fault in the inverter/power unit that controls the contactors (Honda Civic Hybrid)"],["m","Damaged wiring or loose terminal on the hardwired power-off line"],["l","Crash or emergency shutdown signal stored or still active"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Vehicle will not enter ready mode or shuts down while driving","High-voltage system does not power down after key-off"],["shop_only",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0A28":["Hybrid/EV Battery Power Off Circuit Low","The control unit watches the voltage on the line that commands the high-voltage battery to disconnect. P0A28 sets when that signal stays below its valid window for a calibrated time, typical of a short to ground or a lost supply on the line.",[["h","Power-off signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Poor terminal fit or corrosion at the battery or control module connector"],["m","Missing supply or blown fuse feeding the power-off circuit"],["l","Faulty driver stage in the battery or hybrid control module"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Vehicle does not reach ready mode","Electric drive disabled, engine runs constantly"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0A29":["Hybrid/EV Battery Power Off Circuit High","P0A29 is stored when the voltage on the hybrid battery power-off line sits above its plausible range for longer than allowed, which points to an open line pulled up internally or a short to a supply voltage. No manufacturer explains P0A29 publicly; GM merely names it as a keyword in a generic bulletin on terminal fit.",[["h","Open power-off line or unplugged connector"],["m","Power-off line shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Spread or corroded terminals at the connectors"],["l","Faulty input or driver stage in the control module"]],["Hybrid system warning light","Vehicle does not reach ready mode","High-voltage system stays active after switching off"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0A2A":["Drive Motor A Temperature Sensor A Circuit","A thermistor in the stator of traction motor A reports winding temperature so the inverter can cut torque before the insulation overheats. P0A2A is the general circuit fault; the failure type sits in the extension byte.",[["h","Broken or shorted sensor wire between motor and inverter"],["m","Loose or corroded connector at the drive unit"],["m","Thermistor failed inside the motor or transaxle"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the inverter/motor control module"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Reduced electric drive power","Implausible motor temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0A2B":["Drive Motor A Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","This is the rationality check: after a long soak, motor A's temperature should match other temperatures in the drive. GM compares it with the average inverter module temperature on its 2013 two-mode trucks and sets P0A2B, Type B, if they differ by more than 28 °C after at least 360 minutes off; the 2011 table uses 20 °C against coolant and transmission fluid, the 2013 MGU mild hybrid 25 °C.",[["h","Thermistor drifted out of its resistance curve"],["m","Added resistance from a worn or corroded connector (Audi A7 e quattro)"],["l","Fault in a reference temperature such as inverter or fluid temperature"],["l","Motor cooling problem that makes a real temperature look implausible"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Electric drive power limited","Motor temperature far off from other temperatures after a cold soak"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0A2C":["Drive Motor A Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The motor A sensor is an NTC thermistor, so its signal voltage falls as the winding heats; Hyundai's Sonata Hybrid bulletin gives about 11 to 13 kOhm at 20 °C for 2014-15 units. P0A2C means the voltage is too low, which the module reads as an impossibly hot motor: GM sets it on 2013 two-mode trucks above 230 °C (near 0 V), Type B, two trips; the 2011 table uses 184 °C, the 2013 MGU mild hybrid 236 °C.",[["h","Sensor signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Thermistor internally shorted (often only with transaxle or motor)"],["m","Chafed extension harness between drive unit and inverter"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the inverter/motor control module"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Motor temperature shows a very high fixed value in live data","Electric drive power reduced or disabled"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0A2D":["Drive Motor A Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","With an NTC thermistor, a high signal voltage stands for a cold motor, and a voltage close to the 5 V pull-up means the circuit is open. GM sets P0A2D on its 2013 two-mode trucks when the reading falls below -40 °C, Type B over two trips; if the fault is already present at start-up it waits until the motor has warmed up under load for at least 1.5 minutes.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire of the motor temperature sensor"],["m","Connector unplugged or terminal pushed out, often after drive unit work"],["m","Thermistor open inside the motor or transaxle"],["l","Signal wire shorted to a supply voltage"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Motor temperature shows about -40 degrees in live data","Electric drive power limited"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0A2E":["Drive Motor A Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","P0A2E flags short jumps or dropouts in the motor A temperature signal that are too brief or too erratic for the low/high checks. Bentley documents it for the Bentayga PHEV and Flying Spur Hybrid 2020-23: the resistance in the sensor crimp changes suddenly at high temperature, and only the sensor has to be replaced, although the engine and gearbox come out to reach it.",[["h","Worn contacts in a coupling connector of the sensor line (Audi A7 e quattro)"],["m","Resistance jumps in the sensor crimp at high temperature (Bentley hybrids)"],["l","Chafed or loosely routed sensor wire with intermittent contact"]],["Check engine light or high-voltage warning in the cluster","Fault comes and goes, often after hard driving","Gearbox or electric drive warning on some models"],["shop_only",[200,5000]],"c"],"P0A2F":["Drive Motor A Over Temperature","Unlike its siblings, P0A2F reports a real thermal problem: the sensor works, but motor A stays too hot. GM sets it on 2013 two-mode trucks when the temperature exceeds 200 °C and does not fall below the 135 °C reset level, Type B, provided no circuit or plausibility fault is active.",[["h","Motor cooling failure such as a weak pump, low fluid or blocked cooler"],["m","Sustained high load such as towing or long climbs in electric mode"],["l","Temperature sensor reading too high while still within range"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning","Electric drive power drops sharply or is cut","Engine runs more often to support the drive"],["hard",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A30":["Drive Motor B Temperature Sensor Circuit","P0A30 is the general circuit code for the temperature sensor in the second traction motor (motor B). It is meant for faults the control unit can see on the sensor line but cannot classify as too low, too high or implausible.",[["h","Loose, corroded or damaged connector between motor B and its control module"],["m","Chafed or broken sensor wiring at the drive unit"],["m","Thermistor in the motor winding failed (often only replaceable with the motor)"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the motor control module"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine light","Reduced electric drive power","Motor B temperature missing or implausible in live data"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A31":["Drive Motor B Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A31 checks whether the motor B temperature reading makes sense, typically after a long rest when all parts should be close to the same temperature. GM compares it with other temperatures: the 2011 tables flag a gap above 20 °C to the average of power-electronics coolant and transmission fluid after 6 h without propulsion (Type A); the 2013 two-mode trucks allow 28 °C against the inverter module after 360 min key-off (Type B, two trips).",[["h","Motor B thermistor reading drifted out of tolerance"],["m","Corroded terminals adding resistance in the sensor circuit"],["l","Comparison sensor (inverter, coolant or fluid temperature) is wrong"],["l","Uneven cooling of the drive unit, e.g. low fluid level"]],["Hybrid warning light after a cold start","Motor B temperature differs clearly from other temperatures after a long rest","Electric drive limited as a precaution"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A32":["Drive Motor B Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","P0A32 means the motor B temperature signal sits below its valid voltage range. GM uses an NTC thermistor, so a voltage near 0 V reads as an impossibly hot winding: the 2011 hybrid tables set it above 184 °C (Type A, one trip), the 2013 two-mode trucks above 230 °C (Type B, two trips).",[["h","Sensor signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Thermistor shorted inside the motor"],["m","Chafed harness between drive unit and motor control module"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the motor control module"]],["Hybrid warning or check engine light","Motor B temperature shows an extremely high fixed value","Electric drive reduced or switched off"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A33":["Drive Motor B Temperature Sensor Circuit High","P0A33 is stored when the motor B temperature signal stays above its valid voltage range. With GM's NTC thermistor a voltage near 5 V reads as an impossibly cold motor: GM sets it below -41 °C in the 2011 tables (Type A) and below -40 °C on the 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips).",[["h","Open sensor circuit or unplugged connector"],["m","Spread or corroded terminals at the motor or module connector"],["m","Thermistor open inside the motor"],["l","Signal wire shorted to a voltage source"]],["Hybrid warning or check engine light","Motor B temperature shows about -40 °C regardless of conditions","Electric drive power limited"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A34":["Drive Motor B Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent","P0A34 covers a motor B temperature signal that drops out or jumps for short moments instead of failing permanently. A winding temperature cannot change by tens of degrees within milliseconds, so such steps point to a loose contact, a chafed wire or a cracked solder joint.",[["h","Loose or fretting terminal in the sensor connector"],["m","Wire insulation chafed through at a moving harness section"],["l","Cracked joint at the thermistor leads inside the motor"]],["Hybrid warning that appears and clears on its own","Motor B temperature jumps in live data","Occasional loss of electric drive power"],["shop_only",[80,3000]],"c"],"P0A35":["Drive Motor B Over Temperature","Unlike the sensor codes, P0A35 reports that motor B is actually too hot according to a working sensor. GM's 2011 hybrid tables set it above 149 °C (Type A, one trip); the 2013 two-mode trucks use 200 °C, clear it only below 135 °C and run it only while P0A31 is not active (Type B).",[["h","Electric coolant pump of the drive cooling circuit failed or weak"],["m","Low coolant or transmission fluid in the motor cooling path"],["m","Radiator fan not running or radiator blocked"],["l","Extended high load such as towing or long climbs"]],["Noticeable loss of electric drive power","Hybrid system overheat or warning message","Engine runs more often to compensate"],["hard",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0A36":["Generator Temperature Sensor Circuit","P0A36 is the general circuit code for the temperature sensor in the hybrid generator, the machine that is mainly driven by the engine to produce electricity. It covers faults the module detects on the sensor line without classing them as low, high or implausible.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector between generator and its control unit"],["m","Damaged sensor wiring near the transaxle"],["m","Generator coil thermistor failed (usually replaced with the generator)"],["l","Faulty input in the generator control unit"]],["Hybrid warning or check engine light","Generator output or battery charging restricted","Generator temperature missing or implausible in live data"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A37":["Generator Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A37 tests whether the generator temperature reading is believable. Mitsubishi documents it for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: once per drive cycle, after at least 5,400 s of soak and with the inverter switched off, the generator W-phase coil temperature is compared with the front motor coil and with the oil temperature.",[["h","Generator coil sensor reading out of tolerance"],["m","Corroded connector or damaged harness in the sensor circuit"],["m","Fault in the power drive unit that evaluates the sensor"],["l","Comparison sensor (motor coil or oil temperature) reads wrong"]],["Hybrid warning after the first start of the day","Generator temperature differs from oil temperature after a long rest","Generator operation possibly restricted"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A38":["Generator Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","P0A38 means the generator temperature signal has fallen below the range a healthy sensor can produce. Mitsubishi sets it on the 2023 Outlander PHEV when the voltage of the generator W-phase coil sensor is 0.1 V or less for at least 0.5 s while the generator control unit has at least 8 V supply.",[["h","Sensor signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Generator coil sensor shorted internally"],["m","Damaged connector or harness between generator and power drive unit"],["l","Faulty sensor input in the power drive unit"]],["Hybrid warning or check engine light","Generator temperature shows an implausibly high fixed value","Battery charging by the engine restricted"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A39":["Generator Temperature Sensor Circuit High","P0A39 is the high-side counterpart of P0A38: the generator temperature signal stays above the range a working sensor can deliver. Mitsubishi sets it on the 2023 Outlander PHEV when the generator W-phase coil sensor voltage reaches 4.93 V or more for at least 0.5 s.",[["h","Open sensor circuit or unplugged generator connector"],["m","Spread or corroded terminals at the generator or power drive unit"],["m","Generator coil sensor open internally"],["l","Signal wire shorted to a supply voltage"]],["Hybrid warning or check engine light","Generator temperature shows a very low fixed value","Generator output or series mode restricted"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A3A":["Generator Temperature Sensor Circuit Intermittent","P0A3A flags a generator temperature signal that is interrupted or jumps only briefly. Since the thermal mass of a generator winding rules out sudden steps, such behaviour indicates a contact or wiring problem rather than a real temperature change.",[["h","Loose or fretting terminal in the generator sensor connector"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the connector"],["m","Harness chafed against the transaxle housing"]],["Hybrid warning that appears now and then","Generator temperature jumps in live data","Occasionally restricted battery charging by the engine"],["shop_only",[80,3500]],"c"],"P0A3B":["Generator A Over Temperature","P0A3B reports that the hybrid generator itself has become too hot, with the temperature sensor working correctly. Mitsubishi documents it as generator coil temperature high on the 2023 Outlander PHEV: the monitor runs continuously and sets the code when the W-phase coil temperature criterion of 199 °C is met for one second.",[["h","Fault in the oil cooling circuit of the generator (low oil, leak, pump)"],["m","Radiator fan not working"],["m","Internal generator fault such as damaged winding insulation"],["l","Power drive unit commanding excessive generator current"]],["Hybrid system warning or overheat message","Reduced charging and power when the engine drives the generator","Battery state of charge drops during driving"],["shop_only",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0A3C":["Drive Motor A Inverter Over Temperature","P0A3C reports overheating of the inverter that powers drive motor A, usually measured at its IGBT power switches. Mitsubishi sets it on the 2023 Outlander PHEV when the front motor control unit reads 166 °C or more at the U-phase upper IGBT for 0.12 s; other switch positions have their own P30xx codes.",[["h","PCU or inverter radiator damaged or leaking (Honda Accord/Civic Hybrid)"],["m","Internal inverter defect such as a cracked solder joint (Honda Civic Hybrid 2012-15)"],["m","Low coolant or failed electric pump in the inverter cooling circuit"],["l","Radiator fan not running"]],["Hybrid system warning and check engine light","Strongly reduced drive power","Coolant loss from the inverter circuit"],["shop_only",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0A3D":["Drive Motor B Inverter Over Temperature","P0A3D is the over-temperature code for the inverter feeding drive motor B. On the 2023 Outlander PHEV this is the rear motor control unit, and Mitsubishi sets the code when its IGBT temperature is 156 °C or higher for one second.",[["h","Electric water pump of the EV cooling circuit failed"],["m","Low coolant or air in the EV cooling circuit"],["m","Radiator fan not running or radiator blocked"],["l","Internal fault in the rear motor and inverter assembly"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning","Reduced rear-axle drive power","Fault appears after long climbs or high load"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0A3E":["Generator Inverter Over Temperature","P0A3E reports that the inverter controlling the hybrid generator is too hot. Mitsubishi sets it on the 2023 Outlander PHEV when the generator control unit reads 156 °C or more at the U-phase IGBT for 0.12 s; the other switches report via P30AD to P30B1.",[["h","PCU radiator punctured or leaking after road debris (Honda Accord/Civic Hybrid)"],["m","Low coolant or failed electric pump in the inverter cooling circuit"],["m","Radiator fan not running"],["l","Internal fault in the power drive unit"]],["Several hybrid warning lights at once","Reduced hybrid system performance","Coolant loss or visible damage at the front radiator area"],["shop_only",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0A3F":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit","The rotor position of a traction motor is read by a resolver: one excitation coil and two pickup coils that return sine and cosine signals. P0A3F is the basic circuit code for motor A and mostly means the signal is gone.",[["h","Spread or poorly seated terminal at the resolver connector (documented on the Chevrolet Bolt)"],["m","Open or shorted excitation, sine or cosine wire between motor and inverter"],["m","Low 12 V auxiliary battery voltage disturbing the motor control module (Subaru, fixed by reprogramming)"],["l","Resolver coil failed inside the motor"]],["Sudden loss of propulsion, sometimes normal again after a restart","MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Vehicle will not enter ready mode or electric drive is disabled"],["shop_only",[150,6000]],"c"],"P0A40":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A40 means the resolver of motor A still delivers a signal, but its quality is poor or implausible. GM calls this a degraded signal: the sine or cosine value exceeds 4.0 V, judged within 0.2 to 0.4 s of a 2 s window (Type A, one trip in 2016; Type B, two trips on the 2013 mild hybrid).",[["h","Rotor position sensor (resolver) delivering a degraded signal, e.g. Audi G713"],["h","Worn, loose or contaminated contacts in the resolver connectors (Audi, Bentley)"],["m","Sensor wires pinched or misrouted on the gearbox housing after a repair"],["l","Faulty resolver evaluation in the inverter/motor control module"]],["Drive system fault message in the instrument cluster","Vehicle cannot be started or will not enter ready mode","Electric drive disabled or torque jerks while driving"],["shop_only",[200,5000]],"c"],"P0A41":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit Low","P0A41 reports that a resolver signal of motor A sits below its permitted range, as happens with a sine, cosine or excitation wire shorted to ground, a shorted coil or a broken line pulled down by the input circuit. Kia documents it on the 2011-13 Optima Hybrid: an intermittent MIL or hybrid service lamp with P0A41 in the motor control unit, traced to internal shorts or poor pin fit in the sub-harness between the hybrid power control unit and the motor, checked against resistance specs.",[["h","Internal short in the resolver sub-harness between power control unit and motor (Kia Optima Hybrid)"],["h","Poor pin fit at the resolver connectors"],["l","Shorted resolver coil inside the motor"],["l","Faulty resolver input stage in the motor control unit"]],["Intermittent MIL or hybrid service lamp","Electric drive temporarily disabled","Code returns after clearing, often with vibration or heat"],["shop_only",[150,3500]],"c"],"P0A42":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit High","P0A42 is the counterpart to P0A41: a resolver line of motor A reads above its allowed range. Typical triggers are a sine, cosine or excitation wire shorted to a supply voltage, or an open coil whose line is pulled up by the bias resistors of the input circuit, which drives the converter into clipping.",[["h","Resolver wire shorted to a supply voltage inside the sub-harness (Kia Optima Hybrid)"],["m","Open sine or cosine line pulled high by the input circuit"],["m","Poor pin fit or corrosion at the resolver connectors"],["l","Faulty resolver input stage in the motor control unit"]],["Intermittent MIL or hybrid service lamp","Electric drive disabled or vehicle not ready","Reduced power until the fault clears"],["shop_only",[150,3500]],"c"],"P0A43":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent","P0A43 is set when the motor A resolver signal drops out or jumps only briefly, so that neither a steady low, high nor a lasting loss is confirmed. Stellantis uses it on the Pacifica Hybrid: NHTSA files show P0A43-00 stored in the hybrid control processor next to P0A3F and P0A45, and a 2026 parts review tells dealers to check active bulletins and flash the hybrid control processor before ordering a new power inverter module.",[["h","Loose or spread terminal in the resolver connector, losing contact under vibration"],["m","Hybrid control or inverter software needing an update (Stellantis)"],["m","Chafed resolver wiring making intermittent contact"],["l","Intermittent fault in the power inverter module"]],["Hybrid system warning or MIL that comes and goes","Brief loss of propulsion, normal again after restart","Code stored as history without current symptoms"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0A44":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit Overspeed","P0A44 reports that the speed derived from the motor A resolver is above the highest speed the motor may run. Mitsubishi uses it on the 2023 Outlander PHEV front motor: set at 15,500 rpm or more for 0.5 s with module supply above 8 V, judged only when the position sensor itself is fault-free; the guided fix after other codes are ruled out is a new front motor.",[["m","Mechanical fault in the front motor or its drivetrain (Mitsubishi replaces the motor)"],["m","Real overspeed from wheel spin or a slipping coupling"],["m","Disturbed resolver signal producing a false speed value"],["l","Motor control module calculating speed incorrectly"]],["Electric drive torque cut during acceleration or high speed","Hybrid/EV system warning and MIL","Reduced top speed in limp mode"],["shop_only",[100,7000]],"c"],"P0A45":["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit","P0A45 is the general circuit code for the resolver of the second traction motor (B), usually meaning its signal is missing. GM sets it like P0A3F when sine or cosine amplitude falls below 2.3 V (Type A, one trip).",[["h","Damaged or faulty frame/body wire harness to the rear motor (Toyota RAV4 Hybrid)"],["m","Poor contact at the motor B resolver connector"],["m","Resolver or control unit inside the rear motor assembly failed (Mitsubishi)"],["l","Hybrid control software needing an update (Stellantis)"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning message","Loss of rear-axle electric drive or all-wheel drive","Reduced power or vehicle not ready"],["shop_only",[150,6000]],"c"],"P0A46":["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A46 reports that the motor B resolver still delivers a signal, but one that is degraded or not plausible. GM uses the same check as P0A40 for its second motor: sine or cosine above 4.0 V, with a failure counted when it persists for 0.2 to 0.4 s of a 2 s window (Type A, one trip in the 2011 and 2016 tables).",[["h","Resolver of motor B delivering unequal or distorted sine/cosine signals"],["m","High contact resistance or moisture in the resolver connector"],["m","Fault inside the rear motor assembly incl. bearing damage (Mitsubishi)"],["l","Damaged shielding letting inverter noise into the resolver lines"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Rear or second motor disabled, reduced traction or power","Jerky torque delivery before the fault sets"],["shop_only",[200,6000]],"c"],"P0A47":["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit Low","P0A47 means a resolver signal of motor B is below its allowed range, typically from a sine, cosine or excitation line shorted to ground or a shorted coil. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Resolver sine, cosine or excitation line shorted to ground"],["m","Poor pin fit or water ingress at the motor B resolver connector"],["l","Shorted resolver coil inside motor B"],["l","Faulty resolver input stage in the motor control module"]],["MIL or hybrid service lamp","Second motor disabled, reduced traction or power","Fault may come and go with vibration or moisture"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0A48":["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit High","P0A48 reports a motor B resolver line reading above its allowed range, for example a sine, cosine or excitation wire shorted to a supply voltage, or an open coil whose line is pulled up by the input bias circuit until the converter clips. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Resolver line shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Open sine or cosine line pulled high by the input circuit"],["m","Corroded or loose terminals at the motor B resolver connector"],["l","Faulty resolver input stage in the motor control module"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Second motor disabled, reduced traction or power","Vehicle may not enter ready mode"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0A49":["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent","P0A49 covers short dropouts or jumps in the motor B resolver signal that are too brief to count as a lasting loss, low or high fault. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Loose or spread terminal in the motor B resolver connector"],["m","Chafed or stretched resolver wiring near the rear axle or subframe"],["l","Moisture in the connector causing intermittent leakage"],["l","Hybrid control software needing an update"]],["Hybrid warning or MIL that comes and goes","Brief loss of second-motor drive, often over bumps","Code stored as history without current symptoms"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A4A":["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Circuit Overspeed","P0A4A reports that the speed calculated from the motor B resolver exceeds the motor's permitted maximum. Mitsubishi uses it for the rear motor of the 2023 Outlander PHEV: set at 12,500 rpm or more for at least 1 s with supply above 8 V, and only judged once the rear position sensor codes P0A45, P0A46, P0C57, P0C58, P0C61 and P0C62 are ruled out; otherwise the rear motor assembly is replaced, bearing damage being one listed cause.",[["m","Fault in the rear motor assembly, e.g. damaged bearing or control unit (Mitsubishi)"],["m","Real overspeed from rear wheel spin on low grip"],["m","Disturbed resolver signal producing a false speed value"]],["Rear electric drive shut off, front-wheel drive only","Hybrid/EV warning and MIL","Reduced acceleration in limp mode"],["shop_only",[100,7000]],"c"],"P0A4B":["Generator Position Sensor Circuit","P0A4B covers the resolver that tells the inverter where the generator rotor stands; without a valid angle the generator cannot be commutated. Mitsubishi (Outlander PHEV 2023) runs the check continuously once the generator control unit has at least 8.0 V and sets P0A4B when its internal status flags stay failed for 0.23 s or 0.28 s; low and high signal levels go to separate codes there.",[["m","False detection in the inverter monitor software (Volvo, fixed by update)"],["h","Damaged resolver wiring or connector between generator and inverter"],["m","Generator resolver defective"],["l","Resolver evaluation in the power drive unit faulty"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Engine does not start or crank in hybrid mode","Electric drive or charging by the generator disabled"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A4C":["Generator Position Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A4C means the generator resolver delivers signals that are electrically present but implausible, so the computed rotor angle cannot be trusted. Mitsubishi documents the exact limits for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: the sine or cosine winding voltage must stay between 2.1 and 2.9 V; if either leaves that window for 0.28 s, or an internal status fault persists for 0.31 s, the generator control unit stores P0A4C.",[["h","Corroded or loose resolver connector"],["m","Damaged shielding letting interference onto the sine/cosine lines"],["m","Resolver winding drifted out of tolerance"],["l","Faulty resolver input stage in the power drive unit"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Reduced drive power or engine-only operation","Engine start via the generator not possible"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A4D":["Generator Position Sensor Circuit Low","P0A4D reports a generator resolver signal stuck below its permitted range, as caused by a sine, cosine or excitation line shorted to ground or a winding short. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Sine, cosine or excitation line shorted to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the resolver connector"],["l","Shorted resolver winding inside the generator"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Generator disabled, engine-only or no-start condition"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A4E":["Generator Position Sensor Circuit High","P0A4E is set when a generator resolver signal sits above its permitted range, which usually points to an open signal line pulled up by the input circuit or a short to a supply voltage. No manufacturer documents P0A4E publicly.",[["h","Open resolver signal line or pushed-out terminal"],["m","Signal line shorted to a supply voltage"],["l","Faulty resolver input stage in the power drive unit"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Generator disabled, engine-only or no-start condition"],["shop_only",[100,3500]],"c"],"P0A4F":["Generator Position Sensor Circuit Intermittent","P0A4F flags a generator resolver signal that drops out or jumps briefly and then recovers, as opposed to a steady fault. No manufacturer documents P0A4F publicly.",[["h","Loose or fretting terminals in the resolver connectors"],["m","Chafed resolver cable that breaks contact under vibration"],["l","Heat-related cracks in wiring or solder joints"]],["Hybrid warning that appears and clears again","Short loss of electric drive or jerky power delivery","Fault often linked to bumps or heat"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0A50":["Generator Position Sensor Circuit Overspeed","P0A50 says the generator speed derived from its resolver has exceeded the permitted maximum. Mitsubishi documents it for the 2023 Outlander PHEV: with at least 8.0 V at the generator control unit and the position sensor itself judged healthy, a speed of 15,500 rpm or more lasting 0.5 s stores P0A50, and the repair path ends with replacing the generator.",[["h","Internal generator fault (Mitsubishi repair path ends with generator replacement)"],["m","Mechanical fault in the transaxle or clutch letting the generator overrun"],["l","Incorrect speed calculation in the power drive unit"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Generator shut off, reduced power or engine-only drive"],["shop_only",[300,5000]],"c"],"P0A51":["Drive Motor A Current Sensor Circuit","P0A51 reports a fault in the circuit of the current sensor that measures the phase current of drive motor A. The inverter needs this value for torque control and overcurrent protection.",[["h","Current sensor in the inverter assembly failed"],["m","Sensor supply or signal line interrupted inside the inverter"],["l","Evaluation circuit on the motor control board faulty"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Drive motor A disabled, vehicle may not move or runs on engine only"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A52":["Drive Motor A Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A52 means the current sensor of drive motor A delivers values that are electrically valid but do not fit the operating state. Typical checks compare the reading at zero current with its expected offset, or compare redundant sensors with each other.",[["h","Sensor offset drifted beyond the compensation range"],["m","Control and monitoring sensor disagree due to one degraded sensor"],["l","Electrical interference or poor ground on the sensor signal"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Reduced or jerky electric drive","Drive motor A disabled, engine-only operation"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A53":["Drive Motor A Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0A53 is stored when the current sensor signal of drive motor A stays below its valid range, typically from a signal line shorted to ground, a lost sensor supply or a failed Hall element. No manufacturer documents P0A53 publicly.",[["h","Sensor signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply voltage missing"],["m","Hall element of the current sensor failed"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","High-voltage system switched off, vehicle may not move"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A54":["Drive Motor A Current Sensor Circuit High","P0A54 signals that the current sensor output of drive motor A sits above its valid range, as caused by an open ground, a short to the sensor supply or an internal sensor fault that saturates the output. No manufacturer documents P0A54 publicly.",[["h","Open ground of the current sensor"],["m","Signal line shorted to the sensor supply"],["m","Internal sensor fault with saturated output"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Drive motor A disabled or power strongly reduced"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A55":["Drive Motor B Current Sensor Circuit","P0A55 is the general circuit fault of the current sensor that measures the current of drive motor B, typically the second traction motor, for example on a rear axle. The inverter needs this signal for torque control and to protect its power switches.",[["h","Current sensor of drive motor B failed (usually inside the inverter)"],["m","Sensor supply or signal line interrupted or shorted"],["l","Evaluation circuit on the motor B control board faulty"]],["Hybrid or electric drive warning with MIL","Drive motor B disabled, loss of electric all-wheel drive or reduced power"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A56":["Drive Motor B Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A56 means the current sensor of drive motor B delivers electrically valid values that do not match the operating state, for example a zero-current offset outside its band or a reading that disagrees with a redundant sensor. No manufacturer documents P0A56 publicly.",[["h","Current sensor offset drifted or offset learning disturbed"],["m","Current sensor of drive motor B degraded"],["l","Electrical interference on the sensor signal"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Reduced or missing drive from motor B"],["shop_only",[200,4500]],"c"],"P0A57":["Drive Motor B Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0A57 is set when the signal of the drive motor B current sensor stays below its valid range, usually from a signal line shorted to ground, a missing sensor supply or a failed Hall element. No manufacturer documents P0A57 publicly.",[["h","Sensor signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply voltage missing"],["m","Hall element of the current sensor failed"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Drive motor B or the whole high-voltage system switched off"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A58":["Drive Motor B Current Sensor Circuit High","P0A58 reports that the output of the drive motor B current sensor sits above its valid range, for instance from an open sensor ground, a short to the sensor supply or an internal fault that saturates the output. No manufacturer documents P0A58 publicly.",[["h","Sensor ground open or high resistance"],["m","Signal line shorted to the sensor supply"],["m","Current sensor with saturated output"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Drive motor B disabled, reduced power"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A59":["Generator Current Sensor Circuit","P0A59 is the general circuit fault of the current sensor on the hybrid generator, the electric machine driven by the engine that charges the battery and often starts the engine. Its inverter uses the current reading to control generator torque.",[["h","Generator current sensor failed (usually inside the inverter)"],["m","Sensor supply or signal line interrupted or shorted"],["l","Evaluation circuit on the generator control board faulty"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Engine runs constantly or the high-voltage battery is no longer charged","Vehicle may not enter ready mode"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A5A":["Generator Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0A5A means the generator current sensor delivers values that are electrically valid but implausible, for example a wrong zero offset or phase currents that no longer add up. No hybrid manufacturer documents P0A5A publicly with criteria.",[["h","Generator current sensor offset drifted or offset learning disturbed"],["m","Generator current sensor degraded"],["l","Control module calibration requiring an update"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Reduced charging or engine running more often"],["shop_only",[150,4500]],"c"],"P0A5B":["Generator Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0A5B indicates that the signal of the generator current sensor stays below its valid range, as caused by a signal line shorted to ground, a lost sensor supply or a failed sensing element. No hybrid manufacturer documents P0A5B publicly with criteria.",[["h","Sensor signal line shorted to ground"],["m","Sensor supply voltage missing"],["m","Sensing element of the generator current sensor failed"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","High-voltage system switched off or battery no longer charged"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A5C":["Generator Current Sensor Circuit High","P0A5C means the generator current sensor output lies above its valid range, typically from an open sensor ground, a short to the sensor supply or a saturated sensor. No hybrid manufacturer documents P0A5C publicly with criteria.",[["h","Sensor ground open or high resistance"],["m","Signal line shorted to the sensor supply"],["m","Generator current sensor with saturated output"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Generator disabled, engine start or battery charging affected"],["shop_only",[300,4500]],"c"],"P0A5D":["Drive Motor A Phase U Current","P0A5D concerns the current in phase U of drive motor A, as seen by the inverter. Audi documents it on the 2021-23 Q5 e quattro (bulletin 2069917/1): P0A5D00, often with P0A6000 and P0A6300, is stored in the electric drive control module J841, the cluster reads Drive system: fault and the car may not start or drive.",[["h","High-voltage phase cable or weld joint to drive motor A faulty (Audi Q5 e quattro)"],["m","Phase current sensor in the inverter failed"],["m","Internal inverter fault"],["l","Winding fault in phase U of drive motor A"]],["Drive system fault message and MIL","Vehicle does not start or the drive system cannot be activated"],["shop_only",[400,6000]],"c"],"P0A5E":["Drive Motor A Phase U Current Low","P0A5E means too little current flows, or is sensed, in phase U of drive motor A, the classic sign of an open phase. GM describes the check for the motor control processor of the 2016 Chevrolet Volt and in its 2018 hybrid tables: with the inverter in normal PWM above 50 V and a squared current command above 900 A2 (2016) or 4,900 A2 (2018), the absolute U current is sampled while the rotor is within 30 degrees of the U phase axis; below 9 A for 0.4 s of a 0.6 s window, P0A5E sets as Type A, MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Open circuit in the phase U cable, connector or terminal"],["m","Phase U current sensor reads zero"],["m","Phase U switch of the inverter not conducting"],["l","Open winding of phase U in drive motor A"]],["MIL and propulsion system warning","Rough or reduced electric drive, possible engine-only operation"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A5F":["Drive Motor A Phase U Current High","P0A5F reports excessive current in phase U of drive motor A, a condition the inverter treats as overcurrent. Hyundai documents it on the 2017 Ioniq Hybrid (bulletin 17-01-057-1, campaign T2J): coolant leaking inside the traction motor set the Check Engine and hybrid lights with P0A5F, P0A62 or P0A65, often together with the isolation fault P0AA6; drivers noticed weak acceleration and a dead air conditioner.",[["h","Internal coolant leak in the traction motor causing insulation breakdown (Hyundai Ioniq Hybrid)"],["m","Shorted winding or phase lead of drive motor A"],["m","Inverter power stage fault in phase U"],["l","Current sensor falsely reporting overcurrent"]],["Check Engine and hybrid warning lights","Reduced power when accelerating","Air conditioning inoperative (Hyundai Ioniq Hybrid)"],["shop_only",[500,7000]],"c"],"P0A60":["Drive Motor A Phase V Current","P0A60 covers the current in phase V of drive motor A. Toyota uses it with sub codes: a field report filed with NHTSA describes a 2006 Highlander Hybrid whose master warning lit and which would not move; P0A60 sub code 501 had set first, followed by P0A63, P0A78 and P0AEF, all returning at once after clearing, and the inverter assembly was replaced.",[["h","Internal fault in the inverter assembly (Toyota Highlander Hybrid)"],["m","High-voltage phase V cable or weld joint faulty (Audi Q5 e quattro)"],["m","Phase V current sensor failed"],["l","Winding fault in phase V of drive motor A"]],["Master warning or drive system fault message, MIL","Vehicle will not move or start"],["shop_only",[400,6000]],"c"],"P0A61":["Drive Motor A Phase V Current Low","P0A61 indicates that phase V of drive motor A carries, or reports, too little current when it should be near its peak, pointing to an open phase V circuit. GM uses the same peak-axis test as for phase U on the 2016 Chevrolet Volt motor control processor and in its 2018 hybrid tables, here with the rotor within 30 degrees of the V phase axis: inverter above 50 V in normal PWM, squared current command above 900 A2 in 2016 and 4,900 A2 from 2018, a peak phase current under 9 A for 0.4 s within 0.6 s, Type A with MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Open circuit in the phase V cable, connector or terminal"],["m","Phase V current sensor reads zero"],["m","Phase V switch of the inverter not conducting"],["l","Open winding of phase V in drive motor A"]],["MIL and propulsion system warning","Jerky or weak electric drive"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A62":["Drive Motor A Phase V Current High","P0A62 signals overcurrent in phase V of drive motor A. Hyundai lists it in bulletin 17-01-057-1 for the 2017 Ioniq Hybrid next to P0A5F, P0A65 and the isolation fault P0AA6: the bulletin traces them to coolant leaking inside the traction motor and checks the high-voltage insulation first.",[["h","Coolant inside the traction motor with loss of insulation (Hyundai Ioniq Hybrid)"],["m","Phase V lead or winding shorted to ground or to another phase"],["m","Inverter power stage fault in phase V"]],["Check Engine and hybrid warning lights","Weak acceleration, air conditioning inoperative"],["shop_only",[500,7000]],"c"],"P0A63":["Drive Motor A Phase W Current","P0A63 concerns the current in phase W of drive motor A. In the Toyota field reports filed with NHTSA it always appears beside P0A60: on a 2006 Highlander Hybrid with sub code 502, after P0A60 sub code 501, together with P0A78 and P0AEF, master warning on and no drive; the inverter assembly was replaced.",[["h","Internal fault in the inverter assembly (Toyota Highlander Hybrid)"],["m","High-voltage phase W cable or weld joint faulty (Audi Q5 e quattro)"],["m","Phase W current sensor failed"]],["Master warning or drive system fault message, MIL","Vehicle will not move or start"],["shop_only",[400,6000]],"c"],"P0A64":["Drive Motor A Phase W Current Low","P0A64 means phase W of drive motor A shows too little current at the moment it should peak, typically an open W lead or a W sensor stuck at zero. GM completes its three-phase sequence with this code on the 2016 Chevrolet Volt motor control processor and in the 2018 hybrid tables: when the rotor sits within 30 degrees of the W phase axis, the inverter runs normal PWM above 50 V and the squared current command exceeds 900 A2 (2016) or 4,900 A2 (2018), a W peak below 9 A for 0.4 s of a 0.6 s window sets P0A64 as Type A, MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Open circuit in the phase W cable, connector or terminal"],["m","Phase W current sensor reads zero"],["m","Phase W switch of the inverter not conducting"],["l","Open winding of phase W in drive motor A"]],["MIL and propulsion system warning","Uneven or reduced electric drive torque"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A65":["Drive Motor A Phase W Current High","P0A65 reports overcurrent in phase W of drive motor A. It is the third phase code in Hyundai bulletin 17-01-057-1 for the 2017 Ioniq Hybrid, campaign T2J: with an internal coolant leak in the traction motor, P0A65, P0A5F or P0A62 set with Check Engine and hybrid lights, often joined by the isolation fault P0AA6.",[["h","Internal coolant leak in the traction motor (Hyundai Ioniq Hybrid)"],["m","Phase W lead or winding shorted to ground or to another phase"],["m","Inverter power stage fault in phase W"],["l","Current sensor falsely reporting overcurrent"]],["Check Engine and hybrid warning lights","Reduced power when accelerating","Air conditioning inoperative (Hyundai Ioniq Hybrid)"],["shop_only",[500,7000]],"c"],"P0A66":["Drive Motor B Phase U Current","P0A66 is the general fault code for the current in phase U of drive motor B, the second electric machine of a two-motor hybrid or an electric rear-axle motor. No manufacturer documents P0A66 publicly; GM, for instance, only uses the open-phase code P0A67 for this phase.",[["h","Phase U current sensor of motor B failed (inside the inverter)"],["m","Phase U high-voltage cable or terminal between inverter and motor B faulty"],["m","Internal fault in the motor B power stage"],["l","Winding fault in phase U of drive motor B"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Motor B switched off, less power or no electric all-wheel drive"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A67":["Drive Motor B Phase U Current Low","P0A67 reports that phase U of drive motor B carries too little current at the moment it should peak, which GM interprets as an open circuit in that phase. In the 2016 Chevrolet Volt tables for motor control processor B the test runs while the rotor is within 30 degrees of the U axis, the inverter is above 50 V in normal PWM and the squared current command exceeds 900 A²; a peak below 9 A for 0.4 s within a 0.6 s window sets a Type A fault with MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Open circuit in the phase U cable, connector or terminal of motor B"],["m","Phase U switch of the motor B inverter not conducting"],["m","Phase U current sensor stuck at zero"],["l","Open winding of phase U in drive motor B"]],["MIL on the first trip and propulsion system warning","Jerky or weak electric drive, reduced power"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A68":["Drive Motor B Phase U Current High","P0A68 means the current measured in phase U of drive motor B rises above its plausible maximum, either because a real overcurrent flows, for example through damaged insulation, a winding short or a shorted power switch, or because the phase sensor reads too high. No manufacturer documents P0A68 publicly.",[["h","Winding short or insulation breakdown in phase U of motor B (e.g. coolant ingress)"],["m","Shorted power switch in the phase U leg of the inverter"],["m","Phase U current sensor reading too high (gain or offset fault)"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL, often with an insulation fault code","Sudden loss of drive from motor B"],["shop_only",[400,7000]],"c"],"P0A69":["Drive Motor B Phase V Current","P0A69 flags a general fault in the current of phase V of drive motor B. No manufacturer documents P0A69 publicly.",[["h","Phase V current sensor of motor B failed"],["m","Fault on the motor B inverter board or power stage"],["l","Phase V high-voltage cable or terminal loose or damaged"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Reduced power, motor B no longer contributes"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A6A":["Drive Motor B Phase V Current Low","P0A6A is GM's open-circuit test for phase V of drive motor B. The controller checks each phase on its own as the rotor turns; for phase V it looks at the window of 30 degrees either side of the V axis, where the current should be largest.",[["h","Phase V lead, connector or bolted terminal of motor B open"],["m","Phase V switch of the motor B inverter not switching"],["m","Phase V current sensor output stuck at zero"],["l","Open phase V winding in motor B"]],["MIL and propulsion system warning after the first trip","Vibration or uneven torque from motor B before shutdown"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A6B":["Drive Motor B Phase V Current High","P0A6B is stored when phase V of drive motor B reports more current than the operating state allows. No manufacturer documents P0A6B publicly.",[["h","Phase V current sensor gain or offset drifted high"],["m","Shorted power switch in the phase V leg"],["m","Winding or insulation fault in phase V of motor B"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Motor B cut out, reduced acceleration"],["shop_only",[400,7000]],"c"],"P0A6C":["Drive Motor B Phase W Current","P0A6C covers a general fault in the phase W current of drive motor B. No manufacturer documents P0A6C publicly.",[["h","Phase W high-voltage lead or terminal to motor B damaged or loose"],["m","Phase W current sensor failed"],["m","Internal fault of the motor B inverter"]],["Hybrid warning lamp and MIL","Loss of electric drive on the axle driven by motor B"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A6D":["Drive Motor B Phase W Current Low","P0A6D completes GM's set of open-phase tests for drive motor B with phase W. Because each phase is only judged while the rotor sits within 30 degrees of its own axis, the code points to W alone rather than to the motor as a whole.",[["h","Phase W lead or terminal of motor B interrupted"],["m","Phase W switch of the inverter not conducting"],["m","Phase W current sensor stuck at zero"],["l","Open phase W winding in motor B"]],["MIL on the first trip with propulsion warning","Rough or reduced electric drive"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A6E":["Drive Motor B Phase W Current High","P0A6E indicates excessive current, measured or real, in phase W of drive motor B. No manufacturer documents P0A6E publicly.",[["h","Insulation breakdown or winding short in phase W (moisture, coolant)"],["m","Shorted switch in the phase W leg of the inverter"],["l","Phase W current sensor reading too high"]],["MIL and hybrid warning, possibly with an isolation fault","Motor B no longer drives; possible air conditioning shutdown"],["shop_only",[400,7000]],"c"],"P0A6F":["Generator Phase U Current","P0A6F is the general fault code for the phase U current of the hybrid generator, the motor-generator that is mainly driven by the engine, starts it and charges the battery, such as Toyota's MG1 or Hyundai's hybrid starter-generator. No manufacturer documents P0A6F publicly; Toyota's published field reports show generator codes for phases V and W only.",[["h","Generator phase U current sensor failed"],["m","Fault in the generator section of the inverter assembly"],["l","Generator phase U cable or terminal faulty"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Engine cannot be started electrically or battery is no longer charged"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A70":["Generator Phase U Current Low","P0A70 means phase U of the hybrid generator carries or reports too little current while the generator is loaded, typical of an open phase or a sensor stuck near zero. No manufacturer documents P0A70 publicly.",[["h","Open circuit in the generator phase U lead or terminal"],["m","Generator phase U current sensor stuck near zero"],["m","Phase U switch in the generator inverter not conducting"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Engine runs rough at start or battery charge level keeps dropping"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A71":["Generator Phase U Current High","P0A71 is set when the current in phase U of the hybrid generator exceeds its permitted level. No manufacturer documents P0A71 publicly.",[["m","Shorted power switch in the generator phase U leg"],["m","Winding or insulation short in generator phase U"],["m","Faulty phase U current sensor or its evaluation in the inverter"]],["Master warning or hybrid warning with MIL","Hybrid system shuts down; vehicle may not move or restart"],["shop_only",[400,7000]],"c"],"P0A72":["Generator Phase V Current","P0A72 reports a fault in the phase V current of the hybrid generator. Toyota uses it: a field report filed with NHTSA covers a 2006 Highlander Hybrid with the MIL on and P0A72 stored together with P0A75 for generator phase W, P0A60 and P0A63 for drive motor A, and inverter performance codes including P0A78; after checking connector fit and the resistance of the three-phase cables, the inverter/converter assembly was replaced.",[["h","Internal fault in the inverter/converter assembly (Toyota Highlander Hybrid)"],["m","Generator phase V current sensor failed"],["l","Poor pin fit or damaged three-phase cable to the generator"]],["MIL and master warning, several hybrid codes at once","Vehicle may not move or enter ready mode"],["shop_only",[500,6000]],"c"],"P0A73":["Generator Phase V Current Low","P0A73 indicates that phase V of the hybrid generator delivers or reports too little current under load. No manufacturer documents P0A73 publicly.",[["h","Generator phase V current sensor reading near zero"],["m","Generator phase V lead or terminal open"],["l","Phase V switch in the generator inverter not conducting"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Engine runs more often, battery charge does not recover"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A74":["Generator Phase V Current High","P0A74 appears when phase V of the hybrid generator shows more current than its limit allows. No manufacturer documents P0A74 publicly.",[["m","Phase V sensor offset drifted or offset correction failed"],["m","Power switch in the generator phase V leg not turning off"],["m","Winding or insulation short in generator phase V"]],["Master warning or hybrid warning with MIL","Hybrid system shutdown, possibly no propulsion"],["shop_only",[400,7000]],"c"],"P0A75":["Generator Phase W Current","P0A75 stands for a fault in the phase W current of the hybrid generator. Toyota documents it in practice: in a field report from an NHTSA investigation of 2006 Highlander Hybrid inverters, P0A75 appeared next to P0A72, the drive motor phase codes P0A60 and P0A63 and inverter performance codes, and the root cause was inside the inverter/converter, which was replaced.",[["h","Internal fault in the inverter/converter assembly (Toyota Highlander Hybrid)"],["m","Generator phase W current sensor failed"],["l","Generator phase W cable or connector faulty"]],["MIL and master warning, usually with other hybrid codes","Vehicle will not become ready or will not move"],["shop_only",[500,6000]],"c"],"P0A76":["Generator Phase W Current Low","P0A76 signals that phase W of the hybrid generator conducts or reports too little current while the generator should be producing or absorbing torque. No manufacturer documents P0A76 publicly.",[["h","Loose terminal, cracked lead or corroded connector in generator phase W"],["m","Generator phase W current sensor stuck near zero"],["l","Open phase W winding in the generator"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL","Engine start harsh or delayed, charging reduced"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0A77":["Generator Phase W Current High","P0A77 is the excess-current code for phase W of the hybrid generator. No manufacturer documents P0A77 publicly.",[["m","Turn-to-turn short or insulation breakdown in generator phase W"],["m","Power switch in the generator phase W leg failed closed"],["m","Generator phase W current sensor with excessive gain"]],["Master warning or hybrid warning with MIL","Hybrid system shuts down during charging or engine start"],["shop_only",[400,7000]],"c"],"P0A78":["Drive Motor A Inverter Performance","P0A78 means the inverter stage feeding drive motor A has reported a power-switch fault. GM explains the check in its hybrid OBD tables: the IGBT module watches each phase for desaturation, a sign of a short between the upper and lower switch; if that status persists for about 2 ms the code sets as Type A, MIL on the first trip.",[["h","IGBT desaturation or short in the inverter power stage"],["h","Power inverter module defective (replaced on Jeep Wrangler 4xe)"],["m","Hybrid control software requiring update"],["l","Shorted winding or phase cable of drive motor A"]],["MIL and hybrid or high-voltage warning","No electric drive, engine-only operation","Stop safely warning on some models"],["shop_only",[200,6000]],"c"],"P0A79":["Drive Motor B Inverter Performance","P0A79 is the drive motor B counterpart of P0A78: the inverter stage for motor B has detected a power-switch fault. In GM hybrid OBD tables the IGBT module reports a desaturation status on a high or low side switch of any phase; persisting for about 2 ms it sets P0A79 as Type A with MIL on the first trip.",[["h","IGBT desaturation or short in the motor B power stage"],["h","Power inverter module defective"],["m","Loose high-voltage cable at the inverter terminal (Pacifica Hybrid)"],["m","Hybrid control software requiring update"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","No electric drive, engine-only operation","Loss of propulsion in severe cases"],["shop_only",[200,6000]],"c"],"P0A7A":["Generator Inverter Performance","P0A7A reports that the inverter stage driving the generator has signalled a fault, for example overcurrent or a failed power switch. Toyota uses the code across its hybrids and splits it with information codes.",[["h","Internal generator inverter fault with overcurrent (Toyota information code 122)"],["h","Inverter assembly defective"],["m","Fault in the generator inside the transaxle"],["l","Insufficient inverter cooling stressing the power stage"]],["MIL, master warning and Check Hybrid System message","Reduced power or vehicle will not enter ready mode"],["shop_only",[1000,8000]],"c"],"P0A7B":["Battery Energy Control Module Requested MIL Illumination","P0A7B is a request code: the battery energy control module, which has found an emissions-relevant fault of its own, asks the engine control module to switch on the MIL, and the engine controller stores P0A7B. The actual cause sits in a separate code in the battery module.",[["h","Emissions-relevant fault stored in the battery energy control module"],["m","Air trapped at the battery coolant pump (Corvette E-Ray, with P0E3C)"],["l","Frozen power supply relay of the drive motor control module (Subaru Crosstrek Hybrid)"]],["MIL on","Additional codes stored in the battery control module"],["medium",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0A7C":["Motor Electronics Over Temperature","P0A7C reports that the electronics controlling the hybrid motors, mainly the inverter, have run hotter than allowed. Ford uses the code on its hybrids and stores it in the PCM.",[["h","Coolant pump of the motor electronics cooling loop failed"],["m","Low coolant or air in the electronics cooling loop"],["m","PCM calibration triggering the code without real overheating (Ford Fusion Hybrid)"],["l","Blocked electronics radiator or faulty cooling fan"]],["Wrench or hybrid warning light","Reduced power, especially under high load or heat"],["medium",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0A7D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack State of Charge Low","The battery controller watches whether the traction or mild-hybrid pack stays inside its usable state-of-charge (SOC) window. GM sets P0A7D (Type B, two trips) when SOC stays below a program threshold (30 % or 10.5 %) for more than half of a drive cycle longer than 50 km, after allowing generator time to recover.",[["h","Pack discharged by long standing time or repeated crank/no-start attempts"],["m","Generator, DC/DC or charging path not replenishing the pack"],["m","48 V battery in low-voltage protection state (relays open)"],["l","Weak or self-discharging cell modules"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning","No electric driving or reduced motor assist","Engine runs longer to recharge the pack"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A7E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Over Temperature","The pack controller compares the hottest module temperature with a protection limit. GM sets P0A7E, mostly as Type A (MIL after one trip), when the maximum module temperature exceeds a program limit between about 61 and 73.5 °C, unless a temperature sensor fault is active.",[["h","Battery cooling fan inoperative or intake/filter clogged"],["m","Battery controller software logic (update released by Subaru, Porsche)"],["m","Low 12 V system voltage stopping the cooling fan"],["l","Aged cells with high internal resistance heating up"]],["Hybrid system warning and check engine light","Reduced electric assist and regeneration","Auto start-stop no longer works"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0A7F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Deterioration","This is the state-of-health monitor that CARB requires for hybrid storage systems. GM compares the calculated pack power capability with an end-of-life table at mid state of charge and sets P0A7F as Type B (two trips) when it falls short.",[["h","Pack capacity or power capability lost through ageing"],["m","Dust or debris in the battery cooling fan or filter overheating cells"],["m","Battery controller software misjudging health (Honda, Mercedes)"],["l","Individual weak modules pulling the pack down"]],["Check engine or hybrid/IMA warning light","Weak acceleration assist, especially from a stop","Engine runs more often, higher fuel consumption"],["shop_only",[150,8000]],"c"],"P0A80":["Replace Hybrid/EV Battery Pack","The battery controller has concluded that the pack is at end of life. In GM tables this is a resistance test: the maximum cell or pack resistance, measured from current and voltage swings, exceeds a temperature-dependent end-of-life threshold, and the code sets as Type A after one trip.",[["h","Cell or module resistance above end-of-life limit"],["m","Clogged battery cooling fan or intake filter (Toyota)"],["l","Controller software misreading a replaced pack (Honda Accord Hybrid 2018)"]],["Check engine and hybrid system warning on","Noticeably weaker electric assist","Higher fuel consumption, engine runs more"],["shop_only",[150,10000]],"c"],"P0A81":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Control Circuit/Open","The controller watches the output that drives battery cooling fan 1 for an open circuit. In the 2016 GM table the fan is commanded at low duty (under 10 %) and the circuit voltage sits between about 1.6 and 5.85 V instead of being pulled low; 16 failures in 20 samples set it as Type B.",[["h","Fan motor or its internal electronics open"],["m","Loose or corroded fan connector, broken control wire"],["m","Blown fan supply fuse"],["l","Faulty output stage in the battery control module"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning, often no other complaint","Battery cooling fan does not run when commanded","Reduced hybrid power in hot conditions"],["medium",[80,900]],"c"],"P0A82":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Performance/Stuck Off","This code covers a fan 1 that is electrically intact but does not deliver the requested cooling, up to standing still. No manufacturer publishes code-exact criteria; CARB requires a functional check of battery cooling where feasible.",[["h","Dust or debris on fan blades or in the duct"],["h","Clogged or covered battery air intake or filter"],["m","Worn or seized fan motor bearings"],["l","Faulty fan speed feedback signal"]],["Hybrid warning or check engine light","Fan audibly weak or silent under load","Reduced electric assist in warm weather"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0A83":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Stuck On","P0A83 means battery cooling fan 1 keeps turning although the controller has switched it off. No manufacturer publicly documents this exact code.",[["m","Fan control wire shorted to supply voltage"],["m","Welded fan relay keeps power applied"],["m","Fan's internal speed controller ignores off command"],["l","Battery control module output stuck active"]],["Battery fan heard running with the vehicle off or idle","Discharged 12 V battery after parking","Hybrid or check engine warning"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0A84":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Control Circuit Low","The controller detects a short to ground on the control output of battery cooling fan 1. GM tests it while the fan is commanded at low duty: a circuit voltage below about 0.26 V counts as a failure, and 16 fails in 20 samples at 250 ms set a Type B code (MIL after two trips).",[["h","Fan control wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in the fan's control input"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the fan connector"],["l","Defective output stage in the controller"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning after two trips","Battery cooling fan does not follow commands","Reduced hybrid power when the pack gets warm"],["medium",[60,700]],"c"],"P0A85":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Control Circuit High","The controller detects a short to voltage on the control output of battery cooling fan 1. GM runs the test while the fan is commanded between 11 % and 100 % duty: a circuit voltage above 9 V counts as a failure, and 16 fails in 20 samples set a Type B code with MIL after two trips.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Fan control input internally open or shorted to its supply"],["l","Corroded connector creating leakage to supply"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning after two trips","Battery fan runs constantly or not at all","Possible 12 V battery drain when parked"],["medium",[60,700]],"c"],"P0A86":["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor A Circuit","The 14 volt power module is the DC/DC converter that feeds the 12 V network of a hybrid or electric vehicle from the high-voltage battery; GM calls it the Accessory Power Module (APM). Current sensor A measures the current flowing through this converter.",[["h","Current sensor inside the 14 V power module faulty (usually not replaceable separately)"],["m","Damaged wiring or corroded connector between module and hybrid controller"],["l","Software fault in the module or hybrid controller"]],["Hybrid or charging system warning lamp on","12 V battery discharges, low-voltage warnings","Possibly no other noticeable change in driving"],["shop_only",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0A87":["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","This code means the current sensor of the 14 V power module (DC/DC converter, GM: APM) delivers values that are electrically valid but implausible. GM's 2016 eAssist tables (hybrid control processor HPC1) describe a stuck-in-range test: once per key cycle, while the high-voltage contactors are open and then move to precharge, the converter should be off, so the averaged input current must be near zero.",[["h","Current sensor zero-point drift inside the power module"],["m","Converter actually conducting current when it should be off (internal module fault)"],["l","Ground or reference fault distorting the sensor signal"]],["MIL on after two drive cycles","Usually no driveability change","Possible reduced 12 V charging if the converter is limited"],["shop_only",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0A88":["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor A Circuit Low","P0A88 reports that the current sensor of the 14 V power module (DC/DC converter feeding the 12 V system) reads too low while the converter should be delivering power. GM documents it for the Accessory Power Module: in the 2013 eAssist tables the input current must stay below 0.5 A for 1.5 s within a 2 s window while calculated output power is 0.25 to 1.5 kW; the 2016 tables use 0.50 A or less in 640 of 800 samples, enabled only when the APM is on, the hood is closed and the 12 V target is reached.",[["h","Sensor signal shorted to ground or open"],["m","Current sensor in the power module failed"],["m","Corroded or loose module connector"]],["MIL and hybrid/charging warning","12 V battery not charged properly","Start/stop function may be disabled"],["shop_only",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0A89":["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor A Circuit High","P0A89 is set when the current sensor of the 14 V power module reports more current than the converter can physically carry. GM's eAssist tables show how: in 2013 the APM input current reading had to exceed 22.5 A for 1.5 s while calculated output power was 1.5 kW or less; in 2016 the limit is 45.25 A or more in 200 of 250 samples (about 5 s) with the APM commanded on and 9 to 32 V supply.",[["h","Sensor signal shorted to supply voltage"],["m","Current sensor in the power module failed"],["l","Open sensor ground pulling the signal high"]],["MIL and hybrid/charging warning","Converter output limited, 12 V voltage drops","Start/stop function may be disabled"],["shop_only",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0A8A":["14 Volt Power Module Current Sensor A Circuit Intermittent","P0A8A covers short dropouts or jumps in the signal of the current sensor in the 14 V power module, the DC/DC converter that charges the 12 V battery in hybrids and EVs. The signal is valid most of the time but briefly leaves its range or changes faster than physically possible.",[["h","Loose or fretted terminals at the module connector"],["m","Chafed wiring with intermittent short or open"],["l","Intermittent internal fault of the current sensor"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Occasional 12 V voltage fluctuations"],["medium",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0A8B":["14 Volt Power Module System Voltage","The 14 V power module (DC/DC converter) regulates the voltage of the 12 V network in hybrid and electric vehicles. P0A8B is the general code for a fault in the voltage this module produces or senses, without the more specific labels unstable, low, high or performance (P0A8C to P0A8F).",[["m","Internal fault of the DC/DC converter"],["m","Loose or corroded 12 V output cable or ground connection"],["l","Weak 12 V battery distorting the system voltage"]],["Charging or hybrid system warning","Electrical consumers behave erratically","12 V battery discharged"],["shop_only",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0A8C":["14 Volt Power Module System Voltage Unstable","P0A8C indicates that the output voltage of the 14 V power module, the DC/DC converter supplying the 12 V network of a hybrid or EV, fluctuates beyond what the control strategy accepts instead of settling at its target. Possible reasons include a poor 12 V connection, an aged 12 V battery that no longer buffers the network, or a regulation problem inside the converter.",[["h","Loose or corroded 12 V battery terminal or ground strap"],["m","Aged 12 V battery no longer buffering the network"],["m","Unstable regulation inside the DC/DC converter"]],["Flickering lights or display resets","Charging or hybrid system warning","Start/stop disabled"],["medium",[50,1800]],"c"],"P0A8D":["14 Volt Power Module System Voltage Low","P0A8D reports too low a voltage at the 14 V power module (DC/DC converter feeding the 12 V network). GM uses it in its 2013 eAssist tables as the circuit-low check of the APM low-voltage sensor: if that sensor reads below 1 V for 1.5 s within a 2 s window while ignition voltage is 9 to 32 V, the code sets; it passes again above 1.5 V.",[["h","Voltage sense line open or shorted to ground"],["m","Voltage sensor inside the power module failed"],["l","Converter not delivering output (fuse, output cable)"]],["MIL and charging/hybrid warning","12 V battery discharges","Start/stop disabled"],["shop_only",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0A8E":["14 Volt Power Module System Voltage High","P0A8E signals too high a voltage at the 14 V power module, the DC/DC converter supplying the 12 V side. In GM's 2013 eAssist tables it is the circuit-high check of the APM low-voltage sensor: a reading above 20 V for 1.5 s within a 2 s window, with ignition voltage 9 to 32 V, sets the code, and it passes below 19.5 V.",[["h","Voltage sense line shorted to a higher potential"],["m","Voltage sensor inside the power module failed"],["l","Converter regulating its output too high"]],["MIL and charging/hybrid warning","Very bright lights or failing bulbs if overvoltage is real","Converter may be shut down"],["shop_only",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0A8F":["14 Volt Power Module System Performance","P0A8F means the 14 V power module (DC/DC converter for the 12 V network) does not perform as expected even though its signals are electrically in range. GM's 2013 eAssist tables use it as a correlation test: with the APM commanded on and ignition voltage 9 to 32 V, the APM's own low-voltage reading and the run/crank voltage measured by the controller must agree within 4.5 V.",[["h","High resistance in 12 V output cable, fuse link or ground"],["m","Drifting voltage sensor in the module"],["m","Converter unable to hold its target voltage"]],["MIL and charging/hybrid warning","Low or fluctuating 12 V voltage"],["shop_only",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0A90":["Drive Motor A Performance","P0A90 reports that electric drive motor A does not behave as commanded. In GM's eAssist mild hybrid the motor/generator is belt-driven from the crankshaft, so GM uses P0A90 to detect a slipping or broken belt: the motor speed is compared with the engine speed.",[["h","Slipping, worn or broken drive belt on belt-driven motor/generators"],["m","Faulty belt tensioner or contamination on the belt"],["m","Motor, position sensor or inverter fault in full hybrids"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Belt squeal or auto-stop/start not working","Reduced electric assist or limp mode"],["medium",[60,3000]],"c"],"P0A91":["Drive Motor B Performance","P0A91 is set when the second electric drive motor (motor B) of a hybrid or electric vehicle does not deliver the expected speed or torque, although no electrical circuit fault is detected. Motor B exists only on systems with two traction motors, such as power-split or dual-motor layouts.",[["m","Inverter B fault limiting motor current"],["m","Motor position sensor (resolver) signal error"],["l","Mechanical damage to motor B or its gearing"]],["Hybrid/EV system warning and MIL","Reduced power or limp mode"],["shop_only",[200,5000]],"c"],"P0A92":["Hybrid Generator Performance","P0A92 indicates that the generator of a hybrid system, the electric machine mainly used to convert engine power into electrical energy, does not reach the expected output, speed or torque. CARB requires manufacturers to describe how they monitor the generator so that faults preventing it from working are detected.",[["h","Hybrid controller or inverter software needing update (documented by FCA)"],["m","Inverter phase current sensing fault"],["l","Internal generator fault (winding, bearing, position sensor)"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Limp home (turtle) lamp, reduced power","Reduced charging of the high-voltage battery"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0A93":["Inverter A Cooling System Performance","The power inverter of a hybrid has its own coolant circuit with an electric pump, radiator and fan, separate from engine cooling. P0A93 is set when this circuit does not cool as intended.",[["h","Electric inverter coolant pump failed or seized"],["m","Low coolant or air in the inverter circuit"],["m","Pump wiring, fuse or relay fault"],["l","Radiator fan not running or radiator blocked"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","No EV mode, reduced power","Vehicle may not start or may stall"],["medium",[80,800]],"c"],"P0A94":["DC/DC Converter A Performance","P0A94 reports that a DC/DC converter in the high-voltage system does not perform as commanded. In Toyota hybrids this is the boost converter inside the inverter assembly, which raises the battery voltage before it reaches the motor inverters.",[["h","Damaged power transistors in the boost converter / inverter assembly"],["m","Thermal stress from insufficient inverter cooling"],["m","Outstanding recall or software update not performed"]],["MIL, hybrid system and other warning lamps","Fail-safe mode with greatly reduced power","Vehicle may stop and not restart"],["shop_only",[300,4000]],"c"],"P0A95":["High Voltage Fuse A","P0A95 means the battery management detects that a fuse in the high-voltage circuit has opened, usually by comparing the voltage before and after the fuse. Several makers document it: Jaguar for the I-PACE battery energy control module, where a shorted high-voltage auxiliary component blows fuses in the junction box and battery module; Porsche for the Taycan as the main high-voltage fuse with a vehicle that will not become ready to drive; Volvo, whose procedure compares the voltage after the fuses with pack voltage; and Audi, which found an intermittent false entry in hard cornering fixed by software.",[["h","Short circuit in a high-voltage auxiliary component (e.g. compressor, heater)"],["m","Fuse opened after overload or ageing"],["l","Faulty voltage measurement or software misdetection in the battery control module"]],["Vehicle not ready to drive, propulsion warning","MIL or yellow warning lamp","Climate compressor or heater inoperative"],["shop_only",[200,15000]],"c"],"P0A96":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Control Circuit/Open","Some packs use two cooling fans; P0A96 reports an open or general fault in the control circuit of the second one. Ford documents it code-exact: on 2005–12 Escape Hybrid and 2006–11 Mariner Hybrid the traction battery control module may store P0A96, often together with P0A81 for fan 1, and the repair is to replace both high-voltage battery fans with a kit (about 3 hours, pack removal included).",[["h","Fan 2 motor or electronics failed (Ford replaces both fans)"],["m","Loose connector or broken wire to fan 2"],["l","Blown fan fuse"]],["Code found during other diagnosis, often no complaint","Check engine or hybrid warning","Reduced hybrid power in hot conditions"],["shop_only",[150,1000]],"c"],"P0A97":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Performance/Stuck Off","P0A97 is the fan 2 counterpart of P0A82: the second battery cooling fan is electrically reachable but does not reach its requested speed or does not turn at all. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Dust or debris on fan 2 or in its duct"],["m","Worn or seized fan 2 bearings"],["m","Blocked air intake to the battery"],["l","Faulty speed feedback from fan 2"]],["Hybrid warning or check engine light","Weak airflow from the battery vents","Reduced electric assist in warm weather"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0A98":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Stuck On","P0A98 reports that battery cooling fan 2 keeps running after the controller has commanded it off; it mirrors P0A83 for fan 1. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["m","Welded fan 2 relay"],["m","Fan 2 control wire shorted to supply"],["m","Fan 2 internal controller ignores off command"]],["Battery fan audible after the vehicle is switched off","12 V battery discharged after parking","Hybrid or check engine warning"],["medium",[50,700]],"c"],"P0A99":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Control Circuit Low","P0A99 reports a short to ground on the control circuit of the second battery cooling fan. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; Ford's two-fan Escape/Mariner Hybrid packs show that a second fan exists (there with P0A96).",[["h","Fan 2 control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in fan 2 control input"],["l","Moisture or corrosion in the fan 2 connector"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning","Fan 2 does not run when commanded","Reduced hybrid power in hot conditions"],["medium",[60,700]],"c"],"P0A9A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 2 Control Circuit High","P0A9A reports a short to voltage on the control circuit of the second battery cooling fan. No manufacturer publishes this exact code; CARB requires the electronic outputs of battery thermal management to be checked for circuit faults.",[["h","Fan 2 control wire shorted to supply voltage"],["m","Fan 2 control input internally open or shorted to supply"],["l","Corroded connector with leakage to supply"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning","Fan 2 runs constantly or not at all","Possible 12 V battery drain when parked"],["medium",[60,700]],"c"],"P0A9B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor A Circuit","Temperature sensor A is one of the thermistors the battery controller uses to track cell or module temperature for cooling, charge limits and power limits. P0A9B is the general circuit code for this sensor: the controller finds an electrical fault it does not classify further as low, high or erratic.",[["h","Open, corroded or loose sensor wiring or connector inside the pack"],["m","Failed thermistor on the module or cell sensing board"],["l","Battery controller input or software fault"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning and check engine light","Reduced electric assist, regeneration or charging power","Battery cooling fan or pump runs more than usual"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0A9C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","This is a plausibility check: the reading of temperature sensor A is compared with the other sensors in the same pack, which should agree within a few degrees. GM sets P0A9C (Type B, two trips) when sensor A deviates from the average of the others by more than 25 °C for 50 of 67 samples (19OBDG02); Two-Mode hybrids use more than 15 °C against each other sensor for 90 of 100 samples, other programs more than 20 °C.",[["h","Thermistor drifted or poorly seated on its cell or module"],["m","High-resistance connection in the sensor circuit shifting the reading"],["m","Battery controller calibration causing false sets (GM Volt update)"],["l","Genuine local temperature difference from a cooling or cell problem"]],["Check engine light after two drive cycles","Reduced hybrid assist or charging power","Message such as battery too cold, plug in to warm (GM Volt)"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0A9D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","Sensor A is an NTC thermistor fed through a pull-up resistor, so the voltage falls as the temperature rises; a short to ground looks like an absurdly hot cell. GM sets P0A9D (Type B, two trips) when the converted value exceeds 160 °C in 8 of 10 samples (19OBDG02); Two-Mode hybrids use above 95 °C while the other sensors read below 70 °C, other programs above 87.5 °C for 1.4 s.",[["h","Sensor signal wire shorted to ground or chafed inside the pack"],["m","Internally shorted thermistor on the sensing board"],["l","Moisture or coolant in the sensor connector"],["l","Battery controller software causing false sets (GM Volt)"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Strongly reduced electric power or no EV mode","Battery cooling running at high speed without reason"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0A9E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","With the NTC thermistor of sensor A on a pull-up circuit, an open sensor or wire drives the input voltage toward the reference, which the controller reads as an extremely cold cell. GM sets P0A9E (Type B, two trips) when the value falls below -69 °C in 8 of 10 samples (19OBDG02); Two-Mode hybrids flag readings below -45 °C, other programs below -40 °C for 1.4 s.",[["h","Open sensor wire or unplugged connector inside the pack"],["m","Open (failed) thermistor on the sensing board"],["l","Signal shorted to the reference voltage"],["l","Battery controller software causing false sets (GM Volt)"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Battery too cold message although it is not cold","Little or no electric drive and weak regeneration"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0A9F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Cell temperature changes slowly, so a reading that jumps back and forth points to the circuit, not the battery. GM monitors this by summing the absolute sample-to-sample change of sensor A over a 10-sample window every 100 ms; if the sum exceeds 10, that window fails, and four failed windows out of five set P0A9F as Type B (two trips) in the 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables (16OBDG02, 19OBDG02).",[["h","Loose, fretted or corroded pin in the sensor connector"],["m","Partly broken sensor wire that opens with vibration or heat"],["l","Electrical interference on the sensor line from high-voltage components"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Temporarily reduced hybrid or EV power","Battery temperature value jumps in the scan tool data"],["shop_only",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0AA0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Positive Contactor Circuit","The positive main contactor is a high-voltage relay that connects the positive pole of the traction battery to the propulsion bus. P0AA0 is the general fault code for this contactor circuit, used when the controller sees a problem it does not split into stuck closed (P0AA1), stuck open (P0AA2) or the control-circuit codes P0AD9 to P0ADC.",[["h","Positive contactor or battery junction box worn or damaged"],["m","Fault in the contactor coil feed or its connector"],["l","Weak 12 V supply so the contactor does not pull in reliably"]],["Vehicle does not enter ready or propulsion-active mode","Hybrid or EV system warning with check engine light","Possible loss of propulsion while driving"],["shop_only",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0AA1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Positive Contactor Circuit Stuck Closed","P0AA1 means the positive main contactor conducts although it should be open, typically because its contacts have welded. GM checks this with all contactors commanded open for over 120 s (or the bus already below 9 % of pack voltage): more than 30 V on the positive bus in 4 of 6 samples sets Type B (two trips, 19OBDG02).",[["h","Positive contactor contacts welded, e.g. after high current or arcing"],["m","Battery or hybrid controller software (GM Volt 2019, Jeep 4xe updates)"],["m","Faulty battery disconnect unit (GM Bolt EV bulletin)"],["l","Bus voltage sensing error making an open contactor look closed"]],["Vehicle will not switch into ready or propulsion-active mode","Check engine light and service high voltage system message","Often stored together with P0AA4 or a slow discharge code such as P0C76"],["shop_only",[80,3000]],"c"],"P0AA2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Positive Contactor Circuit Stuck Open","P0AA2 means the positive main contactor does not close, or does not conduct, when the controller commands it closed, so the high-voltage bus cannot be energised from the pack. Audi documents it for the 2020-2022 Q5 e quattro and 2021-2022 A7 e quattro plug-in hybrids: the car does not start and the battery management module stores P0AA2 (often with P0AA5).",[["h","Oxidised contact surfaces after long standing or high humidity (Audi)"],["m","Failed battery junction box or burnt contactor"],["m","Contactor coil not energised due to wiring or 12 V supply fault"]],["Vehicle does not start or reach ready mode","Service high voltage battery or hybrid warning message","Possible sudden loss of propulsion if the contactor opens while driving"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0AA3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Negative Contactor Circuit","The negative main contactor switches the negative pole of the traction battery onto the propulsion bus; together with the positive contactor it isolates the pack completely when the car is off. P0AA3 is the general code for this contactor circuit when the fault is not reported more precisely as stuck closed (P0AA4), stuck open (P0AA5) or a control-circuit fault (P0ADD to P0AE0).",[["h","Negative contactor or battery junction box worn or damaged"],["m","Fault in the contactor coil wiring or connector"],["l","Battery controller driver or software fault"]],["Vehicle does not enter ready mode","Hybrid or EV system warning with check engine light","Charging may be refused"],["shop_only",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0AA4":["Hybrid/EV Battery Negative Contactor Circuit Stuck Closed","P0AA4 reports that the negative main contactor still conducts while it should be open, usually through welded contacts, so the pack is no longer fully isolated on the negative side. GM tests it once all contactors have been commanded open for more than 120 s, or the bus has already decayed below 9 % of pack voltage: over 30 V on the negative bus in 4 of 6 samples at 12.5 ms sets Type B (two trips, 19OBDG02).",[["h","Negative contactor contacts welded after high current or arcing"],["m","Battery or hybrid controller software (GM Volt 2019, Jeep 4xe updates)"],["m","Faulty battery disconnect unit (GM Bolt EV bulletin)"],["l","Negative bus voltage sensing error"]],["Vehicle will not start or switch into ready mode","Check engine light and high voltage system message","Often stored together with P0AA1 or the slow discharge code P0C76"],["shop_only",[80,3000]],"c"],"P0AA5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Negative Contactor Circuit Stuck Open","P0AA5 is set when the negative main contactor stays open or fails to conduct after the controller has commanded it closed, so the negative side of the high-voltage bus never connects. Audi reports it, alone or with P0AA2, on the 2020-2022 Q5 e quattro and 2021-2022 A7 e quattro plug-in hybrids with a no-start complaint; the stated cause is oxidation of the contact surfaces in the battery junction box after very long standing or extreme humidity.",[["h","Oxidised contact surfaces after long standing or high humidity (Audi)"],["m","Failed battery junction box or burnt negative contactor"],["l","Contactor coil not energised due to wiring or 12 V supply fault"]],["Vehicle does not start or reach ready mode","Hybrid or high voltage battery warning","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0AA6":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage System Isolation Fault","The high-voltage system floats free of the chassis, and an isolation monitor measures the resistance from each HV rail to body ground. P0AA6 sets when that resistance falls below a calibrated limit.",[["h","Coolant or moisture inside the battery pack, inverter or drive motor"],["m","Chafed or crushed orange HV cable shorting to the body"],["m","Insulation breakdown in the electric A/C compressor or a motor winding"],["l","Electrolyte leakage or contamination inside the battery sections"]],["Check engine and hybrid or EV system warning lights","Vehicle will not charge or will not enter ready mode after restart","Stop safely now message or reduced power"],["shop_only",[200,8000]],"c"],"P0AA7":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Isolation Sensor Circuit","To measure isolation, the battery controller reads the voltage of each HV rail against chassis ground through an isolation sensor circuit and can switch in a known bias resistor. P0AA7 means this measuring circuit itself does not work, so the controller can no longer judge isolation.",[["h","Failed isolation bias switch or measuring circuit inside the battery controller"],["m","Controller software misreading electrical noise (update available on some models)"],["l","Open or loose isolation sense lead or connector at the battery"]],["Check engine and EV system service warnings","Power reduced or speed limited until the system is switched off and on","Regenerative braking warning on some models"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AA8":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Isolation Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","The two isolation sensor readings, positive rail to ground and negative rail to ground, should together equal the total pack voltage. P0AA8 is the plausibility check of that relation.",[["h","Isolation measuring amplifier or reference in the battery controller out of tolerance"],["m","Pack voltage sense lead fault making the comparison value wrong"],["m","Controller software misreading electrical noise (update available on some models)"]],["Check engine and hybrid or EV system warnings","Reduced power or speed limit until restart","Isolation monitoring unavailable, so related faults may go unreported"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AA9":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Isolation Sensor Circuit Low","P0AA9 reports that the isolation sensor signal sits below its valid window, typically because the sense line or the measuring input is pulled toward ground or has lost its supply. GM's 2016 and 2019 tables flag it when the sensor reads under 5 V with the bias switch open in 320 of 400 samples (about 5 s), Type B, two trips; the 2013 GM table uses a negative mid-pack reading under 5 V with contactors closed.",[["h","Isolation sense line shorted to ground or open input"],["m","Failed isolation measuring circuit in the battery controller"],["l","Controller software misreading electrical noise (update available on some models)"]],["Check engine and EV system warning lights","Reduced power or speed limit on some models"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AAA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Isolation Sensor Circuit High","P0AAA is the high-side counterpart of P0AA9: the isolation sensor reports a rail-to-ground voltage above anything the pack can produce. GM's tables tie the limit to the pack: over 333 V in the 2016 table and over 416 V in the 2019 table, each in 320 of 400 samples with the bias switch open, Type B, two trips.",[["h","Open ground or reference in the isolation measuring circuit"],["m","Failed isolation input stage in the battery controller"],["l","Sense lead shorted to another HV potential inside the pack"]],["Check engine and hybrid or EV warning lights","Isolation monitoring disabled while the code is active"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AAB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Isolation Sensor Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AAB covers an isolation sensor signal that jumps or drops out briefly instead of staying wrong. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; GM's hybrid tables use only P0AA7 to P0AAA for the measuring circuit.",[["h","Electrical noise misinterpreted by controller software"],["m","Loose or corroded sense connector at the battery controller"],["l","Failing isolation measuring circuit inside the controller"]],["Hybrid or EV warning lights that come and go","Occasional power limitation that clears after restart"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AAC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor A Circuit","Air-cooled hybrid batteries carry a thermistor in the cooling air path; GM calls sensor A the inlet air sensor. The battery controller uses it to run the fan and to judge cooling.",[["h","Loose, corroded or damaged sensor connector or wiring at the air duct"],["m","Failed air temperature thermistor"],["l","Battery controller input or reference voltage fault"]],["Check engine or hybrid system warning light","Battery cooling fan runs constantly or at fixed speed","Reduced electric assist in hot conditions"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AAD":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0AAD is the plausibility check for battery air temperature sensor A: the signal is electrically valid but does not fit other temperatures. GM documents two methods.",[["h","Thermistor drifted or stuck at a plausible value"],["m","Sensor displaced from the air stream or duct blocked"],["l","Fault in the comparison sensor (outlet air or cell temperature)"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Battery cooling fan behaves unusually"],["medium",[80,600]],"u"],"P0AAE":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The battery air temperature sensor is an NTC thermistor, so a low signal voltage reads as very hot air. P0AAE sets when the signal stays below the lower limit, usually a short to ground.",[["h","Sensor signal wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Thermistor shorted internally"],["l","Battery controller input fault"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Battery cooling fan runs at high speed without reason","Reduced electric assist and regeneration"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AAF":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","Air temperature sensor A is the thermistor GM places at the cooling air inlet of air-cooled hybrid packs. Being an NTC type, a high signal voltage means the controller sees implausibly cold air, typically from an open wire or a lost ground.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire between sensor and battery controller"],["m","Connector at the air inlet duct loose or with pushed-back pin"],["m","Thermistor open internally"],["l","Battery controller input fault"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Scan tool shows inlet air near -40 °C regardless of weather","Battery fan runs on a fixed default speed"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AB0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AB0 flags a battery air temperature sensor A signal that jumps faster than real air temperature can change, usually a sign of a loose contact rather than a dead sensor. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM's two-mode and 2013 tables for sensor A only list the low, high and plausibility codes.",[["h","Loose or corroded contact at the inlet air sensor connector"],["m","Wire chafed through at the duct, intermittently touching ground"],["l","Electrical interference on the sensor line"]],["Hybrid warning light that comes and goes","Inlet air reading jumps in live data","Battery fan speed changes without obvious reason"],["medium",[80,500]],"u"],"P0AB1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor B Circuit","Air temperature sensor B is a second thermistor in the battery cooling air path; in GM's two-mode hybrid tables it is the outlet air sensor behind the modules. P0AB1 is the general circuit code for it, used when the controller sees an electrical fault without sorting it into low, high or erratic.",[["h","Damaged wiring or poor contact at the outlet air sensor"],["m","Air temperature sensor B failed"],["l","Battery controller input circuit fault"]],["Hybrid or check engine warning light","Battery fan runs on a default setting","Possibly reduced electric assist"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AB2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0AB2 checks whether air temperature sensor B reads sensibly. GM uses it on the full-size two-mode hybrids, where sensor B measures outlet air: in the 2011 and 2013 tables, with the battery fan commanded on, the outlet air may not be more than 10 °C warmer than the hottest battery module, 90 of 100 samples, Type B, two trips.",[["h","Outlet air thermistor drifted from its characteristic"],["m","Sensor displaced from its position in the air duct"],["m","Battery module temperature sensor used for comparison reads wrong"],["l","Corroded contact adding resistance"]],["Hybrid or check engine warning light","Outlet air reads clearly above module temperatures in live data","Usually no change in driving behavior"],["medium",[80,700]],"c"],"P0AB3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","P0AB3 means the signal of air temperature sensor B sits below its valid range. Because the sensor is an NTC thermistor, a low voltage translates into very hot air, so a short to ground is the usual cause.",[["h","Sensor B signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Thermistor shorted internally"],["l","Moisture or debris bridging the connector pins"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Battery fan runs loud even when the pack is cool","Reduced electric assist and regeneration"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AB4":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","P0AB4 reports a signal from air temperature sensor B above the valid range. For an NTC thermistor that reads as extremely cold air, which points to an open circuit, a broken ground or a disconnected plug.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire of sensor B"],["m","Connector not refitted after battery or duct work"],["m","Thermistor open internally"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Outlet air shows about -40 °C in live data","Battery fan on default operation"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AB5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AB5 is set when the signal of battery air temperature sensor B fluctuates in a way real air temperature cannot, typically from an intermittent contact. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM's two-mode tables, which use sensor B as the outlet air sensor, list only P0AB2 to P0AB4.",[["h","Loose or corroded terminal at sensor B"],["m","Partly broken wire near the outlet duct"],["l","Interference coupled into the sensor line"]],["Hybrid warning light that appears intermittently","Outlet air value jumps in live data","No noticeable driving change in most cases"],["medium",[80,500]],"u"],"P0AB6":["Engine Mount B Control Circuit/Open","Some engines sit on switchable or actively driven hydraulic mounts whose solenoid or actuator is energised by the engine controller. P0AB6 reports that the driver output for the second mount (B) sees an open or otherwise faulty circuit.",[["h","Broken or chafed control wire to the mount actuator"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the mount (splash zone)"],["m","Mount solenoid or actuator coil open internally"],["l","Output driver in the engine controller defective"]],["Check engine light on","Noticeable vibration at idle or with cylinder deactivation active","Cylinder deactivation may be suspended on some models"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0AB7":["Engine Mount B Control Circuit Low","P0AB7 means the engine controller sees the control line of active engine mount B pulled too low, the usual signature of a short to ground or an actuator coil with too little resistance. Ford's 2020-23 gasoline OBD summary lists it among the active-mount checks for bank 2: the output driver flags the fault continuously and the PCM matures it within roughly 5 seconds.",[["h","Control wire chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Mount solenoid coil shorted internally"],["m","Water ingress in the mount connector"]],["Check engine light on","Increased vibration felt through seat and steering wheel at idle"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0AB8":["Engine Mount B Control Circuit High","With P0AB8 the controller finds the control circuit of active engine mount B at too high a level, for example because the line has a short to battery voltage or the driver cannot pull it down. Ford names the code in its 2020-23 gasoline OBD summary as the bank 2 high fault of the active-mount circuit check, which runs continuously and needs about 5 seconds of driver-reported fault before a code is stored.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a battery-voltage circuit"],["m","Mount actuator defective"],["l","Output driver in the engine controller defective"]],["Check engine light on","Rougher feel at idle and when pulling away"],["medium",[40,450]],"c"],"P0AB9":["Hybrid/EV System Performance","Despite the broad title, GM uses P0AB9 for one concrete check: a failed engine auto-start or auto-stop in its hybrids, including an engine stall during that process. In the 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables the hybrid controller sets the code when the engine still reports no torque about 15 seconds into an auto-start although more than 20 Nm is commanded, provided fuel level is sufficient and no cam or crank fault is present.",[["h","Fuel delivery problem (weak pump, nearly empty tank, clogged filter)"],["m","Ignition or injector fault that prevents the engine from firing"],["m","Starter-generator or motor unable to spin the engine to start speed"],["l","Controller software needing an update"]],["Check engine light on","Engine hesitates, fails or stalls when restarting at a stop","Auto-stop disabled for the rest of the trip"],["hard",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0ABA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense A Circuit","The battery management system measures the total voltage of the high-voltage pack through a dedicated sense circuit and uses it for state of charge, power limits and contactor checks. P0ABA is the general circuit fault for sense circuit A, set when the reading is unusable without being clearly low, high or erratic.",[["h","Damaged or loose voltage sense lead inside the battery pack"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"],["l","Blown sense fuse or failed divider resistor"]],["Check engine or hybrid system warning light","Reduced electric assist or EV range","Vehicle may not enter ready mode"],["shop_only",[150,2000]],"c"],"P0ABB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense A Circuit Range/Performance","P0ABB is a plausibility check: the measured pack voltage is within electrical limits but does not agree with what the individual cells add up to. GM documents it in several hybrid tables.",[["h","Pack voltage measurement drifted (divider or input aging)"],["m","Faulty cell voltage tap distorting the cell sum"],["m","High-resistance busbar or sense connection"]],["Check engine or hybrid system warning light","Erratic state-of-charge display","Reduced electric power"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0ABC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense A Circuit Low","P0ABC is stored when the pack voltage sensor reports a value below anything the battery could really have, pointing to a short to ground or a lost sense signal rather than a discharged pack. GM's 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables set it below 4.8 V with system voltage above 10.2 V, after 8 failed samples out of 10 taken every 25 ms, and rate it Type A with MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Sense line shorted to ground or disconnected"],["m","Sense fuse or link open so no voltage reaches the input"],["l","Battery controller measurement input defective"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning on","Vehicle may refuse to enter ready mode","Engine runs continuously, no electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,2000]],"c"],"P0ABD":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense A Circuit High","The counterpart to P0ABC: the pack voltage reading is above the highest value the battery could physically reach, which suggests an open reference, a failed divider or a damaged input rather than real overcharging. GM's 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables set P0ABD above 392 V, again with 8 failed samples out of 10 at 25 ms intervals and Type A, so the MIL comes on immediately.",[["h","Open ground or reference side of the sense circuit"],["m","Voltage divider component failed in the battery controller"],["l","Battery controller measurement input defective"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning on","Electric drive limited or unavailable"],["shop_only",[150,2000]],"c"],"P0ABE":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0ABE flags a pack voltage signal that jumps or drops out briefly instead of changing smoothly, as a battery voltage physically would. A loose sense connection or a cracked solder joint inside the pack are the classic triggers.",[["h","Loose or fretted voltage sense connection"],["m","Cracked solder or crimp joint on the sense board"],["l","Electrical noise from damaged shielding or ground"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Sudden short drops in electric power"],["shop_only",[150,2000]],"c"],"P0ABF":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor A Circuit","The pack current sensor, usually a Hall-effect unit around the main cable, tells the battery controller how much current flows in and out. P0ABF is the general fault of sensor A.",[["h","Hall current sensor output frozen"],["m","Sensor supply, ground or signal wire faulty"],["l","Sensor not seated around the conductor after repair"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning on","Reduced electric power and erratic charge display","Vehicle may not go ready"],["shop_only",[200,1500]],"c"],"P0AC0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0AC0 belongs to the battery current sensor family, not to battery cooling. It reports a sensor whose reading is electrically valid but implausible.",[["h","Current sensor zero offset drifted"],["m","Unstable sensor supply or reference voltage"],["l","Battery controller input fault"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning on","State of charge display drifts or jumps","Reduced electric assist"],["shop_only",[200,1500]],"c"],"P0AC1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor A Circuit Low","P0AC1 is set when the output of the pack current sensor falls below the valid window, so that the converted current would be an impossible discharge or charge value. GM documents it in several hybrid tables.",[["h","Sensor 5 V supply open or shorted"],["m","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Hall current sensor failed internally"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning on","Electric drive limited or vehicle will not go ready"],["shop_only",[200,1500]],"c"],"P0AC2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor A Circuit High","With P0AC2 the current sensor output sits above its valid window, which after conversion would mean an implausibly large current in the opposite direction to P0AC1. GM's 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables set it when the Hall-effect sensor reads above 274.6 A in 8 of 10 samples taken every 25 ms, again as Type A with immediate MIL.",[["h","Sensor ground open"],["m","Signal wire shorted to the 5 V supply or another voltage"],["m","Hall current sensor failed internally"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning on","Battery power limited, engine runs more often"],["shop_only",[200,1500]],"c"],"P0AC3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AC3 describes a battery current signal that jumps, spikes or drops out in a way real current flow could not explain, typically through a loose connector, a chafed signal wire or interference on the sensor ground. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Loose or corroded current sensor connector"],["m","Chafed signal or supply wire with intermittent contact"],["l","Interference on sensor ground or shield"]],["Warning light that comes and goes","Short drops in electric power while driving"],["shop_only",[150,1500]],"c"],"P0AC4":["Hybrid/EV Powertrain Control Module Requested MIL Illumination","In many hybrids the hybrid powertrain controller has its own emission-related diagnostics but does not drive the malfunction indicator lamp itself. When it detects such a fault it sends a MIL request to the engine controller, which then stores P0AC4 as a pointer.",[["h","Emission-related fault stored in the hybrid controller"],["l","Request still present because codes were cleared only in the engine controller"]],["Check engine light on (caused by the underlying hybrid fault)","Further codes present only in the hybrid controller"],["medium",[50,200]],"c"],"P0AC5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor B Circuit","Battery temperature sensor B is one of several thermistors that measure cell or module temperature inside the high-voltage pack; GM's Volt bulletin calls it sensor 2 of nine. P0AC5 is the general circuit code for it, used for an electrical fault the controller does not classify as low, high or erratic.",[["h","Poor contact or damaged wire between sensor B and sensing board"],["m","Temperature sensor B failed"],["l","Fault in the battery controller input stage"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning","Reduced electric power and regeneration","Charging may be slower on plug-in models"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0AC6":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0AC6 is the plausibility test for battery temperature sensor B: its reading is compared with the other pack sensors. GM documents several calibrations.",[["m","Controller software flags the sensor falsely (GM Volt bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["h","Sensor B drifted from its characteristic"],["m","Corroded contact adding resistance in the sensor circuit"],["l","Real temperature difference from a weak cell or blocked cooling"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","One sensor reading far off the others in live data","Battery too cold message on the Volt in mild weather"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0AC7":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","P0AC7 means the voltage of battery temperature sensor B is below its valid range. GM's sensors are NTC thermistors, so a low voltage equals an extremely hot reading, usually from a short to ground.",[["h","Sensor B signal shorted to ground inside the pack harness"],["m","Thermistor shorted internally"],["l","Controller software setting the code falsely (Volt bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["l","Moisture or coolant at the sensing board connector"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Sensor B shows an implausibly high temperature","Charging and drive power reduced"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0AC8":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","P0AC8 reports that battery temperature sensor B delivers a voltage above its valid range, which for an NTC thermistor reads as extreme cold. An open wire, a disconnected sensing board plug or a broken sensor are typical.",[["h","Open signal or return wire of sensor B"],["m","Thermistor open internally"],["m","Controller software issue on the Volt (bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["l","Signal shorted to the reference voltage"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Battery too cold message although it is not cold","Strongly reduced charging and electric power"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0AC9":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AC9 catches a battery temperature sensor B signal that changes erratically. Pack temperatures move slowly because of the large thermal mass, so rapid swings point to a bad connection.",[["h","Loose terminal or unlatched connector at the sensing board"],["m","Partly broken sensor wire inside the pack"],["l","Interference from high-voltage switching on the sensor line"]],["Hybrid warning light that comes and goes","Sensor B value jumps in live data","Occasional power limitation"],["shop_only",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0ACA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor C Circuit","Battery temperature sensor C is a further thermistor inside the high-voltage pack, corresponding to sensor 3 in GM's Volt bulletin. P0ACA covers a general electrical fault in its circuit that the controller does not narrow down to low, high or erratic.",[["h","Poor contact or damaged wire between sensor C and sensing board"],["m","Temperature sensor C failed"],["l","Fault in the battery controller input stage"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning","Reduced electric power and regeneration","Further battery sensor codes may be stored"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0ACB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","With P0ACB the controller judges battery temperature sensor C implausible: its value deviates too far from what the other pack sensors report. GM applies the same limits as for sensors A and B.",[["h","Sensor C drifted from its characteristic"],["m","Controller software flags the sensor falsely (GM Volt bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["m","Corroded terminal adding resistance"],["l","Real temperature difference in the module monitored by sensor C"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Sensor C reads clearly off the others after a long soak","Possibly reduced charge and drive power"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0ACC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor C Circuit Low","P0ACC sets when the signal of battery temperature sensor C drops below the valid voltage window. For the NTC sensors GM uses, that equals an impossibly hot reading, most often caused by a short to ground in the pack wiring.",[["h","Sensor C signal shorted to ground"],["m","Thermistor shorted internally"],["l","Controller software setting the code falsely (Volt bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["l","Moisture at the sensing board connector"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Sensor C shows an implausibly high temperature","Reduced charging and electric drive power"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0ACD":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor C Circuit High","P0ACD indicates that battery temperature sensor C reports a voltage above its plausible range, which an NTC sensor translates into extreme cold. Common reasons are an open circuit, a pin backed out of the sensing board connector or a cracked thermistor.",[["h","Open signal or return wire of sensor C"],["m","Thermistor open or cracked"],["m","Controller software issue on the Volt (bulletin 18-NA-342)"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Battery too cold message in mild weather","Strongly reduced charging and electric power"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0ACE":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0ACE flags erratic behaviour of battery temperature sensor C, meaning the reading jumps faster than the heavy battery mass could ever warm or cool. In GM's 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables the controller sums the absolute changes of the sensor value across a 10-sample window taken every 100 ms; a sum above 10 fails the window, and 4 failed windows out of 5 store the code as Type B, two trips.",[["h","Loose terminal or unlatched connector at sensor C"],["m","Partly broken sensor wire inside the pack"],["l","Fault on the battery sensing board affecting several inputs"]],["Hybrid warning light that appears intermittently","Sensor C value jumps in live data","Occasional power limitation"],["shop_only",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0ACF":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 3 Control Circuit/Open","Air-cooled traction batteries can use several blowers; P0ACF belongs to the third one and means the battery controller sees an open or otherwise invalid control line while it drives fan 3. CARB 1968.2 (e)(15.2.3)(B) requires the electrical inputs and outputs of the battery thermal management to be monitored like other comprehensive components.",[["h","Loose, corroded or unplugged connector at fan 3"],["m","Broken control wire between battery controller and fan 3"],["m","Fan motor or its built-in driver open internally"],["l","Fan output stage in the battery controller defective"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning light, often with check engine light","Electric drive power reduced when the battery gets warm","No fan noise from the battery area when cooling is expected"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AD0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 3 Performance/Stuck Off","P0AD0 is a functional fault: the control line of battery fan 3 may be electrically fine, but the blower does not deliver what was commanded, or stays still. Depending on design, the controller compares fan speed feedback with the request, or, as a Honda patent describes, compares the expected battery temperature change for the known cooling capacity with the measured one.",[["h","Fan intake or duct blocked by dirt, pet hair or objects (often under or beside a seat)"],["m","Worn bearings or debris slowing the fan wheel"],["m","Speed feedback signal from fan 3 missing or implausible"],["l","Weak 12 V supply or poor ground at the fan"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning light","Power or regeneration limited after longer drives or in heat","Battery temperature rises faster than usual"],["medium",[50,500]],"c"],"P0AD1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 3 Stuck On","P0AD1 is the counterpart to P0AD0: fan 3 of the traction battery keeps turning although the controller has switched it off or commanded a lower level. A speed feedback or the current in the fan circuit shows that the blower still runs.",[["h","Fan output stage in battery controller or fan module stuck on"],["m","Control wire chafed against a supply voltage"],["l","Fan relay contacts welded (relay-switched designs)"]],["Battery fan audible after the vehicle is switched off","12 V battery discharged after standing","Hybrid or EV warning light"],["medium",[60,500]],"c"],"P0AD2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 3 Control Circuit Low","P0AD2 reports that the control signal for battery fan 3 is pulled too low, typically a short to ground on the line between battery controller and blower. Such fans are mostly speed-controlled by a PWM duty cycle, as a Toyota patent on battery fan control explains.",[["h","Control wire chafed through to body ground"],["m","Internal short in fan 3 motor or its electronics"],["m","Moisture or spilled liquid in the fan connector"],["l","Output stage in the battery controller defective"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning light, often with check engine light","Electric drive power reduced when the battery gets warm","No fan noise from the battery area when cooling is expected"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AD3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 3 Control Circuit High","With P0AD3 the battery controller finds the control line of cooling fan 3 at a voltage that is too high for the commanded state, pointing to a short to a supply voltage or a fan input that no longer pulls the line down. No manufacturer publicly documents this code; GM's analogous fan 1 code P0A85 trips when the control voltage exceeds 9 V while the fan is driven, Type B.",[["h","Control wire shorted to 12 V or another supply"],["m","Fan electronics no longer loading the control line"],["m","Bent or bridged pins in the fan connector"],["l","Battery controller output stage defective"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning light","Fan 3 runs constantly or not at all","Reduced electric power when the battery is warm"],["medium",[80,600]],"c"],"P0AD4":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Flow System Insufficient Air Flow","P0AD4 is a system-level check of air-cooled traction batteries: the controller concludes that too little air passes the cells, for example because the cell temperatures rise faster than the commanded fan level allows, as a Honda fan diagnosis patent describes. CARB 1968.2 asks for a functional check of the battery cooling performance where feasible.",[["h","Cabin air intake grille or duct of the battery blocked (bags, clothing, dust, pet hair)"],["m","Battery air filter clogged, where fitted"],["m","Battery fan running too slowly"],["l","Battery controller software sets the code falsely (Ram eTorque, fixed by update)"]],["Check engine or hybrid warning light","Reduced electric assist or start-stop not available","Battery fan runs loud for long periods"],["easy",[0,400]],"c"],"P0AD5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Flow Valve A Control Circuit/Open","Some air-cooled battery packs route their cooling air through a motorized flap that switches between cabin air, recirculation or an outlet. P0AD5 means the battery controller finds the drive circuit of this valve A open or otherwise faulty.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector at the flap actuator"],["m","Broken wire in the actuator harness"],["m","Actuator motor winding open"],["l","Output stage in battery controller defective"]],["Hybrid warning, electric drive may be switched off","Check engine light on","Battery temperature not controlled as usual"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0AD6":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Flow Valve A Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0AD6 flags that the air flow valve A of the battery cooling does not behave as expected: the circuit is intact, but position, travel or the learned end stops do not fit the command. Audi documents exactly this code as P0AD600 on the 2012-15 Q5 hybrid (TSB 2038152): a software error made the battery management module J841 lose the learned end stops of the air supply valve, electric and hybrid drive were disabled, and a software update to version 0543 fixed it.",[["h","Battery controller software loses learned end stops (Audi Q5 hybrid, fixed by update)"],["m","Flap binding from dirt or a deformed duct"],["m","Actuator gears or position feedback worn"],["l","End stops not relearned after battery or actuator work"]],["Hybrid drive system error message in the cluster","Electric and hybrid operation no longer available","Check engine light on"],["medium",[0,500]],"u"],"P0AD7":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Flow Valve A Control Circuit Low","P0AD7 sets when the control circuit of the battery air flow valve A reads too low a voltage, usually a short to ground in the line or actuator. The valve is a motorized flap in the battery cooling duct, as in the Audi Q5 hybrid cooling module, whose bulletin covers the range/performance sibling P0AD6.",[["h","Actuator control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal short in the flap actuator"],["m","Moisture in the actuator connector"],["l","Battery controller output stage defective"]],["Hybrid warning, electric drive may be switched off","Check engine light on","Battery temperature not controlled as usual"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0AD8":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Air Flow Valve A Control Circuit High","With P0AD8 the battery controller sees too high a voltage in the drive circuit of air flow valve A, typically a short to a supply voltage or an actuator that no longer draws current. The component is the motorized flap that directs cooling air through an air-cooled traction battery, documented for example in Audi's Q5 hybrid bulletin on the related code P0AD6.",[["h","Control wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Actuator no longer loading the circuit"],["m","Bridged or bent pins in the connector"],["l","Battery controller output stage defective"]],["Hybrid warning, electric drive may be switched off","Check engine light on","Battery temperature not controlled as usual"],["medium",[80,500]],"c"],"P0AD9":["Hybrid/EV Battery Positive Contactor Control Circuit/Open","The positive main contactor connects the traction battery to the high-voltage bus; its coil is driven by the battery or hybrid controller. P0AD9 covers an open in this coil circuit.",[["h","Open in the contactor coil or its pigtail inside the battery disconnect unit"],["m","Loose or corroded low-voltage connector at the battery disconnect unit"],["m","Broken control wire in the battery harness"],["l","High-side driver in the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle will not enter ready mode or start","Hybrid or EV warning light and check engine light","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0ADA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Positive Contactor Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0ADA describes a positive contactor coil circuit that is electrically present but does not behave plausibly: coil current, voltage or response time outside the expected window, for instance due to high resistance or an unstable PWM hold signal. No manufacturer publicly documents this code; GM, which details the positive contactor driver codes P0AD9, P0ADB and P0ADC with Type B logic and sample counters, does not list P0ADA.",[["h","High resistance in coil wiring or connector (corrosion, fretting)"],["m","Contactor coil degraded or partially shorted"],["m","Low 12 V supply during contactor closing"],["l","Battery controller fault or software"]],["Vehicle will not enter ready mode or start","Hybrid or EV warning light and check engine light","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"u"],"P0ADB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Positive Contactor Control Circuit Low","P0ADB means the drive circuit of the positive main contactor reads too low, mostly a short to ground. GM uses it widely: 2016-19 tables set it when the control voltage stays under 5.35 V with the contactor commanded on, 13 of 16 samples, Type B.",[["h","Coil control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Contactor coil internally shorted"],["m","Battery controller software sets the code (Audi, Dodge bulletins)"],["l","Driver stage in the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle does not start; multiple warnings in the cluster","Hybrid or EV warning light","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0ADC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Positive Contactor Control Circuit High","P0ADC indicates that the positive contactor drive circuit carries too much voltage for the commanded state, usually a short to battery voltage. In GM's 2016-19 hybrid tables the driver output, with the contactor commanded off, reads between 5.27 and 19.7 V for 40 of 50 samples at 12.5 ms, Type B with two trips.",[["h","Coil control wire shorted to 12 V"],["m","Damaged harness or connector at the battery disconnect unit"],["l","Driver stage in the battery controller shorted"]],["Hybrid or EV warning light","High-voltage system disabled, vehicle not ready","Possible related stuck-closed codes"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0ADD":["Hybrid/EV Battery Negative Contactor Control Circuit/Open","The negative main contactor closes the return path of the traction battery; P0ADD reports an open in its coil drive circuit. GM documents the code in parallel to the positive side: tables since model year 2011 detect open load in the HPC2 and store it after two trips; 2016-19 tables evaluate a control voltage between 1.15 and 2.81 V while off, 40 of 50 samples, Type B, and 2019 tables add a coil current check below 194 mA when on.",[["h","Open in negative contactor coil or its pigtail"],["m","Loose or corroded connector at the battery disconnect unit"],["m","Broken control wire in the battery harness"],["l","Driver in the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle will not enter ready mode or start","Hybrid or EV warning light and check engine light","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0ADE":["Hybrid/EV Battery Negative Contactor Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0ADE is set when the coil circuit of the negative main contactor works electrically but its values are implausible, such as coil current or hold voltage out of range or a slow response after the command. No manufacturer publicly documents this code; GM, whose tables cover the negative contactor driver codes P0ADD, P0ADF and P0AE0 in detail, does not use P0ADE.",[["h","High resistance in coil wiring or connector"],["m","Negative contactor coil degraded"],["m","Weak 12 V battery during start-up"],["l","Battery controller fault or software"]],["Vehicle will not enter ready mode or start","Hybrid or EV warning light and check engine light","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"u"],"P0ADF":["Hybrid/EV Battery Negative Contactor Control Circuit Low","P0ADF reports that the drive circuit of the negative main contactor is pulled too low, typically a short to ground in the coil line. GM documents the code in its 2016-19 hybrid tables: control voltage below 5.35 V while the contactor is commanded on, 13 of 16 samples at 12.5 ms (20 of 25 in one 2016 group), Type B with two trips, enabled above about 9 to 10 V on the 12 V system.",[["h","Coil control wire shorted to ground"],["m","Negative contactor coil internally shorted"],["m","Moisture in the battery disconnect unit connector"],["l","Driver in the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle will not enter ready mode or start","Hybrid or EV warning light and check engine light","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AE0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Negative Contactor Control Circuit High","P0AE0 is stored when the negative contactor drive circuit shows too high a voltage for its state, which mostly means a short to battery voltage. GM's 2016-19 hybrid tables set it with the contactor commanded off and the driver output between 5.27 and 19.7 V for 40 of 50 samples at 12.5 ms, Type B, two trips.",[["h","Coil control wire shorted to 12 V"],["m","Damaged harness or connector at the battery disconnect unit"],["l","Driver stage in the battery controller shorted"]],["Hybrid or EV warning light","High-voltage system disabled, vehicle not ready","Possible additional stuck-closed code"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AE1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Precharge Contactor Circuit","Before the main contactors close, a precharge contactor routes battery current through a resistor so the inverter capacitors fill slowly instead of drawing a damaging inrush. P0AE1 is the general fault of this precharge path when it is not split into stuck closed (P0AE2), stuck open (P0AE3) or a driver fault (P0AE4 to P0AE7).",[["h","Defective solder joint in the motor power inverter (Honda IMA)"],["m","Precharge contactor or precharge resistor failed"],["l","Fault logged by battery controller software after an interrupted charging sequence"],["l","Wiring or connector fault in the contactor circuit"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning in the cluster","Vehicle does not become ready to drive","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[150,3000]],"c"],"P0AE2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Precharge Contactor Circuit Stuck Closed","P0AE2 means the precharge path conducts although it should be open, for example welded contacts or a precharge switch that no longer turns off. GM's 2011 Volt table checks it once per precharge event: if the bus voltage already exceeds 80 % of pack voltage before precharge begins, the precharge FET is judged stuck closed, 4 of 4 samples, Type A with MIL after one trip.",[["h","Precharge contactor contacts welded or precharge switch shorted"],["m","Battery controller software sets the code falsely (Audi, Stellantis bulletins)"],["l","Contactor driver held on by a short to power"],["l","Bus voltage not discharged before the next precharge"]],["Vehicle does not become ready to drive","MIL and hybrid or EV warning on","Wrench symbol or service message in the cluster"],["shop_only",[150,6000]],"c"],"P0AE3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Precharge Contactor Circuit Stuck Open","P0AE3 is set when the precharge path does not conduct after it has been commanded closed, so the bus voltage fails to rise and the main contactors are not allowed to close. GM documents several cases: preliminary information PIC6165A for the 2016-17 Chevrolet Volt describes a rare P0AE3 in cool weather right after a completed charge, without driveability effects; parts should not be replaced, a new calibration followed, and the MIL goes out after three clean drive cycles.",[["h","Battery controller calibration sets the code under certain conditions (GM Volt, Audi)"],["m","Precharge contactor contacts burnt or coil not pulling in"],["m","Precharge resistor or its fuse open"],["l","Internal fault in the battery disconnect unit"]],["Vehicle does not become ready to drive","MIL or service engine soon light on","No battery charge level shown after switching on"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0AE4":["Hybrid/EV Battery Precharge Contactor A Control Circuit/Open","P0AE4 concerns the circuit that drives the coil of precharge contactor A, not its contacts. GM uses it in many hybrid and EV tables, with changing logic: the 2016 and 2019 OBDG02 tables look for an open high-side driver circuit, a control voltage between 1.15 and 2.81 V while commanded off, 40 of 50 samples at 12.5 ms, Type B, two trips.",[["h","Open control wire or loose connector at the contactor coil"],["m","Precharge contactor coil open internally"],["l","Control wire shorted to 12 V (GM 2011 and 2019 logic)"],["l","Driver stage in the battery or hybrid controller defective"]],["Vehicle does not become ready to drive","MIL and hybrid or EV warning on","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AE5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Precharge Contactor A Control Circuit Range/Performance","P0AE5 reports that the precharge function works electrically but does not deliver the expected result, so the controller judges the circuit implausible. Ford documents it in special service message SSM 47129: 2013-18 C-MAX and Fusion Hybrid and Energi models and the MKZ Hybrid may not start, with P0AE5 stored in the battery energy control module.",[["h","High-voltage consumer with low internal resistance (DC/DC converter, coolant heater, A/C compressor)"],["m","Precharge resistor degraded or wrong value"],["l","Precharge contactor responding slowly or with high contact resistance"]],["Vehicle does not start or become ready","Red warning triangle or hybrid warning in the cluster","Possible additional codes from the faulty high-voltage consumer"],["shop_only",[300,2500]],"u"],"P0AE6":["Hybrid/EV Battery Precharge Contactor A Control Circuit Low","P0AE6 means the control line of precharge contactor A is pulled too low, usually a short to ground. GM documents it in several programs.",[["h","Control wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Precharge contactor coil shorted internally"],["l","Open circuit on low-side driven designs (GM eAssist)"],["l","Driver stage in the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle does not become ready to drive","MIL and hybrid warning on","Engine start or electric drive unavailable"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AE7":["Hybrid/EV Battery Precharge Contactor A Control Circuit High","P0AE7 is stored when the control line of precharge contactor A carries too much voltage for its commanded state, typically a short to the 12 V supply. In GM's 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables the high-side driver output reads between 5.27 and 19.7 V while the contactor is commanded off, 40 of 50 samples at 12.5 ms, Type B, two trips.",[["h","Control wire shorted to 12 V supply"],["m","Driver stage stuck on in the battery controller"],["l","Outdated battery controller calibration"]],["Vehicle may not start or become ready","MIL and hybrid warning on","Possible additional stuck-closed code P0AE2"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AE8":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor D Circuit","Battery temperature sensor D is a further thermistor inside the high-voltage pack; GM's Volt bulletin 18-NA-342 lists the matching codes as temperature sensor 4 of nine. P0AE8 is the general circuit code for it, used when the controller finds an electrical fault without classifying it as low, high or erratic.",[["h","Poor contact or damaged wire between sensor D and sensing board"],["m","Temperature sensor D failed"],["l","Fault in the battery controller input stage"]],["Hybrid system or check engine warning","Reduced electric power and regeneration","Further battery sensor codes may be stored"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0AE9":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor D Circuit Range/Performance","P0AE9 is the plausibility check for battery temperature sensor D, GM's sensor 4: its reading is compared with the other pack sensors. In GM's 2016 and 2019 tables it fails when the value is more than 25 °C away from the average of the others in 50 of 67 samples; the two-mode hybrid tables of 2011 and 2013 use more than 15 °C in 90 of 100; the eAssist table 13OBDG05C allows 8.55 °C after a long module off time; GM's 2018 table uses 20 °C for 5 s within 6.7 s.",[["m","Outdated controller software (GM Volt, bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["m","Temperature sensor D drifting out of tolerance"],["l","Genuine local temperature difference in one battery module"],["l","Increased contact resistance in the sensor circuit"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two trips","Battery Too Cold message despite mild weather (Volt)","Reduced charge and electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0AEA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor D Circuit Low","P0AEA means the voltage of battery temperature sensor D is below its valid range. GM uses NTC thermistors with a pull-up on the sensing board, so a low voltage reads as an extremely hot cell, usually from a short to ground.",[["h","Sensor D signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Temperature sensor D internally shorted"],["m","Outdated controller software (GM Volt, bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["l","Moisture or damage on the battery sensing board"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two trips","Charge and drive power strongly limited","Battery cooling running more than usual"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0AEB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor D Circuit High","P0AEB reports that battery temperature sensor D delivers a voltage above its valid range. For GM's NTC sensors this reads as extreme cold, pointing to an open wire, a backed-out pin at the sensing board or a cracked thermistor.",[["h","Open circuit or unplugged connector at sensor D"],["m","Outdated controller software (GM Volt, bulletin 18-NA-342)"],["m","Temperature sensor D open internally"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two trips","Battery Too Cold message (Volt)","Charging and electric drive restricted"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0AEC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor D Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AEC flags erratic behaviour of battery temperature sensor D: the reading jumps faster than the heavy battery mass could ever warm or cool. GM documents the method in its 2016 and 2019 hybrid tables.",[["h","Loose terminal or connector not fully latched at sensor D"],["m","Partly broken or chafed sensor wire"],["l","Fault on the battery sensing board"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two trips, may go out again","Jumping battery temperature in live data","Occasional power limitation"],["shop_only",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0AED":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The inverter of an electric drive motor carries thermistors near its power switches so the controller can derate before the IGBTs overheat. In GM's hybrid tables, inverter temperature sensor A is the phase U sensor of the inverter for motor A.",[["h","Internal fault of the sensor inside the power inverter module"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the inverter module"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine lamp","Reduced electric drive power","Possible loss of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0AEE":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0AEE checks whether inverter temperature sensor A reads sensibly. GM's 2016 to 2019 tables compare it after a cold soak with an average temperature of the vehicle; more than 20 °C apart sets Type B, two trips.",[["m","Controller software error (Audi Q5/A7 e quattro bulletin)"],["m","Inverter temperature sensor A drifting out of tolerance"],["m","Failing power inverter module (e.g. Honda MPI solder joints)"],["l","Uneven inverter cooling after soak"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, often without drivability change","Drive system fault warning (Audi)","Vehicle may not start in hybrid mode"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"u"],"P0AEF":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","P0AEF means the signal of inverter temperature sensor A is below its valid range. GM describes it as an NTC thermistor with a pull-up on the sensing board, so a low voltage reads as an impossibly hot inverter, usually a short to ground or an internal sensor fault.",[["h","Shorted sensor or circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to ground in external wiring, where present"],["l","Moisture or coolant inside the inverter housing"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Electric drive power cut back strongly","Vehicle may not start (Jeep Compass PHEV)"],["shop_only",[300,5000]],"c"],"P0AF0":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","P0AF0 reports a signal from inverter temperature sensor A above its valid range, which for an NTC sensor reads as an extremely cold inverter: open wire, lost ground or a broken sensor. GM's 2016 to 2019 hybrid tables set it below -50 °C for 2.6 of 3.65 s, but only after at least 90 s of inverter warm-up at 20 Nm or more, so a really cold car does not trigger it; Type B, two trips.",[["h","Open sensor or circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Motor control unit software (Kia Sorento Hybrid 2025)"],["l","Loose or corroded connector at the inverter"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced electric drive power","Traction motor noise or inoperative A/C (Kia)"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0AF1":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AF1 is meant for an inverter temperature sensor A signal that jumps or drops out briefly instead of failing permanently. Inverter heat sinks are liquid-cooled and change temperature smoothly, so sudden steps point to a contact problem or an internal fault in the sensing circuit.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector at the inverter module"],["m","Intermittent fault inside the power inverter module"],["l","Electrical interference on the sensor signal"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Brief power reductions while driving","Jumping inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"u"],"P0AF2":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit","Inverter temperature sensor B is, in GM's hybrid tables, the phase U thermistor of the second inverter, the one that drives motor B in two-motor systems. P0AF2 is its general circuit code for an electrical fault that the controller does not narrow down to low, high or erratic.",[["h","Internal fault of the sensor inside the power inverter module"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the inverter module"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine lamp","Reduced electric drive or generator power","Higher fuel consumption in hybrid operation"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0AF3":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0AF3 is the plausibility test for inverter temperature sensor B, the phase U sensor of GM's motor B inverter. In the 2016 to 2019 tables the sensor is compared after a cold soak with a calculated average vehicle temperature; a difference above 20 °C sets the code, Type B, two trips.",[["m","Inverter temperature sensor B drifting out of tolerance"],["m","Failing power inverter module"],["l","Uneven inverter coolant flow after soak"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two trips","Often no noticeable drivability change","Further inverter temperature codes may be stored"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"u"],"P0AF4":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit Low","P0AF4 reports a signal of inverter temperature sensor B below the valid range. GM uses an NTC thermistor with a pull-up, so a low voltage reads as an inverter far hotter than possible, usually from a short to ground or an internal sensor fault in the motor B inverter.",[["h","Shorted sensor or circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to ground in external wiring, where present"],["l","Moisture or coolant inside the inverter housing"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Motor B power strongly limited","Reduced hybrid assist and regeneration"],["shop_only",[300,5000]],"c"],"P0AF5":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit High","P0AF5 means inverter temperature sensor B delivers a voltage above its valid range. For GM's NTC sensor that equals an implausibly cold inverter, so an open circuit, a lost ground or a cracked thermistor is likely.",[["h","Open sensor or circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Loose, corroded or unplugged connector at the inverter"],["l","Lost sensor ground"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two trips","Reduced motor B power","Implausibly cold inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0AF6":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0AF6 targets an inverter temperature sensor B signal that jumps or drops out briefly. Since the heat sink of the motor B inverter is liquid-cooled and warms or cools gradually, abrupt changes in the reading indicate a contact problem or an intermittent fault in the sensing circuit rather than real temperature.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector at the inverter module"],["m","Intermittent fault inside the power inverter module"],["l","Electrical interference on the sensor signal"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Brief reductions of motor B power","Jumping inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"u"],"P0AF7":["14 Volt Power Module Internal Temperature Too High","The 14 V power module is the DC/DC converter that feeds the 12 V network from the high-voltage battery in hybrids and EVs. P0AF7 reports that its internal temperature has exceeded the protection limit, a real overheat rather than a sensor circuit fault.",[["h","Restricted cooling, weak coolant pump or failed cooling fan"],["m","Sustained high 12 V load or weak 12 V battery"],["l","Internal fault of the DC/DC converter"]],["Warning lamp or 12 V charging warning","Low 12 V voltage, flickering lights","Comfort consumers switched off"],["shop_only",[80,2000]],"c"],"P0AF8":["Hybrid/EV Battery System Voltage","P0AF8 compares the battery pack voltage reported by the battery controller with an independent measurement on the high-voltage bus. GM calls it system voltage correlation: with the contactors closed, the 2019 tables fail it when the pack reading differs from the inverter bus voltage by more than 13.95 V, or from the charger bus voltage by more than 14.95 V, 400 of 500 samples at 25 ms, Type A.",[["h","Loose or arcing high-voltage battery cable connection"],["m","Pack or bus voltage sensing circuit inaccurate"],["m","Low 12 V supply or loose 12 V power fuse affecting the measuring modules"],["l","High resistance in a pack fuse or main contactor"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV warning on","Battery saver or low battery message","Intermittent no crank or reduced hybrid power"],["shop_only",[100,3000]],"c"],"P0AF9":["Hybrid/EV Battery System Voltage Unstable","P0AF9 is meant for a traction battery voltage that fluctuates more than load changes can explain, for example through a loose terminal, an intermittent contactor or a cell group that collapses under load. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM, for instance, covers system voltage with P0AF8 (correlation), P0AFA and P0AFB (low and high), and reports uneven cell groups as P0BBE pack voltage variation, which bulletin 19-NA-117 ties to degraded cells in the Volt and ELR.",[["h","Loose high-voltage terminal or busbar connection"],["m","Degraded cell group collapsing under load"],["l","Main contactor with intermittent contact"]],["Intermittent hybrid or EV warning","Sudden power reductions while driving","Code may come and go without lasting symptoms"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0AFA":["Hybrid/EV Battery System Voltage Low","P0AFA protects the traction battery from deep discharge: the pack voltage, or in some versions any cell voltage, has fallen below a temperature-dependent limit. GM's 2016 and 2019 tables set it after 320 of 400 samples (about 10 s) for the pack or 112 of 140 for a cell, Type A, and latch it so that only a technician command can clear it.",[["h","Degraded or self-discharging cell groups"],["m","Pack drained by repeated failed engine starts or long standing"],["m","Open high-voltage fuse or manual disconnect"],["l","Loose or high-resistance ground on 48 V systems"]],["Vehicle does not start; restart blocked while the code is latched","Reduced or lost propulsion before the no-start","Plug-in charging not possible"],["shop_only",[200,9000]],"c"],"P0AFB":["Hybrid/EV Battery System Voltage High","P0AFB guards against overcharging: the pack voltage, or a single cell, exceeds a temperature-dependent upper limit. GM's 2016 and 2019 tables use 320 of 400 samples (about 10 s) for the pack and 112 of 140 for a cell, Type A, and latch the code until a technician clears it by command; the 2011 Volt table also uses Type A.",[["m","Loose or arcing battery cable causing voltage spikes"],["m","Control unit software allows overvoltage (Mercedes 48 V bulletins)"],["m","Cell imbalance so that one cell overcharges"],["l","Faulty voltage sensing reporting too high a value"]],["Hybrid system or MIL warning","Start-stop or regenerative braking disabled","Possible no crank on mild hybrids"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0AFC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Sensor Module","Some hybrid packs carry a separate sensor module that measures block voltages and passes them to the battery controller; Toyota calls it the battery voltage sensor or battery smart unit. P0AFC reports a fault of this module itself, such as a failed self-check or disturbed data transfer.",[["h","Radio interference from an aftermarket antenna near the battery (Toyota Camry Hybrid)"],["m","Battery voltage sensor module internally defective"],["l","Supply or communication wiring of the sensor module faulty"]],["MIL and check hybrid system warning on","Hybrid drive disabled or reduced","Code may return when the radio transmitter is used"],["shop_only",[0,1500]],"c"],"P0AFD":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Temperature Too Low","P0AFD indicates that the traction battery is colder than its permitted operating range. At very low temperatures cell resistance rises and charging risks lithium plating, so the controller restricts or blocks charging and discharging.",[["h","Vehicle parked unplugged in extreme cold"],["m","Battery heater or coolant heater not working"],["m","Temperature sensor reading too cold (sensor or software)"]],["Battery too cold message in the cluster","Reduced electric power or electric drive unavailable","Charging slow or not possible until the pack warms up"],["medium",[0,1500]],"c"],"P0AFE":["Hybrid/EV Battery System Voltage Too Low for Voltage Step Up Conversion","Many hybrids raise the battery voltage with a step-up (boost) converter before it reaches the inverters; Toyota's second-generation Hybrid Synergy Drive, for example, boosts a pack of around 200 V to 500 V or more. The converter needs a minimum input voltage to reach its target without excessive current.",[["h","Pack deeply discharged or weak under load"],["m","High-resistance connection between battery and converter"],["l","Converter input voltage measured too low"]],["Hybrid system warning","Reduced acceleration and electric assist","Engine runs more often than usual"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"u"],"P0AFF":["System Voltage Too Low for Voltage Step Down Conversion","A step-down (buck) DC/DC converter, often called the accessory power module, supplies the 12 V system from the high-voltage or 48 V bus. To regulate its output it needs an input voltage above a minimum.",[["h","High-voltage or 48 V bus voltage too low (discharged pack, open contactor)"],["m","High-resistance input connection or fuse of the DC/DC converter"],["l","DC/DC converter internal measuring fault"]],["Battery or charging system warning light","12 V battery discharges, electrical consumers fail","Hybrid or start-stop functions restricted"],["shop_only",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0B00":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase U Current","P0B00 is the general fault for phase U of the three-phase motor that drives the electric auxiliary transmission fluid pump, the pump that keeps line pressure while the engine is stopped. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Loose, corroded or unlatched phase U connection at the pump motor"],["m","Current sensing in the pump inverter faulty (shunt or phase sensor)"],["m","Power stage of phase U in the pump control module faulty"],["l","Winding fault in phase U of the pump motor"]],["MIL or hybrid/transmission warning message","Engine auto-stop disabled or no electric drive mode","Delayed engagement or harsh restart after a stop"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B01":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase U Current Low","P0B01 means the pump inverter sees almost no current in phase U of the auxiliary fluid pump motor, typically an open phase. GM documents the check for the Volt motor control processor (2016) and its hybrid tables of 2018 and 2019: with the drive state in Run, the inverter in normal PWM above 50 V and a squared current command above 5 A2 (2016) or 14.97 A2 (2018), the peak current on the U axis is compared while the rotor is within 30 degrees of it; below 1 A for 0.4 s of a 0.6 s window, the code sets as Type A, MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Pump motor connector not fully seated or latched (Chevrolet Spark EV)"],["m","Open circuit in the phase U lead or terminal of the pump motor cable"],["m","Phase U switch in the pump inverter not conducting"],["l","Open winding of phase U in the pump motor"]],["Service Vehicle Soon light and MIL","Propulsion Power Reduced or Vehicle Speed Limited message"],["hard",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0B02":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase U Current High","P0B02 reports excessive current in phase U of the auxiliary transmission fluid pump motor, an overcurrent condition that the pump inverter must cut off to protect its switches. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Phase U lead shorted to another phase or to ground"],["m","Pump binding mechanically, raising motor current"],["m","Current sensing in the pump inverter reads too high"],["l","Shorted turns in phase U of the pump motor"]],["MIL and transmission or hybrid warning","Auxiliary pump shuts down, auto-stop disabled","Harsh or delayed engagement after an engine stop"],["hard",[200,2500]],"c"],"P0B03":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase V Current","P0B03 covers a general current fault in phase V of the brushless motor of the electric auxiliary transmission fluid pump. No manufacturer publicly describes a monitor for it.",[["h","Poor contact in the phase V lead or pump connector"],["m","Current sensing in the pump inverter faulty"],["m","Phase V power stage in the pump control module faulty"],["l","Winding fault in phase V of the pump motor"]],["MIL or transmission/hybrid warning","Engine auto-stop or electric mode no longer available"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B04":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase V Current Low","P0B04 indicates that phase V of the auxiliary fluid pump motor carries too little current, usually because the phase is open somewhere between inverter and winding. GM tests it on the Volt (2016) and in the 2018 and 2019 hybrid tables like its U and W siblings: in Run, with normal PWM, more than 50 V on the inverter and a minimum squared current command, the V current is sampled while the rotor sits within 30 degrees of the V axis.",[["h","Pump motor connector not fully seated or latched (Chevrolet Spark EV)"],["m","Open circuit in the phase V lead or terminal"],["m","Phase V switch in the pump inverter not conducting"],["l","Open winding of phase V in the pump motor"]],["Service Vehicle Soon light and MIL","Reduced propulsion power or limited vehicle speed"],["hard",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0B05":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase V Current High","P0B05 flags overcurrent in phase V of the electric auxiliary transmission fluid pump motor. No manufacturer publicly documents this specific code.",[["h","Phase V lead shorted to another phase or to ground"],["m","Pump seized or contaminated, motor draws excess current"],["m","Pump restart logic in the inverter software causing overcurrent"],["l","Shorted turns in phase V of the pump motor"]],["MIL and transmission or hybrid warning","Loss of propulsion or no-start in severe cases"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B06":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase W Current","P0B06 is the general current fault for phase W of the auxiliary transmission fluid pump motor. No manufacturer publishes a monitor description for this exact code.",[["h","Poor contact in the phase W lead or pump connector"],["m","Current sensing in the pump inverter faulty"],["m","Phase W power stage in the pump control module faulty"],["l","Winding fault in phase W of the pump motor"]],["MIL or transmission/hybrid warning","Engine keeps running at stops, no auto-stop"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B07":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase W Current Low","P0B07 means phase W of the auxiliary fluid pump motor carries less current than commanded, the typical sign of an open W path. GM uses it on the Volt and later hybrids: below 1 A peak W-axis current for 0.4 s of 0.6 s, with the inverter in normal PWM above 50 V, sets a Type A fault.",[["h","Pump motor connector not fully seated or latched (Chevrolet Spark EV)"],["m","Open circuit in the phase W lead or terminal"],["m","Outdated inverter software (Cadillac CT6 plug-in, service update 18199)"],["l","Open winding of phase W in the pump motor"]],["MIL and Service Vehicle Soon light","Loss of propulsion, vehicle shutdown or no-start"],["hard",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0B08":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Phase W Current High","P0B08 is set when the current in phase W of the auxiliary transmission fluid pump motor rises above the permitted limit. No manufacturer documents this exact code in public sources.",[["h","Phase W lead shorted to another phase or to ground"],["m","Pump binding mechanically, raising motor current"],["m","Current sensing in the pump inverter reads too high"],["l","Shorted turns in phase W of the pump motor"]],["MIL and transmission or hybrid warning","Auxiliary pump shuts down, auto-stop disabled"],["hard",[200,2500]],"c"],"P0B09":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Supply Voltage Circuit/Open","P0B09 concerns the power feed of auxiliary pump A's motor: the controller sees no valid supply voltage, usually because the circuit is open. GM documents it for its 2012-13 eAssist mild hybrids (BAS Plus): the hardware input reports an out-of-range voltage flag on the auxiliary pump circuit, enabled with ignition voltage above 9 V; failing for at least 0.375 s within a 1.2 s sample sets P0B09 as Type B, MIL after two trips.",[["h","Blown fuse or open relay in the pump supply"],["h","Open supply wire or loose terminal at the pump connector"],["m","Poor ground connection of the pump motor"],["l","Voltage sensing input in the controller faulty"]],["Engine auto-stop no longer occurs","MIL after two drive cycles","Shift flare or delayed drive engagement on restart"],["medium",[20,900]],"c"],"P0B0A":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Supply Voltage Circuit Low","P0B0A means the supply voltage measured at the motor of auxiliary pump A is lower than expected while the pump should be running. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM's eAssist tables only cover the open-circuit case as P0B09.",[["h","Corroded terminal or high resistance in the pump feed or ground"],["m","Weak 12 V battery or poor charging"],["l","DC/DC converter or alternator output too low (hybrid)"],["l","Voltage sensing input in the controller faulty"]],["Start-stop function inactive","MIL or transmission warning","Jerky drive-off after an engine stop"],["medium",[20,700]],"c"],"P0B0B":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Supply Voltage Circuit High","P0B0B reports a supply voltage at the motor of auxiliary pump A that is above the expected range. Subaru documents it on the 2014-15 XV Crosstrek Hybrid (bulletin 16-94-15R), together with P172A: a leaking O-ring at the electric oil pump relief valve can let CVT fluid into the pump motor.",[["h","CVT fluid entering the pump motor via a leaking relief valve O-ring (Subaru XV Crosstrek Hybrid)"],["m","Supply circuit shorted to a higher voltage source"],["l","Oil pump inverter or its voltage sensing faulty"]],["Warning lamp on, hybrid or CVT function restricted","Possibly P0C79 stored at the same time"],["shop_only",[150,6000]],"c"],"P0B0C":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Hydraulic Leakage","P0B0C says the circuit fed by auxiliary pump A loses pressure through a leak, so the pump cannot hold the demanded pressure. Stellantis documents it as P0B0C 7A for the 2023-26 Jeep Avenger and 2026 Compass with hybrid dual-clutch transmission outside the US (TSB 21-061-26): an internal leak at the parking pawl locking piston causes jerking and power loss and may stop the engine from restarting after a stop; with P07E4 or P07E6 present and more than 5 km on the clock, the piston or the transmission is replaced.",[["h","Internal leak at the parking pawl locking piston (Jeep Avenger/Compass hybrid DCT)"],["m","Worn seal or valve leaking inside the hydraulic circuit"],["m","External leak and low transmission fluid level"],["l","Internal wear of the auxiliary pump"]],["Jerking and loss of power while driving","Engine fails to restart after a stop","Transmission warning, possibly with P07E4 or P07E6"],["shop_only",[300,6000]],"c"],"P0B0D":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Motor Control Module","P0B0D is an internal fault of the module that drives the auxiliary pump motor. GM documents it for the auxiliary transmission pump controller (ATPC) in its 2011 and 2014 hybrid tables and for the 2016 Cadillac ELR: it checks processor integrity, such as SPI hardware faults or a wrong seed and key between processors, with more than 9.5 V supply; 28 failures out of 32 samples in a 6.25 ms loop, detected within about 200 ms, set a Type A fault.",[["h","Electric pump with integrated control electronics failed (Jeep 4xe 8P75PH)"],["m","Processor or memory fault inside the pump control module"],["l","Supply voltage dips or poor ground at the module"]],["MIL on","Transmission in limp mode","Possibly lost communication with the pump module (U0287)"],["hard",[400,2000]],"c"],"P0B0E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor B Circuit","Many packs measure current with two channels or two sensors so the controller can cross-check them; sensor B is the second channel next to sensor A (P0ABF to P0AC3). P0B0E is its general circuit code.",[["h","Signal or ground wire of channel B damaged or connector loose"],["m","Current sensor or junction block with integrated sensor defective"],["l","Sensor supply voltage faulty"],["l","Battery ECU input defective"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning on","Battery power limited, less electric assist","Implausible state-of-charge display"],["shop_only",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0B0F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor B Circuit Range/Performance","P0B0F flags a current sensor B signal that is electrically in range but implausible, for example an offset at zero current or a value that does not match the other measurement. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Current sensor B offset drift or ageing"],["m","Ground offset or high resistance in the sensor wiring"],["l","Sensor displaced on the busbar or magnetic interference"]],["Hybrid or EV warning, MIL possible","State of charge jumps or drifts","Reduced battery power"],["shop_only",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0B10":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor B Circuit Low","P0B10 means the output of current sensor B lies below its valid range. GM uses a dual-range Hall sensor whose channel B covers small currents with high resolution: the 2019 battery energy control module table sets P0B10 when this fine current reads below -24 A (ADC count under 82) for 1.6 s within a 2 s window, Type A, one trip; the 2011 Volt table uses -23 A for 175 ms within 250 ms, one trip.",[["h","Sensor B signal line shorted to ground"],["m","5 V supply of the current sensor missing"],["m","Current sensor or junction block with integrated sensor defective"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning on","Battery charge and discharge power limited","Engine runs more often, fuel use rises"],["shop_only",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0B11":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor B Circuit High","P0B11 is set when current sensor B delivers a signal above its valid range. In GM's dual-range design, channel B is the fine channel for small currents: the 2019 battery energy control module table fails it above +24 A (ADC count over 4013) for 1.6 s within a 2 s window, Type A, one trip, and the 2011 Volt table above +23 A for 175 ms within 250 ms, one trip.",[["h","Open signal or ground wire of sensor B"],["m","Signal line shorted to the sensor supply or 12 V"],["m","Current sensor or junction block with integrated sensor defective"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning on","Battery power limited, less electric driving","Implausible state-of-charge display"],["shop_only",[100,1500]],"c"],"P0B12":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B12 covers a current sensor B signal that jumps or drops out in a way real current cannot, typically from a loose connector, a chafed signal wire or noise on the sensor ground. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Loose connector or poor terminal contact at the current sensor"],["m","Chafed signal or ground wire"],["l","Electrical noise on the sensor ground"]],["Intermittent hybrid or EV warning","Short power reductions during dropouts","Code stored without lasting symptoms"],["shop_only",[80,1200]],"c"],"P0B13":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Current Sensor A/B Correlation","P0B13 compares the two pack current readings, sensor A and sensor B, and sets when they disagree. On the Chevrolet Volt GM pairs a coarse and a fine channel: the code fails when they differ by more than 10 A while both read between -20 and 20 A, for example 10 s within a 12.5 s window (2016) or 400 of 500 samples at 25 ms (2019), Type A, one trip.",[["h","Battery controller software fault, fixed by update (Audi, GM)"],["m","Disturbance from an external charging station during charging"],["m","Offset drift of one of the two current channels"],["l","Internal communication fault between switching unit and current sensor"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Red battery or Service High Voltage message, request to stop safely","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[80,2000]],"c"],"P0B14":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense B Circuit","SAE J2012 reserves up to four pack voltage measurements, A to D; voltage sense B is the second one. P0B14 is its general circuit code: the battery controller sees an electrical fault on this input, typically an open or implausible raw signal.",[["h","Voltage sense lead or its fuse open inside the pack"],["m","Sense connector at the battery controller loose or corroded"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B15":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense B Circuit Range/Performance","P0B15 marks pack voltage measurement B as electrically valid but implausible: the value lies within the sensor range yet does not match a reference, typically the sum of the cell voltages or another pack measurement. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Drift or calibration error of measurement channel B"],["m","Faulty cell voltage acquisition distorts the reference sum"],["m","Increased contact resistance at sense terminals"],["l","Battery controller software needs an update"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist","Implausible state-of-charge or range display"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B16":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense B Circuit Low","P0B16 sets when pack voltage measurement B reports a value below its plausible minimum, usually an electrical cause such as a sense lead shorted to ground or an open input that pulls the reading down. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Sense lead of channel B shorted to ground"],["m","Fuse or resistor in the sense path open"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B17":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense B Circuit High","P0B17 means pack voltage measurement B reads above its plausible maximum. Mercedes-Benz uses this exact code on the eSprinter 907: inaccurate BMS measurements can store P0B171C, voltage sensor B in the high-voltage battery module shorted to positive with the value out of range, together with P0ABD00 for sensor A.",[["h","BMS measurement inaccuracy, fixed by software update (Mercedes eSprinter)"],["m","Sense lead of channel B shorted to a higher potential"],["l","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system","MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0B18":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B18 reports that pack voltage measurement B jumps or drops out briefly instead of failing steadily. The controller detects implausibly fast changes or short signal losses that disappear again, so the fault often shows up as a history code.",[["h","Loose or corroded sense connector"],["m","Sense lead chafed, intermittent contact"],["l","Battery controller software misinterprets transients"]],["Warning appears and disappears, often stored as history","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B19":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense C Circuit","Voltage sense C is the third of up to four pack voltage measurements defined in SAE J2012. P0B19 is its general circuit code and sets when the battery controller finds this input electrically unusable, for instance open or without a valid raw value.",[["h","Voltage sense lead C or its fuse open"],["m","Sense connector loose or corroded"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B1A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense C Circuit Range/Performance","P0B1A flags pack voltage measurement C as in range but not believable. The controller compares it with a reference, usually the sum of the cell voltages or another pack voltage reading, and sets the code when the difference stays too large.",[["h","Drift or calibration error of measurement channel C"],["m","Cell voltage acquisition faulty, reference sum wrong"],["m","Increased resistance at sense terminals"],["l","Battery controller software needs an update"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Implausible state-of-charge or range display","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B1B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense C Circuit Low","P0B1B sets when pack voltage measurement C delivers a reading below its plausible lower limit. Typical electrical causes are a sense lead shorted to ground, an open fuse or resistor in the sense path, or a defective controller input.",[["h","Sense lead of channel C shorted to ground"],["m","Fuse or resistor in the sense path open"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system","MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B1C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense C Circuit High","P0B1C reports that pack voltage measurement C reads above its plausible upper limit. Usually the measurement path is at fault, for example a sense lead shorted to a higher potential, a defective controller input or a software error in the evaluation, rather than a real overvoltage of the pack.",[["h","Sense lead of channel C shorted to a higher potential"],["m","Evaluation error in battery controller software"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B1D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B1D stands for an unsteady pack voltage measurement C: the signal jumps, drops out for moments or changes faster than a real battery voltage can. Because the fault often clears itself, it is frequently found only as a stored code.",[["h","Loose or corroded sense connector or terminal"],["m","Chafed sense lead with intermittent contact"],["l","Controller software misinterprets transients"]],["Warning comes and goes, often stored as history","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B1E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense D Circuit","Voltage sense D is the fourth and last pack voltage measurement in the SAE J2012 scheme. P0B1E is its general circuit code: the battery controller treats this input as electrically faulty, for example open or without a usable raw signal.",[["h","Voltage sense lead D or its fuse open"],["m","Sense connector loose or corroded"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B1F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense D Circuit Range/Performance","P0B1F says pack voltage measurement D is electrically present but disagrees with what the controller expects, typically the sum of cell voltages or a parallel pack measurement. It is a plausibility fault, not an open or shorted circuit.",[["h","Drift or calibration error of measurement channel D"],["m","Cell voltage acquisition faulty, reference sum wrong"],["m","Increased resistance at sense terminals"],["l","Battery controller software needs an update"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Implausible state-of-charge or range display","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B20":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense D Circuit Low","P0B20 sets when pack voltage measurement D falls below its plausible minimum. The cause is usually electrical: a sense lead shorted to ground, an interrupted fuse or resistor in the measurement path, or a damaged controller input.",[["h","Sense lead of channel D shorted to ground"],["m","Fuse or resistor in the sense path open"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system","MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B21":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense D Circuit High","P0B21 means pack voltage measurement D reports more than its plausible maximum. As with the other high codes, the measurement path is the main suspect, for example a sense lead shorted to a higher potential, a faulty input stage or an evaluation error in software, not an actual overcharged pack.",[["h","Sense lead of channel D shorted to a higher potential"],["m","Evaluation error in battery controller software"],["m","Measurement input of the battery controller defective"]],["MIL and hybrid/EV system warning on","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B22":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Sense D Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B22 completes the voltage sense D series and reports an intermittent or erratic pack voltage measurement D: the reading jumps, drops out briefly or changes implausibly fast, then recovers. Current code lists title it this way; the older label Generator Phase U Current Sensor Circuit does not belong to this number.",[["h","Loose or corroded sense connector or terminal"],["m","Chafed sense lead with intermittent contact"],["l","Controller software misinterprets transients"]],["Warning comes and goes, often stored as history","Battery power limited, reduced electric drive or assist","Vehicle may not reach Ready or may shut down the high-voltage system"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0B23":["Hybrid/EV Battery A Voltage","P0B23 is the general fault for the voltage of hybrid/EV battery A, the first of up to four sub-batteries or energy stores (A to D) that the battery controller evaluates separately; which physical unit carries the letter is up to the manufacturer. The code says that the measured voltage of this unit no longer fits the expected value, without the finer split into unstable (P0B24), low (P0B25) or high (P0B26).",[["m","Weak or failing cells or module in battery A"],["m","Fault in the voltage sense leads or connectors of battery A"],["l","Battery controller software or internal measurement fault"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Battery power limited, less electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B24":["Hybrid/EV Battery A Voltage Unstable","P0B24 reports that the voltage of hybrid/EV battery A is unstable: it swings or jumps more than the battery controller accepts instead of staying steadily too low or too high (P0B25, P0B26). Stellantis lists P0B24-00 with MIL on the Jeep Wrangler 4xe and Grand Cherokee 4xe, together with pack voltage variation, cell balancing and state-of-health codes; the cure is new software for the battery pack control module or, on the 2025 Wrangler, for the hybrid control processor.",[["h","Battery controller software (Jeep 4xe BPCM/HCP, Ford BECM updates)"],["m","Loose busbar, terminal or sense contact in battery A"],["l","Cell with rising internal resistance in battery A"]],["MIL on","Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","Vehicle may not start (Jeep 4xe)"],["shop_only",[80,3000]],"c"],"P0B25":["Hybrid/EV Battery A Voltage Low","P0B25 sets when the voltage of hybrid/EV battery A drops below the window the controller expects. Bentley uses it in two different systems.",[["h","Internal fault of the energy store, e.g. Bentley 48 V super capacitor"],["m","Aged or unbalanced cells pulling the unit voltage down"],["m","Deep discharge after long standstill"],["l","Voltage measurement fault in the controller or sense leads"]],["Bentley Dynamic Ride fault warning, roll control inactive","MIL on","Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on"],["shop_only",[300,5000]],"c"],"P0B26":["Hybrid/EV Battery A Voltage High","P0B26 means the voltage of hybrid/EV battery A is above the permitted value. Bentley documents it in the 48 V Dynamic Ride system of the Continental GT and Flying Spur, stored in address 0021 with symptom code 28672; the prescribed repair is replacing the super capacitor.",[["h","Failing energy store, e.g. Bentley 48 V super capacitor"],["m","Cell group charged too high, balancing not effective"],["l","Voltage measurement fault in the controller or sense leads"]],["Bentley Dynamic Ride fault warning, roll control inactive","MIL on","Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on"],["shop_only",[300,5000]],"c"],"P0B27":["Hybrid/EV Battery B Voltage","P0B27 is the general voltage fault of hybrid/EV battery B, the second separately monitored sub-battery or energy store. It covers a voltage that does not match expectation without saying whether it is unstable (P0B28), too low (P0B29) or too high (P0B2A).",[["m","Weak or failing cells or module in battery B"],["m","Fault in the voltage sense leads or connectors of battery B"],["l","Battery controller software or internal measurement fault"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Battery power limited, less electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B28":["Hybrid/EV Battery B Voltage Unstable","P0B28 flags an unstable voltage of hybrid/EV battery B: the reading fluctuates beyond what the battery controller tolerates. Stellantis documents it with P0B28-00 on the Jeep Wrangler 4xe: the MIL is on, and the bulletin warns that after 49 days with this MIL lit the vehicle is no longer allowed to start.",[["h","Battery pack control module software (Jeep 4xe update)"],["m","Loose busbar, terminal or sense contact in battery B"],["l","Cell with rising internal resistance in battery B"]],["MIL on","Start inhibited after a long period with the MIL lit (Jeep 4xe: 49 days)","Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on"],["shop_only",[80,3000]],"c"],"P0B29":["Hybrid/EV Battery B Voltage Low","P0B29 sets when the voltage of hybrid/EV battery B stays below the range the controller expects for the present state of charge and load. It is the low counterpart to P0B2A; fluctuating readings belong to P0B28 and the general fault to P0B27.",[["h","Aged cells with lost capacity in battery B"],["m","Deep discharge or self-discharge after standstill"],["l","Voltage measurement fault in the controller or sense leads"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Battery power limited, less electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B2A":["Hybrid/EV Battery B Voltage High","P0B2A is stored when the voltage of hybrid/EV battery B rises above its permitted upper limit. It mirrors P0B29 (too low), while P0B28 covers fluctuation and P0B27 the general fault.",[["m","Cell group in battery B charged too high, balancing ineffective"],["m","Voltage measurement fault in the controller or sense leads"],["l","Battery controller software fault"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Charging or recuperation restricted"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B2B":["Hybrid/EV Battery C Voltage","P0B2B is the general voltage fault of hybrid/EV battery C, the third sub-battery or energy store that a controller can monitor on its own. It signals an implausible voltage of this unit without the detail given by P0B2C (unstable), P0B2D (low) or P0B2E (high).",[["m","Weak or failing cells or module in battery C"],["m","Fault in the voltage sense leads or connectors of battery C"],["l","Battery controller software or internal measurement fault"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Battery power limited, less electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B2C":["Hybrid/EV Battery C Voltage Unstable","P0B2C indicates that the voltage of hybrid/EV battery C is unstable, meaning it changes faster or further than plausible for the current load, while a steady deviation would set P0B2D or P0B2E. No manufacturer documents P0B2C publicly.",[["m","Loose busbar, terminal or sense contact in battery C"],["m","Battery controller software (pattern known from P0B24/P0B28)"],["l","Cell with rising internal resistance in battery C"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Warning comes and goes, fault often stored as intermittent"],["shop_only",[80,3000]],"c"],"P0B2D":["Hybrid/EV Battery C Voltage Low","P0B2D means the voltage of hybrid/EV battery C is too low compared with what the controller expects. High readings of the same unit are P0B2E, fluctuating ones P0B2C.",[["h","Aged cells with lost capacity in battery C"],["m","Deep discharge or self-discharge after standstill"],["l","Voltage measurement fault in the controller or sense leads"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Battery power limited, less electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B2E":["Hybrid/EV Battery C Voltage High","P0B2E is set when the voltage of hybrid/EV battery C exceeds its upper limit. It pairs with P0B2D for undervoltage; P0B2C covers unstable readings and P0B2B the general fault.",[["m","Cell group in battery C charged too high, balancing ineffective"],["m","Drifting voltage measurement channel or sense lead fault"],["l","Battery controller software fault"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Charging or recuperation restricted"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B2F":["Hybrid/EV Battery D Voltage","P0B2F is the general voltage fault of hybrid/EV battery D, the fourth and last sub-battery in this code family. The controller reports with it that the voltage of unit D is implausible, without specifying unstable (P0B30), low (P0B31) or high (P0B32).",[["m","Weak or failing cells or module in battery D"],["m","Fault in the voltage sense leads or connectors of battery D"],["l","Battery controller software or internal measurement fault"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Battery power limited, less electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B30":["Hybrid/EV Battery D Voltage Unstable","P0B30 reports an unstable voltage of hybrid/EV battery D: the measured value fluctuates beyond the limit the controller allows. It differs from P0B31 and P0B32, which stand for a lasting low or high voltage, and from the general fault P0B2F.",[["m","Loose busbar, terminal or sense contact in battery D"],["m","Battery controller software (pattern known from P0B24/P0B28)"],["l","Cell with rising internal resistance in battery D"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Warning comes and goes, fault often stored as intermittent"],["shop_only",[80,3000]],"c"],"P0B31":["Hybrid/EV Battery D Voltage Low","P0B31 is stored when the voltage of hybrid/EV battery D stays below the expected range. High voltage of the same unit sets P0B32, fluctuation P0B30 and an unspecified deviation P0B2F.",[["h","Aged cells with lost capacity in battery D"],["m","Deep discharge or self-discharge after standstill"],["l","Voltage measurement fault in the controller or sense leads"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Battery power limited, less electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B32":["Hybrid/EV Battery D Voltage High","P0B32 sets when the voltage of hybrid/EV battery D goes above its permitted maximum. It is the high counterpart of P0B31; P0B30 covers unstable readings and P0B2F the general fault.",[["m","Cell group in battery D charged too high, balancing ineffective"],["m","Defective module in battery D"],["l","Voltage measurement fault in the controller or sense leads"]],["Hybrid/EV system or high-voltage battery warning on","MIL on","Charging or recuperation restricted"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0B33":["High Voltage Service Disconnect Circuit","The high-voltage service disconnect is the removable plug, usually fused, that splits the traction battery for safe servicing. A low-voltage detection loop runs through the plug, and the battery controller reads it to know whether the plug is seated; patents from Ford and Lear describe this loop, which opens before the main contacts part.",[["h","Service disconnect plug not fully seated or locked after service"],["m","Chafed, open or shorted detection loop wiring between plug base and battery controller"],["m","Corroded or spread terminals in the plug or its base"],["l","Battery controller input fault"]],["Vehicle does not enter ready mode or will not start","High-voltage system warning and MIL on","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0B34":["High Voltage Service Disconnect Circuit Performance","P0B34 covers a plausibility fault of the service disconnect detection: the signal is electrically within limits but does not match what the controller expects, for example a plug reported as removed while the high-voltage bus still shows voltage, or a state that changes without a matching event. Audi lists P0B3400 in the controller at address 008C of the 2025 Q5 e-hybrid, together with U029A00 and U059B00, and asks technicians to log the case in its pending service database; no threshold is published.",[["m","Battery controller software fault (Audi case under investigation)"],["m","Loose plug or worn contacts giving an implausible detection state"],["m","Increased resistance in the detection loop"],["l","Communication fault between battery controller and other modules"]],["Electrical system or high-voltage warning","No ready mode or charging interrupted","Related network codes stored at the same time"],["shop_only",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0B35":["High Voltage Service Disconnect Circuit Low","P0B35 sets when the service disconnect detection signal stays below its lower limit. Where the loop is coded with a resistor in the plug, as in a Lear patent, a reading near zero typically points to a short to ground in the loop wiring; the exact meaning depends on how the manufacturer powers the input.",[["h","Detection loop wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the plug base bridging to ground"],["l","Coding element in the plug shorted"],["l","Battery controller input stage fault"]],["No ready mode or stall with a stop-safely message","MIL and high-voltage warning on","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0B36":["High Voltage Service Disconnect Circuit High","P0B36 sets when the service disconnect detection signal stays above its upper limit. With a pulled-up input this usually means a short to a voltage source or a missing coding element in the plug; on some designs an open loop also reads high, so manufacturers separate it with P0B37 only where they can tell the cases apart.",[["h","Detection loop wire shorted to a supply voltage"],["m","Open loop or missing contact read as high signal"],["l","Wrong or damaged service disconnect plug without proper coding"],["l","Battery controller input stage fault"]],["Vehicle not ready to drive","MIL and high-voltage warning on","Charging blocked"],["shop_only",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0B37":["High Voltage Service Disconnect Open","P0B37 means the controller sees the service disconnect as open, either because the plug is really removed or because its detection loop is broken. Ford uses the code in the battery energy control module of the 2013-2016 C-Max Energi: bulletins SSM 46108 and TSB 17-0015 describe intermittent stalls or no crank with a Stop Safely Now message and MIL, stored with U3012 and, in another module, the interlock code P0A0A.",[["h","Low-voltage interlock wiring chafed with intermittent short to ground (Ford C-Max)"],["m","Service disconnect plug removed, loose or not locked"],["m","Open terminal in the plug base or interlock connector"],["l","Battery energy control module fault"]],["Intermittent stall or no crank","Stop Safely Now message and MIL","High-voltage interlock code stored in another module"],["shop_only",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0B38":["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump B Control Circuit/Open","Hybrid and electric drives cool the inverter and motor electronics with electric coolant pumps; J2012 numbers them A, B and C. P0B38 sets when the controller detects an open or general fault in the control line of pump B, typically by checking the driver output for missing load current.",[["h","Pump connector loose, corroded or terminal pushed out"],["m","Open control wire between controller and pump"],["m","Pump electronics or motor failed open"],["l","Driver stage in the controlling module faulty"]],["Hybrid system or coolant warning, MIL on","Reduced electric drive power when electronics get hot","Pump not running audibly after switch-on"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0B39":["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump B Control Circuit Low","P0B39 sets when the control signal of motor electronics coolant pump B is lower than the controller expects, usually a short to ground on the command line or a pump input that pulls the line down. On a low-side switched or PWM line the module sees the voltage stuck low while it commands the pump off.",[["h","Control wire shorted to ground, often chafed at brackets"],["m","Pump electronics pulling the control input low"],["m","Coolant or water in the pump connector"],["l","Driver stage in the controlling module faulty"]],["MIL and hybrid or coolant warning","Pump runs permanently or not at all, depending on circuit design","Power limitation at high load or heat"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0B3A":["Motor Electronics Coolant Pump B Control Circuit High","P0B3A sets when the control signal of motor electronics coolant pump B stays higher than expected, typically a short to battery voltage on the command line or, with some driver designs, an open pump input that floats high. The module then cannot switch the pump as requested.",[["h","Control wire shorted to battery voltage"],["m","Pump control input open so the line floats high"],["l","Controller software misjudging the circuit, fixed by update"],["l","Driver stage in the controlling module faulty"]],["MIL and hybrid or coolant warning","Pump cannot be switched off or on as commanded","Reduced electric power when the inverter heats up"],["medium",[60,600]],"c"],"P0B3B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense A Circuit","Voltage sense A to Z and beyond are the cell or cell-group sense channels of the battery monitoring electronics, unlike the pack voltage sense codes P0ABA and P0B14 ff, which measure the whole pack. P0B3B is the circuit code for channel A.",[["h","Open cell sense line, splice or bus bar connection inside the pack"],["m","Intermittent fault in the flexible printed circuit to the battery controller (Ford Mach-E)"],["m","Sensor software misreading inverter noise, fixed by update (Honda)"],["l","Internal failure of the traction battery requiring pack replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV)"]],["MIL or powertrain warning, Stop Safely Now message possible","No start or no charging","Fail-safe EV mode with limited speed and sudden loss of power"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B3C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense A Circuit Range/Performance","P0B3C flags a cell voltage channel A that reads plausible values but does not follow the other cells. GM runs this check in Hybrid Powertrain Control Processor 2 as a stuck-in-range test: while the average cell voltage moves by at least 0.005 V in one direction between samples, channel A must move as well; 35 failures in 40 samples at 100 ms set the code, Type A, one trip.",[["h","Battery controller software, fixed by reprogramming (FCA Pacifica)"],["m","Cell voltage measurement channel frozen inside the monitoring electronics"],["m","High resistance in the cell sense line or its connection"],["l","Cell monitoring board failure inside the pack"]],["MIL on, often with several voltage sense codes","Reduced electric range or no electric drive","Contactor related codes stored at the same time"],["shop_only",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0B3D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense A Circuit Low","P0B3D sets when cell voltage channel A reads below the lowest value a real cell can have, which points to the measurement rather than the cell. GM sets it in the battery energy control module when cell voltage A is 0.2 V or less for 1.4 s within a 2 s window, Type A, one trip, provided no open sense line, communication or balancing fault is active.",[["h","Battery pack control module software, fixed by update (Ram eTorque)"],["m","Sense line shorted to the adjacent line or to the cell negative"],["l","Measurement input of the cell monitoring chip failed"]],["MIL on","Start-stop and electric assist unavailable","Hybrid battery power limited"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B3E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense A Circuit High","P0B3E sets when cell voltage channel A reads above any value a healthy lithium-ion cell reaches, so the reading is treated as a sensing fault. GM sets it in the battery energy control module when cell voltage A is 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s within a 2 s window, Type A, one trip.",[["h","Battery pack control module software causing a false MIL (FCA)"],["m","Open sense line letting the input float high"],["l","Measurement input of the cell monitoring chip failed"]],["MIL on, possibly without noticeable drivability change","Electric drive or start-stop disabled","Charging limited"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B3F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B3F is reserved for a cell voltage channel A that jumps or drops out briefly instead of failing steadily. No manufacturer documents P0B3F publicly.",[["h","Loose or cracked cell tap connection"],["m","Fretting or vibration damage at the monitoring board connector"],["m","Electrical noise from inverter or motor coupling into the sense lines"]],["MIL or hybrid warning that comes and goes","Occasional loss of electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[80,6000]],"c"],"P0B40":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense B Circuit","P0B40 is the circuit code of cell voltage sense channel B, the second cell or cell group the battery monitoring electronics measures; pack-level voltage uses P0ABA and P0B14 ff instead. GM applies the same open sense line test as on channel A: a 1.3 V jump between neighbouring cells, under 36 mV with balancing on, or splice voltage above 0.1 V (bus bar 0.5 V) for 0.6 s, Type A, one trip.",[["h","Open cell sense line, splice or bus bar connection inside the pack"],["m","Intermittent flexible printed circuit cable in the battery controller (Ford Mach-E, e-Transit)"],["m","Internal traction battery failure requiring replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV)"]],["Wrench indicator or MIL, Stop Safely Now message possible","No start condition","Battery cannot be charged"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B41":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense B Circuit Range/Performance","P0B41 reports that cell voltage channel B gives values inside the normal range that fail a plausibility check against the other cells. In GM's 2019 tables Hybrid Powertrain Control Processor 2 compares sample-to-sample changes: if the average cell voltage trends by at least 0.005 V while channel B stays unchanged, a failure is counted, and 35 of 40 samples at 100 ms set the code, Type A, one trip, with system voltage above 10.2 V.",[["h","Battery controller software, fixed by reprogramming (FCA Pacifica)"],["m","Cell voltage measurement channel frozen inside the monitoring electronics"],["m","High resistance in the cell sense line or its connection"],["l","Cell monitoring board failure inside the pack"]],["MIL on, often with several voltage sense codes","Electric drive reduced or unavailable","Contactor related codes stored at the same time"],["shop_only",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0B42":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense B Circuit Low","P0B42 sets when cell voltage channel B reads implausibly low, below what any usable cell would show. GM's battery energy control module sets it at 0.2 V or less for 1.4 s within a 2 s window, Type A, one trip, and only when no open sense line, slave communication or balancing fault is present.",[["h","Battery pack control module software causing a false MIL (FCA eTorque)"],["m","Sense line shorted to the adjacent line or to the cell negative"],["l","Measurement input of the cell monitoring chip failed"]],["MIL on","Start-stop inoperative","Vehicle behaves as if running on the engine only"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B43":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense B Circuit High","P0B43 sets when cell voltage channel B reads higher than a lithium-ion cell can physically reach in service, so the controller blames the measurement path. GM documents the criteria for its battery energy control module: cell voltage B at 4.8 V or above for 1.4 s within a 2 s window, Type A, one trip, with open sense line, communication and balancing faults ruled out first.",[["h","Open sense line letting the input float high"],["m","Battery controller software misreading the channel"],["l","Measurement input of the cell monitoring chip failed"]],["MIL and hybrid warning on","Charging or electric drive limited"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B44":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense B Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B44 is the J2012 slot for a cell voltage channel B whose reading jumps or drops out for short moments rather than failing permanently. No manufacturer documents P0B44 publicly.",[["h","Loose or cracked cell tap connection"],["m","Fretting or vibration damage at the monitoring board connector"],["m","Electrical noise from inverter or motor coupling into the sense lines"]],["MIL or hybrid warning that comes and goes","Occasional loss of electric drive or assist"],["shop_only",[80,6000]],"c"],"P0B45":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense C Circuit","Voltage sense C is the third cell or cell-group measuring channel of the battery monitoring electronics, not a pack voltage input like P0ABA. P0B45 reports an electrical break in that channel.",[["h","Open cell sense tap, splice or bus bar connection on channel C"],["m","Intermittent flexible printed circuit between cells and battery controller (Ford Mach-E, E-Transit)"],["m","Internal traction battery failure requiring pack replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV)"]],["Vehicle does not start or does not become ready to drive","High-voltage battery no longer charges","Powertrain warning (wrench) and Stop Safely Now message"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B46":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense C Circuit Range/Performance","With P0B46 the channel C wiring is intact; the value on it looks plausible but no longer behaves like a real cell. GM's 2019 tables run the test in Hybrid Powertrain Control Processor 2: whenever the average cell voltage trends by at least 0.005 V in one direction, channel C has to follow; if it stays frozen in 35 of 40 samples at 100 ms, the code sets, Type A.",[["h","Battery controller software misjudging the channel, fixed by reflash (Chrysler Pacifica PHEV)"],["m","Measuring chip input for channel C stuck at a constant value"],["l","High-resistance cell tap damping the channel C signal"]],["MIL on without noticeable driving fault","Reduced electric power or hybrid assist","Contactor or precharge codes stored at the same time (Pacifica)"],["shop_only",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0B47":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense C Circuit Low","P0B47 is stored when channel C delivers a voltage too low to come from a working cell or block, so the controller blames the measurement rather than the battery. GM documents two implementations.",[["h","Sense lines of channel C shorted to each other or to a neighbouring tap"],["m","Failed measuring input in the battery monitoring module"],["m","Loose or corroded block connector shared by blocks 3 and 4 (GM two-mode)"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Electric drive or engine start-stop no longer available","Battery power limited, sluggish acceleration"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B48":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense C Circuit High","No healthy lithium-ion cell reaches 4.8 V, which is why GM's battery energy control module treats a channel C reading of 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s within a 2 s window as a sensing fault and stores P0B48, Type A, one trip. On the 2012 two-mode full-size hybrids the threshold is scaled to a nickel-metal hydride block: block 3 above 23 V in 15 of 20 samples, Type B, two trips.",[["h","Channel C sense line touching a tap of higher potential"],["m","Failed measuring input or reference in the monitoring electronics"],["l","Monitoring software fault, check for controller updates"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV system warning","Reduced charge and discharge power","Plug-in charging refused"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B49":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B49 is reserved for a channel C signal that jumps or drops out briefly instead of failing for good. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; GM, which uses the other four channel C codes, has no erratic test for it in its published tables.",[["h","Loose crimp or connector on the channel C sense line"],["m","Cracked solder joint on the cell monitoring board"],["l","Electrical noise from the inverter coupling into the sense line"]],["Warning light that appears and disappears","Occasional short loss of electric drive power"],["shop_only",[80,6000]],"c"],"P0B4A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense D Circuit","P0B4A is the open-circuit code of cell sense channel D, the fourth measuring channel of the battery monitoring electronics. GM checks it in the battery energy control module with the same open sense line logic as the other channels: a gap of 1.3 V or more between adjacent cells, under 36 mV on a cell being balanced, or splice voltages above 0.1 V or 0.5 V at a bus bar, for 0.6 s, Type A.",[["h","Open channel D sense tap or connector (code set alone)"],["m","Faulty BECM flexible printed circuit affecting several channels (Ford Mach-E, E-Transit)"],["m","Internal traction battery fault (Ford Fusion PHEV)"]],["No start or vehicle will not go into ready mode","Charging not possible","Several sense channel codes stored together"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B4B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense D Circuit Range/Performance","How the plausibility of channel D is judged depends on the pack. On the 2012 GM two-mode hybrids the battery pack control module multiplies block 4 by 20 and compares it with the pack voltage; a gap above 70 V in 160 of 170 samples sets P0B4B, Type B, two trips.",[["m","Outdated battery controller software (Chrysler Pacifica PHEV)"],["m","Block or cell voltage measurement drifting away from the pack voltage"],["m","Measuring input of channel D frozen at a constant value"]],["MIL, often after several drives","Limited hybrid assist or electric range"],["shop_only",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0B4C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense D Circuit Low","From the driver's seat P0B4C looks like a battery problem: the hybrid warning comes on and electric power is cut back. Technically it is a measuring fault on cell channel D, whose reading has fallen below what even a deeply discharged cell would show.",[["h","Short between channel D sense line and a neighbouring line"],["m","Defective input on the cell monitoring module"],["l","Corroded tap shared by blocks 4 and 5 (GM two-mode)"]],["Hybrid warning message and MIL","Electric power and regeneration cut back","Engine runs more often than usual"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B4D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense D Circuit High","P0B4D sets when cell sense channel D reports more voltage than the cell or block behind it can deliver. GM's limits are 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s within 2 s on lithium-ion packs (battery energy control module, Type A, one trip) and above 23 V for block 4 of the 2012 two-mode hybrids (15 of 20 samples, Type B, two trips).",[["h","Channel D sense lead contacting a tap of higher potential"],["m","Damaged measuring input in the monitoring electronics"],["l","Moisture or contamination on the monitoring board creating leakage paths"]],["MIL and battery system warning","Reduced acceleration and regenerative braking","Charging power limited or charging refused"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B4E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense D Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B4E completes the five-code set of cell channel D. Where P0B4A means a lasting open circuit, P0B4B a frozen or implausible value and P0B4C or P0B4D a reading beyond fixed limits, this code is meant for short, irregular dropouts and spikes.",[["h","Connector or tap losing contact under vibration"],["m","Solder joint or crimp cracked by thermal cycling"]],["Intermittent hybrid warning, often on rough roads","Fault not reproducible during workshop test"],["shop_only",[80,6000]],"c"],"P0B4F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense E Circuit","Cell sense channel E is the fifth measuring path between the battery cells and the monitoring electronics; P0B4F means the controller sees it as electrically open. Ford's experience shows how serious this can be: on 2019-2020 Fusion plug-in hybrids P0B4F appears among up to fourteen open-line codes from P0B3B to P0B7C with a no-start condition, and the bulletin prescribes replacing the high-voltage battery.",[["m","Internal failure of the high-voltage battery (Ford Fusion PHEV)"],["m","Intermittent BECM flexible printed circuit (Ford Mach-E, E-Transit)"],["h","Broken channel E sense lead, splice or bus bar contact"]],["No start","Battery cannot be charged","Stop Safely Now message while driving"],["shop_only",[100,9000]],"c"],"P0B50":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense E Circuit Range/Performance","On the 2020 Chrysler Pacifica plug-in hybrid P0B50 is called Voltage Sense 5 Circuit Performance and rarely comes alone: the FCA bulletin lists it with the other sense channels, contactor stuck codes, a long precharge time and wake-up circuit faults, all cured by updating the battery pack control module. Some owners also notice that scheduled charging fails because the pack does not wake up.",[["h","Battery pack control module software, fixed by reflash (Chrysler Pacifica PHEV)"],["m","Channel E reading frozen in the measuring chip"],["l","Block voltage measurement drifting from pack voltage (GM two-mode)"]],["MIL on","Scheduled charging does not start","Premature oil change or fuel refresh message (Pacifica)"],["shop_only",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0B51":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense E Circuit Low","P0B51 tells that cell sense channel E reads near zero. GM's battery energy control module only runs this check under clear conditions: the module is in run, crank or accessory mode, the second protection self-test is not running, and no slave communication, open sense line, balancing or internal performance fault is active.",[["h","Channel E sense line shorted to its neighbour"],["m","Faulty input on the cell monitoring slave board"],["l","Pin backed out of the monitoring harness connector"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Electric-only driving unavailable"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B52":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense E Circuit High","The threshold behind P0B52 is chosen so that only a measurement error can reach it. A lithium-ion cell is fully charged at roughly 4.2 V, yet GM's battery energy control module waits for 4.8 V or more on channel E, for 1.4 s within 2 s, before storing the code as Type A, one trip.",[["h","Channel E sense line contacting a higher cell tap"],["m","Faulty measuring input or reference in the monitoring module"]],["Charging stops early or is refused","MIL and reduced battery power","Shorter electric range"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B53":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense E Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B53 belongs to cell sense channel E and describes a signal that is sometimes fine and sometimes not. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Intermittent contact at the channel E tap or connector"],["m","Chafed sense lead touching briefly under movement"],["l","Marginal component on the monitoring board"]],["Warning that clears and returns","Brief loss of electric power while driving"],["shop_only",[80,6000]],"c"],"P0B54":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense F Circuit","Ford's 2024 special service message for the 2023 Mustang Mach-E and E-Transit is the most instructive case for P0B54: vehicles built up to 9 December 2023 could store this and the other open-line codes from P0B3B to P0B5E, show the wrench light, refuse to start or charge, or display Stop Safely Now, and the cause was an intermittent BECM flexible printed circuit cable rather than the cells. The 2019-2020 Fusion PHEV shows the same code with a battery replacement as remedy.",[["h","Intermittent BECM flexible printed circuit cable (Ford Mach-E, E-Transit built to Dec 2023)"],["m","Open channel F tap or splice"],["l","Internal traction battery failure (Ford Fusion PHEV)"]],["Powertrain malfunction (wrench) indicator on","No start or no charging","Stop Safely Now message"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B55":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense F Circuit Range/Performance","P0B55 is the plausibility code of cell channel F: the signal exists and lies inside its range, yet it does not move like the rest of the pack. On the 2016 Chevrolet Volt the vehicle integration control module checks whether channel F moves in the same direction as the average cell voltage, failing it after 7 seconds in an 8 second window, Type A; GM's 2019 tables use 35 failures in 40 samples of 100 ms.",[["m","Battery controller software, fixed by update (Chrysler Pacifica PHEV)"],["m","Channel F measuring input stuck or slow to respond"],["l","Increased resistance in the channel F tap or connector"]],["MIL with otherwise normal driving","Reduced hybrid or electric performance"],["shop_only",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0B56":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense F Circuit Low","The GM two-mode hybrids of 2012, Tahoe, Yukon, Escalade, Silverado and Sierra, show a detail of P0B56 that lithium-ion packs lack. Their nickel-metal hydride pack is read in blocks, channel F being block 6, and the battery pack control module sets the code only when block 6 and block 7 both read under 2 V in 15 of 20 samples at 100 ms, Type B, two trips; a shared sense wire between the blocks is the obvious suspect then.",[["h","Fault in the sense wire shared by blocks 6 and 7 (GM two-mode)"],["m","Channel F sense line shorted to a neighbouring line"],["m","Failed input on the cell monitoring electronics"]],["Hybrid system warning, MIL after the second drive (two-mode)","Engine runs continuously, no electric launch","Lower fuel economy"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B57":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense F Circuit High","P0B57 stands for an implausibly high value on cell sense channel F, as opposed to P0B56 for an implausibly low one and P0B55 for a frozen value. GM uses fixed upper limits: 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s within 2 s in the battery energy control module of its lithium-ion systems (Type A, one trip), and above 23 V for block 6 in the 2012 two-mode trucks (15 of 20 samples, Type B, two trips).",[["h","Channel F sense lead touching a higher potential"],["m","Measuring input or reference fault in the module"]],["MIL and battery warning","Battery charging limited","Weaker acceleration in hybrid mode"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B58":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense F Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B58 is reserved for an erratic signal on cell sense channel F, and the honest state is short: no manufacturer documents this code publicly. The neighbouring codes are well covered; Ford and GM use the open circuit code P0B54, GM and Chrysler the range code P0B55, GM the fixed low and high limits.",[["h","Sense tap of channel F loosening under vibration"],["m","Damaged section of the flexible sense circuit"]],["Sporadic warning depending on road or temperature","Occasional brief power reduction"],["shop_only",[80,6000]],"c"],"P0B59":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense G Circuit","Voltage sense G is the seventh cell measuring channel; how many cells or groups it covers depends on how the manufacturer splits the pack among monitoring chips. P0B59 is its open-circuit code.",[["h","Open channel G tap, splice or bus bar contact"],["m","Intermittent BECM flexible circuit (Ford Mach-E, E-Transit)"],["l","Internal traction battery failure (Ford Fusion PHEV)"]],["Vehicle not ready to drive","Charging blocked","Wrench light or hybrid warning"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B5A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense G Circuit Range/Performance","P0B5A asks whether cell channel G behaves like a real cell. GM answers that question in three ways across its programmes.",[["h","Channel G measuring input frozen or drifting"],["m","Block 7 reading out of line with pack voltage (GM two-mode)"],["l","Monitoring software issue, check for updates"]],["MIL without obvious drivability change","Limited hybrid assist"],["shop_only",[50,6000]],"c"],"P0B5B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense G Circuit Low","When P0B5B appears, the battery controller has seen cell sense channel G at a level no usable cell would show, and it shuts down or restricts electric operation to stay on the safe side. GM documents the trigger for its lithium-ion systems as cell voltage G at or below 0.2 V for 1.4 s in a 2 s window, Type A, with the test held off while communication, open line or balancing faults are active.",[["h","Channel G sense line shorted to a neighbouring line"],["m","Damaged or backed-out pin in the monitoring connector"],["m","Failed measuring input on the monitoring module"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Electric drive disabled or restricted"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B5C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense G Circuit High","P0B5C marks an out-of-range high reading on cell sense channel G. For a technician the quickest plausibility check is arithmetic: the sum of all cell or block readings should match the pack voltage, so a single channel that reads far too high while the total stays normal is a sensing fault.",[["h","Channel G sense lead touching a higher cell tap"],["m","Faulty measuring input or reference in the module"],["l","Contamination or moisture on the monitoring board"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV warning","Charging limited or refused"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B5D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense G Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B5D is the J2012 code for an unsteady signal on cell sense channel G, a reading that flickers or drops out for moments without staying beyond a limit. No manufacturer documents P0B5D publicly.",[["h","Marginal contact at the channel G tap or connector"],["m","Sense harness under strain, losing contact with pack movement"]],["Warning that disappears after restart and returns","Brief reduction of electric power"],["shop_only",[80,6000]],"c"],"P0B5E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense H Circuit","P0B5E flags a broken measuring path on voltage sense H, the eighth cell tap channel read by the battery monitoring electronics. In GM's 2019 tables the battery energy control module runs an open sense line test within a 0.6 s window: neighbouring cells diverging by 1.3 V or more, a cell under 36 mV while its balancing switch is on, or a splice above 0.1 V (0.5 V at a bus bar splice) sets it as Type A.",[["h","Intermittent flexible printed circuit cable at the battery controller (Ford Mach-E, E-Transit 2023)"],["m","Open cell tap, splice or bus bar connection on channel H"],["m","Internal traction battery failure requiring pack replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV)"]],["Powertrain malfunction (wrench) indicator and Stop Safely Now message","Vehicle does not start or does not become ready to drive","High-voltage battery cannot be charged"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B5F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense H Circuit Range/Performance","With P0B5F the channel H wiring is intact, but the value it delivers does not fit the rest of the battery. GM runs two quite different plausibility tests.",[["h","Block or cell voltage input for channel H drifting away from the true value"],["m","Measuring chip input stuck at a constant value"],["l","Faulty pack voltage reference distorting the comparison"]],["Hybrid system warning with MIL","Reduced electric assist or regeneration","Inaccurate state-of-charge display"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0B60":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense H Circuit Low","P0B60 means channel H reports less voltage than any healthy cell or block could hold, so the measurement itself is suspect. GM publishes three variants.",[["h","Channel H sense lead shorted to ground or to the next lower tap"],["m","Failed measuring input on the cell monitoring board"],["l","Cell or block on channel H actually collapsed"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV system warning","Electric drive power limited or switched off","Charging interrupted"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B61":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense H Circuit High","A lithium-ion cell cannot sit much above about 4.2 V, so when channel H reads 4.8 V or more the GM battery energy control module concludes the sense circuit is wrong and stores P0B61 after 1.4 s within a 2 s window, Type A. GM's BAS Plus mild hybrid sets the same code at more than 4.65 V on the cell sense line in 7 of 10 samples, Type B, while the 2012 two-mode hybrids treat H as nickel-metal hydride block 8 and only object above 23 V in 15 of 20 samples, Type B.",[["h","Channel H sense lead in contact with a higher cell tap"],["m","Lost reference connection letting the measuring input float"],["l","Internal fault of the cell monitoring chip"]],["Charging refused or stopped early","MIL with reduced electric power"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B62":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense H Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B62 describes a channel H signal that jumps or cuts out for moments rather than failing permanently. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Intermittent contact at the channel H connector or flexible circuit"],["m","Cracked solder joint on the monitoring board"],["l","Inverter noise coupling into the sense line"]],["Warning appears occasionally and clears after a restart","Short interruptions of electric drive"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B63":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense I Circuit","Channel I is the ninth sense input of the battery monitoring chain, and P0B63 reports that its circuit is open. GM shows how differently this can be detected.",[["h","Broken channel I sense wire, crimp or cell tap"],["m","Internal traction battery fault, pack replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV)"],["l","Corroded or loose connector at the cell monitoring board"]],["Vehicle will not start or enter ready mode","Service hybrid or EV system warning","Electric mode unavailable"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B64":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense I Circuit Range/Performance","P0B64 is a rationality fault: channel I is connected and reads a believable number, yet it does not move with the other cells. GM's 2016 Chevrolet Volt compares the movement of each cell with the average; if channel I does not follow when the average moves by more than 0.022 V, 7 out of 8 seconds fail the check, Type A.",[["h","Channel I measuring input frozen at a constant value"],["m","Battery controller software misjudging the channel"],["l","High-resistance cell tap damping the signal"]],["MIL on, vehicle drives normally","Reduced hybrid assist or electric range"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B65":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense I Circuit Low","The 2012 GM two-mode hybrids show a detail worth knowing for P0B65: their battery pack control module only declares channel I, nickel-metal hydride block 9, too low when block 9 and its neighbour block 10 both read under 2 V, and only after 15 of 20 samples at 100 ms; the fault is Type B and needs two trips. A single weak block alone therefore does not set it.",[["h","Channel I sense lead shorted to ground or a lower tap"],["m","Connector fault affecting adjacent blocks 9 and 10 (two-mode)"],["l","Failed measuring input in the battery controller"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Engine runs more often, electric assist reduced","Limited power in limp mode"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B66":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense I Circuit High","When channel I claims a voltage above what its cell or block can physically reach, the controller stores P0B66. The thresholds GM publishes depend on chemistry and architecture: lithium-ion packs with a battery energy control module set it at 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s in a 2 s window, Type A, one trip; the BAS Plus mild hybrid at above 4.65 V in 7 of 10 samples, Type B; the 2012 two-mode trucks, which measure nickel-metal hydride block 9 rather than single cells, above 23 V in 15 of 20 samples, Type B.",[["h","Channel I lead touching a higher-potential tap"],["m","Open reference or ground of the measuring chip"],["l","Genuinely overcharged cell after a charging control fault"]],["Charging stops or is refused","MIL and reduced power"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B67":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense I Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","SAE J2012 reserves P0B67 for an unsteady signal on channel I, meaning readings that spike, drop and recover. No manufacturer documents this code publicly, and none of the GM tables that define the other four channel I codes includes an erratic test.",[["h","Sense contact on channel I opening under vibration or heat"],["m","Corroded terminal in the module connector"]],["Sporadic warning without a reproducible pattern","Momentary drop in electric power"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B68":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense J Circuit","For owners of a 2019 or 2020 Ford Fusion plug-in hybrid, P0B68 is best known from bulletin 23-2039: the car will not start, the battery energy control module stores one or more open sense line codes from P0B3B to P0B7C, and the prescribed repair is a complete high-voltage traction battery. Technically the code says that the tenth measuring channel, J, has lost its connection to the cells.",[["h","Internal traction battery failure, pack replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV 2019-2020)"],["m","Open sense wire or cell tap on channel J"],["l","Bad bus bar or splice connection near channel J"]],["No start, vehicle does not become ready","Hybrid system warning on the cluster","Plug-in charging unavailable"],["shop_only",[150,8000]],"c"],"P0B69":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense J Circuit Range/Performance","P0B69 is set when channel J is electrically present but its readings do not behave plausibly. GM's BAS Plus mild hybrid explains the logic most clearly: once the average cell voltage moves by more than 0.045 V, the cell under test must move in the same direction and by at least half as much; 20 failures in 40 samples set the code as Type B, two trips.",[["h","Channel J input reacting too little to real voltage changes"],["m","Corroded or high-resistance cell tap"],["l","Calibration error in the battery controller"]],["MIL without obvious driveability problem","Hybrid assist reduced"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B6A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense J Circuit Low","A reading of almost zero on channel J points to the measurement, not to the cell, and that is what P0B6A records. In GM's lithium-ion systems (2016 and 2019 tables) the battery energy control module sets it at 0.2 V or less for 1.4 s within 2 s, Type A.",[["h","Channel J lead chafed through and shorted to ground"],["m","Damaged measuring input on the monitoring board"],["l","Cell on channel J internally shorted"]],["Electric drive limited or unavailable","MIL and battery warning message"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B6B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense J Circuit High","On GM's 2012 two-mode hybrid trucks the battery is divided into 20 nickel-metal hydride blocks, and channel J is block 10. The battery pack control module samples it every 100 ms while the 12 V supply is between 9 and 18 V; if block 10 reports more than 23 V in 15 of 20 samples, P0B6B is stored as Type B, so the MIL comes on after the second trip.",[["h","Channel J lead shorted to a higher block or cell tap"],["m","Faulty measuring input in the battery pack control module"]],["MIL after the second drive (Type B systems)","Charging or regeneration restricted","Reduced electric power"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B6C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense J Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","No manufacturer documents P0B6C publicly. The code stands for a channel J signal that is unstable, with brief jumps or dropouts, as opposed to the steady faults covered by P0B68 to P0B6B.",[["h","Marginal contact in the channel J harness or connector"],["m","Cracked solder joint on the monitoring electronics"]],["Intermittent warning light","Brief power reductions while driving"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B6D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense K Circuit","P0B6D marks an open circuit on voltage sense K, the eleventh cell measuring channel. GM has used the code since its first published hybrid tables: in 2011 the open-line test already checked bus bar voltages, flagging more than 0.7 V.",[["h","Loose or corroded bus bar connection at the channel K tap"],["m","Internal traction battery fault, pack replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV)"],["m","Broken sense wire between cells and monitoring board"]],["Vehicle not ready to drive","Hybrid or EV system warning with MIL","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B6E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense K Circuit Range/Performance","In a nickel-metal hydride pack with 20 equal blocks, twenty times one block voltage should roughly equal the whole pack voltage. GM's 2012 two-mode hybrids use exactly this for P0B6E, assigned to block 11: the battery pack control module checks blocks 11 and 12, and if both scaled values deviate from the pack voltage sensor by more than 70 V in 160 of 170 samples, the code is set, Type B.",[["h","Block or cell voltage measurement of channel K out of calibration"],["m","Pack voltage sensor reading off, distorting the comparison"],["l","Measuring input stuck at one value"]],["MIL after two trips","Less electric assist, more engine running"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0B6F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense K Circuit Low","GM's BAS Plus mild hybrid, the eAssist system in table 13OBDG05C, gives P0B6F a simple rule: if the cell sense line for channel K measures below 1.4 V in 7 of 10 samples at 200 ms while the 12 V supply is above 8.8 V, the code is stored as Type B, two trips. Since a lithium-ion cell in use never falls that low, the value must come from a short or a failed input.",[["h","Channel K sense line shorted to ground or a lower tap"],["m","Failed measuring input in the battery interface module"],["l","Blown protective element in the sense line"]],["Hybrid warning and MIL after the second trip","Auto stop and electric assist disabled"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0B70":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense K Circuit High","P0B70 says that cell voltage K reads too high to be genuine. GM's 2019 battery energy control module sets it at 4.8 V or more, held for 1.4 s within 2 s, as a Type A fault with immediate MIL, and only when no communication loss, open sense line or internal module fault takes precedence.",[["h","Channel K lead in contact with a higher tap"],["m","Floating measuring input after loss of its lower reference"],["l","Cell monitoring chip fault"]],["Charging blocked","MIL and power limitation"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B71":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense K Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B71 belongs to the unsteady fault type: the channel K reading flickers or drops out momentarily without staying beyond a fixed limit. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Fretting corrosion on channel K connector terminals"],["m","Sense harness under strain losing contact with movement"]],["Warning that comes and goes","Occasional short loss of electric power"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B72":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense L Circuit","P0B72 reports an open circuit on voltage sense L, the twelfth channel. One detail sets it apart from its neighbours: GM's 2011 hybrid tables list every open sense line code from P0B3B upward except P0B72 (and P0B90), so the early system did not use it, while L's range, low and high codes were present.",[["h","Open sense lead, crimp or cell tap on channel L"],["m","Internal traction battery failure, pack replacement (Ford Fusion PHEV)"],["l","High-resistance splice or bus bar joint"]],["No start or no ready mode","Battery or hybrid system warning","Charging aborted"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0B73":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense L Circuit Range/Performance","Channel L delivers a value, but P0B73 tells you that the value is not credible. GM checks this in three ways.",[["h","Channel L input not following real cell voltage changes"],["m","Battery controller software or calibration issue"],["l","Corroded cell tap slowing the signal"]],["MIL with normal driving behaviour","Reduced electric range or assist"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0B74":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense L Circuit Low","P0B74 is set when channel L reads implausibly low. On the 2012 GM two-mode hybrids, where L is nickel-metal hydride block 12 of 20, the battery pack control module needs both block 12 and block 13 under 2 V in 15 of 20 samples before it stores the code as Type B; no battery control module fault P0A1F may be active.",[["h","Channel L sense lead shorted to a lower potential"],["m","Defective measuring input in the battery controller"],["l","Cell or block on channel L actually failed"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Electric operation restricted","Engine runs continuously on hybrids"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B75":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense L Circuit High","With P0B75 the battery controller rejects channel L because it reads above any value a real cell or block can reach. GM's lithium-ion systems in the 2016 Volt and the 2019 tables set it at 4.8 V or more over 1.4 s in a 2 s window, Type A, one trip; GM's BAS Plus mild hybrid at above 4.65 V in 7 of 10 samples, Type B.",[["h","Channel L lead touching a higher tap"],["m","Floating measuring input after an open reference line"],["l","Real overvoltage of the cell after a charging fault"]],["Charging and regeneration limited","MIL and hybrid or EV warning"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B76":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense L Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B76 closes the channel L group and covers an erratic signal: values that jump or vanish briefly while the circuit otherwise works. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; GM, the only maker with public tables for L, defines open, plausibility, low and high tests for this channel but no erratic test, and the Ford bulletins name only the open-circuit code P0B72.",[["h","Intermittent contact at the channel L cell tap"],["m","Fatigued sense harness wire"],["l","Cracked solder joint on the monitoring board"]],["Warning clears after restart and comes back","Momentary power reduction"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B77":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense M Circuit","P0B77 flags an open or high-resistance tap on voltage sense M, the thirteenth cell channel. GM's 2019 battery energy control module checks seven cases: adjacent cells differing by 1.3 V or more, a balancing gate reading below 36 mV, or splice voltages above 0.1 V (0.5 V at a busbar splice).",[["h","Open or corroded tap of sense lead M at the cell connector"],["m","Fault inside the traction battery (Ford Fusion PHEV TSB 23-2039)"],["m","High resistance at a splice or busbar joint in the sense path"],["l","Defective cell monitoring board input"]],["MIL with hybrid or EV system warning","Vehicle may not start or go into ready mode","Charging blocked or limited"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0B78":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense M Circuit Range/Performance","With P0B78 the controller doubts the reading of channel M rather than its wiring. GM's 2019 hybrid powertrain processor watches for a frozen value: whenever the average of all cells moves by more than 0.022 V, cell M must follow by at least 0.005 V in the same direction, and 35 failures in 40 samples at 100 ms set a Type A fault.",[["h","Measuring channel M stuck at a fixed value"],["m","Battery controller software needing an update"],["l","Block 13 voltage drifting from the pack on two-mode NiMH packs"]],["MIL on, often without noticeable driving change","Reduced electric assist or regeneration"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B79":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense M Circuit Low","P0B79 means that the voltage reported for sense M is too low to be real. GM's lithium-ion battery controllers of 2016 and 2019 set it at 0.2 V or less, held for 1.4 s inside a 2 s window, Type A, and only while no open sense line fault is active, so a broken lead is reported by P0B77 first.",[["h","Sense lead M shorted to ground or a lower cell potential"],["m","Failed measuring input on the monitoring board"],["l","Cell or NiMH block on channel M actually collapsed"]],["MIL and service hybrid or EV system message","Electric drive or assist disabled","Engine runs continuously on hybrids"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B7A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense M Circuit High","A reading on channel M above what any healthy cell can deliver produces P0B7A. For lithium-ion packs GM sets the line at 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s in a 2 s window and treats it as Type A, illuminating the MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Sense lead M in contact with a higher cell potential"],["m","Measuring input of channel M reading high"],["l","Real overvoltage of the cell after a balancing fault"]],["MIL on from the first trip (GM lithium-ion, Type A)","Charging stopped or limited","Reduced electric power"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B7B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense M Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B7B is reserved for a sense M signal that flickers: short dropouts or jumps while the circuit is otherwise intact. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Loose crimp or connector at the channel M tap"],["m","Chafed wire in the cell sense harness"],["l","Cracked solder joint on the monitoring board"]],["Hybrid warning appearing and clearing again","Brief drops in electric power"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B7C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense N Circuit","Voltage sense N, the fourteenth channel, reports a broken or high-resistance sense lead with P0B7C. It is the last code in Ford's TSB 23-2039: 2019-2020 Fusion plug-in hybrids that fail to start with any open-circuit code from P0B3B up to P0B7C get a replacement traction battery; the bulletin names no channel beyond N.",[["h","Internal traction battery fault (Ford Fusion PHEV 2019-2020)"],["m","Sense lead N broken or with excessive resistance"],["l","Fault on the cell monitoring board"]],["No start or vehicle does not become ready","MIL and stop-safely or service message","Plug-in charging unavailable"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0B7D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense N Circuit Range/Performance","P0B7D concerns a channel N value that stays inside its limits yet does not behave like a real cell. GM's 2016 tables show how the same check is tuned per vehicle: the Chevrolet Volt fails when cell N does not move with the average for 7 of 8 seconds, while the Cadillac ELR counts 20 of 40 samples at 200 ms once the average shifts by more than 0.006 V; both are Type A.",[["h","Channel N value not tracking the other cells"],["m","Outdated battery controller calibration"],["l","Block 14 or 15 voltage mismatch on two-mode NiMH packs"]],["MIL with hybrid service message","Shorter electric range or less assist"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B7E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense N Circuit Low","P0B7E sets when channel N reads below the lower plausibility limit. GM's 2016 program with a separate battery sense module uses less than 0.20 V and needs 14 failures in 18 samples, about 1.8 s at 100 ms each, before a Type A fault; the open-circuit test must not be failing at the same time.",[["h","Sense lead N shorted to a lower potential"],["m","Damaged input stage in the sense module"],["l","Deeply discharged or failed cell on channel N"]],["MIL from the first trip on lithium-ion GM systems","Hybrid system limits power","Combustion engine does not shut off at standstill"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B7F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense N Circuit High","Channel N reporting more voltage than a cell can hold leads to P0B7F. GM uses three thresholds depending on the system: 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s in 2 s on most lithium-ion battery controllers, above 4.90 V in 14 of 18 samples on the 2016 program with a separate sense module, both Type A, and above 4.65 V in 7 of 10 samples on the BAS Plus mild hybrid, Type B.",[["h","Sense lead N touching a higher potential"],["m","Drifted measuring input in the battery controller"],["l","Real overvoltage of the cell on channel N"]],["MIL and warning in the instrument cluster","Regenerative braking and charging limited"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B80":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense N Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B80 describes an unsteady channel N signal: the voltage drops out or jumps for moments, then looks normal again. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Contact at the N tap opening under vibration"],["m","Fretting corrosion in the sense harness connector"],["l","Solder joint cracking with temperature cycles"]],["Intermittent hybrid warning","Fault not present after restart","Occasional loss of electric power"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B81":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense O Circuit","P0B81 stands for an open sense lead on channel O, the fifteenth cell tap. GM has used it from the start: the 2011 Volt battery tables already list P0B81 among the open sense line codes and detect, among other cases, a busbar connection voltage above 0.7 V.",[["h","Loose or high-resistance busbar joint at the O tap"],["m","Broken sense wire O in the battery harness"],["l","Faulty cell sense module"]],["MIL and propulsion system warning","Reduced power or no electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,6500]],"c"],"P0B82":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense O Circuit Range/Performance","P0B82 is a plausibility verdict on channel O. GM's 2016 program with a battery sense module asks cell O to trend at least 0.002 V in the direction of the average whenever the average moves by more than 0.022 V; 35 failed evaluations out of 40 set a Type A code.",[["h","Channel O reading frozen in the monitoring electronics"],["m","Battery controller software fault"],["l","Rising resistance in the O sense tap distorting the reading"]],["MIL with otherwise normal driving","State of charge display jumps or is inaccurate"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B83":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense O Circuit Low","P0B83 reports that channel O reads implausibly low. It is not the same as an open lead: GM's lithium-ion controllers run this check only while no open sense line fault is present, so a broken wire shows as P0B81 instead.",[["h","Short in the O sense path towards ground or a lower cell"],["m","Monitoring input reading low"],["l","Dead cell or NiMH block 15"]],["MIL and hybrid warning lamp","Electric operation switched off"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B84":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense O Circuit High","P0B84 is stored when channel O sits above the upper limit of a credible cell voltage. For GM lithium-ion packs that limit is 4.8 V, held 1.4 s in a 2 s window, Type A; the 2016 program with a separate sense module tolerates up to 4.90 V and counts 14 failures in 18 samples, also Type A.",[["h","Sense lead O shorted to a higher cell tap"],["m","Monitoring input of channel O reading too high"],["l","Cell overcharged because balancing failed"]],["MIL and battery warning","Charging ends early","Less electric assist"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B85":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense O Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B85 would mark a channel O signal that is present but unsteady, with spikes or brief gaps. No manufacturer documents this code publicly; even GM, whose public tables cover channel O in detail from 2011 onward, defines only the open, rationality, low and high tests for it.",[["h","Loose busbar screw changing contact resistance at tap O"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the sense harness connector"],["l","Cracked joint on the monitoring board"]],["Warning that comes and goes","Short-term power reduction during driving"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B86":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense P Circuit","P0B86 belongs to channel P, the sixteenth sense lead, and indicates that this lead has lost its connection to the cell. Comparing GM's tables shows how the timing evolved: the 2016 Volt battery controller decides within 0.6 s, the 2019 version waits 0.8 s in a 1.6 s window; both use the 1.3 V adjacent-cell, 36 mV balancing gate and splice criteria and are Type A.",[["h","Sense lead P open at the cell tap or connector"],["m","Excessive resistance in the sense line (corrosion, loose screw)"],["l","Defective input of the sense module"]],["MIL and hybrid system fault message","Vehicle runs only on the engine or with reduced power"],["shop_only",[150,6500]],"c"],"P0B87":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense P Circuit Range/Performance","P0B87 says channel P is delivering numbers that do not fit the rest of the battery. On GM's BAS Plus mild hybrid the cell under test must move at least 50 percent as far as the average cell voltage; if not, 20 failures in 40 samples set the code as Type B, i.e.",[["h","Measuring electronics for channel P out of tolerance"],["m","Software or calibration of the battery controller"],["l","NiMH block 16 or 17 out of balance with the pack"]],["MIL after two trips on Type B systems","Engine start-stop or electric assist less frequent"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B88":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense P Circuit Low","A too-low value on sense P, the sixteenth channel, is what P0B88 records. GM's BAS Plus mild hybrid sets it at a cell sense line below 1.4 V for 7 of 10 samples, sampled every 200 ms and only with the 12 V battery above 8.8 V; it rates the fault Type B.",[["h","Lead P shorted to a lower potential or chassis"],["m","Defective input in the battery interface module"],["l","Weak cell or NiMH block 16"]],["MIL and reduced propulsion power","Auto stop no longer active"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B89":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense P Circuit High","P0B89 covers the opposite case to P0B88: the voltage reported on channel P is above the plausible range. GM's 2012 two-mode hybrids assign P to NiMH block 16 and set the code when the block exceeds 23 V in 15 of 20 samples at 100 ms, Type B, provided no battery controller fault P0A1F is active.",[["h","Lead P in contact with a higher cell tap"],["m","Input fault in the cell monitoring electronics"],["l","Genuine overvoltage of the cell or block"]],["Charging restricted","MIL and hybrid warning"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B8A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense P Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B8A is the erratic-signal member of the channel P group. There is no public manufacturer documentation for it: GM describes open, rationality, low and high diagnostics for sense P in its 2016 and 2019 tables, but none for an intermittent signal, and the Ford bulletins stop at channel N.",[["h","Fatigued wire at a bend of the sense harness"],["m","Connector losing contact as the pack warms up"],["l","Sporadic fault in the cell monitoring chip"]],["Warning lamp that goes out again","Occasional engine start without demand on hybrids"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B8B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Q Circuit","P0B8B marks an open circuit on voltage sense Q, the seventeenth channel. In GM's BAS Plus mild hybrid tables Q is the first channel whose open test is blocked by code P1B37 instead of P1B36, which suggests it sits in the next measuring group of the battery interface module; the criterion stays lines N and N-1 below 1.4 V with a change above 0.5 V in 200 ms, 7 of 10 samples, Type B.",[["h","Sense lead Q open between cell tap and module"],["m","Loose or corroded connector at the second measuring group"],["l","Fault in the battery interface module"]],["MIL and service vehicle message","Electric operation blocked or limited","Fuel consumption rises on hybrids"],["shop_only",[150,6500]],"c"],"P0B8C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Q Circuit Range/Performance","P0B8C is set when channel Q reports a voltage that fails the plausibility test against the other cells or the pack. On GM's 2012 two-mode trucks Q is NiMH block 17; the battery pack control module takes block 17 and block 18, multiplies each by twenty and compares with the measured pack voltage, failing beyond 70 V in 160 of 170 samples, Type B.",[["h","Monitoring electronics misreading channel Q"],["m","Outdated battery controller software"],["l","Block 17 or 18 voltage deviating on two-mode NiMH packs"]],["MIL with little change in driving","Usable battery capacity reduced"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B8D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Q Circuit Low","P0B8D reports a voltage on sense Q below the lowest credible cell value. GM's lithium-ion battery controllers in the 2016 and 2019 tables use 0.2 V or less for 1.4 s within 2 s, Type A, and the 2016 program with a separate sense module 0.20 V with 14 of 18 samples.",[["h","Short in the Q sense path to a lower potential"],["m","Sense module input reading low"],["l","Cell or block 17 deeply discharged"]],["MIL and reduced power mode","Engine runs more often on hybrids"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B8E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Q Circuit High","Channel Q reporting an implausibly high voltage sets P0B8E. On the 2012 GM two-mode hybrids, where Q is NiMH block 17, the limit is 23 V for 15 of 20 samples, Type B, checked only while the 12 V system lies between 9 and 18 V.",[["h","Damaged harness letting lead Q touch the next tap"],["m","Measuring input of channel Q reading high"],["l","Real cell overvoltage after a balancing failure"]],["Regeneration and charging limited","MIL and battery system warning"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B8F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Q Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B8F closes the channel Q group and stands for a sense Q signal that is erratic rather than absent or out of range. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Cracked crimp at the channel Q tap"],["m","Damaged flexible circuit between cells and monitoring board"],["l","Cold solder joint at the measuring chip"]],["Sporadic hybrid or EV warning","Power briefly reduced, normal after restart"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B90":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense R Circuit","P0B90 is stored when the battery controller concludes that the tap of sense R, the eighteenth cell channel, is open or too resistive. GM's 2019 battery energy control module runs seven cases for it: neighbouring cells 1.3 V or more apart, a reading under 36 mV while the balancing gate is on, or splice voltages above 0.1 V (0.5 V at a busbar splice).",[["h","Loose, corroded or broken tap of sense lead R"],["m","Excessive resistance at a splice or busbar joint feeding channel R"],["l","Faulty measuring or balancing input on the cell monitoring board"]],["MIL with service hybrid or EV system warning","Reduced propulsion power or no ready mode","Charging interrupted or refused"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0B91":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense R Circuit Range/Performance","Here the wiring of sense R is intact, but its value does not behave like a real cell. GM's 2019 hybrid powertrain processor demands that cell R moves at least 0.005 V in the same direction whenever the cell average shifts by more than 0.022 V; 35 failures out of 40 samples at 100 ms give a Type A fault.",[["h","Measuring channel R reporting a stuck value"],["m","Outdated battery controller or hybrid processor software"],["l","Block 18 or 19 voltage drifting on two-mode NiMH packs"]],["MIL on, vehicle often drives normally","Less electric assist or regenerative braking"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B92":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense R Circuit Low","P0B92 stands for an implausibly low reading on sense R. In GM's lithium-ion battery controllers of 2016 and 2019 the limit is 0.2 V or less for 1.4 s inside a 2 s window, Type A; the test is suspended while an open sense line fault is active, so a broken lead appears as P0B90 rather than P0B92.",[["h","Sense lead R chafed through and touching ground or a lower cell"],["m","Failed input for channel R on the cell monitoring board"],["l","Cell or block on channel R genuinely collapsed"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV system message","Propulsion limited, possibly no restart"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B93":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense R Circuit High","A reading on sense R above anything a healthy cell could produce sets P0B93. GM's lithium-ion battery controllers of 2016 and 2019 use 4.8 V or more, held 1.4 s within 2 s, as a Type A fault.",[["h","Sense lead R in contact with a higher cell potential"],["m","Damaged measuring input for channel R"],["l","Cell on channel R actually overcharged"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Charging or regeneration cut back","Reduced power mode"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B94":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense R Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","No manufacturer documents P0B94 publicly. The code is reserved for a sense R signal that drops out or jumps for short moments instead of failing steadily.",[["h","Crimp or terminal of lead R opening under vibration"],["m","Hairline crack in the flexible circuit carrying channel R"],["l","Moisture or contamination in the monitoring board connector"]],["Warning appears and disappears between trips","Short power reductions, normal after restart"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B95":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense S Circuit","P0B95 reports that the sense S lead, channel nineteen, no longer connects its cell reliably to the monitoring electronics. GM programmes with a separate battery sensor module (16OBDG02) measure this directly: after each normal reading the balancing gates switch in a test pattern, current flows through the sense wires and the voltage drop is turned into a line resistance.",[["h","High resistance in sense lead S from corrosion or a loose terminal"],["m","Sense lead S broken at the cell connector"],["l","Defective input of the battery sensor module for channel S"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV warning","Vehicle may refuse ready mode","Cell balancing no longer effective"],["shop_only",[150,6500]],"c"],"P0B96":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense S Circuit Range/Performance","P0B96 is a plausibility code: the voltage of sense S looks electrically valid but fails to track the rest of the pack. GM's eAssist mild hybrid requires cell S to move at least half as much as the other cells once the average changes by more than 0.045 V, judged over 20 of 40 samples at 200 ms, Type B.",[["h","Measuring channel S frozen or lagging behind the other cells"],["m","Battery controller calibration requiring a software update"],["l","Block 19 or 20 out of step with the pack on two-mode NiMH batteries"]],["MIL with little or no change in driving","Electric assist reduced at times"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B97":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense S Circuit Low","With P0B97 the controller sees sense S pulled close to zero. In the 2012 two-mode trucks, where S is the nickel-metal hydride block 19, the battery pack control module only sets it when block 19 and block 20 are both below 2 V in 15 of 20 samples, Type B, with the 12 V system between 9 and 18 V.",[["h","Sense lead S shorted to ground or to the next lower cell"],["m","Defective channel S input on the monitoring board"],["l","Cell or NiMH block on channel S actually discharged"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV warning message","Restricted power, engine may run more often on hybrids"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B98":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense S Circuit High","P0B98 marks a sense S value that is too high to be a cell voltage. The thresholds vary with the battery chemistry and controller: GM's 2016 Volt and 2019 battery controllers flag 4.8 V or more after 1.4 s in 2 s (Type A), the 16OBDG02 programme 4.90 V in 14 of 18 samples (Type A), and the eAssist mild hybrid more than 4.65 V in 7 of 10 samples with the 12 V supply above 8.8 V (Type B).",[["h","Sense lead S shorted to a higher cell or busbar"],["m","Measuring chip reading channel S incorrectly"],["l","Real overvoltage of the cell or block on channel S"]],["MIL and service hybrid system message","Charging and regeneration limited"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B99":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense S Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B99 is the erratic variant within the sense S group. A public manufacturer description does not exist.",[["h","Fretting corrosion on the terminal of sense lead S"],["m","Cracked solder joint for channel S on the monitoring board"],["l","Harness chafing that only shorts under movement"]],["Sporadic hybrid warning without a pattern","Code often stored as history, hard to reproduce"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B9A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense T Circuit","P0B9A is the open-circuit code for sense T, the twentieth cell channel. GM uses it only on lithium-ion packs.",[["h","Open sense lead T at the cell tab or connector"],["m","Poor busbar or splice joint in the channel T path"],["l","Monitoring board input for channel T failed"]],["MIL, hybrid or EV system warning","Ready mode or charging blocked"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0B9B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense T Circuit Range/Performance","On the 2012 two-mode trucks sense T is block 20, the last of their twenty nickel-metal hydride blocks, and P0B9B shows how GM handles the missing neighbour: block 20 alone is multiplied by twenty and compared with the pack voltage, and a gap above 70 V in 160 of 170 samples sets the code as Type B. All other channels there use a pair of blocks.",[["h","Channel T measurement stuck or sluggish"],["m","Block 20 voltage diverging from pack voltage on two-mode NiMH packs"],["l","Faulty pack voltage reference used for the comparison"]],["MIL, drivability usually unchanged","Hybrid assist or EV range slightly reduced"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B9C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense T Circuit Low","P0B9C signals that sense T reads too low. On the 2012 two-mode trucks this is block 20, and because no block 21 exists the low test there looks at block 20 alone: below 2 V in 15 of 20 samples, Type B, while channels R and S each need their upper neighbour to read low as well.",[["h","Sense lead T shorted to ground or a lower potential"],["m","Weak or discharged NiMH block 20 on two-mode packs"],["l","Failed measuring input for channel T"]],["MIL and hybrid warning","Reduced power, engine may not shut off on hybrids"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B9D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense T Circuit High","If sense T reports more voltage than its cell can hold, the battery controller stores P0B9D. GM documents it on several systems.",[["h","Sense lead T shorted to a higher cell or busbar"],["m","Measuring input for channel T drifting high"],["l","Cell or block T genuinely overcharged"]],["MIL and service hybrid or EV system message","Charging power reduced"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0B9E":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense T Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0B9E would describe a sense T signal that flickers. It lacks any public manufacturer documentation.",[["h","Loose or spread terminal in the channel T connector"],["m","Connection that opens with temperature changes"],["l","Electrical interference coupling into sense lead T"]],["Intermittent warning lamp","Occasional brief loss of electric drive"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0B9F":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense U Circuit","P0B9F covers an open or high-resistance sense U lead, channel twenty-one. From U onward the 2012 two-mode trucks drop out, as their pack has only twenty blocks, so this code is a lithium-ion matter at GM.",[["h","Sense lead U interrupted or detached from its cell"],["m","Corroded terminal raising the resistance of lead U"],["l","Fault in the balancing circuit used for the channel U test"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV system warning","No start or ready mode on some vehicles","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[150,6500]],"c"],"P0BA0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense U Circuit Range/Performance","P0BA0 tells the technician that channel U gives a number, but the number does not move with the pack. GM checks this rationality only on lithium-ion systems, since the two-mode NiMH trucks end at block 20.",[["h","Measuring channel U holding a fixed value"],["m","Software or calibration issue in the battery controller"],["l","Cell U behaving differently from its neighbours due to ageing"]],["MIL, often without driver-noticeable change","Electric range may be reduced"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BA1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense U Circuit Low","P0BA1 is stored when sense U reads close to zero. GM's lithium-ion controllers of the 2016 Volt and ELR and of 2019 treat 0.2 V or less over 1.4 s in a 2 s window as a Type A fault, provided no open sense line, lost slave communication or balancing fault is already active.",[["h","Sense lead U shorted towards ground or a lower cell"],["m","Dead measuring input for channel U"],["l","Cell U internally shorted or deeply discharged"]],["MIL and EV or hybrid system warning","Propulsion power limited"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BA2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense U Circuit High","P0BA2 flags an excessive voltage on channel U. GM's eAssist mild hybrid uses the lowest threshold of the documented systems: above 4.65 V in 7 of 10 samples with the 12 V supply above 8.8 V, Type B.",[["h","Sense lead U in contact with a higher potential"],["m","Measuring IC misreading channel U"],["l","Cell U actually overcharged"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV warning","Charging stopped early"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BA3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense U Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Within the sense U group, P0BA3 is the slot for a signal that is present but unsteady. No manufacturer documents it publicly; GM, the only maker whose tables cover channel U in detail, uses P0B9F to P0BA2 and nothing more.",[["h","Fatigued crimp on sense lead U"],["m","Loose fastener at the busbar tap of channel U"],["l","Vibration damage to the flexible printed circuit"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Brief reduced-power events"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BA4":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense V Circuit","P0BA4 reports an open or poorly connected sense V lead, the twenty-second channel and the last before W begins at P0BA9. GM's 2019 battery energy control module recognises it through seven cases, including 1.3 V between neighbouring cells, less than 36 mV with the balancing gate on and splice voltages above 0.1 or 0.5 V, with 0.8 s of failure in 1.6 s, Type A.",[["h","Broken or unplugged sense lead V"],["m","High-resistance splice or busbar joint on channel V"],["l","Cell monitoring board fault on channel V"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV system warning","Vehicle may not become ready to drive","Charging blocked"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0BA5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense V Circuit Range/Performance","P0BA5 describes a sense V value that stays within limits but fails a rationality check against the other cells. GM's 16OBDG02 programme demands a trend of at least 0.002 V in the same direction as the average whenever that average moves more than 0.022 V, with 35 failures in 40 evaluations giving Type A.",[["h","Channel V reading stuck while other cells move"],["m","Battery controller firmware needing reprogramming"],["l","Aged cell V responding differently to load"]],["MIL, little change in driving","Reduced regeneration or assist possible"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BA6":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense V Circuit Low","A sense V reading that collapses towards zero produces P0BA6. The threshold depends on the system: GM's eAssist mild hybrid uses under 1.4 V in 7 of 10 samples with at least 8.8 V on the 12 V side, Type B, while GM's lithium-ion battery controllers of 2016 and 2019 go down to 0.2 V or less for 1.4 s within 2 s, Type A.",[["h","Sense lead V shorted to ground or a lower cell"],["m","Monitoring board input for channel V failed"],["l","Cell V genuinely collapsed"]],["MIL and service hybrid or EV system message","Power limited, restart may be refused"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BA7":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense V Circuit High","P0BA7 is the high-voltage plausibility code for channel V. The battery controller compares the measured value against a ceiling no healthy cell should reach: 4.8 V for 1.4 s within 2 s in GM's 2016 Volt and ELR and in the 2019 battery energy control module (Type A), 4.90 V in 14 of 18 samples of 100 ms in the 16OBDG02 programme (Type A), and 4.65 V in 7 of 10 samples in the eAssist mild hybrid (Type B).",[["h","Sense lead V touching a higher cell tab or busbar"],["m","Measuring chip with faulty reference reading channel V high"],["l","Cell V really overcharged"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV warning","Charging and regeneration reduced"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BA8":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense V Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BA8 completes the five codes of channel V and is meant for an unstable rather than a failed sense V signal. There is no public manufacturer documentation for it.",[["h","Contact of lead V lost intermittently under vibration"],["m","Joint on channel V opening with thermal cycling"],["l","Corroded connector pin for channel V"]],["Occasional hybrid or EV warning","Fault not reproducible during short workshop test"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BA9":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense W Circuit","P0BA9 opens the W group, the twenty-third cell voltage channel, and flags a sense lead that is open or badly connected. GM's 2019 battery energy control module checks seven cases, ranging from a reading under 36 mV while the balancing gate of that cell is switched on to at least 1.3 V between neighbouring cells and splice voltages above 0.1 V or 0.5 V at busbar joints; 0.8 s of failure within 1.6 s sets it as Type A.",[["h","Broken, chafed or unplugged sense lead W"],["m","High-resistance busbar or splice joint at channel W"],["l","Failed input on the cell monitoring board for W"]],["MIL with hybrid or EV system warning","Reduced propulsion power or no ready mode","Charging interrupted or refused"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0BAA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense W Circuit Range/Performance","Here the W reading is not missing but implausible: it fails to follow the rest of the pack. GM's 2019 hybrid powertrain control processor 2 watches for the average cell voltage to move more than 0.022 V between samples and then expects channel W to trend at least 0.005 V in the same direction; 35 failures in 40 samples at 100 ms give a Type A fault.",[["h","Measuring input for W frozen at a fixed value"],["m","Battery controller software needing an update"],["l","Cell W reacting differently to load because of ageing"]],["Warning lamp with little change in driving","Possibly limited regeneration or electric assist"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BAB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense W Circuit Low","A W channel reading at or near zero sets P0BAB. How low is too low depends on the battery: GM's lithium-ion modules of 2016 and 2019 react to 0.2 V or less held for 1.4 s within a 2 s window, Type A, and the 16OBDG02 programme to under 0.20 V in 14 of 18 samples at 100 ms.",[["h","Sense lead W shorted to a lower cell potential"],["m","Measuring input for W on the monitoring board failed"],["l","Cell W deeply discharged or internally shorted"]],["Hybrid or EV warning and MIL","Reduced power output","Charging limited or blocked"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BAC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense W Circuit High","P0BAC is the upper counterpart to P0BAB: channel W reports more voltage than a single cell can plausibly hold. GM sets the limit at 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s within 2 s in the 2011 and 2016 Volt battery modules, which makes it a one-trip fault.",[["h","Sense lead W in contact with a higher cell potential"],["m","Monitoring board input for W reading too high"],["l","Cell W actually overcharged after a balancing fault"]],["MIL and battery system warning","Charging and regeneration stopped","Possible reduced power mode"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BAD":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense W Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BAD is reserved for a W signal that drops out or jumps for short moments instead of failing outright. No manufacturer documents the code publicly.",[["h","Loose or fretting connector pin on lead W"],["m","Crimp on lead W opening with temperature changes"],["l","Cracked solder joint on the monitoring board input W"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Occasional short power reduction"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BAE":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense X Circuit","Channel X, number twenty-four, starts with P0BAE, the open-lead code. The first-generation Volt of 2011 already detected it through several cases: two neighbouring readings more than 1 V apart, a busbar sense voltage above 0.7 V combined with an implausible cell voltage, or adjacent cells differing by more than 0.5 V on a shared supply line.",[["h","Sense lead X broken or disconnected"],["m","Loose busbar fastener at the X sensing point"],["l","Sense harness X damaged during earlier battery work"]],["MIL and service message for the high-voltage system","Vehicle may stay out of ready mode","Charging stops early"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0BAF":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense X Circuit Range/Performance","P0BAF covers a sense X signal that is electrically present yet does not behave like a real cell. For model year 2016 GM documents two versions in the same booklet.",[["h","Sense X value stuck while the pack voltage changes"],["m","Calibration of the battery controller requiring reprogramming"],["l","Cell X with deviating internal resistance"]],["MIL without obvious driving change","Reduced electric range in some cases"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BB0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense X Circuit Low","When channel X reads too low, P0BB0 is stored. In GM's eAssist mild hybrid the battery interface module sets it below 1.4 V in seven of ten samples at 200 ms, provided the 12 V system is above 8.8 V and the group fault P1B38 is not active, as a two-trip Type B fault.",[["h","Short between sense lead X and a lower potential"],["m","Defective measuring channel X in the monitoring electronics"],["l","Cell X collapsed or internally shorted"]],["Hybrid system warning and MIL","Electric assist or propulsion reduced","Charging not possible"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BB1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense X Circuit High","P0BB1 means the battery controller sees too much voltage on sense X. In GM's 2019 battery energy control module the threshold is 4.8 V or more, held for 1.4 s within a 2 s window, and the code is Type A, so the MIL comes on in the first trip.",[["h","Sense lead X shorted to the next higher cell"],["m","Measuring input X drifting high"],["l","Cell X genuinely over voltage"]],["MIL in the first trip","Charging and regenerative braking disabled"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BB2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense X Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","Of the five X codes, P0BB2 is the only one without any public manufacturer description. It is intended for a sense X signal that flickers, drops out briefly or jumps between values.",[["h","Contact on lead X opening under vibration"],["m","Moisture or corrosion in the X sense connector"],["l","Harness X chafing against the module housing"]],["Intermittent warning lamp","Sporadic hybrid system message"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BB3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Y Circuit","P0BB3 reports that the sense lead of channel Y, the twenty-fifth cell group, is no longer properly connected. GM's 16OBDG02 programme does not wait for odd voltages but calculates the resistance of the sense line and fails it above 30.00 in seven of nine samples, which is a one-trip Type A fault; the unit is missing in the table.",[["h","Open circuit in sense lead Y"],["m","Corroded or loose terminal raising line resistance at Y"],["l","Cell monitoring slave board fault on input Y"]],["MIL and high-voltage system warning","Vehicle may not start in electric mode","Charging aborted"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0BB4":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Y Circuit Range/Performance","P0BB4 is a plausibility code: the controller compares how the Y reading changes over time with the behaviour of all other cells. In the 16OBDG02 programme a sample counts as failed when the pack average moves by more than 0.022 V but Y has not trended at least 0.002 V the same way; 35 such results in 40 evaluations set a one-trip Type A fault.",[["h","Analogue input Y frozen at one value"],["m","Battery controller software fault"],["l","Weak cell Y with sluggish voltage response"]],["MIL, vehicle still drivable","Battery power occasionally limited"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BB5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Y Circuit Low","P0BB5 stands for a sense Y voltage below the plausible minimum. GM's 2016 Volt module and the 2019 battery energy control module both use 0.2 V or less for 1.4 s inside a 2 s window and flag it as Type A.",[["h","Pinched sense wire Y shorting to a lower potential"],["m","Failed measuring channel Y"],["l","Cell Y severely discharged"]],["Hybrid or EV warning with MIL","Noticeably reduced power"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BB6":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Y Circuit High","The battery controller logs P0BB6 when the Y channel climbs above a level no healthy cell reaches. GM's first Volt of 2011 put that limit at 4.8 V or more, 1.4 s within 2 s, with the MIL after one trip.",[["h","Sense input Y bridged to the next cell potential"],["m","Defective input Y on the monitoring board"],["l","Overcharged cell Y after a balancing problem"]],["MIL and charging stopped","Regeneration disabled"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BB7":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Y Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BB7 would describe an unstable sense Y signal, one that is mostly correct but drops out or jumps from time to time. No manufacturer publishes a monitor for it.",[["h","Intermittent contact in the Y sense connector"],["m","Crimp or weld on lead Y opening when hot"],["l","Hairline crack in a solder joint of input Y"]],["Warning lamp appears now and then","Short-lived power limitation"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BB8":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Z Circuit","P0BB8 is the open-circuit code of channel Z, the twenty-sixth and last cell channel with a generic P0 code. GM packs with more channels continue with manufacturer codes, for example P1B28 for the circuit of AA in the 2019 tables.",[["h","Sense lead Z interrupted"],["m","Excessive resistance at the splice or busbar of Z"],["l","Monitoring electronics input Z defective"]],["MIL and stop or service warning for the drive system","Ready mode may be refused","No charging"],["shop_only",[150,7000]],"c"],"P0BB9":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Z Circuit Range/Performance","With P0BB9 the controller says that sense Z does not track the pack: the value is within limits, but it stays still or moves the wrong way while the other cells change. GM's 2019 hybrid powertrain control processor 2 enables the comparison once the system voltage is above 10.20 V and the average cell voltage shifts by more than 0.022 V, then demands a trend of at least 0.005 V on Z; 35 failures in 40 samples are Type A.",[["h","Z reading static despite changing pack voltage"],["m","Monitoring board or controller calibration fault"],["l","Cell Z with abnormal charge and discharge behaviour"]],["MIL, driving largely unaffected","Possibly lower assist or regeneration"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BBA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Z Circuit Low","P0BBA is stored when the voltage measured on channel Z falls below the lower limit. In the 16OBDG02 programme the hybrid processor checks Z every 100 ms and fails it below 0.20 V in 14 of 18 samples, a Type A fault, but only with a system voltage above 10.20 V and no open-circuit fault on the cell.",[["h","Short in the Z sense wiring or connector"],["m","Failed channel Z on the monitoring board"],["l","Voltage collapse of cell Z"]],["MIL and hybrid or EV warning","Limited power and charging"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BBB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Z Circuit High","If the Z channel shows a cell voltage above the upper limit, the controller sets P0BBB. The 2016 Volt battery module uses 4.8 V or more for 1.4 s in a 2 s window, Type A, and checks first that the cell monitoring slave is communicating and no open sense line is reported.",[["h","Sense lead Z shorted towards the higher neighbour cell"],["m","Faulty measuring input Z"],["l","Cell Z overcharged"]],["MIL and charging stopped","Reduced regeneration"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0BBC":["Hybrid/EV Battery Voltage Sense Z Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BBC closes the generic block of 26 cell voltage channels from A to Z; after it J2012 moves on to pack-level codes such as P0BBD and P0BBE. The code is meant for a Z signal that is unstable rather than absent.",[["h","Intermittent contact on sense lead Z"],["m","Joint at channel Z opening with heat cycling"],["l","Corrosion in the Z sense connector"]],["Warning lamp appearing irregularly","Occasional brief loss of electric drive power"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0BBD":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Variation Exceeded Limit","P0BBD means the spread between cells or blocks of the pack has grown beyond what the controller accepts. GM used it on the two-mode hybrids, where a difference of more than 1.5 V between adjacent block voltages in three of three samples at 1 s, with battery current above 0.2 A, set a Type B fault labelled as end-of-life deviation.",[["h","Cells or modules with increased self-discharge or capacity loss"],["m","Controller software or cell capacity adaptation needing an update"],["m","Aged battery pack at end of life"],["l","Cell balancing unable to equalise the pack"]],["MIL or electrical system warning","Reduced propulsion power and capped state of charge","Reduced electric range"],["shop_only",[100,15000]],"c"],"P0BBE":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Voltage Variation","P0BBE watches the gap between the highest and the lowest cell voltage in the pack. GM's first-generation Volt sets it when that difference exceeds 0.3 V in 50 of 245 samples taken every 100 ms, provided the cell voltage rationality fault is not active; it is a one-trip Type A fault.",[["h","Cells drifted apart beyond what balancing can correct"],["m","Battery pack control module software needing an update"],["m","Defective or aged high-voltage battery pack"],["l","Faulty cell voltage measurement simulating a spread"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Reduced electric range or propulsion","Vehicle may not start"],["shop_only",[100,15000]],"c"],"P0BBF":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan Supply Voltage Circuit/Open","Air-cooled hybrid packs draw cooling air through a 12 V blower that is fed through a fuse and often a relay; the controller watches the voltage reaching the fan, and Toyota even uses that measured supply voltage to correct the fan duty. P0BBF means this feed is open or missing while the fan should be powered.",[["h","Blown fuse in the battery fan feed"],["m","Fan relay contacts not closing or relay coil open"],["m","Open supply wire or loose terminal at the fan connector"]],["Hybrid system warning or MIL on","Battery fan silent when it should run","Reduced electric assist in hot weather or under load"],["medium",[10,400]],"c"],"P0BC0":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan Supply Voltage Circuit Low","P0BC0 reports that the voltage feeding the battery cooling fan is lower than expected while the fan is switched on. A short to ground downstream of the fuse, a corroded connector or a relay with burnt contacts drops the voltage, so the fan turns slowly or not at all.",[["m","Fan supply wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Burnt fan relay contacts or corroded terminals causing voltage drop"],["m","Weak 12 V auxiliary battery or poor ground"],["l","Seizing fan motor pulling excessive current"]],["Hybrid system warning or MIL on","Battery fan runs slowly or noticeably weaker","Reduced electric assist when the pack is warm"],["medium",[20,450]],"c"],"P0BC1":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan Supply Voltage Circuit High","P0BC1 flags voltage on the battery fan feed when there should be none, or more than expected. GM documents it for the battery pack control module of its Two-Mode hybrid SUVs and pickups (Tahoe, Yukon, Escalade, Silverado, Sierra; 2012 and 2013 tables) as a welded fan relay: with the fan commanded off, a fan monitor voltage of at least 0.9 V in 10 of 20 samples at 100 ms sets it, provided the 12 V system is between 9 and 18 V and no fan circuit code (P0A81) is active.",[["h","Fan relay contacts welded closed"],["m","Fan feed shorted to a constant battery voltage wire"],["l","Relay control output in the module stuck on"]],["MIL on after two trips","Battery fan keeps running after the vehicle is switched off","Discharged 12 V battery after standing"],["easy",[15,250]],"c"],"P0BC2":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor E Circuit","Battery temperature sensor E is the fifth thermistor in the high-voltage pack; GM's Chevrolet Volt bulletin 18-NA-342 lists the E codes as temperature sensor 5 of nine. P0BC2 is the general circuit code, used when the controller detects an electrical fault without classing it as low, high or erratic.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector of sensor E at the sensing board"],["m","Failed thermistor E"],["l","Faulty input on the battery sensing board or module"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL","Reduced power or charging rate","Battery cooling running more than usual"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0BC3":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor E Circuit Range/Performance","P0BC3 is the plausibility check for battery temperature sensor E, GM's sensor 5: its reading is compared with the other pack sensors, which should agree closely. GM's 2016 hybrid table fails it when E is more than 25 °C away from the average of the others in 50 of 67 samples; the 2018 CT6 plug-in table more than 20 °C for 5 s within 6.7 s; the eAssist mild hybrid (13OBDG05C) allows only 8.55 °C, and only after the module has been off for more than six hours.",[["m","Sensor E drifting or poorly seated on its cell group"],["m","Module software setting the code falsely in the cold (GM Volt)"],["l","Real local hot spot from a weak cell group or blocked airflow"]],["MIL on after two trips","Battery too cold or reduced power message","Limited charge and discharge power"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0BC4":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor E Circuit Low","Sensor E is an NTC thermistor with a pull-up resistor, so its voltage falls as it gets hotter; a short to ground therefore looks like an extremely hot cell group. GM's 2016 table sets P0BC4 when the converted value exceeds 160 °C in 8 of 10 samples; the 2011 Volt table and GM's 2019 battery module table use above 87.5 °C for 1.4 s within 2 s; the eAssist mild hybrid flags a sense voltage under 0.3 V, about 90 °C, in 7 of 10 samples.",[["h","Sensor E signal wire shorted to ground inside the pack"],["m","Internally shorted thermistor E"],["l","Moisture or contamination on the sensing board"]],["MIL on after two trips","Implausibly high temperature for sensor E on the scan tool","Reduced power and battery fan or cooling at full speed"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0BC5":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor E Circuit High","P0BC5 reports that battery temperature sensor E delivers a voltage above its valid range. With GM's NTC sensors that reads as extreme cold, pointing to an open wire, a backed-out pin at the sensing board or a cracked thermistor.",[["h","Open sensor E wire or backed-out pin at the sensing board"],["m","Cracked or open thermistor E"],["l","Module software misreading in the cold (GM Volt, reprogramming)"]],["MIL on after two trips","Battery too cold, plug in to warm message","Very limited power or charging"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0BC6":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor E Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BC6 flags erratic behaviour of battery temperature sensor E: the reading jumps far faster than the heavy battery mass could warm or cool. GM documents the method in its 2016 and 2018 hybrid tables (16OBDG02, 18OBDG02).",[["h","Loose terminal or unlatched connector of sensor E"],["m","Cracked or chafed sensor wire breaking contact intermittently"],["l","Sensing board fault affecting several temperature inputs"]],["MIL on after two trips","Sensor E value jumping on the scan tool","Occasional power reduction"],["shop_only",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0BC7":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Sense Circuit/Open","Many battery cooling fans report their actual speed back to the controller as a pulse signal; Toyota patents describe a rotation sensor on the fan motor whose measured speed is compared with the target. P0BC7 means this feedback line for fan 1 is open or carries no usable signal.",[["h","Open feedback wire or loose pin at the fan connector"],["m","Failed speed sensor or electronics inside the fan unit"],["l","Faulty feedback input in the controlling module"]],["Hybrid system warning or MIL on","Reduced electric assist or charging power","Fan audible even though the code reports no feedback"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P0BC8":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Sense Range/Performance","P0BC8 compares the speed fed back by battery cooling fan 1 with what the controller commanded. GM's 2016 and 2018 hybrid tables run two checks.",[["h","Dust-clogged impeller, blocked intake or worn fan bearing"],["m","Fan keeps running while commanded off (stuck drive or relay)"],["m","Fan electronics reporting a wrong speed"]],["MIL on after two trips","Weak airflow or unusual fan noise from the battery intake","Reduced electric assist when the pack warms up"],["medium",[20,500]],"c"],"P0BC9":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Sense Circuit Low","P0BC9 means the speed feedback of battery cooling fan 1 is implausibly low while the fan is running. GM evaluates the feedback as a frequency: in its 2016 and 2018 hybrid tables the code counts a failure when the frequency stays below 8 Hz with the fan enabled at 11 to 89 % duty for more than 8 s and system voltage above 10.2 V; 16 failures in 20 samples at 250 ms set it as Type B, two trips.",[["h","Fan seized, blocked or without power"],["m","Feedback wire shorted to ground or interrupted"],["m","Failed speed sensor inside the fan unit"]],["MIL on after two trips","No airflow from the battery cooling fan","Reduced power when the battery gets warm"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P0BCA":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Sense Circuit High","P0BCA means the speed feedback of battery cooling fan 1 is implausibly high. GM's 2016 and 2018 hybrid tables count a failure when the feedback frequency exceeds 290 Hz with the fan enabled at 11 to 89 % duty for more than 8 s and system voltage above 10.2 V; 16 failures in 20 samples at 250 ms set it as Type B, two trips.",[["m","Electrical noise on the feedback line from poor routing or a bad ground"],["m","Feedback wire shorted to voltage or another signal"],["m","Fan electronics sending an invalid speed signal"]],["MIL on after two trips","Implausible fan speed reading on the scan tool","Possible power reduction"],["medium",[40,500]],"c"],"P0BCB":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Cooling Fan 1 Sense Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BCB flags a speed feedback from battery cooling fan 1 that drops out or jumps in a way no rotating fan can, typically a loose contact in the feedback line. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM's 2016 and 2018 hybrid tables, which watch the same pulse feedback, list only performance, low and high (P0BC8, P0BC9, P0BCA).",[["h","Loose or corroded terminal in the feedback circuit"],["m","Chafed harness near the fan or battery case"],["l","Intermittent fault in the fan's speed sensor"]],["Hybrid system warning or MIL, sometimes coming and going","Fan speed reading jumping on the scan tool","Occasional power reduction"],["medium",[20,450]],"c"],"P0BCC":["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit","In a two-machine hybrid one inverter feeds the traction motor and a second one the generator, the machine that is mainly driven by the engine to charge the battery or start it. Its power switches carry thermistors so the controller can cut current before they overheat.",[["h","Fault of the sensor or its circuit inside the inverter assembly"],["m","Loose or corroded connector at the inverter"],["l","Software fault in the inverter or hybrid control module"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine lamp","Reduced generator output and battery charging","Engine runs more often or hybrid mode limited"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BCD":["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","P0BCD flags a generator inverter temperature sensor A whose signal is electrically valid but does not fit the operating state, for example a value stuck at one temperature or far from the other temperatures after a long soak. Ford documents this exact code: on 2020 Lincoln Aviator 3.0L hybrids built up to 18 July 2020, P0BCD stored in the inverter system controller (SOBDMC) lights the MIL and is caused by software; the fix is reprogramming that module.",[["h","Inverter controller software (Ford SSM 49120, Lincoln Aviator 2020)"],["m","Sensor drifting or stuck at one value"],["l","Uneven inverter coolant flow distorting the comparison"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Often no noticeable change in driving","Hybrid system warning message possible"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BCE":["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","P0BCE means the signal of generator inverter temperature sensor A is below its valid range. Inverter thermistors are usually NTC types with a pull-up resistor, as GM's tables describe for the drive motor inverter sensors: a short to ground or a shorted element pulls the voltage down, which the controller reads as an impossibly hot inverter.",[["h","Shorted sensor or circuit inside the inverter assembly"],["m","Signal shorted to ground in external wiring, where present"],["l","Moisture or coolant inside the inverter housing"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Generator power strongly limited","Battery charge drops, engine runs more"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BCF":["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","P0BCF reports a signal from generator inverter temperature sensor A above its valid range. With the usual NTC thermistor and pull-up, an open circuit, a lost sensor ground or a cracked element drives the voltage up, which reads as an extremely cold inverter.",[["h","Open sensor or circuit inside the inverter assembly"],["m","Loose, corroded or unplugged connector at the inverter"],["l","Lost sensor ground"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced generator output","Implausibly low inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BD0":["Generator Inverter Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BD0 is reserved for a generator inverter temperature sensor A signal that jumps or briefly drops out instead of failing for good. A liquid-cooled inverter changes temperature slowly, so sudden steps point to a contact problem or to interference: a Hitachi patent on inverter systems explains that the small sensor signal can pick up noise from the high-voltage main circuit and has to be filtered statistically.",[["h","Loose or corroded contact at the inverter connector"],["m","Intermittent fault inside the inverter assembly"],["l","Interference from the high-voltage circuit on the sensor signal"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Brief drops in generator or hybrid power","Jumping inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BD1":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit","Which thermistor is called sensor C depends on the maker. In GM's hybrid tables it is the second sensor of the motor A inverter: the phase V sensor in the 2016 to 2019 tables, labelled temperature sensor 2 in the Volt and ELR.",[["h","Internal fault of the sensor inside the power inverter module"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the inverter module"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine lamp","Reduced electric drive power","Possible loss of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BD2":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Range/Performance","P0BD2 checks whether inverter temperature sensor C, GM's second sensor in the motor A inverter, agrees with the other temperatures after a cold soak. More than 20 °C apart sets it: Type B, two trips, on the 2016 Volt; Type A in a 2019 table; Type A on the 2011 Volt and 2016 ELR, compared with coolant and transmission temperature after 6 h off.",[["m","Inverter module software (GM bulletin 18-NA-064, Bolt EV/Ampera-e)"],["m","Sticking thermostat in the power electronics coolant hose (Porsche Cayenne S E-Hybrid)"],["m","Inverter temperature sensor C drifting out of tolerance"],["l","Failing power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, often without drivability change","Propulsion power reduced message (Bolt EV)","Red hybrid warning in the cluster (Porsche)"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BD3":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Low","P0BD3 means the signal of inverter temperature sensor C, the phase V sensor of GM's motor A inverter, is below its valid range. GM uses an NTC thermistor with a pull-up on the sensing board, so a short to ground reads as an impossibly hot inverter.",[["h","Shorted sensor or circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to ground in external wiring, where present"],["l","Moisture or coolant inside the inverter housing"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Drive motor power strongly limited","Reduced regeneration"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BD4":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit High","P0BD4 reports a voltage from inverter temperature sensor C above its valid range. For GM's NTC sensor this is an implausibly cold inverter, typically caused by an open circuit, a lost ground or a cracked element.",[["h","Open sensor or circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Loose, corroded or unplugged connector at the inverter"],["l","Lost sensor ground"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, depending on model after two trips","Reduced electric drive power","Implausibly low inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BD5":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor C Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BD5 is meant for an inverter temperature sensor C signal that jumps or drops out for short moments. Because the heat sink of a liquid-cooled inverter changes temperature smoothly, sudden steps point to a contact fault or to interference; a Hitachi patent explains that the small thermistor signal can pick up noise from the high-voltage main circuit.",[["h","Loose or corroded contact at the inverter module"],["m","Intermittent fault inside the power inverter module"],["l","Interference from the high-voltage circuit on the sensor signal"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Brief power reductions while driving","Jumping inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BD6":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor D Circuit","Sensor D is, in GM's hybrid tables, the second thermistor of the motor B inverter, the counterpart of sensor C in the motor A inverter: phase V in the 2016 to 2019 tables, temperature sensor 2 of inverter B on the Volt. Other makers assign the letter differently.",[["h","Internal fault of the sensor inside the power inverter module"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the inverter module"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning or check engine lamp","Reduced electric drive or generator power","Higher fuel consumption in hybrid operation"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BD7":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor D Circuit Range/Performance","P0BD7 is the plausibility check for inverter temperature sensor D, GM's second sensor in the motor B inverter. After a cold soak its reading is compared with an average vehicle temperature; more than 20 °C apart sets the code, Type B, two trips, on the 2016 Volt and Type A in a 2019 table.",[["m","Inverter temperature sensor D drifting out of tolerance"],["m","Failing power inverter module"],["l","Uneven inverter coolant flow after soak"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, often after two trips","Often no noticeable drivability change","Further inverter temperature codes may be stored"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BD8":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor D Circuit Low","P0BD8 means the signal of inverter temperature sensor D is below its valid range; with an NTC thermistor and pull-up that reads as an extremely hot inverter, usually from a short to ground or an internal sensor fault. GM sets it above 170 °C for 2.5 of 3.5 s, Type B, two trips, on the 2016 Volt, above 159 °C, Type A, in a 2019 table, above 130 °C, Type A, on the 2011 Volt and above 125 °C, two trips, in the 2012 table.",[["h","Shorted sensor or circuit inside the inverter (Jeep Compass PHEV bulletin)"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to ground in external wiring, where present"],["l","Moisture or coolant inside the inverter housing"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Vehicle does not start, Electric Traction System Fault (Jeep)","Electric drive power strongly limited"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BD9":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor D Circuit High","P0BD9 means inverter temperature sensor D delivers a voltage above its valid range. For GM's NTC sensor in the motor B inverter that equals an implausibly cold inverter, so an open circuit, a lost ground or a cracked thermistor is likely.",[["h","Open sensor or circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Loose, corroded or unplugged connector at the inverter"],["l","Lost sensor ground"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, depending on model after two trips","Reduced motor B power and regeneration","Implausibly low inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BDA":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor D Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BDA covers an inverter temperature sensor D signal that changes in steps or drops out briefly. Real temperatures of a liquid-cooled inverter move smoothly, so such jumps suggest a contact problem or interference from the high-voltage switching, which a Hitachi patent on inverter systems names as a typical disturbance of the small sensor signal.",[["h","Loose or corroded contact at the inverter module"],["m","Intermittent fault inside the power inverter module"],["l","Interference from the high-voltage circuit on the sensor signal"]],["Warning lamp that comes and goes","Brief power reductions while driving","Jumping inverter temperature in live data"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BDB":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor E Circuit","Which thermistor counts as inverter temperature sensor E is set by the manufacturer. In GM's hybrid tables it is the third sensor of the motor A inverter (PIM sensor #3), assigned to phase W in the 2016 to 2019 tables.",[["h","Internal fault of the thermistor or its circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the inverter module"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid system warning","Reduced electric drive power","Further inverter temperature codes stored at the same time"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BDC":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor E Circuit Range/Performance","P0BDC checks whether inverter temperature sensor E, GM's phase W sensor in the motor A inverter, agrees with the vehicle after a long stand. The 2016 and 2018 tables compare it after a cold soak with a calculated average temperature; more than 20 °C apart for 0.525 of 0.625 s sets Type B, two trips.",[["m","Phase W thermistor drifting out of tolerance"],["m","Uneven inverter cooling or residual heat after the soak period"],["l","Failing power inverter module (replace only with P0AC4 per GM PIC6303A)"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two drives, often without any drivability change","Code found during inspection without customer complaint"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BDD":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor E Circuit Low","P0BDD reports that the signal of inverter temperature sensor E, the phase W thermistor of GM's motor A inverter, sits below its valid range. GM uses an NTC thermistor with a pull-up on the sensing board, so a short to ground appears as an impossibly hot inverter.",[["h","Shorted thermistor or signal line inside the power inverter module"],["m","Signal shorted to ground in external wiring, where the design has any"],["l","Moisture or coolant inside the inverter housing"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Drive motor A power strongly limited","Reduced regenerative braking"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BDE":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor E Circuit High","P0BDE means the signal of inverter temperature sensor E is above its valid range. For GM's NTC thermistor with pull-up that reads as an extremely cold phase W in the motor A inverter, typically an open circuit, lost reference ground or a failed sensor.",[["h","Open thermistor or broken connection inside the power inverter module"],["m","Loose or corroded connector at the inverter"],["l","Lost sensor reference ground on the sensing board"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced electric drive power once the inverter has warmed up"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BDF":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor E Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BDF flags an unstable signal from inverter temperature sensor E: the reading jumps or drops out briefly instead of staying out of range, typically from a loose connector, a cracked solder joint or an ageing thermistor. In GM's hybrid tables sensor E is the phase W sensor of the motor A inverter, but GM does not list this erratic code; no manufacturer documents P0BDF publicly.",[["h","Loose, corroded or vibrating connector at the inverter"],["m","Cracked solder joint on the inverter sensing board"],["l","Ageing thermistor with unstable resistance"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp that comes and goes","Occasional loss of electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BE0":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor F Circuit","Sensor F is, in GM's hybrid tables, the third thermistor of the motor B inverter (inverter B sensor #3), the counterpart of sensor E in the motor A inverter; the 2016 to 2019 tables assign it to phase W. Other makers may use the letter differently.",[["h","Internal fault of the thermistor or its circuit inside the power inverter module"],["m","Corroded or loose connector at the inverter module"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid system warning","Reduced power from drive motor B","Higher fuel consumption in hybrid operation"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BE1":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor F Circuit Range/Performance","P0BE1 is the plausibility check of inverter temperature sensor F, GM's phase W thermistor in the motor B inverter. After the car has stood long enough, its reading must match a reference: in the 2016 and 2018 tables a calculated average after cold soak, with more than 20 °C difference for 0.525 of 0.625 s setting Type B over two trips, Type A in a 2019 table.",[["m","Phase W thermistor of inverter B drifting out of tolerance"],["m","Uneven inverter cooling or residual heat after the soak period"],["l","Failing power inverter module (replace only with P0AC4 per GM PIC6303A)"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after two drives, usually without drivability change","Code found during inspection without customer complaint"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BE2":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor F Circuit Low","P0BE2 is set when the voltage from inverter temperature sensor F, the phase W thermistor in GM's motor B inverter, drops below the valid window. Because GM pairs an NTC element with a pull-up resistor, a short to ground is converted into a temperature far above anything the inverter can survive.",[["h","Shorted thermistor or signal line inside the power inverter module"],["m","Signal shorted to ground in external wiring, where the design has any"],["l","Moisture or coolant inside the inverter housing"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Power of drive motor B strongly limited","Engine runs more often in hybrid operation"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BE3":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor F Circuit High","P0BE3 is the high-signal fault of inverter temperature sensor F, GM's phase W thermistor in the motor B inverter. With an NTC element and pull-up, an open circuit or a sensor that has lost ground drives the voltage up, which the controller reads as an implausibly cold inverter.",[["h","Open thermistor or broken connection inside the power inverter module"],["m","Loose or corroded connector at the inverter"],["l","Lost sensor reference ground on the sensing board"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor B once warmed up"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BE4":["Drive Motor Inverter Temperature Sensor F Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0BE4 stands for brief dropouts or jumps in the signal of inverter temperature sensor F, which GM's hybrid tables place at phase W of the motor B inverter. The reading returns to normal between events, which separates it from the steady low and high faults P0BE2 and P0BE3.",[["h","Loose, corroded or vibrating connector at the inverter"],["m","Cracked solder joint on the inverter sensing board"],["l","Ageing thermistor with unstable resistance"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp that comes and goes","Occasional power reduction of drive motor B"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BE5":["Drive Motor A Phase U Current Sensor Circuit","The inverter of drive motor A measures the current in each motor phase with a current sensor; the controller needs these values for torque control and overcurrent protection. P0BE5 is the general circuit code for the phase U sensor, used for an electrical fault that is not classed as offset, low or high.",[["h","Failed phase U current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Supply or signal fault on the inverter's sensor board"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning and malfunction indicator lamp","Reduced power from drive motor A","Possible loss of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BE6":["Drive Motor A Phase U Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","GM uses P0BE6 as the offset check of the phase U current sensor in the motor A inverter. At every start, while no current flows, the controller learns the sensor's zero offset; if it exceeds 30 A (2011 Volt and 2016 tables, Type A; 2012 two-mode trucks, Type B) or 35 A (2019 table, Type A), the code sets.",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","Zero offset of the phase U current sensor drifted beyond the limit"],["m","Faulty sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BE7":["Drive Motor A Phase U Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0BE7 means the output of the phase U current sensor in the motor A inverter has fallen below its valid range. GM converts the sensor voltage into amps and compares it with a limit: below -700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s in the 2016 tables and 0.1 of 0.15 s in a 2019 table, both Type A; below -700 A, Type B, on the 2012 two-mode trucks; below -440 A, Type B, two trips, in the BAS Plus (eAssist) table; the 2011 Volt uses 800 A limits for these sensors.",[["h","Failed phase U current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Sensor signal or supply pulled to ground on the sensor board"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power or no assist from drive motor A","Possible shutdown of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BE8":["Drive Motor A Phase U Current Sensor Circuit High","P0BE8 is the high-side counterpart of P0BE7: the phase U current sensor of the motor A inverter reports a value above its valid range. GM's tables set it above 700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s (2016) or 0.1 of 0.15 s (2019), Type A, one trip; above 800 A, Type A, on the 2011 Volt; above 700 A, Type B, on the 2012 two-mode trucks; and above 440 A, Type B, two trips, in the BAS Plus (eAssist) table.",[["h","Failed phase U current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to the sensor supply voltage or open reference ground"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor A","Possible shutdown of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BE9":["Drive Motor A Phase V Current Sensor Circuit","P0BE9 covers a general circuit fault of the phase V current sensor in the motor A inverter, meaning an electrical problem the controller does not narrow down to an offset, low or high condition. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Failed phase V current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Supply or signal fault on the inverter's sensor board"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning and malfunction indicator lamp","Reduced power from drive motor A"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BEA":["Drive Motor A Phase V Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0BEA is the performance check of the phase V current sensor in the motor A inverter. GM implements it as an offset test: with no current flowing at start-up, the learned zero point must stay within 30 A (2011 Volt, 2016 tables, Type A; 2012 two-mode trucks, Type B, with P0BEB/P0BEC not active) or 35 A (2019 table, Type A, after a 0.4 s delay).",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","Zero offset of the phase V current sensor drifted beyond the limit"],["m","Faulty sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BEB":["Drive Motor A Phase V Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0BEB reports that the phase V current sensor in the motor A inverter outputs a signal below its valid range. GM compares the converted current with a lower limit: below -700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s (2016 tables) or 0.1 of 0.15 s (2019 table), Type A, one trip; below -800 A, Type A, on the 2011 Volt; below -700 A, Type B, two trips, on the 2012 two-mode trucks.",[["h","Failed phase V current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Sensor signal or supply pulled to ground on the sensor board"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor A","Possible shutdown of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BEC":["Drive Motor A Phase V Current Sensor Circuit High","P0BEC is set when the phase V current sensor of the motor A inverter reports a value above its valid range. In GM's tables the limit is above 700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s (2016) or 0.1 of 0.15 s (2019), Type A; above 800 A, Type A, on the 2011 Volt with PWM output disabled; and above 700 A, Type B, two trips, on the 2012 two-mode trucks.",[["h","Failed phase V current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to the sensor supply voltage or open reference ground"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor A"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BED":["Drive Motor A Phase W Current Sensor Circuit","P0BED is the general circuit code of the phase W current sensor in the drive motor A inverter, for an electrical fault outside the offset, low and high categories. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM's hybrid tables from the 2011 Volt to 2019, including the BAS Plus (eAssist) system, use P0BEE, P0BEF and P0BF0 instead.",[["h","Failed phase W current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Supply or signal fault on the inverter's sensor board"],["l","Software fault in the motor control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning and malfunction indicator lamp","Reduced power or assist from drive motor A"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BEE":["Drive Motor A Phase W Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0BEE checks the phase W current sensor of the motor A inverter for a plausible zero point. GM learns the sensor offset at each start while no current flows and sets the code above 30 A (2011 Volt and 2016 tables, Type A; 2012 two-mode trucks, Type B) or above 35 A (2019 table, Type A).",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","Zero offset of the phase W current sensor drifted beyond the limit"],["m","Faulty sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BEF":["Drive Motor A Phase W Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0BEF indicates that the phase W current sensor of the motor A inverter delivers a signal below its valid range. GM's limits: below -700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s (2016 tables) or 0.1 of 0.15 s (2019), Type A; below -800 A, Type A, on the 2011 Volt; below -700 A, Type B, on the 2012 two-mode trucks; below -440 A, Type B, two trips, in the BAS Plus (eAssist) table.",[["h","Failed phase W current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Sensor signal or supply pulled to ground on the sensor board"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power or no assist from drive motor A"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF0":["Drive Motor A Phase W Current Sensor Circuit High","P0BF0 is the high-signal fault of the phase W current sensor in the motor A inverter. GM sets it above 700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s in the 2016 tables or 0.1 of 0.15 s in a 2019 table, Type A, one trip; above 700 A, Type B, two trips, on the 2012 two-mode trucks; above 440 A, Type B, in the BAS Plus (eAssist) table; the 2011 Volt uses 800 A limits for this sensor family.",[["h","Failed phase W current sensor inside the inverter"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to the sensor supply voltage or open reference ground"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor A"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF1":["Drive Motor B Phase U Current Sensor Circuit","On hybrids with two electric machines, the inverter of drive motor B carries its own set of phase current sensors, which the motor control processor uses for torque control and to protect the power switches. P0BF1 is the general circuit code for the phase U sensor of motor B, meant for an electrical fault that is not classified as offset, low or high.",[["h","Failed phase U current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Supply or signal fault on the inverter's sensor board"],["l","Software fault in the motor B control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning and malfunction indicator lamp","Reduced power or no contribution from drive motor B","Engine runs more often because electric drive is limited"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF2":["Drive Motor B Phase U Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","GM runs P0BF2 as the offset test of the phase U current sensor in the motor B inverter. Each time the vehicle is started, while no current flows through the motor, the controller learns the sensor's zero point and compares it with a limit: 30 A on the 2011 Volt (Type A) and in the 2016 tables (Type A, after 0.1 s), 30 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips, power stage open, P0BF3/P0BF4 not active) and 35 A in a 2019 table (Type A, after 0.4 s).",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","Zero point of the motor B phase U current sensor drifted past the limit"],["m","Faulty current sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BF3":["Drive Motor B Phase U Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0BF3 is stored when the phase U current sensor of the motor B inverter reports a value below its valid range. GM converts the sensor voltage into amps and sets the code below -800 A on the 2011 Volt (Type A, 4 of 6 samples), below -700 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips), and below -700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s in the 2016 tables or 0.1 of 0.15 s in a 2019 table (Type A, one trip).",[["h","Failed phase U current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Sensor signal or supply pulled to ground on the sensor board"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power or no contribution from drive motor B","Possible shutdown of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF4":["Drive Motor B Phase U Current Sensor Circuit High","P0BF4 is the upper-limit counterpart of P0BF3 for the phase U current sensor of drive motor B. GM's thresholds: above 800 A on the 2011 Volt, checked with PWM output disabled (Type A); above 700 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips); above 700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s in the 2016 tables and 0.1 of 0.15 s in a 2019 table (Type A, one trip).",[["h","Failed phase U current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to the sensor supply voltage or open reference ground"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor B","Further inverter or phase current codes stored"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF5":["Drive Motor B Phase V Current Sensor Circuit","P0BF5 stands for a general circuit fault of the phase V current sensor in the motor B inverter, an electrical problem the controller does not narrow down to offset, low or high. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Failed phase V current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Supply or signal fault on the inverter's sensor board"],["l","Software fault in the motor B control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning and malfunction indicator lamp","Reduced power from drive motor B"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF6":["Drive Motor B Phase V Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0BF6 is the performance check of the phase V current sensor in the motor B inverter, implemented by GM as a zero offset test. The offset is learned at every start with no current through the motor and must stay within 30 A on the 2011 Volt (Type A, P0BEB/P0BEC not active, as printed in the table) and in the 2016 tables (Type A, 0.1 s), within 30 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips) and within 35 A in a 2019 table (Type A, 0.4 s).",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","Zero point of the motor B phase V current sensor drifted past the limit"],["m","Faulty current sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BF7":["Drive Motor B Phase V Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0BF7 reports that the phase V current sensor of the motor B inverter outputs a signal below its valid range. In GM's hybrid tables the converted current must fall below -800 A on the 2011 Volt (Type A, one trip), below -700 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips, 10 of 15 samples) or below -700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s (2016) and 0.1 of 0.15 s (2019), both Type A.",[["h","Failed phase V current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Sensor signal or supply pulled to ground on the sensor board"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power or no contribution from drive motor B","Possible shutdown of electric drive"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF8":["Drive Motor B Phase V Current Sensor Circuit High","P0BF8 sets when the phase V current sensor of drive motor B reports more current than its valid range allows. GM uses above 800 A on the 2011 Volt with the PWM output disabled (Type A), above 700 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips) and above 700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s in the 2016 tables or 0.1 of 0.15 s in a 2019 table (Type A, one trip).",[["h","Failed phase V current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to the sensor supply voltage or open reference ground"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor B"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BF9":["Drive Motor B Phase W Current Sensor Circuit","P0BF9 is reserved for a general circuit fault of the phase W current sensor in the drive motor B inverter, outside the offset, low and high categories that have their own codes. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM's tables from the 2011 Volt through 2019, and Jeep's 2020 Renegade and Compass PHEV bulletins, refer only to the specific siblings, chiefly P0BFA.",[["h","Failed phase W current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Supply or signal fault on the inverter's sensor board"],["l","Software fault in the motor B control processor"]],["Hybrid system warning and malfunction indicator lamp","Reduced power or no contribution from drive motor B"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BFA":["Drive Motor B Phase W Current Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0BFA checks the phase W current sensor of the motor B inverter for a plausible zero point. GM learns the offset at each start while no current flows and sets the code above 30 A on the 2011 Volt (Type A, power stage open), above 30 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips, only with P0BFB/P0BFC inactive), above 30 A after 0.1 s in the 2016 tables and above 35 A after 0.4 s in a 2019 table (both Type A).",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","Zero point of the motor B phase W current sensor drifted past the limit"],["m","Faulty current sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BFB":["Drive Motor B Phase W Current Sensor Circuit Low","P0BFB indicates that the phase W current sensor of the motor B inverter delivers a signal below its valid range. GM's limits: below -800 A on the 2011 Volt (Type A, 4 of 6 samples at 10.4 ms), below -700 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips) and below -700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s in the 2016 tables or 0.1 of 0.15 s in a 2019 table (Type A, one trip).",[["h","Failed phase W current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Sensor signal or supply pulled to ground on the sensor board"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power or no contribution from drive motor B"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BFC":["Drive Motor B Phase W Current Sensor Circuit High","P0BFC is the signal-high fault of the phase W current sensor in the motor B inverter. GM sets it above 800 A on the 2011 Volt (Type A, one trip), above 700 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type B, two trips, 10 of 15 samples) and above 700 A for 0.05 of 0.1 s in the 2016 tables or 0.1 of 0.15 s in a 2019 table (Type A).",[["h","Failed phase W current sensor in the motor B inverter"],["m","Sensor signal shorted to the sensor supply voltage or open reference ground"],["l","Poor contact at an internal or external inverter connector"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Reduced power from drive motor B","Engine runs more often because electric drive is limited"],["shop_only",[150,4000]],"c"],"P0BFD":["Drive Motor A Phase U-V-W Current Sensor Correlation","In a three-phase motor without a neutral connection the three phase currents must add up to roughly zero. P0BFD checks this for drive motor A: if the absolute sum of the U, V and W readings exceeds a limit, at least one sensor is wrong.",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","One motor A phase current sensor reading wrong but still within its own limits"],["m","Faulty current sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced power from drive motor A"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BFE":["Drive Motor B Phase U-V-W Current Sensor Correlation","P0BFE applies the phase balance test to drive motor B: the controller adds the U, V and W current readings, which should cancel out, and sets the code when the absolute sum stays above a limit. GM uses 156 A on the 2011 Volt and 110 A on the 2012 and 2013 two-mode trucks (Type A, one trip, main relay closed) and 110 A for 0.0032 of 0.0038 s in the 2016 and 2019 tables (Type A).",[["h","Hybrid control processor software (Jeep bulletin 08-056-21/08-057-21)"],["m","One motor B phase current sensor reading wrong but still within its own limits"],["m","Faulty current sensing electronics in the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp and hybrid warning","Turtle (limp home) light and Service 4WD light (Jeep)","Reduced power from drive motor B"],["shop_only",[100,4000]],"c"],"P0BFF":["Drive Motor A Current","P0BFF is the general code for the current flowing through drive motor A, the electric machine the manufacturer labels A. It covers a current fault that is not classed as too low (P0C00) or too high (P0C01).",[["m","Loose, corroded or damaged three-phase cable joint between inverter and motor A"],["m","Fault in the motor A inverter or its current measurement"],["l","Damaged stator winding of drive motor A"],["l","Motor control software fault"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid system warning","Reduced electric drive power or limp mode","Other drive motor A current or phase codes stored"],["shop_only",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0C00":["Drive Motor A Current Low","P0C00 reports that the current of drive motor A is lower than the controller expects for the commanded torque, the counterpart of P0C01 (current high). No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly; GM and Stellantis use only P0C01 from this group.",[["m","High-resistance or loose three-phase connection at motor A or the inverter"],["m","Inverter power stage for motor A not delivering current"],["l","Open or damaged winding in drive motor A"]],["Weak or missing electric drive torque","Malfunction indicator lamp or hybrid system warning","Vehicle runs on the engine only or in limp mode"],["shop_only",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0C01":["Drive Motor A Current High","P0C01 is the overcurrent check of drive motor A, meant to protect the inverter's IGBTs. GM sets it when any phase current exceeds 725 A on the 2011 Volt, 600 A on the 2012 two-mode trucks and in the 2016 to 2019 tables, or 396 A on eAssist; since 2016 a too negative d-axis current (about -540 to -310 A, depending on stator temperature) also counts.",[["h","Damaged or improperly torqued three-phase cable joints (Stellantis S2608000006)"],["m","Damaged stator winding of motor A (GM PIP5246)"],["m","Outdated hybrid control software (Jeep TSB 08-144-23)"],["l","Failing power switch or current measurement in the inverter"]],["Limp mode with warning lamp","Reduced propulsion or engine unavailable message","Rough running or loud noise from the motor generator"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0C02":["Drive Motor B Current","P0C02 is the general current code for drive motor B, the second electric machine, such as the second motor of a two-motor hybrid or the rear motor of an electric all-wheel-drive system. It applies to a current fault that is neither classed as low (P0C03) nor high (P0C04).",[["m","Loose, corroded or damaged three-phase connection of motor B"],["m","Fault in the motor B inverter or its current measurement"],["l","Damaged stator winding of drive motor B"]],["Hybrid or electric drive warning lamp","Reduced power or loss of all-wheel drive","Limp mode"],["shop_only",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0C03":["Drive Motor B Current Low","P0C03 indicates that drive motor B draws less current than the controller expects for the requested torque. It is the low counterpart of P0C04 and the motor B twin of P0C00.",[["m","High-resistance or loose three-phase connection of motor B"],["m","Inverter power stage for motor B not delivering current"],["l","Open or damaged winding in drive motor B"]],["Weak or missing torque from motor B","Warning lamp for the hybrid or electric drive","Reduced performance or limp mode"],["shop_only",[150,5000]],"c"],"P0C04":["Drive Motor B Current High","P0C04 is the overcurrent check of drive motor B and protects the IGBTs of its inverter. GM flags it when the highest phase current exceeds 725 A on the 2011 Volt or 600 A on the 2012 two-mode trucks and in the 2016 to 2019 hybrid tables; the newer tables also fail a d-axis current below a stator-temperature curve of roughly -556 to -315 A.",[["h","Cross-threaded or loose three-phase cable bolts with metal debris (Stellantis S2608000006)"],["m","Shorted or damaged stator winding of motor B"],["l","Failing power switch or current measurement in the motor B inverter"]],["Limp mode with red wrench or malfunction lamp","Sudden loss of electric drive power"],["shop_only",[100,6000]],"c"],"P0C05":["Drive Motor A Phase U-V-W Circuit/Open","P0C05 detects an open or missing phase between the inverter and drive motor A. GM checks each phase as the rotor passes its axis: if the peak phase axis current stays below 9 A while at least 23 A is commanded, with the rotor within 30 degrees of that axis and over 35 V on the inverter, about 200 failed samples set the code.",[["h","Loose or open three-phase terminal between inverter and motor A"],["m","Software fault, cured by update (Jeep TSB 08-144-23)"],["m","Internal fault of the motor generator unit or open stator winding"],["l","One-off event that does not return after clearing (Volvo TJ 36863)"]],["Propulsion system performance message or warning lamp","Reduced electric drive power","Loss of 12 V charging or noise from the motor generator on mild hybrids"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0C06":["Drive Motor A Phase U-V-W Circuit Low","P0C06 belongs to the phase circuit group of drive motor A and reports that a U, V or W phase line sits too low, typically a short to the negative high-voltage rail or to chassis ground. P0C05 covers an open or missing phase, P0C07 the opposite high state.",[["m","Phase cable or winding shorted to ground or housing"],["m","Shorted low-side power switch in the motor A inverter"],["l","Insulation damage from heat, moisture or chafing"]],["Electric drive shut down with warning lamp","Overcurrent or insulation codes stored at the same time"],["shop_only",[200,6000]],"c"],"P0C07":["Drive Motor A Phase U-V-W Circuit High","P0C07 is the high member of the drive motor A phase circuit group: a U, V or W phase line reads higher than it should, most likely through a short to the positive high-voltage rail or a power switch that stays on. Its siblings are P0C05 for an open phase and P0C06 for a phase pulled low.",[["m","Shorted high-side power switch in the motor A inverter"],["m","Phase cable shorted to the positive high-voltage path"],["l","Faulty gate driver holding a switch on"]],["Immediate shutdown of electric drive with warning lamp","Reduced performance or engine-only operation"],["shop_only",[200,6000]],"c"],"P0C08":["Drive Motor B Phase U-V-W Circuit/Open","P0C08 is the open-phase check for drive motor B, the motor B twin of P0C05. GM runs it on the 2011 Volt and the 2012 two-mode trucks: each phase is checked as the rotor passes its axis, and a phase axis current below 9 A while at least 23 A is commanded, with the rotor within 30 degrees and over 35 V on the inverter, counts as a miss.",[["h","Loose or open three-phase terminal between inverter and motor B"],["m","Open stator winding in drive motor B"],["m","Inverter stage for motor B not conducting"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion power","Engine running more often than usual"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0C09":["Drive Motor B Phase U-V-W Circuit Low","P0C09 marks a U, V or W phase line of drive motor B that is pulled low, for example by a short to the negative high-voltage rail, to ground or by a power switch that conducts when it should not. It is the motor B twin of P0C06; the siblings P0C08 and P0C0A cover an open phase and a phase stuck high.",[["m","Phase cable or winding of motor B shorted to ground or housing"],["m","Shorted low-side power switch in the motor B inverter"],["l","Insulation damage from heat, moisture or chafing"]],["Motor B shut down with warning lamp","Overcurrent or insulation codes stored at the same time"],["shop_only",[200,6000]],"c"],"P0C0A":["Drive Motor B Phase U-V-W Circuit High","P0C0A reports that a phase line of drive motor B reads too high, typically a short to the positive high-voltage rail or a high-side power switch that stays on. It mirrors P0C07 for motor A and completes the motor B trio with P0C08 (open) and P0C09 (low).",[["m","Shorted high-side power switch in the motor B inverter"],["m","Phase cable of motor B shorted to the positive high-voltage path"],["l","Faulty gate driver holding a switch on"]],["Immediate shutdown of motor B with warning lamp","Reduced performance or loss of electric all-wheel drive"],["shop_only",[200,6000]],"c"],"P0C0B":["Drive Motor A Inverter Power Supply Circuit/Open","P0C0B flags a lost or interrupted supply inside the motor A inverter. GM reads it from the IGBT module status: when the gate drive supply falls below its threshold, the module reports a bias fault; on eAssist the 5 V rail below 3.5 to 4.3 V or the 15 V rail below 10.4 to 12.6 V counts, with run/crank above 7 V.",[["h","Failed gate-drive power supply inside the motor A inverter"],["m","Low 12 V feed, blown fuse or poor ground to the inverter control board"],["l","Open high-voltage feed to the inverter, e.g. in a belt starter-alternator (Mercedes LI15.40-P-077947)"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Electric drive or motor generator unavailable","Engine cannot be started, only electric driving possible (Mercedes P3 hybrids)"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0C0C":["Drive Motor A Inverter Power Supply Circuit Low","P0C0C reports that the supply voltage of the motor A inverter is present but too low, while P0C0B covers a lost or open supply and P0C0D a supply that is too high. Which supply is meant depends on the maker: GM ties its sibling P0C0B to the gate-drive supply of the IGBTs, Mercedes to the high-voltage feed.",[["h","Internal fault of the drive power electronics (Audi TSB 2081725)"],["m","Weak 12 V supply, poor ground or high-resistance connector at the inverter"],["l","Weak 12 V battery or DC/DC converter output"]],["Red high-voltage system warning asking to stop safely","Loss of drive power or vehicle will not get ready to drive"],["shop_only",[100,7000]],"c"],"P0C0D":["Drive Motor A Inverter Power Supply Circuit High","P0C0D reports that the supply of the motor A inverter is above its permitted range, while P0C0B stands for a lost or open supply and P0C0C for a supply that is too low. No manufacturer documents this code publicly.",[["h","Faulty voltage regulation of the gate-drive supply inside the motor A inverter"],["m","Excessive 12 V system voltage from the DC/DC converter or a charger"],["l","Supply or sense line shorted to a higher voltage in the harness"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, hybrid system warning","Reduced or no electric propulsion from motor A","Engine runs more often or the vehicle enters limp mode"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0C0E":["Drive Motor B Inverter Power Supply Circuit/Open","P0C0E is the motor B counterpart of P0C0B: the inverter for the second motor has lost the supply of its gate-drive board. GM documents it on the Volt and the two-mode trucks.",[["h","Failed gate-drive power supply inside the motor B inverter"],["m","Weak 12 V feed, blown fuse or poor ground at the inverter control board"],["l","Loose or corroded low-voltage connector at the power inverter module"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion message, motor B unavailable","Engine runs continuously instead of electric driving"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0C0F":["Drive Motor B Inverter Power Supply Circuit Low","P0C0F means the supply of the motor B inverter is present but below its permitted range. P0C0E covers a lost or open supply, P0C10 a supply that is too high, and P0C0C is the same fault on motor A.",[["h","Weak 12 V battery or DC/DC converter output feeding the inverter electronics"],["m","Corroded connector or poor ground raising resistance in the inverter supply"],["m","Failing internal gate-drive power supply in the motor B inverter"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, hybrid system warning","Reduced propulsion, motor B unavailable","Fault appears mainly with low 12 V voltage, e.g. after standing"],["shop_only",[50,5000]],"c"],"P0C10":["Drive Motor B Inverter Power Supply Circuit High","P0C10 flags a supply of the motor B inverter that is above its permitted range. Its siblings are P0C0E for a lost or open supply and P0C0F for a low one; P0C0D is the motor A version.",[["h","Faulty voltage regulation of the gate-drive supply inside the motor B inverter"],["m","Excessive 12 V system voltage from the DC/DC converter or an external charger"],["l","Supply or sense line shorted to a higher voltage in the harness"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, hybrid system warning","Reduced propulsion, motor B unavailable","Vehicle limited to engine or reduced-power operation"],["shop_only",[100,5000]],"c"],"P0C11":["Drive Motor A Inverter Phase U Over Temperature","P0C11 is a protective code: the temperature sensor at phase U of the motor A inverter reads a valid but excessive value. GM uses it across its hybrids.",[["h","Low coolant level or air in the power electronics cooling loop"],["h","Failed or weak electric coolant pump of the inverter loop"],["m","Clogged power electronics radiator or inoperative cooling fan"],["l","Sustained heavy load such as towing uphill in high ambient heat"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp, often with a hybrid system overheat message","Reduced propulsion power until the inverter cools down","Electric drive unavailable, engine runs continuously"],["hard",[50,3500]],"c"],"P0C12":["Drive Motor A Inverter Phase V Over Temperature","P0C12 sets when the inverter for motor A measures an excessive temperature at phase V while its sensor itself is plausible. In GM's 2011 Volt table that is PIM sensor C above 98 °C; the two-mode trucks of 2012 use 88 °C with an 85 °C reset, the 2016 Volt 126 °C for 1.2 s out of 2 s and the 2016 ELR 102.5 °C, all Type A, one trip.",[["h","Low coolant level or trapped air in the inverter cooling loop"],["h","Inoperative electric coolant pump of the power electronics"],["m","Blocked low-temperature radiator or failed cooling fan"],["l","Degraded thermal contact or failing power stage inside the inverter"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Propulsion power reduced, especially under load or in hot weather","Hybrid system overheat or service message"],["hard",[50,3500]],"c"],"P0C13":["Drive Motor A Inverter Phase W Over Temperature","P0C13 covers phase W of the motor A inverter: the controller sees a credible sensor reading that exceeds the overtemperature limit. GM's 2011 Volt uses PIM sensor E with 98 °C, the 2012 two-mode trucks 88 °C, the 2016 and 2018 tables 126 °C for 1.2 s within a 2 s window and the 2019 table 128 °C for 1.5 s, always Type A, one trip, so the MIL comes on at once.",[["h","Coolant loss or air pocket in the power electronics loop"],["h","Electric coolant pump for the inverter stopped or running slow"],["m","Debris on the low-temperature radiator or cooling fan not running"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp immediately after the fault","Reduced propulsion until temperatures drop","Engine runs more often, little or no electric driving"],["hard",[50,3500]],"c"],"P0C14":["Drive Motor B Inverter Phase U Over Temperature","P0C14 is the motor B counterpart of P0C11: phase U of the second inverter has become too hot while its temperature sensor reads plausibly. GM documents it on the Volt and the two-mode trucks.",[["h","Low coolant level or air in the power electronics cooling loop"],["h","Failed electric coolant pump of the inverter loop"],["m","Clogged low-temperature radiator or failed cooling fan"],["l","Long periods of high load on motor B, e.g. towing or steep climbs"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion power, especially under load","Hybrid system overheat message"],["hard",[50,3500]],"c"],"P0C15":["Drive Motor B Inverter Phase V Over Temperature","P0C15 reports overtemperature at phase V of the motor B inverter, based on a sensor reading that is valid but above the limit. GM applies it on the Volt and the two-mode trucks: PIM sensor D above 98 °C on the 2011 Volt, 88 °C on the 2012 trucks, 102.5 °C on the 2016 ELR, 131 °C for 1.2 s within 2 s on the 2016 Volt and in the 2018 table, every time Type A with one trip.",[["h","Coolant loss or air in the inverter cooling loop"],["h","Electric coolant pump of the power electronics not working"],["m","Blocked radiator for the power electronics or fan failure"],["l","Poor heat transfer or ageing power stage inside the inverter"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion until the inverter has cooled","Engine runs more often than usual"],["hard",[50,3500]],"c"],"P0C16":["Drive Motor B Inverter Phase W Over Temperature","P0C16 is set by the motor B inverter when phase W runs too hot and the temperature sensor itself shows no fault. GM's tables give PIM sensor F above 98 °C for the 2011 Volt, above 88 °C with an 85 °C reset for the 2012 two-mode trucks, and 131 °C for 1.2 s within 2 s for the 2016 Volt, with 1.5 s in the 2019 table; each is Type A, one trip.",[["h","Low coolant level or air in the power electronics loop"],["h","Electric coolant pump of the inverter failed or blocked"],["m","Dirty low-temperature radiator or inoperative fan"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after one drive","Reduced propulsion, notably at high load or high ambient temperature","Hybrid system overheat message"],["hard",[50,3500]],"c"],"P0C17":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Not Learned","P0C17 means the controller has no valid learned offset for the resolver of motor A, which it needs to align rotor position with the stator field. GM runs the learn at key-off and sets the code if it fails without a stored value: motor speed above 50 rpm, DC voltage below 192 V (200 V on the 2016 Volt), phase current below 15 A or a learn time over 1.4 s.",[["h","Offset learn not performed after replacing the inverter, motor or resolver"],["m","High voltage not reaching the inverter during the learn, e.g. contactor or HV cable fault"],["m","Loose three-phase ring terminals after a repair"],["l","Damaged motor stator winding (GM 21-NA-218)"]],["Engine starts and stalls or will not start after a repair","Malfunction indicator lamp with reduced propulsion message","Electric drive unavailable"],["shop_only",[80,4000]],"c"],"P0C18":["Drive Motor B Position Sensor Not Learned","P0C18 is the motor B version of P0C17: the resolver offset of the second motor has not been learned and no valid earlier value is stored. GM performs the learn at key-off; it fails if motor speed exceeds 50 rpm, DC voltage stays below 192 V, phase current stays below 15 A or the learn takes longer than 1.4 s.",[["h","Offset learn not completed after replacing the inverter, motor B or its resolver"],["m","No high voltage at the drive motor control module, e.g. contactor fault or disconnected HV cable"],["m","Control module needs reprogramming to enable a wide position sensor learn"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp after a repair or module programming","Reduced propulsion or vehicle will not enter ready mode","Motor B unavailable for driving or charging"],["shop_only",[80,4000]],"c"],"P0C19":["Drive Motor A Torque Delivered Performance","P0C19 is a plausibility check: the torque that motor A actually delivers, calculated by the motor control processor, does not follow the slewed torque command. GM sets it when the difference exceeds 123 Nm for 0.1875 s within 0.2 s on the 2016 and 2018 tables, Type A, one trip; eAssist uses 36 Nm as Type B, two trips.",[["h","Outdated power inverter module software (Stellantis 08-059-25, 08-231-24)"],["m","Implausible rotor position signal from the motor A resolver or its wiring"],["m","Faulty phase current sensing distorting the calculated torque"],["l","Internal fault of the inverter power stage or motor winding"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Electric drive shuts off and the engine takes over","Engine stop-start no longer works or a service hybrid system warning appears"],["shop_only",[80,5000]],"c"],"P0C1A":["Drive Motor B Torque Delivered Performance","P0C1A compares the torque command for motor B with the torque the motor control processor calculates as actually delivered. GM's 2016, 2018 and 2019 tables set it when the difference exceeds 132 Nm for 0.1875 s in a 0.2 s window, Type A, one trip, and only while listed resolver and motor control faults are inactive.",[["h","Short in the internal transmission wiring connector (Chrysler Pacifica Hybrid 21-008-23)"],["m","Implausible rotor position signal from the motor B resolver"],["m","Hybrid control or inverter module software requiring an update"],["l","Faulty phase current sensing for motor B"]],["Malfunction indicator lamp","Vehicle in limp mode with reduced propulsion","Hybrid system warning, engine runs more often"],["shop_only",[80,8000]],"c"],"P0C1B":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Too High","P0C1B is a protection code: the electronics that drive the auxiliary transmission fluid pump measure a valid but excessive internal temperature. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","High transmission fluid temperature from heavy load, towing or a weak fluid cooler"],["m","Pump running against high resistance (binding, clogged filter) and heating its driver"],["l","Poor heat transfer from the module (mounting, contamination) or internal module fault"]],["Engine auto-stop temporarily unavailable","Transmission or hybrid warning, MIL depending on manufacturer","Harsh engagement when driving off after a stop"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0C1C":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit","P0C1C is the general circuit fault of the temperature sensor inside the auxiliary pump control module, used when the signal is invalid without fitting the low, high or range checks. No manufacturer documents it publicly.",[["h","Temperature sensor or its measuring circuit on the module board faulty"],["m","Unstable supply or ground of the pump module distorting the sensor reading"],["l","Moisture or fluid inside the module housing"]],["Warning lamp or transmission message","Start-stop function inactive"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C1D":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit Range/Performance","P0C1D means the internal temperature sensor of the auxiliary pump module delivers an electrically valid value that does not fit the other temperatures in the vehicle. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Board temperature sensor drifted or measuring circuit out of tolerance"],["m","Reference value wrong because the transmission fluid or coolant temperature sensor is faulty"],["l","Internal fault of the pump control module"]],["MIL after repeated detection","Start-stop restricted or unavailable"],["hard",[100,1800]],"c"],"P0C1E":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit Low","P0C1E reports that the signal voltage of the module's internal temperature sensor is below its valid range. With the usual NTC thermistor, low voltage corresponds to a very high temperature, so a short to ground looks like an overheated board.",[["h","Board temperature sensor or its signal path shorted to ground inside the module"],["m","Measuring circuit of the pump control module faulty"],["l","Fluid or moisture on the module board"]],["Pump switched off as if overheated, auto-stop unavailable","MIL after two drive cycles"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C1F":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Internal Temperature Sensor Circuit High","P0C1F means the internal temperature sensor of the auxiliary pump module delivers a voltage above its valid range, which for an NTC thermistor reads as an implausibly cold board and usually indicates an open sensor path. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Board temperature sensor open or its solder joint cracked"],["m","Measuring circuit of the pump control module faulty"],["l","Ground offset at the pump module shifting the sensor voltage"]],["MIL after two drive cycles","Start-stop restricted, pump in protective mode"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C20":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Phase U-V-W Circuit/Open","P0C20 means at least one of the three motor phases of the auxiliary transmission fluid pump carries no current, typically an open lead, terminal or winding. GM uses it in the pump controller of the Chevrolet Volt (2011-15 tables) and the 2016 Cadillac ELR: with the inverter in Run, high voltage above 35 V and the rotor within 30 degrees of a phase axis, the peak current of that phase is checked; below 1 A for 200 counts sets the code, Type A, MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Motor connector of the pump not fully seated, or loose phase terminal"],["m","Open phase lead between pump inverter and motor"],["m","Power switch of one phase in the pump inverter not conducting"],["l","Open winding in the pump motor"]],["MIL and Service Vehicle Soon message","Reduced propulsion or no electric drive mode","Engine restart harsh or delayed"],["hard",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0C21":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Phase U-V-W Circuit Low","P0C21 reports a low condition on the phase U, V or W circuits of the auxiliary pump motor, usually a phase lead shorted to ground or a voltage at a phase terminal that stays below what the inverter commands. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Phase lead chafed through and shorted to ground or the transmission case"],["m","Transmission fluid or water in the motor connector creating a leakage path"],["m","Low-side switch or phase sensing in the pump inverter faulty"],["l","Motor winding shorted to the stator core"]],["MIL and transmission or hybrid warning","Auxiliary pump off, no engine auto-stop"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0C22":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Phase U-V-W Circuit High","P0C22 reports a high condition on the U-V-W phase circuits of the auxiliary pump motor, for example a phase lead shorted to the supply or a phase voltage that stays high while the inverter should pull it down. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Phase lead shorted to supply voltage or to another phase"],["m","High-side switch of the pump inverter stuck conducting"],["m","Phase current sensing in the inverter reads too high"],["l","Shorted turns in the pump motor winding"]],["MIL and hybrid or transmission warning","Pump shuts down, harsh engagement after stops"],["hard",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0C23":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Circuit/Open","P0C23 is set by the controlling module, usually the PCM, TCM or hybrid controller, when the circuit to the auxiliary pump control module is open. J2012 does not name the line further; in practice it is the command or enable wire.",[["h","Open command or enable wire between controller and pump module"],["m","Pushed-out or corroded terminal at the pump module or controller connector"],["l","Input stage of the pump control module faulty"]],["Start-stop no longer works","MIL or transmission warning","Harsh engagement after an engine stop"],["medium",[50,1500]],"c"],"P0C24":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Circuit Low","P0C24 means the circuit from the controlling module to the auxiliary pump control module is held low, typically a short to ground on the command line. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Command wire shorted to ground, e.g. chafed at the transmission case"],["m","Fluid or corrosion in the pump connector pulling the line low"],["l","Output stage in the PCM/TCM or input of the pump module faulty"]],["No engine auto-stop","MIL or transmission warning"],["medium",[50,1500]],"c"],"P0C25":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Circuit High","P0C25 reports that the circuit from the controlling module to the auxiliary pump control module sits high, for instance a command wire shorted to battery voltage or an output that cannot pull the line down. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Command wire shorted to battery or another supply voltage"],["m","Output stage in the PCM/TCM stuck high"],["l","Input stage of the pump control module faulty"]],["MIL or transmission warning","Pump runs continuously or start-stop is disabled"],["medium",[50,1500]],"c"],"P0C26":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Current","P0C26 is the general motor current fault of auxiliary pump A: the current drawn does not match what the commanded operating point should need, without clearly being too low or too high. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Low or aerated transmission fluid at the pump intake"],["m","Pump binding or contaminated, changing the motor load"],["m","Current sensing in the pump driver implausible"],["l","Outdated control software of the pump or controlling module"]],["MIL or transmission warning","Harsh engagement after an auto-stop","Start-stop deactivated"],["hard",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0C27":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Current Low","P0C27 means motor A of the auxiliary transmission fluid pump draws less current than expected, a sign that the pump is not loaded, for example because it sucks air or turns without delivering. Ford documents it twice.",[["h","Air entering the pump supply tube (Ford 6F35, new tube with check ball)"],["m","Calibration of PCM and hybrid module (Ford Fusion/C-Max Energi)"],["m","Low transmission fluid level letting the pump draw air"],["l","High resistance or open circuit in the pump motor wiring"]],["MIL on","Harsh transmission engagement after a start-stop event"],["medium",[50,600]],"c"],"P0C28":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Current High","P0C28 is the overcurrent protection of auxiliary pump motor A. GM uses it on Volt, ELR and later hybrids: the inverter trips when any of the U, V or W phases exceeds 35 A, in 2016 within about 4 ms of a 62 ms window, or when the d-axis current falls below a stator temperature limit, Type A, MIL on the first trip.",[["h","Pump control software faulty (Cadillac CT6 update 18199, Ford Energi TSB 18-2282)"],["m","Pump seized, contaminated or running against blocked flow"],["m","Short between motor phases or to ground"],["l","Current sensing or power stage in the pump inverter faulty"]],["MIL or service wrench lamp","Auxiliary pump shuts down, no auto-stop or electric mode"],["hard",[50,2500]],"c"],"P0C29":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Driver Circuit Performance","P0C29 means the driver stage of auxiliary pump A does not behave as commanded, for instance the pump does not reach its target or the activation is reported as implausible. Volkswagen Group documents it on the 7-speed DSG 0GC (DQ381): VW lists P0C29 for the 2018 Golf R as implausible activation of auxiliary hydraulic pump 1 (V475), often with clutch and gear actuator codes as consequences; Audi's bulletin for the 2017-18 A3, A3 Cabriolet and S3 with gearbox dated up to 26 September 2017 calls for replacing the auxiliary hydraulic pump when only P0C29 remains.",[["h","Auxiliary hydraulic pump V475 of early production faulty (VW/Audi 0GC)"],["m","Poor contact at the pump electrical connection"],["l","Transmission control software not updated per service action"]],["Gearbox Malfunction warning in the instrument cluster","Consequential clutch or gear actuator codes, limp mode"],["shop_only",[400,2500]],"c"],"P0C2A":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump A Motor Stalled","P0C2A means motor A of the auxiliary pump is commanded but does not turn, or turns far slower than required. Ford documents it in TSB 17-2056 for the 2016 F-150 2.7L with start-stop built from 1 May 2016: with the fluid above 38 °C the technician commands the pump through the PCM at duty cycles from 20 to 90 percent; if it runs, the PCM gets new software, if not, the auxiliary pump is replaced.",[["h","Auxiliary pump seized or jammed by debris (Ford F-150 2.7L replaces the pump)"],["m","PCM calibration causing false stall detection (Ford TSB 17-2056)"],["l","Very cold, thick fluid or wrong fluid type raising drag"],["l","Open phase so the motor cannot develop torque"]],["MIL on","Start-stop inactive, harsh engagement after stops"],["medium",[50,900]],"c"],"P0C2B":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal","P0C2B concerns the status signal the auxiliary pump control module sends back to the controlling module. GM uses it on the Two-Mode hybrid trucks and SUVs (Tahoe, Yukon, Escalade, Silverado, Sierra, 2ML70): the hybrid controller expects a complete fault status message from the pump controller every 1.5 s; if it is missing or incomplete for 9.75 s with the vehicle in Run or Crank, the code sets as Type B, MIL after two trips.",[["h","Wiring or terminal tension fault between hybrid controller and pump control module"],["m","Early pump control module (GM date code 089-11 or older)"],["l","Key held in crank for a long time during a no-start"]],["MIL after two drive cycles","Bumps, lulls or surges around auto-stops"],["medium",[50,1200]],"c"],"P0C2C":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal Range/Performance","P0C2C means the feedback signal from the auxiliary pump control module is present and electrically valid but does not make sense, for example a reported speed or status that contradicts the command. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Electronics of the pump module reporting wrong status or speed"],["m","Intermittent contact on the feedback line distorting the signal"],["l","Pump not following the command mechanically, so feedback and command differ"]],["Service Vehicle Soon or MIL","Reduced propulsion power or no start-stop"],["medium",[100,1200]],"c"],"P0C2D":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal Low","P0C2D reports that the feedback signal from the electric transmission fluid pump module stays low, below its valid range. GM documents it for the 2021-23 Chevrolet Bolt EV and 2022 Bolt EUV in bulletin 21-NA-148: the driver sees Propulsion Power is Reduced and a Service Vehicle Soon lamp, with P0C2D, P2797 or P2798 stored.",[["h","Faulty solder joints in the electric transmission fluid pump (Chevrolet Bolt, GM 21-NA-148)"],["m","Feedback line shorted to ground"],["l","Corroded or loose pump connector"]],["Propulsion Power is Reduced message","Service Vehicle Soon lamp"],["medium",[250,900]],"c"],"P0C2E":["Electric/Auxiliary Transmission Fluid Pump Control Module Feedback Signal High","P0C2E means the feedback line from the auxiliary pump control module sits above its valid range, for example shorted to supply voltage or no longer pulled down by the module. No manufacturer documents this exact code publicly.",[["h","Feedback line shorted to supply voltage"],["m","Output stage of the pump control module no longer switching"],["l","Lost ground at the pump module letting the line float high"]],["MIL or Service Vehicle Soon lamp","Start-stop or electric drive restricted"],["medium",[50,1200]],"c"],"P0C2F":["Internal Control Module Drive Motor/Generator - Engine Speed Sensor Performance","P0C2F is an internal plausibility check of the hybrid control processor: the engine speed it reads through its own sensor path must match other speed information. In GM's Two-Mode truck tables (12OBDG06, 13OBDG06) the HCP compares its SPI-sensed engine speed with the calculated transmission input speed and with the engine speed received over CAN; a deviation beyond 250 rpm above 1500 rpm, with a wider band at lower speed, in 160 of 320 samples sets it as Type A, MIL after one trip.",[["h","Control module software flagging the comparison falsely (Ford Mach-E, fixed by reprogramming)"],["m","Speed signals disagree because of a faulty input or a disturbed CAN signal"],["l","Internal fault of the hybrid control module"]],["MIL or hybrid system warning","One pedal drive or regenerative functions switched off (Ford Mach-E)","Reduced hybrid assist or limited power"],["shop_only",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0C30":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack State of Charge High","P0C30 is the counterpart of P0A7D: the battery controller finds that the state of charge of the high-voltage pack stays above its usable window. CARB requires hybrid OBD to flag a state of charge that can no longer be kept inside the manufacturer's range.",[["m","Wrong SOC estimate from a drifting pack current or voltage measurement"],["m","Battery or hybrid control software not drawing the charge down as intended"],["l","Electric assist blocked by another fault, so the pack cannot discharge"]],["MIL after two trips","Reduced or no regenerative braking","Changed brake pedal feel and higher fuel consumption"],["shop_only",[100,2000]],"c"],"P0C31":["Inverter B Cooling System Performance","P0C31 reports that the cooling circuit of a second power inverter does not remove heat as expected; inverter A has its own code, P0A93. Vehicles with two separately cooled power electronics units, for example a front and a rear drive unit, need this second code.",[["h","Low coolant level or air trapped in the inverter B circuit"],["m","Weak or failed electric coolant pump of the circuit"],["l","Clogged radiator or inoperative cooling fan"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL","Reduced drive power, especially under load or in heat","Coolant level low in the electronics reservoir"],["medium",[50,1500]],"c"],"P0C32":["Hybrid/EV Battery Cooling System A Performance","P0C32 is the functional check of the battery thermal system that CARB asks for. GM tunes it per vehicle: the 2012 Volt (12OBDG01A) fails it when the average pack temperature stays above 40 °C in active cooling or below −17 °C in active heating, after 4200 s or 9000 s, Type A, one trip.",[["h","Battery cooling fan stopping at low 12 V system voltage (GM eAssist)"],["m","Low coolant, air or a weak pump in a liquid-cooled pack circuit"],["m","Blocked cooling air intake or ducts of an air-cooled pack"],["l","Failed pack heater or chiller valve"]],["MIL and hybrid system warning","Reduced electric range or assist","Charging system or battery warning message (eAssist)"],["medium",[80,1500]],"c"],"P0C33":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit","Battery temperature sensor F is the sixth thermistor in the high-voltage pack; GM's Volt bulletin 18-NA-342 lists the F codes under temperature sensor 6 of nine. P0C33 is the general circuit code for an electrical fault the controller does not classify as low, high or erratic.",[["h","Loose or corroded connector of sensor F at the sensing board"],["m","Failed thermistor F"],["l","Faulty input on the battery sensing board or module"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning and MIL","Reduced power or charging rate","Battery cooling running more than usual"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0C34":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit Range/Performance","P0C34 checks whether sensor F, GM's sensor 6, agrees with the rest of the pack. The controller compares its reading with the average of the other battery temperature sensors.",[["m","Sensor F drifting or poorly seated on its cell group"],["m","Module software setting the code falsely in the cold (GM Volt)"],["l","Real local temperature difference from a weak cell group or uneven cooling"]],["MIL on after two trips","Battery too cold or reduced power message","Limited charge and discharge power"],["shop_only",[60,2500]],"c"],"P0C35":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit Low","Sensor F is an NTC thermistor with a pull-up on the sensing board, so its voltage drops as temperature rises; a short to ground looks like an extremely hot cell group. Low refers to the signal, not to the temperature.",[["h","Sensor F signal wire chafed and shorted to ground"],["m","Internally shorted thermistor F"],["l","Faulty input on the battery sensing board"]],["MIL after two trips","Battery cooling running at high speed","Strongly reduced charge and drive power"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0C36":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit High","P0C36 means the voltage from battery temperature sensor F sits above its valid range. With GM's NTC thermistor and pull-up, an open circuit, a broken wire or an unplugged connector pulls the input up and reads as an impossibly cold cell group.",[["h","Open circuit or unlatched connector of sensor F"],["m","Thermistor F open internally"],["l","Module software setting the code falsely in the cold (GM Volt)"]],["MIL after two trips","Battery too cold, plug in to warm message","Reduced electric power"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0C37":["Hybrid/EV Battery Temperature Sensor F Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","P0C37 targets a sensor F signal that jumps instead of following the slow temperature change of a heavy battery. GM documents the logic in its 2016 to 2018 hybrid tables (16OBDG02, 17OBDG02, 18OBDG02): every 100 ms the controller adds up the absolute changes of the sensor value over a window of 10 samples; a sum above 10 fails the window, and 4 failed windows out of 5 set the code as Type B, two trips, with system voltage above 10.2 V and no communication fault active.",[["h","Loose terminal or connector of sensor F not fully latched after pack work"],["m","Cracked sensor wire with intermittent contact"],["l","Faulty battery sensing board, often with several sensors affected"]],["MIL after two trips","Intermittent hybrid warning, often on rough roads","Temporarily reduced power"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0C38":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The DC/DC converter steps the high-voltage battery down to the 12 V system and replaces the alternator in hybrids and EVs. Its power stage carries temperature sensors so the controller can derate before the semiconductors overheat.",[["h","Control unit software misjudging the sensor after long parking (Hyundai Palisade Hybrid)"],["m","Fault in the circuit of sensor A inside the converter or power control unit"],["l","Internal fault of the DC/DC converter requiring replacement"]],["Hybrid system warning light","12 V charging reduced or switched off","Code appearing after the car has stood for a long time"],["shop_only",[80,2000]],"c"],"P0C39":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Range/Performance","P0C39 is the plausibility check of DC/DC converter temperature sensor A: its reading lies within the electrical range but does not fit the converter's operating state or a second sensor. FCA uses it on the 2021 Ram 1500 with the 3.6 litre eTorque mild hybrid, where the code is called temperature sensor 1 performance.",[["m","Outdated hybrid control software (Ram 1500 eTorque, bulletin 08-037-21)"],["m","Sensor A drifting or with poor thermal contact to the power stage"],["l","Uneven cooling of the converter making the readings disagree"]],["MIL, after one trip with updated Ram software","Mild hybrid assist or 12 V supply reduced"],["shop_only",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0C3A":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Low","P0C3A means the signal of DC/DC converter temperature sensor A is below its valid range. Low refers to the signal voltage: with an NTC thermistor and pull-up, as GM uses for its hybrid temperature sensors, a short to ground or a failed element reads as extreme heat.",[["h","Sensor A signal shorted to ground inside the converter or its harness"],["m","Shorted temperature element A, usually requiring converter replacement"],["l","Converter really overheated due to a cooling fault"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning","12 V battery warning, system voltage dropping","Reduced power or restricted functions"],["shop_only",[150,2500]],"c"],"P0C3B":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A High","P0C3B reports a signal from DC/DC converter temperature sensor A above its valid range, typically a short to positive or an open circuit pulled up by the input. Mercedes-Benz documents it on series 243 (EQB) and 465 (G-Class, including the electric G 580): information LI54.10-P-079533 describes P0C3B00 as a short circuit to positive of temperature sensor A, stored together with P0E9700, P0E5400 and P0C4000 after faulty wake-up behaviour of the converter.",[["h","Faulty wake-up behaviour of the converter putting it into an error state (Mercedes 243/465)"],["m","Open circuit or short to positive in the sensor A circuit"],["l","Internal defect of the DC/DC converter"]],["Red 12 V battery warning in the cluster","White or yellow high-voltage system warning","12 V battery no longer charged while driving"],["shop_only",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0C3C":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor A Intermittent/Erratic","P0C3C covers a signal from DC/DC converter temperature sensor A that drops out or jumps for short moments while staying mostly within range. The heat sink of a converter warms and cools slowly, so fast jumps point to the signal path rather than to real temperature changes.",[["h","Loose terminal or moisture at the converter connector"],["m","Harness damaged by vibration with intermittent contact"],["l","Intermittent fault inside the converter"]],["Intermittent hybrid or EV warning","Occasional 12 V charging interruptions"],["shop_only",[80,2500]],"c"],"P0C3D":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Circuit","P0C3D is the general circuit code for the second temperature sensor of the DC/DC converter, which turns high-voltage battery power into 12 V supply. Converters with two sensors watch separate parts of the power stage or cross-check one sensor against the other.",[["h","Connector or wiring fault in the sensor B circuit"],["m","Failed temperature element B inside the converter"],["l","Converter or hybrid control software misjudging the sensor"]],["Hybrid or EV system warning","12 V battery warning"],["shop_only",[100,2500]],"c"],"P0C3E":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Range/Performance","P0C3E flags DC/DC converter temperature sensor B as electrically valid but implausible, for instance when it disagrees with sensor A or with what the converter load should produce. FCA uses it on the 2021 Ram 1500 with the 3.6 litre eTorque mild hybrid under the name temperature sensor 2 performance.",[["m","Outdated hybrid control software (Ram 1500 eTorque, bulletin 08-037-21)"],["m","Sensor B drifting or with poor thermal contact"],["l","Uneven converter cooling making sensors A and B disagree"]],["MIL, after one trip with updated Ram software","Mild hybrid assist or 12 V supply reduced"],["shop_only",[80,1800]],"c"],"P0C3F":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Low","The ECM detects a low fault on the dc/dc converter temperature sensor b. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal sensor short"]],[],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C40":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B High","The ECM detects a high fault on the dc/dc converter temperature sensor b. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to B+"],["m","Open sensor"]],[],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C41":["DC/DC Converter Temperature Sensor B Intermittent/Erratic","The ECM detects a intermittent/erratic fault on the dc/dc converter temperature sensor b. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Sensor connector not seated"],["m","Noisy ground reference"],["l","Connector with intermittent contact under vibration"]],[],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C42":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit","The ECM detects a circuit fault on the hybrid/ev battery pack coolant temperature sensor a circuit. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Sensor failed"],["m","Wiring or connector fault"],["l","Pack cell imbalance approaching service threshold"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C43":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Range/Performance","The ECM detects a range/performance fault on the hybrid/ev battery pack coolant temperature sensor a circuit. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Sensor characteristic drifted"],["m","Sensor mounting displaced"],["l","Sensor element aged beyond calibration window"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C44":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","The ECM detects a low fault on the hybrid/ev battery pack coolant temperature sensor a circuit. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal sensor short"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C45":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit High","The ECM detects a high fault on the hybrid/ev battery pack coolant temperature sensor a circuit. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to B+"],["m","Open sensor"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C46":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Temperature Sensor A Circuit Intermittent/Erratic","The ECM detects a intermittent/erratic fault on the hybrid/ev battery pack coolant temperature sensor a circuit. Reading cannot be used reliably for control.",[["h","Sensor connector not seated"],["m","Noisy ground reference"],["l","Connector with intermittent contact under vibration"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["easy",[120,600]],"c"],"P0C47":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Circuit/Open","The HV battery pack coolant pump A reports a control circuit/open condition. Battery thermal management may be impaired.",[["h","Open in pump control wire"],["m","Open pump motor"],["l","Pin pushed back out of connector body"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C48":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Circuit Low","The HV battery pack coolant pump A reports a control circuit low condition. Battery thermal management may be impaired.",[["h","Wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal motor short"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C49":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Circuit High","The HV battery pack coolant pump A reports a control circuit high condition. Battery thermal management may be impaired.",[["h","Wire shorted to B+"],["m","Open motor"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C4A":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Control Performance","The HV battery pack coolant pump A reports a control performance condition. Battery thermal management may be impaired.",[["h","Pump output degraded"],["m","Air pocket in coolant loop"],["l","Pack cell imbalance approaching service threshold"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C4B":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Supply Voltage Circuit/Open","The HV battery pack coolant pump A reports a supply voltage circuit/open condition. Battery thermal management may be impaired.",[["h","Open in supply wire"],["m","Supply fuse blown"],["l","Pin pushed back out of connector body"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C4C":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Supply Voltage Circuit Low","The HV battery pack coolant pump A reports a supply voltage circuit low condition. Battery thermal management may be impaired.",[["h","Supply wire shorted to ground"],["m","Supply rail collapsed"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C4D":["Hybrid/EV Battery Pack Coolant Pump A Supply Voltage Circuit High","The HV battery pack coolant pump A reports a supply voltage circuit high condition. Battery thermal management may be impaired.",[["h","Wire shorted to higher voltage"],["m","Alternator overvoltage"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced power output or limp mode","Charging may fail or be slowed"],["hard",[300,1800]],"c"],"P0C4E":["Drive Motor A Position Exceeded Learning Limit","The required adaptation offset for drive motor A position sensor exceeds the calibrated learning window. Sensor or rotor mechanism out of nominal range.",[["h","Sensor reference drifted"],["m","Mechanical drift in rotor mounting"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced motor power or torque","Possible limp mode or vehicle shutdown"],["hard",[400,2500]],"c"],"P0C4F":["Drive Motor B Position Exceeded Learning Limit","The required adaptation offset for drive motor B position sensor exceeds the calibrated learning window. Sensor or rotor mechanism out of nominal range.",[["h","Sensor reference drifted"],["m","Mechanical drift in rotor mounting"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced motor power or torque","Possible limp mode or vehicle shutdown"],["hard",[400,2500]],"c"],"P0C50":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A Circuit","The position sensor channel A of drive motor A reports a circuit fault. Phase commutation feedback impaired on this channel.",[["h","Position sensor failed"],["m","Wiring fault"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced motor power or torque","Possible limp mode or vehicle shutdown"],["hard",[400,2500]],"c"],"P0C51":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A Range/Performance","The position sensor channel A of drive motor A reports a range/performance fault. Phase commutation feedback impaired on this channel.",[["h","Sensor mechanically offset"],["m","Electrical noise on signal line"],["l","Sensor element aged beyond calibration window"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced motor power or torque","Possible limp mode or vehicle shutdown"],["hard",[400,2500]],"c"],"P0C52":["Drive Motor A Position Sensor Circuit A Circuit Low","The position sensor channel A of drive motor A reports a circuit low fault. Phase commutation feedback impaired on this channel.",[["h","Signal wire shorted to ground"],["m","Internal sensor short"],["l","Connector pins corroded - high contact resistance"]],["EV/hybrid system warning light on","Reduced motor power or torque","Possible limp mode or vehicle shutdown"],["hard",[400,2500]],"c"],"P0C53":["Dr